7130,dsg-challenge-1-supervised-learning,"# Description

## Description

Welcome to the ""Supervised Learning Challenge"", the First of our many upcoming challenges! In this competition, you'll use a unique dataset of features extracted from seven videos. Your task is to build machine learning models to identify different gesture phases in these videos.

You can get the data in the data section.
### ANY ADDITIONAL FINDINGS FROM THE DATA WILL BE DULY REWARDED
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Tasks:
- Gesture Classification: Label each segmented part with the type of gesture it represents
- This is an open-ended challenge 

## Bonus Task
- Cluster the data to find various hand movements/positions through unsupervised learning.
----------------------
# SUBMISSION GUIDELINES

## GUIDELINES
1. The solution is customary ```test.csv``` data is NOT the raw data, it is processed data.
2. You are expected to apply multiple approaches and perform Exploratory Analysis.
3. Compile all the results in the beginning of the notebook in a markdown file.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Details:
The dataset includes:

- Seven videos of people making different hand gestures.
- For each video, two files containing 50 carefully chosen attributes that describe the gestures

# Gesture Phase Segmentation Dataset

## Attribute Information:

### Raw Files:

1. `lhx` - Position of left hand (x coordinate)
2. `lhy` - Position of left hand (y coordinate)
3. `lhz` - Position of left hand (z coordinate)
4. `rhx` - Position of right hand (x coordinate)
5. `rhy` - Position of right hand (y coordinate)
6. `rhz` - Position of right hand (z coordinate)
7. `hx` - Position of head (x coordinate)
8. `hy` - Position of head (y coordinate)
9. `hz` - Position of head (z coordinate)
10. `sx` - Position of spine (x coordinate)
11. `sy` - Position of spine (y coordinate)
12. `sz` - Position of spine (z coordinate)
13. `lwx` - Position of left wrist (x coordinate)
14. `lwy` - Position of left wrist (y coordinate)
15. `lwz` - Position of left wrist (z coordinate)
16. `rwx` - Position of right wrist (x coordinate)
17. `rwy` - Position of right wrist (y coordinate)
18. `rwz` - Position of right wrist (z coordinate)
19. `timestamp` - Timestamp of the frame
20. `phase`: 
    - Rest
    - Preparation
    - Stroke
    - Hold
    - Retraction

### Processed Files:

1. Vectorial velocity of left hand (x coordinate)
2. Vectorial velocity of left hand (y coordinate)
3. Vectorial velocity of left hand (z coordinate)
4. Vectorial velocity of right hand (x coordinate)
5. Vectorial velocity of right hand (y coordinate)
6. Vectorial velocity of right hand (z coordinate)
7. Vectorial velocity of left wrist (x coordinate)
8. Vectorial velocity of left wrist (y coordinate)
9. Vectorial velocity of left wrist (z coordinate)
10. Vectorial velocity of right wrist (x coordinate)
11. Vectorial velocity of right wrist (y coordinate)
12. Vectorial velocity of right wrist (z coordinate)
13. Vectorial acceleration of left hand (x coordinate)
14. Vectorial acceleration of left hand (y coordinate)
15. Vectorial acceleration of left hand (z coordinate)
16. Vectorial acceleration of right hand (x coordinate)
17. Vectorial acceleration of right hand (y coordinate)
18. Vectorial acceleration of right hand (z coordinate)
19. Vectorial acceleration of left wrist (x coordinate)
20. Vectorial acceleration of left wrist (y coordinate)
21. Vectorial acceleration of left wrist (z coordinate)
22. Vectorial acceleration of right wrist (x coordinate)
23. Vectorial acceleration of right wrist (y coordinate)
24. Vectorial acceleration of right wrist (z coordinate)
25. Scalar velocity of left hand
26. Scalar velocity of right hand
27. Scalar velocity of left wrist
28. Scalar velocity of right wrist
29. Scalar acceleration of left hand
30. Scalar acceleration of right hand
31. Scalar acceleration of left wrist
32. Scalar acceleration of right wrist
33. `phase`:
    - D (rest position, from Portuguese ""descanso"")
    - P (preparation)
    - S (stroke)
    - H (hold)
    - R (retraction)

## Acknowledgement

The Gesture Phase Segmentation Dataset consists of temporal segmentation of gestures performed by gesture researchers to preprocess videos for further analysis. The description of the gesture phase segmentation problem may be found in MADEO et al. (2013a) and MADEO et al. (2013b).

The dataset includes seven videos recorded using Microsoft Kinect sensors. Three different users were asked to read three comic strips and tell the stories in front of the sensor. For each frame, the dataset contains an image identified by a timestamp and a text file containing positions (coordinates x, y, z) of six articulation points - left hand, right hand, left wrist, right wrist, head, and spine - with each line corresponding to a frame and identified by a timestamp. Manual segmentation of each file by a specialist provided a ground truth for classification.

The dataset is organized into 14 files, seven raw files, and seven processed files, one for each video in the dataset. The name of each file refers to the corresponding video, with the letter corresponding to the user (A, B, C) and the number corresponding to the story (1, 2, 3). The raw files contain information obtained from Microsoft Kinect, as described above. The processed file contains vectorial and scalar velocity and acceleration of left hand, right hand, left wrist, and right wrist. These measures were obtained after normalizing positions of the hands and wrists relative to the position of the head and spine, using a displacement of 3 to measure velocity, as described in MADEO et al. (2013c).
""""""
## Citation
>Madeo,Renata, Wagner,Priscilla, and Peres,Sarajane. (2014). Gesture Phase Segmentation. UCI Machine Learning Repository. https://doi.org/10.24432/C5Z32C.",False,True
7131,sarima-models,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

This contest is an activity of an analytic course in Tecnol¨®gico de Monterrey for industrial engineers. The purpose is to forecast six time series (no particular topic) using Minitab and Python. Minitab is a powerful tool to monitor and optimize
industrial processes, how good could be forecasting stationary and non-stationary time series? This contest will be test in that direction.

## Context

Since this is a course activity, the objective is to test the students knowledge in the Box-Jenkins methodology the purpose is to forecast the time series by using the SARIMA model. Furthermore, since the students have an industrial engineering major the forecast will be obtained by using the statistical software Minitab. Newcomers are invited to use their prefered tools.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error**. 
---

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `y`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,y
791,0.0
792,0.0
793,0.0
794,0.0
795,0.0
796,0.0
```","# Dataset Description

In this competition, you will forecast values for six times series with different behaviours. The training data includes an identifier for each series, an index and the value of the series.

## File descriptions

*  **train.csv.** The training set contains four columns: the id (identifier), Serie (id from 1 to 6), indice (index), and y (the value).
*   **test.csv.** The test set has the same features that the train set, but you will have to forecast the target values y.
*   **sample_submission.csv.** Shows the correct format for the submission.",False,True
