7139,pesumcadatathon23,"# Description

<p><h2> Tic-tac-toe </h2> (also known as noughts and crosses or Xs and Os) is a paper-and-pencil game for two players, X and O, who take turns marking the spaces in a 3¡Á3 grid. The player who succeeds in placing three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal row wins the game.</p>

<p> Given is the some experiences of winning or losing a game when the the player has opted for the moves at each positions in the grid. The total positions being 9. The chance of having values at each column can be X or O or b for blank</p> 
<img src=¡±C:/Users/Thenmozhi/Desktop/xo game¡±>

<h2> Goal <br> Predict the Outcome of the game </h2>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p> Evalation metric is classification accuracy </p>
<h1> Answer Submission </h1>
<p>The submission file should contain the predicted values of the test data given in the Data folder. The predicted values should be written in a csv file along with the id column of the test data. The result file have to be submitted </p>","# Dataset Description

<p> The data represents the possible values of X or O at each location of the 3x3 grid. The grid positions are the attributes and the values are represented as strings where it may have  x, o or b for blank </p>",False,True
7140,tamil-hwcr,"# Description

Welcome to the Tamil Handwritten Character Recognition Challenge, where the beauty and complexity of the Tamil script meet cutting-edge machine learning techniques! In this exciting Kaggle competition, participants are invited to showcase their skills in developing accurate and robust algorithms for recognizing handwritten Tamil characters.

Tamil is one of the oldest languages in the world, with a rich cultural heritage spanning centuries. Its script is intricate and diverse, composed of consonants, vowels, and various diacritic marks that give rise to a multitude of characters. Handwriting styles can vary widely, presenting a unique challenge for character recognition systems.

Your task in this competition is to create a model that can take an image of a handwritten Tamil character as input and correctly classify it into one of the many possible character categories. Participants will be provided with a comprehensive dataset of labeled handwritten Tamil characters, spanning various fonts, styles, and handwriting qualities. This dataset has been carefully curated to reflect the real-world challenges of character recognition in Tamil script.

Whether you're an experienced machine learning practitioner or a curious newcomer, this competition offers an opportunity to make a significant impact on the field of character recognition while immersing yourself in the beauty of the Tamil language. Participants are encouraged to experiment with various neural network architectures, pre-processing techniques, data augmentation strategies, and any other innovative approaches that can lead to higher accuracy and generalization.

Participants will be evaluated based on their model's accuracy in classifying the test set of handwritten Tamil characters. The competition encourages collaboration and learning, so don't hesitate to join the discussion forums, share insights, and exchange ideas with fellow competitors.

Get ready to embark on a journey that combines technology, culture, and creativity in the realm of Tamil Handwritten Character Recognition. We can't wait to see the remarkable solutions you'll develop in this competition!

The dataset provided in this competition is part of the work presented by us in [1].

## Reference
1. N. Shaffi and F. Hajamohideen, ""[uTHCD: A New Benchmarking for Tamil Handwritten OCR](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=9481898),"" in IEEE Access, vol. 9, pp. 101469-101493, 2021, doi: 10.1109/ACCESS.2021.3096823.

2. The uTHCD database homepage, https://www.kaggle.com/faizalhajamohideen/uthcdtamil-handwritten-database","# Dataset Description

**About the data:**
What files do I need? The participant needs images present in the train and test folder, and corresponding train.csv file to train the model.
Number of Train samples: **62870**
Number of Test samples: **28080**

For more details about the dataset, refer: *N. Shaffi and F. Hajamohideen, ""uTHCD: A New Benchmarking for Tamil Handwritten OCR,"" in IEEE Access, vol. 9, pp. 101469-101493, 2021, doi: 10.1109/ACCESS.2021.3096823.*

**What should I expect the data format to be?** The image files are in BMP format. The CSV files consists of two columns (ID) and corresponding class label in the second column.

What am I predicting? The class label of the input image consisting of isolated Tamil handwritten character sample

Files
**train.csv** - the training set consists of two columns. The first column (ID) consists of filename and the second column (Class Label) consists of corresponding ground truth label.
**test.csv** - the test set consists of just the filenames in the only column (ID).
**sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format. The Class Label column is mapped to String format (not int value).
**ReadMe.pdf** - supplemental information about the data
**Columns**
ID - filename of each sample in train/test folder.
Class Label - Represents the class label of the Tamil character image.",False,True
