7164,nfl-quarter-scores,"# Description

Sports gambling and sports prediction gaming have surged in popularity in recent years. As a result, some gamblers/gamers are trying to find an edge any way they can. Here's a thought: what if you could apply machine learning and predictive modeling techniques to determine the number of points each National Football League team earns in each quarter of every game?

That's exactly what this competition is trying to determine. Put another way, the goal is for players to predict what the final box score will look like.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7144740%2Fed8502cc3fb8ad1075ed745580cf0add%2FBOXSCOREFORKAGGLE.jpg?generation=1694025044545309&alt=media)

If you're familiar with NFL scoring rules, you know that quarterly scoring totals are neither random nor linear. For example, it's much more likely for a team to score exactly 7 or 10 points in a quarter than 5. To normalize this somewhat, this competition will award correct predictions as determined by the odds of the specific scoring total occurring. In other words, you'll be awarded more competition points for correctly guessing that a team amassed precisely 13 points in a quarter as opposed to just 3.

The odds will be calculated based on the probability of outcomes seen historically and broken down into four categories (based on team, home vs. away, quarter, and points scored):

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7144740%2F7b4bef9b9a105c30ec3319c7500a7465%2FKaggle%20odds%20table.png?generation=1697488011905630&alt=media)
*- calculate the odds using this formula: 
odds = (100 - percentage) / percentage

You can refer to the **NFL_Scoring_and_Odds_historical.csv** spreadsheet by accessing the Data tab.

In order to get you started, you'll have access to the quarterly score totals for every NFL game from 2000 through 2022; as well as the probability and odds for each team based on home/away, quarter, and points scored. You'll also see the final box scores for this season as they become available. You'll find this information under the Data tab above. 

##Your mission, should you choose to accept it...

The ultimate goal is to predict the quarterly scores for the NFL games played from December 7-31, 2023. That consists of 15 games in Week 14 of the season, and 16 games in Weeks 15, 16, and 17. (We won't ask you to predict Week 18 contests because various end-of-season factors may skew those results.) 

The score predictions are all discrete integers, and they are not cumulative from quarter to quarter. In addition, we won't ask you to predict any potential overtime scoring, and you needn't enter a total final score into your submissions. If you have any questions or comments, feel free to submit them via the Discussion tab above.

So go ahead and sign up your team today, and happy modeling!

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


----------------------
# Evaluation

You do not need to show your model. All you must submit is a csv file with the predicted game scores for each week using the weekly templates provided to you.

You will submit a set of scores for games played each week for four consecutive weeks. The deadlines for submission are as follows:

8:15pm Eastern Standard Time on Thursday, **December 7** (for Week 14 games)
8:15pm Eastern Standard Time on Thursday, **December 14** (for Week 15 games)
8:15pm Eastern Standard Time on Thursday, **December 21** (for Week 16 games)
8:15pm Eastern Standard Time on Thursday, **December 28** (for Week 17 games)

Each correct prediction receives one point multiplied by the odds of that guess. For example, a correct guess of 0 nets you 1 * 2.5(to 1 odds) = 2.5 points, while a correct guess of 4 would earn you a whopping 1 * 275(to 1 odds) = 275 points. 
The standings will be posted weekly under the Leaderboard tab of this page. The entry with the most total points after the games played on Sunday, December 31 will be the winner.","# Dataset Description

To assist you in your predictions, I have compiled a historical database of ALL of the National Football League games played from the year 2000 until last season. The current season's scores will be appended each week with updated scores and compiled in a separate file for your perusal. Of course, you are also welcome (and encouraged) to leverage external data and information about NFL games in order to improve your models.

I've also included four submission scoring templates to make it easy for you to submit your predictions. Just download each template and fill in the quarterly scores for each game. Keep in mind that there are no final scores or overtime points which need to be entered. Finally, be mindful of the weekly deadlines!",False,True
