7185,paper-prophet-challenge,"# Overview

## Paper Multilabel Classification Challenge
In the realm of knowledge, research papers are the building blocks of progress. Covering diverse fields from science to humanities, these papers house invaluable insights. Yet, with the exponential growth of research, organizing and categorizing these documents manually is a complex challenge. To tackle this, we can automate the process of classifying the research papers into the diverse categories in a particular field of research. 


## Problem Statement
You will be provided with a dataset which consists of data about research papers published in the field of computer science such as the title, summary, publishing date, etc. You have to build a ML model which will categorize these research papers into the sub-categories existing in the field of computer science.


## Dataset:
You are provided two splits of data - train and test. Navigate to the Data Section for more details regarding these.


## Duration:
The coding challenge will be open to submissions from the 29th of September to the 6th of October (EOD).


## Submission:
You will have to submit the solution in the form of a .csv file, with the predicted values for each test case (using the ID column to denote the case). A sample submission file (sample_submission.csv) has been provided along with the dataset, you may simply fill the predictions column in the same before uploading it as the solution. But you are not done yet!

You also need to submit the link to your Google Colab Notebook (https://colab.research.google.com/) containing the code you used for generating the submission file. Kindly set view access to 'Everyone' while generating the shareable link.


## Acknowledgements:
This challenge was created by Abhinav Gangil and Soham Pandit.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation will be based on accuracy.
         $$  accuracy= \frac{\text(No. of \quad Correct \quad Predictions)}{(No. of \quad Total  \quad Predictions)} $$



---


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict labels for the TARGET variable(i.e categories). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| ID | categories |
| --- | --- |
 |0607023v1|cs.AI|
 |0607024v1|cs.CV|
 |0607025v1|cs.CV cs.AI |","# Dataset Description

The dataset comprises of three csv files namely trainingDATA.csv,testingDATA.csv,sample_submission.csv.
`trainingDATA` contains columns `ID, title,summary,year_time,categories`.
Below is the description of each attribute-
1)`ID`-specifies a unique name to a row.
2)`title`-gives title of the paper.
3)`summary`-contains the summary of the paper.
4)`year_time`-as the name suggests.
5)`categories`-This is the target (the attribute to be predicted)

labels in the categories will have 40 types of labels-
`      'cs.CV', 'cs.NE', 'cs.MA', 'cs.RO', 'cs.CL', 'cs.LG', 'cs.AI',
       'cs.CR', 'cs.HC', 'cs.IR', 'cs.GT', 'cs.SE', 'cs.CY', 'cs.ET',
       'cs.NI', 'cs.MM', 'cs.SI', 'cs.CC', 'cs.DB', 'cs.IT', 'cs.PL',
       'cs.DS', 'cs.SD', 'cs.LO', 'cs.DL', 'cs.DC', 'cs.OH', 'cs.CE',
       'cs.AR', 'cs.FL', 'cs.GR', 'cs.MS', 'cs.CG', 'cs.SC', 'cs.PF',
       'cs.OS', 'cs.DM', 'cs.NA', 'cs.SY', 'cs.GL'`


## Files

*   **trainingDATA.csv** - the training set
*   **testingDATA.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.csv** - supplemental information about the data",False,True
7186,oemc-hackathon-eu-land-cover-classification,"# Overview

Challenge hosted by the [Open-Earth-Monitor (OEMC) project](https://earthmonitor.org/) aiming to promote the development of machine learning solutions for European land cover mapping, targeting **young researchers**, **students** and **members of the GIS community**. OEMC wants to connect the challenge participants with cutting edge developments and experts in the field of machine learning and remote sensing, allowing them to improve, share knowledge and build a creditable background within Earth Observation applications.

The winners will be awarded with the opportunity to assist and present their results at the [OEMC Global Workshop](https://earthmonitor.org/gw2023/) taking place in **Bolzano, Italy from 4-6 of October**. This international event brings together European and global policy-makers, business managers, researchers and civil society to discuss solutions for environmental monitoring based on open-source, Earth Observation data and existing geospatial applications.

----------------------
# Description

## Goal of the challenge

The participants need to classify **72 land cover** classes (ex. `Bare land`, `Grassland without tree/shrub cover`, `Coniferous woodland`, `Potatoes`, `Vineyards`) over five different years (`2006`, `2009`, `2012`, `2015`, `2018`), using more than 42,237 in-situ samples and 416 features / covariates derived through EO data / satellite images. The test set contains about 42,271 samples that are not present in the train set.

In this challenge, the participants have the chance to push the boundaries of the state of the art knowledge and ML approaches applied to land cover classification using a high thematic resolution dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation


The submissions are evaluated using **weighed F1-score** between the predicted and expected land cover classes for the test set:

```
F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)
```

## Submission File
For each `sample_id` in the test set, the participants must predict the land cover class . The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
sample_id,land_cover
1,16
2,17
3,39
```
----------------------
# Timeline

* **September 1, 2023** - Start date.

* **September 15, 2023** - Final submission deadline.

* **September 19, 2023** - Organization will contact the winners.

* **October 4, 2023** - Official announcement of the winners in the [OEMC Global Workshop](https://earthmonitor.org/gw2023/)

Deadlines are at 11:59 PM CET on each day, unless stated otherwise. Organizers can adjust the timeline if needed.
----------------------
# Prizes

Prizes
* **1st Place** - Travel to [OEMC Global Workshop](https://earthmonitor.org/gw2023/) (Bolzano, Italy - 2023, travel costs will be covered by OEMC; with the option to join the next OEMC Global Workshop in 2024 instead if the participant is not available in 2023) + PeerJ Lifetime subscription 
* **2nd Place** - PeerJ Lifetime subscription

Special prizes:
* Processing credits for [OpenEO Cloud](https://openeo.cloud/) for top-3 most accurate solutions
----------------------
# Acknowledgments

This challenge is hosted by [OpenGeoHub (OGH)](https://opengeohub.org/) and co-organized by the International Institute for [Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA)](https://iiasa.ac.at/), [CREAF](https://www.creaf.cat/), [GILAB](https://gilab.rs/), the [Institute for Geoinformatics of the M¨¹nster University (IFGI)](https://www.uni-muenster.de/Geoinformatics/en/), [Terrasigna](http://www.terrasigna.com/?ref=logo) and, [Eurac Research](https://www.eurac.edu/en). 

The OEMC project has received funding from the European Union's Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under [grant agreement No. 101059548](https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/101059548).","# Dataset Description

The dataset (both train and test) was produced by stratified sampling of the **ground-truth** data provided by LUCAS Survey, funded by the European Commission. The target land cover considered **level-3** classes from the harmonized legend, resulting in **72 classes** distributed over **5 years**(`2006`, `2009`, `2012`, `2015`, `2018`):

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16511159%2Fcf6e3dcd8fecb420dfe4ac7c8c9bdecb%2Ftrain_set.png?generation=1693388819625991&alt=media)

All samples were overlaid with **416** raster spatial layers, including satellite (spectral bands and indices) and temperature images (land surface temperature), climate images (precipitation, air temperature), accessibility and distance maps (highways, water bodies, burned areas), digital terrain model (slope and elevation) and other existing maps (population count and snow covering). The result values were organized in columns, one for each spatial layers, which combined represent the feature space available for ML modeling.  

## Column names:

The columns are formed by six metadata fields separated by `_`:

- Example: **red_landsat.glad.ard_p50_30m_jun25_sep12**
- Metadata fields:
 - F1 - Variable name: **red**
 - F2 - Variable procedure including product name: **landsat.glad.ard**
 - F3 - Position in the probability distribution: **p50**
 - F4 - Spatial resolution: **30m**
 - F5 - Start date: **jun25**
 - F6 - End date: **sep12**

## Column description:

All the columns can be aggregated in six thematic groups according to F1 and F2:

- **Satellite images (spectral reflectance & vegetation indices):**
 - `blue_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat blue band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
 - `blue_mod13q1_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 blue band ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `evi_mod13q1.stl.trend.ols.alpha_{..}`: Alpha coefficient / intercept (derived by [OLS](https://www.statsmodels.org/devel/generated/statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS.html)) over the deseasonalized monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI) index ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `evi_mod13q1.stl.trend.ols.beta_{..}`: Beta coefficient / trend (derived by [OLS](https://www.statsmodels.org/devel/generated/statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS.html)) over the deseasonalized monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI) index ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `evi_mod13q1.stl.trend_{..}`: Deseasonalized monthly time-series (trend component of [STL](https://www.statsmodels.org/dev/generated/statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.STL.html#statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.STL)) for MOD13Q1 Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI) index ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `evi_mod13q1_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI) index ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `green_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat green band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
 - `mir_mod13q1_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 mid-infrared band ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `ndvi_mod13q1_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 normalized vegetation index (NDVI) ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `nir_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat near-infrared band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
 - `nir_mod13q1_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 near-infrared band ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `red_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat red band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
 - `red_mod13q1_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 red band ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v006/))
 - `swir1_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat short-wave infrared-1 band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
 - `swir2_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat short-wave infrared-1 band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
- **Temperature images:**
  - `lst_mod11a2.daytime_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 day time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.daytime.{month}_{..}`: Long-term monthly aggregation (2000¡ª2022) for MOD13Q1 day time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.daytime.trend_{..}`: Deseasonalized monthly time-series (trend component of [STL](https://www.statsmodels.org/dev/generated/statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.STL.html#statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.STL)) for MOD13Q1 day time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.daytime.trend.ols.alpha_{..}`: Alpha coefficient / intercept (derived by [OLS](https://www.statsmodels.org/devel/generated/statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS.html)) over the deseasonalized monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 day time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.daytime.trend.ols.beta_{..}`: Beta coefficient / trend (derived by [OLS](https://www.statsmodels.org/devel/generated/statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS.html)) over the deseasonalized monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 day time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.nighttime_{..}`: Monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 night time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.nighttime.{month}_{..}`: Long-term monthly aggregation (2000¡ª2022) for MOD13Q1 day time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.nighttime.trend_{..}`: Deseasonalized monthly time-series (trend component of [STL](https://www.statsmodels.org/dev/generated/statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.STL.html#statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.STL)) for MOD13Q1 night time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.nighttime.trend.ols.alpha_{..}`: Alpha coefficient / intercept (derived by [OLS](https://www.statsmodels.org/devel/generated/statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS.html)) over the deseasonalized monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 night time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `lst_mod11a2.nighttime.trend.ols.beta_{..}`: Beta coefficient / trend (derived by [OLS](https://www.statsmodels.org/devel/generated/statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS.html)) over the deseasonalized monthly time-series of MOD13Q1 night time land surface temperature ([EarthData](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v006/))
 - `thermal_landsat.glad.ard_{..}`: Quarterly time-series of Landsat thermal band ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478))
- **Climate layers:**
 - `accum.precipitation_chelsa.annual_{..}`: Accumulated precipitation over the entire year according to CHELSA timeseries in `mm` of water ([Karger et al., 2017](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2017.122))
 - `accum.precipitation_chelsa.annual.3years.dif_{..}`: 3-years difference considering the yearly accumulated precipitation according to CHELSA timeseries in `mm` of water ([Karger et al., 2017](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2017.122))
 - `accum.precipitation_chelsa.annual.log.csum_{..}`: Cumulative sum, in logarithmic space, consdering the yearly accumulated precipitation according to CHELSA timeseries ([Karger et al., 2017](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2017.122))
 - `accum.precipitation_chelsa.montlhy_{..}`: Accumulated precipitation for each month according to CHELSA timeseries in `mm` of water ([Karger et al., 2017](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2017.122))
 - `bioclim.var_chelsa.{variable_code}_{..}`: Bioclimatic variables derived variables from the monthly mean, max, mean temperature, and mean precipitation values. For `variable_code` descriptions see [chelsa-climate.org](https://chelsa-climate.org/bioclim/) ([Karger et al., 2017](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2017.122))
- **Accessibility & distance maps:**
 - `accessibility.to.ports_map.ox.{variable_code}_{..}`: Time-required to access ports of different size according to [Nelson et al., 2019](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-019-0265-5)
 - `burned.area.distance_global.fire.atlas_{..}`: Distance to burned areas mapped by [Global Fire Atlas](https://doi.org/10.3334/ORNLDAAC/1642)
 - `cost.distance.to.coast_gedi.grass.gis_{..}`: Cumulative cost of moving (derived by [r.cost](https://grass.osgeo.org/grass83/manuals/r.cost.html)) to the coast
 - `road.distance_osm.highways.high.density_{..}`: Distance to high density of roads according to [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/#map=8/52.154/5.295)
 - `road.distance_osm.highways.low.density_{..}`: Distance to low density of roads according to [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/#map=8/52.154/5.295)
 - `water.distance_glad.interanual.dynamic.classes_{..}`: Distance to permanent / seasonal water bodies according to 
 [Pickens et al., 2020](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2020.111792)
- **Digital terrain model (DTM):**
 - `elev.lowestmode_gedi.eml_{..}`: Mean estimate of the terrain elevation in `dm` filtered using [SAGA GIS Gaussian filter](https://saga-gis.sourceforge.io/saga_tool_doc/6.2.0/grid_filter_1.html) ([Witjes et al., 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15478)) 
 - `slope.percent_gedi.eml_{..}`: Mean slope  in `%` derived from terrain elevation ([Witjes et al., 2023]
- **Other existing maps:**
 - `pop.count_ghs.jrc_{..}`: Annual time-series of population count in number of people mapped by [Schiavina et al., 2023](https://data.jrc.ec.europa.eu/dataset/2ff68a52-5b5b-4a22-8f40-c41da8332cfe)
 - `snow.duration_global.snowpack_{..}`: Annual duration of snow occurrence mapped by [Global SnowPack](https://www.dlr.de/eoc/desktopdefault.aspx/tabid-8297/14218_read-37938/)

## Files

*   **train.csv**: Training set with 42,237 rows and 420 columns, including sample id (`sample_id` - index column), land cover code (`land_cover`), land cover label (`land_cover_label`), reference year (`year`) and 416 features / covariates
*   **test.csv**: Test set with 42,271 rows and 418 columns, including sample id (`sample_id` - index column), reference year (`year`) and 416 features / covariates
*   **sample_submission.csv**: a sample submission file with 42,271 rows and 2 columns, including sample id (`sample_id` - index column) and predicted land cover code (`land_cover`)",False,True
