7190,vpn-classification,"# Overview

CrowdSec is a cybersecurity company that leverages crowdsourced collective intelligence to protect individual and business systems. Our open source software, the CrowdSec Security Engine, detects and reports threats and suspicious activities, allowing users to better guard their digital assets.

Whenever the Security Engine detects an attack, it sends a report back to our backend. This report contains very little information by itself, just the attack that was detected, the attacker¡¯s IP address, and a timestamp. In addition to the report, we also collect who reported the attack in order to build our trust and reputation system. What you are receiving as part of this challenge is an anonymized small sample of our raw backend database. 

In this challenge, we're inviting you to help us improve our threat detection algorithms by building a predictive model that can accurately classify whether an IP address is coming from a VPN or Proxy service. This is a critical element in cybersecurity as malicious users often use these services to anonymize their identity. 

Due to the limited nature of each data point, this challenge will heavily rely on creative feature engineering on the time-series for each attacker. If this is right up your alley, join the fun for a chance to win some swag and a coveted GPU to improve your data science throughput.

----------------------
# Description

Your task is to build a model that can accurately classify whether an IP address is associated with a VPN or Proxy service. Your models will be evaluated based on their performance on an unseen test set.

The provided dataset consists of a random sample of attack signals reported by CrowdSec's software over the past month. Each entry in the dataset represents an attack along with various other features derived from our threat detection systems. Due to the confidential nature of IP addresses in the GDPR, the addresses themselves have been anonymized with an integer ID, in all files `train.csv`, `test.csv` and `shodan_info_hashed.csv`. Please note that the dataset is fairly unbalanced as only around 5% of the entries are identified as originating from VPNs or proxies. 

For a further description of the data, check the `Data` section at the top of this page.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16365256%2Fa794e9af0467fd6b6f08d70167cc7ed1%2FVPN%20schema.png?generation=1695997241675018&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Given the imbalance in the dataset, accuracy would not be a fair metric. Instead, we will use the F1-score to evaluate your models. The F1 Score is configured in ¡®binary¡¯ mode, meaning it is only computed on the positive class (label : 1). For more details refer to [the sklearn doc](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html)

----------------------
# Resources

* Article on the current VPN Proxy classifier model, which gives more context: https://www.crowdsec.net/blog/ai-powered-proxy-and-vpn-detection
* See the demo notebook which provides further details
* How does CrowdSec Work? https://doc.crowdsec.net/docs/intro
----------------------
# Contact

*Ready to get started?* Here¡¯s where you can find support, if you need it.
- For topics related to the competition: **Join CrowdSec Discord Server** following this [link](https://discord.gg/crowdsec). You will find a dedicated **Kaggle Section**  with achat room (#chat) and support forum section (#support). Make sure to click the link in #welcome channel first to verify your account
- For technical help: [Kaggle Contact Us Page](https://www.kaggle.com/contact)

Keep in mind that we¡¯re a small team, so we might take time to respond, but still feel free to ping us on Discord if you need help.


----------------------
# Prizes

The 3 best submissions ranked by Kaggle will be gifted the following prizes
* 1st: GPU NVIDIA RTX 4080
* 2nd: Crowdsec swag
* 3rd: Crowdsec swag

Crowdsec swag are beautiful, fluffy, shiny alpacas under different form (hoodies, t-shirt, beanies, socks, stickers ... )




----------------------
# Acknowledgements

The data contained in the `shodan_info_hashed.csv` file comes from shodan.io, who provide threat intelligence on almost any device connected to the net. Their liberal use policy allowed us to proceed with this challenge without a hassle.","# Dataset Description

The provided dataset consists of a random sample of attack signals reported by CrowdSec's software over the past month. Each entry in the dataset represents an attack along with various other features derived from our threat detection systems. Due to the confidential nature of IP addresses in the GDPR, the addresses themselves have been anonymized with an integer ID, in all files `train.csv`, `test.csv` and `shodan_info_hashed.csv`. Please note that the dataset is fairly unbalanced as only around 5% of the entries are identified as originating from VPNs or proxies. 

## Files

*   **train.csv (6.2GB)** - the training set in CSV format
*   **train.parq (707MB)** - the training set in parquet format. 
*   **test.csv (1.9GB)** - the test set in CSV format
*   **test.parq (209 MB)** - the test set in parquet format
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **shodan_info_hashed.csv** - a supplemental dataset containing portscan information for attackers. It contains the information related to IPs in both train and test dataset.

## Important

**Note 1:** the train and test files contain several rows of signals per IPs, but you should predict only one label per IP in the submission. This also implies that the column ""label"" in the signals contains always the same value for a given IP.
**Note 2:** We provide as well parquet format for train and test files to speed up loading time. The data are of course equivalent between CSV and parquet format. 
Parquet is a binary format developed by Apache Arrow, which plays well with `pandas` (force use with `engine=""pyarrow""`) and `polars`library in python. The data types are already cast in an optimized way which lowers the memory footprint on your laptop.

**Note 3:** The data was split between a train and a test file using a stratified split with a 75/25 ratio based on the number of IP. Test file contains 49420 IPs you need to infer wheres the train file contains 148259 distinct attacker IPs.

## Columns description

| feature_name      | type           | description                                               |
|:------------------|:---------------|:----------------------------------------------------------|
| attack_time       | datetime64[ns] | The timestamp of the attack when received by Crowdsec     |
| watcher_country   | category         | The country linked to the IP address of the watcher       |
| watcher_as_num    | float32        | The AS number of the the IP address of the watcher        |
| watcher_as_name   | category         | The AS name of the the IP address of the watcher          |
| attacker_country  | category       | The country linked to the IP address of the attacker      |
| attacker_as_num   | float32        | The AS number of the the IP address of the attacker       |
| attacker_as_name  | category         | The AS name of the the IP address of the attacker         |
| attack_type       | category       | The attack type detected by Crowdsec                      |
| watcher_uuid_enum | int32          | The watcher unique ID                                     |
| attacker_ip_enum  | int32          | The attacker unique ID                                    |
| label             | int8       | Label of the attacker ID. 1 for VPN / Proxy. 0 Otherwise. |

## Submission file format

For each ID in the test set, you must predict the class of the label.
Your submission should be a csv file with the following header :
| attacker_ip_enum | label |
|:------------------|:-------|
| 2 | 0 | 
| .... | ... |",False,True
