7198,bioverse-hackathon-f,"# Overview

Welcome to the BioVerse Hackathon!

In this hackathon, use your skills in Machine Learning and Deep Learning to classify if breast tumors are beneign, malignant, or the lack of a tumor in the first place!

This is real data provided by Baheya.

The competition also includes an extra challenge. The amount of data is very small. Use your knowledge and research about few-shot learning to try to get the highest score possible!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The F1 score can be interpreted as a harmonic mean of the precision and recall, where an F1 score reaches its best value at 1 and worst score at 0.

The relative contribution of precision and recall to the F1 score are equal. The formula for the F1 score is:

$$F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)$$","# Dataset Description

## Files

* All training images are found in the ""train"" folder and they are organized into 3 folders for each class. The images are also saved in folders that signify patient ID.
*  All test images are found in the ""test"" folder. These are the images you will run the model on for predictions for your submissions.
*  sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7199,oemc-hackathon-global-fapar-modeling,"# Overview

Challenge hosted by the [Open-Earth-Monitor (OEMC) project](https://earthmonitor.org/) aiming to promote the development of monitoring solutions for global vegetation, targeting **young researchers**, **students** and **members of the GIS community**. OEMC wants to connect the challenge participants with cutting edge developments and experts in the field of modeling, machine learning and remote sensing, allowing them to improve, share knowledge and build a creditable background within Earth Observation applications.

The winners will be awarded with the opportunity to assist and present their results at the [OEMC Global Workshop](https://earthmonitor.org/gw2023/) taking place in **Bolzano, Italy from 4-6 of October**. This international event brings together European and global policy-makers, business managers, researchers and civil society to discuss solutions for environmental monitoring based on open-source, Earth Observation data and existing geospatial applications.
----------------------
# Description

## Goal of the challenge

The participants need to model **Fraction of Absorbed Photosynthetic Active Radiation (FAPAR)** of various kind of vegetation based on **46** ground-stations around the world. Each station has monthly FAPAR estimates, resulting in `3,461` individual samples for training and `4,939` in the test set. The samples have `32` features / covariates derived through EO data / satellite images.

In this challenge, the participants have the chance to push the boundaries of the state of the art knowledge for FAPAR modeling and global vegetation monitoring, with a high quality and accurate dataset derived through in-situ measurements. 
----------------------
# Evaluation


The submissions are evaluated using **Median Absolute Error (MedAE)** between the predicted and expected FAPAR values for the test set:

$$ \text{MedAE}(y, \hat{y}) = \text{median}(\mid y_1 - \hat{y}_1 \mid, \ldots, \mid y_n - \hat{y}_n \mid) $$

## Submission File
For each `sample_id` in the test set, the participants must predict the FAPAR value . The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
sample_id,fapar
1,0.1
2,0.5
3,0.7
```
----------------------
# Timeline

* **September 1, 2023** - Start date.

* **September 15, 2023** - Final submission deadline.

* **September 19, 2023** - Organization will contact the winners.

* **October 4, 2023** - Official announcement of the winners in the [OEMC Global Workshop](https://earthmonitor.org/gw2023/)

Deadlines are at 11:59 PM CET on each day, unless stated otherwise. Organizers can adjust the timeline if needed.
----------------------
# Prizes

Prizes
* **1st Place** - Travel to [OEMC Global Workshop](https://earthmonitor.org/gw2023/) (Bolzano, Italy - 2023, travel costs will be covered by OEMC; with the option to join the next OEMC Global Workshop in 2024 instead if the participant is not available in 2023) + PeerJ Lifetime subscription 
* **2nd Place** - PeerJ Lifetime subscription

Special prizes:
* Processing credits for [OpenEO Cloud](https://openeo.cloud/) for top-3 more accurate solutions
----------------------
# Acknowledgments

This challenge is hosted by [OpenGeoHub (OGH)](https://opengeohub.org/) and co-organized by the International Institute for [Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA)](https://iiasa.ac.at/), [CREAF](https://www.creaf.cat/), [GILAB](https://gilab.rs/), the [Institute for Geoinformatics of the M¨¹nster University (IFGI)](https://www.uni-muenster.de/Geoinformatics/en/), [Terrasigna](http://www.terrasigna.com/?ref=logo) and, [Eurac Research](https://www.eurac.edu/en). 

The OEMC project has received funding from the European Union's Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under [grant agreement No. 101059548](https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/101059548).","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains monthly mean FAPAR values aggregated by each ground station. FAPAR represents the fraction of the incoming (photosynthetic active) radiation that is absorbed by vegetation, and is given in the range `0-1`. It is a measure of vegetation health and ecosystem functioning, and a key parameter in light use efficiency models that model primary productivity. 

For each monthly FAPAR value, a set of covariates / features were extracted from **32**  raster spatial layers, including including satellite (spectral bands and indices) and temperature images (land surface temperature), climate images (precipitation) and digital terrain model (slope and elevation). The features are organized by columns, unique data points in time are identified by the `sample_id` column, and data points belonging to the same location are identified by `station_number`. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16511159%2F71ac7998fb6d95e0b08edc6e721a55ae%2Ffapar_data.jpg?generation=1693498335712311&alt=media)

## Column names:

- **sample_id**: Unique identifier of datapoint
- **station**: Ground station number
- **fapar**: Monthly mean FAPAR 
- **month**: Month of measurement 
- **modis_`{..}`**: Normalized Vegetation Index (NDVI),  Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI), spectral reflectance bands 1 (red), 2 (near-infrared), 3 (blue), and 7 (mid-infrared) monthly time-series based on [MOD13Q1](https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod13q1v061/)
- **modis_lst_day_p`{..}`**: Land surface temperatures day time of percentiles 5th, 50th and 95th monthly time-series based on [MOD11A2]( https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v061/)
- **modis_lst_night_p`{..}`**: Land surface temperatures night time of percentiles 5th, 50th and 95th monthly time-series based on [MOD11A2]( https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/mod11a2v061/)
- **wv_yearly_p`{..}`**: Water vapour aggregated yearly by percentiles 25th, 50th and 75th based on derived from [MCD19A2](https://zenodo.org/record/8226282)
- **wv_monthly_lt_p`{..}`**: Water vapour aggregated long-term monthly by percentiles 25th, 50th and 75th based on ([MCD19A2](https://zenodo.org/record/8226282)
- **wv_monthly_lt_sd**: Water vapour aggregated long-term monthly standard deviation based on ([MCD19A2](https://zenodo.org/record/8226282)
- **wv_monthly_ts_raw**: Water vapour monthly time series based on [MCD19A2](https://zenodo.org/record/8226282)
- **wv_monthly_ts_smooth**: Water vapour monthly time series smoothed using the Whittaker method based on ([MCD19A2](https://zenodo.org/record/8226282)
- **accum_pr_monthly**: Monthly accumulated precipitation based on [CHELSA timeseries](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2017.122)
- **dtm_`{..}`**: Several DTM derivatives (Elevation, Slope, aspect (sine, cosine), curvature (up- and downslope), openness (negative, positive), compound topographic index (cti), valley bottom flatness (vbf)) based on [MERIT DEM](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2017AGUFM.H12C..04Y)
**
## Files

*   **train.csv**: Training set with 3,461 rows and 36 columns, including sample id (`sample_id` - index column), ground station (`station`), reference month (`month`), measured FAPAR (`fapar`), and 32 features / covariates
*   **test.csv**: Test set with 4,939 rows and 34 columns, including sample id (`sample_id` - index column), ground station (`station`), reference month (`month`) and 32 features / covariates
*   **sample_submission.csv**: a sample submission file with 4,939 rows and 2 columns, including sample id (`sample_id` - index column) and measured FAPAR (`fapar`)",False,True
7200,bioverse-hackathon-a,"# Overview

Welcome to the BioVerse Hackathon!

In this hackathon, use your skills in Machine Learning and Deep Learning to classify if breast tumors are beneign, malignant, or the lack of a tumor in the first place!

This is real data provided by Baheya.

The competition also includes an extra challenge. The amount of data is very small. Use your knowledge and research about few-shot learning to try to get the highest score possible!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The F1 score can be interpreted as a harmonic mean of the precision and recall, where an F1 score reaches its best value at 1 and worst score at 0.

The relative contribution of precision and recall to the F1 score are equal. The formula for the F1 score is:

$$F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)$$","# Dataset Description

## Files

* All training images are found in the ""train"" folder and they are organized into 3 folders for each class. The images are also saved in folders that signify patient ID.
*  All test images are found in the ""test"" folder. These are the images you will run the model on for predictions for your submissions.
*  sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7201,prismap-831-python-coding-test,"# Description

This is a Python Test hosted by PrismaP Lab, Intelligent Critical Care Center for OPS positions. 

The project will provide sample datasets for patients with their [creatinine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creatinine) test results. 
The goal of the project is trying to annotate the patients who has [acute kidney injury (AKI)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acute_kidney_injury) based on provided criteria shown below.

If any of the following criterion is satisfied, we think patient has AKI.

* Current creatinine value >= 4 mg/dl
* Ratio between current creatinine and reference creatinine > 1.5
* Current creatinine - minimum creatinine within last 48 hours >= 0.3 mg/dl and Current creatinine - reference creatinine >= 0.3 mg/dl
> If there is no creatinine within last 48 hours, skip this condition.

* Patient have dialysis within past 7 days.

Three data frames are given. 
1. Patient reference creatinine dataframe. 
2. Creatinine value dataframe.
3. Dialysis time dataframe.

Please implement the codes and generate the result file:

1. For each creatinine, please label if patient has AKI (Y/N). 

The result will contain two columns, one is called `predicted` while the other one is called `id`.  
**Please check the `Evaluation` section to understand the submission requirements.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on **Absolute Error** metric. 

There are two columns expected in the results:

* **Expected**, it can be a float or integer value, 1 to indicate the existence of AKI, otherwise 0.
* **Id**, it will be used to identify the patient and measurement_datetime, please create the column by concating `patient_id` column and `measurement_datetime` column with `_` symbol. 

Ex. patient_id = 1, measurement_datetime = '2022-09-01 19:08:09',  id = '1_2022-09-01 19:08:09'","# Dataset Description

There are three files of data provided:

* reference_creatinine.csv, the file contains the creatinine test results for reference. 
* creatinine.csv, the file contains the creatinine test results for patients with timestamp of measurement. 
* dialysis_time.csv, the file contains the timestamp of [dialysis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kidney_dialysis) operation.",False,True
