- [Part 4](https://events.mlh.io/events/10136-ghw-web3-week-maching-learning-and-nfts-track-part-4)
- [Part 5](https://events.mlh.io/events/10143-ghw-web3-week-maching-learning-and-nfts-track-part-5)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on Mean Absolute Error (MAE).

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the price. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,price
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **images/images** - a directory containing all of the images for the competition in png format. The name of the file is the id of the row in the train or test dataset.

## Columns

*   `id` - the id of the record
*   `speed`?- the string defining the cooldown time of the cryptokitty in question
*   `image` - the filename of the image of the relevant cryptokitty, relative to the images directory",False,True
7213,ubs-23-nilm,"# Description

Dear participants, you are warmly welcome on UBS'23 hackathon.

Can you disaggregate distinct loads from joint consumption timeseries synthesized from per-second consumption profiles of real-world appliances?

Keywords
Time Series Analysis, Energy Disaggregation, Instant power, Power and Energy Systems
About Dataset

The joint instant active and reactive power consumption of a group of popular home appliances (heaters, lights, computers, etc.) connected in parallel is collected by a smart meter on per-second basis. The appliances are switched on and off, and the problem is to determine the list of appliances being switched on at every instant of time.
The list of 13 appliances and their indices can be found in the file classes.csv.

![Units for identification](https://energy.ipu.ru/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Screenshot-from-2022-08-24-18-03-18-1024x665.png)

In the experiment all 13 appliances were sequentially switched on and then, in some period of time, switched off, three times each, and the load profile for each trial is collected. The file appliance_profiles.csv provides raw data on the first two trials for each appliance. The file contains the timestamp, active power P (Watt), reactive power Q (VAR), voltage V (Volts), conductance G, mkS (calculated as P/V^2), susceptance B, mkS (calculated as Q/V^2), the index of appliance being tested, the trial number (1 or 2) and, finally the description of the class and trial.
The learning data set (file train.csv) is generated using appliances¡¯ load profiles from appliance_profiles.csv. The starting time for each appliance is randomly selected, so that several appliances can be on at one instant of time. The file contains total conductance and susceptance calculated as the sum of conductance/susceptance values of alive appliances. The class1, ¡­, class13 fields show alive appliances (1=alive and 0 otherwise) for every instant of time.
The testing data set contains a similar sequence generated from trials 2 and 3 collected during the above experiment. The class fields are eliminated and stored into results.csv file.
The winner will minimize the total Hamming distance of switch on/off indicatory vectors over the testing data set. In other words, a unit penalty will be assigned to every appliance incorrectly switched on/off in each row of the testing data set.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation file format is csv file with comma separation. The initial scheme you can find next. Basically it's train.csv file but without two columns, G. mkS,B. mkS, that go to test.csv file.

| ts |  class 1 |class 2 |...|class13|
| --- | --- |
| 15:00:00 |  0|0|...|0|
| 15:23:45 |  1|1|...|0|

The winner will minimize the total Hamming distance of switch on/off indicatory vectors over the testing data set. In other words, a unit penalty will be assigned to every appliance incorrectly switched on/off in each row of the testing data set.","# Dataset Description

The joint instant active and reactive power consumption of a group of popular home appliances (heaters, lights, computers, etc.) connected in parallel is collected by a smart meter on per-second basis. The appliances are switched on and off, and the problem is to determine the list of appliances being switched on at every instant of time.
The list of 13 appliances and their indices can be found in the file classes.csv.
In the experiment all 13 appliances were sequentially switched on and then, in some period of time, switched off, three times each, and the load profile for each trial is collected. The file appliance_profiles.csv provides raw data on the first two trials for each appliance. The file contains the timestamp, active power P (Watt), reactive power Q (VAR), voltage V (Volts), conductance G, mkS (calculated as P/V2), susceptance B, mkS (calculated as Q/V2), the index of appliance being tested, the trial number (1 or 2) and, finally the description of the class and trial.
The learning data set (file train.csv) is generated using appliances¡¯ load profiles from appliance_profiles.csv. The starting time for each appliance is randomly selected, so that several appliances can be on at one instant of time. The file contains total conductance and susceptance calculated as the sum of conductance/susceptance values of alive appliances. The class field enumerates the indices of alive appliances for every instant of time.",False,True
