7220,earthquake-prediction,"# Overview

Boston University [Center for Information & Systems Engineering (CISE)](https://www.bu.edu/cise/) is organizing a challenge to help better understand earthquakes and, in the longer term, contribute to advancing earthquake sciences using AI. Our goal as a community is to explore where AI can create a difference in this important field and create new collaborations and sustained interest in the topic going forward.

Participants of this Kaggle challenge will compete in predicting **earthquake timing and surface displacement**, based on the laboratory data provided by Penn State University (PSU) in collaboration with Los Alamos National Laboratory (LANL) supported by the Office of Science Basic Energy Sciences program.

Winners of the challenge (tentatively top 2 teams) will be recognized and awarded at the [AI for Understanding Earthquakes Workshop](https://www.bu.edu/cise/ai-challenge/) that will be held at Boston University on **November 10, 2023**.

This challenge will lead to a second prediction challenge using field data, details of which will be announced at a later time.
----------------------
# Details

The goal of this competition is to be able to predict end-point displacement and time-to-failure of a block-on-block friction sliding experiment based on acoustic emission (AE) data. This laboratory fault experiment is an example of the geological environment in which real-world earthquakes occur and designed to facilitate the understanding of the underlying processes. Details of the experimental setup can be found in [this paper]( https://www.pnas.org/content/pnas/118/5/e2011362118.full.pdf).

This challenge is split into two parts:

## Part 1 - Real-time Prediction 
Given AE waveforms, predict *end-point displacement (EP_disp)* and *time-to-failure (TTF)* at the corresponding timestep.

## Part 2 - Future Prediction 
Given AE waveforms, predict *future displacement*. For this part, you should use a window of 5 AE reading segments (i.e., 5 segments of 6000 readings each) and predict the end-point displacement values associated with the **three subsequent time steps.**

*Your submission should adhere to the defined prediction problem. If you are among the competition winners, you will be asked to provide your code and models.*
----------------------
# Submission File

For each time_stamp in the test dataset, you must predict ttf and the four EP_disp values. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```text
index,time_stamp,ttf,EP_disp,EP_disp_FUT_1,EP_disp_FUT_2,EP_disp_FUT_3
1,2814.286073,0,0,0,0,0
2,2814.286912,0,0,0,0,0
3,2814.287751,0,0,0,0,0
```
----------------------
# Evaluation

Predictions for ttf and all of the EP_disp values will be evaluated using mean absolute error between the predicted and actual values.

----------------------
# References

[1] Johnson, P. A., Rouet-Leduc, B., Pyrak-Nolte, L. J., Beroza, G. C., Marone, C. J., Hulbert, C., et al. (2021). Laboratory earthquake forecasting: A machine learning competition. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 118(5), e2011362118. https://www.pnas.org/content/pnas/118/5/e2011362118.full.pdf","# Dataset Description

The data for this competition was recorded continuously and then split sequentially as ``train`` and ``test`` data. It contains all the information necessary to complete both parts of the challenge.

## **File Descriptions**
**train.csv:** Data you can use to train your models. Includes features and label data.
**test.csv:** Acoustic emission measurements you should use to generate your predictions.
**sample_submission_long.csv:** Example of a properly formatted submission file.
## **Data Fields**
**time_stamp:** time at which acoustic emission, time-to-failure, and end-point displacement measurements were taken
**ae_1 through ae_6000:** acoustic emission signal
**ttf:** time-to-failure reading
**EP_disp:** end-point displacement measurement
**EP_disp_FUT_1:** end-point displacement measurement for the subsequent timestep
**EP_disp_FUT_2:** end-point displacement measurement two timesteps in the future
**EP_disp_FUT_3:** end-point displacement measurement three timesteps in the future

In ``train.csv``, the first column is the ``time_stamp`` at which the measurements in the corresponding row were taken. The next 6000 columns consist of acoustic emission readings. The goal is to predict the final five columns ``ttf``, ``EP_disp``, ``EP_disp_FUT_1``, ``EP_disp_FUT_2``, and ``EP_disp_FUT_3``.

In ``test.csv``, we just provide timestamps and the acoustic emission readings. From this data, your models should predict the ttf as well as displacement values for the current timestep and the next three future timesteps.

Data is collected in Chris Marone's rock physics lab at Penn State (https://personal.ems.psu.edu/~cjm38/).",False,True
