7232,neuronex-datathon-10,"# Overview

# Please Read this document carefully
Welcome to the Traffic Control Center at Illinois, you are a data scientist hired by the Illinois Department of Transportation to help develop a predictive model to understand traffic patterns and predict the traffic volume in the state. Your role will be to analyze data on traffic flow, weather patterns, and other factors to identify trends and make predictions about future traffic patterns. With this information, we hope to improve traffic flow and reduce congestion on Illinois highways and roads.

Traffic Volume is an essential metric for us as it helps us understand trends leading to high or low traffic volume based on historical data and current weather patterns. By doing so, we hope to optimize traffic flow and reduce congestion on Illinois highways and roads.

To help our citizens, we want to find the best performing model and are looking forward to seeing the results of your analysis and the performance of your predictive model in predicting traffic volume in Illinois. Your insights can help us make data-driven decisions to improve traffic flow and reduce congestion on highways and roads. Good luck with your analysis and modeling!

Your task is to analyze the provided dataset and use machine learning techniques to predict the Traffic Volume for the City based on temperature, rainfall, snowfall, cloudy conditions and other features. You will need to preprocess the data, perform feature engineering, and select an appropriate machine learning algorithm to build a predictive model.

To test the accuracy of your model, we will provide a test dataset with unseen data. The model's performance will be evaluated based on the **adjusted R-squared score**

We believe that your analysis and predictive model can help us predict the traffic density and find out what conditions make traffic a problem in the city. So, get ready to pack your bags and explore the world of data science with us!

This Kaggle competition challenges participants to use the Traffic Dataset from Illinois to develop accurate forecasting models. The dataset provides historical traffic flow readings and participants must build models that can predict future temperatures at these locations based on past readings.

# Instructions
To enter the competition, participants must first accept the Competition Rules and gain access to the dataset. They can then download the data and begin building models locally or on Kaggle Notebooks. Once they have generated a prediction file, they can upload it as a submission along with the notebook on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score with a position on the public leaderboard.

# Evaluation
The public leaderboard is calculated based on the adjusted R-squared score of the model created and how well it performs on the given test dataset. This allows participants to see how their models rank against those of other Kagglers.
----------------------
# Submission

Participants are required to submit the following:

CSV file containing the ID and traffic_volume.
Notebook containing EDA and training with reasonings and additional comments.
P.S. This section is important because you will be judged based on your reasoning ability for the choice of model, data pre-processing etc. so feel free to add markdown cells explaining your steps!

Please note, once submitted, your model will be tested against new unseen data points for final evaluation. This will determine your position on the private leaderboard which will be released once the First Round of the Datathon ends!

If you like working with Python, visualizing and understanding large datasets, you¡¯re in the right place. (Psst¡­ also if you took up the Data Analytics course, then brush up on those skills, and this is your time to shine! )

**Refer to sample_submission.csv in the Data tab for the format of the submission file.
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:**

```
ID,traffic_volume
0,100
1,200
3,300
4,400
5,500
etc.
```

ALL THE BEST AND GOOD LUCK TO ALL PARTICIPANTS :)
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric that will be used to test the goodness predictions is Adjusted R-squared score. It is given by the formula:

**$$\text{Adjusted R}^2 = 1 - \frac{(1-R^2)(n-1)}{n-k-1}$$**

where the terms stand for : 
**n** - The number of observations
**R^2** - The R2 of the model
**k** - The number of predictor variables","# Dataset Description

This dataset consists of hourly-daily traffic volume and density along with environmental factors of a city in the US from 2012 to 2018.
 
The data is split into two groups:

- Training set (train.csv)
- Test set (test.csv)
The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the values for each feature and the output variable (traffic_volume). You are free to perform any kind of data preprocessing, feature selection, and apply relevant algorithms to train and improve your model¡¯s performance.
The test set should be used to evaluate your model's performance on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the values for the target variables. It is your job to forecast these values.
However, you are required to submit only the forecasted values for **traffic_volume**.

Note:
Please note that the column ID is an encoding for a recording of data whenever there is a change in the weather. It starts at day 0 (encoded from 2nd of October of 2012) and continues.
The traffic_volume is a floating point value. The sample submission shows traffic_volume as an integer just to act as an easy reference.
In your submission.csv file you should have 2 columns ID (19281 values from 28923 to 48203) and traffic_volume only. You can see this clearly in the sample_solution.csv

Files
- train.csv - the training set
- test.csv - the test set
- sample_solution.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

# Data Dictionary
| Variable | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| holiday | A categorical variable that indicates whether the date is a US national holiday or a regional holiday (such as the Minnesota State Fair). |
| temp | A numeric variable that shows the average temperature in kelvin. |
| rain_1h | A numeric variable that shows the amount of rain in mm that occurred in the hour. |
| snow_1h | A numeric variable that shows the amount of snow in mm that occurred in the hour. |
| clouds_all | A numeric variable that shows the percentage of cloud cover. |
| weather_main | A categorical variable that gives a short textual description of the current weather (such as Clear, Clouds, Rain, etc.). |
| weather_description | A categorical variable that gives a longer textual description of the current weather (such as light rain, overcast clouds, etc.). |
| date_time | A datetime variable that shows the hour of the data collected in local CST time. |
| **traffic_volume** | **A numeric variable that shows the hourly I-94 reported westbound traffic volume. This is the target variable** |",False,True
7233,egoschema-public,"# Overview

We introduce EgoSchema, a diagnostic benchmark for assessing very long-form video-language understanding capabilities of modern multimodal systems. Derived from Ego4D, EgoSchema consists of over 5000 human curated multiple choice question answer pairs, spanning over 250 hours of real video data, covering a very broad range of natural human activity and behavior. For each question, EgoSchema requires the correct answer to be selected between five given options based on a three-minute-long video clip. More details at our [website](https://egoschema.github.io/) and [Github page](https://github.com/egoschema/egoschema).

**Note**:  EgoSchema is intended for a 0-shot evaluation benchmark, hence the entire correct answer file will not be made public.

## :dizzy: Dataset Highlights 

:exclamation: EgoSchema is 10x to 100x more difficult longer temporal reasoning than almost all other video datasets.
:exclamation: Largest OSS Video-Language models with 7B+ parameters achieve QA accuracy of <33% (Random choice is 20%). Humans achieve ~76%.
:exclamation: Even web-scaled trained closed source models with 100B+ parameters achieve <40% accuracy, highlighting the massive latent gap in model capabilities for long-term video understanding.  <br></br>


<iframe src=""https://www.youtube.com/embed/_VVoiSzb5E4"" title=""YouTube video player"" frameborder=""0"" allow=""accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"" allowfullscreen id=""intro_video"" width=""960"" height=""540""></iframe>

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the accuracy score. An example of a submission file will be presented below.

## Submission File
The submission file must be a **CSV file**. For each question in the dataset, you must predict the correct answer out of five. The submission file should have the question identifiers `q_uid` as the first column and the prediction `answer` in the second column. The prediction should be an integer from 0 to 4(one of 0,1,2,3,4). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```python
q_uid, answer
001934bb-81bd-4cd8-a574-0472ef3f6678, 0
0552b32a-de81-42e2-98e2-ad8efb1735fb, 3
02344f2e-3d7a-423a-bcba-a5ef96cde81e, 4
etc...
```
----------------------
# Citation

Please cite the paper at the [following link](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09126)","# Dataset Description

## Files

*  **videos** - folder with all the videos
*   **questions.json** - JSON file that is a list of dictionaries that contain the main question identifier labeled as `q_uid`, the question itself labeled as `question`, and five answer choices labeled as `option [0-4]`

Video file names in the `videos` folder correspond to the q_uid key in `questions.json`",False,True
7234,ctd-datawiz-2023,"# Overview

Read the overview of the competition before diving into it. All the best!
----------------------
# Description

# Problem Statement

In today's highly competitive telecom industry, retaining users is of paramount importance. Subscriber attrition, whether to competitors or due to dissatisfaction, can greatly affect a company's performance and profitability. Your objective is to build a predictive model that, given the historical data of telecom customers, can identify new users who are at risk of attrition in the near future.

## Submission

Participants have to train their model on the training data (`train.csv`). The model has to be tested on the test data (`test.csv`). Finally, you have to make a submission file (refer to `sample_submission.csv` for the correct format) for the results predicted on the test data. Submit this file for evaluation on the platform.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of your Machine Learning model will be evaluated on the basis of accuracy score.

----------------------
# Prize

**1st Place**: ?2000 ?? + Certificate ??

**2nd Place**: ?1000 ?? + Certificate ??

**3rd Place**: Certificate ??

You are only eligible for prize money if you have played fair and you are registered for the DataWiz event. If not, you can register here: [CTD Website](http://ctd.credenz.in/events).

For SCTR's PICT students only!","# Dataset Description

## Files Description

The data consists of three files:
- `train.csv` - the training set
- `test.csv` - the test set
- `sample_submission.csv` - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Data Dictionary

- **User ID**: Unique identifier for each telecom user.
- **Mobile No.**: User's mobile phone number.
- **User DOB**: Date of birth of the user.
- **User Job**: Occupation or job of the user.
- **Duration**: Total call duration (minutes) for the user.
- **Intl_Plan**: Whether the user has an international calling plan (Yes/No).
- **Voicemail_Plan**: Whether the user has a voicemail plan (Yes/No).
- **Voicemail_Messages**: Number of voicemail messages received by the user.
- **Day_Minutes**: Total minutes of daytime calls for the user.
- **Day_Calls**: Total number of daytime calls made by the user.
- **Day_Charge**: Total charge for daytime calls.
- **Eve_Minutes**: Total minutes of evening calls for the user.
- **Eve_Calls**: Total number of evening calls made by the user.
- **Eve_Charge**: Total charge for evening calls.
- **Night_Minutes**: Total minutes of nighttime calls for the user.
- **Night_Calls**: Total number of nighttime calls made by the user.
- **Night_Charge**: Total charge for nighttime calls.
- **Intl_Minutes**: Total minutes of international calls for the user.
- **Intl_Calls**: Total number of international calls made by the user.
- **Intl_Charge**: Total charge for international calls.
- **Service_Calls**: Number of user service calls made by the user.
- **Attrition**: The target variable indicates user attrition (Yes/No).",False,True
7235,hematocrit-prediction,"# Overview

At LiteBC Ltd., we specialize in analyzing microscopy images of capillaries to identify and profile the cells within them. We then create a numerical representation of the image, based on the detection and segmentation of the cells inside the capillary. Further details can be found in the Data Description section.

A two-dimensional cross-section of 3D data blood vessels is presented. The goal is to calculate the relative volume of 3d bodies (blood cells) within the blood vessel.
----------------------
# Description

##Visualization of the features, and sample of original data:

Original microscopy image:![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2324766%2F815c4eb1e0622bb571822aea9d248f1e%2FScreenshot%202023-09-11%20154000.png?generation=1694436011288929&alt=media)


Segmentation features (number of cell components and their intensity) are based on these features: 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2324766%2Fa9dbb774fd6b87bf9aec81952fb15cab%2FScreenshot%202023-09-11%20153828.png?generation=1694435948469048&alt=media)

Detection features (number of red blood cells and their intensity) are based on these features: 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2324766%2F2134111bb7af1bc81309d89fee498449%2FScreenshot%202023-09-11%20153640.png?generation=1694435809634340&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

This task is challenging for two reasons:
- First, we have ground truth `gt` labels for patent venous blood, but our data is capillary-based. The more capillaries we record per patient, the better, but most likely we have only a few capillaries (see `n` column).
- Ground truth distribution is normal, with lots of training items with an average blood cell volume and a few with extreme values. For us, it is important to be more accurate on edges, therefore the `test` set has a pseudo-uniform distribution over the HCT ranges (see histogram distribution on the image below).

## Files

*   **X_train_kaggle.csv** - the training set
*   **X_train_kaggle.csv** - the test set
*   **y_train_kaggle.csv** - ground truths by recording (and so by a patient)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns
This task is challenging for two reasons:
- First, we have ground truth for patent venous blood, but our data is capillary-based. The more capillaries we record per patient, the better, but most likely we have only a few capillaries.
- Ground truth distribution is normal, with lots of patients with average blood-cell volume, and a few with extreme values. For us, it is important to be more accurate on edges, therefore the `test` set has a pseudo-uniform distribution over the HCT ranges.

## Files

*   **X_train_kaggle.csv** - the training set
*   **X_train_kaggle.csv** - the test set
*   **y_train_kaggle.csv** - ground truths by recording (and so by a patient)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns

*   `gt` - ground truth of the blood cell volume for the big vein of the subject.
*   `recording_id` - `id` of the data recording, for that patient. We are recording 1-20 recordings per patient during the screening time.
* `optics_version` - 0 or 1 meaning the device version, important for studying feature distributions.
* 'num_capillaries', 'num_rbc', 'num_wbc', 'rbc_hw_ratio', 'rbc_height', 'total_rbc_area' - detection based
* 'frac_cells', 'num_cell_comps', 'av_cell_comp_size', 'cell_component_hw_ratio' - segmentation based
* 'av_capillary_width' - detection based
* 'hist0', 'hist_low9', 'stddev_row_brightness', 'average_sq_vertical_difference', 'av_capillary_brightness'   - intensity measures
* 'frac_nonempty_rows', 'av_vertical_difference' - std measures


## Test and train distributions:

Here you can see Train distribution - more Gaussian-like, test is more flat. We showed na?ve Linear regression model prediction - it will form a Gaussian-like distribution without special treatment.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2324766%2F4cc489b188a097eef94056858353667a%2FScreenshot%202023-09-11%20173116.png?generation=1694442716451782&alt=media)


## Evaluation

Evaluated with `RMSE`.

Calculated for a `patient` (not for `recording_id`), and it is for you to decide on how to average data belonging to one patient.",False,True
