7242,classification-competition-2,"# Description

## Predicting the popularity of IMDb movies
In this competition, you will have to predict the popularity of IMDb movies by analyzing the data in the proposed dataset. Thus, based on what you learned at the sessions, you'll need to create a model that estimates whether a movie will become popular or not. By accessing this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/stefanoleone992/imdb-extensive-dataset?select=IMDb+movies.csv), you can find more additional information about this data set.


## Rules
- The code must be written in Python. 
- You can use any classifier models that are implemented in Python.
- You will only upload the predictions.csv prediction file.
- You are not allowed to use features directly related to the evaluation of films, such as ratings/scores of movies, number of votes, or reviews (characteristics of the film that are not known when it appeared on the platform).
- You can use features from data sets uploaded to OneFile to drive models.
- Have fun!


## Final evaluation
The participant with the highest Accuracy will win the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation method
To evaluate the results, you will send only one .csv file each time, in the format below. Additionally, during the competition, you will be able to send a maximum of 2 solutions per day.

In the leaderboard, you will see the ranking based on 40% of the final evaluation dataset. The score for the other 60% of the data set will be hidden and shown after the competition ends.

In the end, you will be able to choose two solutions to be evaluated on the final data set.

## Submission Format
For each movie in the dataset, the submission file must contain two columns: movie_id and popularity.

The uploaded file must be saved with the .csv extension in the following format:

<pre>title_index, popularity
0, popular
1, unpopular
2, popular
</pre>

## Evaluation metrics
For evaluation will be used the [""Classification accuracy""](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) metric.

The classification accuracy defines the number of correctly classified examples divided by the total number of examples in the dataset.","# Dataset Description

You can use 'IMDb names.csv' dataset from this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/stefanoleone992/imdb-extensive-dataset?select=IMDb+movies.csv).",False,True
7243,final-project-stats-415-fall-2023,"# Overview

Welcome to the STATS415 Fa2023 Kaggle competition!

This competition concerns a (fictional) study, seeking to understand the ingredients that influence student performance on a standardized test of US History.  A large collection of high school students were sampled from 7 school districts.  For each student, the following forms of data were collected.

* `self_eval` -- the student's evaluation of their own mastery of the content.
* `teacher_eval` -- the student's teacher's evaluation of the student's mastery of the content.
* `district` -- which school district the student came from.
* `SRP_1`,`SRP_2`,...`SRP_50` -- a collection of 50 measurements of SRP blood concentration.  SRP is a kind of protein found throughout human tissues.     The student's SRP concentration was measured using a non-invasive optical method (similar to oximetry).  It was measured each day for the fifty days preceding the test, resulting in 50 columns in all.
* `y` -- the student's performance on the standardized test.

Your task is to understand how the input variables (the self-evaluation, the teacher-evaluation, the district, and the SRP measurements) relate to the response (student performance).  Specifically, you will attempt to learn the conditional expectation of student performance given the input variables.  Your estimated conditional expectation will be evaluated on a secret held-out test-set.

Here's how it works.
* We give you `X_train.csv` and `y_train.csv`, holding the input features and responses for a large number of students.  Each csv file also includes a `SEQN` column, giving a unique identifier for each student.
* We give you a `X_test.csv` file holding the input features for a held-out set of students.
* You must provide us with a `y_test.csv` file, indicating your predictions for performance among the held-out students.  

The files can be found in the ""Data"" tab above.  You can also find an `y_sample.csv` file indicating the format your answer should take.

You can submit up to *five times per day*, using the ""Submit Predictions"" button towards the top of this page.  Each time your group submits, you will learn the R-squared of your predictions on the secret data.  For full credit, you must get an R-squared of at least 0.75.  You can get half-credit if you get an R-squared of at least 0.5.  The points on this are a relatively small fraction of the group project score; don't go crazy on this unless you're having fun.  

Don't hesitate to contact GSI Gabriel (gapatron@umich.edu) if you have any questions.

PS. By the way, despite what the progress bar below seems to indicate, the competition did not, in fact, start 2 months ago.  The competition starts at 9am on November 12th.","# Dataset Description

*   **X_train.csv** holds features from the training set.
*  **y_train.csv** holds responses from the training set.
*   **X_test.csv** holds features from the test set (but the responses are secret!).
*   **y_sample.csv** holds a sample submission file in the correct format, indicating guesses about each sample in the test set.  When you submit, your file should look like this (but with different values for the ""y"" column)",False,True
