This is the home page of the competition. In this section, you should aim to succinctly introduce the competition.

All of the content on Kaggle is written in [markdown](https://github.com/showdownjs/showdown/wiki/Showdown's-Markdown-syntax). If you are copy-pasting from another application, like Word or your browser, you may need to clean up the markdown or html for things to display properly.

If this is your first time creating a competition, please refer to our [Getting Started Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions-setup) for help. Here is a handy link to [Kaggle's competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on submitting predictions.


----------------------
# Description

The description section is used to further explain the details of your competition. You could explain more about the domain or go into more depth about the problem. You can upload images using the ""Insert Image"" icon on the bar that displays above this textbox when in edit mode.

## This is a subtitle
To format pages, stick to the following conventions:
- Inline `code` should be encapsulated by back-ticks \``. 
- Block code should have  \``` as the first and last line of the block to produce
```
# code to print hello
print('hello')
```

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.


Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.



