7271,cool-quiet-city-competition,"# Overview

**2026 Update! The competition results have been [open-access published here](https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1742-6596/3140/11/112017)! (See also citations section below)**

Urban areas face the growing challenge of noise and heat, which affect our comfort, health, and productivity. Effective management of these factors is crucial as they influence the productivity, satisfaction, and stress levels of people in indoor and outdoor environments.

Knowing and predicting locations with comfortable thermal and acoustic conditions empowers people to find refuge from heat and noise. This knowledge could provide relief from the negative impacts, making the city appear cooler and quieter while still accounting for personal comfort preferences. 

Hence, this competition's goal is to use contextual data to predict noise distraction and thermal preference across a diversity of indoor and outdoor spaces.

This competition includes the city-scale collection of 9,808 smartwatch-driven micro-survey responses that were collected alongside 2,659,764 physiological and environmental measurements from 98 people using the open-source Cozie-Apple platform ([website](https://cozie-apple.app/), [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.13977)) combined with geolocation-driven urban digital twin metrics from the [Urbanity Python package](https://urbanity.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html). Figure 1 shows some of these data spatially distributed across Singapore.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F8649844%2Fc5df74fe67c96c97027cf5bf63235ff7%2Fcozie-map.png?generation=1699943959047398&alt=media) Figure 1: Micro-survey response example: location context of participants in Singapore <br/>

An overview of the input data sources and prediction objectives are outlined in Figure 2. The winning solutions of this competition will provide evidence of the types of pre-processing, modeling, and ensembling methods that provide the most accurate solutions for this context.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F23583%2F92cbf24951db70c16defd8737d758829%2Fpaper_cozie_3.png?generation=1696826167498337&alt=media) Figure 2: Overview of competition data and structure<br/>

## If you have questions or comments, please leave a post on the [Discussion Board](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cool-quiet-city-competition/discussion)

The discussion board is also useful for sharing strategies, insights, baselines, etc., that can make the competition richer through sharing. We encourage teams to share exploratory data analysis and other notebooks that could help the community.
----------------------
# Description

The data collection process for this competition utilized the open-source [Cozie Apple platform](https://cozie-apple.app/). This mobile and smartwatch application for iOS devices allows people to complete a watch-based micro-survey and provide real-time feedback about environmental conditions via their Apple Watch. It leverages the built-in sensors of the smartwatch to collect physiological (e.g., heart rate, activity), environmental (sound level), and location (latitude and longitude) data. The dataset from this deployment includes micro-survey responses from 98 people, who each provided at least 100 micro-survey responses from October 2022 to August 2023. The micro-survey question flow is seen in Figure 3. For each survey response location, digital-twin-created spatial indicators were calculated using the [Urbanity python package](https://github.com/winstonyym/urbanity). In addition, publicly available weather data is also provided.

The goal of the competition is to predict the values of the columns `q_noise_nearby`, `q_noise_kind`, `q_earphones`, `q_thermal_preference `as well as possible.

## Micro-survey question flow
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F8649844%2Fa2e63dc3ed89035e7e242230f94b9488%2Fwatch_survey_noise_and_privacy-36fcc4ca7094d37b0f0a79419cd93c4a.png?generation=1699286770839443&alt=media) Figure 3: Micro-survey question flow for this data set. Participants in the study were prompted to answer these questions in a diversity of indoor and outdoor environments over the course of 2-4 weeks. [Click here for a larger image.](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F8649844%2Fa2e63dc3ed89035e7e242230f94b9488%2Fwatch_survey_noise_and_privacy-36fcc4ca7094d37b0f0a79419cd93c4a.png?generation=1699286770839443&alt=media) 

----------------------
# Competition Details

## Team Limits
You may participate alone or create a team with a maximum size of up to 5 people.

## Submission Limits
You may submit a maximum of 2 entries per day. The accuracy of these submissions will be reflected on the *Public Leaderboard*, which contains roughly half of the test data set. The remaining portion of the test data set will be scored after the competition completion. You may select up to 2 final submissions for this final *Private Leaderboard* scoring.

#Timeline
* November 15, 2023 - Start Date.
* ~~February 1, 2024~~ February 23, 2024 (Extended deadline) - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM GMT on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is *accuracy*. The metric is calculated as:

$$accuracy = \frac{TP+ TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}$$

Where:

- TP is *true positive*, positive cases predicted as positive
- TN is *true negative*, negative cases predicted as negative
- FP is *false positive*, negative cases predicted as positive
- FN is *false negative*, positive cases predicted as negative
- TP+TN+FP+FN is equivalent to the number of cases predicted

As we have four targets here (`q_noise_nearby`, `q_noise_kind`, `q_earphones`, `q_thermal_preference`), accuracy for each target column is calculated and the average of the four of them is returned.

## Notebook Submissions
You can make submissions directly from Kaggle Notebooks. By adding your teammates as collaborators on a notebook, you can share and edit code privately with them.

## Submission File
For each `id_unique` in the test set, you must predict the target variables: `q_noise_nearby`, `q_noise_kind`, `q_earphones`, `q_thermal_preference`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```python
id_unique,q_noise_nearby,q_noise_kind,q_earphones,q_thermal_preference
1,None,Not applicable,Not applicable,Cooler
2,A little,Appliances,No earphones,No change,Public
3,A little,Talking,No earphones,Cooler
```
----------------------
# Winning Solutions

We will announce the winning teams in the Discussion Board and on LinkedIn. We plan to highlight the winning solutions as part of a Workshop at the ACM BuildSys 2024 conference. Members of the teams that created the winning solutions may be invited to participate in publications.
