7295,llm-prompting-with-makersuite,"# Overview

Large Language Models (LLMs) are capturing the imagination of millions of people around the world. The goal of this competition is to tap into the expertise of the Kaggle community to develop practical, creative, and innovative prompts that demonstrate real-world use-cases for LLMs. We have several categories in this competition, including prompts for education, developers, utilities, and more. Winning prompts will be included in [MakerSuite](https://makersuite.google.com/), a tool from Google that provides an easy to use interface to interact with LLMs including Google¡¯s PaLM API.

**Please note that MakerSuite and the PALM API are only available in certain regions. [Please consult this page for region availability.](https://developers.generativeai.google/available_regions)**
----------------------
# Description

In this analytics competition, participants will design prompts in MakerSuite in one of the following categories: 

- **Developer tools:** Prompts that enable the model to assist a developer with any aspect of development (from debugging, to code generation, to question answering, to idea generation).
- **Data science tools:** A subset of ""developer tools"", these prompts enable the model to help guide data scientists during the project formulation step, or might assist the data scientist in producing robust data science code.
- **Education and interactive tutors:** Prompts that enable the model to act as excellent tutors. You can pick a subject commonly taught in schools (physics, english literature, chemistry), or you can explore coding tutors, or tutors that teach practical skills (home improvements), or anything you like.
- **Utilities for everyday life (at home, or at work):** Prompts that enable the model to help you solve problems in everyday life (for example, to act as a tool that helps you use formulas in a spreadsheet, or assists with your presentation or emails, or provides tech support), or that helps you fix a leak, or understand the best way to get a tricky stain out of clothing.
- **Well explained reasoning:** Prompts that encourage the model to provide a great explanation for why it gave an answer, or to justify its response, or to explain what it does and doesn¡¯t know. 
- **Storytelling and interactive games:** Prompts that have that model act as a storyteller, or that have it work with you to craft a story.
- **Other ideas:**  All ideas are welcome. You are not limited to the above categories, these are just to get you started. We¡¯re interested in your fun, creative, helpful, or practical ideas, too.

Your submissions will be evaluated by Google, and kept private until winners and prizes are announced. This competition will not have a leaderboard. This competition is a collaboration between MakerSuite, Google Developer Relations, and Kaggle.
----------------------
# Submission Instructions

There is a three step process to submitting your prompt. You may use any prompt type for this competition (text, data, or chat). 

1) First, you will draft your prompt in [MakerSuite](https://makersuite.google.com).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F115173%2F2c18e9667b8118794333dd48626b2e39%2Fstart_prompt.gif?generation=1696545139879691&alt=media)

2) Next, you will [share your MakerSuite prompt / URL](https://screenshot.googleplex.com/C7HJzzyq46K8Mxy.png) (view-only access would be sufficient) with the relevant email alias (kaggle-makersuite-competition@google.com)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F115173%2F98af4d672f97d2674bfd3eb8ae7856f9%2Frun_prompt.gif?generation=1696545162787138&alt=media)

3) Finally, you will submit this [Google Form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe6CJO4ksKRK_9jsRx6LtawU_Txyh5RUpAK3tKcUB8S9Xcgtg/viewform?usp=sharing&resourcekey=0-xj3z9FptHD-DN7MCZ7WnPw) with your name, Kaggle account, MakerSuite URL, and two usage examples.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1314380%2F1654508e3117e5ceafae6b5fc5cd9d8e%2FScreenshot%202023-10-17%20at%209.19.42%20AM.png?generation=1697556142415856&alt=media)

**Important rules:**
* Only the most recent submission will be considered. (Per competition, not per category.)
* There will be no teams - only individual submissions are allowed.


# Sample Submission

At the top of the form, fill in your email, then choose a category and upload your two screenshots.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F115173%2F5b2687ffd524a09eeca5eaba99d684f3%2Ftop%20of%20form.png?generation=1696621719258580&alt=media)


At the bottom of the form, add your MakerSuite URL, then click the checkboxes affirming that you have shared your URL and accepted the rules.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F115173%2Fbea601866d57d042d9d9fd97d47f5adc%2Fbottom%20of%20form.png?generation=1696621760983322&alt=media)


You can find the Google Form [here](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe6CJO4ksKRK_9jsRx6LtawU_Txyh5RUpAK3tKcUB8S9Xcgtg/viewform?usp=sharing&resourcekey=0-xj3z9FptHD-DN7MCZ7WnPw).
----------------------
# Timeline

* **October 11, 2023** - Start Date. 
* **November 6, 2023** - Entry and Final Submission Deadline. 

All submissions are evaluated after the deadline and all deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.


----------------------
# Prizes

Prizes will be given in the following categories:
- Developer tools
- Data science tools
- Education and interactive tutors
- Utilities for everyday life
- Well-explained reasoning
- Storytelling and interactive games
- Other

Judges from Kaggle and Google will select a winner in each category. Each winner will receive a Kaggle hoodie if they agree to create a discussion post detailing their solution.

![](https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/Makersuite/4cgJPFCjiwD8ib2.png)

----------------------
# Evaluation Rubrics

All eligible submissions are evaluated on the following criteria:

<table>
  <tr>
   <td><strong>Practical: </strong>
<p>
The prompt solves a real-world problem that helps the user.
   </td>
   <td>[0-10pts]
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><strong>Useful:</strong>
<p>
The model produces outputs that are helpful or high quality.
   </td>
   <td>[0-10pts]
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><strong>Robust:</strong>
<p>
The prompt works well for many different types of input. 
   </td>
   <td>[0-10pts]
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><strong>Novel:</strong>
<p>
The project was unique or creative.
   </td>
   <td>[0-10pts]
   </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td><strong>Delightful:</strong>
<p>
We were impressed with both the idea and the results, and we felt compelled to share it with Google's broader developer community.
   </td>
   <td>[0-10pts]
   </td>
  </tr>
</table>","# Dataset Description

This competition does not require use of a dataset. The `submission_instructions.md` and `prompt_categories.md` files are included below for reference.",False,False
7296,egg-sales-predict-challenge-23sl,"# Overview

Unearth the Patterns of Egg Sales!

Eggs, a staple in many diets worldwide, have a sales pattern that's influenced by a myriad of factors - cultural events, global happenings, seasonal shifts, and more. In this challenge, we invite you to dive deep into 30 years of egg sales data from a shop in Sri Lanka.

While the dataset might seem simple, it encapsulates various intricacies. From the bustling sales during Sinhala & Tamil New Year to the calm of Wesak and the unforeseen global events like lockdowns - every year tells a story. Your mission, should you choose to accept, is to forecast the egg sales for the year 2022.

Whether you're a time series novice eager to learn the ropes or a seasoned expert aiming to refine your techniques, this dataset offers a unique playground. Harness the data, experiment with models, and uncover the hidden rhythms of egg sales!




----------------------
# Description

Dive into the world of time series forecasting with the Egg Sales Forecasting Challenge! This competition provides a rich dataset detailing 30 years of egg sales from a Sri Lankan shop. As participants, you'll uncover the nuances of seasonal fluctuations influenced by cultural festivities, global events, and even unexpected lockdowns. Beyond just raw numbers, this dataset tells a story of traditions, market dynamics, and the resilience of businesses. Your mission? Harness this wealth of information to predict the egg sales for 2022. Whether you're a newbie in forecasting or a seasoned data scientist, this challenge offers a unique opportunity to hone your skills
Your task is to build a machine learning model that can predict future egg sales based on the provided historical data. Whether you're a beginner just starting out with time series forecasting or an expert looking to test new algorithms, this challenge is for you!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between your predicted and the actual egg sales for 2022.

Submission Format:
Your submission should be a CSV file with two columns:

Date: The date for which the prediction is made (covering all dates in 2022).
Egg Sales Prediction: Your predicted number of eggs sold on that date.
Ensure your predictions are in the same chronological order as the dates of 2022, starting from January 1st to December 31st. Any deviation from this format might result in submission errors.
----------------------
# Acknowledgments


We'd like to thank the shop owner in Sri Lanka for providing this dataset and allowing us to use it for educational purposes.","# Dataset Description

This dataset provides a detailed look into the sales of eggs in a shop in Sri Lanka over a span of 30 years, capturing seasonal variations, cultural events, and even global incidents. Dive in to uncover patterns and predict future sales.

Participants should be able to answer these types of questions after reading the data description:

What files do I need?
What should I expect the data format to be?
What am I predicting?
What acronyms will I encounter?

Files
train.csv - The training dataset containing egg sales data from 1993 to the end of 2021.
test.csv - The test dataset with dates for the year 2022. You will make sales predictions for these dates.
sample_submission.csv - A sample submission file in the correct format, illustrating how to structure your predictions.
metaData.csv - Supplemental information about the dataset columns and any specific notes.
Columns
Date - Represents the date for which the sales data is recorded. It spans from the year 1993 to 2022.
Egg Sales (only in train.csv) - The number of eggs sold on the corresponding date. This is the target variable you'll be predicting for the test dataset.

* Update - Please note that while some of you figured out the correct columns of the submission format, Some had confusion. I have made the necessary adjustment and also added an updated submission column with corrected column names.",False,True
7297,machine-learning-competition-hw06,"# Description

<p>This is the right time to apply everything you have learned so far. So, let's compete to see if we can cope with this imbalanced dataset, which resembles real-life data. </p>
I hope you guys enjoy it!

**Complete two subtasks:**
a) Make 3 submissions obtained with different learning algorithms
b) Get a score of AUC=0.92 on the public leaderboard
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is AUC, which finds an area under the curve.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>

<p>For every row in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and the corresponding <strong>probability</strong> score between 0 and 1.</p>

<p>Kaggle expects class probabilities for each row in the test file and not binary classes. (It is important for getting good AUC scores)</p>

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,y
1,0.826006
2,0.602440
3,0.098063
4,0.7212663
5,0.08391414
etc.
</pre>","# Dataset Description

<ul>
<li><strong>traindata.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>testdata.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<br>
More information about the attributes of data can be found <a href=""https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/bank+marketing#"">here</a>.",False,True
7298,ml-project-11-build-handwritten-digit-recognizer,"# Description

<h3>Start here if...</h3>
<p>You have some experience with Python and machine learning basics, but you¡¯re new to computer vision. This competition which Dr. Gaddisa set, is the perfect introduction to techniques like neural networks using a classic dataset including pre-extracted features.</p>

<p>In this competition, you aim to correctly identify digits from a dataset of tens of thousands of handwritten images (given as a CSV file). 

