7307,ss4gg-hackathon-nir-neospectra,"# Overview

This predictive soil spectroscopy challenge is hosted by the [Soil Spectroscopy for Global Good](https://soilspectroscopy.org/) network aiming to connect international teams and experts in the fields of data science, soil spectroscopy, and pedometrics for a machine learning contest.
SS4GG seeks to accelerate the pace of scientific discovery in soil spectroscopy by developing open source solutions to consistently predict various soil properties from the spectra.
This challenge is targeting the participation of young researchers, students, and members of the data science community.
The winners will be awarded with a PeerJ lifetime individual membership and effectively cooperate with the SS4GG to make the Open Soil Spectral Library more robust and generally applicable! 
----------------------
# Description

##Goal of the challenge
Diffuse reflectance infrared spectroscopy has become an indispensable tool for rapid estimation of numerous soil properties to support natural resource assessment, sustainable food production, and climate mitigation projects.
Recent advances in hardware technology have enabled the development of handheld sensors with great capacity, however they still achieve inferior predictive performance as compared to laboratory-grade infrared spectrometers.
In this predictive soil spectroscopy challenge, we present a database consisting of diverse mineral soil samples scanned using the [Si-Ware NeoSpectra](https://www.si-ware.com/products-and-software/neospectra-scanner) near-infrared (NIR, 1350 - 2550 nm) scanner and landforming, climatic and vegetation related geo-covariates that have impact on soil organic carbon (SOC) levels in the soils. The winners will be announced in the [Tri-Society meeting](https://www.acsmeetings.org/). The results will be presented and discussed also in the [Soil Spectroscopy Visioning Workshop](https://soilspectroscopy.org/applications-open-for-soil-spectroscopy-visioning-workshop/).

![Figure 1](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6592816%2Fdc6bc58e512e6868cb4219a8ab049953%2Ftrain_data.png?generation=1697021532800953&alt=media)
Figure 1: Soil samples for training

Considering the limited capacity of a NIR handheld device, can we build better machine learning models to precisely predict SOC using this device and optionally geo-covariates?
We are interested in leveraging the [Neospectra open-access library](https://soilspectroscopy.github.io/ossl-manual/neospectra-database.html) published as part of [Open Soil Spectral Library](https://soilspectroscopy.github.io/ossl-manual/index.html) (OSSL) for predicting soil organic carbon content (SOC) at several farms in Massachusetts and New York State, USA.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submissions are evaluated using the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted and observed 
log-scaled SOC percentage content of soil samples in the test set:
$$ RMSE = \sqrt{(\frac{1}{n})\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i} - \hat{y}_{i})^{2}} $$


## Submission File
For each `sample_id` in the test set, the participants must predict the SOC content `soc_perc_log1p` . The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
sample_id,soc_perc_log1p
S1012,0.54321
S5006,1.54321
S9999,2.54321
 ```  
----------------------
# Timeline

- **October 10, 2023** - Start date.
- **October 29, 2023** - Final submission deadline.
- **October 30, 2023** - Organization will contact the winners.
- **October 30, 2023** - Official announcement of the winners in the [Tri-Society meeting](https://www.acsmeetings.org/)

Deadlines are at 11:59 PM CET on each day, unless stated otherwise. Organizers can adjust the timeline if needed.

----------------------
# Prizes

We offer two awards:
1. **1st Place** on Private Leaderboard - PeerJ Lifetime individual Enhanced Membership
2. **Subjective award** for the best workflow according to organizers based on computation notebook evaluation - PeerJ Lifetime individual Enhanced Membership

The winners will present their computation notebook (results and workflow) online/in-person in [Soil Spectroscopy Visioning Workshop](https://soilspectroscopy.org/applications-open-for-soil-spectroscopy-visioning-workshop/).


----------------------
# Acknowledgments

This challenge is hosted by the [Soil Spectroscopy for Global Good ](https://soilspectroscopy.org/)and co-organized by [Woodwell Climate Research Center](https://www.woodwellclimate.org/research-area/carbon/), [OpenGeoHub Foundation ](https://opengeohub.org/)and [University of Florida](https://www.ufl.edu/).

The SS4GG project has received funding from the USDA NIFA award #2020-67021-32467.

----------------------
# Citation

Robert Minarik, Jos¨¦ Lucas Safanelli, Xuemeng Tian, Tomislav Hengl and Jonathan Sanderman (2023). SS4GG hackathon: NIR soil spectroscopy modelling. Kaggle. https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ss4gg-hackathon-nir-neospectra","# Dataset Description

The NIR spectra used in this challenge is represented as reflectance factor (R) in percent fraction after measuring the portion of light diffusely reflected (R) by the soil surface (particle size <2 mm) to a Fourier Transform (FTIR) detector. The reflectance factor is represented in the range between 0 and 1. The soil spectra have absorption features that are associated with its mineral and organic constituents, therefore making possible the prediction of soil properties by the direct or indirect relationship. The effective NIR range used in this dataset is 1350 - 2550 nm. All spectra (train and test) were trimmed to this range and resampled to an even interval of 2 nm.
Soil organic carbon (SOC) is a measurable component of soil organic matter. Organic matter makes up just 2-10% of most soil's mass and has an important role in the physical, chemical and biological function of agricultural soils [USDA](https://www.agric.wa.gov.au/measuring-and-assessing-soils/what-soil-organic-carbon). SOC is represented in percent units (0-100%) and have a highly right skewed probability distribution. It is determined by dry combustion with correction for calcium carbonate, when present.
The training set is a subsample of the Neospectra database available through the Open Soil Spectral Library. The [Neospectra database](https://soilspectroscopy.github.io/ossl-manual/neospectra-database.html) has 1760 samples with WGS84 lat-lon, equivalent to 1194 unique locations (several locations have multiple depths measured). This database has about 22 soil properties, with varying sample sizes. We provide 1202 soil samples with SOC content, measured NIR spectra ranging from 1350 to 2550 nm, spatial coordinates and relevant publicly available raster spatial layers/geo covariates related to main soil SOC forming factors.
The test set contains NIR spectra from several distinct farms sampled in Massachusetts and New York states. Soils were scanned in the lab after drying. This dataset has precise location coordinates and the year of sampling.
All soil samples were overlaid with 214 raster spatial layers, including landscape forms, wetlands, bioclimatic variables such as regime of precipitation, temperatures and water vapor, vegetation indices, cropland and forest percentage. The result values were organized in columns, one for each spatial layer, which combined represent the feature space available for ML modeling.

##Column names
Columns are formed with multiple metadata fields, separated by underscores. The general naming convention described below provides a guide to interpret the variables based on their name. While there may be slight variations in the number of metadata fields due to version difference, this doesn't affect the overall understanding of the variable.

##Naming Convention Structure
`{Generic Variable Name}_{Variable Procedure Combination}_{Position in the Probability Distribution/Variable Type}_{Spatial Support}_{Depth Reference or Depth Interval}_{Time Reference Begin}_{End Time}_{Bounding Box}_{EPSG Code}_{Version Code}.tif`

- Example: **snow.prob_esacci.m01_p90_500m_s_2000_2012_go_epsg.4326_v20231002.tif**
- Meta-data field:
 - F1 - variable name and type: **snow.prob** 
 - F2 - variable procedure including product name: **esacci.m01**
 - F3 - position in the probability distribution/variable type: **p90** (""p90"" represents the 90th percentile aggregation over a certain time period, indicating that 90% of the observed data falls below this value. Similarly, ""m"" signifies an aggregation based on the mean, while ""sd"" denotes aggregation determined by the standard deviation.)
 - F4 - spatial resolution: **500m**
 - F5 - depth reference: **s** (s-surface, b-below, a-above)
 - F6 - start time: **2000**
 - F7 - end time: **2012**
 - F8 - bounding box: **go** (global)
 - F9 - coordinate system: **epsg.4326**
 - F10 - version: **v20231002** (by date - October 2, 2023; or by number - v1.0)

##Column Description
All the columns can be aggregated into different thematic groups according to F1, F2 and F3:
- Landform and landscape parameters ([Hengl, 2018](https://zenodo.org/record/1464846), static):
 - `landform.alluvial.fan..pediment..bajada..pediplain_terrain.class_p_{...}`: the percentage of various landforms like alluvial fans, pediments, bajadas, and pediplains being present based on the terrain classification.
 - `landform.alluvial.or.coasttal.plain.gentlest.lake.plain.playa_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of very gentle landforms such as those in alluvial or coastal plains, lake plains, and playas based on terrain classification.
 - `landform.alluvial.or.coasttal.plain.pediplain_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of landforms in alluvial or coastal plains and pediplains based on terrain classification.
 - `landform.alluvial.plain.pediplain_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of landforms specifically in alluvial plains and pediplains.
 - `landform.dissected.terrace.moderate.plateau_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of dissected terraces and moderate plateaus being present based on terrain classification.
 - `landform.hills.rough.in.small.and.large.scale._terrain.class_p_{...}` and `landform.hills.rough.in.small.and.large.scale_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of rough hills on both small and large scales. The difference in naming might be due to a typo or could denote slight differences in classification methods.
 - `landform.middle.large.slope_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of middle to large slopes' presence based on terrain classification.
 - `landform.moderate.mountain.rough_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of moderately elevated, rough mountains.
 - `landform.moderate.mountain.smooth_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of smooth, moderate mountains.
 - `landform.slope.in.and.around.terrace.or.plateau_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of slopes in and around terraces or plateaus being present.
 - `landform.steep.mountain.rough_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of steep and rough mountains.
 - `landform.steep.mountain.smooth_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of smooth, steep mountains.
 - `landform.terrace.smooth.plateau_terrain.class_p_{...}`percentage of smooth terraces and plateaus being present.
 - `landform.upper.large.slope_terrain.class_p_{...}`: percentage of the presence of upper parts of large slopes.
 - `landform_terrain.class_c_{...}`:categorical classification of various landforms.
 - `bare.surface_landsat.frequency_m_{...}`:  the mean frequency of bare surfaces detected by Landsat imagery.
 - `downslope.curvature_merit.dem_m_{...}`: mean downslope curvature derived from the MERIT Digital Elevation Model (DEM).
 - `log1p.upstream.area_merit.hydro_m_{...}`: the log1p transformed upstream area, derived from MERIT hydrological data.
 - `pos.openess_merit.dem_m_{...}`:  mean positive openness value derived from the MERIT DEM.
 - `slope_merit.dem_m_{...}`: mean slope value derived from the MERIT DEM.
 - `upslope.curvature_merit.dem_m_{...}`: mean upslope curvature derived from the MERIT DEM.
 - `vbf_merit.dem_m`: : valley bottom flatness derived from the MERIT DEM.
 - `wetlands.cw_upmc.wtd_c_{...}`: categorical data about wetlands associated with ""cw"" (possibly ""cold waters"" or another type) from the UPMC water table depth dataset. 
 - `wetlands.groundwater-driven_upmc.wtd_p_{...}`: percentage of groundwater-driven wetlands derived from the UPMC water table depth dataset.
 - `wetlands.permanent_upmc.wtd_p_{...}`: percentage of permanent wetlands from the UPMC water table depth dataset.
 - `wetlands.regularly-flooded_upmc.wtd_p_{...}`:  percentage of regularly flooded wetlands derived from the UPMC water table depth dataset.

- BioClim v1.2 mean aggregated over 1981-2010 ([CHELSA-climate](https://chelsa-climate.org/bioclim/), static):
 - `bioclim.var_chelsa.bio2_{...}`: mean diurnal range of temperatures averaged over year 1981-2010.
 - `bioclim.var_chelsa.bio3_{...}`: ratio of diurnal variation to annual variation in temperatures.
 - `bioclim.var_chelsa.bio16_{...}`: mean monthly precipitation amount of the wettest quarter.
 - `bioclim.var_chelsa.bio12_{...}`: accumulated precipitation amount.
 - `bioclim.var_chelsa.bio13_{...}`: precipitation of the wettest month.

- Climate variables:
 - `snow.prob_esacci.m{month}_{...}`: long term aggregation of monthly snow probability ([Hengl, 2021](https://zenodo.org/record/6011200), static).
 - `wv_mcd19a2v061.seasconv_{...}`: water vapour monthly ([Parente et al., 2023](https://zenodo.org/record/8226292), dynamic, overlay by year). 
 - `clm_lst_mod11a2.{day or night}time_{...}`: MODIS LST monthly in daytime or night time ([Hengl et al., 2022](https://zenodo.org/record/6458406), dynamic, overlay by year).
 - `CHELSA_pr_{month}_{year}_V.2.1_{...}`: CHELSA rainfall V2.1 monthly ([CHELSA-climate](https://chelsa-climate.org/timeseries/), dynamic, overlay by year).

- Vegetation index:
 - `monthly.evi_mod13q1.v061.m{month}_{...}`: long-term (2000-2021) aggregation of monthly MODIS EVI ([MODIS](https://modis.gsfc.nasa.gov/data/dataprod/mod13.php), static).

- Land cover:
 - `lcv_globalcropland_bowen.et.al_p_{...}`: ropland percent at 1km resolution ([Bowen et al., 2021](https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/13/5403/2021/), dynamic, overlay by year).
 - `lcv.forest_hilda_dv1_{...}`: HILDA+ forest percent at 1km resolution ([HILDA+](http://hilda.plus/hildaplus_map.html), dynamic, overlay by year).

## Files

*   **train.csv**:  Training set with 1202 rows and 603 columns containing ID, observed values of SOC and spectra of soil samples:
 * `sample_id` string, soil sample ID;
 * `soc_perc_log1p` numeric, natural log(1+x) of Soil Organic carbon content in percent units (%);
 * `1350` numeric, first spectral wavenumber at 1350 nm;
 * ` 2550` numeric, last spectral wavenumber at 2550 nm.
*   **train_geocovariates.csv**:  Training set with 1202 rows and 220 columns containing additional geo covariates:
 *  `sample_id` string, soil sample ID;
 *  `longitude.point_wgs84_dd` numeric, longitude of soil sampling point in WGS84 (decimal degrees);
 *  `latitude.point_wgs84_dd` numeric, latitude of soil sampling point in WGS84 (decimal degrees);
 *  `observation.year` numeric, observation year used for extraction of dynamic geo covariates (see Column names section);
 *  `layer.upper.depth_usda_cm` numeric, upper depth of a soil sample in a soil profile;
 *  `layer.lower.depth_usda_cm`  numeric, lower depth of a soil sample in a soil profile; 
 *  `CHELSA_pr_09_{year}_V.2.1` numeric, first geo covariate, CHELSA rainfall V2.1 for September of the observation year (see Column names section);
 *  `CHELSA_pr_01_{year}_V.2.1` numeric, last geo covariate, CHELSA rainfall V2.1 for January of the observation year (see Column names section).

*   **test.csv**: Test set with 772 rows and 602 columns containing IDs and spectra of soil samples:
 * `sample_id` string, soil sample ID;
 * `1350` numeric, first spectral wavenumber at 1350 nm;
 * ` 2550` numeric, last spectral wavenumber at 2550 nm.

*   **test_geocovariates.csv**:  Test set with 772 rows and 220 columns containing additional geo covariates:
 *  `sample_id` string, soil sample ID;
 *  `longitude.point_wgs84_dd` numeric, longitude of soil sampling point in WGS84 (decimal degrees);
 *  `latitude.point_wgs84_dd` numeric, latitude of soil sampling point in WGS84 (decimal degrees);
 *  `observation.year` numeric, observation year used for extraction of dynamic geo covariates (see Column names section);
 *  `layer.upper.depth_usda_cm` numeric, upper depth of a soil sample in a soil profile;
 *  `layer.lower.depth_usda_cm`  numeric, lower depth of a soil sample in a soil profile; 
 *  `CHELSA_pr_02_{year}_V.2.1` numeric, first geo covariate, CHELSA rainfall V2.1 for February of the observation year (see Column names section);
 *  `landform_terrain.class_c_250m_s0..0cm_2017_2018_go_epsg.4326_v2023100` numeric, last geo covariate, categorical classification of various landforms (see Column names section).

*   **sample_submission.csv**: a sample submission file with 772 rows and 2 columns:
 *  `sample_id` string, soil sample ID;
 * `soc_perc_log1p` numeric, natural log(1+x) of Soil Organic carbon content in percent units (%).",False,True
7308,ss4gg-hackathon-mir-soil-spectroscopy,"# Overview

This predictive soil spectroscopy challenge is hosted by the [Soil Spectroscopy for Global Good - SS4GG](https://soilspectroscopy.org/) network aiming to connect international teams and experts in the fields of data science, soil spectroscopy, and pedometrics for a machine learning contest.

SS4GG seeks to accelerate the pace of scientific discovery in soil spectroscopy by developing open-source solutions to consistently predict various soil properties from the spectra.

This challenge is targeting the participation of young researchers, students, and members of the data science community.

The winners will be awarded a PeerJ Lifetime Individual Membership and effectively cooperate with the SS4GG to make the Open Soil Spectral Library more robust and generally applicable!
----------------------
# Description

## Goal of the challenge

Diffuse reflectance infrared spectroscopy has become an indispensable laboratory tool for the rapid estimation of numerous soil properties to support natural resource assessment, sustainable food production, and climate mitigation goals.

Considering the lack of interoperability among mid-infrared (MIR, 4000-650 cm<sup>-1</sup>) soil spectroscopy laboratories, can we build machine-learning models from the MIR spectra for consistently making precise estimates of soil properties regardless of the origin and the spectral composition of the inputs?

For this, we are interested in leveraging the Open Soil Spectral Library (OSSL) for predicting clay content across five distinct instruments. Several laboratories participated in a ring trial experiment and collected the spectra for the same soil samples (n = 50) using their own instrument configurations and standard operating procedures ([Preprint](https://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4548803)).

In this experiment, we found that the performance can be highly variable. Some instruments achieve great explained variance (>90%) and low average error (<5%), while others barely reach a satisfactory fit (explained variance close to 0 and average error around 50%). In this challenge, the participants have the chance to push the boundaries of model building and interoperability with a high-quality and accurate dataset designed by the SS4GG network. We are interested in seeing good performance across distinct instruments by measuring their pooled performance.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The submissions are evaluated using the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted and actual clay content in the test set:

![](https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20200622171741/RMSE1.jpg)

## Submission File
For each unique_id in the test set, the participants must predict the clay content (`clay_perc`, %). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    unique_id,clay_perc
    'U1',34
    'U2',12
    'U3',65
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The MIR spectra used in this challenge are represented as pseudo absorption (`A`) in logarithm units (base 10) after measuring the portion of light diffusely reflected (`R`) by the soil surface (after finely milling the soil to a particle size <0.2 mm) to a Fourier Transform (FTIR) infrared detector. The pseudo absorbance is calculated as `A = log10(1/R)`, where `R` ranges between 0 and 1. A soil spectrum has absorption features that are associated with its mineral and organic constituents, therefore making possible the prediction of soil properties by the direct or indirect relationship with spectra. The effective MIR range used in this dataset is 4000-650 cm<sup>-1</sup>, which is equivalent to 2,500 to 15,384 nm. All spectra (train and test) were trimmed to this range and resampled to an even interval of 2 cm<sup>-1</sup>.

Clay content is a soil compositional variable, i.e., together with sand and silt, it composes the fine earth fraction of the bulk soil (particles <2 mm), generally known as soil texture. The summation of sand, clay, and silt is equal to exactly 100% and they are determined by sieving and the Pipette method. In the training set all three variables are provided, but the prediction must be done only for clay. We particularly found that soil texture is one of the most sensitive components to instrument dissimilarity.

The training set is a copy of the middle-infrared (MIR) data available through the [Open Soil Spectral Library](https://soilspectroscopy.github.io/ossl-manual/). We provide the original dataset provider (`dataset`), OSSL unique id (`id`), the response variable (`clay_perc`), additional fine earth fractions (`sand_perc` and `silt_perc`), the texture components summation (`texture_summation`), and the spectra ranging from 4000-650 cm<sup>-1</sup>.

The test set contains MIR spectra from 50 soil samples scanned across 5 different instruments (total n = 250). They were prepared as part of an interlaboratory comparison experiment developed by the SS4GG network. More information can be found in this [Preprint](https://dx.doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4548803).

The 50 soil samples are identified by the column `sample_id`, while the instruments are identified by column `instrument`. The combination of both results in 250 distinct `unique_id` of the test set. We don't provide additional information about the other fine earth fractions (`sand_perc` and `silt_perc`), so users must predict these fractions if they are interested in exploring compositional correlations. The MIR spectra range from 4000-650 cm<sup>-1</sup>.

The following FTIR MIR instruments compose the test set:
- `INST1`: Bruker Alpha.
- `INST2`: Perkin Elmer Spectrum 100.
- `INST3`: Bruker Invenio.
- `INST4`: Thermo Fisher Nicolet.
- `INST5`: Agilent 4300.

A spectral plot of the same soil sample scanned across the five distinct instruments is represented below:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F15849021%2F78bcdadaddf116efbe304cffd52d205d%2Fspectral_variation.png?generation=1696601613642269&alt=media)

## Files and Columns

**train.csv**: Training set with 57,268 rows and 1682 columns:
- `dataset`: string, original soil spectral library producer.
- `id`: string, Open Soil Spectral Library (OSSL) unique identifier.
- `clay_perc`: numeric, clay content in percent units (%).
- `sand_perc`: numeric, sand content in percent units (%).
- `silt_perc`: numeric, silt content in percent units (%).
- `texture_sum`: numeric, the summation (exact to 100%) of clay, sand, and silt that composes the fine earth fraction of the bulk soil (particles <2 mm) and is generally known as soil texture.
- `650`: numeric, first spectral wavenumber at 650 cm<sup>-1</sup>.
- `4000`: numeric, last spectral wavenumber at 4000 cm<sup>-1</sup>.

**test.csv**: Test set with 250 rows and 1679 columns:
`unique_id`: string, unique identifier of the spectral samples.
`instrument`: string, identifier of the instruments.
`sample_id`: string, identifier of the soil samples.
`650`: first spectral wavenumber at 650 cm<sup>-1</sup>.
`4000`: last spectral wavenumber at 4000 cm<sup>-1</sup>.

**sample_submission.csv**: A sample submission file with 250 rows and 2 columns:
`unique_id`: string, unique identifier of the spectral samples.
`clay_perc`: numeric, clay content in percent units (%).",False,True
7309,stockpicker,"# Overview

Welcome to the first project for Quantitative Trading at Brown. This is designed to be an opportunity for you to both learn and flex your analytical and technical knowledge when it comes to financial data.

**Goal:** Use 9 sets each of (sequential) financial time series data (for 10 financial instruments) to predict the expected return and volatility (including 95% confidence bounds) for the time instance that is 10 time steps past the final data point.
----------------------
# Description

**Getting Started:** Attached to this competition are 90 `.parquet` (Apache Parquet) files. These files will be your only set of input data, and you will have to use Python's Pandas library to [convert](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_parquet.html) them into DataFrames. 

**File Name Details:** Each `.parquet ` file will be formatted as ID_ERA. ID corresponds to an actual financial instrument, and ERA refers to the actual time period. These values have been deliberately obscured.

**Teams:** You must work in groups (we recommend 2-4 but are allowing teams up to 8) members of Quantitative Trading at Brown. Kaggle allows for multiple users to be [attached](https://www.kaggle.com/discussions/getting-started/44861) to any given submission. Note that your accuracy levels will be normalized according to the number of members in your group. In other words, your project should have better results as you have more people.

**Need Help?** Feel free to use the Discussion tab above to ask for help from other club members!
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Submissions:** Once you generate your set of 180 predictions, you can submit to this competition to have your accuracy determined and ranked against other participants. You may only submit *twice* per day. 

**Note:** Although your submission file will not contain your 95% confidence bounds, we still expect you to generate this data independently. When it comes time to meet with a leadership member of Quantitative Trading at Brown to present your results, they will expect you to have analyzed your confidence bounds and conducted a sensitivity analysis of your predictions.

## Submission File
For each security in the training set, you must predict an expected return and an expected volatility for the time instance that is 10 time steps past the final data point. Your submission file should contain a header, and have the following CSV format:

    Id,Return,Volatility
    11,0,0
    12,0,0
    ...
    108,0,0
    109,0,0

The **price units** of your Return and Volatility columns should match that of the input data.

For submissions, the Id column will be equal to ID * 10 + ERA (from the file name format). This is required in order to perform a check on your accuracy. For example, if we are determining the Id for an item with ID = 5 and ERA = 7, then Id = 57.","# Dataset Description

**File Name Details:** Each `.parquet ` file will be formatted as ID_ERA. ID corresponds to an actual financial instrument, and ERA refers to the actual time period. These values have been deliberately obscured. Each file is a single time series of the price of a financial instrument.

You can download all 90 `.parquet` files with the **Download All** button below.",False,True
7310,applications-of-deep-learning-wustl-fall-2023,"# Overview

You must use AI to determine if pictures of people have glasses on or not. You can see examples of these images here.

