7329,social-media-post-approval-prediction-with-marky,"# Overview

Welcome to the ""Social Media Post Approval Prediction"" with Marky.ai! In this challenge, participants are tasked with leveraging AI to predict the approval status of social media posts generated by Marky¡ªa cutting-edge web application designed for promotional purposes. A Marky user sees the content we generate for them and can approve the content to post on their socials or can reject it:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7572944%2F17d5119644c27441fc054f385ff48053%2Fmarky-kaggle.png?generation=1698344963827113&alt=media)

The dataset we provide to you consists of various parameters related to each post, including captions, images, and more. Dive deep into this intriguing dataset, build your model, and aim for the top spot on the leaderboard!

**Goal:** Your primary objective is to accurately predict whether a generated post will be approved or rejected based on the given features.
----------------------
# Description

Marky is a modern AI-powered web application that crafts social media posts for promotional activities such as books and podcasts. Each post consists of multiple parameters that define its content and appearance. Once generated, users have the discretion to either approve or reject these posts. This competition revolves around a dataset from Marky's database, offering a detailed insight into these generated posts.

Participants are provided a labeled dataset from this database. Your challenge? Build a model that can anticipate the ""approved"" status of a post¡ªwhether it will be given the green light (true) or turned down (false).

## Dataset Insights

- `Parameters`: The dataset has individual columns about certain parameters of a post such as `parameters_chapter_title`, `parameters_chapter_summary`, `parameters_tone`, and more. These columns provide information about the associated chapter, book summary, theme, and tone of the post.
- `Caption`: Limited to 280 characters, it's the primary textual content visible to users.
- `Image`: A URL or reference linked to the post's image.
- `user_edited`: A binary value indicating whether the post was modified by the user.

Please reference the Data tab at the top for more information regarding the datasets provided.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the accuracy of the model on a hold-out test dataset. Participants should aim for a higher accuracy score, ensuring their model's robustness and generalizability. 

**The winning team will also be able to provide suggestions for creating content that has a higher approval rating.**

## Submission File
For each post ID in the test set, participants must predict a boolean value of approved, where true signifies that the post is approved and false indicates it's rejected. The submission file should contain a header and be formatted as follows:

    id,approved
    91d05aa7-7ca3-4f09-b3ff-50d46eeed61b,true
    dfd01d42-faae-4bd4-a762-2f8cd5fb365f,false
    etc.","# Dataset Description

This dataset is derived from Marky's database table. Marky is a web application that uses AI to generate social media posts. The dataset contains various parameters related to the generated post, the post's caption, its associated image, the template values which represent the visual layout of the post, and a boolean column indicating if the post was edited by a user.

Participants are expected to use this dataset to predict the ""status"" of the post, specifically whether it will be approved or rejected.

## Files

- **train.csv** - the training set which you will use to develop your models. This set constitutes 80% of the original dataset and contains the 'approved' column, which indicates whether a post was approved (`true`) or not (`false`).
- **test.csv** - the test set which you will use to make predictions. This set constitutes the remaining 20% of the original dataset but does not contain the 'approved' column. Your task is to predict the approval status for each post in this set, using a boolean value (`true` or `false`).

## Columns

- `id` - Unique identifier for each post.
- `user_edited` - A boolean value indicating if the user edited the post.
- `font` - URL or reference to the font used in the post.
- `image` - URL or reference to the associated image for the post.
- `caption` - The content of the post, limited to 280 characters.
- `parameters_chapter_title` - The title of the ""talking point"" we are creating a post for.
- `parameters_chapter_summary` - The chapter summary from the PostParameters class.
- `parameters_tone` - The tone of the post content.
- `created_at` - Timestamp when the post was created.
- `user_id` - Identifier for the user associated with the post.
- `parameters_switchboard_template_name` - The name of the canvas we are using in this post (theres around 100 unique of these).
- `parameters_theme` - Theme associated with the post.
- `parameters_prompt_template_name` - The name of the prompt we use to create this post (theres around 100 unique of these).
- `parameters_photo_search_term` - The search term used for the photo associated with the post.
- `user_created_at` - Timestamp when the user was created.
- `approved` - A boolean value indicating the post's approval status.
- `has_logo` - A boolean value indicating if the post has a logo.",False,True
7330,advanced-horse-speed-forecast-time-climate,"# Overview

Welcome to the ""Elite Horse Speed Prediction Challenge""!

In this competition, participants are tasked with predicting the speed of horses under varying conditions. Drawing from a rich dataset of over 100,000 races, competitors will need to consider factors such as weather and race time to make their predictions.

Harnessing the power of machine learning and neural networks, this challenge offers an intricate problem that tests the mettle of both seasoned data scientists and enthusiastic newcomers. With intricate patterns and a myriad of influencing factors, it's a race to the top of the leaderboard!

Goal:
Your objective is to build a model that accurately predicts a horse's speed based on given conditions, pushing the boundaries of predictive analytics. Good luck and may the best model win!
----------------------
# Description

The world of horse racing is dynamic and ever-changing, with countless factors influencing the outcome of a race. In this competition, we delve deep into the nuances of horse speeds, presenting a challenge that requires participants to predict the speed of a horse during a race.

The provided dataset encapsulates over 100,000 races, each with its unique set of conditions. The primary variables include the date of the race, the time the race took place, and the weather condition during the race. The challenge is to use this data to predict the speed of a horse under different conditions.


Dataset
The dataset is divided into training and testing sets. The training set consists of 100,000 rows, each row representing a unique race. The columns in the dataset are:

Date: The date the race took place.

Race_Time: The time of the day the race occurred.

Weather: The weather condition during the race. It has categories such as 'Sunny', 'Cloudy', 'Rainy', etc.

Horse_Speed: The speed of the horse during the race. This is the target variable for the training set.
The testing set follows a similar structure but lacks the Horse_Speed column. 


Participants are expected to predict this missing column using the given conditions.

Evaluation


The Mean Absolute Error (MAE) is used to evaluate submissions in this competition. It measures the average magnitude of errors between predicted and actual horse speeds. It's calculated as:

MAE = (1/n) * SUM(|y_i - yhat_i|)

Where:

n is the number of observations.
y_i is the actual horse speed for the ith race.
yhat_i is the predicted horse speed for the ith race.
Aim to minimize the MAE for a higher leaderboard position. The closer the MAE is to zero, the better the predictions





?

Challenge
The intricate patterns and relationships between the variables in this dataset make it an elite-level challenge. While the variables might seem simple at first glance, their combined interactions create a complex landscape for prediction. This competition is designed to push the boundaries of traditional machine learning and encourage innovative approaches.

Are you ready to take on the challenge and gallop your way to victory?





----------------------
# Evaluation



Submissions are evaluated on the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) between the predicted horse speed and the actual horse speed. MAE measures the average magnitude of errors between predicted and actual horse speeds. It's calculated as:

MAE = (1/n) * SUM(absolute(y_i - yhat_i))

Where:
- n is the number of observations.
- y_i is the actual horse speed for the ith race.
- yhat_i is the predicted horse speed for the ith race.

Participants should aim to minimize the MAE for a higher leaderboard position. The closer the MAE is to zero, the better the predictions.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, participants must predict the horse speed. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Horse_Speed
    2023-01-01_13:00:00,15.5
    2023-01-01_13:01:00,16.2
    2023-01-01_13:02:00,15.8
    etc.

Use the provided sample submission file as a reference for the expected format.","# Dataset Description




Files
train_final.csv - This is the training dataset. It contains historical data on horse races, including the date, race time, weather conditions, and the actual horse speed.
  
test_final.csv - This is the test dataset. It provides information on the races' date, race time, and weather conditions but omits the actual horse speed. Your task is to predict the horse speed for these races.

sample_submission_final.csv - A sample submission file in the correct format, demonstrating how to format your predictions.

Columns

Date - The date on which the race took place.
  
Race_Time - The specific time the race started.
  
Weather - Describes the weather condition during the race. It can be 'Sunny', 'Cloudy', 'Rainy', or 'Stormy'.
  
Horse_Speed (only in training data) - Represents the speed of the horse during the race.
  
ID (specifically for submission) - A combination of the date, race time, and an index, used to uniquely identify each race in the test set.

Objective

Your main objective is to predict the Horse Speed for each race in the test set based on the given conditions.

**Important**: Please use the latest version of the ""sandbox_grand_final.csv"" file for sandbox submissions. Older versions are deprecated.",False,True
7331,heart-attack-risk-analysis,"# Overview

The aim of this competition is to train a model to predict whether or not a patient is at **high risk** or **low risk** of a heart attack.
----------------------
# Description

This dataset provides a comprehensive array of features relevant to heart health and lifestyle choices, encompassing patient-specific details such as age, gender, cholesterol levels, blood pressure, heart rate, and indicators like diabetes, family history, smoking habits, obesity, and alcohol consumption. Additionally, lifestyle factors like exercise hours, dietary habits, stress levels, and sedentary hours are included. Medical aspects comprising previous heart problems, medication usage, and triglyceride levels are considered. Socioeconomic aspects such as income and geographical attributes like country, continent, and hemisphere are incorporated.

## Getting Started
If you don't want to start from scratch, you may clone the public notebook under the ""Code"" tab of this competition.

To do so, navigate to the ""Code"" tab and click on the public notebook. Then, click ""Copy & Edit"" at the top right of the notebook. A personal copy of the notebook will be created.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be scored by [Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html)

Accuracy is the number of predictions (that your model made) that match the true labels of the data. Consider a dataset with two possible ""classes"", 0 and 1. Given the dataset and the model predictions below, we say that the model's **accuracy** is 4/5 = 0.8, since it labelled 4 data points correctly.

DATASET
---------
| ID | FEATURES | TARGET  |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | ... | 1 |
| 2 | ... | 1 |
| 3 | ... | 1 |
| 4 | ... | 1 |
| 5 | ... | 1 |

MODEL PREDICTIONS
-----------------------
| ID | TARGET  |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 1 |
| 2 | 1 |
| 3 | 1 |
| 4 | 1 |
| 5 | 0 |



## Submission File
For each Patient ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the ""Heart Attack Risk"" variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

Patient ID,Heart Attack Risk
BMW7812,0
XXM0972,0
RQX1211,0
RVN4963,0
etc.","# Dataset Description

*Dataset credits:* https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/iamsouravbanerjee/heart-attack-prediction-dataset

You will train your model on the ""train.csv"" file, and predict the ""Heart Attack Risk"" column for the ""test.csv"" file. You may refer to the ""sample_submission.csv"" file for submission format. Note that if you clone the public notebook, submission file creation will be automatically handled, so you don't need to write additional code to handle that.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

* `Patient ID` - Unique identifier for each patient
* `Age` - Age of the patient
* `Sex` - Gender of the patient (Male/Female)
* `Cholesterol` - Cholesterol levels of the patient
* `Blood Pressure` - Blood pressure of the patient (systolic/diastolic)
* `Heart Rate` - Heart rate of the patient
* `Diabetes` - Whether the patient has diabetes (Yes/No)
* `Family History` - Family history of heart-related problems (1: Yes, 0: No)
* `Smoking` - Smoking status of the patient (1: Smoker, 0: Non-smoker)
* `Obesity` - Obesity status of the patient (1: Obese, 0: Not obese)
* `Alcohol Consumption` - Level of alcohol consumption by the patient (None/Light/Moderate/Heavy)
* `Exercise Hours Per Week` - Number of exercise hours per week
* `Diet` - Dietary habits of the patient (Healthy/Average/Unhealthy)
* `Previous Heart Problems` - Previous heart problems of the patient (1: Yes, 0: No)
* `Medication Use` - Medication usage by the patient (1: Yes, 0: No)
* `Stress Level` - Stress level reported by the patient (1-10)
* `Sedentary Hours Per Day` - Hours of sedentary activity per day
* `Income` - Income level of the patient
* `BMI` - Body Mass Index (BMI) of the patient
* `Triglycerides` - Triglyceride levels of the patient
* `Physical Activity Days Per Week` - Days of physical activity per week
* `Sleep Hours Per Day` - Hours of sleep per day
* `Country` - Country of the patient
* `Continent` - Continent where the patient resides
* `Hemisphere` - Hemisphere where the patient resides
* `Heart Attack Risk` - Presence of heart attack risk (1: Yes, 0: No)",False,True
