7348,face-classification-deep-learning,"# Overview

Welcome to the Face Classification (Deep Learning) competition! In this exciting challenge, you are to harness the power of cutting-edge neural networks for the task of associating different identities through facial images.

## Goal:

The primary objective of this competition is to develop deep learning models that can accurately classify faces, connecting each image with its corresponding identity. By leveraging the potential of deep neural networks, you'll have the opportunity to advance the field of computer vision and contribute to the development of facial recognition technologies.

Prepare to showcase your skills, compete with a community of data scientists, and make significant strides in the world of face classification through DL models. Get ready to dive in, and may the best models prevail!
----------------------
# Description

In the Face Classification (Deep Learning) competition, we delve into the fascinating domain of computer vision, where the task at hand is to classify faces with the power of deep learning models. This competition challenges participants to create models that can associate various facial images with their respective identities.

## The Challenge:

As the foundation of numerous applications, including facial recognition, security systems, and human-computer interaction, the accurate classification of faces plays a pivotal role in technology and society. Deep learning has revolutionized this field, offering immense potential for improving accuracy and efficiency.

The competition dataset is a diverse collection of facial images, spanning various demographics, expressions, and conditions. Your goal is to train DL models that can not only identify the presence of faces but also correctly label them with the corresponding identity. Participants are encouraged to explore different architectures, optimization techniques, and data preprocessing methods to achieve superior results.

Your innovative solutions may also gain recognition within the machine learning and data science communities. This competition welcomes participants of all skill levels. You can also find valuable resources, forums, and tutorials to assist you on your journey.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your models will be evaluated based on classification accuracy. The competition allows you to make multiple submissions, enabling you to fine-tune your models and improve your performance throughout the competition. However, only the final submission will be marked at the end.

## Metric:

The primary evaluation metric for this competition is **classification accuracy**.

## Submission Format:

Participants are required to submit a CSV file that contains two columns:

**""image_name"":** A unique identifier for each test image.
**""label"":** The predicted label or identity associated with each test image.
The ""label"" should be a representation corresponding to the identity you predict for each image in the test dataset.

## Scoring:

The score on the leaderboard is calculated based on the accuracy of your model's predictions on the test dataset. The higher the accuracy of your model, the better your leaderboard score.

## Final Ranking:

The final competition rankings will be based on the scores from the leaderboard as well as your code / notebook submissions. These will be revealed at the end of the competition. You are encouraged to fine-tune your models, experiment with different architectures, and optimize your approaches to achieve the highest classification accuracy possible. Make sure your submissions adhere to the specified format to ensure accurate evaluation and ranking.

Best of Luck!","# Dataset Description

## Directories:

- **train** - the training set; each identity has its own folder with 20 images
- **test** - the test set; it contains a total of 35k images (5 for each person)
- **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format showing you what needs to be submitted

You are free to create a validation set from the training set. Once your model has been completely trained, run it on the test set. Your final submission will be in the form of a CSV file that contains test set image names in the first column and their predicted labels in the second column. Make sure that the CSV file you submit is exactly in the same format as sample_submission.csv
 
**Note:** You still have to submit your code / notebook. The CSV file is just for automatic ranking on the test set for Kaggle leaderboard. For submission on Kaggle, create a zip file that contains both the CSV as well as the code file.

Also, make sure that your team name is set to your full name as it is present on Qalam.",False,True
7349,audio-driven-co-speech-gesture-video-generation,"# Overview

**£¡£¡£¡[New]£¡£¡£¡ Update: The final ranking is announced and the leader of the top 3 teams please send a confirmation email to [zenghaoqi@huawei.com]. Please also indicate your team name in the email.**


| #Rank|Team Name | Total 1-Place Number | Total 2-Place Number | The highest ranking except for first and second place |
|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| #1|DragonBooOOM | 4 | - | - |
| #2|USTC_IAT_United|  1| 1 | - |
| #3|damNull|  1| 0 | 5 |


Congratulations to all the winners! For ranking details, please refer to the following Public Per-Metric Leaderboard Results.

------------------------------------------

**Public Per-Metric Leaderboard Results**

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7542929%2Fbaf4731e9a496885fae29a5d1b40f0c5%2Fresult.png?generation=1704960241973483&alt=media)

**Note**: Only valid submissions that meet the correct format were evaluated, we recruited over 30 participants to do subjective evaluations. Moreover,  we also observe that objective metrics are not consistent with human subjective evaluations, which is the same as the findings of Kucherenko et al. [1].

**Reference**
[1] Kucherenko, Taras, et al. ""Evaluating gesture-generation in a large-scale open challenge: The GENEA Challenge 2022."" arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.08737 (2023).

------------------------------------------


**Happy New Year! The competition is now closed for submission and we will evaluate the results and announce the final results once we finish all the evaluations.**

**Please verify that your submission meets the correct format (a zip file containing videos with a resolution of 288x512 and a CSV file). Submissions with videos of other resolutions or in illegal formats will be rejected.**

Nonverbal behaviors, especially co-speech gestures, are vital for face-to-face communication. Studies have shown that gestures can help express intentions, convey emotions, and complement the information conveyed by speech. Recent research has demonstrated that machine learning-based approaches play an increasingly critical role in motion synthesis, which has become the primary method for co-speech gesture synthesis.

In this task, participants are required to synthesize co-speech gesture videos of a target person based on any given audio. The final output should be a rendered video, instead of motion sequences. Generally, the synthesized gesture motions in this task are expected to be natural, difficult to distinguish from captured videos, and consistent with audio in terms of rhythm, semantics, and style. We encourage participants to propose novel ideas for synthesizing high-fidelity co-speech gestures.

Specifically, we will provide two video clips for each target person, one (about 5 minutes) for training, and one (about 1 minute) for validation. We will also provide a testing audio for the participants to generate the final result video for the target person.

### Prizes
$1,800 will be awarded to the Top 3 teams with the highest scores at the conclusion of the competition:
- 1st Place - $ 1,000
- 2nd Place - $ 500
- 3rd Place - $ 300

**Note that teams that win the prize should submit their code and technical reports at the end of the competition.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

In this task, participants are supposed to generate co-speech gesture videos for given audio.
To evaluate the quality of the generated  co-speech gesture videos, we use both objective metrics and subjective metrics.

###Objective metrics
- **Fr¨¦chet Video Distance** (**FVD**) [3] to measure the similarity between two video sequences. It takes into account both spatial and temporal relationships between the videos, allowing for a more accurate assessment of their similarity.
- **Fr¨¦chet Gesture Distance** (**FGD**) [4] to evaluate the distance between the real and synthetic gesture distribution. We train an auto-encoder on poses extracted from both the competition dataset and PATS dataset to get motion features. Fr¨¦chet distance between the real and synthetic gesture is calculated in motion feature space.
- **Beat Consistency Score** (**BC**) [5] to measure the speech-motion alignment. 
- **Diversity** (**Div.**) [6] to measure the diversity among generated gestures.

**Note 1**:  **FGD**, **BC** and **Div.**  *evaluate generated videos from a human motion perspective. We first use [MMPose](https://github.com/open-mmlab/mmpose/tree/main) to estimate poses of videos.*

###Subjective metrics
- **Realness** to evaluate the generation quality on the video level, whether the video is generated or captured. 
- **Human-likeness** to evaluate whether the generated gestures look natural and are difficult to distinguish from captured human motions.
- **Gesture-speech appropriateness** to evaluate whether the generated gestures are consistent with the input audio, in terms of both rhythm and semantics.
- **Diversity (Div.)** to evaluate whether the generated gestures are diverse enough.

**Note 2**: *The rating scale for the subjective metrics is 1 to 5, with 1 being the poorest and 5 being the best.*
**Note 3**: *The Leaderboard only shows the ranking of **FVD**.  Other metrics will only be evaluated on the final submission.*

The final ranking would be determined by the number of ""first place"" across all metrics. If multiple teams win the same number of ""first place"" metrics, the final ranking would be determined by the number of ""second place"" across all metrics, and so on. 

## Submission File
Your team ultimately needs to submit a compressed zip file(*.zip), for instance, ""**submission.zip**"".
The compressed zip file contains two parts: submission.csv and your generated videos.
```
|--submission.zip
|----submission.csv
|----PersonA_test.mp4
|----PersonB_test.mp4
```
For submission CSV files, you should use the ""**extraction_FVD_to_submission.py**"" (submission_form.zip from Dataset) tool to process your generated videos. 
**!!!!! Tips: Please ensure that the file name of the video you generate matches our requirements: PersonA_test.mp4, PersonB_test.mp4**
For each ID in the test set, you must generate a video for a given audio and the video should be named according to the following format ""ID_test.mp4"", for example, ""PersonA_test.mp4"".

##Reference
[1] Audio-Driven Co-Speech Gesture Video Generation, NeurIPS 2022: ANGIE https://github.com/alvinliu0/ANGIE.
[2] Audio-Driven Neural Gesture Reenactment with Video Motion Graphs, CVPR 2022:  vid-reenact https://github.com/yzhou359/vid-reenact.
[3] Towards accurate generative models of video: A new metric & challenges. arXiv:1812.01717, 2018.
[4] Learn to Dance with AIST++: Music Conditioned 3D Dance Generation, ICCV2021: https://github.com/google-research/mint
[5] Speech Gesture Generation from the Trimodal Context of Text, Audio, and Speaker Identity, TOG2020: https://github.com/ai4r/Gesture-Generation-from-Trimodal-Context#gesture-generation-from-trimodal-context
[6] Learning Hierarchical Cross-Modal Association for Co-Speech Gesture Generation, CVPR2022: HA2G https://github.com/alvinliu0/HA2G","# Dataset Description

~~Our co-speech generation dataset contains training and validation videos for **4** different IDs (PersonA, PersonB, PersonC, and PersonD), each video contains a single speaker, PersonA and PersonB speak Chinese, PersonC and PersonD speak English. The training videos last for at least 5 minutes with a resolution of 2160¡Á3840 and the validation videos last for at least 1 minute with a resolution of 2160¡Á3840. ~~

**Update: 2023-12-01**


Our co-speech generation dataset contains training and validation videos for **2** different IDs (PersonA, PersonB), each video contains a single speaker, PersonA and PersonB speak Chinese. The training videos last for at least 5 minutes with a resolution of 2160¡Á3840 and the validation videos last for at least 1 minute with a resolution of 2160¡Á3840. 

The audio of the test set is also released for participants to generate their results for the competition.

###Note
1. We also notice that directly generating high-resolution 2D videos is very challenging and time-consuming, so in this competition, the participants may downsample to video resolution to 288¡Á512 to train their model, we will evaluate the results with a resolution of 288¡Á512.
1. This dataset can only be used for this competition.
1. Due to some legal issues, we fail to collect a suitable English dataset for this competition. As the competition will end within about 1 month, given little time left, we decide to use the 2 uploaded IDs only in this competition.",False,True
7350,simple-housing-price-prediction,"# Overview

**Goal:** Predict the sale price of houses.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Mean Absolute Error](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.mean_absolute_error.html). Mean absolute error represents how far away your predictions were from the true target value on average. Consider the dataset below:
| house_id | actual_price |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 10 |
| 2 | 20 |
| 3 | 30 |

and predictions:
| house_id | predicted_price |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 15 |
| 2 | 20 |
| 3 | 25 |

The Mean Absolute Error of the predictions would be (| 10 - 15 | + | 20 - 20 | + | 30 - 25 |) / 3 = 3.33. The lower this value, the better!

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the ""price"" variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    house_id,price
    2,100000
    5,123000
    6,214000
    etc.","# Dataset Description

You will be predicting the price of houses sold in the ""test.csv"" file. Your submission should have the following columns: ""house_id"" and ""price"".

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training data
*   **test.csv** - the test data
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

