7351,stat462-862project1,"# Overview

This is the page for the Kaggle competition for STAT 462/862 Project 1 in fall 2023
----------------------
# Description

In this project, you will analyze a subset of data derived from the dataset ""Red Wine Quality"". For background information, please visit the website (https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uciml/red-wine-quality-cortez-et-al-2009). 

Visit the **Data** tab above for explanations of data files. See the **Evaluation** part for evaluation metric, and the **Submission** part for submission.
 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the Root Mean Squared Prediction Error.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a value for the **quality** variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

   id, quality
   1001,5.744135
   1002,5.337353
   1003,5.176501
   1004,5.790692 
    etc.
----------------------
# Requirement 

The goal of the project is on getting better prediction on the quality of red wines. You are required to build **three predictive models**. Options of the predictive models include linear regression models, generalized additive models, decision trees, random forests, boosting, and any other reasonable methods (that may not be covered by the course). Note that you cannot use the same class of model with different parameters.  That is, the three fitted models you propose should be different in nature. For each of the models, you should have a score after submitting your prediction on Kaggle. To submit, hit the **submission** tab, and upload a csv file in the required format. In the end of your report, you should include a table summarizing these scores, as well as taking a screenshot of your best performance. You also need to attach on Crowdmark your submission files to Kaggle for verification.

Your report should contain the following components in a structured way.
        * A brief introduction (goal, data, background)
        * Exploratory data analysis and discussions (summary statistics, graphical analysis)
        * Descriptions and discussions on the proposed three predictive models
        * Your prediction scores on Kaggle

To help you get started,   I provide a file on OnQ for constructing a regression model, and generating the submission file to Kaggle in the required format. 

More information
    * Due: **11:59 PM EST, Monday, Nov 27** on Crowdmark.
    * Your grade will be based on the quality of your report, effort, and most importantly the performance of your prediction rules. Your submission on Crowdmark should include:  (1) A report, with no more than 10 pages. Be descriptive and concise. See the above **Requirement** section for details; (2) All your code files; (3) Three submission files to Kaggle in the required format. You should of course include those files with your best performance.  Note that all the files to Crowdmark must be in PDF, JPG or PNG form. 
    * You will need to create a Kaggle account to be able to make submissions. For privacy, you do NOT need to use your name as the user name (any user name will do).
    * You can only have three submissions per day","# Dataset Description

These are the input variables:
