7369,aocr2024,"# Overview

Aligned with the 22nd Asia Oceanian Congress of Radiology (AOCR 2024) held in Taipei, Taiwan from March 22-25, this AI challenge explores the significant role of AI in Radiology. The congress, expected to attract around 1,500 attendees, serves as an important platform for discussions and innovation in Radiology. Winning teams of this challenge will have the prestigious opportunity to present their solutions at the AOCR2024 conference. Besides waiving their registration fees, these teams will also receive special recognition and monetary prizes awarded at the AOCR2024 conference.  For more details, please refer to the [AOCR2024 official website](https://www.aocr2024.org/program/ai_challenge.asp).

This competition focuses on identifying acute appendicitis from CT scans of acute abdomen cases. Participants will engage in a classification task, distinguishing the presence or absence of acute appendicitis using contrast-enhanced CT scans.

Your contributions will aid in predicting if acute appendicitis is responsible for specific acute abdominal pain episodes. Furthermore, it will highlight the critical slices in the CT scan that medical professionals should examine to detect the inflamed appendix.
----------------------
# Description

Acute appendicitis is the most common cause of acute abdominal pain. Despite many previous studies, the diagnosis of acute appendicitis remains challenging. Misdiagnosis or delayed diagnosis can increase the incidence of perforation, peritonitis, and complications of laparoscopic surgery, all of which are associated with morbidity and mortality. Therefore, rapid and accurate diagnosis of acute appendicitis is crucial for effective treatment of acute abdominal pain. However, its symptoms are often unclear and overlap with other diseases, making the diagnosis of acute appendicitis difficult even when clinical physicians perform physical examinations and have blood test results. The clinical importance and numerical advantage of acute appendicitis make it the most frequently studied topic in AI-assisted diagnostic processes. 

Various modeling approaches, such as classification, detection, and segmentation, can be used. Rule-based pre- and post-processing methods for calculations are also permitted. Annotations can be manually performed and combined with multiple model calculations. If teams utilize additional data for training, it is necessary to make those data publicly accessible.

Please also note that it might be beneficial to keep an eye on the discussion board for potential insights.

****Competition Format****

This competition is organized into two stages:

1. **First Stage:** All participating teams will compete. After this stage, the top 20 teams or the top 20% of the teams (whichever is greater) will be selected based on their performance. Teams that fall into this category must fill out the organizing committee's Google form to facilitate contact and transition into the second stage.
2. **Second Stage:** The qualified teams from the first stage will compete. The rankings and winners will be determined at the end of this stage.

Please ensure that you provide accurate and up-to-date contact information in the Google form to avoid any miscommunications or missed opportunities.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Each CT slice is evaluated to identify the presence of an abnormal appendix indicative of appendicitis. Furthermore, the entire CT scan is assessed to determine a diagnosis of acute appendicitis for that particular scan session. 

Submissions will be assessed based on the F1 score, comparing the predictions to the actual targets.

Two distinct F1 scores will be determined: one for the scan-level predictions and another for the slice-level predictions. The final leaderboard ranking will be derived from a composite of these two F1 scores.


----------------------
# Submission File

The submission .csv file should contain two columns: ""id"" and ""label"". The ""label"" column should store the prediction results as either 1 or 0. A value of 1 indicates the presence of an inflamed appendix from acute appendicitis, while a value of 0 indicates the absence.

- For scan-level predictions, only enter the filename in the ""id"" column.
- For slice-level predictions, combine the filename with the slice count in the format: `filename_sliceCount` and enter it in the ""id"" column. Start the slice count at 0 and increment sequentially from the feet to the head direction.

Please refer to the illustration below.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2238168%2Fdefee0453bf15d2c90368c9b3b2fbc83%2Fsubmission.jpg?generation=1698894171955682&alt=media)
----------------------
# Timeline

- **November 1, 2023:** Stage 1 begins.
- **January 24, 2024:** Stage 1 ends.
- **January 29, 2024:** Stage 2 begins.
- **February 4, 2024:** Stage 2 ends & Final submission deadline.
- **February 21, 2024:** Confirmation deadline for attending the AOCR2024 conference.
- **March 23, 2024:** AOCR 2024 AI challenge winner presentation and award.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM GMT+8 on the specified day, unless otherwise stated. The challenge organizers reserve the right to modify the contest timeline if necessary.
----------------------
# Recognition for winning teams

Following the second stage, up to five leading teams will be offered the unique opportunity to present their solutions at the AOCR2024 conference. For these teams, not only will their conference registration fees be waived, but they will also receive special recognition and benefits that will be detailed on the AOCR2024 official website. Further information can be found on the AOCR2024 official website.
----------------------
# Acknowledgments

**Challenge Organizing Team**

Wen-Jeng Lee, MD - National Taiwan University Hospital

Kuei-Hong Kuo, MD - Far Eastern Memorial Hospital

Weichung Wang, PhD - National Taiwan University

Shu-Huei Shen, MD - Taipei Veterans General Hospital

Li-Jen Wang, MD - Linkou Chang-Gung Memorial Hospital

Hong-Jen Chiou, MD - Taipei Veterans General Hospital

**Data Contributor**
Far Eastern Memorial Hospital in New Taipei City, Taiwan


**Special Thanks**
- The Asian Oceanian Society of Radiology (AOSR) Emerging Trends Committee for their invaluable suggestions.
- Taiwan Radiological Society (TRS) AI and Imaging Informatics Committee for their continued support.","# Dataset Description

Far Eastern Memorial Hospital in New Taipei City, Taiwan has provided the image data and annotations. From this collection, we've chosen contrast-enhanced abdominal and pelvic CT scans from adults over 18 years of age. These scans, taken in the axial section, have a slice thickness of 5mm and cover a span of the last ten years.

- The training dataset comprises 500 CT scans from patients confirmed with acute appendicitis and an additional 500 scans from individuals who exhibited acute abdominal pain but were diagnosed without appendicitis. We've pre-divided the data into Train and Validation sets; adhering to this suggested split is advised.
- Test data: 200 CT scans from acute abdomen condition, but the composition ratio is unknown.
- We've created mask files for the CT scans showing acute appendicitis by delineating the affected appendix, as illustrated below in NIfTI format. Annotations were focused on the abnormal appendices, and inflammation in adjacent areas was not marked. We have taken extensive precautions to ensure the accuracy of both the images and annotations. However, in the unlikely event of a minor annotation error, we believe it will not compromise the fairness of the competition.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6662693%2F220773eed1672c3f4fe6c5e06de57bf7%2FUntitled.png?generation=1698375679755972&alt=media)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2238168%2F369ac1d079cb0ceb9ab21ee1acbe6e57%2Fdata.jpg?generation=1698823061860686&alt=media)


**Evaluation**

- Each CT slice is evaluated to identify the presence of an abnormal appendix indicative of appendicitis. Furthermore, the entire CT scan is assessed to determine a diagnosis of acute appendicitis for that particular scan session. The final score combines evaluations from both slice-level and scan-level assessments.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2238168%2F051221d82e3405c2133b8a318df061b6%2Fevaluation.jpg?generation=1698630943951479&alt=media)",False,True
