7389,fall2023-programming-assignment-3,"# Description

Here, you are asked to predict the forest cover type (the predominant kind of tree cover) from strictly cartographic variables (i.e., geographic mapping data etc.). The actual forest cover type for a given 30 x 30 meter cell was determined from US Forest Service (USFS) Region 2 Resource Information System data. Independent variables were then derived from data obtained from the US Geological Survey and USFS. The data is in raw form (not scaled) and contains binary columns of data for qualitative independent variables such as wilderness areas and soil type.
----------------------
# Evaluation

It's a 7-class classification problem. Please consider accuracy as the evaluation metric.","# Dataset Description

The study area includes four wilderness areas located in the Roosevelt National Forest of northern Colorado. Each observation is a 30m x 30m patch. You are asked to predict an integer classification for the forest cover type. The seven types are:

* 1 - Spruce/Fir
* 2 - Lodgepole Pine
* 3 - Ponderosa Pine
* 4 - Cottonwood/Willow
* 5 - Aspen
* 6 - Douglas-fir
* 7 - Krummholz


The training set, i.e, ""dataset.csv"" (15120 observations) contains both features and the Cover_Type. The test set (i.e., judge-no-labels.csv) contains only the features. You must predict the Cover_Type for every row in the test set (565892 observations).

**Data Fields**

* Elevation - Elevation in meters
* Aspect - Aspect in degrees azimuth
* Slope - Slope in degrees
* Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology - Horz Dist to nearest surface water features
* Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology - Vert Dist to nearest surface water features
* Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways - Horz Dist to nearest roadway
* Hillshade_9am (0 to 255 index) - Hillshade index at 9am, summer solstice
* Hillshade_Noon (0 to 255 index) - Hillshade index at noon, summer solstice
* Hillshade_3pm (0 to 255 index) - Hillshade index at 3pm, summer solstice
* Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points - Horz Dist to nearest wildfire ignition points
* Wilderness_Area (4 binary columns, 0 = absence or 1 = presence) - Wilderness area designation
* Soil_Type (40 binary columns, 0 = absence or 1 = presence) - Soil Type designation
* Cover_Type (7 types, integers 1 to 7) - Forest Cover Type designation

The wilderness areas are:

* 1 - Rawah Wilderness Area
* 2 - Neota Wilderness Area
* 3 - Comanche Peak Wilderness Area
* 4  - Cache la Poudre Wilderness Area

The soil types are:

* 1 Cathedral family - Rock outcrop complex, extremely stony.
* 2 Vanet - Ratake families complex, very stony.
* 3 Haploborolis - Rock outcrop complex, rubbly.
* 4  Ratake family - Rock outcrop complex, rubbly.
* 5  Vanet family - Rock outcrop complex complex, rubbly.
* 6 Vanet - Wetmore families - Rock outcrop complex, stony.
* 7 Gothic family.
* 8 Supervisor - Limber families complex.
* 9 Troutville family, very stony.
* 10 Bullwark - Catamount families - Rock outcrop complex, rubbly.
* 11  Bullwark - Catamount families - Rock land complex, rubbly.
* 12 Legault family - Rock land complex, stony.
* 13 Catamount family - Rock land - Bullwark family complex, rubbly.
* 14 Pachic Argiborolis - Aquolis complex.
* 15 unspecified in the USFS Soil and ELU Survey.
* 16 Cryaquolis - Cryoborolis complex.
* 17 Gateview family - Cryaquolis complex.
* 18 Rogert family, very stony.
* 19 Typic Cryaquolis - Borohemists complex.
* 20 Typic Cryaquepts - Typic Cryaquolls complex.
* 21 Typic Cryaquolls - Leighcan family, till substratum complex.
* 22 Leighcan family, till substratum, extremely bouldery.
* 23 Leighcan family, till substratum - Typic Cryaquolls complex.
* 24 Leighcan family, extremely stony.
* 25 Leighcan family, warm, extremely stony.
* 26 Granile - Catamount families complex, very stony.
* 27 Leighcan family, warm - Rock outcrop complex, extremely stony.
* 28 Leighcan family - Rock outcrop complex, extremely stony.
* 29 Como - Legault families complex, extremely stony.
* 30 Como family - Rock land - Legault family complex, extremely stony.
* 31 Leighcan - Catamount families complex, extremely stony.
* 32 Catamount family - Rock outcrop - Leighcan family complex, extremely stony.
* 33 Leighcan - Catamount families - Rock outcrop complex, extremely stony.
* 34 Cryorthents - Rock land complex, extremely stony.
* 35 Cryumbrepts - Rock outcrop - Cryaquepts complex.
* 36 Bross family - Rock land - Cryumbrepts complex, extremely stony.
* 37 Rock outcrop - Cryumbrepts - Cryorthents complex, extremely stony.
* 38 Leighcan - Moran families - Cryaquolls complex, extremely stony.
* 39 Moran family - Cryorthents - Leighcan family complex, extremely stony.
* 40 Moran family - Cryorthents - Rock land complex, extremely stony.",False,True
7390,audio-driven-talking-head-generation,"# Overview

###Final Announcement
**Happy new year!** The competition is now closed for submission and we would announce the most realistic results for both settings once we finish all the evaluations.
**Update: The final ranking is announced and the leader of the top3 team please send a confirmation email to [gjn0310@sjtu.edu.cn]. Please also indicate your team name in the email.**


In this task, we specifically focus on the ability to generate talking heads with realistic facial expressions and natural head poses that match the accompanying audio. By learning from both the audio and visual data, this task focuses on developing a **multi-modal learning model for talking head generation**. We further design the following two settings to motivate the participants to tackle this task with 2D and 3D methods, respectively.

- **Single image setting**: Participants are allowed to train their model with external data, and we will provide a **single image** for their models to animate.

- **Video setting**: Participants are not allowed to train their model with external data, while a **video** is provided for training a personalized talking head model. Participants are allowed to use pretrained audio models to process audios but are not allowed to use pretrained talking head (visual) model and finetune under this setting.

The final output of both settings should be a talking head model that can be driven by any input audio. For the image setting, the synthesized talking head videos are expected to be lip-synchronized and with high fidelity.  As for the video setting, we encourage the participants to design 3D-based models so that the speakers can have various natural poses during talking while preserving their identity. 

The official website of the workshop is provided as https://digitalhumanworkshop.github.io/

###Prizes
1st Place - $ 1000
2nd Place - $ 500
3rd Place - $ 300
The first place of the competition would win 1000$ as the prize, the second place would win 500$ and the third place would win 300$. An additional ""Most Realistic"" Award would be chosen at the end of the competition by the host for both tracks. **Note that teams that win the prize should submit their code and a brief summary at the end of the competition.**

If you have any questions, please raise an issue in the discussion.

**Please check all the requirements in the submission section before you submit your results.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

In this task, participants are supposed to generate talking head videos for both the image and video settings. 
To evaluate the quality of the generated talking heads, we use the following metrics:
- **SSIM** and **PSNR** to measure the generation quality on the image level for **Video Setting**. 
- **FID** to measure the generation quality on thefeature level for **Single Image Setting**.
- **AV offset and AV confidence** to evaluate the lip synchronization. Note that we use the absolute value of AV offset for evalution.
- **L1 Distance** of 3DMM landmarks to evaluate the generated head motion quality (Only be used in Video Setting).

**The ranking would be determined by the number of ¡°first place¡± across all metrics.** 
**IMPORTANT NOTICE**: Note that you could participate in either settings but the ranking would be based on the number of ¡°first place¡± **across all metrics of both settings**. For example, Team A only participate in the Single Image setting and get 4 ""first place"" while B participates in both settings and got 4 ""first place"" in Video setting and 1 in Single Image setting, the score of B is then 5, which is greater than A.
----------------------
# Submission

We recommend participants to zip all the generated results and save it to your own **Google Drive**. Since Kaggle accepts a **csv** file normally, participants should submit a **csv** file including the team name, the link of the google drive, and the setting that you choose to participate in.  A general submission format is provided as follows:
| ID    | Usage   | Vid          | Setting | Team_Name       |
|:-------:|:---------:|:------------:|:---------:|:-----------------:|
| Task3 | Public  | [Your Link]  |    0/1   | [Your Team Name]|

You need to fill in the **link** and **team name** in the table above, and fill in 0 for single image setting and 1 for video setting, **the rest of the table should be kept**. 
- The video in the zip file should **be named as xxx_pred.mp4**. The resolution should be **512¡Á512** and the frame rate should bd **25FPS**, where xxx is the original name of the training video or driving image. For example, for the result for the data named ""French"" would be French_pred.mp4.
- Note that after your submission,**the score would be the choice of your setting**, so please don't be worry if you get 0.0 or 1.0 for your score, we would evaluate the results as soon as possible and update the results in the **Public Per-Metric Leaderboard** in this page. 
- Participants can only submit **up to 5 ""valid"" results** during the competition. If the submission could not be evaluated (e.g. Participant does not submit in the request format), the submission would not be counted as ""valid"" submission. The final ranking would be based on the best submissions.
- If you met the some errors like ""ID column ID  not found in submission"", you can check whether you type an additional space after the column name. For example, please use ""ID"" instead of ""ID "".
- If you encounter ""Submission has been administratively invalidated"", you can check whether you have submitted **all 10 videos** in the zip file or whether you have successfully **shared the zip file**. In addition, please check whether you have **concated the audio** and video together and **name the video in the correct format**.
----------------------
# Baseline

In this task, we provide reference implementations of the baseline methods:
-Single image setting: **SadTalker** https://github.com/Winfredy/SadTalker.
-Video setting: **AD-NeRF** https://github.com/YudongGuo/AD-NeRF.
We acknowledge the authors for these two wonderful projects.
----------------------
# Public Per-Metric Leaderboard

Since we employ multiple metrics for evaluation, we would maintain a per-metric leaderboard here as reference. **The best evaluation results would be chosen**. 
If the final ranking appears to be the same for two team, FID would be used for video setting ranking. Subjective score would be given based on the realism of the generated results for both settings if ranking is still the same for tied teams.
###Top3 Overall Ranking
