####Latest Update: 2024.1.1","# Dataset Description

Our talking head dataset contains **10 videos**, where each video contains a single speaker. These videos are in different languages and all the videos last for at least two minutes with a resolution of 512¡Á512. 

- For the **Single image setting**, the first frame of each video's testing set is selected as the driving image.
- For the **video setting**, about 9/10 of the videos are given as the training set.

We choose the last 1/10 of the video to be the testing set. The audio of the testing set will be released for participants to generate their results for both the image and video settings.

Training videos and testing audios for video setting are in **Video.zip** while driving images and testing audios for single image setting are in **Single_Image.zip**.

We've also uploaded the **ground-truth camera poses** for testing set so that the PNSR and SSIM metrics would be more effective. The camera poses are generated following the convention of AD-NeRF. Please raise an issue in the discussion if you have any questions related to the dataset.",False,True
7391,skull-reconstruction,"# Overview

## **!!!News**
**Update: The final ranking is announced **
 Rank | team name       | final score 
------|-----------------|-------------
 #1   | PPW             | 0.8221      
 #2   | USTC_IAT_United | 0.8157      
 #3   | zhanggod        | 0.8151    

---

**Final Announcement:**Happy new year! The competition is now closed for submission.
**Update: The final ranking is announced and the leader of the top3 team please send a confirmation email to [zhengfan@mail.nwpu.edu.cn] Please also indicate your team name in the email.**

Efficient, accurate, and robust algorithms for 3D skull reconstruction can play a significant role in achieving internal and external consistency in digital human parametric models. In this task, we expect the cranial reconstruction algorithm to reconstruct the anatomical geometry of the skull based on the information provided by MRI, including but not limited to the precise geometry of the external surface of the face, the internal soft tissue structures of the face, and the characteristic points of the skull. This competition encourages participants to develop a semi-supervised learning-based cranial segmentation algorithm for MRI images using machine learning and deep learning techniques. The segmentation algorithm can fully exploit the information of unlabeled training samples based on a small number of labeled samples while improving the performance and generalization of the segmentation model. Ultimately, the high-quality segmentation results obtained on MRI will serve as the basis for 3D skull reconstruction.
The official website of the workshop is provided as https://digitalhumanworkshop.github.io/

# Prizes
+ 1st Place - $ 1,000
+ 2nd Place - $ 500
+ 3rd Place - $ 300
The first place of the competition would win 1000$ as the prize, the second place would win 500$ and the third place would win 300$.**Note that teams that win the prize should submit their code and a brief summary at the end of the competition.**

If you have any questions, please raise an issue in the discussion.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Quantitative evaluation
To evaluate the anatomical consistency of the reconstructed skull, we will use quantitative metrics such as [**Chamfer Distance (CD)**](https://gist.github.com/sergeyprokudin/c4bf4059230da8db8256e36524993367), [**Precision**, **Recall**, **F-score**](https://towardsdatascience.com/a-look-at-precision-recall-and-f1-score-36b5fd0dd3ec)(same as Dice score in this competition) to evaluate the reconstructed structure. The precision and recall have different emphases and cannot accurately evaluate the pros and cons of the reconstruction results. To this end, we also use the harmonic mean of them as the F-score. The expected/predicted pixel locations are normalized by image size to create a bounded 0-1 CD score.
The four metrics are combined, with a weight of 0.2 for the CD, 0.2 for the Precision, 0.2 for the Recall, 0.4 for the F-score(Dice score).
We use the baseline to perform 3D segmentation supervised learning on the training set, then perform skull mask inference on the test set. 
----------------------
# Baseline

To accomplish this task, we provide the nnU-Net[2] as a baseline method, a deep learning-based segmentation method that automatically configures itself using preprocessing, network architecture, training, and post-processing techniques. The official code can be found at https://github.com/MIC-DKFZ/nnUNet.
We acknowledge the authors for this wonderful project.
----------------------
# Submission file

**In order to reduce the submission file size, our metric uses run-length encoding on the pixel values**.  Instead of submitting an exhaustive list of indices for your segmentation, you will submit pairs of values that contain a start position and a run length. E.g. '1 3' implies starting at pixel 1 and running a total of 3 pixels (1,2,3).

Note that, at the time of encoding, the mask should be binary, meaning the masks for all objects in an image are joined into a single large mask. A value of 0 should indicate pixels that are not masked, and a value of 1 will indicate pixels that are masked.

The competition format requires a space delimited list of pairs. For example, '1 3 10 5' implies pixels 1,2,3,10,11,12,13,14 are to be included in the mask. The metric checks that the pairs are sorted, positive, and the decoded pixel values are not duplicated. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```txt
id,predicted
1,1 1 5 1
2,1 1
3,2 4 10 1
4,3 5 12 2
etc.
```

The following is an example code for converting between RLE and mask (2D):
```python
# mask(2D) to rle
def rle_encode(img):
    pixels = img.flatten()
    if np.all(pixels == 0):
        return '0'
    pixels = np.concatenate([[0], pixels, [0]])
    runs = np.where(pixels[1:] != pixels[:-1])[0] + 1
    runs[1::2] -= runs[::2]
    return ' '.join(str(x) for x in runs)
# rle to mask
def rle_decode(encoded_str, shape):
    if encoded_str == '0':
        return np.zeros(shape)
    s = encoded_str.split() 
    starts, lengths = [np.asarray(x, dtype=int) for x in (s[0:][::2], s[1:][::2])] 
    starts -= 1 
    ends = starts + lengths 
    img = np.zeros(shape[0]*shape[1], dtype=np.uint8) 
    for lo, hi in zip(starts, ends): 
         img[lo:hi] = 1 
    return img.reshape(shape)
```
----------------------
# Reference

[1] IXI Dataset: http://brain-development.org/ixi-dataset/.
[2] Isensee, F., Jaeger, P. F., Kohl, S. A., Petersen, J., & Maier-Hein, K. H. (2021). nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image segmentation. Nature methods, 18(2), 203-211.","# Dataset Description

To foster the advancement of skull reconstruction algorithms, we offer a comprehensive head MRI dataset derived from IXI[1] (http://brain-development.org/ixi-dataset/) along with its corresponding ground-truth data for evaluating the quality of reconstructed skulls. We have taken measures to ensure the privacy of the individuals in the dataset by implementing techniques such as anonymization and de-identification, and we strive to comply with all relevant laws and regulations.
The complete dataset consists of 110 facial MRI scans divided into two parts: training and testing sets. The training set comprises 20 MRIs with corresponding gold standards and 80 unlabeled MRIs that participants may use for training and model validation purposes. Additionally, 10 unpublished facial MRI scans serve as test sets for evaluating the performance of each algorithm.

+ **train** contains 100 facial MRI scans for training and model validation.
+ **test**  contains 10 facial MRI scans without ground truth.
+ **submission-example.csv** is a submission example, which represents the gold standard RLE-encoded form of the Guys002 example from the training set. It contains a total of 150 rows of data. 
## Submission file
The final submission CSV file contains only two columns: 'id' and 'predicted'. The 'id' column is sequentially encoded from 1 to 1500, representing the slice index. The 'predicted' row represents the RLE encoding of a mask in a two-dimensional slice. Every 150 slices form a three-dimensional image, which corresponds to a test sample. The CSV file contains a total of 1500 rows, including all 10 predicted masks for the test set. 
## Attention
+ In the submission_example.csv file, '0' represents that there is no segmentation target in that particular slice.
+ It is important to encode them in ascending order according to the test sample numbers, such as 002, 023, 089. In 'Guys-002', '002' represents the sample number.
+ To obtain the RLE encoding of the test samples, follow these steps: Firstly, align the predicted mask with the corresponding image orientation. Secondly, use SimpleITK to read the mask and obtain a numpy matrix of size (150, 256, 256). Finally, perform RLE encoding on the slice dimension (256, 256) according to the first dimension(150). Note that the order of reading with the nibabel package is different from SimpleITK, so please refer to the results in the **submission-example.csv** file for encoding.",False,True
