7400,kaggle-quest-ignite-ii,"# Overview

PEC ACM presents you Kaggle Quest Ignite II, a competition where you will get to display your Machine Learning skills. This challenge will cover Data Preprocessing, Dimensionality Reduction and Classification Techniques. Participants have to implement these on a dataset and make predictions.
Please fill the form below for marking your attendance and providing feedback.
[Attendance Form](https://forms.gle/B8NVDxx8r8Ui3Wnf6)
----------------------
# Description

Hey there all the ML Enthusiasts! ???

Wish for a chance to display your machine learning knowledge and skills? Guess what, the wait is over. We present you the Kaggle Quest Ignite II where you'll get the opportunity to apply your learnings practically and stand out from the crowd ?.

So, get ready to learn new techniques and put them to the test in this exciting challenge. The following resources have been selectively brought to you for reference. Feel free to share any doubts or queries in the ?ai-discussion discord channel.

[Resources](https://docs.google.com/document/d/15QYd1qfjBKlULz5mgFtMtOJ6pba3Km0GzVebCq997lA/edit?usp=sharing)

Expecting your exuberant participation! ??
----------------------
# Evaluation

For the provided dataset, participants are required to complete the following tasks:

1) Find and remove all missing values.
2) Remove the top and bottom 1% outliers.
3) Split the dataset into training and testing sets.
4) Handle columns with categorical data.
5) Remove redundant columns from the data.
6) Apply scaling on the data.
7) Apply Gaussian Naive Bayes, K Nearest Neighbors and Logistic Regression to predict type of attacks. Display accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score and confusion matrix for each technique.
8) Compare the accuracy results and conclude the model which best fits the data.

**Note: For the given test_data.csv, you need to make a csv file having the labels predicted by your model and submit it.**","# Dataset Description

The dataset provides information about the type of cyber attack according to the given specifications of the attack such as Flow Duration, Total Forward Packets, Total Backward Packets, Flow Bytes, etc. Participants are required to use classification models to predict attack type using the attack specifications.

## Files
**train_data.csv** - the training set (having the labels also)
**test_data.csv**- the test set (whose labels you need to predict and submit)",False,True
7401,artificial-text-detection-homework,"# Overview

**Artificial text detection** 

Motivated by the urgent need for artificial text detection, we suggest you develop your own classifier capable of distinguishing between human-written and machine-generated texts.
----------------------
# Description

This assignment extends from the hands-on session on artificial text detection.

* We use the **IMDB** dataset as a source of human-written texts
* Eight text generation models and three decoding strategies are used to generate the testing data
* The specific text generation models will remain undisclosed until the homework deadline.
* We will only reveal that *GPT2-large* is one of the eight models under consideration.

This assignment is a binary classification task, wherein you are required to train a classifier that predicts whether a text is human-written (`H`) or machine-generated (`M`). 






----------------------
# Evaluation

Accuracy is used as a main evaluation metric.


----------------------
# Baselines

We provide three baselines for reference:
* The random baseline randomly assign one of two labels to a text (`H` or `M`)
* The weak baseline re-uses the model, trained in the practical session 
* The strong baseline improves upon weak baseline by incorporating data from various text generation models
* The super-strong baseline outperforms other baselines by integrating data from all text generation models employed in the test set
----------------------
# Submission Format

For every Id in the test set, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Class.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Class
0,H
1,M
2,M
```
----------------------
# Data

- We will provide development data only as an only labeled set 
- We will not disclose the text generation models used to create the test set
- You are welcome to utilize any text generation model and any sources of your choice for training data
----------------------
# Grading

You will receive:
- 1 point if your top submission surpasses the random baseline
- +2 points if your leading submission outperforms the weak baseline
- +2 points if your top submission outperforms the strong baseline
- +1 bonus point if your best submission outperforms the super strong baseline

Grading will be determined by the scores achieved on the **private** leaderboard.
----------------------
# Reference 

- Use [this Kaggle tutorial](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission) to learn how to get started 
- Refer to [this Quora discussion](https://www.quora.com/What-is-the-difference-between-public-and-private-leaderboard-in-Kaggle) to familiarize yourself with the concept of public/private leaderboards","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **dev.csv** - the development set
*   **test.csv** - the test set


## Columns

* ID -- the ID 
* Text  -- the text 
* Class -- the target class, `H` stands for human-written texts, `M` for machine-generated text",False,True
