7404,tunadromd-malware-detection,"# Overview


TUNADROMD dataset contains 4465 instances and 241 attributes. The target attribute for classification is a category (malware vs good ware). (N.B. This is the preprocessed version of TUANDROMD)
Dataset Characteristics

# Instances - 4465  # Features-  242

----------------------
# Description

""TUNADROMD"" refers to a dataset used for classification tasks in the field of cybersecurity, specifically for distinguishing between malicious software (malware) and legitimate software (goodware). The dataset you mentioned, with 4,465 instances and 241 attributes, has been preprocessed and is presumably designed for use in machine learning and data analysis tasks.

Here's an explanation of some of the key terms and concepts related to this dataset:

    Dataset Size: The dataset contains 4,465 instances. An instance typically represents a single data point, which in this context may correspond to a particular software application.

    Attributes: The dataset has 241 attributes. Attributes are the characteristics or features associated with each instance. In the context of malware classification, these attributes could include various characteristics of software files or behaviors, such as file size, code patterns, permissions, and many other factors that may be relevant for distinguishing between malware and legitimate software.

    Target Attribute: In a classification task, there is typically a target attribute that you want to predict or classify. In this case, the target attribute is a binary category that distinguishes between ""malware"" and ""goodware."" Each instance in the dataset is labeled as either malware or goodware based on this attribute.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on accuracy


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Label
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

