7446,skku-ml-2023-hw5,"# Overview

The theme of this competition is Multi-Class Imbalanced Classification, and the dataset is the same as Homework 4. **You are free to use any models and methods.**
----------------------
# Description

Imbalanced classification refers to a type of data where the quantity of data points in each class is different. This is a common occurrence in real-world datasets. For this competition, the number of data points for each class is as follows:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5279496%2F2856710b7bc61a2b3f343839f539c9fa%2Fclass_count.png?generation=1700495915185450&alt=media)

Imbalanced classification can be addressed in various ways. we introduce some of the methods below.

Data-level approaches:
Down sampling - This method involves reducing the number of data points for each class to the same quantity as the class with the smallest number of data points. For example, if the dataset consists of [5000 dogs, 100 cats], down-sampling would involve randomly selecting a subset of the dog data points to match the number of cat data points.
Up sampling - This method involves increasing the number of data points for each class to the same quantity as the class with the largest number of data points. For example, if the dataset consists of [5000 dogs, 100 cats], up-sampling would involve duplicating the cat data points until there are 5000 data points for each class.
Synthetic Sampling- Replicating the same data multiple times during training may not be very effective in some cases. To address this, we can add random noise to the duplicated data points to create more diverse training data.
Loss-level approaches:
Weighted loss function - In this approach, we assign higher weights to the data points in the minority class and lower weights to the data points in the majority class during training. This way, the model gives more attention to the minority class during training, improving its ability to correctly classify those data points.
There are many ways to address imbalanced classification problems.

The following URL provides solutions for class imbalance:
1. https://www.tensorflow.org/tutorials/structured_data/imbalanced_data?hl=ko
2. https://imbalanced-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html#user-guide
3. **https://github.com/ZhiningLiu1998/awesome-imbalanced-learning**
