7464,the-titanic-bin318,"# Overview

The Challenge
The sinking of the Titanic is one of the most infamous shipwrecks in history.
On April 15, 1912, during her maiden voyage, the widely considered ¡°unsinkable¡± RMS Titanic sank after colliding with an iceberg. Unfortunately, there weren¡¯t enough lifeboats for everyone onboard, resulting in the death of 1502 out of 2224 passengers and crew.
While there was some element of luck involved in surviving, it seems some groups of people were more likely to survive than others.
In this challenge, we ask you to build a predictive model that answers the question: ¡°what sorts of people were more likely to survive?¡± using passenger data (ie name, age, gender, socio-economic class, etc).

----------------------
# Description

The data contains the following:
    0: Id (Passenger ID)
    1: Outcome (Survived Indicator)
    2: Passenger Class  -- Pclass
    3: Name
    4: Sex
    5: Age
    6: Siblings/Spouses Aboard -- Sibsp
    7: Parents/Children Aboard -- Parch
    8: Fare paid in ¡ês

Variable Notes
Pclass: A proxy for socio-economic status (SES)
1st = Upper
2nd = Middle
3rd = Lower

Sibsp: The dataset defines family relations in this way...
Sibling = brother, sister, stepbrother, stepsister
Spouse = husband, wife (mistresses and fianc¨¦s were ignored)

Parch: The dataset defines family relations in this way...
Parent = mother, father
Child = daughter, son, stepdaughter, stepson
Some children travelled only with a nanny, therefore parch=0 for them.

Age: Age is fractional if less than 1. If the age is estimated, is it in the form of xx.5
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Accuracy score between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
Submission File Format:

For each Id in the test set, you must predict a probability for the Outcome variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
You should submit a csv file with exactly 171 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond Id and Outcome) or rows.
The file should have exactly 2 columns:
    Id (Passenger ID sorted in any order)
    Outcome (contains your binary predictions: 1 for survived, 0 for deceased)

    Id,Outcome
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.

##Citation

Will Cukierski. (2012). Titanic - Machine Learning from Disaster. Kaggle. https://kaggle.com/competitions/titanic

DrAshok (2023). The Titanic - BIN318 Mid-term. Kaggle. https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/the-titanic-bin318","# Dataset Description

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each passenger. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± like passengers¡¯ gender and class. You can also use **feature engineering** to create new features.

Design a network with **two hidden layers.** Also use measures to **control overfitting** of deep learning models. 

The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each passenger. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each passenger in the test set, use the model you trained to predict whether or not they survived the sinking of the Titanic.",False,True
7465,nju-dmco2023fall-final-project,"# Overview

This competition pertains to the completion of the final project for the course ""Data Mining for Complex Data Objects"" in Fall 2023 at Nanjing University. 
The project is under the guidance of Professor Li. 

**Notice**: 
-  **Please set your team name to your student ID.**
- **You should complete this competition by yourself, cooperation is not allowed.**

> If you have any questions regarding the project, please feel free to contact Mr. Ding-Wei Li (mailto: lidw@lamda.nju.edu.cn) for assistance.
----------------------
# Description

The task in this competition is to predict the click-through rate (CTR) for online advertisements. The dataset provided to participants includes 22 anonymous categorical features and a binary label that indicates whether a user clicked on the ad or not. 

**The training set consists of two parts: the labeled part and the unlabeled part**, which is indicated with `target = 2`. It is important to design an algorithm that can utilize both parts of the data.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Area Under the ROC curve, AUC for short](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target. AUC measures the model's ability to rank the instances correctly, with a higher value indicating better predictive performance.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a binary label. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,target
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0","# Dataset Description

The dataset provided for this competition has the following column names:

| Column Name | Description                                                     |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| target       | Binary label {0, 1} indicating whether a user clicked on the ad or not, if label=2 which means it is unlabeled data |
| feat_1      | Anonymous categorical feature 1                                 |
| feat_2      | Anonymous categorical feature 2                                 |
| ...         | ...                                                             |
| feat_22     | Anonymous categorical feature 22                                |",False,True
