7477,who-life-expectancy-prediction,"# Overview

WHO Life Expectancy Prediction
----------------------
# Description

**Welcome to AICVS competition on WHO life expectancy prediction
**
AICVS strongly believes that hands-on learning is the best learning. Here we are with this beginner-friendly InClass Kaggle Competition so that you can apply all that you have learned so far.

**The Challenge**
The dataset spans 193 countries and covers the years 2000-2015, showcasing the dynamic landscape of global health over this period.

In this competition, participants are tasked with constructing a robust predictive model to precisely forecast life expectancy.
**Predictive Factors:**
Immunization:
Indicators linked to immunization practices and coverage.
Mortality:
Variables associated with mortality rates, including the number of deaths.
Economy:
Economic variables reflecting country financial status and development, potentially including GDP.
Social:
Factors tied to the social fabric, encompassing indicators contributing to overall population well-being.

**The Opportunity**
This competition serves as an opportunity for participants to demonstrate their prowess in data analysis and prediction within the critical context of global health. By leveraging machine learning techniques, participants can uncover patterns and relationships within the dataset to build a predictive model that accurately forecasts life expectancy.

You will find the data description along with train and test datasets under the Data tab. Check out the evaluation metric and submission file format in the Evaluation section. You are allowed to make 20 submissions per day.

About Kaggle's free accelerator quota
Learn efficient GPU usage tips: https://www.kaggle.com/docs/efficient-gpu-usage
Learn how to use TPUs: https://www.kaggle.com/docs/tpu

**Acknowledgements**
The dataset has been taken?from?Kaggle.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation Criteria is **Mean Absolute Error** .","# Dataset Description

**Using the given csv data file, the participants have to develop a prediction model based on appropriate machine learning or any other algorithms they might find fit to predict the life expectancy   .**

Files
**testing data.csv** - To train the model
**training data.csv**- To test the model.
**sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

**Columns**

Country : Country
Year : Year
Status : Country Developed or Developing status
Life expectancy : Life expectancy in age
Adult Mortality : Adult Mortality Rates of both sexes (probability of dying between 15 and 60 years per 1000 population)
infant deaths : Number of Infant Deaths per 1000 population
Alcohol : Alcohol, recorded per capita (15+) consumption (in litres of pure alcohol) percentage expenditure: Expenditure on health as a percentage of Gross Domestic Product per capita(%)
Hepatitis B : Hepatitis B (HepB) immunization coverage among 1-year-olds (%)
Measles : Measles - number of reported cases per 1000 population
BMI : Average Body Mass Index of entire population
under-five deaths : Number of under-five deaths per 1000 population
Polio : Polio (Pol3) immunization coverage among 1-year-olds (%)
Total expenditure : General government expenditure on health as a percentage of total government expenditure (%)
Diphtheria : Diphtheria tetanus toxoid and pertussis (DTP3) immunization coverage among 1-year-olds (%)
HIV/AIDS : Deaths per 1000 live births HIV/AIDS (0-4 years)
GDP : Gross Domestic Product per capita (in USD)
Population : Population of the country
thinness 1-19 years : Prevalence of thinness among children and adolescents for Age 1 to 19 (%)
thinness 5-9 years : Prevalence of thinness among children for Age 5 to 9(%)
Income composition of resources : Human Development Index in terms of income composition of resources (index ranging from 0 to 1)
Schooling : Number of years of Schooling(years)",False,True
