7505,kidney-segmentation-of-novel-microscopy-images,"# Overview

This is not a real competition, since we do not publish annotated data. It's just a problem statement. Please, look at the [data description page](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/kidney-segmentation-of-novel-microscopy-images/data).
----------------------
# Evaluation

There is no evalution.","# Dataset Description

**Overview**
Images are large cross sectional images of mouse kidneys (cortex to medulla) imaged using NoRI microscopy coupled with multi-colour fluorescence microscopy (example in Figure 1 and 2). 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2F2791500724b51624ec00366b9ebb64a5%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2014.52.06.png?generation=1702067569156656&alt=media)
*Figure 1 - Protein and Lipid of Kidney by NoRI Microscopy*

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2Ff6c3ce5e9205849bb94907be8edc00c7%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2014.53.21.png?generation=1702067601232844&alt=media)
*Figure 2 - NoRI and Immunofluorescence Images overlaid with example structures highlighted*

Images comprise of 6 channels, channel 1 and 2 representing protein and lipid, respectively (actual content in mg/ mL owing to the nature of NoRI). Channels 3-6 represent immunofluorescence labelling of specific proteins within the tissue to enable to identification of tubule types (e.g., proximal tubules = LTL+ tubules (red; Channel 5), collecting ducts = AQP2+ tubules (purple; Channel 6), distal tubules = Uromodullin+ tubules (green; Channel 4), vasculature = endomucin+ staining (cyan; Channel 3)). Therefore, files are presented in this format:

*Image Channel Key (Displayed in Figure 3)*
Channel 1: NoRI derived protein content
Channel 2: NoRI derived lipid content
Channel 3: Antibody staining for vasculature (endomucin+)
Channel 4: Antibody staining for distal tubules (Uromodullin+)
Channel 5: Antibody staining for proximal tubule brush borders (LTL+)
Channel 6: Antibody staining for collecting ducts (AQP2+)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2Fba4c10aef7d855f81ee5156513234caa%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2015.25.22.png?generation=1702067152706983&alt=media)
*Figure 3 - Channels examples*

*Image file type, number, and size*
Data are in TIFF format, at 184.9MB per file, representing each 7820x1970 pixels in 16-bit format, with 20 images total, each with a sample number (S1-S10, representing a different biological replicate, and two (denoted by a (2)) technical replicates per biological replicate (e.g., S1_fullpanel_translated.tif and S1(2)_fullpanel_translated.tif for one biological replicate).

**Specific task requirements for the challenge**
The task for this challenge is two-fold. First, using Channels 1 and 2 only (protein and lipid) we need to first segment whole tubules (see reference images), then within each tubule, we need to be able to identify three main structures for segmentation of each tubule into four regions of interest for analysis; these being 1, Nuclei; 2, lumen; and 3, brush border; leaving the cytoplasm as the forth region of interest (see attached images for reference to each region in Figure 4 and 4). 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2F685b53be32c9808f0c67736e8b3874dc%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2015.16.08.png?generation=1702067699844403&alt=media)
*Figure 4 - Outline of structures of interest for segmentation*

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2F987c2a9634abbf24d908bbad971be0dd%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2015.01.33.png?generation=1702067777479383&alt=media)
*Figure 5 - Tubule types of interest for segmentation*

Secondly, once this task has been performed, we need to be able to assign each individual tubule a specific ID that relates to the immunofluorescence labels in Channels 4-6; namely, proximal tubules (characterised by LTL+ brush border stain), distal tubules (characterised by uromodullin+ stain), and collecting ducts (characterised by AQP2+ stain). With this information we will be able to successfully quantify - for the first time - the protein and lipid content of cells, subcellular and larger-scale structures within whole organs, using the kidney as the model organ in this instance (an outline of NoRI + IF for structural identification is presented in Figure 6. This new way of viewing tissue biology/ physiology will pave the way for a new, deeper understanding of the way we view tissue biology in health and disease.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2F16f2ef7459ded4aeb29a3a503a564f07%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2015.37.00.png?generation=1702067855152529&alt=media)
*Figure 6 - NoRI and IF side by side to highlight structures of interest in IF used to identify tubule types, post-segmentation from NoRI images*

An example of tubule and structure segmentation was produced by manual segmentation of tubules and Ilastik segmentation of structures of interest for reference/ as a Gold standard for this segmentation challenge is presented in Figure 7.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2F0973c51e11555ff0cb19cd7a645739d4%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2015.09.22.png?generation=1702067993471958&alt=media)
*Figure 7 - Overview of manual segmentation of kidney tubules and structures of interest. For reference, raw images (far left) were hand segmented for tubules (white outline), which were taken into Ilastik for identification of tubules (centre, top) brush borders (centre, middle), and nuclei (centre, bottom) from NoRI and/or immunofluorescence channels. Finally, post-structure segmentation in Ilastik, the resultant cytoplasm mask is produced for segmentation.*

As organs are heterogenous, complex environments, there are also certain discernable structures that won't be part of the present segmentation challenge; namely, other minor tubule types in the medulla, arteries/large veins, and glomeruli. For reference, each of these have been identified as reference images for structures to ignore (Figure 8).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18139339%2F11621a13680270044ce5d9a0a46cd152%2FScreenshot%202023-12-08%20at%2015.00.09.png?generation=1702068150786898&alt=media)
*Figure 8 - Structures to be omitted/ ignored from the present segmentation task*",False,True
7506,widsdatathon2024-challenge1,"# Overview

Join the WiDS Datathon to hone your data science skills while solving an interesting and critical social impact challenge! Utilizing a unique oncology dataset, you will develop a model to predict if patients received metastatic cancer diagnosis within 90 days of screening.

----------------------
# Description

# Background: Equity in Healthcare
[Women in Data Science (WiDS) Worldwide](widsworldwide.org) is on a mission to increase participation of women in data science to benefit societies worldwide. 

Healthcare inequity is a global challenge.  Addressing this challenge has an extensive positive impact on women¡¯s health, which is key for societies and economies to thrive.  This datathon is designed to help you discover whether disparate treatments exist and to understand the drivers of those biases, such as demographic and societal factors.

# Registration
All participants are required to register with WiDS Worldwide to participate in the WiDS Datathon. You only need to register once, regardless of the datathon 2024 competition(s) that you choose to compete in.
>**Registration is now closed**. 

In order for a participant under the age of 18 (or the age of contractual majority in their country) to participate in the Kaggle competition, the child¡¯s parent or legal guardian must also fill out and submit a [Parent/Guardian Consent Form](https://www.kaggle.com/consent-minors-process). 

# Overview: The Dataset and Challenge
[Gilead Sciences](https://www.gilead.com/) is the sponsor for this year¡¯s [WiDS Datathon](https://www.widsworldwide.org/learn/datathon/). They provided a rich, real-world dataset which contains information about demographics, diagnosis and treatment options, and insurance provided about patients who were diagnosed with breast cancer from 2015-2018. The dataset originated from Health Verity, one of the largest healthcare data ecosystems in the US. It was enriched with third party geo-demographic data to provide views into the socio economic aspects that may contribute to health equity. For this challenge, the dataset was then further enriched with zip code level toxicology data NASA/Columbia University. 

## Challenge task: 
You will be asked to predict if the patients received metastatic cancer diagnosis within 90 days of screening.

## Why is this important? 
Metastatic TNBC is considered the most aggressive TNBC and requires most urgent and timely treatment. Unnecessary delays in diagnosis and subsequent treatment can have devastating effects in these difficult cancers. Differences in the wait time to get treatment is a good proxy for disparities in healthcare access.

The primary goal of building these models is to detect relationships between demographics of the patient with the likelihood of getting timely treatment. The secondary goal is to see if environmental hazards impact proper diagnosis and treatment.  

## Questions?
Email: [datathon@widsworldwide.org](datathon@widsworldwide.org)
[widsworldwide.org/learn/datathon](https://www.widsworldwide.org/learn/datathon/)
----------------------
# Acknowledgements

Thank you [Gilead Sciences](https://www.gilead.com/) for providing the WiDS Datathon 2024 dataset!

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16304969%2Ff944353a526b16b6463c9f5efab5f0e4%2FGilead_Logo_ex_sm.png?generation=1703103087863269&alt=media)

*Gilead Sciences, Inc. is a biopharmaceutical company that specializes in the research, development, and commercialization of medicines, primarily in the fields of virology, infectious diseases, oncology, and other areas of unmet medical need. At Gilead, we are committed to creating a healthier world for everyone. And ???????through bold and transformative science, we¡¯re driving innovation that has the potential to become the next generation of life-changing medicines.*
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions for the leaderboard will be evaluated on the [Area under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted and the observed target `DiagPeriodL90D `. Learn more about AUC in this [developer crash course](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc), [this video](https://www.dataschool.io/roc-curves-and-auc-explained/), or [this Kaggle Learn Forum post](https://www.kaggle.com/discussions/getting-started/53782).



## Submission Format
For every patient (i.e. row) in the test dataset (`test.csv`), submission files should contain two columns: `patient_id` and `DiagPeriodL90D`. `patient_id` should be an integer and `DiagPeriodL90D` should be a percentage (eg: .5). For each row, these two values should be separated by a comma.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
patient_id,DiagPeriodL90D 

372069 ,.5
981264, .5
```

## Leaderboard Standings
During the competition the leaderboard is calculated with approximately 51% of the test data. After the competition closes, the final standings will be computed based on the other 49%. Thus, **the final leaderboard standings may be different from those during the competition.**
