> ####??&nbsp;  Recommended Competition
> We highly recommend [Titanic - Machine Learning from Disaster](https://kaggle.com/c/titanic/overview) to get familiar with the basics of machine learning and Kaggle competitions.

Welcome to the year 2912, where your data science skills are needed to solve a cosmic mystery. We've received a transmission from four lightyears away and things aren't looking good.

The *Spaceship Titanic* was an interstellar passenger liner launched a month ago. With almost 13,000 passengers on board, the vessel set out on its maiden voyage transporting emigrants from our solar system to three newly habitable exoplanets orbiting nearby stars.

While rounding Alpha Centauri en route to its first destination¡ªthe torrid 55 Cancri E¡ªthe unwary *Spaceship Titanic* collided with a spacetime anomaly hidden within a dust cloud. Sadly, it met a similar fate as its namesake from 1000 years before. Though the ship stayed intact, almost half of the passengers were transported to an alternate dimension!

<img title=¡±ID 52¡± src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/Spaceship%20Titanic/joel-filipe-QwoNAhbmLLo-unsplash.jpg"" style=""float:center; height:320px; width:404px"">

To help rescue crews and retrieve the lost passengers, you are challenged to predict which passengers were transported by the anomaly using records recovered from the spaceship¡¯s damaged computer system.

Help save them and change history!

> ####??&nbsp;  Getting Started Notebook
> To get started quickly, feel free to take advantage of [this starter notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/gusthema/spaceship-titanic-with-tfdf/notebook).

**If you want to talk with other users about this competition, come join our Discord! We've got channels for competitions, job postings and career discussions, resources, and socializing with your fellow data scientists. Follow the link here:** https://discord.gg/kaggle

### Acknowledgments

Photos by <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@joelfilip?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"">Joel Filipe</a>, <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@uncle_rickie?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"">Richard Gatley</a> and <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@actionvance?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"">ActionVance</a> on Unsplash.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric

Submissions are evaluated based on their [classification accuracy](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy), the percentage of predicted labels that are correct.

### Submission Format

The submission format for the competition is a csv file with the following format:

```
PassengerId,Transported
0013_01,False
0018_01,False
0019_01,False
0021_01,False
etc.
```
----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

<h3>What is a Getting Started competition?</h3>
<p>Getting Started competitions were created by Kaggle data scientists for people who have little to no machine learning background. They are a great place to begin if you are new to data science or just finished a MOOC and want to get involved in Kaggle.</p>
<p>Getting Started competitions are a non-competitive way to get familiar with Kaggle¡¯s platform, learn basic machine learning concepts, and start meeting people in the community. They have no cash prize and are on a rolling timeline.</p>
<h3>How do I create and manage a team?</h3>
<p>When you accept the competition rules, a team will be created for you. You can invite others to your team, accept a merger with another team, and update basic information like team name by going to the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/team"" target=""_blank"">Team</a> page.</p>
<p>We've heard from many Kagglers that teaming up is the best way to learn new skills AND have fun. If you don't have a teammate already, consider asking if anyone wants to team up in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">discussion forum</a>.</p>
<h3>What are Notebooks?</h3>
<p>Kaggle Notebooks is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Notebooks support scripts in Python and R, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. You can visit the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/notebooks"" target=""_blank"">Notebooks</a> tab to view all of the publicly shared code for the Spaceship Titanic competition. For more on how to use Notebooks to learn data science, check out our <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/learn/overview"" target=""&quot;_blank"">Courses</a>!</p>
<h3>Why did my team disappear from the leaderboard?</h3>
<p>To keep with the spirit of getting-started competitions, we have implemented a two month rolling window on submissions. Once a submission is more than two months old, it will be invalidated and no longer count towards the leaderboard.</p>
<p>If your team has no submissions in the previous two months, the team will also drop from the leaderboard. This will keep the leaderboard at a manageable size, freshen it up, and prevent newcomers from getting lost in a sea of abandoned scores.</p>
<p><i>""I worked so hard to get that score! Give it back!""</i> Read more about our decision to implement a rolling leaderboard <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion/6240"" target=""_blank"">here</a>.</p>
<h3>How do I contact Support?</h3>
<p>Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team so you¡¯ll typically find that you receive a response more quickly by asking your question in the appropriate forum. (For this competition, you¡¯ll want to use the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">Spaceship Titanic discussion forum</a>).</p>
<p>Support is only able to help with issues that are being experienced by all participants. Before contacting support, please check the discussion forum for information on your problem. If you can¡¯t find it, you can post your problem in the forum so a fellow participant or a Kaggle team member can provide help. The forums are full of useful information on the data, metric, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you'll find that others will share a lot in turn!</p>
<p>If your problem persists or it seems to be effective all participants then please <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact"" target=""_blank"">contact us</a>.</p>","# Dataset Description

In this competition your task is to predict whether a passenger was transported to an alternate dimension during the *Spaceship Titanic*'s collision with the spacetime anomaly. To help you make these predictions, you're given a set of personal records recovered from the ship's damaged computer system.

# File and Data Field Descriptions #

- **train.csv** - Personal records for about two-thirds (~8700) of the passengers, to be used as training data.
    - `PassengerId` - A unique Id for each passenger. Each Id takes the form `gggg_pp` where `gggg` indicates a group the passenger is travelling with and `pp` is their number within the group. People in a group are often family members, but not always.
    - `HomePlanet` - The planet the passenger departed from, typically their planet of permanent residence.
    - `CryoSleep` - Indicates whether the passenger elected to be put into suspended animation for the duration of the voyage. Passengers in cryosleep are confined to their cabins.
    - `Cabin` - The cabin number where the passenger is staying. Takes the form `deck/num/side`, where `side` can be either `P` for *Port* or `S` for *Starboard*.
    - `Destination` - The planet the passenger will be debarking to.
    - `Age` - The age of the passenger.
    - `VIP` - Whether the passenger has paid for special VIP service during the voyage.
    - `RoomService`, `FoodCourt`, `ShoppingMall`, `Spa`, `VRDeck` - Amount the passenger has billed at each of the *Spaceship Titanic*'s many luxury amenities.
    - `Name` - The first and last names of the passenger.
    - `Transported` - Whether the passenger was transported to another dimension. This is the target, the column you are trying to predict.
- **test.csv** - Personal records for the remaining one-third (~4300) of the passengers, to be used as test data. Your task is to predict the value of `Transported` for the passengers in this set. 
- **sample_submission.csv** - A submission file in the correct format.
    - `PassengerId` - Id for each passenger in the test set.
    - `Transported` - The target. For each passenger, predict either `True` or `False`.",False,True
7517,ai-serving-grid-stability,"# Overview

 
----------------------
# Description

# Introduction

Europeans are used to a constant electricity supply and rarely experience blackouts. TransnetBW¡¯s control room is one of the places where people are working 24/7 to maintain this seemingly inexhaustible flow. The company is one of four transmission system operators in Germany and ensures the stability of our electricity grid. To do this, the same amount of electricity must be fed into the grid as is taken out at any point in time to maintain a frequency of 50 Hertz. To ensure this, TransnetBW uses automated control signals to instruct power plant operators to ramp up or down their infeed. In this challenge, we look at the secondary reserve (a primary and a tertiary version also exists) that must be active after a maximum of 5 minutes.

Until recently, every European state used power plants in their own country to maintain the system balance. This has regularly led to the following situations: In Germany, for example, a power plant is ramped up and a few kilometers further across the border another power plant is ramped down. The costs that are incurred by this inefficient use of reserves are ultimately paid by all electricity customers. Since 2022, TransnetBW has operated the PICASSO platform that coordinates the deployment of secondary reserves in real-time, ensuring optimal usage of reserves in a European domestic balancing market. This makes the PICASSO platform the world`s largest system for real-time optimization of electricity flows. It leads to macroeconomic savings of several hundred million euros per year and contributes to the decarbonization of the balancing market.

To ensure the correct functionality of the platform, information on imbalances, reserves, transmission capacities, and many other time series are constantly monitored. These monitored features sometimes behave strangely, indicating to the control room engineers that it might be necessary to take action. Algorithms that recognize anomalies early and precisely may be of great help here. That¡¯s why TransnetBW, in cooperation with Fraunhofer IEE, provides a previously unpublished data set consisting of various PICASSO platform time series to participants. 

# Challenge

This is an unsupervised learning challenge where we ask you to develop an (AI) algorithm to detect anomalies in the operation of the PICASSO platform. If this is successful, AI-based solutions could support TransnetBW and other transmission system operators in recognizing deviations from the regular operation. The challenge aims at evaluating different solution methodologies in a short period of time and establishing personal contacts between industry and AI developers. You can find a more technical problem description and the data itself in the [data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/transnetbw-test-challenge/data).

# Important Links 

- If you are not yet registered, please register on the [registration page](https://eveeno.com/anomalydetectionineuropeanenergy) (even if the challenge has already started). 
- Get an even more detailed description of the challenge and information about the final event on our [website](https://hessian.ai/industry-challenge/).
- View the [recording](https://tu-darmstadt.zoom.us/rec/play/bioWA7CnZFIXCWuUgrKvRe18gtM31VJPyIVSnBuPMvPDFfxs37OBNXjDX8uk2KHY6rknE0o5CV8jMo6J.3cr37bYPyXI3ngKK?canPlayFromShare=true&from=my_recording&continueMode=true&componentName=rec-play&originRequestUrl=https%3A%2F%2Ftu-darmstadt.zoom.us%2Frec%2Fshare%2Fpyuas5ZK8xA6v68P03OCdEr5PLyJR307We7uZWSNL17zJpFpcOqGz25yT6n_qMYS.3QusCIA4t1A74Epu%20Kenncode%3A%20a%23OS8EVq) of the kickoff presentations and the [documentation](https://app.mural.co/t/quantchengluckgmbhcokg7492/m/quantchengluckgmbhcokg7492/1699602119184/d836574a2883a4aeb50b480a128b16da5e299882?sender=u6ee4d51e9cc36f12620a7561) of the kickoff event.
- Join us at [discord](https://discord.gg/Q9ZPhTQbS3) to stay up to date and to get in contact with other participants.
- Link to Q&A session on 11.01.2024 at 4pm [here](https://tu-darmstadt.zoom-x.de/j/64122381186?pwd=TXVOMGZhUks0VlFHeDY1WXhGUnB1Zz09)
- Link to final event on 14.02.2024 at 1-3:30pm [here](https://tu-darmstadt.zoom-x.de/j/68385147444?pwd=UmpsRUw1bHI2Z0I2WU16ZUNWM0NjQT09)

----------------------
# Organizers

| <!-- -->  | <!-- -->  | <!-- -->  | <!-- -->  | <!-- -->  |
| --- | --- |--- | --- |  --- | 
| <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2F90952d5d0605b8ffc83dbdef728adfc8%2F2560px-TransnetBW_logo.svg.png?generation=1701350694668532&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""250""/> |  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Fdefd0ac717869e803d2d9425b5b3f5f0%2F22_iee_web.svg?generation=1701351066910905&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""250""/>  | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Ffa7cadb62495326b9708327bcb7a6acc%2FLogo_HoE_RGB_website.png?generation=1701351272173775&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""200""/>  | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Fb5f0e65c45a8e60c35fac55b113b99e2%2Fhessian-ai-logo.svg?generation=1701351225919954&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""200""/>  | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Fdb38cec7ff95e31dd700e1750c7a1d9e%2FAISR_Logo_hessianAI_RGB.svg?generation=1701351322411882&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""240""/>  |
| <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Ff3db40c708536c276b71e198211d1ba2%2Fhodt%20logo.svg?generation=1701351442461349&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""200""/> | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Fa387b6b9b529569841aef529d25337ff%2FHMWEVW_Logo_klein.png?generation=1701351513382303&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""200""/> | <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2F70c26a55a67f7d856ff9d246c59af88f%2FEDITH_RGB_3000px.svg?generation=1701351472710640&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""300""/> |  <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2F4c4d58f1a996f2bbfecb22f65ab07fcb%2FEN_Co-fundedbytheEU_RGB_POS.png?generation=1701351560289359&alt=media"" alt=""drawing"" width=""300""/> |  | 

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation of the challenge will consist of 2 parts:

### 1. Detection Score
We use the F-beta-score with a beta value set to 1.75 as the error metric.?You can find more information about the F-beta-score [here](https://medium.com/@douglaspsteen/beyond-the-f-1-score-a-look-at-the-f-beta-score-3743ac2ef6e3). You can view the score of your submission based on the error metric on the leaderboard at any time. Here you can also see how well your algorithm performs compared to other teams. It is important to note that the leaderboard does not show the final score as it is only determined based on around 37% of the test data. We will use the other 63% of the test data only at the end of the challenge for a final evaluation.

### 2. Best Complete Package
We are not only interested in the pure error score but also in your ideas. Participants are invited to present their solutions at the final event. There we will crown the winner for the best overall solution approach. The price for this category will be awarded by the participants themselves as a community decision.

----------------------
# Example Anomalies

Since this is an unsupervised challenge and you have no information about what anomalies are and what they are not, we will describe some examples of anomaly types. Once you have developed your algorithm and discovered the first potential anomalies, you can use the descriptions to check if your algorithm detects valid anomalies. But beware, we do not list all anomalies that are present in the data here ;) 

Note that you can find additional information on example anomalies in the [recording of the kick-off](https://tu-darmstadt.zoom.us/rec/play/bioWA7CnZFIXCWuUgrKvRe18gtM31VJPyIVSnBuPMvPDFfxs37OBNXjDX8uk2KHY6rknE0o5CV8jMo6J.3cr37bYPyXI3ngKK?canPlayFromShare=true&from=my_recording&continueMode=true&componentName=rec-play&originRequestUrl=https%3A%2F%2Ftu-darmstadt.zoom.us%2Frec%2Fshare%2Fpyuas5ZK8xA6v68P03OCdEr5PLyJR307We7uZWSNL17zJpFpcOqGz25yT6n_qMYS.3QusCIA4t1A74Epu%20Kenncode%3A%20a%23OS8EVq) around minute 14:00 and 23:00.

# Example Anomaly 1

The values of `aFRRrequest` and `aFRRactivation`usually follow a time-delayed relationship. In this example, you can see that the values are even partially asynchronous.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Ff853ce426a65698240c155c19cd5049a%2Fexample_anomaly1.png?generation=1702454220906675&alt=media)

# Example Anomaly 2

Due to transmission errors, freezes of signals can occur in some of the features.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Fb7e732ddced53655398d85335376db9d%2Fexample_anomaly2.png?generation=1702454513763275&alt=media)

# Example Anomaly 3

Anomalies can also occur in the other variables, as can be seen here for `FRCE` and `LFCInput`. Yet the relationship of these two variables is unknown.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1811734%2Fe32af38e1185d1e46ca70dbc7e72ed6e%2Fexample_anomaly3.png?generation=1702454739170118&alt=media)
----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

## Participate as a Team or Individual?
It's up to you ?? During our Kick-Off event, we will provide the possibility to create teams with other participants (if you don't have a team yet). At our Discord server, you can also search for buddies to team up with.

## I Have Trouble Understanding the Data
Please see the detailed description of the data in the Data tab. If that is not sufficient, please reach out to us via Discord.

## Is the Leaderboard Showing the Final Score?
No. We don't want you to overfit your solution to the leaderboard score. The leaderboard score which you see until the end of the challenge, is only determined based on 37% of the data. We will use the other 63% of the data only at the end of the challenge for a final evaluation.

## I am a Student. Can I Somehow Get Credit Points for this?
In some studies, there is an optional module like ""research and development"". Sometimes those modules provide the possibility to work on interesting research projects. You can contact a Prof. at your uni to set this up. You can also contact us for any help or questions on this.

## I am new to this Topic. Any hints on where to start?
Anomaly Detection, also known as Outlier Detection or Novelty Detection, is a vital research field. There are plenty of books and surveys on this topic from renowned researchers available. As a starting point, you can read the following publications.

## I am Hyped on the Topic and want to Continue Working on It
Great. That was one of our goals. Just reach out to us and we will try to find a way :)
----------------------
# Further Readings


###Foundations:
- V. Chandola, A. Banerjee, and V. Kumar, ""Anomaly detection,"" ACM Comput. Surv., vol. 41, no. 3, pp. 1¨C58, 2009, https://doi.org/10.1145/1541880.1541882.
- C. C. Aggarwal, Outlier Analysis. Cham: Springer International Publishing, 2017. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-47578-3
- K. G. Mehrotra, C. K. Mohan, and H. Huang, Anomaly Detection Principles and Algorithms. Cham: Springer International Publishing, 2017. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-67526-8
- D. Samariya and A. Thakkar, ""A Comprehensive Survey of Anomaly Detection Algorithms,"" Ann. Data. Sci., vol. 10, no. 3, pp. 829¨C850, 2023. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40745-021-00362-9.

###AI/ML:
- C. C. Aggarwal, Neural Networks and Deep Learning. Cham: Springer International Publishing, 2018. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-94463-0
- Z. Z. Darban, G. I. Webb, S. Pan, C. C. Aggarwal, and M. Salehi, ""Deep Learning for Time Series Anomaly Detection: A Survey,"" Nov. 2022. https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.05244
