7544,ristek-data-challenge-s2-e1,"# Overview

Selamat datang di Ristek Data Challenge Season 2! Untuk mengasah kemampuan pengolahan data mahasiswa, kami akan secara rutin mengadakan sebuah kompetisi yang memiliki berbagai objektif yang berbeda!
----------------------
# Description

[Sumber gambar](https://news.mynavi.jp/kikaku/20190802-870634/)
<img src=""https://www.getaroundjapan.jp/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/tenki-rain.png"" />

Dek Esa, yang sedang dalam suasana liburan yang tenang, terhanyut dalam kesedihan karena kesadaran bahwa [Arkavidia](https://www.instagram.com/arkavidia/), lomba yang begitu dicintainya, tidak akan hadir lagi tahun depan. Namun, semangatnya untuk mengulang kembali momen indah dalam lomba tersebut begitu besar.

Dek Esa ingin mereplikasi pengalaman lomba Arkavidia tahun ini, namun menghadapi kendala yang membuatnya kesusahan. Di tengah kebingungan itu, Dek Esa tidak sendirian. Dia meminta bantuan dari ""kamu"" untuk menelusuri data yang serupa, membuka kembali lembaran-lembaran kenangan yang berharga.

Untuk membantu ""kamu"" dalam memahami bentuk data yang besar dan kompleks, pendahulu dari Dek Esa meninggalkan [sebuah prasasti kuno](https://www.overleaf.com/read/mxdpbnntvmjy#ad7d3a). Dek Esa berharap setelah kompetisi berakhir, kamu bersedia untuk menjelaskan solusimu kepada orang banyak melalui [bagian diskusi](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ristek-data-challenge-s2-e1/discussion/).
----------------------
# Evaluasi Leaderboard

## Penilaian
Setiap submisi akan dinilai menggunakan metrik **Mean Squared Error** dengan rumus matematis

$$
\text{MSE}=\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}(Y_i-\hat{Y_i})^2
$$

dengan `Y_i` adalah nilai asli dan `Y_i hat` adalah nilai prediksi. Alasan dibalik penggunaan metrik ini adalah untuk memberikan penalisasi lebih terhadap prediksi yang memiliki error tinggi.


## File submisi
File submisi adalah prediksi dari **curah hujan harian** (`rain_sum (mm)`) setiap daerah (bukan dari Indonesia doang, entah kenapa *Overview* nggak bisa diedit) yang ada dari rentang waktu 1 Januari 2023 hingga 22 Desember 2023. Setiap kota memiliki jumlah 356 row yang diprediksi sehingga total row yang perlu diprediksi adalah 356*8=2848. Urutan `id` terurut sesuai dengan urutan tanggal, disusul dengan urutan daerah (bisa saja nama kota, bisa saja nama prefektur atau negara bagian) berikut: `Temanggung`, `Riau`, `Bandung`, `Makassar`, `Hokkaido`, `Dublin`, `Rio`, `Florida`. Berikut adalah contoh file submisi csv yang diterima:

    id,rain_sum (mm)
    0,0
    1,0
    2,0
    ...
    2847,0

Berdasarkan contoh di atas, maka `id` 0 hingga 355 merupakan prediksi kabupaten `Temanggung` dengan waktu yang terurut, disusul dengan `Riau` dari `id` 356 hingga 711, hingga seterusnya.
----------------------
# Disclaimer

Task ini memang nggak layak disebut sebagai *time series forecasting*, kalau mau mengutip dari hal yang pernah ditulis pada kompetisi [Datathon](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ristek-datathon-2023/rules), penjelasannya sebagai berikut.

#### a. Data yang diperbolehkan
Pada *forecasting*, terdapat dua jenis *time series* yang dapat digunakan, yaitu **kovariat lampau** dan **kovariat masa depan**. Kovariat lampau adalah data yang nilainya diketahui saat waktu prediksi, sedangkan kovariat masa depan adalah data di masa depan yang nilainya sudah diketahui. Berikut adalah contoh dari kedua jenis kovariat tersebut:
- **Kovariat lampau**: jumlah kendaraan yang melewati jalan tersebut pada hari sebelumnya.
- **Kovariat masa depan**: hari-hari istimewa, kita sudah mengetahui data ini pada waktu prediksi sebab sudah ada pola yang dapat ditangkap dalam menentukan kasus-kasus spesial seperti ini.

Semua data yang nilainya sudah diketahui pada waktu prediksi pasti masuk ke salah satu dari kovariat lampau atau masa depan. Oleh karena itu, selama masuk akal untuk menganggap data yang ditambahkan sudah diketahui saat waktu prediksi, tidak perlu khawatir apakah data tersebut termasuk ke dalam kovariat lampau atau masa depan.

#### b. Data yang tidak diperbolehkan (kalau di kompetisi ini boleh)
Segala bentuk kejadian di masa depan seperti data kecelakaan dan bencana alam yang tidak dapat diprediksi tidak boleh digunakan. Menggunakan data rerata kecepatan tambahan untuk meningkatkan prediksi model juga dilarang dengan keras dalam kompetisi ini. Untuk mengeneralisasi kedua contoh ini, semua data yang tidak mungkin diketahui saat waktu prediksi, dengan kata lain, tidak termasuk ke dalam kovariat lampau atau masa depan tidak boleh digunakan.

Namun karena memang fokus lomba ini adalah di bagian *feature engineering*, jadi tolong anggap saja ini kompetisi *time series* tanpa harus khawatir melanggar aturan dari *time series* itu sendiri ??.
----------------------
# Credits

* Problem ini terinspirasi dari task Datavidia 8.0, yaitu [weather forecasting](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/weather-forecasting-datavidia/).","# Dataset Description

## Deskripsi file
Berikut adalah struktur sederhana dari dataset kompetisi ini.
```
©À©¤©¤ train
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ daily
©¦   ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ daerah_daily.csv
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ hourly
©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ daerah_hourly.csv
©¸©¤©¤ test
    ©À©¤©¤ daily
    ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ daerah_daily.csv
    ©¸©¤©¤ hourly
        ©¸©¤©¤ daerah_hourly.csv
```

Terdapat dua buah data, yaitu `train` dan `test`. Pada setiap data akan mengandung data harian dan data tiap jam yang dapat diproses lebih lanjut melalui rekayasa fitur untuk meningkatkan daya tangkap model terhadap data.

## Deskripsi fitur
### Fitur harian
- `temperature_2m_max`, `temperature_2m_min`: Suhu udara harian maksimum dan minimum pada 2 meter di atas tanah.
- `apparent_temperature_max`, `apparent_temperature_min`: Suhu nyata harian maksimum dan minimum.
- `rain_sum`: Jumlah total hujan harian.
- `sunrise`, `sunset`: Waktu terbit dan terbenam matahari.
- `wind_speed_10m_max`, `wind_gusts_10m_max`: Kecepatan angin maksimum dan hembusan maksimum dalam sehari.
- `wind_direction_10m_dominant`: Arah angin dominan.
- `shortwave_radiation_sum`: Jumlah radiasi surya pada hari tertentu dalam Megajoules.
- `et0_fao_evapotranspiration`: Jumlah harian ET? Evapotranspirasi Referensi dari lapangan rumput yang terairi dengan baik.

### Fitur tiap jam
- `temperature_2m`: Suhu udara pada 2 meter di atas tanah.
- `relative_humidity_2m`: Kelembaban relatif pada 2 meter di atas tanah.
- `dew_point_2m`: Suhu titik embun pada 2 meter di atas tanah.
- `apparent_temperature`: Suhu nyata adalah suhu yang dirasakan seperti menggabungkan faktor angin dingin, kelembaban relatif, dan radiasi matahari.
- `pressure_msl`, `surface_pressure`: Tekanan udara atmosfer dikurangi ke tingkat laut rata-rata (msl) atau tekanan di permukaan. Biasanya, tekanan pada tingkat laut rata-rata digunakan dalam meteorologi. Tekanan permukaan menurun dengan meningkatnya ketinggian.
- `cloud_cover`: Total penutupan awan sebagai fraksi area.
- `cloud_cover_low`: Awan rendah dan kabut hingga ketinggian 2 km.
- `cloud_cover_mid`: Awan tingkat menengah dari 2 hingga 6 km ketinggian.
- `cloud_cover_high`: Awan tingkat tinggi dari ketinggian 6 km.
- `shortwave_radiation`: Radiasi surya gelombang pendek sebagai rata-rata dari jam sebelumnya. Ini sama dengan total iradiasi horizontal global.
- `direct_normal_irradiance`: Radiasi surya langsung sebagai rata-rata dari jam sebelumnya pada bidang horizontal dan bidang normal (tegak lurus dengan matahari).
- `diffuse_radiation`: Radiasi surya difus sebagai rata-rata dari jam sebelumnya.
- `wind_speed_10m`, `wind_speed_100m`: Kecepatan angin pada 10 atau 100 meter di atas tanah. Kecepatan angin pada 10 meter adalah level standar.
- `wind_direction_10m`, `wind_direction_100m`: Arah angin pada 10 atau 100 meter di atas tanah.
- `wind_gusts_10m`: Hembusan angin pada 10 meter di atas tanah dari jam yang ditunjukkan. Hembusan angin didefinisikan sebagai hembusan angin maksimum dari jam sebelumnya.
- `et0_fao_evapotranspiration`: ET? Evapotranspirasi Referensi dari lapangan rumput yang terairi dengan baik. Berdasarkan persamaan FAO-56 Penman-Monteith, ET? dihitung dari suhu, kecepatan angin, kelembaban, dan radiasi matahari. Air tanah tidak terbatas diasumsikan. ET? umumnya digunakan untuk memperkirakan irigasi yang diperlukan untuk tanaman.
- `vapour_pressure_deficit`: Defisit Tekanan Uap (VPD) dalam kilopascal (kPa). Untuk VPD tinggi (>1,6), transpirasi air tanaman meningkat. Untuk VPD rendah (<0,4), transpirasi berkurang.
- `soil_temperature_0_to_255cm`: Suhu rata-rata dari berbagai tingkat tanah di bawah tanah.
- `soil_moisture_0_to_255cm`: Kandungan air tanah rata-rata sebagai rasio pencampuran volumetrik pada kedalaman 0-7, 7-28, 28-100, dan 100-255 cm.",False,True
7545,garbage-guru-challenge,"# Overview

Calling all aspiring data scientists, machine learning enthusiasts, and sustainability champions! Dive into the **Garbage Guru Challenge**, a Kaggle competition designed to level up your AI skills while tackling the critical issue of waste management.
----------------------
# Description

**The Playground, Not the Pressure Cooker**: This competition prioritizes **learning and experimentation** over cutthroat competition. We're here to provide a supportive environment where you can:

- **Sharpen your AI skills**: Hone your expertise in image classification, transfer learning, and model interpretability.
- **Get hands-on with real-world data**: Train your models on a massive dataset of diverse garbage images, reflecting the complexities of real-world waste sorting.
- **Test and iterate**: Experiment with different approaches, analyze your results, and refine your models without the pressure of a high-stakes leaderboard.

----------------------
# Mission

Train an AI model to accurately classify different types of garbage from images. It's like playing a detective game, but with pixels instead of clues! ????
----------------------
# Challenge

Craft an AI model that can:

- **Ace the Sorting Exam**: Pinpoint the correct category for each garbage item with impressive precision and recall.
- **Handle the Messy Real World**: Deal with diverse image variations like different lighting, angles, and cluttered backgrounds.
- **No More Peek-a-Boo Trash**: Be robust to occlusions and partial visibility of waste items.
- **Think Beyond the Training Ground**: Generalize well to unseen data and real-world scenarios, becoming a true waste-sorting master.
----------------------
# Rewards

- **Knowledge is Power**: Gain valuable experience and insights into AI for environmental applications.
- **Sharpen Your Portfolio**: Showcase your skills and expertise to potential employers and collaborators.
- **Contribute to a Greener Future**: Your learnings can pave the way for smarter waste sorting systems, promoting a more sustainable world.
----------------------
# Evaluation

- **Macro F1 score**: The heart of the matter! We'll measure how often your model correctly classifies each type of garbage using Macro F1-Score. See https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html.
- **Robustness**: Can your model handle real-world challenges like blurry images and mixed waste streams? We'll test its adaptability.
- **Interpretability**: Bonus points for models that explain their reasoning, helping us understand and improve their decision-making.

----------------------
# Bonus Quests 

- **Transfer Learning Mastery**: Utilize pre-trained image classification models and fine-tune them for garbage sorting.
- **Data Augmentation Guru**: Unleash the power of data augmentation techniques to boost your model'sgeneralizability.
- **Explainable AI**: Develop models that reveal their reasoning behind each classification, fostering trust and understanding.
----------------------
# Submission

Submission File
For each id in the test set, you must predict a type of class (or label). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
id,label
a762df180,0
24c5cf439,0
7581e896d,0
eb4b03b29,0
etc.
```
----------------------
# Summary

Let's turn waste into a springboard for learning and innovation! ??

Remember, this competition is designed to be a supportive and educational experience. We're here to cheer you on as you level up your AI skills and contribute to a more sustainable future!","# Dataset Description

Collection of images of garbages grouped into 10 classes (metal, glass, biological, paper, battery, trash, cardboard, shoes, clothes, and plastic). This data is a subset of a [Garbage dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/sumn2u/garbage-classification-v2). The number of files in respective classes is as follows: 

- **Metal**: 1000
- **Glass**: 1000
- **Biological**: 1000
- **Paper**: 1000
- **Battery**: 1000
- **Trash**: 1000
- **Cardboard**: 1000
- **Shoes**: 1000
- **Clothes**: 1000
- **Plastic**: 1000",False,True
7546,mlo2024mlact,"# Overview

The mesmerizing oceans, home to a diverse array of marine life, face a growing threat¡ªplastic pollution. To address this critical issue, the competition will be about distinguishing between different species of jellyfish and plastic pollution.

In response to this urgent environmental call, we present a competition that transcends data science alone. This challenge goes beyond algorithms¡ªit is a call to action, an opportunity to raise awareness about the peril faced by our marine companions, the sea turtles. The competition centers on distinguishing between different species of jellyfish and plastic pollution, mimicking the real-world challenge sea turtles encounter as they forage for food as they navigate their ocean home.

**Goal**
Develop a classification model to distinguish between different species of jellyfish and plastic pollution in ocean imagery, simulating the challenge faced by sea turtles.


----------------------
# Message from MLAct team

Let this competition be a beacon, illuminating the need for awareness and collective action. Each algorithm developed is not just a line of code; it is a testament to our commitment to safeguarding the homes of sea turtles and preserving the delicate balance of marine ecosystems.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11123146%2F3462e2337def899114513831502c009b%2Fturtle.png?generation=1703628354932551&alt=media)

----------------------
# Evaluation

In the pursuit of developing a robust model to distinguish between the various classes of jellyfish and plastic pollution, we recognize the varying degrees of impact associated with different types of misclassifications. To provide a more nuanced evaluation, the following penalty scheme will be applied:

   **Correct Classification**
        Upon making the correct class prediction, no penalty will be applied.

   **Classifying Plastic Pollution as any Class of Jellyfish:**
        This error will incur a heavy penalty. Misclassifying plastic pollution as jellyfish has more significant consequences in the context of our mission to raise awareness about the impact of plastic on marine ecosystems.

   **Misclassifying any Class of Jellyfish as Plastic Pollution:**
        This error will incur a moderate penalty. This will emphasize the need to maintain a balanced approach in the classification task.

   **Confusing Different Classes of Jellyfish:**
        Errors within the spectrum of jellyfish classes will incur a minor penalty. While accuracy in distinguishing between jellyfish types is valued, our primary focus remains on minimizing the misclassification of plastic pollution.



This penalty structure aims to align the competition evaluation with the broader goal of raising awareness about the dangers of plastic pollution. It reflects the real-world consequences associated with misclassifying instances of plastic pollution, which is a critical aspect of the challenges faced by marine life.
----------------------
# Submission Format

Participants are required to submit their predictions on the images of the test set in a standardized format to facilitate the evaluation process. The submission file should be a CSV (Comma-Separated Values) file containing two columns:
```python

ImageID, PredictedClass
img0001, ""barrel_jellyfish""
img0002, ""compass_jellyfish""
img0003, ""plastic_pollution""
...

```

**ImageID being the name of the image file in the provided test set (type string) and PredictedClass being the predicted name of the class (provided in the train folder) for the respective image by your model (type string).** 

----------------------
# Prizes

The top two (2) participants will receive awesome prizes.","# Dataset Description

In order to raise awareness about the potential dangers posed to sea turtles, a dataset has been compiled. This dataset features images (244x244) of six (6) classes in total : 
* Five (5) classes of jellyfish
* One (1) class depicting plastic pollution

Participants are challenged to optimize their models for the precise classification of images into these six distinct classes **(not binary classification)**.


## Dataset
The dataset is devided in two folders:

*   **train** - containing six (6) sub-folders, one for each class, each sub-folder's name is the respective class' label.
*   **test** - containing 480 images to be used for the submission file.",False,True
7547,lmsys-chatbot-arena,"# Overview

This competition challenges you to predict which responses users will prefer in a head-to-head battle between chatbots powered by large language models (LLMs). You'll be given a dataset of conversations from the [Chatbot Arena](https://chat.lmsys.org/), where different LLMs generate answers to user prompts. By developing a winning machine learning model, you'll help improve how chatbots interact with humans and ensure they better align with human preferences.
----------------------
# Description

Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly entering our lives, but ensuring their responses resonate with users is critical for successful interaction. This competition presents a unique opportunity to tackle this challenge with real-world data and help us bridge the gap between LLM capability and human preference.

We utilized a large dataset collected from Chatbot Arena, where users chat with two anonymous LLMs and choose the answer they prefer. Your task in this competition is to predict which response a user will prefer in these head-to-head battles.

This challenge aligns with the concept of ""reward models"" or ""preference models"" in reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF). Previous research has identified limitations in directly prompting an existing LLM for preference predictions. These limitations often stem from biases such as favoring responses presented first (position bias), being overly verbose (verbosity bias), or exhibiting self-promotion (self-enhancement bias).

We encourage you to explore various machine-learning techniques to build a model that can effectively predict user preferences. Your work will be instrumental in developing LLMs that can tailor responses to individual user preferences, ultimately leading to more user-friendly and widely accepted AI-powered conversation systems.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the [log loss](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/log-loss?scriptVersionId=151169978) between the predicted probabilities and the ground truth values (with ""eps=auto""). 


## Submission File
For each id in the test set, you must predict the probability for each target class. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

     id,winner_model_a,winner_model_b,winner_tie
     136060,0.33,0,33,0.33
     211333,0.33,0,33,0.33
     1233961,0.33,0,33,0.33
     etc
----------------------
# Timeline

* **May 2, 2024** - Start Date. 
* **July 29, 2024** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete. 
* **July 29, 2024** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams. 
* **August 5, 2024** - Final Submission Deadline. 

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $25,000
* 2nd Place - $20,000
* 3rd Place - $20,000
* 4th Place - $20,000
* 5th Place - $15,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`
- Submission runtimes have been slightly obfuscated. If you repeat the exact same submission you will see up to 15 minutes of variance in the time before you receive your score.

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.","# Dataset Description

The competition dataset consists of user interactions from the [ChatBot Arena](https://chat.lmsys.org/). In each user interaction a judge provides one or more prompts to two different large language models, and then indicates which of the models gave the more satisfactory response. The goal of the competition is to predict the preferences of the judges and determine the likelihood that a given prompt/response pair is selected as the winner.

Please note that this is a [Code Competition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lmsys-chatbot-arena/overview/code-requirements). When your submission is scored, this example test data will be replaced with the full test set. There are 55K rows in the training data, and you can expect roughly 25,000 rows in the test set.

##Files

**train.csv** 

*   `id` - A unique identifier for the row.
*   `model_[a/b]` - The identity of model_[a/b]. Included in train.csv but not test.csv.
*   `prompt` - The prompt that was given as an input (to both models).
*   `response_[a/b]` - The response from model_[a/b] to the given prompt.
*   `winner_model_[a/b/tie]` - Binary columns marking the judge's selection. The ground truth target column.

**test.csv** 

*   `id`
*   `prompt`
*   `response_[a/b]`

**sample_submission.csv** A submission file in the correct format.

*   `id`
*   `winner_model_[a/b/tie]` - This is what is predicted from the test set.

*Note: the dataset for this competition contains text that may be considered profane, vulgar, or offensive.*",True,True
7548,pii-detection-removal-from-educational-data,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to develop a model that detects personally identifiable information (PII) in student writing. Your efforts to automate the detection and removal of PII from educational data will lower the cost of releasing educational datasets. This will support learning science research and the development of educational tools.

Reliable automated techniques could allow researchers and industry to tap into the potential that large public educational datasets offer to support the development of effective tools and interventions for supporting teachers and students.
----------------------
# Description



In today¡¯s era of abundant educational data from sources such as ed tech, online learning, and research, widespread PII is a key challenge. PII¡¯s presence is a barrier to analyze and create open datasets that advance education because releasing the data publicly puts students at risk. To reduce these risks, it¡¯s crucial to screen and cleanse educational data for PII before public release, which data science could streamline.

Manually reviewing the entire dataset for PII is currently the most reliable screening method, but this results in significant costs and restricts the scalability of educational datasets. While techniques for automatic PII detection that rely on named entity recognition (NER) exist, these work best for PII that share common formatting such as emails and phone numbers. PII detection systems struggle to correctly label names and distinguish between names that are sensitive (e.g., a student's name) and those that are not (e.g., a cited author).

Competition host Vanderbilt University is a private research university in Nashville, Tennessee. It offers 70 undergraduate majors and a full range of graduate and professional degrees across 10 schools and colleges, all on a beautiful campus with state-of-the-art laboratories. Vanderbilt is optimized to inspire and nurture cross-disciplinary research that fosters groundbreaking discoveries.

For this competition, Vanderbilt has partnered with The Learning Agency Lab, an Arizona-based independent nonprofit focused on developing the science of learning-based tools and programs for the social good.

Your work in creating reliable automated techniques to detect PII will lead to more high-quality public educational datasets. Researchers can then tap into the potential of this previously unavailable data to develop effective tools and interventions that benefit both teachers and students.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on micro [\\(F_{\beta}\\)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score), which is a classification metric that assigns value to recall and precision. The value of \\(\beta\\) is set to 5, which means that recall is weighted 5 times more heavily than precision.

## Submission File
For each `document` in the test set, you must predict which `token` values have a positive PII `label`. You should only include predictions of positive PII `label` values. Outside labels `O` **should not** be included. Each row in the submission should correspond to a single label found at a unique `document-token` pair. Additionally, the evaluation metric requires a `row_id` with an enumeration of predicted labels.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    row_id,document,token,label
    0,7,9,B-NAME_STUDENT
    1,7,10,I-NAME_STUDENT
    2,10,0,B-NAME_STUDENT
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **January 17, 2024** - Start Date. 
* **April 16, 2024** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete. 
* **April 16, 2024** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams. 
* **April 23, 2024** - Final Submission Deadline. 

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

**Leaderboard Prizes**
* 1st Place - $13,000
* 2nd Place - $10,000
* 3rd Place - $5,000

**Efficiency Prizes**
* 1st Place - $15,000
* 2nd Place - $12,000
* 3rd Place - $5,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met: 
- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time 
- Internet access disabled 
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models 
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv` 

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.
----------------------
# Efficiency Prize Evaluation

## Efficiency Prize

We are hosting a second track that focuses on model efficiency, because highly accurate models are often computationally heavy. Such models have a stronger carbon footprint and frequently prove difficult to utilize in real-world educational contexts. We hope to use these models to help educational organizations that have limited computational capabilities.

For the Efficiency Prize, we will evaluate submissions on both runtime and predictive performance.

To be eligible for an Efficiency Prize, a submission:
- Must be among the submissions selected by a team for the Leaderboard Prize, or else among those submissions automatically selected under the conditions described in the [**My Submissions**](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pii-detection-removal-from-educational-data/submissions) tab.
- Must be ranked on the Private Leaderboard higher than the `sample_submission.csv` benchmark.
- Must not have a GPU enabled. **The Efficiency Prize is CPU Only.**

All submissions meeting these conditions will be considered for the Efficiency Prize. A submission may be eligible for both the Leaderboard Prize and the Efficiency Prize.

An Efficiency Prize will be awarded to eligible submissions according to how they are ranked by the following evaluation metric on the private test data. See the [**Prizes**](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pii-detection-removal-from-educational-data/overview/prizes) tab for the prize awarded to each rank. More details may be posted via discussion forum updates.

## Efficiency Score

We compute a submission's **efficiency score** by:
\\[ \text{Efficiency} = \frac{F_5} { \text{Benchmark} - \max F_5 } + \frac{ \text{RuntimeSeconds} }{ 32400 } \\]

where \\(F_5\\) is the submission's score on the [main competition metric](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pii-detection-removal-from-educational-data/overview/evaluation), \\(\text{Benchmark}\\) is the score of the benchmark `sample_submission.csv`, \\( \max F_5 \\) is the maximum \\( F_5 \\) score of all submissions on the Private Leaderboard, and \\(\text{RuntimeSeconds}\\) is the number of seconds it takes for the submission to be evaluated. The objective is to minimize the efficiency score.

During the training period of the competition, you may see a leaderboard for the *public* test data in the following notebook, updated daily: **[Efficiency
Leaderboard](https://www.kaggle.com/ryanholbrook/pii-detection-efficiency-lb)**. After the competition ends, we will update this leaderboard with efficiency scores on the *private* data. During the training period, this leaderboard will show only the rank of each team, but not the complete score.
----------------------
# Acknowledgements

Vanderbilt University would like to thank The National AI Institute for Adult Learning and Online Education (AI-ALOE) for their support in making this work possible.","# Dataset Description

---
 **Update**

You may read more about PII Data from the following publication:
-   Holmes, L., Crossley, S. A., Sikka, H., & Morris, W. (2023). PIILO: An open-source system for personally identifiable information labeling and obfuscation.  _Information and Learning Science, 124_ (9/10), 266-284. [[link](https://zenodo.org/records/10529644 ""https://zenodo.org/records/10529644"")]

---


The competition dataset comprises approximately 22,000 essays written by students enrolled in a massively open online course. All of the essays were written in response to a single assignment prompt, which asked students to apply course material to a real-world problem. The goal of the competition is to annotate personally identifiable information (PII) found within the essays. 

In order to protect student privacy, the original PII in the dataset has been replaced by surrogate identifiers of the same type using a partially automated process. A majority of the essays are reserved for the test set (70%), so competitors are encouraged to use external datasets that are publicly available to bolster the training data.

## PII Types
The competition asks competitors to assign labels to the following seven types of PII:
 - `NAME_STUDENT` - The full or partial name of a student that is not necessarily the author of the essay. This excludes instructors, authors, and other person names.
 - `EMAIL` - A student¡¯s email address.
 - `USERNAME` - A student's username on any platform.
 - `ID_NUM` - A number or sequence of characters that could be used to identify a student, such as a student ID or a social security number.
 - `PHONE_NUM` - A phone number associated with a student.
 - `URL_PERSONAL` - A URL that might be used to identify a student.
 - `STREET_ADDRESS` - A full or partial street address that is associated with the student, such as their home address. 

## File and Field Information

The data is presented in JSON format, which includes a document identifier, the full text of the essay, a list of tokens, information about whitespace, and token annotations. The documents were tokenized using the SpaCy English tokenizer.

Token labels are presented in BIO (Beginning, Inner, Outer) format. The PII type is prefixed with ¡°B-¡± when it is the beginning of an entity. If the token is a continuation of an entity, it is prefixed with ¡°I-¡±. Tokens that are not PII are labeled ¡°O¡±.

*   **{test|train}.json** - the test and training data; the test data given on this page is for illustrative purposes only, and will be replaced during Code rerun with a hidden test set.
 - (int): the index of the essay
    - `document` (int): an integer ID of the essay
    - `full_text` (string): a UTF-8 representation of the essay
    - `tokens` (list)
      - (string): a string representation of each token
    - `trailing_whitespace` (list)
      - (bool): a boolean value indicating whether each token is followed by whitespace.
    - `labels` (list) [training data only]
      - (string): a token label in BIO format


*   **sample_submission.csv** - An example of the correct submission format. See the [Submission File section](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pii-detection-removal-from-educational-data/overview/evaluation) of the Overview page for details.",True,True
7549,bgu-recommendation-systems-click-through-rate,"# Description

In this challenge, we provide a data set from our large-scale recommendation system, which serves billions of recommendations per day. Each row represents an event of recommendation and contains information about the user, the context, and the recommended item. The rows are labeled as clicked/ not clicked. Your task is to build a model that predicts if a user will click on an item.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Area Under the Curve (AUC)](https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5). AUC - ROC curve is a performance measurement for the classification problem at various threshold settings. ROC is a probability curve and AUC represents the degree or measure of separability. It tells how much the model is capable of distinguishing between classes. Higher the AUC, the better the model is at predicting 0s as 0s and 1s as 1s.


## Submission Format
For every index in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Predicted probability between [0,1]. The file should contain a header and have the following format: Id, Predicted 1,0.3 2,0.8 3,0.4 4,0.7 etc.","# Dataset Description

