7557,electrance-predicting-superconductivity-peaks,"# Overview

Superconductivity is a phenomenon observed in certain materials at extremely low temperatures where electrical resistance drops to zero. This property has transformative implications for various technological applications, such as energy transmission, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and high-speed transportation. The critical temperature is the temperature at which a material transitions from a normal to a superconducting state. Predicting this critical temperature is crucial for identifying materials with desirable superconducting properties. Accurate predictions facilitate the discovery of new materials and contribute to the development of more efficient and practical superconductors for real-world applications.

----------------------
# Description

Problem Statement:
Predict the critical temperature for the materials using the given parameters.

Submit a CSV file, with the predicted values for each test case (using the 'Id' column to denote the case).


----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of your models will be evaluated using the RMSE between your predictions and the actual critical temperatures. The closer your predictions align with reality, the higher your chances of claiming victory.","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of a collection of superconductors, each characterized by a set of parameters. These parameters include atomic mass, entropy atomic mass, the number of elements, atomic radius, density, electron affinity, fusion heat, thermal conductivity, and valence. Participants will use this information to train their machine learning models to find the critical temperature of superconductors.",False,True
7558,prompt-engineering-math,"# Overview

This competition is about prompt engineering.

A simple way to participate is to start with the [baseline](https://www.kaggle.com/code/dremovd/baseline-gpt) and change the prompt text only.
However, you can change any part of the generation pipeline. You only need to be sure it's reproducible (it depends only on public libraries and models).

A separate test set with generation reproduced on my side will judge the final position on the leaderboard (for the top 10 participants).

**Goal**: You'll need to build the best prompt to help LLM solve a simple math task.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric is **accuracy**. 
The **best solution** is the one that will have **more right answers**. 

The secondary metric is the **average cost of work per task**. This metric will judge any ties between solutions with the same score.

Two separate tracks for each of the two LLMs: OpenAI and Anthropic.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **test_with_translation.csv** - the test set with an extra column of [automatic translation](https://www.kaggle.com/dremovd/translation)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Answer format

Each test answer should be a number¡ªeither an integer or finite decimal float with ""."" as a separator.
Correct format examples:
4231
-12
0.75
Incorrect format examples:
4 2 3 1
-12.0
0,2

## Source

All the problems are from [the Open problem bank for the Unified State Exam (Mathematics)](https://base.mathege.ru/).

## See [baseline](https://www.kaggle.com/code/dremovd/baseline-gpt)",False,True
