7567,ba2023-regression,"# Overview

Have fun!
----------------------
# Description

Within the context of Eleme delivery service, at every moment the command platform of ¡°Smart Logistics¡± sends orders from customers to delivery-men for instant delivery. The decisions being made by the delivery-men are mainly two-fold: (i) pick-up from a store, and delivery to a customer. At any specific moment, a delivery-man may receive new orders assigned to him, while he has some unfinished orders which were previously assigned. In this situation, he needs to decide what to do next (to pick up a new order or to deliver an old one), based on his order status and geographical location.

There are two main tasks for this project, each counting 5% towards your final course grade. The first task is a classification problem, in which you are asked to build a model to predict whether the delivery-man¡¯s next move is to pick up an order or to deliver an order. The second task is a regression problem, in which you are asked to build a model to predict the expected time of the delivery-man¡¯s next action. For both tasks, you will use his historical decisions and current status as features. For each task, you need to submit a CSV file in the same format as the sample to the corresponding Kaggle competition.

The submission to the classification problem should be in 0-1 format, with 1 representing the ¡±DELIVERY¡± action. The data has been preprocessed, but you should decide your own approach to the problem: Which features to use, what models to try, how to deal with outliers, etc. 

Besides submitting your prediction results to Kaggle, you are also required to submit a PDF explaining your ideas and solution approach, together with the Jupyter Notebook with the computational results. This will be MORE important than your Kaggle score to determine your grade.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is MAE-Score, see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error

Submission Format:
For every item in the dataset, submission files should contain one column called ID, with a sequence from 0 to 25467. and a column of predicted expected time. The file should have a header, ""ID"" and ""expect_time"" for the two columns, and the file should have 22468 rows in total. The submission should be in CSV format","# Dataset Description

Dataframe_train and dataframe_test are the main dataset. The difference is that for the test data set, the ¡°action type¡± (for the classification task) and ¡°expected use time¡± (for the regression task) variables are masked, and you are asked to predict them.

The data was already pre-processed. Below is some brief overview of the variables, the rest can be explored on your own.

## Variables
*   ¡¯courier_id¡¯, ¡¯wave_index¡¯, ¡¯tracking_id¡¯, ¡¯date¡¯, ¡¯group¡¯, ¡¯id¡¯ - These are the demographic information of the order and its courier. The column names are somewhat self-explanatory.

*  ¡¯courier_wave_start_lng¡¯, ¡¯courier_wave_start_lat¡¯ - These are the starting longitude and latitude of that wave of a certain courier.

*  ¡¯level¡¯, ¡¯speed¡¯, ¡¯max_load¡¯ - These are the courier information: The level of the courier, the speed of the courier and the max load of the courier.

* ¡¯weather_grade¡¯ - This is the weather condition of the order.

* ¡¯aoi_id¡¯ - The id of the Area of Interest (i.e. the delivery destination).

* ¡¯shop_id¡¯ - The id of the shop.

* ¡¯source_type¡¯, ¡¯source_tracking_id¡¯, ¡¯source_lng¡¯, ¡¯source_lat¡¯ - The information of the courier¡¯s previous action.

* ¡¯target_lng¡¯, ¡¯target_lat¡¯ - The geographical information of the target.

* ¡¯grid_distance¡¯ - The shortest traversable distance to the target provided by the GPS.

* ¡¯hour¡¯ - The hour in the day.

* ¡¯urgency¡¯ - Identifies how urgent the order is

* ¡¯action_type¡¯ - The outcome variable in the classification task. This is masked in the test set.

* ¡¯expected_use_time¡¯ - The outcome variable in the regression task. This is also masked in the test set

Finally, we want to remind you that NOT ALL VARIABLES ARE NECESSARILY NEEDED. So, you should use your own judgment to decide what to use for your model.

## Files

*   **dataframe_train.csv** - the training set
*   **dataframe_test.csv** - the test set
*   **regression_example.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **questions_project.pdf** - Q&A",False,True
7568,facebook-ad-campaign-budget-prediction,"# Overview

Facebook Ad Budget Predictor: Forecast campaign spend based on historical data. Optimize ad performance with a predictive model. #DataScience #PredictiveAnalytics #KaggleCompetition
----------------------
# Description

Participants will be provided with historical data from Facebook advertising campaigns, including information about the ad creative, targeting parameters, and performance metrics. The goal is to develop a predictive model that can accurately estimate the budget required for a new advertising campaign based on the given features.

**Data**:
The dataset will include information such as:

- Ad creative details (images, text, format).
- Targeting parameters (demographics, interests, location).
- Ad performance metrics (impressions, clicks, click-through rate, conversion rate).
Budget and spending details for previous campaigns.
- Participants will need to preprocess the data, perform feature engineering, and build a regression model to predict the budget for a new campaign.

**Prizes**:
Kaggle competitions typically offer cash prizes, Kaggle swag, and recognition on the Kaggle platform for top-performing participants.

**Benefits**:

1. **Practical Application**: Predicting campaign budgets is a real-world problem faced by digital marketers, making the competition relevant to industry needs.
2. **Data Analysis Skills**: Participants will enhance their skills in preprocessing data, feature engineering, and regression modeling.
3. **Insights for Marketers**: The competition results could provide valuable insights into the factors that most influence Facebook ad campaign budgets.
This competition aligns with the growing importance of data-driven decision-making in digital marketing and can attract participants interested in both machine learning and marketing domains.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on the accuracy of the budget predictions. The competition can use performance metrics like Mean Absolute Error (MAE) or Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) to measure how well the models predict the actual campaign budgets.","# Dataset Description

Explanation of columns:

Ad_ID: Unique identifier for each ad.
Ad_Creative: Description of the ad content (text, image, video, carousel, etc.).
Target_Demographics: Age and gender targeting criteria for the ad.
Target_Interests: Interests targeting criteria for the ad.
Location: Targeted location for the ad campaign.
Impressions: Number of times the ad was displayed.
Clicks: Number of clicks on the ad.
Conversions: Number of users who completed the desired action (e.g., made a purchase) after clicking the ad.
Budget_Spent: Amount spent on the campaign.
Please note that this is a basic example, and a more comprehensive dataset would include additional features and variability. In a real-world scenario, you would have a more extensive dataset with a larger number of ads, diverse targeting criteria, and a broader range of performance metrics.",False,True
7569,copy-of-binary,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use binary classification to predict a patient's smoking status given information about various other health indicators. Good luck!
