7616,dl-enigma-10-sust-cse-carnival-2024,"# Overview


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5328059%2F9b3067b3ff9d867fd242ccb1b6bd9634%2Fsdasdasdas.jpg?generation=1705692279083086&alt=media)

Welcome to the **Autonomous Vehicles for Bangladesh Roads Kaggle competition**!?? In this exciting challenge, participants are invited to tackle the unique and complex task of object detection in diverse driving environments across 9 districts in Bangladesh. **SUST CSE Carnival** presents a Deep Learning Event **DL Enigma 1.0** where the goal is to advance the Autonomous Driving Technology for Bangladesh where participants can build unique approaches to develop solutions that can be used for Autonomous Navigation in Self Driving Cars. **BadODD: Bangladeshi Autonomous Driving Object Detection Dataset** is a diverse dataset for Object Detection on Bangladesh Roads that the participants will explore during the competition period.



----------------------
# Description

**This Competition is open to All from any institution in Bangladesh.**

Student from the **SUST CSE 18** batch is not allowed to participate in this competition.

## **How to participate**
Anyone can join the kaggle competition for Phase 1. However, to be eligible to participate in Phase 2 and Prizes a team must fill out the Registration Form.

**Registration Link**: https://csecarnival.sust.edu.bd/dlenigma/registration
**FB Event Link**: https://fb.me/e/vepwR8e5e

## **Competition Schedule**

**Phase 1**: 
Duration: 20 Jan - 10 Feb 2024.
Participants will submit their solutions on Kaggle. The top 10 teams will be invited for Phase 2.

**Phase 2**:
Date: 18 February
Participants will have to submit an IEEE format paper and slide to present their work on-site. More details regarding the onsite presentation will be given to the eligible participants for Phase 2.

All teams will submit working Kaggle notebooks for Train and Test with appropriate instructions and documentation for reproducibility after Phase 1. Test notebook must run on any accelerator of Kaggle under the Kaggle Runtime.

## Scoring

Phase 1: (50%)
- Public: 30% mAP score
- Private: 20% mAP score

Phase 2: (50%)
 - Private Test Data: 25% mAP score
 - Judge + Presentation + Paper : 15% 
 - Inference time: 10%

*For any inquiries, please feel free to contact us via email at `sustdlenigma@gmail.com`*
----------------------
# Evaluation

For this competition, we are using mAP ( Mean Average Precision) as our evaluation metric.

The mAP is computed by first calculating the Average Precision (AP) for each class and then taking the mean across all classes. AP is determined by computing the area under the precision-recall curve, where precision is plotted against recall for various confidence thresholds.

**For this competition we are evaluating mAP50 where IoU is set to 0.5 rather than calculating the mean of different sets of IoU thresholds.**
## Submission File

You will submit a submission csv file where each row of the csv file will contain one or multiple Predicted Bounding Box for each Image. Multiple bounding boxes will be comma separated for one image id. `submission.csv` will contain 3 columns: `id`, `ImageID`, and `PredictionString_pred`.

`id`: This column will contain the row number that will start from 0. 
`ImageID`: This column will be composed of image_id which is the name of the image file without the extension(.jpg).
`PredictionString_pred`: This will contain Bounding Boxes for multiple objects for each ImageID. Each Bounding Box will be comma(`,`) separated and the format is `class_id confidence_score xmin ymin width height`. Each of these values are floating value.

If the model predicts no prediction or `NaN` for one ImageID return `0 0 0 0 0 0`. `NaN` values in `submission.csv` will result in a submission error.

**Each value in the PredictionString_pred is normalized.**

In this image x1, y1 denotes xmin, ymin.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5328059%2Fd200a198d4e72532b928fe2229c7e819%2FGroup%208%20(1).png?generation=1705755050400160&alt=media)


    id, ImageID, PredictionString_pred
    0, image1, 3.0 1.0 0.3484375 0.4222222222222222 0.11510416666666666 0.08888888888888889
    1, image2, 4.0 0.7 0.6484375 0.2222222222222222 0.21510416666666666 0.18888888888888889,10.0 0.5 0.9484375 0.1222222222222222 0.31510416666666666 0.48888888888888889
  
    etc","# Dataset Description

Dataset Paper Link: https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.10659

## **Dataset Overview:**
The dataset covers the following 9 districts in Bangladesh: Sylhet, Dhaka, Rajshahi, Mymensingh, Maowa, Chittagong, Sirajganj, Sherpur, and Khulna. This dataset contains 9825 images with 78,943 Objects with 13 different classes. It is annotated with rectangular bounding box.Participants will encounter a wide range of road types, including towns, expressways, highways, and village roads. This diversity in locations aims to challenge algorithms to perform well across various driving contexts commonly encountered on Bangladesh roads. 

## **Data Collection:**
To ensure the authenticity of the dataset, all images were collected using smartphone cameras, simulating the real-world conditions faced by autonomous vehicles. The absence of online images ensures that participants are working with a dataset representative of the actual driving scenarios in Bangladesh, promoting the development of models with practical applicability.

**Goal**: Participants will develop solutions to detect objects in various Road Conditions fo Bangladesh.

## **Classes** : 13
- auto_rickshaw
- bicycle
- bus
- car
- cart_vehicle
- construction_vehicle
- motorbike
- person
- priority_vehicle
- three_wheeler
- train
- truck
- wheelchair

## **Sample Dataset**:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5328059%2F2c69790db325e71f9bfa40fb8d3012d0%2Fimage%20(1).png?generation=1705692179963469&alt=media)

## **Dataset Format**

This dataset is in Yolov5 format. The train and test images can be found in in `dlenigma1/BadODD/images/`, and the labels for the train dataset can be found in `dlenigma1/BadODD/labels/` directory.

- Train Image Count: 5896
- Test Image Count: 1964

**Each training image has a txt file of the same name in the labels directory.**

**Test**
Test set images are in `dlenigma1/BadODD/images/test`.

The test set has no provided annotations. Your task is to learn the bounding box from the train set and predict it for the test set images.",False,True
