7625,event-based-eye-tracking-ais2024,"# Overview

## [New] IMPORTANT ANNOUNCEMENT
Please check this [news post](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/event-based-eye-tracking-ais2024/discussion/484138) for understanding the submission of code and factsheet (deadline March 25, 2024 (!!)) and challenge paper submission (deadline April 5, 2024(!!)).

## [ AIS: Vision, Graphics and AI for Streaming Workshop at CVPR 2024 ](https://ai4streaming-workshop.github.io/)

This challenge aims to develop an event-based eye-tracking system for precise tracking of rapid eye movements to produce lighter and more comfortable devices for a better user experience.
The task is to predict the eye center position (x,y) given the event camera inputs. The event recordings are captured with an Inivation DVXplorer Mini event camera. See the youtube video below using event-based eye-tracking system for playing a simple video game!


**Official Challenge Site** [https://eetchallenge.github.io/EET.github.io/](https://eetchallenge.github.io/EET.github.io/)

**Official GitHub Demo Code Repository** [https://github.com/EETChallenge/challenge_demo_code](https://github.com/EETChallenge/challenge_demo_code)

<br>

![event-based eye-tracking video game](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aRB5mDNfrHM)
----------------------
# Description

For the full coding pipeline and tutorial please refer to the GitHub repository: https://github.com/EETChallenge/challenge_demo_code

## Training Pipeline Description
The default training pipeline is very handy to use. Simply run the following command with the code in the GitHub repository:
```python
python3 train.py --config sliced_baseline.json
```
This should give a baseline result for a basic CNN+GRU model trained on voxel-grid event representation. The training progress, checkpoints and metrics are all logged with the mlflow library. See the repo for more details.

![Eye movement recorded with event camera](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18771626%2Fe78190165f02d967a7bd8bc992e5ca64%2Fevent-based-eye-movement.gif?generation=1706607242333944&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

#### scoring functions
There are two evaluation metrics for the challenge:
**p10 accuracy** and **mean euclidean distances**.

The p10 accuracy is defined as the percentage of the predictions that are within 10 pixels of the ground truths (in the downsampled 80x60 spatial space). The mean Euclidean distance is defined as the average Euclidean distance between the predictions and the ground truths. 


## Submission File
Please reference the test.py to load your model checkpoint and generate the submission csv file. The file should contain a header (already included in the script) and have the following format:

    x, y
    35.20940399169922,22.15359878540039
    34.5449104309082,25.2341251373291
    40.37540817260742,21.507827758789062
    37.30218505859375,23.70339012145996
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description
There are 13 subjects in total, each having 2-6 recording sessions. The subjects are required to perform 5 classes of activities: random, saccades, read text, smooth pursuit, and blinks. Figure 2 visualizes one real recording sample by making the raw events into event frames. The total data volume is approximately 1 GB in the compressed .h5 form. Depending on your method, you may have very different data representations and processing pipelines based on the raw events. We provide an event [voxel-grid-based](https://tonic.readthedocs.io/en/latest/auto_examples/representations/plot_tovoxelgrid.html#sphx-glr-auto-examples-representations-plot-tovoxelgrid-py) data loading pipeline in the GitHub repository. It will automatically slice the raw event recordings into subsequences. For more details please refer to the GitHub repository.

#### Labeling
The ground truth is labeled at 100Hz and consists of two parts for each label (x, y, close) with 

* labeling of the pupil center coordinates (x,y).
* a binary value 'close' indicating whether the eye blinks or not (0 for opening, 1 for closing). 

Labels (x,y, close) for the train split are provided at a frequency of 100Hz. The user is free to decide if they would like to downsample this frequency and whether to use the close label or not. In the training sample pipeline we provided, the labels are downsampled to 20Hz and we do not use the close label by default. For the test set, we will evaluate the prediction at **20Hz** only. If you would like to alter the data loading pipeline, please be aware of this downsampling.

#### Dataset splitting
We use 12 recordings for testing (test_files.txt) and the remaining recordings are for the user to train (dataset/train_files.txt ) and validate (dataset/val_files.txt) their methods. The users are free to re-divide the training and validation sets in train_files.txt and val_files.txt.",False,True
7626,aist4010-spring2024-a1,"# Description

This is the Kaggle part of Assignment 1.

Given an image of a person¡¯s face, the task of classifying the ID of the face is known as face classification. The input to your system will be a face image and you will have to predict the ID of the face. The ground truth will be present in the training data and the network will be doing an
Multi-class Classification to get the prediction. You are provided with a validation set for fine-tuning your model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is accuracy.

$$
accuracy = \frac{Number\ of\ correctly\ classified\ images}{Number\ of\ total\ images}
$$

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, the submission '.csv' file should contain two columns: `id` and `label`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,label
0.jpg,23
1.jpg,5
2.jpg,764
```","# Dataset Description

- `classification-data`: Each sub-folder in train, val and test contains images of one person, and the name of that sub-folder represents their ID.
    - `train`: You are supposed to use the train data set to train your model for the classification task.
    - `val`: You are supposed to use val data to validate the classification accuracy.
    - `test`: You are supposed to assign IDs for images in test data and submit your result.
- `test_list.txt`: This file contains the trials for Classification Test. The first column is the images name. Your task is to assign ID to each image and generate submission file based on the order given here.
- `sample_submission.csv`: This is a sample submission file for this competition.",False,True
7627,viagem-ao-espaco,"# Description

Bem-vindo ao ano de 2912, onde suas habilidades em ci¨ºncia de dados s?o necess¨¢rias para resolver um mist¨¦rio c¨®smico. Recebemos uma transmiss?o de quatro anos-luz de distancia e as coisas n?o parecem boas.

A *Spaceship Titanic* foi um navio de passageiros interestelar lan?ado h¨¢ um m¨ºs. Com quase 13.000 passageiros a bordo, o navio iniciou a sua viagem inaugural transportando emigrantes do nosso sistema solar para tr¨ºs exoplanetas recentemente habit¨¢veis que orbitam estrelas pr¨®ximas.

Ao contornar Alfa Centauri a caminho do seu primeiro destino ¨C o t¨®rrido 55 Cancri E ¨C a incauta *Nave Espacial Titanic* colidiu com uma anomalia do espa?o-tempo escondida dentro de uma nuvem de poeira. Infelizmente, teve um destino semelhante ao do seu hom¨®nimo de 1000 anos antes. Embora o navio tenha permanecido intacto, quase metade dos passageiros foram transportados para uma dimens?o alternativa!


Para ajudar as tripula??es de resgate e recuperar os passageiros perdidos, voc¨º ¨¦ desafiado a prever quais passageiros foram transportados pela anomalia usando registros recuperados do sistema de computador danificado da nave espacial.




----------------------
# Avalia??o

### M¨¦trica

Os envios s?o avaliados com base na [precis?o da classifica??o](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy), a porcentagem de r¨®tulos previstos que est?o corretos.

### Formato de envio

O formato de inscri??o para o concurso ¨¦ um arquivo csv com o seguinte formato:

```
PassengerId,Transported
0013_01,False
0018_01,False
0019_01,False
0021_01,False
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

In this competition your task is to predict whether a passenger was transported to an alternate dimension during the *Spaceship Titanic*'s collision with the spacetime anomaly. To help you make these predictions, you're given a set of personal records recovered from the ship's damaged computer system.

# File and Data Field Descriptions #

- **train.csv** - Personal records for about two-thirds (~8700) of the passengers, to be used as training data.
    - `PassengerId` - A unique Id for each passenger. Each Id takes the form `gggg_pp` where `gggg` indicates a group the passenger is travelling with and `pp` is their number within the group. People in a group are often family members, but not always.
    - `HomePlanet` - The planet the passenger departed from, typically their planet of permanent residence.
    - `CryoSleep` - Indicates whether the passenger elected to be put into suspended animation for the duration of the voyage. Passengers in cryosleep are confined to their cabins.
    - `Cabin` - The cabin number where the passenger is staying. Takes the form `deck/num/side`, where `side` can be either `P` for *Port* or `S` for *Starboard*.
    - `Destination` - The planet the passenger will be debarking to.
    - `Age` - The age of the passenger.
    - `VIP` - Whether the passenger has paid for special VIP service during the voyage.
    - `RoomService`, `FoodCourt`, `ShoppingMall`, `Spa`, `VRDeck` - Amount the passenger has billed at each of the *Spaceship Titanic*'s many luxury amenities.
    - `Name` - The first and last names of the passenger.
    - `Transported` - Whether the passenger was transported to another dimension. This is the target, the column you are trying to predict.
- **test.csv** - Personal records for the remaining one-third (~4300) of the passengers, to be used as test data. Your task is to predict the value of `Transported` for the passengers in this set. 
- **sample_submission.csv** - A submission file in the correct format.
    - `PassengerId` - Id for each passenger in the test set.
    - `Transported` - The target. For each passenger, predict either `True` or `False`.",False,True
7628,classification-competition-2024,"# Description

## Predicting the popularity of IMDb movies
In this competition, you will have to predict the popularity of IMDb movies by analyzing the data in the proposed dataset. Thus, based on what you learned at the sessions, you'll need to create a model that estimates whether a movie will become popular or not. By accessing this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/stefanoleone992/imdb-extensive-dataset?select=IMDb+movies.csv), you can find more additional information about this data set.


## Rules
- The code must be written in Python. 
- You can use any classifier models that are implemented in Python.
- You will only upload the predictions.csv prediction file.
- You are not allowed to use features directly related to the evaluation of films, such as ratings/scores of movies, number of votes, or reviews (characteristics of the film that are not known when it appeared on the platform).
- You can use features from data sets uploaded to OneFile to drive models.
- Have fun!


## Final evaluation
The participant with the highest Accuracy will win the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation method
To evaluate the results, you will send only one .csv file each time, in the format below. Additionally, during the competition, you will be able to send a maximum of 2 solutions per day.

In the leaderboard, you will see the ranking based on 40% of the final evaluation dataset. The score for the other 60% of the data set will be hidden and shown after the competition ends.

In the end, you will be able to choose two solutions to be evaluated on the final data set.

## Submission Format
For each movie in the dataset, the submission file must contain two columns: movie_id and popularity.

The uploaded file must be saved with the .csv extension in the following format:

<pre>title_index, popularity
0, popular
1, unpopular
2, popular
</pre>

## Evaluation metrics
For evaluation will be used the [""Classification accuracy""](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) metric.

The classification accuracy defines the number of correctly classified examples divided by the total number of examples in the dataset.","# Dataset Description

You can use 'IMDb names.csv' dataset from this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/stefanoleone992/imdb-extensive-dataset?select=IMDb+movies.csv).",False,True
7629,classificationcompetition2024,"# Description

## Predicting the popularity of IMDb movies
In this competition, you will have to predict the popularity of IMDb movies by analyzing the data in the proposed dataset. Thus, based on what you learned at the sessions, you'll need to create a model that estimates whether a movie will become popular or not. 


## Rules
- The code must be written in Python. 
- You can use any classifier models that are implemented in Python.
- You will only upload the predictions.csv prediction file.
- You are not allowed to use features directly related to the evaluation of films, such as ratings/scores of movies, number of votes, or reviews (characteristics of the film that are not known when it appeared on the platform).
- Have fun!


## Final evaluation
The participant with the highest Accuracy will win the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation method**
To evaluate the results, you will send only one .csv file each time, in the format below. Additionally, during the competition, you will be able to send a maximum of 2 solutions per day.

In the leaderboard, you will see the ranking based on 40% of the final evaluation dataset. The score for the other 60% of the data set will be hidden and shown after the competition ends.

In the end, you will be able to choose two solutions to be evaluated on the final data set.


**Submission Format**
For each movie in the dataset, the submission file must contain two columns: movie_id and popularity.

The uploaded file must be saved with the .csv extension in the following format:

<pre>title_index, popularity
0, popular
1, unpopular
2, popular
</pre>

**Evaluation metrics**

For evaluation will be used the [""Classification accuracy""](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) metric.

The classification accuracy defines the number of correctly classified examples divided by the total number of examples in the dataset.","# Dataset Description

You can use 'IMDb names.csv' dataset from this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/stefanoleone992/imdb-extensive-dataset?select=IMDb+movies.csv).",False,True
7630,db001,"# Overview

Welcome to the Diabetes Risk Prediction competition! 

In this challenge, participants are tasked with developing predictive models to identify individuals at risk of diabetes based on a set of medical and demographic features. 

The dataset provided includes information such as age, gender, and various symptoms that may be indicative of diabetes.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Accuracy]
https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the class variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
ID,class
0,Positive
1,Positive
2,Negative
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset (diabetes_risk_prediction_dataset.csv) contains the following columns:

age: Age of the individual
gender: Gender of the individual (Male/Female)
polyuria: Presence of polyuria (excessive urination) - Yes/No
polydipsia: Presence of polydipsia (excessive thirst) - Yes/No
sudden_weight_loss: Sudden weight loss - Yes/No
weakness: Weakness - Yes/No
polyphagia: Polyphagia (excessive hunger) - Yes/No
genital_thrush: Presence of genital thrush - Yes/No
visual_blurring: Visual blurring - Yes/No
itching: Itching - Yes/No
irritability: Irritability - Yes/No
delayed_healing: Delayed healing - Yes/No
partial_paresis: Partial paresis - Yes/No
muscle_stiffness: Muscle stiffness - Yes/No
alopecia: Alopecia (hair loss) - Yes/No
obesity: Obesity - Yes/No
class: Target variable indicating the presence or absence of diabetes (Positive/Negative)

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7631,sdsc-4016-hw-1,"# Overview

Wind energy is one of the most developed technologies worldwide. The U.S Department of Energy has put together a guide to achieving operational efficiency using predictive maintenance practices. Predictive maintenance uses sensor information and analysis methods to measure and predict degradation and future component capability. The idea behind predictive maintenance is that failure patterns are predictable and if component failure can be predicted accurately and the component is replaced before it fails, the costs of operation and maintenance will be much lower.







----------------------
# Description

The sensors fitted across different machines involved in the process of energy generation collect data related to various environmental factors (temperature, humidity, wind speed, etc.) and additional features related to various parts of the wind turbine (gearbox, tower, blades, break, etc.).

Objective: The objective is to build various classification models, tune them, and find the best one that will help identify failures so that the generators could be repaired before failing/breaking to reduce the overall maintenance cost. 







----------------------
# Evaluation

We will use F1 score to evaluate the model.","# Dataset Description

The data provided is a transformed version of the original data, which was collected using sensors.
train.csv - To be used for training and tuning of models. 

test.csv - To be used only for testing the performance of the final best model.
Train dataset consists of 40 features and 1 target variable
Test dataset consists of 40 features

You need to train your model using training data in train.csv, and then predict labels and submit your answer for testing data in test.csv. You should submit your answer through Kaggle (link).",False,True
