7634,esa-eo4soilprotection-2024-predicting-soc-density,"# Overview

Welcome to our hackathon events hosted by the [OpenGeoHub](https://opengeohub.org/) Foundation in collaboration with the European Space Agency (ESA) as part of the **[ESA Symposium on EO 4 Soil Protection and Restoration 6¨C7 March 2024 at ESA-ESRIN center, Frascati, Italy](https://www.eo4soilprotection.org/)**! The European Space Agency (ESA), along with EO research communities, recognizes the urgency of improving monitoring systems for soil health. These hackathons aim to unite researchers, students, scientists, and technology enthusiasts to address these challenges using open-source remote sensing data and legacy soil data.
----------------------
# Description

##Predicting Soil Organic Carbon (SOC) Density:
SOC is a vital indicator of soil health, affecting fertility and carbon sequestration. In this hackathon, participants are invited to develop innovative models for predicting SOC density in space and time. For this we use a unique 3D+T dataset USDA-NRCS Kellogg Soil Survey Laboratory (KSSL), which provides SOC density measurements at various depth intervals across multiple locations for cca 24,000 samples.

![Figure 1](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6592816%2F60983c9a9373c35a6812f495225b1c68%2FScreenshot%20from%202024-01-30%2006-50-18.png?generation=1706593840637433&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submissions are evaluated using the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) between the predicted and observed **log-scaled (log1p) SOC density** of soil samples in the test set:
$$ RMSE = \sqrt{(\frac{1}{n})\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i} - \hat{y}_{i})^{2}} $$


## Submission File
For each `sample_id` in the test set, the participants must predict the SOC content `logoc_d.f` . The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
sample_id,logoc_d.f
1012,0.54321
5006,1.54321
9999,2.54321
 ```
----------------------
# Timeline

- Start: 2nd of February, 2024
- Close: **16th of February 23:59 CET, 2024**
- Winners announced: 7th March 2024 at the closing session of the Symposium.
- **The deadlines will be followed strictly.** May the best team win!
----------------------
#  Rewards and Recognition

Winners of the hackathon will be awarded the chance to showcase their results at ESA¡¯s Symposium on Earth Observation for Soil Protection and Restoration, scheduled in Frascati, Italy, from March 6¨C7, 2024. Additionally, the winning teams will receive:

- A €15k voucher for cloud processing at ESA¡¯s NoR.
- A life-long Enhanced Membership for the Open Access PeerJ journals.
- A 15-minute slot to present their method at the Symposium.
----------------------
# Acknowledgements

 These hackathons were made possible thanks to the AI4SoilHealth and Open-Earth-Monitor Cyberinfrastructure projects. [AI4SoilHealth](https://ai4soilhealth.eu/) project has received funding from the European Union¡¯s Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under grant agreement [No. 101086179](https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/101086179). [EarthMonitor.org](https://earthmonitor.org/) project has received funding from the European Union¡¯s Horizon Europe research an innovation programme under grant agreement [No. 101059548](https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/101059548). 


----------------------
# Literature of interest

- Bouasria, A., Bouslihim, Y., Gupta, S., Taghizadeh-Mehrjardi, R., & Hengl, T. (2023). Predictive performance of machine learning model with varying sampling designs, sample sizes, and spatial extents. Ecological Informatics, 78, 102294. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecoinf.2023.102294 
- Hengl, T., Sorenson, P., Parente, L., Cornish, K., Battigelli, J., Bonannella, C., ... & Nichols, K. (2023). Assessment of soil organic carbon stocks in Alberta using 2-scale sampling and 3D predictive soil mapping. FACETS, 8, 1-17. https://doi.org/10.1139/facets-2023-0040 
- Maxwell, T. L., Hengl, T., Parente, L. L., Minarik, R., Worthington, T. A., Bunting, P., ... & Landis, E. (2023). Global mangrove soil organic carbon stocks dataset at 30 m resolution for the year 2020 based on spatiotemporal predictive machine learning. Data in Brief, 50, 109621. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dib.2023.109621 
- Sanderman, J., Savage, K., & Dangal, S. R. (2020). Mid©\infrared spectroscopy for prediction of soil health indicators in the United States. Soil Science Society of America Journal, 84(1), 251-261. https://doi.org/10.1002/saj2.20009 
- Ugbaje, S. U., Karunaratne, S., Bishop, T., Gregory, L., Searle, R., Coelli, K., & Farrell, M. (2024). Space-time mapping of soil organic carbon stock and its local drivers: Potential for use in carbon accounting. Geoderma, 441, 116771. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geoderma.2023.116771","# Dataset Description

##Training Dataset (train_soc.csv):
- Dataset Name: KSSL dataset
- As a part of the SS4GG project, this dataset comprises 5165 unique locations with a total of 23898 laboratory measurements of SOC density.
##Validation Dataset (test_soc.csv)
The dataset contains both public and private test points. Public points are available for internal validation of your models. It will help you fine-tune and assess the accuracy of your predictions during the development phase. Private dataset represents the final test of your model's effectiveness in real-world scenarios. Your goal is to predict these hidden values accurately. 

## Columns
Explanation of columns (variables) in the training dataset can be found [here](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IbzrJuyOBRFNTzXzcvw_y0O25fVfj2osJFs1TukHflw/edit?usp=sharing).

##Important columns are:
* `sample_id`: unique ID
* `logoc_d.f`: target variable in log-scale
* `X`, `Y`: easting and northing in EPSG 5070
* `observation_year`: observation year
* `olc_id`: Open Location ID (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Location_Code) this allows you to check unique locations of points
* `UUID`: Universally Unique Identifier (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier) this allows you to point to exact sample / unique row; 


Note that columns `hzn_top` and `hzn_bot` indicate upper and lower boundary of the soil layer (for example 0¨C30 cm depth interval has upper depth of 0 and lower depth of 30).",False,True
7635,playground-series-s4e2,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** The goal of this competition is to use various factors to predict obesity risk in individuals, which is related to cardiovascular disease. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) score.

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the class value of the target, `NObeyesdad`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,NObeyesdad
    20758,Normal_Weight
    20759,Normal_Weight
    20760,Normal_Weight
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - February 1, 2024
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  February 29, 2024

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise
* 2nd Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise
* 3rd Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise

**Please note:** In order to encourage more participation from beginners, Kaggle merchandise will only be awarded once per person in this series. If a person has previously won, we'll skip to the next team.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Obesity or CVD risk](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/aravindpcoder/obesity-or-cvd-risk-classifyregressorcluster) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

**Note:** This dataset is particularly well suited for visualizations, clustering, and general EDA. Show off your skills!

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `NObeyesdad` is the categorical target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the class of `NObeyesdad` for each row
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
7636,impute-missing-values,"# Description

## Description

Please impute the missing values in `Data.csv`.

The missing data is generated as follows:

Let $$X_1,\ldots,X_N$$ follow a multivariate normal distribution with mean $$\bf 0\in\mathbb{R}^p$$ and covariance matrix $$\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times p}$$ where the inverse covariance matrix $$\Theta = \Sigma^{-1}$$ is sparse (i.e. underlying graphical model is sparse). 

Let $$X=(X_1,\ldots,X_N)^T\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$$ be the data matrix.

Let $$\beta = (\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p)^T \in \mathbb{R}^p$$ be some $p$-dimensional vectors and let $$\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_p$$ be $p$ real numbers.

Let $$X_{ij}$$ denote the i,j-th element of the matrix $X$ and it is missing if and only if $$X_i \beta >\tau_j.$$

You are welcome to leverage this data generation mechanism to help your imputation, but you don't have to.

##  Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean Squared Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error).

## Submission Format
You should only submit your imputed values, rearranged by columns.

The following R codes can help you quickly extracted your imputed missing values.

`MissingData=read.csv('Data.csv')`
`IsMissingMatrix=is.na(MissingData)`
Suppose `CompleteData` is the matrix, where missing values have been filled by imputed values.
`ImputedValues=as.vector(CompleteData)[as.vector(IsMissingMatrix)]`
`output=data.frame(Id=c(1:length(ImputedValues)), Predicted=ImputedValues)`
`write.csv(output,'output.csv',row.names = FALSE)`


Now this `output.csv` is the final file you need to submit. Remember to double check that it is in the same format as the **sample_submission.csv**.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **Data.csv** - the data set with missing values. It is a 500*50 matrix. All missing values are marked by NA.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
In **Data.csv**,
*   `V1` - `V50` are column names.

In **sample_submission.csv**
*   `Id`?- index of rows.
*   `Predicted` - predicted (or imputed) missing values.",False,True
