## Scoring
The scoring weights are divided in the following way - 
**Round 1 Public Standing** : 30%
**Hidden Evaluation** : 50%
**Presentation** : 20%
----------------------
# Description

**Goal of the Competition**
The goal of this competition is to recognize model IPA transcription from Regional Bengali texts(Remember the greek characters in the dictionary, to help you find out the accurate pronunciation of words? That was International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) transcription! You will build a model trained on a linguist validated dataset containing Bengali text from different domains.

Your efforts could improve Bengali computational linguistics and NLP research using the first Bengali sentence level IPA transcription dataset from Bengali.AI . In addition, your submission will be among the first open-source IPA transcription methods for regional Bengali, which will further extend the capabilities of generalized Bengali Text-to-Speech systems. 


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by a mean Word Error Rate (The number of errors divided by the total words), proceeding as follows:

The WER is computed for each instance in the test set.
The WERs are averaged within domains, weighted by the number of words in the sentence.
The (unweighted) mean of the domain averages is the final score.
This Python code computes the metric:


```python
import jiwer  # you may need to install this library

def mean_wer(solution, submission):
    score = jiwer.wer(solution['string'].to_list(), submission['string'].to_list())
#     print(score)
    return score
```

Submission Format
The submission files should contain two columns: index and ipa. You will need to predict the sentence for each sentence in the test dataset file.

The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:

index, region, ipa
0007, Chittagong,  t?o?????? z?b?n t?o s?????d?? ??? d????o go?? g??e?
0042, Chittagong,  t?o?????? z?b?n t?o s?????d?? ??? d????o go?? g??e?","# Dataset Description

In the Bengali language, there are seven vowel phonemes: ?, ?, e, i, o, ?, you. For example, ???? which means 'injury¡¯ is transcribed as /?g??t?/, ??? as /???/, ?????????? as /?k?u?nt/. We have employed the phonetic chart created by Peter Ladefoged[1] to represent the phonetic sounds of the Bengali language.

The Bengali consonant phonemes are: k, k?, g, g?, ?, c, c?, ?, ??, t, t?, d, d?,n, t?, t??, d?, d??, p, p?, b, b?, m, ?, l, ?, s, h, ?, ??. While some Bengali linguists suggested using affricate sounds for ?/t?/, ?/t??/, ?/d?/, ?/d??/, we're recommending plosive sounds for our dataset which is more appropriate in the context of Bengali language. For the ? and ? consonant, we are using the alveolar /t/, /t?/ sounds. For example, ????? /t?meto/, ????? /cet?on?/ ??? /g?o??/, ????? /?o??pe/ and so on.

Due to having many borrowed words from languages like Arabic, Chinese, English, Farsi, Portuguese, etc. in the Bengali language, the speaker affects the pronunciation of these borrowed words. Regional dialects of Bengali often incorporate these consonants as well as Diphthongs that are different from canonical Bengali. In spoken Bengali, people tend to use the 'z' sound instead of 'j' and the 'f' sound instead of 'p?', which is uncommon in Bengali consonant sounds. For instance, ""f?t?em?"" is pronounced instead of ""p??t?em?"", and ""f??mes?"" is used instead of ""p???mes?"". Similarly, ""z??d?"" is used instead of ""???d?"", and so forth.

Below are some IPA transcribed regional Bengali sentences:

?????? ???????? ???, ???????? ??? ?? ??? ???? ?? ?????? ???	/k?nt?u t?o?????? l????, t?o?????? l???? t?o ?hon x??to ???? g???e d?e?/
?? ?? ?????? ?? ???????? ??? ??? ???? ??????? ??????? ????????? ???????? ??? ????? ???? ????, ???? ??? ?????? ??? ????? ?? ???????	/s? ?? ?????e d?e t?o?????? mone g??o okgo help?? h?nd v??s?t?? mo???d?d?e ?t?e be?k k?su d??bu, ?t?e??? zod?? e???ll? go?e t?????le t?o ??mo????/
????? ???? ????? ????? ?????? ? ???, ??????? ????? ???????	/????? s????? e???g?n kl?s etend n? go?e, ???n? ????? ??mo????/


We have proposed the /?/ phoneme instead of /a/ and /?/ instead of /?/ which are more commonly used to transcribe Bengali language.


**Files and Field Descriptions**

   -  The dataset was extracted from a speech corpus's transcriptions.
   -  The dataset contains dialects from 6 different districts, those are Rangpur, Kishoreganj, Narail, Chittagong, Narsingdi, and Tangail.
   -  The train and validation set comprises of respectively 30311 and 8940 sentences from various regions.
   -  train.csv Sentence labels for the training set.
   -  ""Index"" column is a unique identifier for the entries corresponding to the file {Index} in train/.
   -  Every Bengali sentence has a corresponding IPA transcription. Your goal is to predict the IPA transcriptions of these Bengali sentences in the test set.
   -  Split Whether train or valid. The annotations in the valid split have been manually reviewed and corrected, while the annotations in the train split have only been algorithmically cleaned. The valid samples will generally have higher quality annotations than the train samples, but are otherwise drawn from the same distribution.
   -  Sample_submission.csv A sample submission file in the correct format. See the Evaluation page for more details.


**NOTE:** There are ""<>"" in places, since this dataset was derived from regional speech transcriptions, One of the transcription protocols that was provided to the transcribers was to put ""<>"" if they encountered something unclear or could not decode any meaning from an entire audio clip or from a part of it.
Ignore them and they should be treated as it is and in corresponding places when doing modeling.",False,True
7684,predictive-maintenance-of-a-robot-ii,"# Overview

You are the manager of the company ¡°Zhiguo nuclear service¡±. This company provides a service for changing the nuclear fuel during a nuclear refueling cycle. It provides fully automatic service based on robots. 
Each service will require **10** robots, operating for **6** months.

According to the contract, failing to finish the operation within the agreed duration will result in a large penalty.
Your company owns a fleet of **12** robots. Your goal is to make an operation plan (including maintenance of course) for a planning horizon of the next **120** months, so that your profits will be maximized.

To fulfil this tasks, you are asked to develop a decision-support model, which can make a decision at a particular time slot $t$, given historical and condition-monitoring data up to $t$, whether you should replace some of your robots before you start a new mission for $6$ months that requires at least $10$ working robots.
----------------------
# Description

This challenge is based on a previous challenge (https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/predictive-maintenance-for-industrial-robots-i) that aims at developing a RUL prediction model to tell whether the RUL of a robot is larger than $6$ months. We make decisions in this challenge based on this model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The participant is presented 10 scenarios, where under each scenario, he/she has a condition-monitoring data until the current time for a fleet of $12$ robots. He/she needs to decide, for the current time, if we have a mission that requires $10$ robots to fulfil, if we need to replace some robots before we can start the mission (1 - yes, 0 - no). The decision for a scenario is given as a vector of lenth $12$, where each element is 1 or 0 (1 - replace the robot, 0 - otherwise).


## Submission File
The participant is asked to submit a .csv file following the template here: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/predictive-maintenance-of-a-robot-ii/data?select=submission_template.csv

For each item, he/she needs to make a decision (0/1), where 
- 0 means do not replace the robot and start the mission directly
- 1 means replace the robot before start the mission


## Evaluation metric

The score will be decided based on whether the mission succeed and how much remaining useful life of the robot is wasted.
    - If a mission succeed (less than 3 robots failed within the next six months), a reward of 2 units will be given
    - If a mission failed (>=3 robots failed within the next six months), a penalty of -4 will be given
    - The replaced robots will have a penalty of -1/60*true_rul

The following function is used to calculate the evaluation metric

```
import pandas as pd
import pandas.api.types

def score(solution: pd.DataFrame, submission: pd.DataFrame, row_id_column_name: str) -> float:
    '''
    This metric is customized to measure the performance of the predictive maintenance planning. 
    The participant is presented 10 scenarios, where under each scenario, he/she has a condition-monitoring data until the current time for a fleet of $12$ robots.
    He/she needs to decide, for the current time, if we have a mission that requires $10$ robots to fulfil, if we need to replace some robots before we can start the mission (1 - yes, 0 - no).

    The decision for a scenario is given as a vector of lenth $12$, where each element is 1 or 0 (1 - replace the robot, 0 - otherwise).

    The score will be decided based on whether the mission succeed and how much remaining useful life of the robot is wasted.
    - If a mission succeed (less than 3 robots failed within the next six months), a reward of 2 units will be given
    - If a mission failed (>=3 robots failed within the next six months), a penalty of -4 will be given
    - The replaced robots will have a penalty of -1/60*true_rul

    TODO: Add unit tests. We recommend using doctests so your tests double as usage demonstrations for competition hosts.
    https://docs.python.org/3/library/doctest.html
    # This example doctest works for mean absolute error:
    >>> import pandas as pd
    >>> row_id_column_name = ""scenario_idx""
    >>> solution_data = {'scenario_idx': ['scenario_0', 'scenario_1'], 'item_0': [36, 4], 'item_1': [36, 4], 'item_2': [36, 4], 'item_3': [36, 4], 'item_4': [36, 4], 'item_5': [36, 4], 'item_6': [36, 4], 'item_7': [36, 4], 'item_8': [36, 4], 'item_9': [36, 4], 'item_10': [36, 4], 'item_11': [36, 4]}
    >>> submission_data = {'scenario_idx': ['scenario_0', 'scenario_1'], 'item_0': [0, 0], 'item_1': [0, 0], 'item_2': [0, 0], 'item_3': [0, 0], 'item_4': [0, 0], 'item_5': [0, 0], 'item_6': [0, 0], 'item_7': [0, 0], 'item_8': [0, 0], 'item_9': [0, 0], 'item_10': [0, 0], 'item_11': [0, 0]}
    >>> solution = pd.DataFrame(solution_data)
    >>> submission = pd.DataFrame(submission_data)
    >>> score(solution.copy(), submission.copy(), row_id_column_name)
    -2.0
    '''

    # Initialize rewards and penalties
    reward = 2
    penalty_mission_failure = -4
    penalty_early_replacement = -1 / 60

    # Compare labels and calculate rewards/penalties
    cummulative_reward = 0
    for i in range(len(solution)):
        ruls = solution.iloc[i][1:]
        cummulative_reward += sum(ruls[submission.iloc[i][1:]==1])*penalty_early_replacement
        
        ruls[submission.iloc[i][1:]==1] = 60
        if sum(ruls<=6)>=3:
            cummulative_reward += penalty_mission_failure
        else:
            cummulative_reward += reward
    
    return cummulative_reward

```","# Dataset Description

## Training dataset

The training dataset is the same as the phase-I, which is accessible here: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/predictive-maintenance-for-industrial-robots-i/data

## Testing dataset

In the testing dataset, you will find ten folders corresponding to ten scenarios you need to make decision. In each folder, e.g., scenario_0, you will find $12$ .csv files, corresponding to the condition-monitoring data for the $12$ robots. Please note:
- The last row of the csv files corresponds to the same time. This is the moment you need to make decisions for this scenario.
- Some robots the time does not start from zero. This is because it has failed and was replaced before. In the csv file, the time starts from the previous replacement of the robot. It is assumed that after replacement, the robot is as-good-as-new.

## Submission template
You can find a file ""submission_template.csv"". Please use it as a submission template.",False,True
7685,ml-olympiad-detect-hallucinations-in-llms,"# Overview

In this Kaggle competition - part of the #MLOlympiad - participants are challenged to develop algorithms capable of discerning hallucinatory responses generated by the Mistral 7B Instruct model. Given a set of responses generated by the Mistral 7B Instruct model, participants are tasked with designing machine learning models or algorithms that can accurately identify which responses are most likely to be hallucinations. The goal is to distinguish between genuine, contextually appropriate responses and those that are nonsensical, misleading, or otherwise erroneous. This competition aims to advance the field of natural language processing by addressing the challenge of detecting and mitigating hallucinations in language generation models.
----------------------
# Description

We executed instructions from the Open-Orca dataset using a Mistral 7B Instruct large language model.  Constructed from a vast repository of enriched FLAN data, this dataset presents a goldmine for individuals involved in Natural Language Processing (NLP). It is intricately aligned with the distributions outlined in the Orca paper, showcasing its pivotal role in producing top-notch model checkpoints and serving as a prized resource for NLP aficionados.

Based on the answers we got, we labeled the feedback as a proper answer or a hallucination based on internal metrics, heuristics, and direct scrutiny. In this competition, your task is to develop an algorithm to score answers probabilistically, pointing out answers more likely to be hallucinations.

Research has shown that LLM hallucinations can arise from various factors, including source-reference divergence in training data, the exploitation of jailbreak prompts, reliance on incomplete or contradictory datasets, overfitting, and the model¡¯s propensity to guess based on patterns rather than factual accuracy. Methods such as prompt tuning, measuring perplexity, and evaluating anti-hallucination techniques have been proposed to address this.

In addition, recent research pointed out the usage of LLMs themselves to point out hallucinations. The use of Large Language Models (LLMs) to detect the level of hallucinations in the responses of other LLMs is an emerging area of research. Several tools and frameworks, such as G-Eval, GPTScore, SelfCheckGPT, TRUE, ChatProtect, and Chainpoll, are being developed to measure and prevent LLM hallucinations. These tools aim to reduce the likelihood of hallucinations by adopting a metrics-driven approach to LLM evaluation, defining test cases, and setting up automated checks to quantify the effectiveness of hallucination countermeasures.

It is up to you to find the best way to score the degree of hallucinations of the produced text.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,Target
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id` - the row id
*   `Prompt`?- The prompt provided to Mistral 7B Instruct
*   `Answer`?- The received answer with temp=0.0 and top_p=0.95
*   `Target`?- Heuristic labelling if the answer is a hallucination (0=probably not a hallucination 1=definitely an hallucination)",False,True
7686,measuring-student-persistence-and-completion-rate,"# Overview

Welcome to Tuwaiq Academy, a non-profit training institute in Saudi Arabia. With millions of applicants vying for limited seats in advanced AI programs, this challenge is your chance to revolutionize the allocation process. Your task is to build a predictive model that identifies top candidates likely to successfully complete the training program.

**Goal:**
The goal of the competition is to develop an efficient model that sifts through the pool of applicants, pinpointing those with the highest likelihood of completing Tuwaiq Academy's rigorous training programs. By achieving this, you contribute to the strategic utilization of limited seats, ensuring they are granted to individuals your model identifies as having the best potential to excel in the program. Seize the challenge, make a lasting impact!
----------------------
# Description

**Overview:**
Tuwaiq Academy, a non-profit educational institute in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, is on a mission to advance AI and emerging technologies. With a surge in applications and limited seats for our coveted advanced programs, we invite you to participate in this competition for educational excellence.

**Objective:**
In the face of millions of applicants, Tuwaiq Academy aims to streamline the selection process for our advanced training programs. Your challenge is to develop a predictive model that can accurately identify individuals with the highest likelihood of successfully completing the rigorous training curriculum.

**Why Participate:**
- **Impactful Contribution:** Your efforts directly influence the strategic allocation of limited seats, ensuring they are granted to those most likely to excel in the program.
- **Advancing Education:** By participating in this competition, you become a key player in shaping the future of  education, contributing to the growth of knowledge and expertise in the field.

**Key Details:**
- **Dataset:** We provide registration information for accepted trainees in a variety of training programs, including a completion target column.
- **Objective:** Develop a robust model to identify candidates with the best potential for successful program completion.
- **Outcome:** The winning models will significantly enhance the efficiency of the selection process, allowing Tuwaiq Academy to optimize resource allocation and make a lasting impact on the education landscape.

**How to Win:**
- **Accuracy:** Build a model that accurately predicts trainees' likelihood of completing the program.
- **Innovation:** Stand out by incorporating innovative approaches, algorithms, or features that elevate the predictive power of your model.
- **Explanation:** Provide insights into the factors driving your model's predictions, demonstrating a clear understanding of the problem.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Precision and Recall or F1 score] between the predicted class and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each student ID in the test set, you must predict a label for the TARGET variable (Y). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| ID | Y |
|----|---|
| 1  | 0 |
| 2  | 1 |
| 3  | 0 |
| 4 |   0 |
|  . |  . |

----------------------
# Prizes & Awards

1st, 2nd and 3rd Place:

-Achievement Badge.
-Certificates of achievement.
-Acknowledgment for outstanding contributions.","# Dataset Description

In this dataset, you will delve into the diverse facets of students' registration information and program participation at Tuwaiq Academy. Each entry is a window into the student's profile, encompassing personal details, program specifics, and academic achievements. From attendance records to technology types and employment statuses, this dataset encapsulates a wealth of information. Aspiring data scientists and analysts are invited to explore, analyze, and uncover patterns that could revolutionize the way we understand and optimize educational experiences. Let the journey into the Tuwaiq Academy dataset commence, where insights await discovery!

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
* **registeration.csv** - supplement information about the trainer number of registrations attempts in all programs by category
## Columns
* `Student ID`: String - A unique identifier for each student.
* `Age`: Int - The age of the student.
* `Gender`: String - Gender of the student.
* `Home Region`: String - Student's home region.
* `Home City`: String - Student's home city.
* `Program ID`: String - Unique identifier for each program.
* `Program Main Category Code`: String - Main category of the program (Encoded).
* `Program Sub Category Code`: String - Sub-category of the program (Encoded).
* `Technology Type`: String - Type of technology used in the program.
* `Program Skill Level`: String - Skill level of the program.
* `Program Presentation Method`: String - Presentation method of the program (in-person or online).
* `Program Start Date`: Date - The date the program started.
* `Program End Date`: Date - The date the program ended.
* `Program Days`: Int - Count of days in the program.
* `Completed Degree`: String - Indicates if the student completed a university/college degree.
* `Level of Education`: String - Highest university/college degree received by the student.
* `Education Specialty`: String - University/college degree specialty.
* `College`: String - The student's university/college.
* `University Degree Score`: String - The score of the student in university/college.
* `University Degree Score System`: String - The scoring system used for the student's university/college score.
* `Employment Status`: String - Current employment status of the student.
* `Job Type`: String - Type of employment for the student.
* `Still Working`: String - Indicates if the student is currently working.
* `Y (Target)`: Bool - Indicates if the student completed the program by achieving the minimum attendance percentage.(1 = Did not complete the program, 0 = Successfully completed the program)",False,True
7687,machine-learning-band-gap-prediction,"# Overview

Welcome to the Machine Learning Challenge hosted as part of Amalgam 2024, the annual technical festival hosted by the Metallurgical and Materials Engineering Students Association?of?IIT?Madras.  In this competition, participants will have the opportunity to apply their machine-learning skills to predict the band gap of polymers.

The goal of this competition is to develop the most accurate machine learning model that can predict the band gap of polymers based on the given data.

Participation is open to anyone who has a passion for machine learning. The competition provides a platform for individuals to showcase their skills and win prize money.
----------------------
# Evaluation

In this round, all teams must build a machine-learning model to predict the target column, which is the band gap, based on the given data. The teams must submit their predictions using this model on the test dataset provided. The competition will be judged using the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) metric.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

The second column, ""polymer"", in the train and test data is the name of the polymer in SMILES (simplified molecular-input line-entry system) format. The 84 columns following the polymer name are material fingerprints that have been generated for the polymers. 

The target column is the ""band_gap"" column, which represents the band gap of the polymers.",False,True
7688,brouillonpourmescompetitions,"# Overview

Dear Participants welcome to the main round of the ENAS Winter School Hackathon! 

# **Challenge Brief:**
One of our primary objectives at the Fraunhofer Institute of Electronic Nanosystems is to use AI to automate and streamline the analysis of metrological measurements, that is because manual analysis can be tedious and error prone.
To achieve this goal, we employ state-of-the-art image processing and computer vision methodologies. These advanced techniques enable us to transition from manual analysis to semi-automatic and fully-automatic processes, significantly enhancing the speed, accuracy, and reliability of optical metrological measurements analysis. **A slightly challenging analysis task is to assess errors in metrological scans. one of these errors is the ""Alignment Offset Error"", which will be your task here.**


# **What is a Through-Silicon Via (TSV)?**
Imagine a microscopic highway running through the heart of a silicon wafer, connecting different layers of electronic circuits. That's precisely what a TSV is¡ªa vertical channel etched through silicon substrates, allowing for the integration of various components and enhancing the performance and efficiency of semiconductor devices. TSVs play a pivotal role in enabling advanced technologies such as 3D integrated circuits, stacked memory architectures, and micro-electro-mechanical systems (MEMS).


# **What to Expect:**
- Data Exploration: Dive into a curated dataset containing semiconductor images paired with the corresponding Area.

- Algorithm Development: Build and fine-tune your algorithms to predict the Area based on the given dataset.

- Evaluation: Your solutions will be assessed based on their ability to align with the ground truth Area values.
----------------------
# Description

During the manufacturing and analysis phases, meticulous attention to TSVs is essential, as several defects can potentially arise, impacting device functionality and reliability.

In our competition, we will delve into the realm of semiconductor error assessment, focusing on one particular defect: 
**Alignment Offset Error:** This error occurs during the exposure of a lacquer/paint in the lithography process. 
Alignment Offset Error refers to the offset that occurs during the exposure from the upper layer to the lower layer. This is typically due to the precision of the alignment performed during lithography. 
This specific anomaly manifests as a visible misalignment, often appearing as a dark area within the TSV structure, typically situated at the bottom of the via. However, it is imperative to note that the Alignment Offset Error may also manifest in other locations within the via as you will see later on while navigating through the provided data, highlighting the complexity of error detection and characterization in semiconductor devices.

For instance, in a well-aligned via with no misalignment errors, the area of the defect should be 0.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7429498%2Fb79feea9cf1e8de0ae570c89efeb90ea%2FGood.jpg?generation=1708506348499843&alt=media)
Conversely, in a via exhibiting misalignment at the bottom, the area of the defect would likely be greater than 0, 
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7429498%2F32a3a5f037b77a635ac8b2ae66a84dcc%2Falignment%20err.jpeg?generation=1708506366055979&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Participants' submissions will be evaluated based on their ability to accurately predict the area of errors in the provided images. The evaluation metric for this competition will be the **mean squared error.**","# Dataset Description

## Data:
In this competition, participants will have access to a meticulously curated dataset comprising 128 images of vias, each measuring 129x129 pixels in size, **note that in all the provided images the via is always located at the center of the image**. These images have been carefully selected to contain the characteristics of the ""Alignment Offset Error"".

## Ground Truth Data:
Accompanying the image dataset is a CSV file containing ground truth values for the evaluated area of the Alignment Offset Error, measured in **pixels**. This CSV file is linked with the names of the corresponding images, this provides the participants with the ability to use these labeled data to train a model if desired, to facilitate the process for the participants we have already split the data into training and testing subsets, that can be found in the following files:

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format. **note that you are required to provide values for the area of all provided images.**

**Columns**

*   `index` - name of the image
*   `area`?- calculated area of Alignment Offset Error

**It is important to note that the CSV file named ""Ground_Truth.csv"" and therefore the combination of the ""training.csv"" and ""testing.csv"" contains ground truth values for only 102 images out of the total 128 images in the dataset**. This intentional omission of ground truth values for certain images serves two primary purposes: to facilitate model evaluation and validation and to discourage overfitting by penalizing models that rely excessively on the provided data. 

## Data Format:
Participants are welcome to utilize the provided CSV file to read the images as input data and extract the corresponding ground truth values for the evaluated area of Alignment Offset Error. The ultimate goal of this competition is to through the development of an algorithmic approach or training a machine learning model to predict the area of the Alignment Offset Error in nanometers within the images accurately.

## Image Format:
All images in the dataset are encoded in JPEG format, ensuring compatibility and ease of access for participants across various computational environments and platforms. 

## Conversion factor:
**The Ground Truth data provide the Misalignment error Area in pixels, in order to convert it and therefore get the area in nanometers participants can multiply their predictions by the current conversion factor:**
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7429498%2Fc3b4203b1ca89f97f916be52103a3786%2FCodeCogsEqn.gif?generation=1709653147371695&alt=media)

## The Expected Output:
Keep in mind that what is expected from the participants is to **provide either csv file or notebook** that would generate a file named **""submission.csv""** that should contain the predicted area in nanometers **for ALL the provided images**. Please refer to the provided ""sample_submission.csv"" file.",False,True
