Embrace the opportunity to redefine your understanding of data!

Welcome to the Stock Sentiment Analysis Challenge! In this competition, participants are tasked with leveraging sentiment analysis techniques to predict the movement of stock prices based on historical news headlines.

Sentiment Analysis, a powerful analytical method, allows computers to understand natural language and deduce whether a message is positive, neutral, or negative. In this context, participants will apply sentiment analysis to news headlines to determine their impact on stock prices. By analyzing the emotional tone conveyed in news articles, participants aim to predict whether stock prices will rise or fall/stay the same on any later date.

The dataset provided for this challenge, Combined_News_DJIA.csv and Reddit news, spans from 2008 to 2015.

Participants will merge these datasets to create a comprehensive training set for building predictive models. The challenge involves analyzing the relationship between news sentiment and stock price movements, with the ultimate goal of accurately predicting market trends.

Join us in this exciting competition to develop innovative solutions for predicting stock market movement using sentiment analysis techniques. Put your data wrangling, machine learning, and predictive modeling skills to the test and compete to become the champion of stock sentiment analysis!
----------------------
# Evaluation

For this Stock Sentiment Analysis Challenge, submissions will be evaluated based on their ability to predict stock price movements accurately. The evaluation metric for this competition is the Mean Squared Error (MSE), a widely used metric for regression tasks.

The Mean Squared Error is defined as follows:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F15962257%2Fc95af09cb0ef09f4eb8d00d9866de020%2FCapture.PNG?generation=1708708033875153&alt=media)

For each prediction, the Mean Squared Error computes the squared difference between the true label and the predicted probability. This loss function penalizes large errors more heavily, making it sensitive to outliers in the data.

To calculate the overall performance of a submission, the average Mean Squared Error across all predictions in the test dataset will be computed. Lower values of the Mean Squared Error indicate better predictive performance.

Participants are encouraged to develop models that minimize the Mean Squared Error by effectively leveraging sentiment analysis techniques and capturing the relationship between news headlines and stock price movements. Successful submissions will demonstrate robustness to noise and outliers in the data while providing accurate predictions of market trends.

Participants can use the provided training dataset to train their models and submit predictions for the test dataset through the Kaggle platform. Submissions will be scored based on the Mean Squared Error metric, and leaderboard rankings will reflect the performance of each participant's predictive model.


We wish all participants the best of luck in this challenging yet rewarding competition!
----------------------
# Output

Output file should be in the format of a csv file containing close value of test dates multiplied by 100000.","# Dataset Description

**Description:**

There are two channels of data provided in this dataset:

News data: The news headlines are from Reddit. They are ranked by reddit users' votes, and only the top 25 headlines are considered for a single date.
(Range: 2008-06-08 to 2015-07-01)

Stock data: Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) is used to ""prove the concept"".
(Range: 2008-08-08 to 2015-07-01)

**You have to predict the close value multiplied by 10<sup>5</sup> and submit the csv file.**


## Files

*   **Combined_News_DJIA.csv** - Contain news ranked from top to bottom based on their relevance (25 lines for each date)
*   **RedditNews.csv** - Contain top 25 news headlines for each date
*   **DJIA_table.csv** - stock price data
*   **Test_dates.csv** - dates for prediction
*   **sample_submission.csv** 


## Columns
*   **Combined_News_DJIA.csv(train)**
*   `Date` - date on which the news is published
*   `Label` - ""1"" when DJIA Adj Close value rose or stayed as the same, ""0"" when DJIA Adj Close value decreased.
*   `Top1 - Top25`?- Top25 news headlines on that particular date

*   **RedditNews.csv(train)**
*   `Date` - date on which the news is published
*   `News`?- news headlines on that particular date

*   **DJIA_table.csv(train)**
*   `Date` - date 
*   `Open`?- Opening price of stock
*   `High`?- Highest price of stock on that particular date
*   `Low`?- Lowest price of stock on that particular date
*   `Close`?- Closing price of stock
*   `Volume`?- The number of trades registered
*   `Adj Close`?- Adjusted Closing price

*   **Test_DJIA_dates.csv**
*   `Date` - dates for prediction
*   `Open`?- Opening price of stock
*   `High`?- Highest price of stock on that particular date
*   `Low`?- Lowest price of stock on that particular date
*   `Volume`?- The number of trades registered
*   **Test_Combined_news.csv**
*   `Date` - date on which the news is published
*   `Label` - ""1"" when DJIA Adj Close value rose or stayed as the same, ""0"" when DJIA Adj Close value decreased.
*   `Top1 - Top25`?- Top25 news headlines on that particular date

*   **Test_Redit_news.csv**
*   `Date` - dates for prediction
*     `News` -- news headlines on that particular date

*   **sample_submission.csv**
*   `id` - index for test dates (from July to January)
*     `Close` -- Predicted close value on that particular date",False,True
7693,binary-classifier-for-jugglingballs,"# Overview

Your objective is to construct action recognition models on a subset of [the UCF101 dataset](https://www.crcv.ucf.edu/data/UCF101.php). The UCF101 dataset consists of a total of 13,320 videos spanning diverse human action categories. However, for the final project, our focus narrows down to a subset of the dataset, comprising 2,355 training videos and 969 test videos distributed among 30 action categories. You will have to build one binary classifier and one multi-class classifier.

In this competition, your task is to build a classifier for the class *JugglingBalls*. Your goal is to train a model to classify JugglingBalls videos as *positive* and the remaining videos as *negative*. You are free to choose any machine learning models from the class. 

For more details regarding the tasks, including the dataset download, please refer to the information provided in [this Google Colab link](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/19IOk0hmokk726V2WRwNsX9TQDlcRECuU?usp=sharing).

Please note that **the deadline for project submission is Tuesday, March 5, 11:59 pm**. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **F1 score**.","# Dataset Description

For more details regarding the tasks, including the dataset download, please refer to the information provided in [this Google Colab link](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/19IOk0hmokk726V2WRwNsX9TQDlcRECuU?usp=sharing).",False,True
7694,classifying-the-brain-on-music,"# Overview

Your objective is to classify fMRI brain images taken while listening to music in five different genres: label 0=Ambient Music, 1=Country Music, 2=Heavy Metal, 3=Rock 'n Roll, 4=Classical Symphonic. The data consists of train.csv,  labels.csv, and test.csv, for a one-person subset of a larger 20-subject study.

Please note that **the deadline for project submission is Tuesday, March 5, 11:59 pm**. 
----------------------
# Description

The training data (train.csv) consist of 160 event-related brain images (trials), corresponding to twenty 6-second music clips, four clips in each of the five genres, repeated in-order eight times (runs). The labels (labels.csv) correspond to the correct musical genres, listed above, for each of the 160 trials.

There are 22036 features in each brain image, corresponding to blood-oxygenation levels at each 2mm-cubed 3D location within a section of the auditory cortex. In human brain imaging, there are often many more features (brain sites) than samples (trials), thus making the task a relatively challenging multiway classification problem.

The testing data (test.csv) consists of 40 event-related brain images corresponding to novel 6-second music clips in the five genres. The test data is in randomized order with no labels. You must predict, using only the given brain images, the correct genre labels (0-4) for the 40 test trials.

For more details regarding the tasks, including the dataset download, please refer to the information provided in [this Google Colab link.](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1tbP5miE-5DXXZ5507Rh9eMlLDCWi5L2y?usp=sharing)

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Accuracy**.","# Dataset Description

For more details regarding the tasks, including the dataset download, please refer to the information provided in [this Google Colab link](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1tbP5miE-5DXXZ5507Rh9eMlLDCWi5L2y?usp=sharing).",False,True
