7721,ml-olympiad-sustainable-urban-living,"# Overview

Finding a suitable place to live is a crucial decision, significantly impacting your well-being. While online listings provide information, it can be challenging to assess a property's true ""habitability"" based solely on descriptions. This is where AI can lend a helping hand!

This competition invites you to leverage Machine Learning techniques to develop a model that predicts the habitability score of a property based on available details. Your model will contribute to a user-friendly system that empowers individuals to make informed decisions about their living spaces. #MLOlympiad

**Goal:** Develop an ML model that predicts the Sustainable Habitability Score of a property based on the provided data.
----------------------
# Description

We provide a dataset containing various properties from across the United States. Your challenge is to develop a machine learning model that predicts the ""habitability score"" for each property. This score should capture the overall livability of the property, considering various factors that contribute to a comfortable and enjoyable living experience.

Explainability is crucial! Strive to build models that not only predict accurately but also provide understandable insights into the factors influencing habitability scores. This will help users interpret the results and make informed decisions.

## Who can Participate
This competition is open to everyone interested in applying machine learning for social good and contributing to a sustainable future. We encourage individuals of all skill levels to participate and learn alongside the community.

ML Olympiad is an associated Kaggle Community Competition hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google for Developers. Ready to make a difference? Join the challenge and code your way towards a more sustainable future for everyone!

## Special Reward for Students
The top student teams will not only win recognition for their innovative solutions, but will also receive a valuable GDSC Lead referral code. This code can give you a head start in your application to lead a Google Developer Student Club (GDSC) at your university!

To be eligible for the referral code, submit your entries by March 23rd and fill this form: [forms.gle/21oMHyDPjzjjTPmWA](https://forms.gle/21oMHyDPjzjjTPmWA)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your model's performance will be evaluated based on its ability to accurately predict habitability scores. The primary metric for evaluation will be the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).","# Dataset Description

The dataset originates from an ML Hackathon organized by HackerEarth.  It contains information on various properties located in the United States and aims to facilitate the prediction of their Habitability Score.

The **training set** (train.csv) should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each property. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± like water supply and crime rate. You can also use feature engineering to create new features.

The **test set** (test.csv) should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each property. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each property in the test set, use the model you trained to predict their habitability score

Check the **sample_submission.csv** file for an example of how to format submissions for evaluation.

---

**Column Descriptions:**

| Column Name | Description |
|---|---|
| **Property_ID** | A unique identifier for each property. |
| **Property_Type** | The type of property (e.g., apartment, bungalow). |
| **Property_Area** | The total area of the property in square feet. |
| **Number_of_Windows** | The total number of windows in the property. |
| **Number_of_Doors** | The total number of doors in the property. |
| **Furnishing** | The level of furnishing (fully furnished, semi-furnished, or unfurnished). |
| **Frequency_of_Powercuts** | The average number of power cuts per week (numerical value). |
| **Power_Backup** | Indicates the presence or absence of power backup (binary). |
| **Water_Supply** | The water supply schedule (all-time, once daily - morning/evening, or once every two days). |
| **Traffic_Density_Score** | A numerical score representing traffic density on a scale of 1 (low) to 10 (high). |
| **Crime_Rate** | The crime rate in the neighborhood categorized as well below average, slightly below average, slightly above average, or well above average. |
| **Dust_and_Noise** | The level of dust and noise in the neighborhood (high, medium, or low). |
| **Air_Quality_Index** | The numerical Air Quality Index of the neighborhood. |
| **Neighborhood_Review** | The average rating given to the neighborhood by residents. |
| **Habitability_Score** | The target variable representing the overall habitability score of the property. |",False,True
7722,ml-olympiad-ai-ml-malaysia,"# Overview



----------------------
# Description

## What is ML Olympiad
The Google ML Community Network (AI/ML GDEs, TFUGs, and 3rd-party ML communities) will be hosting Community Competitions and participating in active competitions on Kaggle. The campaign is supported by Google for Developers. 

## Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning Malaysia
Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning Malaysia User Group (AIML UG), formerly known as TensorFlow Malaysia User Group (TFMUG) was founded in 2017. The group's aim is to enable people to create and deploy their own Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning models built with various frameworks. AIML UG aims to build the essential key skillsets for this group from the combination of both beginner and intermediate models as well as advancing to the next level with knowledge sharing and presentations about cutting edge papers and techniques.
Facebook Group Page: [https://www.facebook.com/groups/aimlmalaysia
](https://www.facebook.com/groups/aimlmalaysia)

## ML Olympiad's Objective
Launching and participating in Kaggle competitions will create hands-on learning opportunities for AI/ML communities and developers. This is the 3rd round of ML Olympiad operated by AI/ML Developer Programs team to help build and nurture strong AI/ML communities.

## Datasets
Anonymised Digital Bank Marketing Funnel dataset is used for this hackathon.

## Task
In this competition, you are given a Digital bank Marketing Funnel for micro loan application with some missing values. You are required to build a model to predict Loan approval status.

## Prizes
Top three winners will receive swags to be sent by AI & ML UG. *Note that only winners from Malaysia will receive swags.*

----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation is based on **Probabilistic F-Score Beta (Micro)**. This leaderboard is calculated with approximately 40% of the test data. The final results will be based on the other 60%, so the final standings may be different. The final winners will be determined by **combining both public (40%) and private (60%) results.**","# Dataset Description

The dataset is a sample of Digital Bank Marketing Funnel Micro Loan dataset. Based on the dataset, build a prediction model to predict loan approval status.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
* id : Customer ID
* 02 : Gender (categorical)
* 03 : Date of Birth of the applicant
* 04 : Lead Creation Date
* 05 : City Code (categorical)
* 06 : Area Code (categorical)
* 07 : Employer Code (categorical)
* 08 : Employer Category1 (categorical)
* 09 : Employer Category2 (categorical)
* 10 : Monthly Income 
* 11 : Bank Code 
* 12 : Bank Account Type (categorical)
* 13 : Marketing Source (categorical)
* 14 : Marketing_Category1 (categorical)
* 15 : Marketing_Category2 (categorical)
* 16 : Current Loan Installment
* 17 : Requested Loan Amount
* 18 : Loan Repayment (Years)
* 19 : Interest Rate
* 20 : Requested Loan Installment
* class : Loan Approval Status (Yes=1, No=0)",False,True
7723,arabic-poem-classification,"# Overview

Welcome to the Arabic Poem Classification Challenge, hosted by KAUST Academy Society! This competition, brought to you by KAUST Academy Society, provides an exciting opportunity for participants to explore the realm of Arabic Natural Language Processing (NLP) and contribute to the enrichment of the Arabic language. It offers an entry-level introduction to Arabic NLP while focusing on the captivating task of classifying Arabic poems accurately. So, get ready to challenge yourself, test your abilities, and embark on a journey of discovery and growth.![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F12645857%2Fbe2938b0664263c13086dda269a58abb%2Fistockphoto-481253439-612x612.jpg?generation=1708873618725956&alt=media)
----------------------
# Description

The Arabic Poem Classification Challenge, hosted by KAUST Academy Society, invites participants to showcase their expertise in Arabic NLP and contribute to the enrichment of the Arabic language. This competition provides an entry-level introduction to Arabic NLP and focuses on accurately classifying Arabic poems.

Participants will have access to a diverse dataset of Arabic poems and must develop a robust classification model that accurately categorizes each poem based on its distinct characteristics. NLP techniques such as text preprocessing, feature extraction, and machine learning algorithms can be employed to train the models.

By participating in this challenge, you can enhance your skills in Arabic NLP and contribute to the field's advancement. The top performers will be recognized for their achievements.

Join us in the Arabic Poem Classification Challenge, created by KAUST Academy Society, and embark on a journey of exploration, innovation, and the enrichment of the Arabic language.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Participants should ensure that their notebook includes all necessary code for data preprocessing, model development, and evaluation. It should also contain clear explanations and comments to guide the reader through the code and methodology.

Please make sure to make the title of your notebook under your name.

The submissions for the Arabic Poem Classification Challenge will be evaluated based on the accuracy of the classification predictions. Accuracy is a common evaluation metric for classification tasks and measures the proportion of correctly classified poems in the test set.

To calculate accuracy, the number of correctly classified poems will be divided by the total number of poems in the test set. The higher the accuracy, the better the performance of the classification model.

---
## Submission File
Submission Files:

Participants are required to submit the following files for their challenge entry:

Notebook: Participants should submit their Jupyter notebook or any other relevant notebook format containing the code used to develop the classification model. The notebook should be well-documented and organized, making it easy to understand and reproduce the results.

Submission File: Participants are required to generate a submission file utilizing the provided test data. This file must include two columns, specifically named ""ID"" and ""Category"".

```python
ID,Category
1,????? ????????
1,????? ???????
3,????? ????
4,????? ????????
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

The Arabic Poem Classification Challenge dataset consists of a collection of Arabic poems with the following columns:

1. Title: This column contains the title of each poem. The title provides a brief description or name for the poem, often reflecting its theme or subject matter.

2. Author: The author column indicates the name of the poet who wrote the respective poem. It provides attribution to the creative minds behind the poetic works.

3. Era: The era column represents the historical period to which each poem belongs. It classifies the poems based on the specific era in which they were written, providing insights into the cultural, social, and literary context of that time.

4. Poem: This column contains the actual text of the poem. It includes the verses, stanzas, or lines that make up the poetic composition. The poem column serves as the main focus of analysis and classification in this challenge.

The dataset aims to offer a diverse selection of Arabic poems from different historical periods, allowing participants to explore and analyze the poetry of various eras. The objective of the challenge is to develop classification models that accurately categorize the poems based on their era, showcasing the ability to understand and identify the distinctive features and characteristics associated with each time period.

By working with this dataset, participants can gain a deeper appreciation for the evolution of Arabic poetry throughout different eras and delve into the unique artistic expressions that have shaped the Arabic literary heritage over time.",False,True
7724,ml-olympiad-toxic-language-ptbr-detection,"# Overview

Bem-vindos ¨¤ competi??o de Detec??o de Linguagem T¨®xica em M¨ªdias Sociais para o Portugu¨ºs Brasileiro, uma iniciativa que visa abordar um dos desafios mais prementes da nossa era digital: a toxicidade online. Com o crescente uso das redes sociais, a comunica??o online tornou-se uma faca de dois gumes, oferecendo tanto oportunidades para conex?es significativas quanto espa?os para comportamentos prejudiciais e discursos t¨®xicos. A detec??o eficaz de linguagem t¨®xica ¨¦ crucial para promover ambientes online mais saud¨¢veis e inclusivos.

Esta competi??o ¨¦ inspirada pelo trabalho inovador de Jo?o A. Leite, Diego F. Silva, Kalina Bontcheva e Carolina Scarton (2020), que desenvolveram o dataset [Told-br](https://github.com/JAugusto97/ToLD-Br) um recurso valioso para o estudo da toxicidade em conte¨²do online em portugu¨ºs brasileiro. O objetivo desta competi??o ¨¦ convidar entusiastas de dados, cientistas e pesquisadores a desenvolver modelos de machine learning capazes de classificar tweets em portugu¨ºs brasileiro como t¨®xicos ou n?o t¨®xicos, utilizando o dataset Told-Br.
----------------------
# Description

Bem-vindos ¨¤ competi??o de Detec??o de Linguagem T¨®xica em M¨ªdias Sociais para o Portugu¨ºs Brasileiro, uma iniciativa que visa abordar um dos desafios mais prementes da nossa era digital: a toxicidade online. Com o crescente uso das redes sociais, a comunica??o online tornou-se uma faca de dois gumes, oferecendo tanto oportunidades para conex?es significativas quanto espa?os para comportamentos prejudiciais e discursos t¨®xicos. A detec??o eficaz de linguagem t¨®xica ¨¦ crucial para promover ambientes online mais saud¨¢veis e inclusivos.

Esta competi??o ¨¦ inspirada pelo trabalho inovador de Jo?o A. Leite, Diego F. Silva, Kalina Bontcheva e Carolina Scarton (2020), que desenvolveram o dataset [Told-br](https://github.com/JAugusto97/ToLD-Br) um recurso valioso para o estudo da toxicidade em conte¨²do online em portugu¨ºs brasileiro. O objetivo desta competi??o ¨¦ convidar entusiastas de dados, cientistas e pesquisadores a desenvolver modelos de machine learning capazes de classificar tweets em portugu¨ºs brasileiro como t¨®xicos ou n?o t¨®xicos, utilizando o dataset Told-Br.

**Por que ¨¦ importante?**

A detec??o autom¨¢tica de linguagem t¨®xica tem implica??es significativas para a seguran?a e o bem-estar dos usu¨¢rios online. Ao identificar e mitigar discursos prejudiciais, podemos contribuir para a cria??o de espa?os digitais mais acolhedores e respeitosos, onde todos se sintam seguros para expressar suas ideias sem medo de ass¨¦dio ou discrimina??o. Al¨¦m disso, ao focar no portugu¨ºs brasileiro, esta competi??o destaca a necessidade de solu??es inclusivas e multil¨ªngues em tecnologias de modera??o de conte¨²do, garantindo que comunidades de l¨ªngua n?o inglesa tamb¨¦m sejam protegidas.

**Desafio para os Participantes:**

Convidamos voc¨º a mergulhar no dataset Told-Br e a aplicar suas habilidades anal¨ªticas e criativas para desenvolver modelos que n?o apenas sejam precisos na detec??o de conte¨²do t¨®xico, mas que tamb¨¦m sejam sens¨ªveis ao contexto cultural e lingu¨ªstico ¨²nico do portugu¨ºs brasileiro. Este ¨¦ um desafio complexo que requer uma compreens?o profunda de nuances lingu¨ªsticas e sociais, bem como uma abordagem ¨¦tica na constru??o de algoritmos.

**Pr¨ºmio**
- Os tr¨ºs primeiros colocados ser?o convidados para apresentar suas solu??es em um evento do Tensorflow User Group S?o Paulo;
- TBD (possibilidade de swags para os 3 primeiros colocados, iremos atualizar com mais informa??es)

**Restri??es**
- O Google n?o oferece suporte a competi??es na Crimeia, Coreia do Norte, Quebec e onde proibido por lei. Este programa segue os Termos das Competi??es de Codifica??o do Google, consulte a se??o 1. Elegibilidade nos regulamentos da competi??o para obter mais detalhes.
https://codingcompetitions.withgoogle.com/terms/
----------------------
# Evaluation

O problema envolve uma classifica??o bin¨¢ria de textos sendo t¨®xicos ou n?o t¨®xicos. A fim de manter a simplicidade da competi??o, a m¨¦trica a ser utilizada na avalia??o ser¨¢ a **acur¨¢cia**.

$$accuracy= \frac{TP +TN}{TP + TN + FP + FN}$$

## Submission File
Dentro da aba de dados voc¨º pode encontrar um arquivo de submiss?o de exemplos. Esse arquivo segue o formato abaixo:

    id,label
    0,0
    1,0
    2,1
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - dados de treino
*   **test.csv** - dados de teste (daqui ser?o calculados os resultados do leaderboard p¨²blico e privado)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - exemplo de arquivo de submiss?o no formato correto

## Columns

*   `id` - caso o dataset tenha id, ser¨¢ usado para identificar o exemplo na hora do c¨¢lculo da m¨¦trica para o leaderboard
*   `text` - tweet
*   `label`?- 0: n?o t¨®xico; 1: t¨®xico",False,True
7725,ml-olympiad-tfugsurabaya-2024,"# Overview

#**Welcome to the 2024 edition of ML Olympiad - Know Your Customer Opinion**
**ML Olympiad is an associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers.**

This competition is hosted by **TFUG Surabaya** and was prepared by TensorFlow User Group Surabaya Team:
1. Joan Santoso ([santosojoan](https://www.kaggle.com/santosojoan))
2. Esther Irawati Setiawan ([starismile](https://www.kaggle.com/starismile))
3. Ong, Hansel Santoso ([hanselsantoso](https://www.kaggle.com/hanselsantoso))
4. Billy Kelvianto Cahyadi  ([billykelvianto](https://www.kaggle.com/billykelvianto))
5. Patrick Sutanto ([dragooxdesu](https://www.kaggle.com/dragooxdesu))

This event has a collaboration with **GDG Surabaya and WTM Surabaya**. This event is supported by **Institut Sains dan Teknologi Terpadu Surabaya (ISTTS)**. This event also take a collaboration as media partner with **GDSC ISTTS and GDSC IBIKKG** !

`#MLOlympiad`

**Goal:** 
The goal of this competition is to classify each customer opinon into several Likert scale. You will work to develop a model that will train on our data to be able to classify each opinion into 1 to 5 likert scale using some machine learning Model.

**Dataset**
The dataset that is used in this competition was gathered by our team from Google Reviews and create it as dataset in this competition. This opinion was taken from restaurant in Surabaya.  The `train.tsv` was used for creating a model in training phase. The `test.tsv` will be used to produce a submission files.

**Prizes**
Swag prizes for top winners are sponsored by Google Developers. Top three winners will receive swags. Note that only winners from Indonesia will receive swags.
----------------------
# Description

ML Olympiad is an associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers. This year competition focus on the Sentiment Analysis Task for analyzing a review from restaurant in Surabaya.

## Dataset
The dataset that is used in this competition was divided into 2 parts.
- `train.tsv` consist of 3 column, which are id, review, and the label.
- `test.tsv` consist of 2 column, which are id and review.
Your task is to create a model to determine each opinion sentiment in likert scale from 1 to 5. The highest number represent that the customer is satisfied with the restaurant.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation that is used by this competition use Macro F1-Score. 
---

Submissions are evaluated on Macro F1-Score between the predicted result and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict for the output variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,LABEL
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset that is used in this competition was divided into 2 parts:
- `train.tsv` consist of 3 column, which are id, review, and the label.
- `test.tsv` consist of 2 column, which are id and review.

Your task is to create a model to determine each opinion sentiment in likert scale from 1 to 5. The highest number represent that the customer is satisfied with the restaurant.

## Files

*   **train.tsv** - the training set
*   **test.tsv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `ID` - definition of unique id
*   `REVIEW`?- definition of review that will be used in this competition
*   `LABEL`?- definition of target label that will be used to create a model or output from predicted model in this competition",False,True
7726,24-exercise1,"# Overview

In this exercise, you'll delve into the world of wearable technology, focusing on heart rate (HR) monitoring features found in devices from brands like Apple, Samsung, Polar, and Garmin. These devices rely on reflective photoplethysmography (PPG) sensors for HR estimation, offering non-invasive, all-day monitoring capabilities. Your challenge is to interpret raw PPG signal data, recorded over an entire day, to accurately extract HR information. The term ""raw"" here means the data is exactly as it was captured by the sensor, without any preprocessing. You'll need to tackle the complexities of maintaining high accuracy in HR estimation, especially when faced with poor signal quality due to factors like intense physical activity or different device positioning.

**Before getting started, make sure you have read the accompanying PDF that describes this exercise.**

**Interpreting the Leaderboard**
The public leaderboard is based on your predictions for samples from Phases 0, 2, and 4. The private leaderboard, which will be used for grading, is based on your algorithm's performance for samples from Phases 1, 3, and 5. Since the ground-truth labels for Phases 0, 2, and 4 have been released, be cautious when drawing conclusions based on the public leaderboard. The rankings and results on the private leaderboard may significantly differ.
----------------------
# Description

In the tabs above, you can navigate through the different functions of this competition.

- In the *Data* section, you can find the dataset of this competition and an example notebook called *groupXX_PPGHR.ipynb*. In the example notebook, we give examples of how to load and plot the data, calculate the evaluation metric, and create the *submission.csv* file for this competition on Kaggle.
- In the *Code* section, you can create a new notebook and directly work here on Kaggle with this notebook without having to install anything on your computer.
- The *Models* section is not relevant to this competition.
- In the *Discussion* section, you can ask questions and discuss. However, we encourage you to ask all questions on Moodle and only guarantee to answer your questions on Moodle.
- In the *Leaderboard* section, you can see how your score with your predicted heart rates compares to other teams of this course. We explain the evaluation metric below.
- In the *Rules* section, we repeat the rules of this exercise.
- In the *Teams* section, you can form your team with a maximum of two other team members.

----------------------
# Advice

- We recommend ignoring the accelerometer data at first and only starting to incorporate it once you have a fully working algorithm based on PPG data alone. Our baseline algorithms achieved full points without the use of accelerometer data at all.
- Our testing indicates that the difficulty of accurate HR extraction varies by phase. You may find it helpful to first test your algorithm during periods when the PPG data quality is high.
- PPG HR detection is a well-understood problem and you will find many resources online to help you solve this problem. While we do not recommend you to re-invent the wheel, it is advisable to really understand methods if you plan on using them. Usually, this is a prerequisite to being able to tweak an algorithm for maximum performance.
- As a starting pointer, you may want to use some functionalities of the scipy package. More specifically, it provides functions for filtering the signal and the function find_peaks(), which can be helpful in detecting peaks in the PPG signal and offers a range of parameters to optimize. Please note: These are helping pointers for students with limited signal processing experience and not necessarily pointers toward an optimal solution.
- Plot, plot, plot, and then do some more plotting. Visualize everything you are doing - is it what you expect? Can you identify signal sections where your algorithm fails? Why? In signal processing and data analysis as a whole, plotting is key to success. Just assuming that something works is usually not a great strategy. The recordings are long and therefore interactive plots are recommended (with the option to zoom and move around). A possible method to do so locally on your computer is explained in the example notebook.
----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate your results, we first calculate the absolute difference between your predicted and the ground truth heart rates for each 30-second window (Error). Afterward, we calculate your final score (FinalScore) as the mean of the mean error and the median error between your predictions and the ground truth heart rates:

$$ \text{Error} = \text{abs}(\text{PredictedHR} - \text{GroundTruthHR}) $$

$$ \text{FinalScore} = 0.5 \text{mean}(\text{Error}) + 0.5 \text{median}(\text{Error}) $$

Note that the HR may not be constant throughout the 30¨Csecond window you compute it for. The ground truth HR is equivalent to the average HR over the window.


## Submission File
To obtain a score on the leaderboard, at least one of your team has to submit a *submission.csv* file, which consists of two columns with the names ""Id"" and ""Predicted"". The column ""Id"" contains the name of the phase and a counter of your predicted windows in the format displayed below. The column ""Predicted"" contains your predicted heart rate for the corresponding window as a float. **The displayed heart rates below are only examples and are not the correct heart rates!**

In the *example_notebook.ipynb*, we have prepared an example of how to create a correct *submission.csv* file so that you do not have to code that yourself.

    Id, Predicted
    phase0_0, 70.12
    phase0_1, 130.5
    phase0_2, 51.3
    etc.
----------------------
# Final Submission

Follow the three steps below to submit your solution:
1. Include **all relevant** code in the groupXX_PPGHR.ipynb and replace XX with your group number from the Google sheet (i.e., group03_PPGHR.ipynb for group 3). Follow the instructions at the top of the notebook to enter your student ID, email, and Kaggle Team ID. If run in Kaggle, the notebook (without importing any other custom scripts) should create the submission file you want to be graded.
2. Make sure to score your final submission before the deadline. From all your entries on the Kaggle leaderboard, choose the one you wish to be evaluated. This submission must originate from your ""groupXX_PPGHR.ipynb"" notebook. Mark your chosen submission on Kaggle by selecting the checkbox next to it. Afterward, download the submission file and rename it to ""groupXX_submission.csv"". We included a grace period of 15 minutes. At 12:15pm on April 8 (this is harsh on the second), Kaggle will lock preventing further uploads or changes to your chosen submission for grading.
3. Place the groupXX_PPGHR.ipynb file and the groupXX_submission.csv file in a .zip folder called groupXX_ex1.zip and **upload the zipped folder** to this polybox: https://polybox.ethz.ch/index.php/s/EVB3FTmyaxAZpFI. You can upload as often as you like, all uploaded versions will be saved. To keep it tidy, however, please try to only upload your final submission.","# Dataset Description

The data set is stored in the file *mhealth24_data_public.npy*. You can either download it from here and work locally on your computer or directly work with it in a notebook in this competition on Kaggle.

In the *groupXX_PPGHR.ipynb* example notebook, which you can download here, we show in detail how you can load and plot the data, and create a submission file for this Kaggle competition. You have to download the notebook from here and you can then upload it in the *Code* section to use it.

The total recording length is 14 hours and 9 minutes. It is split into 6 phases. The ground truth HR data is only provided for phases 0, 2, and 4 (training data), phases 1, 3, and 5 will be used for testing and grading (test data). In phases 0 and 3, you are provided with PPG and accelerometer data from the wrist, in phases 1, 2, 4, and 5 with PPG and accelerometer data from the forehead.

The data set contains the following sensor data:
- Raw PPG recordings from the forehead and the wrist
   - sampling rate: 128 Hz
   - 19-bit unsigned integer (possible values in the interval [0, 524288])
   - These measurements do not have a unit but correspond to the light intensity measured by a photodiode in the PPG sensor while a green LED was on.
- Accelerometer data from the forehead and the wrist
   - sampling rate: 128 Hz
   - 3-axes (X, Y, Z) 12-bit signed integer (possible values in the interval [-2040, 2040])
   - 2040 corresponds to a measurement of +2g, -2040 is -2g
   - orientation of the axes is not given.
- Ground Truth HR data (only provided for half the data)
   - as a list of values, each element of which corresponds to the average heart rate in a 30-second window.",False,True
7727,ooocean-eeeddy-detection,"# Description

Welcome to Mercator Ocean AI competition. You chose this topic as you may be interested in geophysics and environmental field. Do not hesitate to take contact with Mercator Ocean team members to know more about our activities and missions.

You will find below some useful information about your project. 

Oceanic Eddies are  vortices of the order of ~10km in horizontal scale, whose signature is clearly visible in satellite products and well reproduced in ocean models. Sea Level Anomalies (SLA) are used for detecting such object by means of numerical techniques, yet such approach lacks accuracy in detection, namely because Sea Level Anomaly contains errors. Eddy signature is also visible in other variables such as Sea surface Temperature (SST) that do not suffer from such limitation.

The objective here is to develop a Deep Learning approach to identify eddies using SST, SLA and the ocean velocity field. For this purpose you have a dataset consisting of  SLA, SST, and velocity components images which are slightly distorted relative to reality (we call this distorted reality - the reanalysis product), together with labels (the eddy contours, see figure) obtained directly from reality.

Since we can not obtain the real state of the ocean, the so called reality comes from it's virtual emulation. Consequently, we suppose that the ocean is a perfect fluid which can be described by primitive equations (e.g. Navier-Stokes). This virtual reality is then considered as a real time ocean state. 

On the other hand, in reality oceanographers have to model the ocean dynamics driven among other by various sources forcing, such as wind or atmosphere-ocean interactions. Furthermore, thanks to the remote sensing advances, a significant amount of satellite tracked and in-situ observations data are available all around the globe. This data is extremely useful as it is the only source providing the knowledge of the real ocean dynamics. In operational oceanography the process of incorporating the real ocean observations (remote and in-situ) into the modeled ocean an assimilation. However, the assimilation process is in it's core a numerically challenging task, firstly due to the sparse spatial and temporal distribution of observations, and secondly due to the resolution issues. It is worth mentioning, that a number of compromises have to be accepted during the assimilation process so that the modeled ocean contains as much real observations data as possible whilst the overall flui dynamics is not disturbed. Those compromises are source of inaccuracies in modeled ocean representation. Operational oceanography is in constant research of novel ways of data assimialtion, model validation and correction. 

This project presents a big interest for operational oceanography regarding the importance of a reliable ocean dynamics modeling for real time analysis,  forecast, as well as for numerous applications, for instance floating trajectories simulation. being able to detect eddies in the distorted virtual reality, called here and after "" NatRun OSSE reanalysis "", would provide a novel way for oceanographers to detect eddies in real modeled ocean, called here and after "" Reanalysis product "". Thus, your contribution may open a brand new way of reanalysis product validation through AI eddies detection framework.  
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Classification Accuracy, called from now on "" Acc "". 

The accuracy is estimated as follows : 

$$ Acc =  \frac{\text{Nb of correct predictions} }{\text{total Nb of predictions}} $$

This metric will concern all three classes, as so you will be evaluated regarding three values : 
$$ Acc_{cyclonic}, Acc_{anti-cyclonic}, Acc_{no-eddies} $$","# Dataset Description

In this section we will explain you the data and how you can use it. 
As described in Overview section, the main goal of the project is to build a model performing segmentation through supervised learning. FYI, we will study the Gulf Stream region. 
Data from various sources is available : classified eddies maps from PyEddyTracker tool run on NatRun product (virtual reality ~ real ocean); physical variables from NatRun OSSE 3NADIRS product (distorted reality ~ modeled ocean). Thus, we aim to train a model which detects eddies from the distorted reality, i.e. NatRun OSSE 3NADIRS product (as generally it is the only information about the ocean state available), given the ground-truth provided by a numerical (statistical) tool. 

You will work here with .nc files, this is a usual format for climatology and geophysical data storage. Each .nc file contains dimensions (coordinates, e.g. Latitude, Longitude, Time) and associated variables (velocities, temperature, salinity, sea surface height). A useful tool to work with such file format is xarray library. 

## Files

*   **eddies_train.nc** - the training set of eddies maps for the given region
*   **OSSE_U_V_SLA_SST_train.nc** - the training set of OSSE 3NADIRS physical variables for the given region
*   **OSSE_U_V_SLA_SST_test.nc** - the test set of OSSE 3NADIRS physical variables for the given region
*   **submission_example.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

The eddies maps are images of [Time, Latitude, Longitude] dimension. Each map (image) is regularly sampled, one cell corresponds to 1/12¡ã (~100km). They contain the information on the eddy type per individual pixel (cell). We distinguish 3 classes (that should be predicted by your model) : 0 - no eddy detected, 1 - cyclonic eddy, 2 - anti-cyclonic eddy. An additional fourth class is given to take into account the coast (Gulf Stream being closely situated to the coast), we gave it a 999 identifier. Your model is not supposed to predict this class, however it should be able to process the ground correctly. 
Physical variables are also given as images of the same spatio-temporal resolution as eddies maps. Note that in physical variables maps the ground corresponds to NaN values. It is important to be careful with NaN values while training your model, take time to brainstorm with your team mates on how you can work with such values (pre-processing / mask / etc). You will find following variables in your .nc files : vozocrtxT - horizontal velocity component; vomecrtyT - vertical velocity component; sossheig - Sea Surface Height (SSH); votemper - Sea Surface Temperature (SST). You do not need to use all physical variables at a time. Take time to test and conclude on the influence of each variable on your model's performance (both in prediction ability and computational effectiveness). 


## Columns in your submission file
Your submission file should contains two columns : Id and Predicted. The Id column corresponds to each individual pixel of each spatio-temporal map. Each Id is a string: DayNumber_LatitudePixelNumber_LongitudePixelNumber. For this purpose, once eddies types are predicted (attention to the fourth ground's class), you should flatten your final predicted array of size [Time, Latitude, Longitude] to the new size [Time x Latitude x Longitude], fill the 'Predicted' column in the corresponding .csv file and add the corresponding Id column.  Please, refer to our team members for further details if necessary.",False,True
7728,aist4010-spring2024-a2,"# Description

This is the Kaggle part of Assignment 2.

In this task, your system will be given raw sequence protein sequences (translated from genes) as input. First, your system needs to classify the input into ARG or non-ARG. Then if the input is a non-ARG, your system needs to output the class 'nonarg'. If the input is an ARG, your system needs go futher to predict resistant antibiotic type. Here we have 14 antibiotic families. The system should decide which family the predicted ARG resistant to.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric is macro F-score. 

## Submission Format

The submission '.csv' file should contain two columns: `id` and `label`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,label
SEQ0,10
SEQ1,12
SEQ2,9
...,...
```","# Dataset Description

- `train.fasta`: You are supposed to use train data set to train your model for the task.
- `val.fasta`: You are supposed to use val data to validate the classification F-score.
- `test.fasta`: You are supposed to assign classes for images in test data and submit your result.
- `sample-submission.csv`: This is a sample submission file for this competition.",False,True
7729,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-c57,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.
----------------------
# FAQs

### How do I contact support?

For this competition, any questions, concerns, or technical difficulties will be addressed at the Discussion forum on UpGrad Learn platform. Any discussion posted regarding support on this competition page will have to be redirected to the same portal.

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

The Kaggle leaderboard has a public and private component to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

**Public Leaderboard:** For all participants, the same 70% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the public leaderboard. The score you see on the public leaderboard reflects your model¡¯s accuracy on this portion of the test set.

**Private Leaderboard:** The other 30% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the private leaderboard. The private leaderboard is not visible to participants until the competition has concluded. At the end of a competition, we will reveal the private leaderboard so you can see your score on the other 30% of the test data. The scores on the private leaderboard are used to determine the competition winners.

### What are kernels?

Kaggle Kernels is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels support scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the Kernels tab to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
7730,spr-head-ct-age-prediction-challenge,"# Overview

The [Radiology and Diagnostic Imaging Society of S?o Paulo (SPR)](http://www.spr.org.br) is organizing the Artificial Intelligence Challenge to determine age using head computed tomographies. All healthcare professionals and data scientists worldwide are invited to participate.

The challenge is to create a model capable of predicting the patient's age group through head CTs. To ensure the success of this activity, SPR invited institutions to collaborate with the donation of thousands of images; this data allows participants to create artificial intelligence algorithms to indicate the patient's age.

This action aims to encourage initiatives to identify age biomarkers from imaging studies, in addition to promoting scientific and educational activities for the Radiology and Diagnostic Imaging community.

The top three winners of the competition will be acknowledged during Jornada Paulista de Radiologia (JPR) 2024. All participants are invited to [register ](https://www.jpr.org.br/) and participate in JPR.

## About SPR

For 55 years, SPR has promoted scientific updating and continuing education within the highest principles of professionalism.

With an eye on the future, the organization is often looking to bring dynamism to its work and great prominence to everyone in its community, aiming to be well-recognized nationally and intentionally.

SPR is responsible for the S?o Paulo Radiological Meeting (JPR), its most significant event, and is also considered the largest congress of the specialty in Latin America.

JPR2024 will take place in the Transamerica Expo Center in S?o Paulo - SP, on May 2-5.




----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean absolute error ([MAE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error))


## Submission Format

**For every study in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `StudyID` and `age`. 

Age should be a number of years old.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
StudyID,age
1,23
```

