7736,ml-olympiad-ai-powered-job-description-generator,"# Overview

Efficiently crafting job descriptions is crucial for businesses but often requires considerable time
and effort. Your challenge is to simplify this task by developing an AI-powered system that
generates complete job descriptions from minimal input, using an interactive chatbot to collect
this information.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Requirements:
1. Chatbot Interface
Develop an interactive chatbot that engages users in a conversation to gather essential details
about the job, including or more(as per your knowledge and research):
- Job Title
- Company Overview
- Key Responsibilities
- Required Skills and Qualifications
- Desired Characteristics
- Job Location (or if it's remote)
- Salary Range (optional)
The chatbot should be designed to ask questions in a friendly and engaging manner, guiding the
user through the input process without it feeling like a chore.

2. AI Model
Leverage a Generative AI model to generate job descriptions from the inputs collected by the
chatbot. Focus on integrating the chatbot with the AI model seamlessly, ensuring that the
conversation flow leads to comprehensive and accurate job descriptions.

3. Integration and Output
Ensure the chatbot and AI model work together fluently, generating a job description
immediately after collecting all necessary information. Allow users to review, edit, and approve
the final job description directly in the chat interface.

4. Feedback Mechanism

Incorporate a feedback option within the chatbot conversation, allowing users to provide
immediate feedback on the generated job description. Use this feedback to continually improve
the system.","# Dataset Description

Description
This dataset contains information about job postings on LinkedIn. The data is divided into several files, each containing different aspects of the job postings:

job_postings.csv: This file contains detailed information about each job posting, including the job title, description, salary, work type, location, and more.
companies.csv: This file contains detailed information about each company that posted a job, including the company name, website, description, size, location, and more.
company_industries.csv: This file contains the industries associated with each company.
company_specialities.csv: This file contains the specialties associated with each company.
employee_counts.csv: This file contains the employee and follower counts for each company.
benefits.csv: This file contains the benefits associated with each job.
job_industries.csv: This file contains the industries associated with each job.
job_skills.csv: This file contains the skills associated with each job.
Usage
This dataset can be used for various purposes such as:

Analyzing the job market
Analyzing company trends
Analyzing salary trends
Building a job recommendation system
Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks such as keyword extraction, topic modeling, etc.
Acknowledgements
This dataset was collected from LinkedIn. Please note that the data may be subject to LinkedIn's terms of use.",False,True
7737,inventum-2k24-practice-test,"# Overview

The classification problem for this hackathon involves training a machine learning model to correctly classify images of famous ships in history. The training set contains 816 images of size 500x500, while the testing set contains 541 images of the same size.

Participants are required to create a model that takes an image as input and predicts the correct label for that image. The labels are provided in a CSV file that contains two columns: image_id and label. The label column contains the correct label for each image.

Participants can use any machine learning algorithm or technique they prefer to build their model. They may choose to use deep learning models such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs), or traditional machine learning models such as decision trees, support vector machines (SVMs), or random forests.
----------------------
# Description

This is a practice test.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Classification Accuracy  will be used as the evaluation criteria.","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of photographs depicting renowned ships from history, such as the Titanic, INS Vikrant, USS Arizona, and Wonder of the Seas. Each image is sized at 500x500 pixels. The training set consists of 816 pictures, while the testing set has 541 images. The train.csv file contains the image_id in the first column, and the second column presents the label.

Files

train.csv - the training set

image - the image ID.

labels - 0 for Titanic, 1 for Vikrant, 2 for wonderseas and 3 for Battleship USS Arizona

sample_submission.csv - A sample submission file in the correct format.

image

labels

train_images - The training set images.

test_images - The test set images.",False,True
