7741,machine-failure-prediction-iti-data-science,"# Overview

# Machine Failure Prediction Competition

Welcome to the Machine Failure Prediction competition! This challenge focuses on leveraging Machine Learning to predict the likelihood of machine failures based on a variety of features. Participants are tasked with creating models that can accurately forecast potential downtimes, enhancing maintenance strategies and reducing operational disruptions.

## Competition Overview

In this competition, you will use a dataset containing several features related to machine operation and performance. Your goal is to develop a predictive model that can identify the probability of failure in these machines, enabling preemptive action to avoid costly breakdowns.

### Why Participate?

- **Skill Development**: Hone your ML skills, particularly in predictive modeling and time series analysis.
- **Real-world Application**: Contribute to solving practical problems faced by industries relying heavily on machinery.
- **Community and Collaboration**: Engage with a community of data scientists and ML practitioners to share insights and learn together.



**Goal:** Your task is to build a model that predicts the probability of machine failure. By participating, you'll explore the intricacies of machine learning forecasting, tackle a real-world issue, and contribute to improving industrial efficiency.

Good luck, and we look forward to seeing your innovative solutions!


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

 ```
id,Machine failure
136429,0.5
136430,0.1
136431,0.9
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

**Dataset Description**

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the Machine Failure Predictions. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

**Files**
- train.csv - the training dataset; Machine failure is the (binary) target (which, in order to align with the ordering of the original dataset, is not in the last column position)
- test.csv - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of Machine failure
- sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7742,aiml-general-championship,"# Overview



Dermoscopic lesion imaging, like many diagnostic medical exams, produces a long-tailed distribution of clinical findings; while a small subset of diseases are routinely observed, the vast majority of diseases are relatively rare. This poses a challenge for standard AIML methods, which exhibit bias toward the most common classes at the expense of the important, but rare, ¡°tail¡± classes. Many existing methods have been proposed to tackle this specific type of imbalance, though only recently with attention to long-tailed medical image recognition problems.

Diagnosis on dermoscopic lesion images is a multi-class problem, as each image is associated with a single disease class. This dataset has 10, 015 skin images with 7 lesion classes. This dataset is characterized by an imbalance ratio (IR) of 58, where IR is defined as the ratio of the sample size of the largest majority class and that of the smallest minority class. Thus the larger the value of IR, the larger the imbalance extent.

![lesion imaging](https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Aimilios-Lallas/publication/236336605/figure/fig4/AS:669293918298134@1536583513722/Dermoscopy-in-the-treatment-selection-and-follow-up-A-Clinically-this-superficial_Q640.jpg)

Since most large-scale image classification benchmarks contain single-label images with a mostly balanced distribution of labels, many standard AIML methods fail to accommodate the class imbalance problem posed by the long-tailed nature of tasks like disease diagnosis on dermoscopic lesion images.

To develop a benchmark for long-tailed, multi-class medical image classification, we will use the HAM10000 Dataset that was acquired with a variety of dermatoscope types that are categorized into one of seven possible disease categories.


----------------------
# Description

We are thankful to Dr. Raghvendra as well as KCDH for their efforts in curating the dataset as well as the problem statement for this General Championship.

#**Goal**


Submit automated predictions of disease classification within dermoscopic images.
Possible disease categories are:


1. [Melanoma (MEL)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Melanoma)
2. [Melanocytic nevus (NV)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Benign_Melanocytic_lesions)
3. [Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Basal_cell_carcinoma)
4. [Actinic keratosis / Bowen¡¯s disease (intraepithelial carcinoma) (AKIEC)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Actinic_keratosis_/_Bowen%27s_disease_/_keratoacanthoma_/_squamous_cell_carcinoma)
5. [Benign keratosis (solar lentigo / seborrheic keratosis / lichen planus-like keratosis) (BKL)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Solar_lentigines_/_seborrheic_keratoses_/_lichen_planus-like_keratosis)
6. [Dermatofibroma (DF)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Dermatofibromas)
7. [Vascular lesion (VASC)](https://dermoscopedia.org/Vascular_lesions)


#**Getting Started**

Please review the detailed guidelines in the Data, Rules, and Evaluation sections. Download the provided dataset, develop your model, and submit your predictions before the competition deadline. We encourage open discussion and collaboration among participants on the competition's discussion forum.


----------------------
# Evaluation

#**Goal Metric**

The metric: Balanced Accuracy Score

The balanced accuracy in binary and multiclass classification problems to deal with imbalanced datasets. It is defined as the average of recall obtained on each class.

**This is the sole metric that will be considered to determine your ranking.**

#**Other Metrics**


Participants will be ranked based only on the multiclass accuracy metric. However, for scientific completeness, predicted responses will also have the following metrics computed (comparing prediction vs. ground truth) for each image:

##**Individual Category Metrics**

* [sensitivity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sensitivity_and_specificity#Sensitivity)
* [specificity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sensitivity_and_specificity#Specificity)
* [accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification)
* [area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic#Area_under_the_curve)
* [mean average precision](http://fastml.com/what-you-wanted-to-know-about-mean-average-precision/)
* [F1 score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score)
* average AUC across all diagnoses
* [positive predictive value (PPV)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Positive_and_negative_predictive_values)
* [negative predictive value (NPV)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Positive_and_negative_predictive_values)
----------------------
# Submission Format

For each ID (or name of the image) in the test set, you must predict the corresponding class. The submission file should be a CSV having the following format (header must be included):

The header should be the text written except the brackets.

    ID(name of the image),Class(number of image)
    image a,0
    image b,1
    image c,2
    image d,3
    image e,4
    image f,5
    image g,6

Here, the numbers correspond to the classes in the same order as they are given in the ground truth file, which is:
0 - MEL
1 - NV
2 - BCC
3 - AKIEC
4 - BKL
5 - DF
6 - VASC","# Dataset Description

#**Input Data**


The input data are dermoscopic lesion images in JPEG format.

All lesion images are named using the scheme ISIC_ .jpg , where  is a 7-digit unique identifier. EXIF tags in the images have been removed; any remaining EXIF tags should not be relied upon to provide accurate metadata.

The lesion images come from the HAM10000 Dataset, and were acquired with a variety of dermatoscope types, from all anatomic sites (excluding mucosa and nails), from a historical sample of patients presented for skin cancer screening, from several different institutions. Images were collected with approval of the Ethics Review Committee of University of Queensland (Protocol-No. 2017001223) and Medical University of Vienna (Protocol-No. 1804/2017).

The distribution of disease states represent a modified ""real world"" setting whereby there are more benign lesions than malignant lesions, but an over-representation of malignancies.


#**Ground Truth Provenance**


As detailed in the HAM10000 Dataset description, diagnosis ground truth were established by one of the following methods:

* Histopathology
* Reflectance confocal microscopy
* Lesion did not change during digital dermatoscopic follow up over two years with at least three images
* Consensus of at least three expert dermatologists from a single image
* In all cases of malignancy, disease diagnoses were histopathologically confirmed.



#**Supporting papers**

(1) Robust Asymmetric Loss for Multi-Label Long-Tailed Learning ([Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05542) / [Code](https://github.com/kalelpark/RAL))
(2) Focal Loss for Dense Object Detection ([Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.02002v2) / [Code]https://github.com/unsky/focal-loss))
(3) Asymmetric Loss For Multi-Label Classification ([Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.14119) / [Code](https://github.com/Alibaba-MIIL/ASL))
(4) Simple and Robust Loss Design for Multi-Label Learning with Missing Labels ([Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.07368) / [Code](https://github.com/xinyu1205/robust-loss-mlml))



## Files

*   **Training Images** - The training images are stored in ""KCDH2024_Training_Input_10K"" folder. All of the images are of .jpg type.
*   **KCDH2024_Training_GroundTruth.xlsx** - The spreadsheet containing the ground truth of the training images, it has 8 columns with the first being the image name in the ""KCDH2024_Training_Input_10K"" folder and the corresponding Boolean values, with a ""1"" means that the image belongs to the category of that particular column.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **KCDH2024_Training_LesionGroupings.xlsx** - It is an additional information about the training images. You may or may not use it for building your model.

## Columns

*   `id` - The ID or name of the Image as corresponded in the **KCDH2024_Training_GroundTruth.xlsx**
*   `prediction`?- The predicted class of the image.",False,True
7743,isit-2024-bits-and-bots,"# Overview

Web traffic today is increasingly dominated by automated agents and web scanners, with the share of automated traffic almost equaling the share of human traffic as of 2022. Detecting malicious automated agents in real-time amidst legitimate web traffic presents an urgent and critical challenge posing problems in the intersection of information theory, statistics, machine learning, and cybersecurity. The ISIT Bits and Bots Competition focuses on this problem through a set of multidisciplinary tasks. The competition leverages a dataset of more than two million events collected over several months, capturing the diverse website activities of human users and automated agents in a real-world setting.

----------------------
# Description

The International Symposium of Information Theory (ISIT) Bits and Bots Competition focuses on the critical challenge of real-time bot detection and attribution amidst human web traffic. As automated agents become more sophisticated, distinguishing them from human users is vital for web security and privacy. This task can be posed as a hypothesis-testing problem, and its potential solutions lie in the intersection of cybersecurity, information theory, and machine learning.
The dataset for the competition has been collected over several months by monitoring the user interactions with a real-world website. These interactions include mouse movements, scrolls, clicks, clipboard actions (copy, paste, and cut), among other interactions. In addition to human visitors, we have simulated automated agent website visits using various off-the-shelf bot models, including Gremlins [1], HLISA [2] and ZAP [3].

The competition involves three main tasks: two defensive and one offensive task.
In the defensive tasks, the competitors are required to design an algorithm for the host to detect the bots
In the offensive task, the competitors are required to design an algorithm for the bots to elude the host detection. 
More precisely, the first two tasks are defensive tasks in which the competitors are required to design hypothesis testing mechanisms to detect bots and attribute their type based on mouse movements only (unimodal defense in Task 1.A) and mouse movements and other interaction event types (multimodal defense in Task 1.B). The last task is an offensive task in which automated agents are designed to produce mouse movements  that elude detection mechanisms. The performance of the defense tasks are evaluated using an evaluation dataset available to the organizers (hidden dataset). 
The performance of the defensive task is measured over the hidden dataset in terms of accuracy and time-to-detection. 
The performance of the offense task is evaluated by comparing the detection accuracy of other teams¡¯ detectors from Task 1, when applied to the automated agent of a given team in Task 2. A complete description of the dataset and competition task are given in the following sections.

[1] Pierre Haller Richard and Maxime. 2020. Gremlins.js 2: The new batch. https://marmelab.com/blog/2020/06/02/gremlins-2.html

[2] Daniel Go?en, Hugo Jonker, Stefan Karsch, Benjamin Krumnow, and David Roefs. 2021. HLISA: Towards a more reliable measurement tool. In Proceedings of the 21st ACM Internet Measurement Conference. 380¨C389

[3] OWASP. 2017. OWASP Benchmark. https://www.owasp.org/index.php/ Benchmark. Available: https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Benchmark
----------------------
# Dataset Description

The dataset provides a comprehensive record of user interactions on a specific website, capturing both human and bot activities. This  data was collected over a time-span ranging from January 8, 2023, to February 27, 2023, and encompasses a total of 2,085,711 recorded events. Eighty-five percent of the collected data is shared with the competitors for training and test purposes, and the remaining fifteen percent will be used for evaluation purposes. 


A complete description of the dataset can be found in the following [link](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/isit-2024-bits-and-bots/data), and the corresponding data loaders are given in this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/code/tebs89/bit-and-bots-dataloader/notebook). 
----------------------
# Competition Tasks

The competition consists of two main tasks, with the first task divided into unimodal and multimodal subtasks.

The first task is a defensive task which involves detecting automated agents and attributing the type of the automated agent. The first subtask uses only mouse movement data for detection and attribution (unimodal), whereas the second subtask uses other event types such as clicks, scrolls, and clipboard action information (multimodal).

The objective of the second task is the development of a bot capable of emulating human behavior well enough to avoid detection. Since generation is generally considered a more difficult task, this task will only require the generation of unimodal data. A complete description of the tasks is given in the following. 


## 1.A. Defense Task: Detection and Attribution Using Unimodal Data
### Objective
The objective of this task is to develop a hypothesis test capable of classifying visitor types based on the collected data. The hypothesis test can be implemented using conventional machine learning and statistical testing techniques such as hidden Markov models, random forests, support vector machines, neural networks, etc. In this first task, the model is only allowed to use mouse movement data along with time-differences  as input to the classifier. In other words, only the `time_diff` and `position [x,y]`  elements recorded for each event can be used as input to the classifier during the test and evaluation phases. For ease of implementation, the preprocessing snippet extracts the data which is allowed to be used in this step into the  *df_dict_init_unimodal* dataframe.

The competitors should write a program that takes a randomly selected sub-dataframe of `df_dict_init_unimodal[name]` dataframe as input for a `name` chosen randomly and uniformly among the user types, and outputs its estimate of `name` (i.e., attributes the input to a class type. The objective is to minimize the length of recorded mouse positions required for detection with a desired error probability. The input and output specifications are summarized in the following:

#### Input
- `name` chosen randomly and uniformly from `[¡®hlisa_traces¡¯, ¡®gremlins¡¯, ¡®za_proxy¡¯, ¡®survey_desktop¡¯, ¡®random_mouse_with_sleep_bot¡¯]`
-  Randomly and uniformly chosen starting index `idx` chosen from  `range(len(df_dict_init_unimodal[name]))`. This indicates the starting point of monitoring a specific visitor for attribution and detection.
- The dataframe values starting from the randomly chosen index `df_dict_init_unimodal[name][idx:]`

#### Output
- An estimate `estimated_name` of the type of the user from `[¡®hlisa_traces¡¯, ¡®gremlins¡¯, ¡®za_proxy¡¯, ¡®survey_desktop¡¯, ¡®random_mouse_with_sleep_bot¡¯]`
- Number of events read before outputting the name estimate (the lower the better).

#### Deliverables
The following plots are required (plots provided with examples for further illustration)

1.  Uni-Modal Classification Accuracy (Accuracy of correct class attribution among all classes)
