# Description

![dimantitos](https://media.giphy.com/media/46rXT61bNDxAc/giphy.gif)

The goal of this competition is the prediction of the price of diamonds based on their characteristics (weight, color, quality of cut, etc.), putting into practice all the machine learning techniques you know.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric chosen for this competition is the RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error):

## Submit results

- Submissions are limited to 5 times a day.
- The test set is divided into a public part (with which the public leaderboard is calculated, accessible during the competition) and another private part, with which the final positions are calculated, after the end of the competition.
- Predictions will be sent in the format indicated in the `sample_submission.csv` file in the data section.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3824396%2Ff6235a3402992f0fc94af9e0ca3465c2%2Finbox_3824396_4554b5824665256889dafdd5c4c59890_Bbf0GWk.jpg?generation=1588773703548655&amp;alt=media)","# Dataset Description

### Data

In this section you will find data needed to train your model and a detailed description of it.

### Files

- `train.csv`: training set
- `test.csv`: test set
- `sample_submission.csv`: sample submission

### Features

- **id**: only for test & sample submission files, id for prediction sample identification
- **price**: price in USD
- **carat**: weight of the diamond
- **cut**: quality of the cut (Fair, Good, Very Good, Premium, Ideal)
- **color**: diamond colour
- **clarity**: a measurement of how clear the diamond is
- **x**: length in mm
- **y**: width in mm
- **z**: depth in mm
- **depth**: total depth percentage = z / mean(x, y) = 2 * z / (x + y) (43--79)
- **table**: width of top of diamond relative to widest point (43--95)",False,True
7748,mis-466-kaggle-practice,"# Overview

The objective of this competition is to predict the breast cancer status of patients - whether it is benign or malignant - by utilizing the available features.
----------------------
# Description

## Evaluation Metric  

The evaluation metric for this competition is Accuracy.   


## Submission Format   

**For every data record in the testing dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `ID` and `Class`. 
The file should contain a header and have the following format:    

```   
ID,Class   
1,2  
2,4  
3,4  
4,2  
5,4  
6,4  
7,4  
8,4  
9,2  
10,4  
............  
............  
```","# Dataset Description

You have been provided with the following files.

## Files

*   **breast-cancer-train.csv** - the training set and validation set
*   **breast-cancer-test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.txt** - supplemental information about the data

## Columns

*   `ID` - Sample code number: id numbers  
*   `Clump Thickness`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Uniformity of Cell Size`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Uniformity of Cell Shape`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Marginal Adhesion`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Single Epithelial Cell Size`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Bare Nuclei`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Bland Chromatin`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Normal Nucleoli`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Mitoses`?- Domain: 1-10  
*   `Class`?- 2 or 4, 2 for benign, 4 for malignant  
  
Note:  
  
- Some of the rows of the table may have some of these values missing  
- The prediciton goal is `Class`  
- Class distribution:    Benign: 65.5%, Malignant: 241 (34.5%)  

## Dataset Citation  
Dua, D. and Graff, C. (2019). UCI Machine Learning Repository [http://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml]. Irvine, CA: University of California, School of Information and Computer Science.",False,True
7749,forecasting-coffee-production-2024,"# Overview

Bienvenidos a la primera entrega del proyecto del curso de Gerencia de Producci¨®n
----------------------
# Description

""El regreso de la cultura del caf¨¦ de calidad superior est¨¢ empoderando a los agricultores colombianos para forjar un mejor futuro"" en la regi¨®n del Caqueta. ""Con el fin de ayudarlos a recuperar el cultivo del caf¨¦ de manera sostenible, la Federaci¨®n Nacional de Cafeteros (FNC) se asoci¨® con el Programa AAA Sustainable Quality? de Nespresso para apoyar a los caficultores de manera que puedan mejorar la calidad del caf¨¦, fortalecer la producci¨®n y tener un acceso m¨¢s amplio al mercado internacional."" (National Geographic - Mayo 2020)

En el marco de este programa, en la regi¨®n se esta fomentando la producci¨®n de la variedad Esperanza Colombia, un caf¨¦ amaz¨®nico que busca posicionarse ante el mundo, la cual se cultiva en la mitad de las hect¨¢reas disponibles en el departamento. La otra mitad se siembra con variedades m¨¢s tradicionales en el pa¨ªs, la variedad Caturra y la variedad Castillo.

Sin embargo, el mercado internacional ha presentado variaciones importantes en los ¨²ltimos 20 a?os, durante los cuales se ha venido desarrollando la nueva variedad, aunque est¨¢ se fortaleci¨® en el a?o 2016 con la alianza con Nespresso y, precisamente, esta compa?¨ªa est¨¢ interesada en incluir mejores procesos de planificaci¨®n y gesti¨®n en toda la l¨ªnea productiva y log¨ªstica a su cargo, por esa raz¨®n, desea establecer un modelo cuantitativo que le ayude con las proyecciones de demanda para las tres variedades de caf¨¦ que se producen en la regi¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

En la base de datos suministrada, usted podr¨¢ encontrar las demandas, en sacos de 60 kg de caf¨¦ verde equivalente, para las variedades Caturra y Castillo de las fincas asociadas al Programa AAA Sustainable Quality?. Adem¨¢s, Nespresso ha estimado que antes de su intervenci¨®n, es decir entre enero del a?o 2000 y diciembre del a?o 2017, un 25% de la producci¨®n de caf¨¦ de todo el Caquet¨¢ correspond¨ªa a la variedad Esperanza, pero a partir del a?o 2018, este porcentaje aumento al 50%. Usando estas proporciones, consulte las cifras de producci¨®n mensual por regi¨®n de la Federaci¨®n Nacional de Cafeteros de Colombia (https://federaciondecafeteros.org/wp/estadisticas-cafeteras/) y complete las cifras, en sacos de 60 kg de caf¨¦ verde equivalente, para la variedad Esperanza Colombia hasta diciembre del a?o 2023. Una vez complete la base de datos, usted deber¨¢ pronosticar la demanda de las tres variedades para el a?o 2024 de tal forma que se minimice el MAPE promedio, es decir, el promedio aritm¨¦tico de los tres MAPEs. ## Submission Format Para esta primera parte del caso, cada usted deber¨¢ subir los pron¨®sticos para cada una de las variedades de caf¨¦, para los 12 meses del a?o 2024, en una ¨²nica columna. Primero los 12 pron¨®sticos para la variedad **Caturra**, luego los 12 pron¨®sticos para la variedad **Castillo** y, finalmente, los 12 pron¨®sticos para la variedad **Esperanza Colombia**. Los n¨²meros deber¨¢n ser enteros, no utilice cifras decimales.","# Dataset Description

En el archivo Coffee.cvs usted podr¨¢ encontrar las demandas, en sacos de 60 kg de caf¨¦ verde equivalente, para las variedades Caturra y Castillo de las fincas asociadas al Programa AAA Sustainable Quality? entre los a?os 2000 y 2023. Adem¨¢s, con base en las proporciones estimadas por Nespresso y las estad¨ªsticas de la Federaci¨®n Nacional de Cafeteros de Colombia, usted deber¨¢ estimar la demanda de la variedad Esperanza para el horizonte de tiempo considerado, para lo cual, deber¨¢ seguir los siguientes pasos:

Usando la hoja ""**8. ¨¢rea cult. seg¨²n tecnifi**"" estime la proporci¨®n de la producci¨®n nacional que corresponde al Caquet¨¢, asuma
que la proporci¨®n de hect¨¢reas cultivadas es un buen estimador de la participaci¨®n de cada departamento.
Usando la ""**9. Producci¨®n mensual**"" y la proporci¨®n hallada en el numeral anterior, estime la cantidad de sacos que se produjeron en el Caquet¨¢ entre enero del a?o 2000 y diciembre del a?o 2023.
Utilice las proporciones estimadas por Nespresso para calcular la producci¨®n correspondiente a la variedad Esperanza Colombia, redondee sus c¨¢lculos al entero m¨¢s cercano.
Una vez complete la base de datos, encuentre el mejor m¨¦todo de pron¨®sticos y estime las demandas para todos los meses del a?o 2024. Descargue el archivo Forecast.cvs y reporte all¨ª sus pron¨®sticos.

Aseg¨²rese de no da?ar el formato del archivo cvs, los separadores deber¨¢n seguir configurados como comas, no como punto y comas y el archivo deber¨¢ tener las dos columnas con los mismos nombres. En cada participaci¨®n, nombre su archivo con su n¨²mero de grupo y la versi¨®n, por ejemplo ""Grupo1V01.csv"", de no seguir estas indicaciones, su participaci¨®n no ser¨¢ v¨¢lida.",False,True
7750,bugnist2024fgvc,"# Overview

##Goal:
We welcome you to the BugNIST challenge, which is part of the FGVC11 workshop at CVPR 2024. Here you will compete in detecting and classifying objects in volumes under domain shift and hereby contribute to advancing methods for the analysis of volumetric data. Based on volumes containing one bug each, your task is to train a model that can detect and classify bugs in volumes that contain several bugs mixed with other materials. 

##Collaborative paper!
After the FGVC workshop, we will collect short descriptions from the participating teams to write a collaborative paper on approaches for detection under domain shift. We will reach out to you for your input.

##Baseline implementation!
There is now a baseline implementation for detection available. See the *U-Net baseline* under the *Code* tab.

##Why does it matter?
In volumetric imaging, it is often necessary to detect and classify objects that are densely packed in a mixture of other objects or materials that are not of interest. Annotating such data is tedious and difficult, and instead, it is much easier to scan some representative examples of the type of objects that should be detected. The problem is that individually scanned objects are placed in a context that is very different from the mixed objects that we wish to detect. This is a domain shift, where the objects of interest have the same appearance, but the context surrounding the objects is different.

To promote the development of new methods for domain adaptation in volumetric scans, we have created the BugNIST dataset, which is the basis for the BugNIST challenge. The dataset is based on 12 types of bugs such as larvae, pupae, grasshoppers, etc. The training data is more than 700 micro-CT scans of individual bugs of each type, whereas the test data is mixtures. 


----------------------
# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18910303%2F86b7c7c4511352005f3b429a657ead89%2Fillustration2.png?generation=1709301396521694&alt=media)
We provide the scans of individual bugs for training, a validation set of mixtures with annotated center point coordinates and bug type, and two test sets of mixtures without annotations for the public and private leaderboards. Your task is to build a model that predicts the center points and bug type for the mixed volumes.

For training the model, the total number of volumes of individual bugs is 9185. There are 388 mixtures, where a validation set of 78 is provided with center point annotation and 155 mixture volumes are for the public and private leader boards respectively. 




----------------------
# Evaluation

See ""Data"" section for overview over submission format and evaluation metric.","# Dataset Description

# BugNIST Dataset and Submission Guidelines

The [BugNIST](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01838) dataset is specifically designed for the task of volumetric object detection under domain shift. The training data consists of volumes of individual bugs, while the test data is a mixture of bugs and other materials such as leaves, wood shavings, cotton, and shredded paper.

## Dataset Structure

### Training Data
The training dataset is located in the `BugNIST_DATA/train` folder. It is divided into 12 subfolders, each containing single-volume scans of a specific class of bug, as indicated by the folder name. Each volume measures 128-by-64-by-64 voxels (z-axis corresponds to the longest axis). For submission the abbreviated name should be used.

Here is a breakdown of the individual volumes:

| Abbreviation     | **AC**        | **BC**        | **BF**   | **BL**        | **BP**            | **CF**         |
|------------------|---------------|---------------|----------|---------------|-------------------|----------------|
| Bug Type         | Brown Cricket | Black Cricket | Blowfly  | Beetle Larvae | Blowfly Pupae     | Curly-wing Fly |
| # of Volumes     | 724           | 762           | 734      | 780           | 752               | 756            |
| **Abbreviation** | **GH**        | **MA**        | **ML**   | **PP**        | **SL**            | **WO**         |
| Bug Type         | Grasshopper   | Maggot        | Mealworm | Fly Pupae     | Soldierfly Larvae | Woodlice       |
| # of Volumes     | 716           | 765           | 738      | 769           | 740               | 949            |

### Validation Data
The validation dataset is located in the `BugNIST_DATA/validation` folder. It contains 78 volumes of bug mixtures in `.tif` file format. The dimensions are (128, 92, 92) corresponding to the z,x,y-axis. The true labels are provided in the `validation.csv` file within the same folder.

### Test Data
The test dataset is located in the `BugNIST_DATA/test` folder. It contains 310 volumes of bug mixtures in `.tif` file format. The dimensions are (128, 92, 92) corresponding to the z,x,y-axis. For submission of predictions on the test set, use the `Sample_solution.csv` file.

## Submission Format
To submit predictions for evaluation on the validation data and for participation in the Kaggle competition, you must adhere to a specific format. Use the provided `Sample_solution.csv` file as a reference for formatting your predictions.

Your prediction file should be a CSV with two columns: 'filename' and 'centerpoints'.

- 'filename': This column should contain the name of the volume file. This is already given in the file `Sample_solution.csv`.
- 'centerpoints': This column should contain the centerpoint and class prediction for each bug detected in the volume.

The format for each bug's centerpoint is as follows:
- Class label (as given in the table above), followed by a semicolon (;)
- x-coordinate of the centerpoint, followed by a semicolon (;)
- y-coordinate of the centerpoint, followed by a semicolon (;)
- z-coordinate of the centerpoint

If multiple bugs are detected in the same volume, their centerpoints should be combined in the same cell, separated by semicolons (;).

For example, if a bug of class ""Beetle Larva"" (""bl"") is detected with a centerpoint at x-coordinate: 48.61, y-coordinate: 39.14, and z-coordinate 96.19, it should be written as a string ""bl;48.61;39.14;96.19"". If an additional bug of type ""Soldier Fly Larva"" (""sl"") is detected at position (77.71, 53.14, 57.17), it can be combined in the cell as a string ""bl;48.61;39.14;96.19;sl;77.71;53.14;57.17"".

Here is an example of a submission file:

| Filename                    | Centerpoints                                      |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| mix_02_006_centroids.csv    | bl;48.61;39.14;96.19;sl;77.71;53.14;57.17        |
| mix_02_011_centroids.csv    | ml;77.86;46.26;57.79;sl;41.14;61.93;32.37;sl;56.09;48.52;56.20 |

Ensure your submission file follows this format for proper evaluation and scoring using the F1 class label score.

The notebook `BugNIST_example_load.ipynb` contains an example load function. It loads an image and a string of formatted centerpoints which it uses to display the centerpoints in a figure.

Nb. z-coordinate corresponds to the longest axis i.e. we expect a center coordinate value between 0 and 128. Both the x and y-axis have dimensions of 92.

## Evaluation 
Predictions are evaluated by matching the predicted centerpoints to the true centerpoints. This is done by calculating the `scipy.spatial.distance.cdist` and then matching the points using `scipy.optimize.linear_sum_assignment`. The labels of the matched points are then compared to calculate the F1 score.

The evaluation metric is provided in the `BugNIST_metric.py` file. You can use this to locally evaluate the validation data.",False,True
