</ul>",False,True
7754,gt-cse6250-spring-2024-hw4,"# Overview

The deadline for the Kaggle submission can be different from the homework deadline, check the exact competition deadline at the Kaggle page. NO LATE SUBMISSION will be accepted after the competition is closed.
----------------------
# Description

A gatech.edu email address is required to participate.


Follow the sign-up direction using your GT email address, or if you already have an account, change the email on your existing account via your profile settings so that you can participate. Make sure your display name (not necessarily your username) matches your GT account username so that your Kaggle submission can be linked to the grading system. In the homework4.pdf, we ask you to improve the performance of your RNN model with variable-length sequences on mortality prediction. In this challenge, you will submit your my_predictions.csv to compete with your classmates. You will be required to submit the predicted labels for the test patients. After you submit, your accuracy on 50% of the test dataset will be visible to the class on the public leaderboard. You can use this accuracy as a sanity check to make sure your classifier is performing as intended and improve your model.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The file should contain a header and have the following format: 
SUBJECT_ID, MORTALITY 
1, 0.5432 
8, 0.9123 
9, 0.1251 
10, 0.3253 
etc.
----------------------
# Citation

Jimeng Sun. (2024). GT CSE6250 Spring 2024 HW4. Kaggle. https://kaggle.com/competitions/gt-cse6250-spring-2024-hw4","# Dataset Description

Use the data files provided in our Homework 4. Below gives a sample submission file.
## Files

*   **kaggle_sample_solution.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Data Field

*   `SUBJECT_ID` - an anonymous id unique to a given patient
*   `MORTALITY` - a predicted probability of mortality",False,True
7755,pub-1-copy-of-tokka-labs-quant-challenge-2024,"# Overview

Tokka Labs is a leading proprietary trading firm based in Singapore. Our team is focused on developing quantitative trading strategies aimed at providing liquidity in the most diverse and challenging environments. We are constantly seeking international talents skilled in quantitative modelling, maths, and computer science to enable us to continuously push the boundaries of decentralized finance. 


In this competition, we invite you to showcase your machine learning skills in predicting short-term returns in the ever-changing crypto markets. 

You will build a model that captures alpha signals and makes predictions against futures. Your final score is calculated over the next 10 days using live data and promises valuable insights into financial forecasting amidst evolving market dynamics and transient signals.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by?[Pearson correlation coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearson_correlation_coefficient). We will calculate the score by averaging the correlation coefficient between your predicted return series and the real market return series of the 10 assets.
$$ Score=\frac{1}{10} \sum\limits_{i=1}^{10} corr(PredReturnSeries_i, RealReturnSeries_i) $$

----------------------
# Submission

- Your solution notebook should take **test.csv** (and any other necessary public data) as input and produce prediction results. Save these results in a file named **submission.csv**.
- **All final submissions need to be submitted through this [Submission Form](https://forms.gle/6HLiAC2xj3Daiynb7)** by sharing the link of your solution notebook. The scores on kaggle leaderboard are only for reference. Please submit the form by **29 Feb 2024, 14:00 (GMT+8).** and ensure that your **solution notebook** and any associated **datasets** is **set to public by 29 Feb 2024, 14:30 (GMT+8).**
- Participants are encouraged to **make a successful submission of the solution notebook on this Kaggle competition page at least once before the training period concludes**. This ensures that your notebook is functioning correctly, as the computational environment remains consistent between the training and forecasting periods.

### Other Requirements
- During the training phase, you are allowed to use external public data to help with model building. However, it is strictly prohibited to access external data or employ any public APIs for data retrieval (Internet access is disabled) during the forecasting phase.
----------------------
# Timeline

### **Training Timeline** - *this timeline is for candidates to continuously train and improve their models.*

- **15 January 2024** - Challenge launch date
- **29 February 2024** - Final submission deadline

All deadlines are set at **14:00 PM (GMT+8)** on the specified day, unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers may adjust the contest timeline if necessary.

### **Forecasting Timeline** 
- **1 - 10 March 2024 (GMT+8)**

**13 March 2024** - Announcement of winners




----------------------
# Prizes

The winners will be contacted via the university emails used to submit the solutions. 
- 1st place: **USD 10,000**
- 2nd place: **USD 5,000**
- 3rd place: **USD 2,000**
- Consolation prizes: **3 x USD 1000**

**All winners and high performers will be invited to pursue an internship or full-time opportunity with Tokka Labs.**


----------------------
# Tokka Labs Contact Details

Throughout the duration of the challenge, participants are encouraged to use the Discussion Forums to discuss techniques and ask questions. 

For all other questions related to the rules of the challenge or interest in a career opportunity at Tokka Labs, kindly see below:

Tokka Labs Website: https://tokkalabs.com/
Tokka Labs Careers Page: https://tokkalabs.hire.trakstar.com/
Recruitment email address: recruitment@tokkalabs.com
----------------------
# Code Requirements

**This is a Code Competition**
Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
Internet access disabled
Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.","# Dataset Description

The Dataset contains historical 1 min bar data for 10 cryptocurrencies pairs, i.e. <Coin>/USDT. The  objective is to make a prediction for their future returns.

**Data Files**

- **train.csv** - The training dataset
    - `timestamp`?- The bar open time.
    - `open`?- Open USDT price.
    - `high`?- The highest USDT price within the minute.
    - `low`?- The lowest USDT price within the minute.
    - `close`?- Close USDT price.
    - `volume`?- The trading volume of base asset(Coin).
    - `quote_asset_volume` - The trading volume of quote asset(USDT).
    - `number_of_trades` - The number of trades has been made during the minute.
    - `taker_buy_volume` - The volume contributed by the taker buy orders during the minute.
    - `taker_sell_volume` - The volume contributed by the taker sell orders during the minute.
    - `symbol` - The symbol of the cryptocurrency.
    - `log_return` - The prediction target of this competition, for each row in the dataset, it represents the logarithmic return (continuously compounded return) of the asset over next 10 minutes.
    
    $$
    R_t=log(P_{t+10}/P_t)
    $$
    
- **test.csv** - The testing dataset, with same structure as train.csv, used for testing model and getting a score on LeaderBoard. 
    - Notes: As all the data are public available, the score on leader board can only be used as a reference, and we'll only evaluate model's performance during the forecasting period. There is a more detailed elaboration about submission and evaluation is in the overview session.

- **sample_submission.csv** - An example of the submission data.",False,True
7756,llm-coursestable-diffusion-evaluation,"# Overview

In this Kaggle competition, your task is to assess the performance of your stable diffusion model iterations. Your objective entails generating an image of size 512 x 512 and utilizing the CLIP image processor to create an embedding clip. This embedding clip will aid in measuring the cosine similarity between the generated image and the ground truth images.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The assessment involves determining the cosine similarity between the CLIP vector of the ground truth, which has a length of 512, and the CLIP vector of the image you've generated.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you're required to submit a comma-separated string containing the CLIP vector of the generated image.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - Train set with prompts and generated CLIP image embeddings
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns in **train.csv**

*   `id` - index
*   `prompt`?- prompt to generate image
*   `Value` - CLIP image embeddings of the generated image

## Columns in **sample_submission** 
*   `id` - index
*   `prompt`?- prompt to generate image
*   `Value` - CLIP image embeddings of the generated image (this file does not represent real values)",False,True
