7761,neuralclash,"# Overview

***'Tom and Jerry Showdown'!***
Dive into the classic rivalry between the mischievous duo, Tom and Jerry, in this thrilling competition.,hosted by IEEE SBM
----------------------
# Description

""Welcome to the Tom and Jerry Classifier Challenge! Your task is to train a CNN to distinguish between the mischievous duo¡ªTom the cat and Jerry the mouse¡ªin a dataset filled with their playful antics. Can you develop a model with the precision of Tom's schemes and the agility of Jerry's escapes? Unleash your inner detective and embark on this thrilling adventure in the world of animated cat-and-mouse!""
----------------------
# Evaluation

""The competition metric is accuracy score, evaluating the proportion of correctly classified images out of the total. A higher score indicates better performance, with 1.0 representing perfect accuracy.""

---

## Submission File
For every image in the dataset, the submission file should contain three columns: image_name,jerry and tom. The file should contain a header and should be in the following format:

    Image_name,jerry,tom
    fram1.jpg,0,1
    fram2.jpg,0,1
    fram3.jpg,0,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The Tom and Jerry dataset comprises a collection of 5478 images featuring scenes from the iconic animated series ""Tom and Jerry."" The dataset is divided into two main parts: a training set consisting of 4384 images and a test set containing 1098 images.",False,True
7762,super-resolution-in-video-games,"# Overview

Recently the [Super Resolution Gaming Dataset (SRGD)](https://github.com/Pe4enIks/SRGB) project has been released and It's time to use it. In this competition, you¡¯re challenged to demonstrate how to solve a super resolution problem on a specific dataset in the domain of video games.
----------------------
# Description

## Context
Video games require a lot of computing resources such as GPU, CPU, RAM, etc. To reduce the computing cost, some companies such as [NVIDIA](https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/), [AMD](https://www.amd.com/en.html) create their own solutions based on super resolution technologies. This is because it may be faster to render at a lower resolution and then upscale the image than to just render at high resolution.

They use not only RGB, but also other types of data (you can read about it in their reports), but in this competition only RGB images collected using the [Unreal Engine](https://www.unrealengine.com/en-US) game engine are submitted.

There aren't a lot of datasets for super resolution in games, so we hope that the collected dataset will give independent developers more room to get started in this area. And this competition is a great opportunity to learn about super resolution technologies.

## Materials for beginners
- [Deep Learning for Single Image Super-Resolution: A Brief Review](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.03344)
- [Collection of Super Resolution papers](https://github.com/ChaofWang/Awesome-Super-Resolution)
- [NVIDIA DLSS](https://developer.nvidia.com/rtx/dlss)
- [AMD FSR](https://www.amd.com/en/technologies/fidelityfx-super-resolution)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peak_signal-to-noise_ratio) between the predicted high resolution images and the observed high resolution images.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you need to take the `filename` image from the `test/lr` folder, predict the high-resolution image and encode it using [rle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run-length_encoding), [zlib](https://docs.python.org/3/library/zlib.html#zlib.compress), and [base64](https://docs.python.org/3/library/base64.html#base64.b64encode). Note that the `rle` column consists of `str` with `bytes` in it, the metric calculation script will use the `eval` function to get `bytes` from `str`.

    id,filename,rle
    0,""00321.png"",""b'eNpj4AIAAAwACw=='""
    1,""02346.png"",""b'eNpj4AIAAAwACw=='""
    2,""00505.png"",""b'eNpj4AIAAAwACw=='""
    etc.

## Encoding Script
To encode a single frame, use the example below. Other scripts that may be useful can be found in the [SRGB](https://github.com/Pe4enIks/SRGB/tree/main/kaggle) project.

```python
import base64
import zlib

import cv2
import numpy as np


def encode(img: np.ndarray) -> bytes:
    """"""
    Lossless encoding of images for submission on kaggle platform.

    Parameters
    ----------
    img : np.ndarray
        cv2.imread(f) - BGR image in (h, w, c) format, c = 3 (even for png format).

    Returns
    -------
    bytes
        Encoded image as bytes.
    """"""
    img_to_encode = img.astype(np.uint8)
    img_to_encode = img_to_encode.flatten()
    img_to_encode = np.append(img_to_encode, -1)

    cnt, rle = 1, []
    for i in range(1, img_to_encode.shape[0]):
        if img_to_encode[i] == img_to_encode[i - 1]:
            cnt += 1
            if cnt > 255:
                rle += [img_to_encode[i - 1], 255]
                cnt = 1
        else:
            rle += [img_to_encode[i - 1], cnt]
            cnt = 1

    compressed = zlib.compress(bytes(rle), zlib.Z_BEST_COMPRESSION)
    base64_bytes = base64.b64encode(compressed)
    return base64_bytes


img = cv2.imread(""filename"")
encoded_img = str(encode(img))
```","# Dataset Description

## Overview

Your goal in this competition is to increase the resolution and quality of low resolution images in the video game domain using super resolution technologies. To achieve this goal, we provide a training dataset consisting of paired 270p and 1080p images collected from different projects using the [Unreal Engine](https://www.unrealengine.com/en-US) game engine.

All images in the `train/lr` folder have a corresponding file in the `train/hr` folder. There is no reason to train models to upscale directly from 270x480 to 1080x1920 pixels, you can choose any resolution you think is best for quality and will give you a higher metric score, but you need to create models that use `x4` resolution upscaling because metrics will be calculated between 64x64 and 256x256 pixel images. After training, create a `submission.csv` using files from `test/lr`. You will need to encode your upscaled 256x256 images using the algorithm described on the **[Evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/super-resolution-in-video-games/overview/evaluation)** page.

## Files

* **train/{lr|hr}** Folders containing low resolution and high resolution images (matches filename) for model training. Low resolution images are 270x480 pixels, high resolution images are 1080x1920 pixels.
* **test/lr** A folder of low resolution test images for metric evaluation, images are cropped to 64x64 pixels to not exceed the submission.csv file size. High resolution test images are not publicly available and are cropped in the same way to 256x256 pixels. The type of cropping is random paired cropping.
* **sample_submission.csv** A sample submission file in the correct format. See the [**Evaluation**](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/super-resolution-in-video-games/overview/evaluation) page for more details.",False,True
