7764,aml-2024-project1,"# Overview

We present here the first practical project, part of the students¡¯ evaluation process of the Advanced Machine Learning course of the Master in Engineering and Data Science of the University of Coimbra. This work is to be done autonomously by a group of \textbf{two students}. The deadline for delivering the work is **7 Abril of 2024** via Inforestudante.
The quality of your work will be judged as a function of the value of the technical work, the written description, and the public defence. All sources used to perform the work (including the code) must be clearly identified. The document may be written in Portuguese or in English, using a word processor of your choice (Latex is preferred). **The written report is limited to 8 pages long**.
The document should be well structured, including a general introduction,  a description of the problem, the approach, the experimental setup, an analysis of the results, and a conclusion. The report should follow the Springer LNCS format. The Latex and Word templates are available in the Support Material of the course. The final mark will be given to each member of the group individually.
To do the work the student may consult any source he/she wants. Nevertheless, plagiarism will not be allowed and, if detected, it will imply failing the course. While doing the work and when submitting it, you should pay particular attention to the following aspects (whose relative importance depends on the type of work done):

- description of the approach to the problem
- description of the general architecture of the methods used;
- description of the experiment, including a table with the parameters used which should allow full replication;
- description of the evaluation metrics used for the validation: quality of the final result, efficacy, efficiency, diversity, or any other most appropriate;
   

Do not forget, besides what was just said, that it is fundamental: (1) to do a correct experimental analysis; (2) to do an informed discussion about the results obtained; (3) to put in evidence the advantages of the chosen alternative.
----------------------
# Description

Image classification is a computer vision problem where Machine Learning has obtained impressive results. In the past to tackle this task practitioners had to use expert knowledge and perform feature engineering, carefully crafting and creating visual features for Machine Learning models to learn. After this step, classical and shallow models were usually employed such as Adaboost, RandomForests, Support Vector Machines, and Multi-layer Perceptrons. 

Despite making their first appearance in the late 80s early 90s, Convolution Neural Networks (CNNs) became popular since 2010 due to technological advances, namely in GPU computing, and have been heavily used for image classification problems with great success surpassing most classical approaches. The main feature that this type of Artificial Neural Network provides is that feature extraction and learning is an integral part of the neural network, performing automatic feature extraction using the whole image as input, where the features are then passed to dense layers, providing the classification power as typical fully connected Multi-Layer Perceptrons are able to. 

In this work, we are going to tackle an image classification dataset of traffic sign images using Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs).
----------------------
# Objective

The main objective is to analyse the dataset and create a machine learning approach with neural networks as the models that can perform supervised image classification of the dataset that is addressed in Section Dataset. To do that you should attend to the following objectives:

Prepare the machine learning pipeline for the image classification dataset.
Explore solutions and compare the results using at least, the following ANNs models:
- Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLP)
- Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)

And for each model, experimentation with different layer architectures, loss functions, optimisers and hyperparameters. Explore at least:
- Loss function: MSE, Cross Entropy
- Optimizers: RMSprop, Adam

Exploring other solutions than the listed ones that are suitable for the problem at hand and considered as extra work, can be as compensation points to cover problems in the listed ones above.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset is based of the ``Traffic Sign Dataset'' with 54 different classes. For this work we will work with the dataset at the 75*75 pixel resolution and a subset of 9 classes that are the following: 

- 6 - Speed limit (70km/h)
- 12 - Don't go left or right
- 13 - Don't go right
- 24 - Go right
- 37 - Children crossing
- 38 - Dangerous curve to the right
- 39 - Dangerous curve to the left
- 44 - Go left or straight
- 50 - Fences

The dataset set is composed of 518 images, with different distribution of examples per class. 

## Folders

*   **TRAIN** - The training set
*   **TEST** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **labels.csv** - supplemental information about the data",False,True
7765,urban-reid-challenge,"# Overview

The objective of this competition is to encourage the **development** of algorithms for **long-term video-based re-identification of urban elements**, specifically focusing on trash bins, waste containers, and crosswalks. To achieve this goal, a **new database** of almost 5000 images of 397 differenet identities along with a baseline based on Part-aware Transformer [1] is proposed. ([Click here to visit the official Challenge website](http://www-vpu.eps.uam.es/UrbanReIDChallenge/))


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F18862821%2F5190f7d363937379baeac674cd9fd19c%2Flogov3%20.png?generation=1712135811693234&alt=media)
----------------------
# Description

# Motivation

In recent decades, rapid urban growth has posed significant challenges in waste management and the maintenance of urban infrastructure in the most developed countries. Many governments and waste management companies are making major efforts in order to develop and implement intelligent systems that allow addressing these problems efficiently and sustainably in terms of resources. Image re-identification (also know as image ReID), which is based on identifying the same element in images captured at different times, with different lighting and/or perspectives, could be a very useful tool in oder to address this problem.

To date, various challenges have been conducted for re-identification of people and vehicles, but, to our knowledge. none have been undertaken in the domain of urban elements. Therefore, this work aims to create competition using a new long-term database of urban elements (crosswalks, containers and rubbish bins) and a baseline code to serve as a starting point for the development and evaluation of new algorithms that could potentially enable a better understanding and management of the urban environment.

# Urban elements database

The proposed database is a continuation of the work done for the UrbAM-ReID dataset [2] by our colleagues at VPULab. It consists of images of crosswalks, containers and rubbish bins extracted from four different videos of the same route through an unspecified city in different directions (three recordings in one direction and one in the opposite direction used for evaluation). To ensure that this database is as realistic as possible and provides sufficient variability for this type of task, the videos were recorded over a period of four months. In total, the database comprises 4961 images from 397 different identities. Among these, 3607 images representing 288 identities are utilized for the training set, while 1354 images from 109 distinct identities are reserved for evaluation (1008 images and 109 identities for the 'gallery' split, and 346 images of 76 identities for the 'query' split). Further details about the database, its distribution, and capture can be found in ""[Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-reid-challenge/data)"" .

# Baseline code

As baseline  Part-aware Transformer [[1]](https://github.com/liyuke65535/Part-Aware-Transformer) has been used for the development of new algorithms for urban elements ReID. It has been decided to employ this algorithm due to its great generalization capacity compared to other state-of-the-art algorithms.
The code and instructions for setting up the system is publicly available in GitHub [[click here to access](https://github.com/vpulab/Urban-Elements-ReID---baseline/tree/main)].
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Task
The task for the evaluation is to match, for each query, the top-100 most similar images of the test set.

## Evaluation metric
For the competition, the **mean Average Precision** (mAP) for the top-100 predictions for each query  has been established as the evaluation metric for the generated track file. However, we highly encourage the use of **Cumulative Match Characteristic** for the first K predictions (CMC-K) for the development of proposed algorithms.

## Submission File
The submission format must be a .csv file consisting of two columns. The first column, *'imageName'*, serves as the identifier for the query images in descending numerical order. The second column,*'corresponding Indexes'*, should contain, for each row, the top-100 identifiers of the elements in the test set (test_labels.csv) sorted in descending order (with the most confident assignments first). An example submission can be found in ['Data'](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urbam-reid-challenge/data).
----------------------
# Timeline

02/04/2025 - Launch of the competition
~~28/05/2025~~ 11/06/2025- Final submission deadline
14-17/09/2025 - Leaderboard Announcement

Submissions made after the final deadline will not be considered in determining the winning team.
----------------------
# Doubts and discussion

If you have any questions, suggestions, or need clarification to participate in the competition, please use the [Discussion](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urbam-reid-challenge/discussion) section, and we will try to respond as quickly as possible.
----------------------
# License

Urban Elements ReID dataset is licensed under CC BY-ND 4.0. This enables reusers to copy and distribute the material in any medium or format in unadapted form only, and only so long as attribution is given to the creator. Reference this work as shown in section 'Cite this proyect'. This license allows for commercial use. CC BY-ND includes the following elements:
BY: credit must be given to the creator
ND: no derivates or adaptions of the work are permited.
----------------------
# Cite this proyect

If this work has been useful for your research, you are welcome to cite it as:

Javier Gal¨¢n, ¨¢lvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn . Kaggle. Urban Elements ReID Competition. VPULab, Universidad Aut¨®noma de Madrid (2024). URL: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/urban-reid-challenge/overview
----------------------
# Registration to ICIP 2025 Challenge

Each team is required to register their participation in kaggle also via EMAIL to urban.reid.challenge@uam.es. The email should contain:

? Challenge name and team short-name Participation as the SUBJECT LINE.

? Team name in Kaggle and team member names and affiliation in the BODY OF THE EMAIL.

? Contact number and email address in the BODY OF THE EMAIL.
----------------------
# Acknowledgments

This work has been supported by the Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovaci¨®n y Universidades of the Spanish Government under project SEGA-CV (TED2021-131643A-I00) and HVD (PID2021-125051OB-I00).
----------------------
# Related References

[1]. Ni, Hao & Li, Yuke & Shen, Heng & Song, Jingkuan. (2023). Part-Aware Transformer for Generalizable Person Re-identification. 
[2]. Paula Moral, Alvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn, Jose M. Mart¨ªnez. (2024). Long-term geo-positioned re-identification dataset of urban elements. IEEE International Conference on Image Processing (ICIP), pp. 124-130, doi: 10.1109/ICIP51287.2024.10647759.
[3]. Javier Gal¨¢n, Miguel Gonz¨¢lez, Paula Moral, ¨¢lvaro Garc¨ªa-Mart¨ªn, Jos¨¦ M. Mart¨ªnez. (2025). Transforming urban waste collection inventory: AI-Based container classification and Re-Identification. Waste Management, Volume 199, pp. 25-35, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.wasman.2025.02.051.
