# CVPR 2024 Workshop

This competition is part of the [Image Matching: Local Features and Beyond workshop](https://image-matching-workshop.github.io/) at CVPR'24. Selected submissions to the competition will be invited to give talks at the workshop on June 17-18, 2024, in Seattle, USA (in person or remotely). Attending the workshop is not required to participate in the competition.

CVPR 2024 will be a hybrid conference. Attendees presenting in person are responsible for all costs associated with expenses and fees to attend CVPR 2024.","# Dataset Description

Building a 3-D model of a scene given an unstructured collection of images taken around it is a longstanding problem in computer vision research. Your challenge in this competition is to generate 3-D reconstructions from image sets showing different types of scenes and accurately pose those images.

This competition uses a hidden test. When your submitted notebook is scored, the actual test data (including a sample submission) will be made available to your notebook. Expect to find roughly 1,000 images in the hidden test set.


## Files

**sample_submission.csv** A valid, randomly-generated sample submission with the following fields:

* `image_path`: The image filename, including the path.
* `dataset`: The unique identifier for the dataset.
* `scene`: The unique identifier for the scene.
* `rotation_matrix`: The first target column. A 3x3 matrix, flattened into a vector in row-major convention, with values separated by `;`.
* `translation_vector`: The second target column. A 3-D dimensional vector, with values separated by `;`.


**[train/test]/\*/images** A batch of images all taken near the same location. Some of training datasets may also contain a folder named **images_full** with additional images. The published test folder comprises a subset of the `church` scene from train and is provided solely for example purposes. The training data usually has a sequential capture ordering and significant image-to-image content overlap while the test set has limited image-to-image overlap and the image ordering is randomized. 


**train/\*/sfm** A 3-D reconstruction for this batch of images, which can be opened with [colmap](https://colmap.github.io/), the 3-D structure-from-motion library bundled with this competition.

**train/\*/LICENSE.txt** The license for this dataset.

**train/train_labels.csv** A list of images in these datasets, with ground truth.

* `dataset`: The unique identifier for the dataset.
* `scene`: The unique identifier for the scene.
* `image_path`: The image filename, including the path.
* `rotation_matrix`: The first target column. A 3x3 matrix, flattened into a vector in row-major convention, with values separated by `;`.
* `translation_vector`: The second target column. A 3-D dimensional vector, with values separated by `;`.",True,True
7772,sales-predictionoptima2024,"# Overview

Optima
----------------------
# Description

Dataset contains sales data for an e-commerce website aggregated for all locations in the region. The dataset contains sales values for 2 regions i.e USA and Germany. The day wise sales data is given .
Your task is to use this data to predict the sales for the 2 regions for the mentioned time periods (refer test.csv for the dates).
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on  **Sum of Absolute error**
https://support.numxl.com/hc/en-us/articles/214286506-SAE-Sum-of-Absolute-Errors","# Dataset Description

Dataset contains standardised(standard scaler) sales data for an e-commerce website. The dataset contains sales values for 2 regions as mentioned in column A. The day wise sales data is given from 1st august 2015 to 31st september 2016 , and then again from 16th august 2016 to 23rd december 2016.
Your task is to use this data to predict the sales for the 2 regions for the 2 missing time periods i.e 1st august 2016 to 15th august 2016 and 24th december 2016 to 31st december 2016 (refer test.csv for the dates)

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7773,msu-robust-speech-commands-classification,"# Overview

As part of this competition, participants are invited to develop a robust classifier of voice commands. It is assumed that the developed classifier will be resistant to malicious distortions of the input data.

# Evaluation.
## Goal.
The goal of this competition is to classify speech commands correctly. As a result, each of the samples must be associated with a command.

## Evaluation Metric.
$$Accuracy = \frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}$$

## Submission File.
The csv file must contain a header and be in the following format:
```
image_id, label 
1, right
2, happy
3, no
4, happy
```","# Dataset Description

