7840,ben10,"# Overview

**Goal Of The Competition**

The prime goal of this competition is to come up with a solution system to transcribe Bengali speech with various regional dialects following the orthography set by linguists. The speech corpus for this competition has been developed by Bengali.AI from one of their ongoing large-scale research projects. The corpus consists of spontaneous speech on everyday topics from **373 individuals** with various regional dialects from **ten** different geographical locations such as **Rangpur, Kishoreganj, Narail, Chittagong, Narsingdi, Tangail, Barishal, Habiganj, Sylhet & Sandwip.**  The cumulative length of this entire speech corpus is over **79+ hours**. Your efforts could improve the Bengali speech recognition technology using this unique speech recognition dataset for the very first time in history which is dealing with the regional speech domain. In addition, your submission will be among the first open-source speech recognition methods for Bengali.

**Footnote:** This dataset is the subset version of the dataset from an ongoing research project conducted by Bengali.AI which BRAC University is currently funding.

##Sponsors

**Brainstation 23**
BrainStation23, a globally leading one of the best software development company in Bangladesh. It helps to transform your business into the digital sphere.

**APSIS**
It is a company where professionals from both technical and functional fields group together with an objective of providing fit-for-purpose business solutions. It realizes the importance of functional knowledge and its impact in developing business solutions. We constantly strive to be a leading technology firm with profound business and functional knowledge.

Both companies, being strong advocates for open-sourced research, have been generous enough to sponsor the different aspects of this competition. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1515169%2F832b3be97ed834213a3a60ce2c421a14%2FUntitled%20Diagram.drawio.png?generation=1713863357086185&alt=media)

##Powered by

**Bengali.AI**
Bengali.AI is a community that works to create  Bangla AI language technologies & open-source datasets for research. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7662209%2F02ea43bdf4b2c647e404d429dfa1153a%2Fbengali.ai_400x400.png?generation=1714348850731458&alt=media)

**BRAC University**
BRAC University is currently funding this research named ""Cross-domain 10k hour Bengali Speech Corpus for state-of-the-art Bengali Speech Recognition, Diarization and Synthesis"" under the ""Brac University Research Seed Grant Initiative (RSGI)"" research grant.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7662209%2Fb44ffbd42f6fdc621a2bb2b51d7b1c3b%2Fbracu_logo_ai%20Converted.png?generation=1714348863039559&alt=media)




----------------------
# Description

## Challenge Overview
This project of Bengali.AI bridges the gap between the modern speech recognition system and resource development. The primary aim of this competition is to come up with multiple solution systems that can enable the computer to better understand different ways people speak in various regions, focusing on Bengali, a language not extensively supported in terms of technology for its local dialects.

## Challenge Highlights
Our findings revealed that current advanced technology struggles to accurately understand these local dialects, both when using it directly (zero-shot) and when trying to adapt or fine-tune it for better performance. This challenge exists despite significant efforts and advancements in speech recognition technologies, highlighting a crucial area for further research and development.

## Why Join the Challenge?
To contribute to this field and aid in the development of more inclusive and effective speech recognition systems, we've made our dataset and the tools developed during this study freely available. We hope that these resources will serve as a foundation for future work, aiming to improve how computers understand spoken language in all its variety, including the rich tapestry of Bengali dialects. This project not only sheds light on the existing limitations of speech recognition technology in handling dialectal variations but also provides a valuable benchmark resource for testing and enhancing the capabilities of automatic speech recognition systems under resource-constrained?conditions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by a mean Word Error Rate, proceeding as follows:

- The WER is computed for each instance in the test set.

- The WERs are averaged within domains (dialects), weighted by the number of words in the sentence.

- The dialect WERs are summed using varying weights.

The dialect weights are given below:
domain_weights = {
        'Barishal': 0.125,
        'Chittagong': 0.083,
        'Habiganj': 0.125,
        'Kishoreganj': 0.083,
        'Narail': 0.083, 
        'Narsingdi': 0.083,
        'Rangpur': 0.083,
        'Sylhet': 0.125,
        'Sandwip': 0.125,
        'Tangail': 0.083,
    }

The full kaggle evaluation code is published [**here**](https://www.kaggle.com/code/reasat/regional-asr-wer)","# Dataset Description

The recordings are 16kHz *.wav audio files. The data are coming from 373 different speakers from 10 regions in Bangladesh (rangpur, kishoreganj, narail, chittagong, narsingdi, tangail, habiganj, barishal, sylhet, sandwip) There are in total 13610 samples in the train set (63:08:42 hrs) and 1703 samples in the phase 1 test set (8:01:11 hrs).

<> notation is present throughout the train and test sets where the there exists a word, but not intelligible (due to noise, slurred speech, overlap etc). Newline operators (\n) are present only in the training data but not in the test ground truth files. 

The unicode of the validation (Phase 1 test set)/test (Phase 2 test set) dataset ground truth is normalized using [bnunicodenormalizer](https://github.com/mnansary/bnUnicodeNormalizer). 

## Files

* The **16_kHz_train_audio** folder contains the train files, and the **16_kHz_valid_audio** folder contains the phase 1 test set files.  
*   **train.csv** - the training set transcriptions
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns

*   `id` - wav file name. (lorem1.wav)
*   `sentence`?- transcript of file.",False,True
7841,TweetSentimentBR,"# Overview

Welcome to the Tweet Sentiment Analysis Challenge! This challenge aims to assess participants' ability to analyze and predict Brazilians' sentiments in tweets using machine learning and natural language processing (NLP) techniques. Participants will be challenged to develop models that can correctly identify and classify sentiment expressed in a wide range of tweets.
----------------------
# Description

For this competition, I will provide the dataset for analyzing the sentiments of Brazilians on Twitter, here we have a collection of tweets that were analyzed to determine **Positive** `:)` or **Negative** `:(` sentiment. This dataset is a valuable tool for participants, allowing them to develop and test sentiment analysis models in a realistic environment.

### The dataset is composed of five main columns:

1. id: A unique identifier for each tweet
2. tweet_text: The text of the tweet. This field contains the content of the tweet
3. tweet_date: The date and time the tweet was posted. This field can be useful for analyzing temporal trends or understanding the context in which the tweet was written.
4. sentiment: The sentiment label of the tweet, which indicates whether the sentiment expressed is Positive `1`, Negative `0`. This field is the target variable for sentiment analysis. **Tweets were modified to remove the characters `:)` and `:(` so as not to be obvious for model classification**
5. query_used: Character used for tracking tweet `:)` and `:(`

#### Data Types:
- `id`: Integer
- `tweet_text`: String
- `tweet_date`: Date and time
- `sentiment`: Categorical (`1` for Positive, `0` for Negative)
- `query_used`: Categorical ( `:(`, `:)` )
----------------------
# Evaluation

Participants must use the dataset to develop machine learning models that can predict the sentiment expressed in tweets. They must train their models using the provided training set and submit their predictions to the test set for evaluation. Good luck

---

Participants will be evaluated based on the accuracy of their sentiment predictions on tweets from the provided test set


## Submission File
The file must contain a header and have the following format:
| id | sentiment |
| --- | --- |
| 2129535486121049552 | 1 |
| 2130527465221556147 | 0 |","# Dataset Description

We will have two sets of data in csv format, the data for training the model, with 50000 lines, and the data for classification with 5000 lines. The main variable you should sort on will be `sentiment`.

##Files

* **Train.csv** ¨C the training set
* **Test.csv** ¨C the test suite
* **Sample_Submission.csv** ¨C a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

- `id`: Integer
- `tweet_text`: String
- `tweet_date`: Date and time
- `sentiment`: Categorical (`1` for Positive, `0` for Negative)
- `query_used`: Categorical ( `:(`, `:)` )",False,True
7842,inceptez-ds-batch-22-ml-hackathon,"# Description

**Insurance Claim Prediction:**
Predict whether the policyholder will file a claim in the next 6 months or not.

**Problem Statement:**
Our company would like to use Data Science to identify the policyholders whose chances of filing a claim are high in the next 6 months. The company challenges the Data Science community to build a high-performance algorithm to predict if the policyholder will file a claim in the next 6 months or not based on the set of car and policy features.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric
The evaluation metric for this hackathon would be F1 score

## Submission File Format

The solution file should follow a format similar to that of the sample submission file. sample_submission.csv contains 2 variables - policy_id and is_claim.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
|Variable	Description|
|:----|
|policy_id:	Unique identifier of the policyholder|
|is_claim	Outcome: Boolean flag indicating whether the policyholder file a claim in the next 6 months or not|","# Dataset Description

**About the Dataset:**
You are provided with information on policyholders containing the attributes like policy tenure, age of the car, age of the car owner, population density of the city, make and model of the car, power, engine type, etc and the target variable indicating whether the policyholder files a claim in the next 6 months or not.

**Data Dictionary:**
You are provided with 3 files - train.csv, test.csv, and sample_submission.csv

**Train and Test Set:**
The train and test set contains information about different insurance policy holders. The train set includes the target variable is_claim whereas in the test set, you need to predict the target variable is_claim.

|Variable|Description|
|:----|:----|
|policy_id|Unique identifier of the policyholder|
|policy_tenure|Time period of the policy|
|age_of_car|Normalized age of the car in years|
|age_of_policyholder|Normalized age of policyholder in years|
|area_cluster|Area cluster of the policyholder|
|population density|Population density of the city (Policyholder City)|
|make|Encoded Manufacturer/company of the car|
|segment|Segment of the car (A/ B1/ B2/ C1/ C2)|
|model|Encoded name of the car|
|fuel_type|Type of fuel used by the car|
|max_torque|Maximum Torque generated by the car (Nm@rpm)|
|max_power|Maximum Power generated by the car (bhp@rpm)|
|engine_type|Type of engine used in the car|
|airbags|Number of airbags installed in the car|
|is_esc|Boolean flag indicating whether Electronic Stability Control (ESC) is present in the car or not.|
|is_adjustable_steering|Boolean flag indicating whether the steering wheel of the car is adjustable or not.|
|is_tpms|Boolean flag indicating whether Tyre Pressure Monitoring System (TPMS) is present in the car or not.|
|is_parking_sensors|Boolean flag indicating whether parking sensors are present in the car or not.|
|is_parking_camera|Boolean flag indicating whether the parking camera is present in the car or not.|
|rear_brakes_type|Type of brakes used in the rear of the car|
|displacement|Engine displacement of the car (cc)|
|cylinder|Number of cylinders present in the engine of the car|
|transmission_type|Transmission type of the car|
|gear_box|Number of gears in the car|
|steering_type|Type of the power steering present in the car|
|turning_radius|The space a vehicle needs to make a certain turn (Meters)|
|length|Length of the car (Millimetre)|
|width|Width of the car (Millimetre)|
|height|Height of the car (Millimetre)|
|gross_weight|The maximum allowable weight of the fully-loaded car, including passengers, cargo and equipment (Kg)|
|is_front_fog_lights|Boolean flag indicating whether front fog lights are available in the car or not.|
|is_rear_window_wiper|Boolean flag indicating whether the rear window wiper is available in the car or not.|
|is_rear_window_washer|Boolean flag indicating whether the rear window washer is available in the car or not.|
|is_rear_window_defogger|Boolean flag indicating whether rear window defogger is available in the car or not.|
|is_brake_assist|Boolean flag indicating whether the brake assistance feature is available in the car or not.|
|is_power_door_lock|Boolean flag indicating whether a power door lock is available in the car or not.|
|is_central_locking|Boolean flag indicating whether the central locking feature is available in the car or not.|
|is_power_steering|Boolean flag indicating whether power steering is available in the car or not.|
|is_driver_seat_height_adjustable|Boolean flag indicating whether the height of the driver seat is adjustable or not.|
|is_day_night_rear_view_mirror|Boolean flag indicating whether day & night rearview mirror is present in the car or not.|
|is_ecw|Boolean flag indicating whether Engine Check Warning (ECW) is available in the car or not.|
|is_speed_alert|Boolean flag indicating whether the speed alert system is available in the car or not.|
|ncap_rating|Safety rating given by NCAP (out of 5)|
|is_claim|Outcome: Boolean flag indicating whether the policyholder file a claim in the next 6 months or not.|


**Submission File Format:**
The solution file should follow a format similar to that of the sample submission file. sample_submission.csv contains 2 variables - policy_id and is_claim.

|Variable|Description|
|:----|:----|
|policy_id|Unique identifier of the policyholder|
|is_claim|Outcome: Boolean flag indicating whether the policyholder file a claim in the next 6 months or not|


**Evaluation metric:**
The evaluation metric for this hackathon would be F1 score",False,True
