7870,sentiment-analysis-for-movie-review,"# Overview

Sentiment Analysis of Movie Reviews
Task Description
In this competition, participants are tasked with developing a machine learning model to perform sentiment analysis on a dataset of movie reviews. The dataset consists of a large collection of movie reviews, each labeled with a sentiment category indicating whether the review is positive, negative, or neutral.

Dataset Description
The dataset is provided in a CSV format and contains two columns:

Freshness: This column contains the text of the movie review.
Sentiment: This column contains the sentiment label for each review, which can be one of the following categories: ""positive,"" ""negative,"" or ""neutral.""

Evaluation Metric
The performance of the models will be evaluated using accuracy metric.

Submission Format
Participants are required to submit a CSV file containing their predictions for the sentiment label of each review in the test dataset.

Acknowledgements
The dataset used in this competition is provided and has been preprocessed for the task of sentiment analysis. Participants are encouraged to explore additional preprocessing techniques and feature engineering to improve the performance of their models.
----------------------
# Description

Sentiment Analysis of Movie Reviews
Task Description
In this competition, participants are tasked with developing a machine learning model to perform sentiment analysis on a dataset of movie reviews. The dataset consists of a large collection of movie reviews, each labeled with a sentiment category indicating whether the review is positive, negative, or neutral.

Dataset Description
The dataset is provided in a CSV format and contains two columns:

Freshness: This column contains the text of the movie review.
Sentiment: This column contains the sentiment label for each review, which can be one of the following categories: ""positive,"" ""negative,"" or ""neutral.""

Evaluation Metric
The performance of the models will be evaluated using accuracy metric.

Submission Format
Participants are required to submit a CSV file containing their predictions for the sentiment label of each review in the test dataset.

Acknowledgements
The dataset used in this competition is provided and has been preprocessed for the task of sentiment analysis. Participants are encouraged to explore additional preprocessing techniques and feature engineering to improve the performance of their models.
----------------------
# Section 2

Evaluation 

Precision, Recall, and F1 Score: These metrics are particularly useful for imbalanced datasets. Precision is the proportion of correctly predicted positive instances out of all instances predicted as positive. Recall is the proportion of correctly predicted positive instances out of all actual positive instances. The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, providing a balance between the two.","# Dataset Description

Freshness: This column likely contains information about the freshness of a product or service. It could be a rating, score, or label indicating how fresh something is perceived to be. For example, in a movie review dataset, ""freshness"" might indicate whether a movie review is positive or negative.

Review: This column likely contains the actual text of the review. It could be a customer review, critic review, or any other type of written feedback about a product, service, or experience. The review text provides context for the freshness rating and can be used to analyze the sentiment or content of the review.

To analyze this dataset, you could perform text analysis on the ""review"" column to extract insights about the reviews, such as sentiment analysis to understand the overall sentiment expressed in the reviews. You could also analyze the relationship between the ""freshness"" rating and the content of the reviews to see if there are any patterns or trends.",False,True
7871,probabilistic-forecasting-i-temperature,"# Overview

Welcome to the  **Probabilistic forecasting I: Temperature** competition.

To get going the following Starter notebooks are provided:
* [[PFI Starter from NannyML] - Locally Weighted PI](https://www.kaggle.com/code/rabitwhte/pfi-starter-from-nannyml-locally-weighted-pi) thanks to the [NannyML](https://www.nannyml.com/) team
* [[PFI Starter] Nixtla Statsforecast TBATS](https://www.kaggle.com/code/predaddict/pfi-starter-nixtla-statsforecast-tbats) written by Valeriy Manokhin ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/valeriy-manokhin-phd-mba-cqf-704731236/))
* [[PFI Starter] Skforecast example](https://www.kaggle.com/code/carlmcbrideellis/pfi-starter-skforecast-example) written by Carl McBride Ellis
----------------------
# Description

The task is to submit an ensemble of quantile predictions corresponding to the following symmetric prediction intervals: 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90 and 95% coverages, along with the ""zero coverage"" 0.5 quantile, *i.e.* the median point forecast (for a list of the specific quantiles see the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/probabilistic-forecasting-i-temperature/data) section).
----------------------
# Evaluation

The leaderboard score is calculated as the mean of all the continuous ranked probability scores (CRPS), calculated  for each timestamp where the CRPS is given by

$$ CRPS(F,y) = \int \left( F(x)  - ??(x \ge y)\right)^2 ~ dx $$

which can be split into two integrals, one either side of the true value

$$ CRPS(F,y) = \int_{-\infty}^{y}  F(x) ^2 ~ dx  + \int_y^{\infty}  \left(F(x) -1 \right)^2 ~ dx $$

where \\(F\\) is the forecast [cumulative distribution function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulative_distribution_function), ?? is the [Heaviside step function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heaviside_step_function) and \\(y\\) is the ground truth value.

Example for a single timestamp:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4051350%2F70ff697cf2f8212eb59ca2ffa6d8db18%2Frevised_CRPS_graphic.png?generation=1711524802331156&alt=media)

Here no *a priori* parametric distribution is assumed for the forecast CDF, thus the CRPS is evaluated numerically from our ensemble of quantiles using the [trapezoidal rule](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trapezoidal_rule).

Visually we can see that, the closer the forecast CDF is to the empirical CDF the smaller the area, thus the smaller the CRPS. In the special case of a single point forecast the CRPS reduces to become the absolute error.

* [James E. Matheson, Robert L. Winkler ""*Scoring Rules for Continuous Probability Distributions*"", Management Science **22** pages 1087-1096 (1976)](https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.22.10.1087)
* [Tilmann Gneiting and Adrian E Raftery ""*Strictly Proper Scoring Rules, Prediction, and Estimation*"", Journal of the American Statistical Association, **102**, pp. 359-378 (2007)](https://doi.org/10.1198/016214506000001437) (Section 4.2)
* [Micha?l Zamo, Philippe Naveau ""*Estimation of the Continuous Ranked Probability Score with Limited Information and Applications to Ensemble Weather Forecasts*"", Math Geosci **50** pages 209-234 (2018)](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11004-017-9709-7)
* [Johannes Bracher, Evan L. Ray, Tilmann Gneiting, Nicholas G. Reich ""*Evaluating epidemic forecasts in an interval format*"",  PLOS Computational Biology **17** e1008618 (2021)](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008618) 

### Calculation

A [utility script](https://www.kaggle.com/discussions/product-feedback/91185) has been provided to facilitate local evaluation of ones work in the notebook [[Utility Script] CRPS score](https://www.kaggle.com/code/carlmcbrideellis/utility-script-crps-score)

To import the script, when editing look on the right hand side and go to **Notebook** then **+ Add Input** and search for ""`[Utility Script] CRPS score`"" then attach the metric script to your work with ¨’, then:

```python
import utility_script_crps_score as us
help(us.crps)

us.crps(submission_df, solution_df)
```

(Note that the CRPS integral spans from \\(-\infty\\) to \\(\infty\\), representing 100% coverage. However here, for competition reasons, the numerical integral is fixed to span from -30 to +60).

----------------------
# Submission File


Ostensibly the submission file has an `id` column, followed by 21 columns representing the predictions for quantile values as a result of using the 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90 and 95% symmetric coverages, along with the ""zero coverage"" 0.5 quantile, *i.e.* the median point forecast. See the `sample_submission.csv` for an example.

Note that both the actual column names are simply indicative as the names are not used, and the order of the columns is also unimportant as the \\( x \\) values are sorted in the scoring routine so that there is no quantile crossing.
----------------------
# Acknowledgements

The competition hosts would like to thank Valeriy Manokhin, PhD, MBA, CQF ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/valeriy-manokhin-phd-mba-cqf-704731236/)) and both Jakub Bia?ek ([Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/rabitwhte)/[LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/bialekjakub/)) and Wojtek Kuberski ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/wojtek-kuberski/)) from [NannyML](https://www.nannyml.com/) for their work in \\(\beta\\)-testing this competition  and making excellent suggestions for improvements.

(Note: The \\(\beta\\)-testers have at no point had access to the competition solution file, thus are totally free to participate in the competition)
