7873,gvu-spring-2024-project-2,"# Overview

Welcome to the second project of DATA-448 in Spring-2024. In this project, students will build regression models to predict a numeric variable.
----------------------
# Description

Submissions are evaluated on mean absolute error.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a number for the Concrete compressive strength variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Concrete compressive strength(MPa, megapascals)
    1,35
    2,35
    3,35
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

* Cement (component 1) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Blast Furnace Slag (component 2) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Fly Ash (component 3) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Water (component 4) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Superplasticizer (component 5) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Coarse Aggregate (component 6) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Fine Aggregate (component 7) -- quantitative -- kg in a m3 mixture -- Input Variable
* Age -- quantitative -- Day (1~365) -- Input Variable
* Concrete compressive strength -- quantitative -- MPa -- Target Variable",False,True
7874,nitro-language-processing-3,"# Overview

 
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, participants will be challenged to develop a pipeline for binary text classification in the Romanian language. Specifically, the task is classifying between satirical and non-satirical news. The data for this competition has been collected from a series of real and composed news.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Balanced accuracy is a performance metric designed to address class imbalances within a classification problem. It computes the average accuracy across different classes, adjusting for their relative frequencies in the dataset.

Submissions will be evaluated based on balanced accuracy, which measures the overall classification performance while considering class imbalances. The balanced accuracy is defined as:

$$Balanced Accuracy = \frac{1}{2}(\frac{TP}{TP + FN} + \frac{TN}{TN + FP})$$

Where TP represents true positives, TN true negatives, FP false positives, and FN false negatives.

### Submission File
For each ID in the test set, participants are required to predict a class for the TARGET variable. The submission file should adhere to the following format:

    ID,CLASS
    2,0
    5,1
    6,0
    etc.

where 0 denotes not-satire, while 1 signifies satire.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **random_seed_setter.py** - simple python script for setting all the random seeds to a certain value so that the experiments will have replicability
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `title` - title of the article
*   `content`?- content of the article (do note that sometimes the content field may contain only one paragraph and not the entire article as it was extracted)
*   `label` - satirical / non-satirical (encoded with 1 / 0)
Note: Some articles may not have either title or content, but all the articles have at least one field non-empty

## Random Seed Setting

In the random_seed_setter.py there is code provided to you that you should use throughout your code by importing in all your working files if you work with "".py"" files or by pasting it in a cell if you are working with "".ipynb"" files when using something like Jupyter Notebook, Kaggle Notebooks or Google Colab.

Changing your seed will not significantly influence your score, but if you feel that your code can do better with a different one you can do that as long as you specify your seed at the beginning.",False,True
