7888,byu-locating-bacterial-flagellar-motors-v2,"# Overview

Help locate bacterial flagellar motors in cryo-electron microscopy scans using machine learning models
----------------------
# Description

## Introduction
The bacterial flagellum is a nano-machine known for its specialized cell-wall-mounted engine. This engine harnesses chemical energy to turn a filament, thereby allowing the bacterium to navigate towards nutrient-rich environments.

Cryogenic electron tomography (cryo-ET) offers a view of these motors inside cells. Samples are prepared by rapid freezing, preserving cells in non-crystalline ice.  These frozen samples are imaged by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) at different angles relative to the imaging beam; this ""tilt-series'' is then computationally reconstructed into a tomogram of the cell¡¯s volume.

![](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Cryogenic_electron_microscopy_workflow.svg/1920px-Cryogenic_electron_microscopy_workflow.svg.png)

[Here](https://www.cellstructureatlas.org/6-2-flagellar-motor.html) is a video that walks through slices of the tomogram highlighting the different complexes that make up the bacteria. 

Sub-tomogram averaging is a technique used to enhance the signal-to-noise ratio and improve the resolution of macromolecular structures within sub-cellular volumes. The images below present an example of a set of sub-tomograms being layered and averaged to produce a higher resolution picture of the flagellar motor. The averaging process enhances the structural details common to the aligned sub-tomograms while reducing noise. The refined structure can be interpreted and analyzed to gain insights into the biological processes occurring within the cell.

Sub-tomogram averaging necessitates numerous images, generally identified manually. Below is an example of sub-tomogram averaging. The top images are averaged to produce the larger, clearer image below.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4640061%2F3b0a259fdbbb49740c4d8ec6420ba9a5%2Fsub_tomo.jpg?generation=1710191595195251&alt=media)

In this contest, your task is to build a machine learning model capable of processing 2D images of bacteria to identify the location of the flagellar motor. The data provided are slices of tomograms from different bacteria with a corresponding label that denotes the width, height, and center coordinates of the bounding box for the flagellar motor (if present).
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will be evaluated using a proximity scoring function. The evaluation metric contains a circular region surrounding the motor. If your x,y coordinate prediction is within 20 pixels of the original truth (x,y), you will be given full credit for that image. There is a linear dropoff depending on how far your guess is from the full-credit scoring region, and a threshold radius of 40 pixels at which you are given a score of 0. 
If the truth label is ""no motor visible"" and the prediction label is ""no motor visible"" a score of 1 is received. If the truth label is ""no motor visible"" and the prediction label is ""motor visible"" a score of 0 is received.
If the truth label is ""motor visible"" and the prediction label is ""no motor visible"" a score of 0 is received.

Scores for all the images will be averaged to one final culminating score.

##Submission Format
You should submit a csv file with the following as column headers, in this order:<br>
- `image_name`: name of corresponding image
- `x`: x coordinate of the location of the flagellar motor
- `y`: y coordinate of the location of the flagellar motor
- `motor_visible`: this column should contain a 1 or a 0. 1 represents a motor present and 0 the absence of a motor<br>

For example:
```
image_name,x,y,motor_visible
0001.png,50,100,1 
```
This indicates that image Hylemonella_20009 has a motor present at the coordinates (50, 100).<br>
An example of an image containing no motor:
```
0002.png,-1.0,-1.0,0
```
This indicates that image Hylemonella_20009 has no motor present.","# Dataset Description

As seen below, a diverse assortment of bacterial species showcase the presence of flagellar motors. Our dataset encompasses a few different species. However, for this competition, you can anticipate encountering a predominant number of images featuring the **pseudomonas aeruginosa** species. While the understanding function of flagellar motors is not essential to this contest, it is important to know that flagellar motors generally share a structural core across most species, each species has also evolved variations of the flagellar motor to respond its unique needs. This variety can make visual recognition of the flagellar motor more challenging across species.

![Chen S, Beeby M, Murphy GE, Leadbetter JR, Hendrixson DR, Briegel A, Li Z, Shi J, Tocheva EI, M¨¹ller A, Dobro MJ, Jensen GJ. Structural diversity of bacterial flagellar motors. EMBO J. 2011 Jun 14;30(14):2972-81. doi: 10.1038/emboj.2011.186. PMID: 21673657; PMCID: PMC3160247.](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4640061%2F485c488741b09f29effade8fe117a8f1%2Fdiverse_motors.jpg?generation=1710191560505352&alt=media)
<small>Chen S, Beeby M, Murphy GE, Leadbetter JR, Hendrixson DR, Briegel A, Li Z, Shi J, Tocheva EI, M¨¹ller A, Dobro MJ, Jensen GJ. Structural diversity of bacterial flagellar motors. EMBO J. 2011 Jun 14;30(14):2972-81. doi: 10.1038/emboj.2011.186. PMID: 21673657; PMCID: PMC3160247.</small>

## What should I expect the data format to be?
The **train** directory contains of images of bacteria, with corresponding `x` and `y` points indicating the location of each identified flagella motor. **NOTE: (0,0) is the index of the top left most pixel.** There are also many 'negative' images that do not contain a flagellar motor. Each image was created by averaging 15 tomogram slices.

The train.csv data simplifies the information, where the `image_name` corresponds to the filename of a given image. Each row in train_labels.csv represents a point associated with a flagella motor, and an indication of the presence/absence of a motor denoted by the `visiblemotor` element as a 0 or 1, where 0 indicates no motor and 1 indicates that there is a motor.

The **val** directory contains images of bacteria that are used to score your submission. You should submit a .csv file in the same format indicated below. This .csv file will be scored based off of the labels of these validation images.

## What am I predicting?
You are tasked with predicting points where the flagella motor is located when they are present. If there are no flagella motors in an image, your prediction should include no point (This will be represented in your submission file by a 0 in the motor_visible column and -1.0 in the `x` and `y` columns)

## Files
train.csv: Training data (labels)
train: Directory with 2770 512x512 .png training images of bacteria
sample_submission.csv: Sample submission file in the correct format
validation_labels.csv: You will not have access to this file. This is the file that is used to score your submission. You will see your score based on 50% of the rows in this file. The other 50% of the data will be kept private for use in our final scoring.
validation: Directory containing images that will be used for scoring

## Column info
image_name: Unique image ID
x: the x direction pixel number of motor
y: the y direction pixel number of motor
motor_visible: Contains either 0 or 1. 0 indicates no motor, 1 indicates a motor.

Feel free to explore and utilize the provided files to develop your machine learning models.

Use your Kaggle API key and the command below to download the dataset to your local computer.

NOTE: all images are 512x512",True,True
