7890,web-mining-competition,"# Overview

Penambangan web (web mining) merupakan salah satu bentuk penambangan data di mana data yang diperoleh berasal dari web. Ada berbagai metode untuk melakukan penarikan data dari web, diantaranya adalah crawling dan scrapping. Pada tugas ini, Anda telah diberikan dataset hasil crawling dari situs berita kompas.com untuk berita trending dari 8 April 2024 - 12 April 2024. Anda diminta untuk melakukan clustering untuk menentukan kelompok topik berita yang trending pada periode tersebut.
----------------------
# Description

Diberikan dataset dengan kolom:
- ID -> bersifat unik dan menandakan nomor berita
- Title -> judul berita
- Urls -> link berita

Anda perlu untuk melakukan text preprocessing dari data yang ada untuk kemudian dianalisis menggunakan clustering untuk mencari jumlah topik yang relevan. Dalam pencarian jumlah topik, Anda dapat menggunakan metode-metode yang sudah dipelajari di kelas (elbow method, silhouette index) dan melakukan hyperparameter tuning. Nantinya model Anda akan diimplementasikan pada test case yang sudah dibuat untuk diuji apakah jumlah pengelompokan topik dan identifikasi topik sudah sesuai. Model Anda akan diuji dengan menggunakan kriteria **adjusted_rand_index** terhadap hasil asli nya. Untuk dokumentasi selengkapnya bisa mengunjungi link berikut: [https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.adjusted_rand_score.html](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.adjusted_rand_score.html)

Anda dibebaskan untuk menggunakan library machine learning, NLP, ataupun library pendukung lainnya (tidak terbatas pada pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, nltk).

Hasil yang dikumpulkan di Kaggle adalah hasil prediksi dalam bentuk csv dengan format kolom berurutan sebagai berikut:
- ID (integer)
- Cluster (integer)

Kemudian Anda juga diminta untuk mengumpulkan file code dalam bentuk ipynb dengan format nama file **Assignment8_NIP_Nama.ipynb** melalui scele dan wajib memberikan komentar pada code terkait tahapan yang dilakukan.

Untuk pengumpulan di Kaggle dihimbau untuk menggunakan nama asli. Dilarang keras menyontek. Plagiarisme tidak akan ditoleransi dan akan dikenai penalti maksimal nilai akhir E.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Model Anda akan dievaluasi terhadap hasil asli dengan kriteria **adjusted_rand_index**.","# Dataset Description

Dataset terdiri dari 2 file:
- Train data -> Dataset Publish 230424.xlsx
- Test data -> Sample Case 230424.xlsx

Setiap dataset terdapat kolom:
- ID -> bersifat unik dan menandakan nomor berita
- Title -> judul berita
- Urls -> link berita",False,True
7891,mev-detection-ethereum,"# Overview

The world of blockchain and cryptocurrency is constantly evolving, with new opportunities and challenges emerging at a rapid pace. One such challenge is the phenomenon known as Maximal Extractable Value (MEV). This occurs when miners or validators exploit their ability to include, exclude, or reorder transactions within a block to their advantage. 

There are various types of MEV attacks that actors execute to gain an advantage. Among these, one notable strategy involves the use of bots that monitor the mempool for transactions indicating pending price updates. These bots are designed to identify potential liquidation opportunities, capitalizing on the brief window between the detection of a price change and its official recording on the blockchain.


This specific type of MEV attack, often referred to as ""backrunning,"" exploits the inherent latency in transaction processing. By adjusting the gas price, attackers can strategically position their transaction immediately after a crucial price update, ensuring their action takes precedence in the execution order. This tactic relies on a sophisticated understanding of blockchain dynamics and transaction fee economics, enabling the attacker to execute liquidations or other trades with a higher degree of certainty about the market's immediate future direction.


Backrunning, while considered one of the less harmful forms of MEV attacks compared to frontrunning or sandwich attacks, underscores the complexity and competitive nature of blockchain interactions. 

The objective of this competition is to use the power of machine learning to detect potential MEV extraction opportunities within the Ethereum network. Participants are tasked with designing models that can identify these activities.

To facilitate your analysis, real-time data can be accessed via [Bitquery's Mempool API](https://docs.bitquery.io/docs/examples/mempool/mempool-api/). This resource offers a comprehensive view of the Ethereum mempool, providing critical insights into in-flight transactions, recommended fees, token trades, and more. Participants are encouraged to leverage this API to fuel their predictive models and pattern recognition algorithms.

**Support**

For any queries or technical difficulties, participants are encouraged to reach out through the following channels:

Telegram: Join the conversation on https://t.me/Bloxy_info for community support and discussion.
Support Ticket: For critical issues, please create a ticket at support.bitquery.io.
----------------------
# Description

There have been several efforts to detect MEV transactions and label the MEV transactions into sandwich, arbitrage, liquidations, and so on.


#### What You Need to Do:

1.  **Data Analysis**: Start by analyzing Ethereum mempool data to identify patterns and signatures characteristic of MEV transactions. Understand the different types of MEV strategies and how they manifest in transaction data.
    
2.  **Model Development**: Develop a machine learning model or any computational approach that can accurately detect and classify MEV transactions based on your analysis. Your model should be able to predict the occurrence of an MEV transaction and its type before it gets executed in a block.
    
3.  **Prediction Accuracy**: Ensure your model's predictions are as accurate and timely as possible. The goal is to identify potential MEV transactions with high precision and recall, providing insights into the nature of the transaction and its timing relative to block creation.
    
4.  **Documentation and Reproducibility**: Document your methodology, including data preprocessing, model selection, training process, and evaluation metrics. Ensure your code is well-commented and organized for reproducibility.

#### Submission Requirements:

-   A detailed report outlining your approach, model architecture, evaluation results, and insights gained from the mempool data analysis.
-   A GitHub repository containing all the code used in your project, including data preprocessing scripts, model training code, and prediction scripts. Please ensure your repository is shareable with the competition organizers.
    -   The repository should include a `README.md` file that provides an overview of the project, setup instructions, and a guide to running the code.
- We will not publish or use it without compensating you appropriately


----------------------
# Evaluation

#### Evaluation Criteria:

Submissions will be evaluated based on the following criteria:

-   **Accuracy of MEV Detection**: Ability to correctly identify and classify MEV transactions.
-   **Predictive Performance**: Accuracy of predicting the specific transaction executing the MEV strategy in a future block.
-   **Innovation and Creativity**: Novelty of the approach and techniques used in detecting and predicting MEV transactions.
-   **Documentation and Code Quality**: Clarity of documentation, organization, and readability of the code.

---

## Submission File

For each transaction ID in the test set, you must predict a binary outcome for the MEV_DETECTED variable, where 1 indicates MEV activity is detected and 0 indicates no MEV activity is detected. Your submission file should include a header and be formatted as follows:

```javascript
TRANSACTION_ID,MEV_DETECTED
tx1234,1
tx1235,0
tx1236,1
etc.

```

Please ensure that your submission file strictly adheres to this format, as any deviations could result in errors during the evaluation process or potentially disqualify your entry.","# Dataset Description



The dataset provided as part of this competition is a preliminary sample to get you started. Participants interested in expanding their dataset for a more comprehensive analysis and improved model accuracy can access additional data for free by signing up for the developer plan available [here](https://account.bitquery.io/auth/signup).

  

#### Dataset Overview:

The initial dataset consists of recent trades and transactions from the Ethereum mempool, captured at the time of competition creation. This dataset is designed to give you a foundational understanding of the kind of data you will be working with and to assist in the early stages of your model development.

  

-   **kaggle-mempool-trades.json**: This file contains a sample of 1000 trades from the Ethereum mempool. Each trade is a potential candidate for MEV activity, and your task is to analyze these trades for patterns that could indicate such strategies.
    
      
    
-   **kaggle_mempool_tx.json**: This file includes 10000 mempool transactions. These transactions represent a broader set of activities within the Ethereum mempool and serve as a critical resource for identifying and predicting MEV transactions.
    
      
    

#### Utilizing the Data:

To effectively use this data, participants are encouraged to:

  

-   Analyze the structure and contents of the provided JSON files to understand the types of information available about each trade and transaction.
-   Consider the relationships between different data points, such as the timing of transactions, gas prices, and transaction values, which could indicate MEV strategies.
-   Explore additional data obtained through the developer plan for a more extensive dataset that could uncover more complex patterns and improve the accuracy of your MEV detection model.

The provided dataset is a starting point for your exploration into MEV detection within the Ethereum blockchain. By leveraging this data, along with additional information obtained through the developer plan, you have the opportunity to develop a robust solution that addresses the challenge of detecting and classifying MEV transactions with high precision.",False,True
7892,dlav-vehicle-trajectory-prediction-medium,"# Overview

In this project, you will learn to use the concepts we have seen in the lectures and practiced in the labs on a challenging real-world problem. You will develop a framework that is able to predict vehicle trajectories. 


----------------------
# Evaluation

ADE: Average Displacement Error, which measures the mean squared error between the predicted and ground truth trajectory across all time frames.

minADE_6:  **The leaderboard is using minADE_6**, which is the minimum ADE across 6 future trajectory predictions.","# Dataset Description


