7916,bird-genus-multi-class-image-classification,"# Overview

Below is an overview for this competition:
----------------------
# Description

# Goals of the competition

In this competition, you will be using images of birds to classify the genus a particular bird belongs to. Train a model that accurately predicts the class for the test set images.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is F1 score (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). It is the harmonic mean of the precision and recall.


## Submission File
For each **id** in the test set, you must predict a class for the **genus** variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,genus
    2, Strigidae
    5, Muscicapidae
    6, Motacillidae","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description

In this competition, you will predict the genus of a particular bird seen in the image. The dataset is composed of over 4000 images, where 25 random images of each class (genus) are put into the test set, and the rest can be used for training. There are 27 classes in total, representing the different genera a particular bird belongs to. All images are the same size (300x300x3).

## File Descriptions and Data Field Information

**train.csv** - training set information (id, filename, genus)
**test.csv** - test set information (id, filename)
**sample_submission.csv** - sample submission file in the correct format (id, genus)

## Columns

*   `id` - identifying number for image
*   `filename`?- image path
*   `genus` - predicted class",False,True
7917,surrogate-playground-series-s4e4,"# Description

## Welcome to the surrogate version of the 2024 Kaggle's Playground S4E4!

The original competition is [**here**](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/playground-series-s4e4) and both its data size and shape are mimicked in this competition. **The main difference is that in this competition we have known distributions between train and test datasets, which are uniform. The same is true for the public and private subsets within test data.**

Because this dataset mimics the S4E4 data sizes and column names, all scripts that work for the original S4E4 dataset should work in this competition as well without any modifications.

## Why take this competition?

Hopefully, it will give you some idea how well your approaches work when train and test data are known to have identical distributions. If the same approach gives you higher placement in the original S4E4 Abalone competition than in this one, there is a chance are that you are overfitting in the original S4E4 competition.

At the end of this competition, which is a week before the original S4E4 Abalone competition deadline, the public and private data subsets will be released. It may help with the planning of your final submissions.

## Synthetically-Generated Datasets

While we don't know the exact way Kaggle makes these synthetic datasets for playground competitions, some hints and breadcrumbs indicate that they are GAN-based. For this competition [**SDV**](https://github.com/sdv-dev/CTGAN) package was used. While Kaggle chose to make a synthetic Abalone S4E4 dataset easier than the original (RMSLE scores of ~0.145 vs ~0.17, respectively), for this competition the dataset was synthesized to be closer to the original. Expect best RMSLE scores at ~0.165.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error**. See [**the explanation for this metric**](www.kaggle.com/competitions/playground-series-s4e4/overview/evaluation) in the original Playground S4E4 competition.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `Rings`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Rings
    90615,10
    90616,10
    90617,10
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - April 7, 2027
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  April 23, 2024

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

**Please note:** There are no official awards for this competition. Hopefully your reward will be that taking part in this competition will help with the actual S4E4 Abalone competition. Top 3 scorers and their solutions will be recognized in a S4E4 Abalone discussion post.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [**Abalone**](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/rodolfomendes/abalone-dataset) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Rings` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict `Rings`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
