7932,t16-tree-species-segmentation,"# Overview

Considering our passion for nature and our drive to develop segmentation models, we've embarked on a journey with the tree dataset. 

The goal of this challenge is to tackle one of the primary challenges in forestry: semantic segmentation of trees 

By accurately segmenting trees from images, we can gain valuable insights into forest health and composition, which is crucial for forest management and conservation efforts. 
----------------------
# Description

The dataset is open-source and consists UAV-based RGB-orthomosaics (drone pilot images) and corresponding tree species masks over temperate forests in the Southern Black Forest in Germany. The usage of aerial photography captured by UAVs (Unmanned Aerial Vehicles) in remote sensing applications has seen a significant boost due to their size and flexibility making them advantegous over satellite imagery.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Dice Coefficient.

The evaluation is done on the testing set that is divided into public and private sets. Public test set results are available immediately after the submission. Private test results will be shown after the competition deadline.

You are allowed to make 10 submissions per day. You can choose 2 submission for the final evaluation, so the best score out of 2 will be used in the private leaderboard.","# Dataset Description

This is a short overview of the data files that are needed for the competition. We strongly recommend to use the starter notebook in **Code** to explore the structure of the data.

## Files

*   **X_train.npy** - numpy array with training images
*   **y_train.npy** - numpy array with masks
*   **X_test.npy** -  numpy array with test images
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7933,team9,"# Overview

The binary segmented dataset of squirrels comprises a collection of images featuring squirrels that have been segmented into binary form. Each image in the dataset represents a distinct scene where one squirrel is present, and the images have been segmented by hand to isolate the squirrels from the background. In total we labeled 450 images which we then split into train and test sets. We chose to split the dataset with the ratio 80:20. There are 100 images in the final validation set.
We didn¡¯t perform any augmentation on the dataset since we decided to leave it to the participants.

Dice is used as evaluation metric

**The predicted mask need to be the same size as the original image when they are submitting**","# Dataset Description

The binary segmented dataset of squirrels comprises a collection of images featuring squirrels that have been segmented into binary form. Each image in the dataset represents a distinct scene where one squirrel is present, and the images have been segmented by hand to isolate the squirrels from the background.

## Folders

