7937,sfhmmy-xanthi-2024-algorithms,"# Overview

This challenge is about comparison-based searching with cost for each comparison operation. Participants are challenged to develop an algorithm that is given a range of integer values and has to identify the target value in this range by using a given comparison function. Each comparison has a cost which however depends on the the outcome of the comparison. If the algorithm guesses below the target, the comparison costs a, if it guesses above, it costs b, where a and b are positive integers. If the algorithms guesses correctly the target, there is no cost for the operation.

Given a range of integer values, the worst-case cost, i.e. the maximum cost, of the search algorithm on any target in the range, is the score of the algorithm. Consequently, The objective is to develop a search algorithm of minimum worst-case cost.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Each call of the comparison function compare(value) has the following behavior:
- if **value < target**, it **returns -1**, and the **comparison cost is a**,
- if **value > target**, it **returns 1**, and the **comparison cost is b**, 
- if **value == target**, it **returns 0**, and the **comparison cost is 1**. 

For example, given a range of integer values for 0 up to 2000 (inclusive), the target value can be any of the integer values 0, ..., 2000. The search algorithm has to identify the target value with minimum worst-case cost.
More precisely, given a range of integer values, the search algorithm will be evaluated on each possible target value. 

If the search algorithm fails to find the target in one or more cases, than the submission is considered **failed**.
If the search algorithm finds the target correctly in all cases, than the **score** of the submission is the maximum (i.e. the worst-case) cost to find any single target.

A toy-case example: if the range is [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] and the submitted algorithm finds each possible target correctly, with costs 5, 8, 4, 12, 9, respectively, then the score of the submission is 12.

## Submission File
You must submit your version of the search notebook (Code Tab) with your implementation of the search function. The notebook will generate a csv file. You are not permitted to submit a csv file that has been generated offline. Moreover, for a notebook submission to be considered valid you have to follow the rules specified in the Rules tab and share the note the notebook with the accounts pefraimi and vasilisperifanis, to facilitate the verification of the submission.","# Dataset Description

None",False,True
7938,hand-sign-classification-cv-in-machine-learning,"# Overview

Communication is a cornerstone of human interaction, and sign language is one of the most profound ways individuals convey thoughts and emotions non-verbally. Recognizing the diversity and richness of hand sign languages, this competition aims to bridge the gap between technological advancements and accessibility by developing algorithms capable of classifying hand sign languages accurately.

## Competition Goals 
Participants will engage with a meticulously curated dataset, featuring a diverse range of hand sign expressions corresponding to 10 unique classes. This competition is structured to encourage breakthroughs in image recognition and gesture classification to interact more naturally with human users, especially those using sign language as their primary mode of communication.
----------------------
# Description

The dataset provided is a collection of visual sign language elements that represent key communicative gestures. Through advanced image recognition and machine learning techniques, the goal is to achieve precise interpretation of these signs. The challenge emphasizes the importance of nuanced gesture recognition, contextual understanding, and the minimization of classification error, which are crucial for real-world application and the improvement of AI interaction with sign language users.

## Technical notes
- To display images or use visualization in your model's analysis, employ libraries such as Matplotlib, OpenCV, or PIL in Python.
- For model development, consider using deep learning frameworks like TensorFlow or PyTorch for their powerful image processing and neural network capabilities.
`# Example code to load an image using OpenCV
import cv2
image = cv2.imread('path_to_image.jpg')
cv2.imshow('Hand Sign', image)
cv2.waitKey(0)
`

## Equations and Modeling
Some participants may wish to implement or reference mathematical models or equations in their approach. For example, if you're calculating the geometric features of hand signs, you might use an equation like this:

$$ \text{Feature Ratio} = \frac{\text{Width of Hand Sign}}{\text{Height of Hand Sign}} $$

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


---



## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

For the Hand Sign Language Classification Challenge, we have prepared a set of image files organized into separate folders for training and testing. Our goal is to make models that can classify these images into ten different hand sign language words. Below, you will find a description of the file you need to develop and evaluate your models with.

**Files  Provided**

**train/** - This folder contains labelled images to train the model. Each image is named as [classname]_[id].jpg
**val/** - This folder contains unlabelled images for model validation. Each image is named as [id].jpg
**test.csv** - This file contains contains a table with the id, label and usage of each train image

**Data Format**

The images are provided in JPEG format, which can be processed using most image processing libraries. Participants should expect to work with raw image files and may need to implement or use pre-existing image processing pipelines to extract features suitable for their models.

**Prediction Task**

The challenge is to classify each image in the test/ folder as one of the ten hand sign words provided in the training set. The submission should be a CSV file with the predicted labels for each test image.

**Expected Submission Format**

Your submission file should be a CSV with two columns: image_id, label, and usage. The image_id should match the filename of the test images without the file extension, and the label should be the predicted word represented by the hand sign in the image.

Before you start working with the dataset, please ensure you understand the file structure and the format required for submissions. If there are any questions or issues with the data, don't hesitate to reach out in the competition forum.

Good luck, and we look forward to seeing your innovative approaches to sign language classification!",False,True
