7940,where-am-i-emajogi,"# Overview

Welcome to the ""Wherami?"" Kaggle competition! This unique challenge tests the capability of deep learning models to estimate the distance to a designated reference point using a novel regression dataset. Our dataset, based around visual captures from the Emaj?gi river, provides a real-world scenario to advance regression modeling techniques.
----------------------
# Description

The ""Wherami"" dataset comprises images taken along a roughly 2km stretch on both sides of the Emaj?gi river, captured over multiple passes to ensure variety. Each image features views of the river, the sky, and the surrounding environment, captured mostly perpendicular to the river's flow.

Training Set: Consists of video frames converted into images, captured by a GPS-enabled camera app, and labeled with precise distances from a predefined anchor point. The training images are collected from specific sections along the river to facilitate focused learning.
Test Set: The test images are similarly gathered but from different passes and include challenging segments not covered in the training set.

All of the images are of shape 1280x720. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on their Mean Squared Error (MSE) against the true distances. The lower the MSE, the more accurate and effective the model is considered.","# Dataset Description

The ""Where am I"" dataset consists of images captured along a roughly 2-kilometer path on both sides of the Emaj?gi River, featuring consistent views of the river, sky, and the surrounding environment. The images were taken perpendicularly to the river's flow, ensuring a standardized perspective.

The dataset was created by splitting videos into images at intervals of every third second. No preprocessing has been applied to the images, leaving participants free to preprocess and augment the data as they see fit.

Labeling was achieved using a GPS-camera app that provided continuous location data. Images are labeled with their distance from a designated anchor point, calculated through cumulative sums of distances between consecutive image locations.

The total size of the dataset is 1253 images:
- 1033 training images
- 220 testing images",False,True
