7948,trashcan-segmentation-public,"# Overview

The TrashCan dataset is comprised of annotated images (7,212 images currently) which contain observations of trash, ROVs, and a wide variety of undersea flora and fauna. The imagery in TrashCan is sourced from the J-EDI (JAMSTEC E-Library of Deep-sea Images) dataset, curated by the Japan Agency of Marine Earth Science and Technology (JAMSTEC). The aim of this dataset is to develop and refine waste management solutions and recycling systems, contributing to environmental sustainability efforts.

In this competition, we will only be interested in the segmentation of trash. We purposely modified this dataset by erasing the annotations that were not showing trash elements, and erased the trash subtypes. In a nutshell, your goal is to segment marine trash without categorizing it.

You can find the [starter_notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/yarochka270602/starter-notebook-trash-segmentation) in the Code section. It will help you to easily load and explore the data. This notebook produces the sample_submission.csv.
----------------------
# Description

There are 21,000 pieces of plastic in the ocean for each person on Earth, and plastic pollution has been doubling every six years. Only a small fraction of that gets recycled. The rest is burned, buried, or ends up in the environment, including the sea. Some estimates suggest that by 2050, there could be more plastic in the oceans, by weight, than fish. The plastic in the sea slowly breaks down into smaller and smaller pieces, known as microplastics, which are less than 5 millimeters in length and can be eaten by marine life. Detecting microplastic particles with machine vision could have really high potential because camera and computer technology have become more and more advanced over time, thus making it also a lot cheaper. Plastic has been found near the summit of Mount Everest and inside the deepest point on Earth, the Mariana Trench ¡ª as well as in the human bloodstream. 

The end goal of this project would be to develop an efficient and accurate trash detection model suitable for an autonomous trash collector submarine. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using [dice coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8rensen%E2%80%93Dice_coefficient) between the predicted mask and the correct mask.

In the given dataset, you will be provided a val-without_segmentation.json which contains the image_id of every image in the val folder. You will be asked to submit your predictions from the lowest image_id to the highest.
As said previously, we are not asking you to classify the type of trash on each image, simply where is the trash.

## Submission File
For each ID in the val set, you must predict a segmentation mask corresponding to the presence or the abscence of trash in each pixel of the image. Each masks should have pixel values equal to 0 (background) or 1 (trash). These masks then have to be flattened and finally are encoded using [Run-length encoding (RLE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run-length_encoding). An example on how to make a submission in the correct format will be given in the starter notebook.
To be noted: Some image have no trash, so if your model guesses correctly that there is no trash, you will have to submit a blank mask for this image.
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    image_id,Mask
    6066,0.0 24 1.0 56 0.0 62 ...
    6067,0.0 54 1.0 2 0.0 23 ...
    6068,1.0 20 0.0 45 1.0 2 ...
    6069,0.0 129600
    etc...","# Dataset Description

Your goal is to perform segmentation on marine trash images. You do not have to classify them, so we only ask you to submit one mask for each val image, where a 1 pixel value corresponds to the presence of trash, while a 0 corresponds to the background. 
All the images have already been resized to the same dimensions.

## Files

*   **train** - Folder containing the train images
*   **train_json** - json file inside which are contained the image_id & annotations for each train image 
! Be careful, some images have no annotations (as they do not show trash) while other images have multiple different annotations
*   **test** - Folder containing the images for which you have to predict the segmentation
*   **test-without_segmentation.json** - json file inside which are contained the image_id for each test image
! Your solution csv file must be ordered from lowest image_id to highest 
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format, only for the first test image",False,True
