# Overview

The International Conference on Pattern Recognition (ICPR) 2024 Competition on ¡°**Beyond Visible Spectrum: AI for Agriculture**¡± presents  an exciting opportunity for researchers and practitioners to advance computer vision techniques in agricultural crop disease monitoring. By leveraging the vast multi/hyper spectral remote sensing image datasets, participants are encouraged to develop innovative deep learning algorithms. The primary aim is to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of identifying crop diseases, thereby contributing to sustainable agricultural practices and global food security.

Participants in this challenge will have the chance to contribute to significant advancements in precision farming and crop monitoring techniques. By improving the deep learning vision models, the competition aims to drive forward the capabilities of agricultural technology, making it more efficient, accurate, and sustainable. The ICPR 2024 Competition on ¡°Beyond Visible Spectrum¡± is not just a competition; it is a call to action for the computer research community to bring about transformative changes in how agricultural data is analyzed and utilized, paving the way for smarter, more sustainable farming practices that can benefit communities worldwide.


Early accurate diagnosis and quantification of crop diseases are crucial for precision management by allowing targeted interventions at the field level. Effective diagnosis and control measures are dependent on symptom recognition and severity assessment (in the vast majority of cases, diseases exhibit a range of visual symptoms, e.g. coloured spots or streaks). At present, these are mainly achieved by visual observation and estimation.  

Recent advancements in digital imaging have facilitated the development of a variety of image-based methods for plant management that are capable of performing automated image acquisition and analysis. These methods have shown great potential for automated crop disease diagnosis based on images from RGB (red, green and blue), thermal, multispectral, and hyperspectral sensors.

Particularly, hyperspectral imagery (HSI), which contains more narrow spectral bands over a contiguous spectral range, provides detailed spectral¨Cspatial information of the disease infestation and offers the potential to provide better diagnostic accuracy.

In this challenge, we will mainly focus on the accurate diagnosis of crop diseases from hyperspectral imagery (in this case fusarium head blight, FHB). The benchmark model for this competition is based on our published paper on remote sensing [1]. 

[1. A Deep Learning-Based Approach for Automated Yellow Rust Disease Detection from High-Resolution Hyperspectral UAV Images](https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/13/1554)

# Competition Schedule
 
**Launch Date:** April 15, 2024

**Submission Opening:** April 15, 2024

**Submission Deadline:** July 21, 2024

**Submission of Competition Reports Deadline:**  August 18, 2024

# Awards

All submissions before the deadline will receive a participation certificate

The top three places of each task will be awarded prizes to the value of:


**1st Place:**	$300

**2nd Place:**	$200

**3rd Place:**	$100




# Challenge keywords
Remote sensing; Hyperspectral; Crop disease; Deep learning ; Fusarium Head Blight


# Workshop/conference

This challenge is part of the conference on ""The International Conference on Pattern Recognition (ICPR) (2024)"".




----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the categorization accuracy.

Three types are set as follows: Rust, Health and Other

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Category`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id	Category
0508_eeedf1ea8bf14dc5875a5ec121408f7d.pt	1
0508_d9573017385c4a27b7dfc80032a76378.pt	1
0508_142e8472d61347b68a8697a57567e8a1.pt	2
----------------------
# People

# Organisers
Prof. Liangxiu Han, Manchester Metropolitan University
Prof. Wenjiang Huang, Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences

# Technical Chairs
Dr. Xin Zhang, Manchester Metropolitan University
Dr. Yingying, Dong Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences
Tam Sobieh, Manchester Metropolitan University

# Primary contact
Liangxiu Han - Email: l.han@mmu.ac.uk
Wenjiang Huang - Email: huangwj@radi.ac.cn","# Dataset Description

The hyperspectral imagery retrieval was carried out in two times, on 3 May and 8 May 2019. On 3 May 2019 wheat in the experimental area was in the pre-grouting stage, which is a critical period for the spread of downy mildew infestation. On 8 May 2019 wheat in the experimental area was in the middle of the grouting stage, which is the period when the symptoms of downy mildew are more obvious.

A DJI M600 Pro UAV system (SZ DJI Technology Co Ltd., Gungdong, China) with a snapshot hyperspectral sensor was used for data acquisition. The model of the hyperspectral sensor was S185 which can obtain reflect radiation from the visible to near-infrared band between 450-950nm wavelength. The spectral resolution was 4nm. The raw data was recorded as a 1000*1000 panchromatic image and a 50*50 hyperspectral image with 125 bands.  Since UAV hyperspectral data are interfered by noise in post-processing, which has a significant impact on the data at both ends of the bands, the first 10 bands as well as the last 14 bands were excluded from the study, leaving 100 bands. In this work, all the images were obtained at an altitude of 60 meters, with a spatial resolution close to 4cm per pixel. 

# Dataset Overview Paper 
[A Deep Learning-Based Approach for Automated Yellow Rust Disease Detection from High-Resolution Hyperspectral UAV Images](https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/13/1554)


## Files

The training dataset<br/>
The validation dataset

##Submission Columns

*   `Id` - file name
*   `Category`?- 1: Mild FHB 2:  Serious FHB",False,True
7976,beyond-visible-spectrum-ai-for-agriculture-2024p2,"# Overview


The ""Beyond Visible Spectrum: AI for Sustainable Agriculture"" challenge unites cutting-edge hyperspectral imaging with advanced AI techniques to revolutionize precision farming. This initiative invites researchers, industry experts, and students from around the globe to develop innovative solutions that address the pressing issues of crop disease detection and data scarcity in agriculture. There are two main tasks in the challenge. 

# Hyperspectral Data Analysis for Crop Disease Classification

The first task focuses on hyperspectral data analysis for crop disease classification. Participants are tasked with designing deep learning models that accurately distinguish between diseased and healthy crop areas using real hyperspectral imagery. This approach leverages the unique capability of hyperspectral sensors¡ªwhich capture detailed spectral information beyond the visible spectrum¡ªto detect subtle physiological changes in crops. By enabling early and precise disease detection, the task aims to reduce yield losses and mitigate the economic impacts of crop diseases.

# Exploring Synthetic Hyperspectral Data Generation

The second task centers on synthetic hyperspectral data generation. Here, participants explore the potential of generative models such as GANs, VAEs, and diffusion models to create high-fidelity synthetic datasets that mirror the spectral characteristics of real agricultural imagery. This synthetic data serves as a critical augmentation tool, helping to overcome the challenges posed by limited annotated datasets and enhancing the robustness and generalization capabilities of AI models. The ability to simulate a range of crop conditions, including varying stages of disease progression, is expected to foster the development of more resilient and scalable AI-driven solutions.

The challenge is built on the success of previous competitions, which have shown growing participation and interest.  In last year, a total of 190 people from more than 10 countries in Europe, Asia and North America entered the competition by the deadline date, with a total of 126 submissions of results.

Participants will work with comprehensive, pre-processed hyperspectral remote sensing datasets obtained via UAV-mounted sensors covering wavelengths from 450 to 950 nm. These datasets, comprising high-resolution patches with rich spectral detail, provide a realistic testing ground for innovative techniques aimed at addressing modern agricultural challenges.

Ultimately, the ""Beyond Visible Spectrum"" challenge is more than just a competition¡ªit is a dynamic forum for interdisciplinary collaboration that bridges the gap between state-of-the-art AI research and real-world agricultural applications. By driving advancements in crop disease monitoring and synthetic data generation, the initiative aspires to contribute significantly to global food security and sustainable farming practices, paving the way for a smarter, more resilient future in agriculture.

# Workshop/conference
This challenge is part of the conference on ""IEEE International Conference on Image Processing, IEEE ICIP 2025"". 

----------------------
# Description

#Important Information


