# FAQ

You can find the list of common questions for this competition: [**FAQ_GoogleDoc**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GVgkA1bUmrtcXaqyi5EzPUeUNdNBI9dB_si6-50eKRY/edit?usp=sharing).
If you have further questions about this competition, feel free to fill out this [**competition QA form**](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeDvpSBrNkXlQv4lT8tGNdyjwsKYsNqHhN-zMH1tZF-m2egyQ/viewform). The organising teams will answer your questions within three (3) working days.","# Dataset Description

Our [**Form-NLU**](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3539618.3591886)  is a subset of the publicly available financial form data source for [Form 604 (notice of change of interests of the substantial] (https://asic.gov.au/regulatory-resources/forms/forms-folder/604-notice-of-change-of-interests-of-substantial-holder/) collected by SIRCA. It provides the text records of each substantial shareholder notice form submitted to the [Australian Stock Exchange (ASX)](https://www2.asx.com.au/) from 2003 to 2015. To better comprehend the form designer's intentions, we included the twelve most essential form fields. Each form field expects a **Value** from the user, which should contain the specific information being asked by the corresponding **Key** that expresses the form designer's intention.  The statistics of the dataset are shown in the following table.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2F4a6559348e7e22bbe38e012926de3d32%2Ftable.jpg?generation=1706681472291138&alt=media)

In this task, Key and Value components are defined based on the twelve Key intentions, which include: 
- (1) Company Name (com\_nm)
- (2) Company ID (com\_id)
- (3) Holder Name (hold\_nm)
- (4) Holder ID (hold\_id)
- (5) Change Date (chg\_date)
- (6) Previous Notice Given Date (gvn\_date)
- (7) Previous Notice Date (ntc\_date)
- (8) Class of Securities (class)
- (9) Previous Share (pre\_shr)
- (10) Previous Voting Percentage (pre\_pct)
- (11) Share (new\_shr)
- (12) New Voting Percentage (new\_pct)

Key (1)-(2) and Key (3)-(4) ask for the identity of the listed company and the substantial holder, respectively, whereas Key (5)-(12) query the value of the shares being changed. We simply appended the identifier Key and Value separately to the twelve(12) Key names and created twenty-four(24) labels for the key-value pairs accordingly, e.g., Each key-value pair would produce 2 labels; for example, ""Company Name Key"" and ""Company Name Value"". Based on these different key intentions, we inspected the value patterns to explore the potential noise caused by the diversified user understanding and uncertainty. 

**Note**: **We provide the official tutorials ([Data Exploration](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1EzzS6R17DGuADk8EU4IMtE5T87AwocLb?usp=sharing), [Baseline Model](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1oQisiGy8tX1CpiXSXPnZ6nZWxaaGZGyP?usp=sharing)) for assisting participants to understand the data structure and start this competition easily.** You can also find the tutorial links in under the Code menu. 

## Files
This dataset consists of three main splits: training, validation, and testing. For each split, we provide two types of files:

CSV File: This file contains the name of the image file of the document's page,  the key text, and the label represented by the global id of the \\(RoI\\) in the document. We only provide the answer for the training and validation set to support you in developing a model to predict global ID based on the given key text and the image file.

PKL File: The pkl file is a valuable resource as it contains essential document information, including the bounding box coordinates of each document layout component, textual contents inside each bounding box,  the index of the \\(RoI\\), and the image id of the document. This information is crucial for understanding the structure of the visually rich documents and identifying relevant \\(RoI\\)s that might contain the answers to the questions.

In this competition, participants can use the training dataset for developing their model, and the original validation and testing dataset will be used for leaderboard evaluation. **We offer an [official tutorial](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1oQisiGy8tX1CpiXSXPnZ6nZWxaaGZGyP?usp=sharing) to help users quickly understand the dataset format and structure, making it easier for participants to get started with the competition. Here is the link to the tutorial notebook.**

### CSV files
The dataset contains three CSV files containing different splits: training, validation, and testing. Each file contains document IDs (PMCID) and corresponding questions for each item. Note the ground truth answers (global-ID list) are only provided for the training dataset during the competition period. The ground truth for the validation dataset will be made available after the competition has finished.

   *   `train_dataframe.csv`- the training set which provides the questions and corresponding answers. 
   *   `val_dataframe` - the validation dataset which provides the questions and corresponding answers.
   *   `test_printed_dataframe.csv` - the leaderboard testing set 73% for public and 27% for private, including the printed documents.

   *   `submission.csv` - a **sample** submission file in the correct format

The details of the columns in the CSV files can be found in the data preview below.
### PKL files
We've provided detailed pkl files containing essential information about document splits. Follow our [official tutorial](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1EzzS6R17DGuADk8EU4IMtE5T87AwocLb?usp=sharing) to harness this data effectively and gain insights for building winning models. 

### Image Files
We provide the document images for each data split. Participants can use the information provided by the PKL file to locate the document images of each target document. Image files can provide additional layouts and visual cues to make the feature representation more comprehensive. 
 
The pkl files provided detailed information about each document used in the Google doc. 
With this dataset, you can explore the challenging task of training your model to predict the correct global id of the corresponding layout component. The provided files are meant to assist you in building a robust and accurate model for this competition. We hope this dataset will inspire creative solutions and foster progress in Form-NLU. Happy modelling!",False,True
8047,vrd-iu2024-trackb,"# Overview

 
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the Visually Rich Form Document Intelligence and Understanding Kaggle competition! This competition is the sub-activity of **The Second Competition on Visually Rich Document Intelligence and Understanding (VRDIU)** in conjunction with the **33rd International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI 2024)** in **Jeju, Korea**. Please go to the VRDIU [competition website](https://doc-iu.github.io/) for detailed competition schedule and important dates. The objective of this exciting competition is to develop a cutting-edge model that takes as inputs a natural language question and output the coordinates of the bounding box corresponding to the question. 

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2Fe6d8614d85ffd9a62ce2cfb4f4a2c907%2Ftrack_b.png?generation=1712377854634246&alt=media"" width = ""70%"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />

The dataset for this competition is derived from Task B of Form-NLU Dataset ([accepted by SIGIR 2023](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3539618.3591886)). This task challenges participants to develop models that can interpret designers' intentions and user uncertainties, extracting valuable information from form documents. Unlike [Track A of the Visually Rich Form Document Understanding competition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/vrd-iu2024-tracka), where participants are asked to predict bounding box coordinates from document images based on input keys, Track B does not provide ground truth bounding boxes for form semantic entities. Instead, **participants must rely solely on form image inputs and key queries during inference**. However, competitors are permitted to use the dataset from Track A to train a layout analyzer (object detection module) as a submodule. Successfully completing Track A equips participants with a crucial component for excelling in Track B. The primary evaluation metric for this track is the Mean Average Precision (MAP) of the predicted bounding boxes, emphasizing precision and accuracy in localization tasks. **The strong baseline of track B can be found when you finish track A.**

We invite participants to think beyond the conventional and push the boundaries of what's possible in document understanding. By doing so, you can contribute to developing innovative solutions with practical applications, such as automated document retrieval, question-answering systems, and intelligent information extraction. Before conducting this competition, please ensure you read and understand the **Rules** of this competition first. Please get in touch with us anytime if you get confused about this competition. 

Therefore, gather your expertise in computer vision, natural language processing, and deep learning, and join us in this competition. Engage with a vibrant community of like-minded individuals, exchange ideas, and strive for the top spot on the leaderboard. Together, let's push the boundaries of document-based visual question answering and shape the future of intelligent document analysis.

You can scan the following QR codes for more details.
    <div style=""display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;"">
    <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2Febc27421ac1e6d48a93b51eb8ecdffd5%2Fpaper.png?generation=1716777664696886&alt=media"" width=""15%"" style=""margin: 0; vertical-align: middle;"">
    <img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2F4702e014e8ed5ea6b590f9426cbb2ee0%2Fvrdiu.png?generation=1716777681548011&alt=media"" width=""15%"" style=""margin: 0; vertical-align: middle;"">
</div>

This competition is sponsored by Google Research.
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2F85dd2b01ab5aa6b43303ded31b0c4fb7%2FGR2.png?generation=1712370580265464&alt=media"" width = ""50%"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />

If you have **more questions or inquiries about this competition**, feel free to fill out this [competition QA form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeDvpSBrNkXlQv4lT8tGNdyjwsKYsNqHhN-zMH1tZF-m2egyQ/viewform). Our organising teams will answer your questions within three working days.
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use Mean Average Precision (MAP) of the predicted bounding box as the primary evaluation metric for representing overall performance. The MAP is calculated as follows:
$$mAP_{IoU} = \frac{1}{C} \sum_{i=1}^{C} AP_{IoU}^{(i)}$$
Where C is the number of classes, i.e., the number of the types of key_fix_text.
----------------------
# Kaggle Competition Submission Guidelines

Thank you for participating in our exciting competition! We are delighted to see the enthusiasm and dedication from all of you. To ensure that the competition runs smoothly and that everyone has a fair chance, we have outlined the submission guidelines below. Please follow these guidelines carefully to submit your entries successfully.

The submit button is at the right corner of the competition main page
![alt](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2Fd8c8dd49bb12e9432fee82c827dbd731%2Ftbsubb.png?generation=1712372951054090&alt=media =70%x70%)

## **Submission Components:**
1. **Leaderboard(submission.csv):**
When submitting your predictions for the Kaggle competition, please make sure to adhere to the following guidelines:

   - Submission File Name: The file containing your prediction results **must be named** **""submission.csv""**.

   - Format: Your submission file **MUST use the ""(confidence, x, y, w, h)""** of the predicted bounding box of the layout components (RoIs). Please use the ""ID"" corresponding to the ""ID"" in the test_hand_dataframe.csv file.

   - Evaluation: Ensure that the format of your submission file matches the sample submission file provided, ""submission.csv."" This will allow us to evaluate your performance correctly.

2. **Code (Notebook):**
   - The code should be well-documented, organized, and self-explanatory.
   - Use Jupyter Notebook/Kaggle Notebook/Colab Notebook as the preferred format for code submission.
   - Include all necessary libraries and dependencies required to run the code.
   - Ensure your code can be executed end-to-end without any errors.
   - Make sure to handle any external data dependencies and include instructions on how to download and use them, if applicable.
   - The code should be structured to allow your results to be reproducible.
   - Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. For the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:
      - CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
      - GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time

3. **Abstract (IJCAI Format):**
   - Prepare a one-page abstract in the [IJCAI (International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence) format] (https://ijcai24.org/call-for-workshops/)  to accompany your code submission.
   - The abstract should follow the IJCAI guidelines for formatting and describe the results and methods.
   - The results section should present your findings, experiments, and any evaluation metrics used.
   - The methods section should describe the approach and algorithms employed in your solution.
   - Make sure to highlight the strengths and limitations of your approach.
   - **Aim for a paper length of one page**.

##**Submission Process:**
###**During the competition**###
   - Submit the ""submission.csv"" file to evaluate your model's performance.
###**Final Evaluation**###
   - Compress your **submission.csv**,  **code (Notebook)** and the **Abstract** into **a single zip file**. The zip file should be named appropriately, e.g., ""YourTeamName_Submission.zip.""
   - Upload your submission to the Kaggle competition platform under the ""Submit"" tab. Participants can submit up to two zip files, which will count towards their final leaderboard score.
   - Select the two zip submissions in the **Submissions** page. If you submit fewer than two zip files, Kaggle will automatically select from your best-scoring submissions for this competition. **Note that for the final evaluation, only teams that submit at least one comprehensive zip file will be considered**.

![alt](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6506645%2Fc9e4f365983f4824f11934d2748cda96%2Ftbsubm.png?generation=1712372975349548&alt=media =70%x70%)

   - Provide a brief description of your submission and the key highlights in the ""Description"" field.
   - Ensure your submission whether the three submission components are included and that the code runs without errors.
   - Submit your entry before the competition deadline. Late submissions will not be considered.

## **Evaluation:**
- Submissions will be evaluated based on the performance on a hidden test dataset using relevant evaluation metrics, as defined in the competition guidelines.
- **Additionally**, **the clarity, innovation, and quality of the one-page abstract** will also be **taken into account** during the evaluation process.

## **Note:**
- Plagiarism and copying from other participants or external sources will not be tolerated. Any such action will lead to disqualification.
- Participate in the competition with honesty and integrity, following the competition rules and guidelines.

Good luck with your submissions! We are eager to see the remarkable work you all have done. If you have any questions or face any issues during the submission process, feel free to contact our support team.
----------------------
# Timeline

