8052,llm-for-environmental-review,"# Overview

The usage of large language models (LLMs) for text generation is now almost commonplace, and more use cases for LLMs are coming to light. One of the tasks that LLMs have become increasingly sophisticated in is retrieving information from long-context and complex subjective documents.

In this competition, we invite data scientists, natural language processing (NLP) enthusiasts, and AI researchers to develop LLMs capable of sifting through National Environmental Policy Act (NEPA) documents to accurately retrieve information with the goal of solving the environmental review question answering task.
----------------------
# Description

Under NEPA, an Environmental Impact Statement (EIS) is a comprehensive review document that analyzes the environmental impact of proposed Federal action. NEPA EISs are crucial tools for environmental management and decision-making, ensuring that potential environmental impacts are thoroughly considered and mitigated where possible for major Federal actions. Retrieving information from historical EISs could help NEPA experts expedite and improve future reviews.

This challenge aims to advance the field of information retrieval in NEPA documents by leveraging the capabilities of AI models to process, understand, and extract relevant information from long context and complex subjective documents. We focus on question and answering task to assess the ability of such models to read text and then answer questions about it. This requires both understanding of natural language and knowledge about the NEPA domain.

We release a new question and answering dataset, NEPAQuAD1.0 (National Environmental Review Question and Answering Dataset), consisting of 1,450 semi-synthetic questions and answers generated by a LLM (GPT-4) with human oversight on a set of environmental permitting documents, where the answer to each question relies on the corresponding passage from where the question is generated.

The task is to develop LLM (finetune, in-context learning, etc.) that can perform on the NEPAQuAD1.0 dataset.

## Acknowledgements
We would like to thank the Department of Energy (DOE) Office of Policy, the US DOE and Pacific Northwest National Laboratory for supporting this challenge. NEITHER THE UNITED STATES NOR THE UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF ENERGY, NOR ANY OF THEIR EMPLOYEES, MAKES ANY WARRANTY, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, OR ASSUMES ANY LEGAL LIABILITY OR RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE ACCURACY, COMPLETENESS, OR USEFULNESS OF ANY DATA, APPARATUS, PRODUCT, OR PROCESS DISCLOSED, OR REPRESENTS THAT ITS USE WOULD NOT INFRINGE PRIVATELY OWNED RIGHTS.
<div>
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F20577645%2Fe58013df009c66ed7906b1becd04b698%2FWeb%20Size-PNNL%20Horizontal%20Logo%20(.jpg).jpg?generation=1714602853845418&alt=media"" width=""200""> &nbsp; &nbsp;
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F20577645%2F075f3dcf12a4f0fa2737cae006121d41%2Foffice_of_policy_logo.png?generation=1714602985077336&alt=media"" width=""200"">
</div>
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metrics
Submissions will be automatically evaluated on using the [BLEU](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BLEU) score, which will be reflected in the leaderboard. Second, we will go beyond text similarity by leveraging more holistic scores from [GPT-4 Turbo](https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-4-turbo-and-gpt-4), which will be the evaluator to assess the factual correctness of the predicted answers. For each predicted answer, GPT-4 Turbo will give a score in between 0 and 1 reflecting whether the facts presented in the predicted answer are accurate with the ground-truth answer. These scores will be aggregated to compute the F1-score. To achieve a comprehensive evaluation, we will also engage NEPA subject matter experts to assess the performance of the models.  We aim to gain a more holistic view of the models' performance, particularly in terms of their proficiency in managing domain-specific queries.

During the training period of the competition, you may see a leaderboard with a BLEU score for the public test data. After the competition ends, we will update this leaderboard with BLEU score, BERTScore and GPT-4 scores on the private test data. Note, we will use all three metrics on private test data to select the winners. During the training period, this leaderboard will show only the rank of each team, but not the complete score.

## Submission File
You need to upload your prediction on the test set. Please tag your uploaded bundle as nepaquad1-test-submit-\<YOUR NAME>. The bundle should consist of a CSV file where for each ID in the test set, you must provide your model generated answer as PREDICTED_ANSWER. 
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

     ID,PREDICTED_ANSWER
     EIS01114,yes
     EIS01115,no
     etc.","# Dataset Description

The NEPAQuAD1.0 dataset comprises 1,548 questions and answers distributed among the training (1,079) and test (469) split. We first extracted text chunks (each chunk around a page in length) from each document. Then, we leveraged each chunk to prompt GPT-4 (OpenAI¡¯s large-scale language-generation model) for generating questions of a specific type as well as their answers and explanations. An example prompt for closed type of question is: ¡°Can you generate 10 questions given the content provided in the following paragraph? Restrict the type of generated questions as 'yes/no' questions and provide the corresponding answer and the related quotes in the document. Return ONLY the list of questions both in answer and in csv format with three columns: question/ answer/ proof. Return the csv format only, do not add other things. Content of the paragraph:'''{}'''. We used, on average, 10 chunks (2 from the beginning, 6 from the middle, and 2 from the end sections) from each document to generate questions. These synthetic questions were manually reviewed/edited to ensure quality and relevancy in the dataset. The task is to generate the response given the context and question. The prediction needs to be grounded in the context. You may wish to generate more triplets from the EIS PDFs provided in the metadata to use as training data.

However, we understand that semi-synthetic question and answer pairs may contain some noise, which refers to irrelevant or incorrect information that could potentially affect the performance of the models being tested. Despite this challenge, one of our main objectives is to assess how well the models can handle and interpret these semi-synthetic datasets. Specifically, we aim to evaluate the robustness of the models in filtering out the noise and accurately extracting the relevant information. This assessment will help us determine the models' effectiveness in a real-world setting where noisy data is common.

Along with the public test split, we will also evaluate the model on the private (hidden) test split, which is similar to public test split in terms of fields. The private test split is chosen by NEPA experts for its operational relevance and its ability to accurately reflect real-world conditions. 

## Files

*   **NEPAQuAD1_train.csv** - the training set
*   **NEPAQuAD1_test.csv** - the test set
*   **NEPAQuAD1-test-submit-XYZ.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.zip** - A set of 7 NEPA documents in PDF format from where Questions in train set have been generated

## Columns

*   `ID` - Unique ID for each document.
*   `EIS_filename` - Name of the EIS document from which question has been generated. This is not present in the test split.
*   `question_type` - Question format out of 10 different types (closed/open/evaluation/process/recall/convergent/funnel/comparison/divergent/inference).
*   `context` - Passage of the document from where the question has been generated. 
*   `question` - The question from the context that needs to be answered by the model.
*   `groundtruth_answer` -The true answer. This field is the target with which we will match the model predictions. This is not present in the test split.",False,True
8053,road-traffic-accidents,"# Overview

The data set has been prepared from manual records of road traffic accident of the year 2017-20. All the sensitive information have been excluded during data encoding and finally it has 32 features and 12316 instances of the accident. Then it is preprocessed and for identification of major causes of the accident by analyzing it using different machine learning classification algorithms. RTA Dataset.csv is the dataset before preprocessing.

**Goal:** 
First, you will clean the database. Then, you will make a prediction of the severity of each accident.
----------------------
# Description

This competition is being launched as part of the TekTopia event. This event is taking place in its inaugural version at TekUP  University.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the accuracy rate.

## Submission File
For each Num in the test set, you must predict the severity of the accident (Slight Injury: Slight, Serious Injury: Serious, Fatal Injury: Fatal).
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

   Num, Slight, Serious, Fatal
    2,0,1,0
    5,1,0,0
    6,0,0,1
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The data has been split into two groups:

- train set (train.csv)
- test set (test.csv)
The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (presented by the column ¡°Accident_severity¡±).
Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± presented on the others columns like ""Driving_experience"", ""Weather_conditions"" . You can also use feature engineering to create new features.

The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each passenger. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each passenger in the test set, use the model you trained to predict whether or not they survived the sinking of the Titanic.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set",False,True
8054,desafo-clasificacin-de-proveedores-activos,"# Overview


En esta competencia, trabajar¨¢s con un problema de clasificaci¨®n cuyo objetivo es determinar si un proveedor recibe un pedido o realiza una transacci¨®n durante los meses de marzo o abril de 2024. Queremos saber si un proveedor recibe un pedido en marzo, o si tambi¨¦n lo recibe en abril.

Entrenaras modelos con informaci¨®n de ¨®rdenes de compra que se realiza mediante la plataforma de Per¨² Compras; que funciona como un gran cat¨¢logo de productos y servicios que siempre est¨¢ comprando el Estado. Su rol principal es facilitar la articulaci¨®n de las entidades p¨²blicas con los proveedores.

La finalidad de la construcci¨®n del modelo es enfocarse en proveedores activos y fortalecer la relaci¨®n comercial e identificar patrones de compra.


----------------------
# Description

En esta competencia se les proporcionara datos sobre ordenes de compras mensuales donde cada proveedor realiza m¨²ltiples transacciones, se mostrar¨¢n solo las efectuadas o formalizadas. 
El dataset no cuenta con una variable respuesta, este debe ser construido en base al objetivo y los datos. 
Para la predicci¨®n se tomaran en cuenta todos los proveedores que se encuentran en la data de entrenamiento, el envio debe contener la misma cantidad de filas y columnas  tal como se muestra en el submission.csv 


----------------------
# Evaluation 



Los env¨ªos se eval¨²an en f1_score(binary) entre la TARGET prevista y el objetivo observado.




## Submission File
Para cada CODMES_RUC_PROVEEDOR que se obtiene al concatenar el CODMES extra¨ªda de la variable FECHA_FORMALIZACI¨®N y el RUC_PROVEEDOR; se debe predecir si el TARGET es de clase recibi¨® pedido o no. El archivo (csv), debe contener dos encabezados y tener el siguiente formato:
```
 CODMES_RUC_PROVEEDOR,TARGET
 202403_10000223234,0
 202403_10000957777,0
 202404_10000223234,0
 202404_10000957777,0
  etc.
```","# Dataset Description


El conjunto de datos corresponde a las ¨®rdenes de compra realizadas a trav¨¦s de la plataforma de Cat¨¢logos Electr¨®nicos.

Cada registro hace referencia a una orden de compra formalizada, la cual cumple con las reglas de selecci¨®n y operatividad.

**Para la evaluaci¨®n publica se utilizara los datos del mes de marzo 202403 y la privada del mes de abril 202404.**

## Files

*   **train_202201-202402.csv** -data de entrenamiento.

*   **sample_submission.csv** - ejemplo de envio 
*   **diccionario.xlsx** - diccionario de datos.",False,True
8055,anomaly-dectection-for-financial,"# Description

Welcome to DS108.O21 Assignment 6 competition. In this competition, you are given a VDI Process dataset that was binary encoded. From this dataset, you will write code to clean and predict the status of samples of all time in the testing set.

When you submit a prediction, a 40% subset of it will be evaluated against the true labels and you get a ""public score"" for some feedback. The final score will be evaluated on the other 60%.

Give it your best shot and have fun!?? ?? ??
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Macro F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.csv** - supplemental information about the data

## Columns

*   `Anomaly_label` - the target label
*   `Vision`?- the features that represent the increase (0) or decrease (1) of VDI compared to the previous quarter.",False,True
