8061,ps1cd2024,"# Overview

## Practice Session 1: CD 2024 - Obesity Level Prediction

This competition is designed to be a basic introduction to machine learning classification problems. You'll be tasked with building a model to predict the obesity level (NObeyesdad) of individuals based on their eating habits and physical characteristics.
----------------------
# Description

**Predict obesity levels based on diet and lifestyle!**

This competition is designed for beginners in machine learning. You'll build a model to predict an individual's obesity level using data on their eating habits and physical characteristics.

* **Dataset:** Mix of synthetic and real user data (2111 records, 17 features).
* **Target Variable:** Obesity Level (Categorical with 7 classes).
* **Challenge:** Develop a model for accurate obesity level prediction.

**Sharpen your skills in:**

* Data exploration and pre-processing
* Classification algorithms
* Model evaluation and improvement

**Get ready to crack the code and predict obesity levels!**

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation: Accuracy Score

The performance of your model in this competition will be measured using **accuracy score**. 

**Accuracy score** simply represents the proportion of predictions your model makes that are correct. It can be calculated using the following formula:

**Accuracy = (Number of Correct Predictions) / (Total Number of Predictions)**

**Here's how to interpret accuracy score:**

* **Higher accuracy** (closer to 1) indicates a better model that can predict obesity levels more precisely.
* **Lower accuracy** (closer to 0) suggests there's room for improvement in your model's ability to make accurate predictions.

**While accuracy is a common metric, it's important to consider limitations** depending on  the class distribution in your data. We'll explore this further during the competition.

## Submission File

The predictions for the obesity levels (NObeyesdad) need to be submitted in a specific format. Here's what your submission file should look like:

* **File format:** CSV (Comma-Separated Values)
* **Two columns:**
    * **id:** This column should contain a unique identifier for each data point (likely from the original dataset).
    * **NObeyesdad:** This column should contain your predicted obesity level for each data point. The predictions should match the categories present in the target variable (e.g., Insufficient Weight, Normal Weight, Overweight Level I, etc.).

**Here's an example of a submission file:**

```csv
id,NObeyesdad
1,Normal Weight
2,Overweight Level I
3,Obesity Type II
4,Insufficient Weight
... (more predictions)
```

**Important Note:**

* Ensure the order of your predictions matches the order of the IDs in the submission file.
* Double-check your file format and content before submission.

We recommend creating your submission file after finalizing your model and predictions.","# Dataset Description

* **Data Source:** The dataset includes a mix of synthetic data (77%) generated using the Weka tool's SMOTE filter and real data (23%) collected from users through a web platform.
* **Number of Records:** 2111
* **Number of Features:** 17
* **Target Variable:** NObeyesdad (Obesity Level) - Categorical with values:
    * Insufficient Weight
    * Normal Weight
    * Overweight Level I
    * Overweight Level II
    * Obesity Type I
    * Obesity Type II
    * Obesity Type III

**Features:**

* **Demographic:** Gender, Age, Height, Weight, family_history_with_overweight
* **Dietary:** FAVC (Frequent High Calorie Food), FCVC (Vegetable Consumption Frequency), NCP (Number of Daily Meals), CAEC (Consumption of Food Between Meals)
* **Lifestyle:** SMOKE (Smoking Habit), CH2O (Daily Water Intake), SCC (Calorie Monitoring), FAF (Physical Activity Frequency), TUE (Technological Device Usage Time), CALC (Alcohol Consumption Frequency), MTRANS (Main Mode of Transportation)

**Challenge:**

Develop a machine learning model that accurately predicts an individual's obesity level based on the provided dataset.",False,True
8062,regularization-in-linear-regression,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.","# Dataset Description

**NOTE:** You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Wild blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shashwatwork/wild-blueberry-yield-prediction-dataset). (Since this is Playground 3.14, it seems like we need a Blueberry Pie joke here?) Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8063,d-4-computer-vision,"# Overview

This is a project on using Computer Vision for the D4 program.


**Goal:** Using computer vision CNN to detect healthy vs diseased plant leaves.
----------------------
# Description

In this lab, you will design a CNN that can detect diseased or healthy plant leaves. 

**#Computer Vision****
""Computer vision is an area of machine learning dedicated to interpreting and understanding images and video. It is used to help teach computers to ¡°see¡± and to use visual information to perform visual tasks that humans can."" (https://www.run.ai/guides/deep-learning-for-computer-vision#:~:text=Computer%20vision%20algorithms%20analyze%20certain,commonly%20used%20for%20computer%20vision.) 

The link above is a great source to learn more about computer vision and how the CNN behind it work.

# **Code Example of CNN Model**
\```model = models.Sequential()
model.add(layers.Conv2D(32, (3, 3), activation='relu', input_shape=(300, 150, 3)))
model.add(layers.MaxPooling2D((2, 2)))
model.add(layers.Conv2D(64, (3, 3), activation='relu'))\```

# **Instructions**
1. Load and look through the data
2. I suggest some potential image augmentation, this is a large dataset
3. Design a CNN that is able to accurately detect diseased and healthy leaves (Example above)
- Try and find parameters that will give the best accuracy 
4. Look at sample submission on what you submission should look like 
----------------------
# Evaluation

This lab will be graded on accuracy, the percentage of times the model is able to correctly identify diseased and healthy leaves. 

---

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

         Images        Labels
    1  0011_0100.JPG    0
    2  0009_0053.JPG    1
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Plant disease detection via computer vision could be a very important to protecting the agriculture industry. This dataset contains 4235 images of healthy and/or diseased leaves. Within this dataset, there are 10 different plant species with a variety of leaf shapes and degrees of disease progression. There are 3 csv files: train, test, and sample_submission. The train file has one column being the image (with the image_id) and the other being the label (0: healthy and 1: for diseased), the test file only having the image column (no answers).

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Images` - JPG image id
*   `Labels`?- 0: healthy and 1: diseased

## Data Source
Dataset modified from the ""Healthy vs. Diseased Leaf Image Dataset"" (License CC0: Public domain) on Kaggle at https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/amandam1/healthy-vs-diseased-leaf-image-dataset",False,True
8064,binary-prediction,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use binary classification to predict a patient's smoking status given information about various other health indicators. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `smoking`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,smoking
    159256,0.5
    159257,0.5
    159258,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Smoker Status Prediction using Bio-Signals](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/gauravduttakiit/smoker-status-prediction-using-biosignals) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `smoking` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of positive `smoking`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8065,which-vaccine-class-was-it,"# Overview

In this competition you will be building a machine learning classifier to determine what vaccine was given to each mouse in a test study. You will design and implement a machine learning algorithm of your choosing to best determine each of the hidden classifications. 
----------------------
# Description

# Background
Mouse models are used by scientists all around the world to determine the efficacy of a medicine. Often times mice are first dosed with a vaccine at varying concentrations or ""titers"" and later ""challenged"" by infecting them with a pathogen. Mouse models are not just conducted with standard mice. These mice are typically bread and raised without any disease exposure helping to isolate the effects of a specific vaccine type. During a mouse study a number of target organs and metrics are maintained to understand how the virus is affecting them.

Influenza is a particularly challenging virus to battle due to it's ability to mutate it's genetic code. However, like certain traits passed on through a family tree, influenza retains some key genetic sequences that can be exploited to make vaccines affective.

The data used in this study was heavily adapted to not represent any current research results or datasets. The measurements include mice body weight change, unitless max change for the mouse's respiratory tract, the conserved influenza target protein, a dose size for challenge, and a vaccine type associated with each mouse.

# Challenge
Your challenge is to help Dr. Mixup. Dr. Mixup was prepping multiple studies, when he lost one of his lab notebooks for a group of mice. Dr. Mixup continued to sample the mice and was able to reorganize most of his data but he lost the names of each vaccine type those mice were given. What he doesn't know is Dr.Moneybags actually stole the data to sabotage Dr. Mixup. Although, Dr. Mixup isn't planning to publish this study it is important for him to get the data back as quickly as possible so he can prepare for a bigger study in a few weeks.

You need to help Dr. Mixup to figure out which four vaccine types he gave his mice. Use a machine learning multi-class classifier to predict which vaccines were given to which mice. Hopefully the research ethics board can get the answers back from Dr. Moneybags and we can get the true answers later.

#Instructions
You will use the train.csv file to train a classifier to determine what vaccine was given to each mouse. The data contains a Vaccine Column, which you are trying to predict. You will need to determine a train test split ration that best trains your model, then choose a classifier from scikit learn to predict the vaccine type that was given to each mouse.

Then utilize the trained model to make predictions on the test data set. Submit your files with the same format as the sample submission files. **Important:** Na?ve is not the same as Naive, please use Na?ve in your solution file to be scored correctly.

1. Evaluate your train data to look for correlations using pairplots/correlation plots of the data
2. Implement several models from scikit learn and test the accuracy of each model by train_test_split ing your data
3. Try improving your models by adding or removing data
4. Evaluate the test.csv file and submit your predictions to be scored.
5. Provide a brief explanation of the model you chose and how it works. (There are many resources such as stack overflow that can help you understand multiclass classifiers)

##Learning Outcomes 
1. Evaluate data using graphical methods. 
2. Implement several multi-class classifiers to evaluate data. 
3. Critically evaluate the shortcomings of a given model
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by prediction accuracy. How accurately were you able to predict the vaccine type given to your mouse. accuracy = correct/total samples


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. Create a numbered column 0-14 for each vaccine prediction. Save your submission file without an index. The file should contain a header and have the following format:



    ID, Vaccine
    0, IN
    1, IIAV
    2, Naive
    3, SC
    etc.","# Dataset Description

This page appears alongside the data files. It describes?what files have been provided and?the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitions, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.

Participants should be able to answer these types of questions after reading the data description:

What files do I need?  
What should I expect the data format to be?  
What am I predicting?  
What acronyms will I encounter?

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Mouse Number` - Mouse ID
*   `Vaccine`?- Identifier given for the type of vaccine, Naive mice have no vaccine given, IN is an intranasal, SC is a subcutaneous tissue vaccine, and IIAV is a nanoparticle subcutaneous tissue vaccine
*   `Max1delta Penh` - Penh is the dimensionless number assigned to changes in lung tissue of mice, Max1delta is the ratio of the first day measurement to the maximum change in lung tissue
*   `STD Penh`?- The standard deviation of lung tissue changes over a 14 day trial
*   `Weight Change` - weight change of mice over a 14 day trial
*   `Construct`?- conserved influenza protein in the virus given to each mouse that makes that strain of influenza unique
 *  `Dose`?- dose of each vaccine given to a mouse.
*   etc.",False,True
8066,spaceship,"# Description

2912-yilga xush kelibsiz, u yerda kosmik sirni yechish uchun ma?lumotlarshunoslik ko?nikmalaringiz zarur. Biz to¡®rt yorug¡®lik yili masofasidan uzatma oldik va ishlar yaxshi ko¡®rinmayapti.

Titanik kosmik kemasi bir oy oldin uchirilgan yulduzlararo yo'lovchi layneri edi. Bortida 13 000 ga yaqin yo'lovchi bo'lgan kema o'zining birinchi sayohatiga quyosh sistemamizdan muhojirlarni yaqin atrofdagi yulduzlar orbitasida joylashgan uchta yangi yashashga yaroqli ekzosayyoraga olib chiqdi.

Alpha Centauri'ni birinchi manziliga - shiddatli 55 Cancri E - yo'lda aylanib o'tayotganda, ehtiyotsiz Titanik kosmik kemasi chang buluti ichida yashiringan kosmik vaqt anomaliyasi bilan to'qnashdi. Afsuski, u 1000 yil oldin o'z nomiga o'xshash taqdirga duch keldi. Kema buzilmagan bo'lsa-da, yo'lovchilarning deyarli yarmi boshqa o'lchamga o'tib qoldi.

<img title=¡±ID 52¡± src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/Spaceship%20Titanic/joel-filipe-QwoNAhbmLLo-unsplash.jpg"" style=""float:center; height:320px; width:404px"">

Qutqaruv ekipajlariga yordam berish va yo'qolgan yo'lovchilarni olish uchun siz kosmik kemaning shikastlangan kompyuter tizimidan tiklangan yozuvlar yordamida anomaliya tufayli qaysi yo'lovchilar boshqa o'lchamga o'tib qolganini taxmin qilishingiz kerak.

Ularni saqlashga va tarixni o'zgartirishga yordam bering!


