8071,aiss-hackathon,"# Overview

##Baseline paper : 
- Large Multi-Modal Foundation Model for Traffic Accident Analysis [https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.03040 ] 
- ?LLM Multimodal Traffic Accident Forecasting [ https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/23/22/9225 ] 

##Brief Overview: 
Multi-Modal Traffic Accident Analysis for Safer Roads:-  Develop an innovative model to analyze diverse traffic data, uncover accident root causes, and proactively suggest preventive solutions.

To ensure smooth communication, please join our WhatsApp group via the link below:
https://chat.whatsapp.com/Cj3JNa2RmsIArWOznmR1HN
----------------------
# Description

##The Challenge
Traffic accidents remain a persistent global threat despite extensive safety efforts.
Traditional models often centre on single data sources, failing to capture the complex interplay of factors contributing to accidents.
A holistic, multi-modal approach is needed to understand and mitigate traffic risks effectively.

##The Task
Construct a model that seamlessly integrates and analyzes data from various sources:
- Vehicular data (speed, GPS, sensor readings)
- Pedestrian behavior (movement patterns, crossings)
- CCTV footage (traffic flow, potential incidents)
- Weather conditions (visibility, precipitation)
- Road infrastructure (layout, signage, condition)

The model's insights should pinpoint the leading causes of accidents and inform potential preventive measures.

Given a dataset containing multimodal information such as images, videos, and textual descriptions of road scenes, the goal is to develop a robust accident detection system using a Multimodal Language Model (LLM). The system should accurately classify each scene into one of two categories: ""no accident"" (label 0) or ""accident"" (label 1). 

##The Impact
This competition has the power to catalyze breakthroughs in transportation safety.
Successful models could become crucial tools for:
- Improving road design and traffic management
- Developing targeted awareness campaigns
- Informing future vehicle safety systems
Let's make a difference. Join the challenge to design a data-driven future for safer road networks!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Model performance will be measured by its F1 scores in predicting and analyzing actual traffic accidents.
Solutions offering actionable insights and demonstrable potential to reduce accident frequency and impact will be favoured.

Scoring
Data preparation - 20%
Evaluation - 20 %
Plots - 20 %
Model finetuning - 40%

Google Form Submission Link - https://forms.gle/bePnpZZZZL6nG7vX9

Top 5 winners will be called for offline presentation of their solution for finals at IIIT-Delhi Campus.","# Dataset Description

##Accident Detection From CCTV Footage

Dataset link :- https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ckay16/accident-detection-from-cctv-footage

Labels:- Not Labeled/annotated; Train, Test and Validation split with Accident and Non-accident folders

Description:- 
Accident Detection dataset collected from the CCTV footages containing a total of 990 accident and non-accident frames collected from road videos available on YouTube. The 990 files are split in the 791 training frames, 101 test frames and 98 validation frames. 
791 (369-accident, 492-non accident) Training, 101 Test and 98 Validation (52-accident, 46-non accident) frames split in Accident and Non-accident frames in all the three folders.


Size:- 262 MB",False,True
8072,nzmsa-2024,"# Overview

This competition is part of Phase 2 of the Data Science Section in Microsoft Student Accelerator New Zealand (MSA NZ) 2024. The participants need to develop a deep-learning model to do 10-class image classification. We hope that the participants can improve their skills in building, training, and evaluating deep-learning models during this competition. Good luck.
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to MSA NZ 2024 - Data Science - Phase 2 Competition! We hope you've gained a solid understanding of deep learning concepts, models, and training processes from the previous tasks. Now it's time to put that knowledge into practice by implementing a real image classification model! We encourage you to apply all your understanding of neural networks, optimization techniques, and innovative problem-solving skills to achieve the best performance.

Regardless of the models, techniques, and processes you choose to use, please ensure to justify your code step by step clearly and properly.

---

## Models
Please note that in this competition, you must build your own model instead of using pre-packaged models or pre-written code.

You can implement one or more models in this competition and compare their classification accuracy. Potential models include, but are not limited to:
* Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)
* Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)
* Vision Transformer (ViT)

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision). Accuracy is defined as the proportion of correctly classified images to the total number of images. Considering that CIFAR-10 has the same number of images in each class without any bias, accuracy can provide a straightforward measure of the overall performance of the classification model.

$$
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Number of Predictions}}
$$

---

## Submission File
For each image in the test set, you must predict a class for the ```label``` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,label
    0,10
    1,10
    2,10
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The official CIFAR-10 dataset consists of 60,000 32x32 colour images in 10 classes, with 6,000 images per class. There are 50,000 training images and 10,000 test images. The dataset is well-known in the machine learning community and provides a diverse set of categories, including:

* Airplane
* Automobile
* Bird
* Cat
* Deer
* Dog
* Frog
* Horse
* Ship
* Truck

In this competition, 500 images from each class are randomly selected in the test set, resulting in a total of 5000 images to evaluate the accuracy of the participants' models. The training set is the same as the official version. 

**Please note that it is essential to use the dataset provided by this competition for both training and testing!**

## Files

*   **train** - stores all training images
*   **test** - stores all testing images
*   **train.csv** - maps image ID of training images to labels
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - the image id. This image is located in the corresponding folder with the file name of ""image_```id```.png"".
*   `label`?- label of the image",False,True
