## Competition Description

Welcome to the **Wild Blueberry Yield Prediction Challenge**! ????

In this competition, your task is to predict **wild blueberry yields** using a comprehensive dataset that includes various **environmental** and **pollination-related variables**. You'll work with key features such as:

- **Bee Density**: The presence of pollinators like **honeybees**, **bumblebees**, **Andrena bees**, and **Osmia bees**, which play a crucial role in pollination.
- **Temperature**: Minimum, maximum, and average temperatures from both the **upper** and **lower ranges** during the bloom season.
- **Raining Days**: The total number of days with rainfall during the bloom season, which affects both pollination and plant hydration.
- **Clone Size**: The average blueberry clone size, influencing yield potential in the field.

Your goal is to use this data to develop a **regression model** that can accurately predict the **wild blueberry yield** for the upcoming season. Whether you're a **beginner** or an experienced **data scientist**, this challenge is an excellent opportunity to apply your machine learning skills to real-world agricultural and ecological data.

By participating, you'll help farmers and researchers better understand how environmental factors and pollinators affect wild blueberry yields, contributing to **more efficient farming** and **sustainable agricultural practices**. ??

So, jump in and aim to create the most **accurate and insightful model** possible! Together, let¡¯s work to improve **wild blueberry harvest predictions** and make farming more fruitful! ??

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.","# Dataset Description

**NOTE:** You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).

The **Wild Blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset** is sourced from the **Wild Blueberry Pollination Simulation Model**, an open-source, spatially-explicit computer simulation tool. This model enables researchers to explore how factors such as **plant arrangement**, **pollination dynamics**, **bee species compositions**, and **weather conditions**¡ªboth individually and in combination¡ªaffect pollination efficiency and yield in the wild blueberry agroecosystem.

The dataset, validated through field observations over 30 years in **Maine, USA** and the **Canadian Maritimes**, provides valuable data for predicting wild blueberry yields and testing hypotheses related to pollination research.

## Features and Descriptions

- **Clonesize (m2)**: Average size of blueberry clones in the field.
- **Honeybee (bees/m2/min)**: Density of honeybees present in the field.
- **Bumbles (bees/m2/min)**: Density of bumblebees present in the field.
- **Andrena (bees/m2/min)**: Density of Andrena bee species in the field.
- **Osmia (bees/m2/min)**: Density of Osmia bees in the field.
- **MaxOfUpperTRange (¡ãC)**: Maximum upper range of daily air temperature during the bloom season.
- **MinOfUpperTRange (¡ãC)**: Minimum upper range of daily air temperature.
- **AverageOfUpperTRange (¡ãC)**: Average upper range of daily air temperature.
- **MaxOfLowerTRange (¡ãC)**: Maximum lower range of daily air temperature during the bloom season.
- **MinOfLowerTRange (¡ãC)**: Minimum lower range of daily air temperature.
- **AverageOfLowerTRange (¡ãC)**: Average lower range of daily air temperature.
- **RainingDays (Days)**: Total number of days with precipitation during the bloom season.
- **AverageRainingDays (Days)**: Average number of rainy days across the bloom season.

## Acknowledgements

This dataset was provided by **Qu, Hongchun; Obsie, Efrem; Drummond, Frank (2020)**, as part of their research on wild blueberry yield prediction. The data was originally published on **Mendeley Data** and can be found here:  
Qu, Hongchun; Obsie, Efrem; Drummond, Frank (2020), ¡°Data for: Wild blueberry yield prediction using a combination of computer simulation and machine learning algorithms¡±, Mendeley Data, V1, doi: [10.17632/p5hvjzsvn8.1](https://doi.org/10.17632/p5hvjzsvn8.1).

This dataset provides an excellent foundation for developing predictive models that leverage environmental and ecological factors to improve wild blueberry yields.


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
