8180,superclass-learning,"# Overview

Welcome to the Incremental Image Classification Challenge!

In this competition, you will leverage a pre-existing image classification model and dataset to enhance its performance in an incremental image classification task. The provided repository includes a model and a dataset derived from CIFAR-100, restructured into a 4-class dataset. Each class represents a broader category, such as 'animals,' which encompasses various subclasses like cats, dogs, frogs, and more.

The key challenge in this competition is to improve the model's performance with new data while preventing catastrophic forgetting. The training and test set includes multiple subclasses within each superclass. Newly added data introduces unseen subclasses (e.g., sea lions and zebras within the 'animals' class). Your goal is to **fine-tune the model with the newly added data** to enhance its accuracy on this unseen data without degrading its performance on the original training data.

Participants will:

- Utilize the given image classification model and dataset.
- Develop strategies to improve the model's performance on new dataset within the same superclass while preventing catastrophic forgetting.
- Submit their results on the test set.
The success of your model will be determined by its accuracy in predicting the correct superclass for images in the test set. This competition is an excellent opportunity to explore techniques in incremental learning, data augmentation, and model fine-tuning.

Get ready to dive into the world of incremental image classification and showcase your skills in machine learning!


----------------------
# Description

## Background
In the realm of image classification, the ability to correctly categorize images into broad classes, or superclasses, is crucial for developing robust machine learning models. Superclass classification tasks challenge models to not only identify specific subclasses but also to generalize across various unseen subclasses within the same broad category. 

## The Dataset
The origine dataset used in this competition is derived from the CIFAR-100 dataset, which contains 100 different subclasses grouped into 20 superclasses. For the purpose of this challenge, we have restructured the dataset into 4 main superclasses:

1. Animals - Includes subclasses like cats, dogs, frogs, etc.
2. Insects and Reptiles - Includes subclasses like butterflies, spiders, lizards, etc.
3. Plants - Includes subclasses like trees, flowers, mushrooms, etc.
4. Man-made Objects - Includes subclasses like chairs, tables, lamps, etc.

Each superclass is designed to include a diverse range of subclasses. During the training phase, participants will have access to images from these subclasses. However, the test set will include new, unseen subclasses (e.g., sea lions and zebras in the 'Animals' class), pushing the models to demonstrate their ability to generalize effectively.
### Newly added data
The newly added dataset used in this competition is derived from the Tiny ImageNet dataset. It alse be grouped into the same 4 main superclasses. You can check the data in aug_data.zip.


## Pipline

*The model and dataset are referenced from the CVPR 2023 paper ""Superclass Learning with Representation Enhancement.""*https://github.com/ZyGan1999/Superclass-Learning-with-Representation-Enhancement

We provide a training example of SCLRE. You can generate the reorganized CIFAR100-4 dataset and train it. After training, you can check the result with the help of tensorboard. Some main package versions used in the program:
### Step0 Dependencies
```
python: 3.8.12
pytorch: 1.11.0
torchvision: 0.12.0
CUDA Version: 11.4
tensorboard-logger: 0.1.0
tensorboard: 2.7.0
tqdm: 4.62.3
```

## step1 Generate Data
Original and newly added data are prepared for u guys in origine_data.zip and aug_data.zip. The file needs to be placed under the /data/re_cifar100/4_catagories path.
You can use the cifar100.py file in the ./ptgenerate folder to generate the original training and test data.
You also can use the cifar100_extendby_imagenet.py in the ./ptgenerate folder to generate the newly added training and test data.

## Step2 Train with SCLRE
In the main directory, run
```
python main.py
```
to train with SCLRE. The settings of GPUs are in `main.py`, and you can change it based on your hardware.
The training process will be shown in the terminal with the help of `tqdm`.
## Step3 Check the Result
After training, the result will be recorded with the help of `tensorboard`. By default, it will be restored in `./runs/`, and you can run
```
tensorboard --logdir=runs/
```
to check the training result. We evaluate our model in accuracy, recall, precision and F1 score. We also record the loss in the training process.
### Step4 Change the Hyperparameters
All the hyperparameters are listed in `hyperparameter.py`, and the meanings of them are also listed in the comments in this file. You can change the hyperparameters and re-train with Step2. 

To change the architecture of the model, set `attention=False` to drop CIA, set `contrastive=False` to drop contrastive adjustment loss, set `TARGET=False` to drop targeted adjustment loss.

Note that when the backbone is set as a CNN smaller than ResNet50, some network params in `model/TC2.py` need to be reset because these backbones generate representations with smaller dimensions. So we recommend to set this hyperparameter only as `ResNet50`, `ResNet101` and `ResNet152`.

 



We encourage you to dive into the data, experiment with different techniques, and push the boundaries of what's possible in superclass image classification. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation


Submissions are evaluated on [accuracy](https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Accuracy_and_precision).


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the label variable. Each ID  represents one picture. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
    id,label
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.
```
**The order of the submitted results should match the order of the IDs in the test.pt file in the aug_data directory.**
The file should contain a total of 11,981 different IDs.","# Dataset Description

## Overview
The dataset for this competition is derived from CIFAR-100, a well-known image classification dataset. It has been restructured into 4 superclasses, each containing multiple subclasses. You need to use the newly added data to enhance the model's performance. The new data includes training and test sets, consisting of previously unseen subclasses within the four superclasses.

## Data Files
The following files are provided in this competition:

- Original training and test data: pics or .pt files.
- Newly added training and test data:  pics or .pt( .pt files including both original and newly added data) files.
- Data generator: Code to generate the training and test data.£¨Included in Model.zip£©
    - You can use the cifar100.py file in the ./ptgenerate folder to generate the original training and test data.
    - You also can use the cifar100_extendby_imagenet.py in the ./ptgenerate folder to generate the newly added training and test data.


## Data Structure
### Original training and test Set
The original training and test set is built from the CIFAR-100 dataset. CIFAR-100 contains 100 different subclasses, each with 500 images. These subclasses are grouped into 4 main superclasses, with each superclass containing 25 subclasses. The total number of images in the training set is 50,000. The total number of images in the test set is 10,000.

### Superclasses and Subclasses:
- Animals: 25 subclasses (e.g., cats, dogs, frogs)
- Insects and Reptiles: 25 subclasses (e.g., butterflies, spiders, lizards)
- Plants: 25 subclasses (e.g., trees, flowers, mushrooms)
- Man-made Objects: 25 subclasses (e.g., chairs, tables, lamps)
### The newly added training and test data
The newly added training and test set is built from the Tiny ImageNet dataset. 20 previously unseen subclasses from the original dataset were selected. Each set of 5 subclasses belongs to one of the aforementioned 4 superclasses. The total number of images in the training set is 8000. The total number of images in the test set is 2000.",False,True
