©À©¤©¤ categories.csv
©À©¤©¤ train_labeled.csv
©¸©¤©¤ sample_submission.csv
```

`train_labeled.csv` contains the mapping from labeled training images to the category IDs, and `sample_submission.csv` follows the same format. `categories.csv` provides the metadata about mapping from category IDs to category names.
----------------------
# Implementation

Although this dataset is fine-grained, meaning that certain categories look very similar to each other, you should still be able to train a image classifier with >40% accuracy without any fine-grained classification techniques as long as you utilize the semi-supervised learning properly. Fine-grained classfication techniques such as *Mixture of Experts* are only recommended but **not required** in your final solution.

**HINT**
1. Modern models are usually very large and takes a long time to train. Try to train on a very small subset and make sure your model can overfit these data to ensure your code is correct before training and tuning any hyperparameters on the actual training set.
----------------------
# Reproducibility

Please make sure your final training and test accuracies are reproducible. In other words, with your submitted code and documents, someone else should be able to reproduce most of the results you get with similar means and standard deviations. Good practices of reproducibility include using a manual seed, repeating experiments and reporting the averaged training and validation accuracies/losses, providing detailed instructions on how to run your code to produce the results, etc. **If your final accuracy cannot be reproduced by the code submitted, it will have an impact on your score of this competition.**
----------------------
# Submission

You need to submit a `.csv` file with image filenames and predicted category id on Kaggle following the provided `sample_submission.csv`.
The public leaderboard will give you a rough estimated score. Note that you should not use this score to infer your final accuracy, because the public leaderboard is based on only ¡«20% samples from the final test set.

Additionally, you are required to submit your code/model on Canvas and instructions on how to reproduce your experiments. In your submission to Canvas, please include
1. A report including instructions on how to run your code, experimental setup etc;
2. `.py` or `.ipynb` files containing all the code to reproduce the results (including the predictions);
3. a link to your final model weight in Google Drive. Do not upload your model directly to Canvas.","# Dataset Description

This customed dataset has 15 plant categories and 120 dog categories in total. The training set contains 9854 labeled images and 22995 unlabeled images. The test set contains 8213 images.

