8222,snow-cholera-deaths,"# Overview

Please read the description bellow.
----------------------
# Description

The goal of this competition is to recreate John Snow's analysis of cholera deaths, using modern GIS tools.

More specifically, the goal is to **count all reported cholera deaths, within 50 m of a water pump**. In this way, we will be able to identify which water pumps pose a danger to public health.

Use the available datasets on the ""data"" section and this notebook, to get started.

Use the sample.csv file, as template for submission in the correct format.

## References
V. Olaya. Introduction to GIS. CreateSpace Independent Publishing Platform, 2018. https://volaya.github.io/gis-book/en/index.html

## Spatial reference systems
https://spatialreference.org/

https://store.usgs.gov/assets/mod/storefiles/PDF/16573.pdf

## John Snow & Classics
http://blog.rtwilson.com/john-snows-cholera-data-in-more-formats/

https://www.theguardian.com/news/datablog/2013/mar/15/john-snow-cholera-map#data

## Thematic Cartography
www.geog.ucsb.edu/~kclarke/Geography183/Lecture03.pdf

http://www.gitta.info/ThematicCart/en/html/index.html

https://www.axismaps.com/guide/

https://www.axismaps.com/guide/univariate/choropleth/

https://www.axismaps.com/guide/data/data-classification/
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submission Format
The submission file should be a [""csv"" file](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comma-separated_values) with exactly two columns: one called ""Id_2"" and another one called ""Count"". ""Id_2"" contains the pump ID, from 1 to 8 (in this order). ""Count"" contains the number of reported cholera deaths within 50 m of the pump. They are calculated by intersecting the buffer layer, with the reported cholera deaths layer. If a particular buffer does not contain any deaths within it, you should assign 0 to ""Count"". The file should have exactly 8 rows: the number of pumps! 
## Submission Scoring
The submission will be scored, by counting the number of correct answers (correctly guessed counts), against the total. In the data section, you may find an example of a submission file with the correct format: ""sample.csv"". Good luck! (:
----------------------
# Citation

doublebyte. (2024). Snow Cholera Deaths. Kaggle. https://kaggle.com/competitions/snow-cholera-deaths","# Dataset Description

Here you can find the datasets used in the recreation of Snow's analysis. We use two geospatial formats: [Shapefile](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shapefile) and [GeoTiff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeoTIFF).

## Files
- **Cholera_Deaths** - Shapefile cover with reported cases of cholera deaths.
- **Pumps** - Shapefile cover with location of water pumps.
- **polys** - Shapefile cover with representation of building blocks.
- **SnowDSM50CM.tif** - Digital Elevation Model.
- **SnowMap.tif** - Original Snow's background map.
- **sample.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8223,cp-3501-retinamnist-v-2024,"# Overview

This is an in-class competition for the CP3501 course. It is based on the RetinaMNIST dataset of [https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-022-01721-8](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-022-01721-8) and available from [https://medmnist.com/](https://medmnist.com/) and [https://github.com/MedMNIST/MedMNIST](https://github.com/MedMNIST/MedMNIST)



----------------------
# Description

## Objective

The RetinaMNIST is based on the DeepDRiD24 challenge, see [https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-022-01721-8](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-022-01721-8), which provides a dataset of 1,600 retina fundus images. The task is ordinal regression for 5-level grading of diabetic retinopathy severity. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Mean absolute error regression loss](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.mean_absolute_error.html) between the predicted values and the given true values.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a value (in the 0-5 range, fractional values are allowed) for the LABEL variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format, see the supplied submission_sample.csv:

    image_id,label
