8229,artificial-text-detection-homework-3,"# Overview

**Artificial text detection** 

Motivated by the urgent need for artificial text detection, we suggest you develop your own classifier capable of distinguishing between human-written and machine-generated texts.
----------------------
# Description

This assignment extends from the hands-on session on artificial text detection.

* We use the **IMDB** dataset as a source of human-written texts
* Eight text generation models and three decoding strategies are used to generate the testing data
* The specific text generation models will remain undisclosed until the homework deadline.
* We will only reveal that *GPT2-large* is one of the eight models under consideration.

This assignment is a binary classification task, wherein you are required to train a classifier that predicts whether a text is human-written (`H`) or machine-generated (`M`). 






----------------------
# Evaluation

Accuracy is used as a main evaluation metric.


----------------------
# Baselines

We provide three baselines for reference:
* The random baseline randomly assign one of two labels to a text (`H` or `M`)
* The weak baseline re-uses the model, trained in the practical session 
* The strong baseline improves upon weak baseline by incorporating data from various text generation models
* The super-strong baseline outperforms other baselines by integrating data from all text generation models employed in the test set
----------------------
# Submission Format

For every Id in the test set, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Class.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,Class
0,H
1,M
2,M
```
----------------------
# Data

- We will provide development data only as an only labeled set 
- We will not disclose the text generation models used to create the test set
- You are welcome to utilize any text generation model and any sources of your choice for training data
----------------------
# Grading

You will receive:
- 1 point if your top submission surpasses the random baseline
- +1 points if your leading submission outperforms the weak baseline
- +1 points if your top submission outperforms the strong baseline
- +1 bonus point if your best submission outperforms the super strong baseline

Grading will be determined by the scores achieved on the **private** leaderboard.
----------------------
# Reference 

- Use [this Kaggle tutorial](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission) to learn how to get started 
- Refer to [this Quora discussion](https://www.quora.com/What-is-the-difference-between-public-and-private-leaderboard-in-Kaggle) to familiarize yourself with the concept of public/private leaderboards","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **dev.csv** - the development set
*   **test.csv** - the test set


## Columns

* ID -- the ID 
* Text  -- the text 
* Class -- the target class, `H` stands for human-written texts, `M` for machine-generated text",False,True
8230,brightinc,"# Description

## Introduction
The night is dark and full of terrors. Two teams must fight off the darkness, collect resources, and advance through the ages. Daytime finds a desperate rush to gather the resources that can carry you through the impending night whilst growing your city. Plan and expand carefully -- any city that fails to produce enough light will be consumed by darkness.
 
**Welcome to the Lux AI Challenge Season 1!**

<img src=""https://github.com/Lux-AI-Challenge/Lux-Design-2021/raw/master/assets/daynightshift.gif"" width=""50%"" />

The Lux AI Challenge is a competition where competitors design agents to tackle a multi-variable optimization, resource gathering, and allocation problem in a 1v1 scenario against other competitors. In addition to optimization, successful agents must be capable of analyzing their opponents and developing appropriate policies to get the upper hand.
 
All code can be found at our [Github](https://github.com/Lux-AI-Challenge/Lux-Design-2021), make sure to give it a star while you are there!
 
Make sure to join our community [discord](https://discord.gg/aWJt3UAcgn) to chat, strategize, and learn with other competitors! We will be posting announcements on the Kaggle Forums and on the discord.
 
## Sponsors
We would like to thank our 3 sponsors, [QuantCo](https://quantco.com/), [J Ventures](http://thejiangmen.com), and [QAImera](https://qaimera.com) this year for allowing us to provide a prize pool and exciting opportunities to our competitors!  For more information on them, go to the [sponsors tab](https://www.kaggle.com/c/lux-ai-2021/overview/sponsors)

----------------------
# Evaluation

Each day your team is able to submit up to 5 agents (bots) to the competition. Each submission will play Episodes (games) against other bots on the ladder that have a similar skill rating. Over time skill ratings will go up with wins or down with losses and evened out with ties. 

Every bot submitted will continue to play episodes until the end of the competition, with newer bots playing a much more frequent number of episodes. On the leaderboard only your best scoring bot will be shown, but you can track the progress of all of your submissions on your Submissions page.

Each Submission has an estimated Skill Rating which is modeled by a Gaussian N(¦Ì,¦Ò2) where ¦Ì is the estimated skill and ¦Ò represents the uncertainty of that estimate which will decrease over time.

When you upload a Submission, we first play a Validation Episode where that Submission plays against copies of itself to make sure it works properly. If the Episode fails, the Submission is marked as Error and you can download the agent logs to help figure out why. Otherwise, we initialize the Submission with ¦Ì0=600 and it joins the pool of All Submissions for ongoing evaluation.

We repeatedly run Episodes from the pool of All Submissions, and try to pick Submissions with similar ratings for fair matches. Newly submitted agents will be given an increased rate in the number of episodes run to give you faster feedback.

After an Episode finishes, we'll update the Rating estimate for all Submissions in that Episode. If one Submission won, we'll increase its ¦Ì and decrease its opponent's ¦Ì -- if the result was a draw, then we'll move the two ¦Ì values closer towards their mean. The updates will have magnitude relative to the deviation from the expected result based on the previous ¦Ì values, and also relative to each Submission's uncertainty ¦Ò. We also reduce the ¦Ò terms relative to the amount of information gained by the result. The score by which your bot wins or loses an Episode does not affect the skill rating updates.

## Ranking System
After an Episode finishes, we'll update the Rating estimate for all Submissions in that Episode. If one Submission won, we'll increase its ¦Ì and decrease its opponent's ¦Ì -- if the result was a draw, then we'll move the two ¦Ì values closer towards their mean. The updates will have magnitude relative to the deviation from the expected result based on the previous ¦Ì values, and also relative to each Submission's uncertainty ¦Ò. We also reduce the ¦Ò terms relative to the amount of information gained by the result. The score by which your bot wins or loses an Episode does not affect the skill rating updates.

## Final Evaluation

At the submission deadline on December 6th, additional submissions will be locked. From December 7th to December 20th, we will continue to run games. At the conclusion of this period, the leaderboard is final and is used to determine who gets various ranking based prizes.
----------------------
# Timeline

* August 16, 2021 - Start Date.
* December 6, 2021 - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.
* December 6, 2021 - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.
* December 6, 2021 - Final Submission Deadline.
* December 16, 2021 - Submission Deadline for J Ventures Innovative Strategy Award
* **December 7, 2021-December 20, 2021** - Final games are played.
* **December 21, 2021** - Winners announced.

The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

## Main Prize Distribution
Monetary prizes are distributed to the top 5 competitors on the leaderboard at the end of the competition
* 1st Place - $4000
* 2nd Place - $2500
* 3rd Place - $1500
* 4th Place - $1000
* 5th Place - $1000

Additionally, the top 8 teams who have not received competition t-shirts will get free competition t-shirts.
 
## Special Prizes

### J Ventures Innovative Strategy award
 
This year we will be giving out the J Ventures Innovative Strategy award. This award will be given out to the competitor who develops the most innovative, creative strategy and writeup. We will give out honorable mentions to at most 4 submissions if they also have really great strategies and write ups but were just edged out by the winner. 
 
To be considered for this award, please fill out this [Google Form](https://forms.gle/o82vymAYQMPyLN9r8). Your submission will be judged by the Lux AI Challenge team.
 
We will be evaluating write ups on the following factors
 
- Innovation: We're looking for strategies that were unique to this competition and would easily be something you couldn't figure out just by reading the Kaggle Forums or our Discord or Googling online. For this factor, we weigh uniqueness, novelty, and it's strategic strength in the competition. This is the highest weighted factor
 
- Writing Quality: We want to encourage competitors to learn from each other and good writing goes a long way to helping other competitors understand your strategy and work! This is the second highest weighted factor
 
- Visual Quality: Visuals really help people learn visually what is going on and we will be looking for write ups that make great use of visuals to aid in comprehension. (Doesn't have to be high quality art, just needs to be understandable). This is the least weighted factor.
 
To be eligible for this award, you must submit a document with a maximum of 3 pages in English or Chinese. You are free to structure your write up however you see works best! Please do not cram text, we recommend sticking to 11 point font as a default.
 
The deadline to make a submission is December 16th 11:59PM UTC. Late submissions will not be considered under any circumstances. You may edit your submission any time before the deadline. By creating a submission, you accept that your submission and Kaggle Username may be shared to the public.
 
### QuantCo Top Performance Award
 
QuantCo will be giving out the QuantCo Top Performance Award, which is awarded to the best 10 individual competitors (single member teams) and the best 10 teams (multiple member teams) in the competition based on the final results.
 
QuantCo will potentially also be giving out additional informal awards/prizes, please stay tuned on the Kaggle forums and community discord for what these might be.

## Lux AI Challenge Sprint Award
At the end of each month of active competition, the top 2 on the leaderboard will receive the **Lux AI Challenge Sprint award** for developing strong bots early on! This award can only be won once per team and if a team in the top 2 has already won the Sprint award, it will be given to the next highest ranking team who has not won the award. All teams are automatically entered into this competition.
 
In addition to the Sprint Award, teams will also be awarded competition t-shirts in addition to potential swag from our sponsors as well.
 
To pick the top 2 on the leaderboard, we will check the state of the leaderboard at 11:59PM UTC on the last day of each month. Thus these dates are August 31, September 30, October 31, and November 30.

