*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8232,df-detection-challenge,"# Overview

With the rapid development of technology in the field of AI, Deepfake technology has emerged as a double-edged sword, which has not only created a large amount of AI-generated content but also posed unprecedented challenges to the digital security field. To this end, the ""2024 Ant Group Technology Innovation Competition: Global Deepfake Attack and Defense Challenge"" is held. This challenge aims to advocate the negative impact of AIGC, motivate innovative defense strategies, inspire scientific and technological innovation, and improve Deepfake¡¯s recognition accuracy. With such a challenge, we aim to improve the Deepfake detection efficiency and comprehensive prevention capabilities to defend various types of Deepfake attacks in real-world scenarios, fully empowering the industry and society.
----------------------
# Description

The challenge is divided into two tracks: an image track and an audio-video track. The competition provides high-quality data sets and model training resources, which can comprehensively and fairly test the participating teams when facing complex and changeable scenes, a wide range of crowd characteristics, and different generation techniques. At the same time, a million-scale prize pool is set up to encourage scholars, engineers, educators, students, and independent developers to actively participate in the challenge.
This is a technical competition, and it is also an exploration of the boundaries of information security in the intelligent era. We sincerely invite all parties to join us for the feast of intelligent competition and jointly protect the digital future of authenticity and trust.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For the performance evaluation, we mainly use the AUC (Area under the ROC Curve) in the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve. Area Under Curve (AUC) is defined as the area under the ROC curve, and the value range is generally between 0.5 and 1. TPR@FPR=1E-2, 5E-3, 1E-3 will be used as a secondary reference only if needed, e.g. AUC metric can't distinguish the rankings. Please refer to [1].
Note:
True Positive Rate (TPR): TPR = TP/(TP+FN)
False Positive Rate (FPR): FPR = FP/(FP+TN)
TP: The attacks are recognized as the attacks;
TN: The real samples are recognized as the real ones;
FP: The real samples are recognized as the attacks;
FN: The attacks are recognized as the real samples;


[1] Aghajan, H., Augusto, J. C., & Delgado, R. L. C. (Eds.). (2009). Human-centric interfaces for ambient intelligence. Academic Press.","# Dataset Description

This competition will release the Multi-dimensional Facial Forgery Dataset (MultiFF), which specifically includes the multi-dimensional Facial Forgery Image Dataset (MultiFFI) and the Multi-dimensional Facial Forgery Video Dataset (MultiFFV). These two subsets are used for the image Deepfake detection task in Track 1 and the audio-video Deepfake detection task in Track 2, respectively.
The MultiFF data set is built based on the ""Technical Specifications for Financial Applications of Fake Digital Face Detection"" launched by Ant Group. At the same time, it is deeply combined with the actual defensive experience in the digital business to include truly diversified deep forgery attacks, which mainly contain:
1. Wide range of attack types: including face-swapping, activation, attribute editing, full-face synthesis, audio-driving face generation, face restoration, digital adversarial samples, automated PS, and other common types;
2. Multiple generation methods: There are more than 50 Deepfake generation methods applied to the entire data set. In addition to the classic GANs model, the generation model also fully covers the new generative AI model of the Diffusion series. The attack generation paradigm includes image-to-image generation, text-to-image generation, text-to-speech generation, text-to-video generation, etc.
The test set will cover new Deepfake generation methods to simulate the emerging new attack methods in real scenarios and test the generalization ability and practical application performance of the defense algorithm. In addition, in order to ensure the diversity of samples, we have established a test set including race, age, expression, light, occlusion, indoor and outdoor scenes, and other factors to fully test the effectiveness of the detection methods in various scenes and different demographic groups.
Different from the existing open source Deepfake data sets such as FaceForensics++, DiFF, LAV-DF, and AV-Deepfake1M, which only focus on a certain type of generation model or a certain part of the generation method, the MultiFF data set released in this competition is much more comprehensive and diversified in multiple dimensions such as types, generation methods, generation paradigms, and face material attributes.

**Track 1: Deepfake Image Detection**
The task of the competition is to determine whether a face image is a Deepfake image and output its probability score of being a Deepfake image. 

The competition is divided into three phases:
Phase 1: The training set and validation set are released. The training set train_label.txt is used to train the model, and the validation set val_label.txt is only used for model tuning. For example, each line in train_label.txt or val_label.txt contains two parts separated by commas. The first part is the image file name, and the second part is the label value (label=1 means a Deepfake image, label=0 means a real face image).


train_label.txt
```js
00000001.jpg,1  # the fisrt line of train_label.txt
00000002.jpg,0  # the second line of train_label.txt
¡­
```
val_label.txt
```js
00000003.jpg,1  # the fisrt line of val_label.txt
00000004.jpg,0  # the second line of val_label.txt
¡­
```

Phase 2: After Phase 1, we will release the test set in Phase 2. Contestants need to submit the prediction score file prediction.txt of the test set in the system, and we will feedback on the test score results online. Each line in the file contains two parts separated by commas. The first part is the image file name, and the second part is the Deepfake score corresponding to the model prediction (i.e. the probability value that the sample belongs to a Deepfake image).
prediction.txt
```js
00000005.jpg,0.123456
00000006.jpg,0.123456
¡­
```

Phase 3: After Phase 2, the top 10 teams can advance to the final Phase 3. At this stage, contestants need to submit code docker and technical reports. Docker requirements include the original training code and test API (the function input is the image path, and the output is the deepfake score predicted by the model). We will check and rerun the algorithm code to reproduce the training process and test results. The final ranking will be based on the test set scores submitted by the players, the test set scores we reproduced, and the weighted scores of the technical reports, with weights of 0.4, 0.4, and 0.2 respectively.

**Track 2: Deepfake Audio-Video Detection**
Coming soon",False,True
8233,huasuan-test-001,"# Overview

Welcome to HUASUAN machine learning testing. This test is aimed at assessing the capabilities of HUASUAN's machine learning engineers.

Here we provide 220 steel components and yield strength data obtained from Ctrine. Participants need to find a suitable model to describe the relationship between steel components and yield strength.


----------------------
# Description

Machine learning is developing so rapidly in materials science. The suitable machine learning model can help researchers reduce repeated experiments. However, data in materials science is very precious, so small datasets are usually encountered. Here we have 220 steel yield strength data. We hope you can build a suitable model and predict the yield strength in the test set.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
It is your job to predict the yield strength for each steel. For each Id in the test set, you must predict the value of the YieldStrength variable. 

## Metric
Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the predicted value and the observed sales price. 

## Submission File
For each ""index"" in the test set, you must predict a probability for the ""strength"" variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    index,strength
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `index` - index of steel
*   `formula`?- Chemical formula
*   `c` - 	weight percent of C
*   `mn` - weight percent of Mn
*   `si` - 	weight percent of Si
*   `cr` - weight percent of Cr
*   `ni` - 	weight percent of Ni
*   `mo` - weight percent of Mo
*   `v` - 	weight percent of V
*   `n` - weight percent of N
*   `nb` - weight percent of Nb
*   `co` - weight percent of Co
*   `w` - 	weight percent of W
*   `al` - 	weight percent of Al
*   `ti` - 	weight percent of Ti
*   `strength`?- yield strength of steel, unit: MPa",False,True
8234,2024-not-controllable-electricity-balance-forecast,"# Description

## Welcome to the 2024 Not Controllable Electricity Balance Forecast Challenge

The goal is to create innovative forecast models of the French Not Controllable Electricity Balance. This Kaggle In-Class platform enables you:
. to work as a team
. access data
. code online (notebook or scripts)
. submit your predictions	

## Data context

We will dive into the electricity consumption or the renewable powers world to predict more accurately the French Not Controllable Electricity Balance (MW).

The data features come from:
. an open platform of the French government for the calendar features
. a synop weather station network for the climatic features
. a selection of index meter reader (M+1 ¨C M+12), eventually corrected by a temporal reconciliation (Y+2) for the targeted features
. some feature engineering that you are invited to challenge

## Success tips

Find best models, think about an online solution, feel free to invent new features, surprise us!


```
# dataset features

""""""
## Generic calendar columns
*   **Id** index
*   **date** date - format yyyy-mm-dd HH:MM:SS
*   **Date** date - format yyyymmdd
*   **month** month - format mm
*   **year** year - format yy
*   **tod** time of day 0 = 00:00, 1 = 00:30, ..., 46 = 23:00, 47 = 23:30
*   **toy** time of year 0 (1st of January) to 1 (31st of December)
*   **week_number** number of the week
*   **period_hour_changed** 1 summer time 0 winter time

## Electric consumption related features
*   **temperature** French temperature (K), by regional electrical consumption weights
*   **nebulosity** French nebulosity (%), by regional electrical consumption weights
*   **wind** French wind speed (m/s), by regional electrical consumption weights
*   **day_type_week** 0 = Monday, 1 = Tuesday, 2 = Wednesday, 3 = Thursday, 4 = Friday, 5 = Saturday, 6 = Sunday
*   **day_type_jf** 1 on bank holidays, else 0
*   **day_type_week_jf** 1  for bank holidays or week-ends, else 0
*   **period_holiday** 1 on French national holidays (zones a,b and c), else 0
*   **period_christmas** 1 on Christmas holidays, else 0
*   **period_summer** 1 on summer holidays, else 0

## Solar Power related features
*   **nebulosity_by_solar_power_weights** French nebulosity (%), by regional solar power weights

## Wind Power related features
*   **wind_by_wind_power_weights** French wind speed (m/s), by regional wind power weights

## Targeted features
*   **Load** French national load (MW)
*   **Solar_power** French solar power (MW)
*   **Wind_power** French wind power (MW)
*   **Electricity_balance_not_controllable** = **Load** - **Solar_power** - **Wind_power**
```

Sources :
- https://www.data.gouv.fr/fr/datasets/vacances-scolaires-par-zones/ (Licence Ouverte / Open Licence https://www.etalab.gouv.fr/wp-content/uploads/2014/05/Licence_Ouverte.pdf)
- https://www.data.gouv.fr/fr/datasets/jours-feries-en-france/ (Licence Ouverte / Open Licence version 2.0 --- https://etalab.gouv.fr/licence-ouverte-open-licence)
- https://public.opendatasoft.com/explore/dataset/donnees-synop-essentielles-omm/export/?sort=date (Licence Ouverte v1.0 https://www.etalab.gouv.fr/wp-content/uploads/2014/05/Licence_Ouverte.pdf)
- https://www.rte-france.com/en/eco2mix/download-indicators (Licence Ouverte / Open Licence version 2.0 --- https://etalab.gouv.fr/licence-ouverte-open-licence)","# Dataset Description

