8246,recosys2,"# Description

Afin de satisfaire leurs consommateurs, les entreprises sont amen¨¦es de plus en plus souvent ¨¤ tenter de pr¨¦dire les pr¨¦f¨¦rences de leurs utilisateurs. Un bel exemple est celui de la plateforme de location de vid¨¦os qui souhaite pr¨¦dire, pour chacun de ses utilisateurs, quels sont les films les plus susceptibles de lui plaire. Pour cela, la firme peut demander ¨¤ chaque utilisateur de remplir un questionnaire sur ses go?ts cin¨¦matographiques, et ensuite comparer ces go?ts avec les caract¨¦ristiques des films de son catalogue (drame, com¨¦die, horreur, catastrophe, documentaire...). Cette approche suppose de disposer de ces caract¨¦ristiques et, ce qui est moins r¨¦aliste, de la bonne volont¨¦ des utilisateurs pour remplir ledit questionnaire. Cette approche s'appelle le *content filtering*. 

Une approche plus naturelle pour les utilisateurs est de leur proposer d'attribuer une note aux films qu'ils visionnent. Il s'agit alors pour la plateforme de tirer parti de cette masse de donn¨¦es pour en d¨¦duire des recommandations pertinentes. Votre d¨¦fi est de proposer des solutions algorithmiques qui accomplissent cette tache. Cette approche s'appelle le *collaborative filtering*.  Dans ce projet, on se concentrera sur les approches bas¨¦e sur la factorisation matricielle. 

Voir par exemple: 
- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n3RKsY2H-NE&ab_channel=ArtoftheProblem  pour une introduction assez haut niveau, et 
- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GIcuSNAAa4g&ab_channel=MachineLearningandAI (Lectures 16.x) pour une introduction plus d¨¦taill¨¦e. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

La m¨¦trique d'¨¦valuation pour ce travail est le root mean square error (RMSE), d¨¦fini comme suit  
<p></p>
<p>
\[ 
RMSE = \sqrt{ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n ( rating(i) - prediction(i) )^2}, 
\]
</p><p>
</p> 
o¨´ rating contient les vrais ¨¦valuations inconnues et prediction est votre pr¨¦diction. 
Etant donn¨¦ que les rating sont entre 1 et 5 (¨¦toiles), le RMSE devrait se trouver aux alentours de 1 (erreur moyenne de 1 ¨¦toile). En effet, en utilisant la moyenne pour pr¨¦dire toutes les entr¨¦es, on arrive ¨¤ un RMSE=1.116 (sur le test set). 

Pour la phase d'entrainement de votre approche, Kaggle vous renvoie pour chaque soumission la RMSE pour un sous-ensemble des donn¨¦es manquantes (contenant 50% du total). L'¨¦valuation finale se fera sur le sous-ensemble compl¨¦mentaire.","# Dataset Description

Ce fichier .zip contient l'¨¦nonc¨¦ du projet, quelques r¨¦f¨¦rences utiles, ainsi que les donn¨¦es en format 

## Matlab

*   **inputX.mat** - contient une matrice creuse (X) o¨´ les entr¨¦es non nulles sont les entr¨¦es donn¨¦es. 
*   **inputEval.mat** - contient la liste des entr¨¦es de X ¨¤ pr¨¦dire, aux positions (Eval(i,1), Eval(i,2)) pour i=1,2,...,200041. 

et en format 

## csv

*   **ratings_given.csv** - les entr¨¦es de la matrices qui sont donn¨¦es sous format (j,k,eval(j,k)) o¨´ eval(j,k) est l'¨¦valuation de l'utilisateur k pour le film j. 
*   **ratings_test_eval.csv** - contient (i,j,k) o¨´ i=1,2,...,200041 et (j,k) sont les positions des entr¨¦es ¨¤ pr¨¦dire. 


Il contient ¨¦galement 
- un exemple de pr¨¦diction: sol\_all\_means.csv o¨´ toutes les entr¨¦es sont pr¨¦dites avec la moyenne. 
- la liste des films (movies.dat) et des propri¨¦t¨¦s des utilisateurs (users.dat).",False,True
8247,icc-mens-t-20-world-cup-2024-winner-prediction,"# Overview

To predict the outcomes of the ICC Men's 2024 T20 World Cup semifinals and finals using the provided dataset
----------------------
# Description

, an overview of the predictive approach can be outlined as follows:

Data Preparation
Data Cleaning: Ensure that the dataset is free from missing values, inconsistencies, and errors. Standardize formats for dates, player names, etc.
Feature Engineering: Create new features that may help in prediction, such as:
Player Averages: Calculate average runs, strike rates, and economy rates for players.
Team Strength: Aggregate individual player statistics to estimate overall team strength.
Recent Form: Use recent performance metrics to gauge current form.
Venue Influence: Analyze how different teams and players perform at different venues.
Labeling Data: Define the target variable for predictions, such as ""Win"" or ""Loss"" for each match.
Predictive Modeling
Model Selection: Choose appropriate models for the task. Common models for such predictions include:
Logistic Regression: For binary outcomes like win/loss.
Random Forests: For handling non-linear interactions between features.
Gradient Boosting Machines (GBMs): For robust performance with complex data.
Neural Networks: For potentially capturing more complex patterns in the data.
Training and Testing: Split the dataset into training and testing sets to evaluate model performance.
Cross-Validation: Use cross-validation to ensure that the model generalizes well to unseen data.
Evaluation Metrics
Accuracy: Percentage of correct predictions.
Precision and Recall: For understanding the balance between false positives and false negatives.
F1 Score: Harmonic mean of precision and recall.
ROC-AUC: For evaluating the performance of binary classifiers.
Implementation Steps
Data Ingestion: Load and preprocess the dataset.
Feature Extraction: Compute relevant features from the raw data.
Model Training: Train the selected models on the training data.
Hyperparameter Tuning: Optimize model parameters using techniques like grid search or random search.
Model Evaluation: Assess model performance using the test set and cross-validation.
Prediction: Use the trained model to predict outcomes of future matches, including semifinals and finals.
Visualization and Interpretation
Feature Importance: Identify which features are most influential in the model¡¯s predictions.
Scenario Analysis: Simulate different scenarios to see how changes in team composition or venue might affect outcomes.
Prediction Confidence: Provide confidence intervals or probabilities for predictions to understand uncertainty.
----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate the model using Mean Squared Error (MSE), we need to understand that MSE is a metric commonly used for regression tasks, where the objective is to predict a continuous value. For classification tasks, such as predicting the winner of a match, MSE is not typically used. However, we can adapt it to evaluate a model that outputs probabilities or scores rather than categorical predictions.

Mean Squared Error (MSE) in Regression
MSE measures the average of the squares of the errors, i.e., the average squared difference between the predicted and actual values. It is defined as:

from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error

# Example predicted probabilities and actual outcomes
predicted_probabilities = [0.8, 0.4, 0.6, 0.3, 0.9]
actual_outcomes = [1, 0, 1, 0, 1]

# Calculate MSE
mse = mean_squared_error(actual_outcomes, predicted_probabilities)
print(f'Mean Squared Error: {mse}')","# Dataset Description

let's go through each column in the dataset for the ICC Men's 2024 T20 World Cup:

Match: The identifier or name/number of the match.
Venue: The location where the match is played (e.g., stadium name, city).
Times: The timing details of the match, possibly including the start time and date.
Country: The country of the team whose statistics are being recorded.
Against_Country: The country of the opposing team.
Scored_total: The total runs scored by the team in that match.
Player: The name of the player whose individual performance is being recorded.
Bat_Status_: The batting status of the player (e.g., ""not out"", ""bowled"", ""caught"").
Runs_scored: The number of runs scored by the player.
Ball_scored: The number of balls faced by the player.
scored_4s: The number of boundaries (fours) hit by the player.
scored_6s: The number of sixes hit by the player.
scored_SR: The strike rate of the player, calculated as (Runs_scored / Ball_scored) * 100.
Overs_Conseded: The number of overs bowled by the player.
MOvers_Conseded: The number of maiden overs (overs in which no runs were conceded) bowled by the player.
Runs_Conseded: The number of runs conceded by the player while bowling.
Wickets_Conseded: The number of wickets taken by the player.
NB_Conseded: The number of no-balls bowled by the player.
WD_Conseded: The number of wides bowled by the player.
ECO_Conseded: The economy rate of the player, calculated as (Runs_Conseded / Overs_Conseded).
These columns provide a comprehensive set of data for analyzing both team and individual performances in the T20 World Cup matches.",False,True
8248,gears-fault-detection-through-lubrication,"# Overview

Welcome to the Gearbox Fault Detection Challenge!

In this competition, participants are tasked with developing a robust classification model to predict faults in gearboxes based on industrial lubrication data. Gearboxes are crucial components in various mechanical systems, and predicting faults early can significantly enhance maintenance practices and prevent costly breakdowns.
----------------------
# Description

Gearboxes are critical components in industrial machinery, responsible for transmitting mechanical power through a system of gears. Predicting faults in gearboxes is essential for preventing costly breakdowns and optimizing maintenance schedules. In this competition, participants are challenged to develop machine learning models that can accurately classify the condition of gearboxes based on data collected from their lubrication systems.

**Task:**

Participants will work with a dataset containing various multivariate features extracted from gearbox lubrication processes. These features include temperature, viscosity, pressure, metal particle content, water content, additive levels, pH, density, conductivity, and oxidation index. The objective is to build a classification model that can predict whether a gearbox is in a normal operating condition or if it exhibits signs of potential faults.

**Data Description:**

Training Data: The training dataset consists of labeled examples where each instance includes measurements of the aforementioned variables along with a label indicating the gearbox's state (Good or Faulty).

Test Data: The test dataset contains instances with the same set of variables but without the corresponding labels. Participants are required to submit predictions for these instances, indicating the likelihood of a gearbox fault.

**Evaluation Metric:**

Submissions will be evaluated based on their classification accuracy using metrics such as precision, recall, and F1-score. The primary goal is to correctly identify gearboxes with faults while minimizing false positives and false negatives.

**Submission Format:**

Participants are required to submit a CSV file with predictions for each instance in the test set. Each prediction should indicate the probability or confidence score of the gearbox being faulty.

**Prizes:**

Exciting prizes await the top-performing participants based on their leaderboard rankings. Additionally, participants will gain recognition within the Kaggle community for their innovative solutions to gearbox fault detection.

**Join Us:**

Gear up and join this competition to showcase your machine learning expertise in predicting gearbox faults based on industrial lubrication data. Together, we can revolutionize gearbox maintenance practices and improve operational reliability across industries.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation Process: Accuracy**

Accuracy is a fundamental metric used to evaluate classification models in machine learning. It measures the ratio of correct predictions to the total number of predictions made by the model.

**Step-by-Step Evaluation using Accuracy:**

**True Positives (TP):**
- Number of correctly predicted instances where the gearbox is faulty.

**True Negatives (TN):**
- Number of correctly predicted instances where the gearbox is in good condition.

**False Positives (FP):**
- Number of incorrectly predicted instances where the model predicted the gearbox is faulty, but it is actually in good condition.

**False Negatives (FN):**
- Number of incorrectly predicted instances where the model predicted the gearbox is in good condition, but it is actually faulty.

**Confusion Matrix:**
- Before calculating accuracy, a confusion matrix is constructed from the predictions and the true labels of the test dataset:

|                | Predicted: Good      | Predicted: Faulty    |
|----------------|----------------------|----------------------|
| Actual: Good   | True Negative (TN)   | False Positive (FP)  |
| Actual: Faulty | False Negative (FN)  | True Positive (TP)   |


**Accuracy Calculation:**

- Accuracy is calculated as the ratio of correct predictions (both true positives and true negatives) to the total number of predictions:

Accuracy = (TP+TN) \ (FP+FN+TP+TN?)

- It represents the overall correctness of the model's predictions.

**Mean Accuracy:**

For competitions with multiple classes or instances, the mean accuracy is often used to average the accuracies across all classes or instances. This provides a comprehensive measure of the model's overall performance.
Submission Requirement:

Participants are required to submit predictions in the form of probabilities or confidence scores for each instance in the test dataset.
Kaggle's evaluation system will calculate the accuracy based on these predictions against the true labels of the test dataset.
The leaderboard ranks participants based on their accuracy scores, with higher scores indicating better-performing models in predicting gearbox faults.

**Conclusi¨®n:**

Accuracy is a straightforward and widely used metric for evaluating classification models in this competition. It measures the model's ability to correctly classify gearboxes as faulty or in good condition based on the provided data.
It is essential for participants to aim for high accuracy scores to demonstrate the effectiveness of their models in gearbox fault detection.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the Prediction variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Prediction
    2,Good
    3,Good
    4,Bad
    etc.","# Dataset Description

#**Variables:**

##**1. Temperature (Temp):**

- **Description:** Temperature of the gearbox lubricant.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Degrees Celsius.

##**2. Viscosity (Visc):**

- **Description:** Viscosity of the lubricant, a measure of its resistance to flow.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Centistokes (cSt).

##**3. Pressure (Pres):**

- **Description:** Pressure within the gearbox.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Pascals (Pa).

##**4. Metal Particle Content (Metal):**

- **Description:** Concentration of metal particles in the lubricant.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Parts per million (ppm).

##**5. Water Content (Water):**

- **Description:** Percentage of water content in the lubricant.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Percentage (%).

##**6. Additive Levels (Additive):**

- **Description:** Levels of additives present in the lubricant (e.g., anti-wear additives).
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Parts per million (ppm).

##**7. pH:**

- **Description:** pH value of the lubricant.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** pH scale (0-14).

##**8. Density (Dens):**

- **Description:** Density of the lubricant.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Grams per cubic centimeter (g/cm3).

##**9. Conductivity (Cond):**

- **Description:** Electrical conductivity of the lubricant.
- **Type:** Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Siemens per meter (S/m).

##**10. Oxidation Index (Oxid):**

- **Description:** Oxidation stability index of the lubricant.
- **Type:**Numeric (float).
- **Units:** Arbitrary units (AU).

#**Target Variable:**

##**Gearbox State (Target):**
**Description:** Binary variable indicating the state of the gearbox.

**Values:**
- 0: Good condition.
- 1: Faulty condition.

#**Dataset Details:**

***Training Data:*** Contains labeled examples of gearbox states based on lubrication data for model training.

***Test Data:*** Unlabeled dataset used for final evaluation, where participants submit predictions for gearbox states.

***Note:*** Ensure that missing values (NaNs) and outliers are properly handled before training your models to ensure robust performance.

##**Files**

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.csv** - supplemental information about the data

## Columns

*   `example_id` - definition of example_id
*   `feature_1`?- definition of feature_1
*   etc.",False,True
8249,ViTextVQA-evaluation,"# Overview

Welcome to ViTextVQA (Vietnamese Text-based Visual Question Answering) dataset! This dataset is the first high-quality large-scale dataset in Vietnamese specializing in understanding text appearing in images.

This is the dedicated portal for submitting and evaluating the Exact Match (EM) scores of your models. Here, you can test your model¡¯s performance on our dataset and see how it compares to other models in the community.
----------------------
# Description

**Measuring System Performance in Question-Answering Tasks Using EM**

Exact Match (EM) score is a metric commonly used in NLP tasks, particularly in Question Answering systems. It measures the percentage of predictions made by a model that exactly match the target or ground truth answers.

In the context of question answering, an answer is considered to be correct only if it matches the reference answer exactly. The EM score is a straightforward evaluation metric that provides a clear indication of how often a model produces answers that are exactly right.

The formula for EM score is:

$$
\textbf{EM} = \frac{\text{Count of answers predicted exactly}}{\text{Count of answers}}
$$


----------------------
# Evaluation


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a answer for the question. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
ID,Answer
2,t?i y¨ºu b?n
5,anh y¨ºu em
6,i love you
etc.
```

Participants are required to submit predictions for both the development (dev) set and the test set. The results for the dev set will be used for public leaderboard rankings, while the results for the test set will be used for private leaderboard rankings. The dev set results are evaluated and ranked before the test set results.","# Dataset Description

ViTextVQA contains over 16,000 images and over 50,000 questions with answers. The dataset is designed to evaluate the ability of AI models to comprehend text within images and answer questions based on that understanding.
The purpose of ViTextVQA is to provide a benchmark for evaluating the reading comprehension ability of Visual Question Answering (VQA) models in the Vietnamese language. As a developing country, Vietnam is still in need of resources and benchmarks to advance research in AI and machine learning.

