8280,train-a-strong-stockfish-wdl-model,"# Overview

This competition serves as a continuation from the previous competition, [""Training your own Stockfish NNUE""](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/train-your-own-stockfish-nnue). The purpose of this casual competition is to allow for leisure coding and collaboration in building something that has the potential to be *BIG* in the future, as well as being beginner-friendly at the same time. The predicted WDL of a chess game is usually defined as 3 separate values with the softmax activation function to output, (Prob(Win), Prob(Draw), Prob(Loss)), but for simplification for this competition, it will be provided as one single value between 0 - 1, where 0 means white *lost*, 0.5 means the game is *drawn* and 1 means white has *won*.  
**Goal:** To use the dataset provided in train.csv or your own, and train a neural network Stockfish WDL model that can predict the outcome of a game between Stockfish itself. (More information will be provided in the data description section)
**Deadline:** Christmas day 2024 (25th December 2024 | 12:00 AM)
----------------------
# WDL

### Evaluation of model
Submissions are evaluated on the Absolute Loss (I can't get it to work with categorical cross-entropy) between their predicted values and the actual values.

## Submission File
For each ID, FEN in the test set, you model must predict a value for the WDL. The file should have the following format (check out the [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/train-a-strong-stockfish-wdl-model/data) for more examples)

| ID | WDL |
|----|-----|
| 0 | 1.0 |
| 1 | 1.0 |
| 2 | 1.0 |
| 3 |  0.5 |","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains ~ 6 million chess positions in FEN (Forsyth Edwards Notation) along with their corresponding WDL value under their respective columns ""FEN"" and ""Evaluation"" in **shuffled_fens.csv**. **test.csv** is another dataset with 5000 FENS but with their corresponding evaluations missing. You are to train your model on the data in **shuffled_fens.csv** and then test it on **test.csv**, saving the results in **submission.csv** under the columns ""ID"" and ""WDL"", an example is given in **sample_submission.csv**. 

**There is no limit on what architecture you use**

## Files

*   **shuffled_fens.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

**shuffled_fens.csv**
*   The fens are pre-shuffled for convenience 
*   `FEN` - The chess position in Forsyth Edwards Notation
*   `WDL`?- The WDL of the game played between 2 identical Stockfish versions with a time control of 2+0.02s

**test.csv**
*   `ID` - To identify the position when calculating the loss (To be tied to their respective predicted values and saved in submission as such)
*   `FEN` - The chess position in Forsyth Edwards Notation (WDL to be predicted by model)

**sample_submission.csv**
*   `ID` - 14 IDs in sequence with test.csv with example WDL (But you have to submit all of them - all 5000)
*   `WDL`?- The example WDL prediction of a model, respective to the IDs in test.csv",False,True
