8302,llm-merging-competition,"# Overview

## Aims and Focus
Training high-performing large language models (LLMs) from scratch is a notoriously expensive and difficult task, costing hundreds of millions of dollars in compute alone. These pretrained LLMs, however, can cheaply and easily be adapted to new tasks via fine-tuning, leading to a proliferation of models that suit specific use cases. Recent work has shown that specialized fine-tuned models can be rapidly merged to combine capabilities and generalize to new skills.

## Get started
The competition allows any current model that follows the general conditions (e.g., existed when the competition was announced and is up to 8Gb) see *Rules* for explicit conditions.
A starter kit with an end-to-end submission flow can be found [in our GitHub repo](https://github.com/llm-merging/LLM-Merging), or as a [Kaggle notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/margsli/starter-kit) in the *Code* tab. The version in the github currently does *not* generate the `submission.csv` file. We are working on an updated version and will push it ASAP.
We also have a [website](https://llm-merging.github.io/index) with more information and a [Discord](https://discord.com/invite/VYfFexfSpZ) where you can ask questions, form teams, etc.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submission scores are based on Accuracy and ROUGE metrics. 

## Code submissions

To place your submissions on the leaderboard, we will be using the `submission.csv` file you submit. However, you must *also* submit your code in order for your submission to count. Please do so through the same process previously detailed [here](https://github.com/llm-merging/LLM-Merging?tab=readme-ov-file#submissions). 

## Submission CSV File
If you use our starter kit, you don't need to worry about the submission file format. As long as your merging method works, the starter kit will handle the output format for you. The below is an FYI only. 
For each line in the test set,  you must generate some text. You must output this text, along with the id of the input it corresponds to.
The `submission.csv` file should contain a header and contain at least the following columns:

    id,predicted_text
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.

## Final Results
The final results of the competition will be calculated after the conclusion of the competition. At that point, we will release the inputs for our final held out evaluation, and you will have a week to run your model code on this input. The input will be in the same format as the `test.csv` file in this competition. You will then be responsible for submitting this final output file to us. For all top placers, we will be verifying that the code you submitted via the form before the closing of the competition does indeed yield the submission csv.","# Dataset Description

We have provided you with a `test.csv` file, which includes all of the inputs and associated features needed. The features are described below, but the starter kit code we provide already reads in the inputs and generates the outputs, assuming that you filled in the merging code.

You will submit the `submission.csv` file generated by running your merging method on the provided test inputs. 

At the conclusion of the competition, we will be releasing a new, held out test set, which will _not_ be identical to this test set. That final test set will determine the final competition results. 

## Files

*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - identifying number for the example 
*   `input`?- string input for the model
*   `eval_type` - either `multiple_choice` or `generation`. Used by our evaluation code (in the starter kit) to determine how to evaluate each example. You do not need to use this field, though you may if you want to.
* `answer_choices` - null if the `eval_type` is `generation`. If `eval_type` is `multiple_choice`, a list of multiple choice options",False,True
