8313,can-i-get-into-relationship,"# Overview

This is the home page of the competition. This competition tries to create a fun exercise on beginner level machine learning and data science.


**Goal:**Suppose we have surveyed it over 500 college students in Kolkata, and collected their relationship status, along with some other things, like age, weight, family income, favourite sports, complexion, college grades and rated their looks, behaviour in a uniform metric. Now we want to see if we can predict the relationship status, analyzing these attributes.
N.B.- This is a fake dataset and just a fun exercise in machine?learning
----------------------
# Description


## What is in the data
The test data contains eight columns, namely age, grades, behaviour, complexion, looks, annual family income, weight, sports involvement and (relationship) status. 1 means he or she is in a relationship and 0 means otherwise. You have to come up with your model, and submit the prediction for test dataset, which contains all these columns except status. Your answer can be probabilistic, (e.g. status=0.5)
----------------------
# Evaluation

We have done the same randomization process for the test data and stored it. We will evaluate upon accuracy to that data.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [Euclidean distance] between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The csv file should contain a header and have the following format:

  ID STATUS
    2                       0
    5                      0.5
    6                       1
    etc.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains three files, namely train_data, test_data and sample_submission. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns

*   `student_no.` - serial no. of the student surveyed
*   `age`?- age of the student
*   `grades` - college grade of the student
* `behaviour` - Behaviour of the student, rated from 1 to 10, in a uniform metric
* `complexion` - skin complexion of the student
* `looks` - Looks of the student, rated from 1 to 10, in a uniform metric
* `Annual_family_income` - Annual family income of the student
* `weight` - weight category of the student
* `sports_involvement` -  The sport in which the student is most involved
* `status` - Relationship status of the student, 1 if in a relationship, 0 otherwise",False,True
8314,cis-ieee-unb-churn,"# Overview

Esta competi??o tem como objetivo servir como treinamento para o segundo per¨ªodo do programa de trainee do IEEE-CIS UnB. O desafio consiste em desenvolver um modelo capaz de prever a classifica??o da coluna ""Churn Label"", que indica se um cliente deixou a empresa durante um determinado trimestre.
----------------------
# Description

Esta competi??o tem como objetivo servir como treinamento para o segundo per¨ªodo do programa de trainee do IEEE-CIS UnB. O desafio consiste em desenvolver um modelo capaz de prever a classifica??o da coluna ""Churn Label"", que indica se um cliente deixou a empresa durante um determinado trimestre.


# Source

O dataset ¨¦ detalhado em:
https://community.ibm.com/community/user/businessanalytics/blogs/steven-macko/2019/07/11/telco-customer-churn-1113

Download:
https://community.ibm.com/accelerators/?context=analytics&query=telco%20churn&type=Data&product=Cognos%20Analytics
----------------------
# Evaluation

## M¨¦trica

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F14413999%2Fb7ae14245c9703c90d6d1d261398f897%2FScreenshot%20from%202024-07-10%2013-20-33.png?generation=1720628465378005&alt=media)

## Submission File
Para cada ID no conjunto de teste, voc¨º deve prever a vari¨¢vel Churn Label. O arquivo enviado deve ser um .csv e ter o seguinte formato:

    CustomerID,Churn Label
    2134-AADWWA, No
    5412-FSGGSA, Yes
    3244-LLLMAK, No
    etc.","# Dataset Description

O dataset Telco Customer Churn ¨¦ uma cole??o abrangente de dados que fornece informa??es detalhadas sobre clientes de uma empresa de telecomunica??es, com o objetivo de analisar e prever a rotatividade de clientes (churn). A rotatividade de clientes ¨¦ um problema cr¨ªtico para as empresas, pois indica a perda de clientes para concorrentes. Este dataset inclui diversas vari¨¢veis que podem influenciar a decis?o de um cliente de permanecer ou deixar a empresa, facilitando a cria??o de modelos preditivos para identificar padr?es e fatores de churn.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
* CustomerID: Um ID ¨²nico que identifica cada cliente.

* Count: Um valor usado em relat¨®rios/dashboards para somar o n¨²mero de clientes em um conjunto filtrado.

* Country: O pa¨ªs da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* State: O estado da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* City: A cidade da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* Zip Code: O c¨®digo postal da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* Lat Long: A combina??o de latitude e longitude da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* Latitude: A latitude da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* Longitude: A longitude da resid¨ºncia principal do cliente.

* Gender: O g¨ºnero do cliente: Masculino, Feminino.

* Senior Citizen: Indica se o cliente tem 65 anos ou mais: Sim, N?o.

* Partner: Indica se o cliente tem um parceiro: Sim, N?o.

* Dependents: Indica se o cliente vive com dependentes: Sim, N?o. Dependentes podem ser filhos, pais, av¨®s, etc.

* Tenure Months: Indica o total de meses que o cliente est¨¢ com a empresa at¨¦ o final do trimestre especificado acima.

* Phone Service: Indica se o cliente assina o servi?o de telefone residencial com a empresa: Sim, N?o.

* Multiple Lines: Indica se o cliente assina v¨¢rias linhas telef?nicas com a empresa: Sim, N?o.

* Internet Service: Indica se o cliente assina o servi?o de Internet com a empresa: N?o, DSL, Fibra ¨®ptica, Cabo.

* Online Security: Indica se o cliente assina um servi?o adicional de seguran?a online fornecido pela empresa: Sim, N?o.

* Online Backup: Indica se o cliente assina um servi?o adicional de backup online fornecido pela empresa: Sim, N?o.

* Device Protection: Indica se o cliente assina um plano adicional de prote??o de dispositivos para o equipamento de Internet fornecido pela empresa: Sim, N?o.

* Tech Support: Indica se o cliente assina um plano adicional de suporte t¨¦cnico da empresa com tempos de espera reduzidos: Sim, N?o.

* Streaming TV: Indica se o cliente usa o servi?o de Internet para transmitir programas de televis?o de um provedor terceirizado: Sim, N?o. A empresa n?o cobra uma taxa adicional por este servi?o.

* Streaming Movies: Indica se o cliente usa o servi?o de Internet para transmitir filmes de um provedor terceirizado: Sim, N?o. A empresa n?o cobra uma taxa adicional por este servi?o.

* Contract: Indica o tipo de contrato atual do cliente: M¨ºs a M¨ºs, Um Ano, Dois Anos.

* Paperless Billing: Indica se o cliente optou pela fatura sem papel: Sim, N?o.

* Payment Method: Indica como o cliente paga sua conta: D¨¦bito Banc¨¢rio, Cart?o de Cr¨¦dito, Cheque Enviado.

* Monthly Charge: Indica o total mensal atual do cliente por todos os seus servi?os da empresa.

* Total Charges: Indica o total de cobran?as do cliente, calculado at¨¦ o final do trimestre especificado acima.

* Churn Label: Sim = o cliente deixou a empresa neste trimestre. N?o = o cliente permaneceu na empresa. Diretamente relacionado ao Churn Value.

* CLTV: Valor do Tempo de Vida do Cliente. Um CLTV previsto ¨¦ calculado usando f¨®rmulas corporativas e dados existentes. Quanto maior o valor, mais valioso ¨¦ o cliente. Clientes de alto valor devem ser monitorados quanto ao churn.",False,True
8315,cnn-hands-on-practice,"# Overview

In this hands-on practice session, we will focus on building, training, and evaluating a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for image classification. CNNs are a class of deep learning models widely used for analyzing visual data. They are designed to automatically and adaptively learn spatial hierarchies of features from input images, making them highly effective for tasks such as image recognition, object detection, and segmentation.

Our objective is to apply a CNN model to classify a dataset of images into three categories: COVID, normal, and virus. The dataset consists of labeled images stored in separate directories, and we will use TensorFlow and Keras libraries to implement the CNN. The process involves several steps, including data preprocessing, model building, training, evaluation, and generating predictions.","# Dataset Description

## Context

The whole world is suffering from COVID-19 disease and its proper and timely diagnosis is the need of the hour. So for building an efficient AI-based diagnostic system we have collected Chest ray images from different sources so that we can train our CNN model for automating the whole diagnosis method. Therefore we have collected Chest ray images from different sources and research papers and combined them to create one comprehensive dataset that can be used by research community.

This dataset is also used in COVID Lite paper which has shown significant results by building novel CNN based solution.

## Content

This dataset consists of a posteroanterior (PA) view of chest X-ray images comprising Normal, Viral, and CVOID-19 affected patients. There are total 1823 CXR images.

## Acknowledgements

Please cite these papers if you are using it for any scientific purpose:

1. D. Kermany, K. Zhang, M. Goldbaum, Large dataset of labeled optical coherence tomography (oct) and chest x-ray images, Mendeley Data, v3 http://dx. doi. org/10.17632/rscbjbr9sj 3 (2018).
2. C. J. P, P. Morrison, D. L, Covid-19 image data collection, arxiv, arXiv preprint arXiv:2003.11597 (2020). URL https://github.com/ieee8023/covid-chestxray-dataset
3. Z.-H. Chen, Mask-rcnn detection of covid-19 pneumonia symptoms by employing stacked autoencoders in deep unsupervised learning on low-dose
    high resolution ct (2020). doi:10.21227/4kcm-m312.URL http://dx.doi.org/10.21227/4kcm-m312
4. D. S. et.al, Covid19action-radiology-cxr (2020). doi:10.21227/s7pw-jr18.
    URL http://dx.doi.org/10.21227/s7pw-jr18",False,True
8316,prediciton-of-ocean-temperature-profile-2024,"# description

# You get 3 files about competetion.
The file named ""train_data_target.npz"" include data of training and corresponding target. You can use the program `np.load(filename)[part needed]` to get the data you need.
Data represents the temperature profiles of a ocean region about 138¡ã-141¡ãE and 1¡ã-4¡ãN. The max depth of the data is 2000m.
The shape of every data is  (625, 37, 12) which represents number of horizontal vertices, number of vertical profiles and number of time strides used to predict future region temperature respectively. **You can reshape it in (25, 25, 37, 12) datasize to observe or utilize the spatial feature.**
*Attention:*
(25, 25, 37, 12) is just the shape of one piece of data. There is an dimension of num of data at position 0. Just use the `data_train.reshape(-1, 25, 25, 37, 12)`
The corresponding target is (625, 37, 1).
If you decide to use torch structure to make your deeplearning net model, we advice you to use the program to transform the numpydata to torch tensor:

```
train_x_tensor = torch.from_numpy(data_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N, F, T)

train_target_tensor = torch.from_numpy(target_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N,F T)
```


Then, you can use the program to make your data set by your needs

```
train_dataset = torch.utils.data.TensorDataset(train_x_tensor, train_target_tensor)

train_loader = torch.utils.data.DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True/Flase)
```

# test data
The shape of test data is (304, 625, 37, 12), the target is (304, 625, 37, 1)(*Attention: The first location represents the number of all test data, and the last one is the prediction time. The others are as same as the training data.*)  **which is the data you need to submit but you need to reshape it as the (304, -1) using the program.**
```
reshaped_data = target.reshape(304, -1)

df = pd.DataFrame(reshaped_data)

#add ID
df.insert(0, 'ID', range(1, 304+1))

df.to_csv('output.csv', index=False)
```


# reference: 
We thank the National Science and Technology Resources Sharing Service Platform-National Marine Science Data Center (http://mds.nmdis.org.cn/) for providing data support","# Dataset Description


## train_data_target.npz
The file named ""train_data_target.npz"" include data of training and corresponding target. You can use the program ""np.load(filename)[part needed]"" to get the data you need.
Data represents the temperature profiles of a ocean region about 138¡ã-141¡ãE and 1¡ã-4¡ãN. The max depth of the data is 2000m.
The shape of every data is  (625, 37, 12) which represents number of horizontal vertices, number of vertical profiles and number of time strides used to predict future region temperature respectively. **You can reshape it in (25, 25, 37, 12) datasize to observe or utilize the spatial feature.**
** (25, 25, 37, 12) is just the shape of one piece of data. There is an dimension of num of data at position 0. Just use the data_train.reshape(-1, 25, 25, 37, 12)**
The corresponding target is (625, 37, 1).
If you decide to use torch structure to make your deeplearning net model, we advice you to use the program to transform the numpydata to torch tensor:

```
train_x_tensor = torch.from_numpy(data_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N, F, T)

train_target_tensor = torch.from_numpy(target_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N,F T)
```
Then, you can use the program to make your data set by your needs
```
train_dataset = torch.utils.data.TensorDataset(train_x_tensor, train_target_tensor)

train_loader = torch.utils.data.DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True/Flase)
```
## test_data.npz
The shape of test data is (304, 625, 37, 12), the target is (304, 625, 37, 1)(Attention: The first location represents the number of all test data, and the last one is the prediction time. The others are as same as the training data.)  which is the data you need to submit but you need to reshape it as the (304, -1) using the program.
```
reshaped_data = target.reshape(304, -1)

df = pd.DataFrame(reshaped_data)

#add ID
df.insert(0, 'ID', range(1, 304+1))

df.to_csv('output.csv', index=False)

```

## sample_submission.csv
This one is the sample file you need to submit. You can observe it to adjust your file's format before submission.",False,True
8317,underwateracousticsignal-modulation-recognition-24,"# Description

## Acknowledgements
The underwater acoustic signal data for this competition was simulated based on the publicly available underwater acoustic channel dataset Watermark[1]. 
[1] P. A. van Walree, F. Socheleau, R. Otnes, and T. Jenserud, ¡°The watermark benchmark for underwater acoustic modulation schemes,¡± IEEE J. Ocean. Eng., vol. 42, no. 4, pp. 1007¨C1018, Oct. 2017.","# Dataset Description

The dataset is a underwater acoustic communication signal based on the simulation of the underwater acoustic channel environment, including a total of nine modulation types at eight signal-to-noise ratios.

Modulation Type: OFDM, 2FSK, 4FSK, 8FSK, BPSK, QPSK, 8PSK, 16QAM, 64QAM.
SNR: -10dB, -6dB, -2dB, 2dB, 6dB, 10dB, 14dB, 18dB.

## Files

*   **trainDataset.npy** - the training set
        The training set data is stored as a dictionary, including four keys: 'ID', 'SNR', 'labels', 'features'.
               - 'ID': A unique identifier for each signal.
               - 'SNR': The SNR of each signal.
               - 'labels': The modulation type of each signal, indicated by 0-8.
               - features: I (In-phase) /Q (Quadrature) features of each signal. Each signal consists of 2048 features, the first 
                                 1024 being the I-component of the signal and the last 1024 being the Q-component.

*   **testDataset.npy** - the test set
        The test set data is stored as a dictionary, including four keys: 'ID', 'features'.
               - 'ID': A unique identifier for each signal.
               - features: I (In-phase) /Q (Quadrature) features of each signal. Each signal consists of 2048 features, the first 
                                 1024 being the I-component of the signal and the last 1024 being the Q-component.

*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
       An example of submitting test set results.  Submit a two-column "".csv"" file, the first column is the ID number and the second column is the classification result, i.e. 0-8.",False,True
8318,bootcamp-the-basics-of-ml,"# Overview

 Competition Overview
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the Airbus Finance NLP Kaggle Competition on classifying Twitter comments regarding airlines.

Participants will be provided with a dataset of tweets related to various airlines, along with their sentiment labels (positive or negative)(airline_sentiment_train.csv)
We will process the text using common Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques
We will train a ML Classification Model
Participants will also be provided with a dataset of tweets without a sentiment label (airline_sentiment_test.csv)
Each team will submit their predictions and see their ranking in the Leaderboard section
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric used will be Accuracy: The number of instances correctly classified divided by the total number of instances. In this case our dataset is very balanced (same number of positives and negatives) so this metric is an appropriate one.
----------------------
# Prize

None","# Dataset Description

The Twitter airline dataset is a collection of comments posted by Twitter users about their travel experiences with different airlines. The dataset is composed of a total of 5,313 tweets that were gathered over a period of six months.

Each tweet has been labelled as either positive or negative based on the sentiment of the tweet towards the airline.

The data has been split into training (3,963 rows) and testing (1,350 rows) datasets
`airline_tweets_train.csv` - The training set, we will use this dataset to train our models
`airline_tweets_test.csv` - The test set, we will evaluate and rank teams with this dataset
Columns
`Id` - Numeric Id of the tweet
`airline_sentiment` - Sentiment of the tweet, 'positive' 'negative' or 'neutral'
`airline` - Name of the airline referenced in the tweet
`text` - Content of the tweet
`retweet_count` - Number of retweets performed on tweet
`user_timezone` - Timezone of the user posting tweet",False,True
