8322,fit5215-2024-inclas-competition,"# Overview

Welcome to the FIT5215 model competition! This challenge focuses on developing models for Big Five personality analysis and recognition (regression task). Participants are required to submit their models for evaluation, with a chance to earn bonus points based on your performance.

1. Your task is to predict the following traits based on the provided features: extraversion, neuroticism, agreeableness, conscientiousness, and openness. This is a regression task where you will predict continuous values for each trait.
2. The top 3 participants/teams with the lowest average MAE (Mean Absolute Error) in the top 3 will receive a bonus.
3. Performance Benchmark: The top 3 results must exceed the performance of this paper https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.05119.
4. The bonus pool starts at 10 points. If no one submits a model in any given week, the bonus will increase by 1 point. The bonus pool accumulates, with a maximum of 8 weeks, resulting in a maximum of 18 points.
5. The competition will last for 2 months, starting on July 1st and ending on August 31st.
6. If you are participating as a team, your bonus points will be divided equally among team members.
7. Any model can be used, including LLMs (Large Language Models).
8. Your team name must include your student ID or Name to facilitate marking.
**Note: The top 3 participants/teams need to submit their complete code and training data for verification.**


**Dataset:**
The training and validation datasets are available through this competition data link, and you can download them directly without the need for cumbersome unzipping.
Dataset description is accessible via https://chalearnlap.cvc.uab.es/dataset/24/description/. 
***Note: Although this competition's test data is different from the dataset link, please only use the training and validation data for training your model.  We will check your code and training data after the competition.***

**Competition Timeline:**
Start Date: July 1, 2024
End Date: August 31, 2024
The competition runs for a total of 2 months.

**References:**
https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.05119
https://github.com/yagguc/deep_impression
https://chalearnlap.cvc.uab.es/dataset/24/description/
https://chalearnlap.cvc.uab.es/
https://gesture.chalearn.org/2016-looking-at-people-eccv-workshop-challenge

We hope you enjoy this competition. Have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Submission File
For each ID (in test_index_mapping.csv) in the test set, you must predict its values. The file (e.g., csv) should contain a header and have the following format:

ID,extraversion,neuroticism,openness,conscientiousness,agreeableness
1,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
2,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
3,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
4,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
...
2999,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
3000,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5","# Dataset Description

The training and validation datasets are available through this competition data link, and you can download them directly without the need for cumbersome unzipping.
Dataset description is accessible via https://chalearnlap.cvc.uab.es/dataset/24/description/. 

***Note: The test data for this competition differs from the original. Please ensure you only use the training and validation data provided for training your model. We will review your code and training data after the competition.***",False,True
8323,dlai-8-digit-recognizer,"# Description

***This is based on Kaggle's Digit Recognizer competition at [https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/digit-recognizer/overview](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/digit-recognizer/overview). The labels of the test set from the Digit Recognizer competition are available widely and may be used by some as part of the training set, leading to perfect accuracy scores which skews the actual performance of the prediction models. This competition uses a near leakage-free test set that would provide a better assessment of the model performance on the Leaderboard. The initial test data set (source to be disclosed) likely contains out-of-distribution samples that shows potential biases in the MNIST data set. Updated test data set may be used before the end of the competition.***

The following is taken from the Kaggle competition description with minor changes.

### Competition Description

MNIST (""Modified National Institute of Standards and Technology"") is the de facto ¡°hello world¡± dataset of computer vision. Since its release in 1999, this classic dataset of handwritten images has served as the basis for benchmarking classification algorithms. As new machine learning techniques emerge, MNIST remains a reliable resource for researchers and learners alike.

In this competition, your goal is to correctly identify digits from a dataset of tens of thousands of handwritten images. We¡¯ve curated a set of tutorial-style kernels which cover everything from regression to neural networks. We encourage you to experiment with different algorithms to learn first-hand what works well and how techniques compare.
Practice Skills

-     Computer vision fundamentals including simple neural networks
-     Classification methods such as SVM and K-nearest neighbors

### Acknowledgements 

More details about the dataset, including algorithms that have been tried on it and their levels of success, can be found at [http://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/index.html](http://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/index.html). The dataset is made available under a [Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 3.0 license](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Goal

The goal in this competition is to take an image of a handwritten single digit, and determine what that digit is.
For every in the test set, you should predict the correct label.

### Metric

This competition is evaluated on the macro f-score.

### Submission File Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

&gt; ImageId,Label
1,0
2,0
3,0
etc.","# Dataset Description

***The following description is based on the Digit Recognizer competition by Kaggle.***  *The training data set is the same as the one from Digit Recognizer.*  *However, it is likely there are a few out-of-distribution data and possibly some data leakage in the test set.*

The data files train.csv and test.csv contain gray-scale images of hand-drawn digits, from zero through nine.

Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255, inclusive.

The training data set, (train.csv), has 785 columns. The first column, called ""label"", is the digit that was drawn by the user. The rest of the columns contain the pixel-values of the associated image.

Each pixel column in the training set has a name like pixelx, where x is an integer between 0 and 783, inclusive. To locate this pixel on the image, suppose that we have decomposed x as x = i * 28 + j, where i and j are integers between 0 and 27, inclusive. Then pixelx is located on row i and column j of a 28 x 28 matrix, (indexing by zero).

For example, pixel31 indicates the pixel that is in the fourth column from the left, and the second row from the top, as in the ascii-diagram below.

Visually, if we omit the ""pixel"" prefix, the pixels make up the image like this:

> 000 001 002 003 ... 026 027
>
> 028 029 030 031 ... 054 055
>
> 056 057 058 059 ... 082 083
>
> ...
>
> 728 729 730 731 ... 754 755
>
> 756 757 758 759 ... 782 783 

The test data set, (test.csv), does not contain the ""label"" column.

Your submission file should be in the following format: For each of the images in the test set, output a single line containing the ImageId and the digit you predict. For example, if you predict that the first image is of a 3, the second image is of a 7, and the third image is of a 8, then your submission file would look like:

> ImageId,Label
>
> 1,3
>
> 2,7
>
> 3,8 
>
> (... more lines)


The evaluation metric for this contest is the categorization accuracy, or the proportion of test images that are correctly classified. For example, a categorization accuracy of 0.97 indicates that you have correctly classified all but 3% of the images.",False,True
