8349,internal-waves,"# Overview

## Goal

*The **goal** of this competition is to automate the identification of simplified satellite images that have internal waves.*

The exercise consists of an image classification challenge of satellite images from Sentinel 1 (Copernicus Constellation) for the presence of internal waves.
----------------------
# Description

## What are Internal Waves?
Oceanic internal waves propagate within the ocean when it is stratified and disturbed by physical mechanisms. These waves, typically nonlinear (Internal Solitary Waves -ISWs), can reach amplitudes of over 100 meters. They generate the highest vertical velocities in the ocean and strong horizontal shear currents, often causing underwater navigation accidents and damaging sea platforms (Osborne and Burch 1980). Furthermore, ISWs resuspend sediments over the continental shelf (Quaresma et al. 2007) and can produce intense mixing in the deep ocean. ISWs can be detected by remote rensing ratellites througth variations in sea surface roughness, observable  with optical and radar sensors (Magalhaes and da Silva 2017; Zhang et al. 2020; Santos-Ferreira et al. 2018, 2019, 2022, 2023). However, the most effective method for observing ISWs across the global ocean is using SAR imaging, as the Sentinel-1 images used in this competition (due e.g. to the cloud contamination in case of the optical images).

Example of an image with internal waves
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F374896%2Fd6b3366aab525682cc3cb8d209a3a362%2F525_ISW_train.png?generation=1720677890346245&alt=media)

Example of an image without internal waves
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F374896%2Fe6de68d77a1891c1588bd564b1b54add%2F596.png?generation=1720678042984610&alt=media)

### References
Osborne, A. R., and T. L. Burch, 1980. Internal solitons in the Andaman Sea. Science, 208, 451¨C460, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.208.4443.451.        
Quaresma, L. S., J. Vitorino, A. Oliveira, and J. C. B. da Silva, 2007: Evidence of sediment resuspension by nonlinear internal waves on the western Portuguese mid-shelf. Mar. Geol., 246(2-4), 123-143, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.margeo.2007.04.019.        
Magalhaes, J. M., and J. C. B. da Silva, 2017: Satellite altimetry observations of large-scale internal solitary waves. IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., 14, 534-538, https://doi.org/10.1109/LGRS.2017.2655621.         
Santos-Ferreira, A. M., J. C. B. da Silva, and J. M. Magalhaes, 2018: SAR Mode altimetry.        
Santos-Ferreira, A. M., J. C. B. da Silva, J. M. Magalhaes, and M. Srokosz, 2019: SAR mode
altimetry observations of internal solitary waves in the Tropical Ocean Part 2: A method of detection. Remote Sens., 11(11), 1339, https://doi.org/10.3390/rs11111339.          
Santos-Ferreira, A. M., J. C. B. da Silva, J. M. Magalhaes, S. Amraoui, T. Moreau, C. Maraldi, F. Borde, N. Picot, and F. Borde, 2022: Effects of Surface Wave Breaking Caused by Internal Solitary Waves in SAR Altimeter: Sentinel-3 Copernicus Products and Advanced New Products. Remote Sens., 14, 587, https://doi.org/10.3390/rs14030587.         
Santos-Ferreira, A. M., J. C. B. Silva, B. St-Denis, D. Bourgault, and L. R. M. Maas, 2023: Internal Solitary Waves within the Cold Tongue of the Equatorial Pacific generated by buoyant gravity currents. J. Phys. Oceanogr., 53(10), 2419-2434, https://doi.org/10.1175/JPO-D-22-0165.1.       
Zhang, X., J. Zhang, J. Meng, Ch. Fan, and J. Wang, 2020: Observation of internal waves with OLCI and SRAL on board Sentinel-3. Acta Oceanol. Sin., 39(3): 56¨C62, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13131-019-1510-7. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Kaggle's Roc Auc Score [Version 6], which is documented [here.](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/roc-auc-score)

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,predicted_probability
    2,0.3457437
    5,0.9999934
    6,0.2753333
    etc.
----------------------
# Getting Started (optional)

To prepare the competition, namely to validate files and have a baseline, I developed two approaches (which you can find on my [GitHub](https://github.com/jpinelo/internal_wave_competition)) A Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) classifier, and a Convolutional Neural network (CNN). I did not optimise them but the low baseline seems to indicate that the data set has signal. So, with with some work you might get to a strong classifier.

https://github.com/jpinelo/internal_wave_competition","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of over 4,000 images that can be though of as photographs and they are presented on .PNG format. More detailed information about the images below.

## Images
All images were acquired and prepared through the same exact method.
Dimensions: 539?¡Á?528 pixels
Colour space: RGB
Alpha channel: Yes
Format: .PNG
Disk Volume: between 

## Dataset Balance
During the preparation of the dataset, we made sure that both the training (70%) and test (30%) datasets contain a balanced number (50%) of images with the target (feature that we aim to detect) *internal waves*.

## Data Augmentation
We have performed a basic data augmentation task based on image rotation, whereby each original was rotated by 90, 180 and 270 degrees. These images are included in the dataset, which is distributed by the Train and Test folders.

## Folders
* Train folder: Contains images for training models, in .PNG format. The name of each file corresponds to the id of the image in the folders; (e.g. image 5.png corresponds to id 5 on the tables).
* Test folder: Contains images for testing models, in .PNG format.

## Files
*   **train.csv** - A table listing the images of the training set (contents of the Train folder). The table contains the columns 'id' and 'ground_truth'. 70% with guaranteed 50% positive.
*   **test.csv** - A table listing the images of the testing set (contents of the Test folder). The table contains the column 'id'. 30% with guaranteed 50% positive.
*   **solution.csv** - A table listing the images of the testing set (contents of the Test folder). The table contains the columns 'id', 'ground_truth' and 'usage'. 
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the expected format.

## Columns
*   `id` - Unique identifier of an image in the whole dataset. In other words, id's do not repeat on the test and trains datasets. The id of an image is the file name plus the extension.png (e.g. id 5 on tables referes to image 5.png).
*   `ground_truth`?- Confirmation by experts wether the image contains (1) or not (0) the feature of interest (internal wave).
*    'usage'  - This column randomly classifies rows as ""Private"" or ""Public"", corresponding to a 50% split of the test set on testing and validation segments.

#### Source of the Images & Access Conditions
The images were sourced from: https://xwaves.ifremer.fr/#/quicklook
The use of the images is regulated by: http://en.data.ifremer.fr/All-about-data/Data-access-conditions

#### The Images (optional information)
The images that make up the dataset can be thought of as photographs. We believe that one can, without compromising the understanding of the competition or performance, think of them as regular cat photos. However, if you are curious, they are WV mode vignettes (also known as imagettes) from Sentinel 1. These images are acquired in a single polarisation over 20 km by 20 km every 100 km along the orbit. The images in this dataset were collected and annotated (labelled or classified) manually by a team of experts with between seven and 30 years of experience with internal waves. Images include specimens from three oceans (Atlantic, Indic and Pacific), at different dates and hours, along the last four years.",False,True
