8363,nwds-batted-balls,"# Overview

# Nick Wan's Hit Prediction Community Competition 

New swing data from MLB provides information on how fast a batter swings their bat and how long the swing is given the swing path. Launch angle and exit velocity have provided new metrics like barrels and hard hit rate, what will bat speed and swing length generate? To accelerate development in the public space, I've created a contest to predict batted ball outcomes. My hope is that we can dive deeper into how to interpret the interaction of bat speed and swing length in the context of ball contact and if we're lucky we might be able to find a new batter metric. Overall, creativity using bat speed and swing length should help generate stronger batted ball outcome predictions.  

The competition lasts until October 1st at 8:00pm US Eastern Time and winners will be announced on stream October 1st at 9:00pm US Eastern Time.

Check out the stream here:
https://twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci

Join the discord to talk about data science and sports analytics here:
https://discord.gg/3sHgSPsPzS  

NOTE: This has no relation to the Cincinnati Reds, it's all for my community who support my streams every night! For those looking for the next Cincinnati Reds Hackathon, please check back in the winter. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The competition will evaluate submissions based on AUC. While log loss is a popular evaluation choice for multiclass problems, there tends to be some strong Kaggle competitors who are able to create very aggressive predictions that may not represent the spirit of this competition. For example, triples are very rare compared to other hit types, so predicting 0% triples might be a good strategy when optimizing for log loss. However, you wouldn't predict 0 triples in practice since they are events that do happen. AUC helps penalize a submission that completely exclude triples.

If you are interested in sharing your notebook and walking through your code with Nick on stream, leave a message in the discord, on the discussion board, or submit your notebook!

The competition lasts until October 1st at 8:00pm US Eastern Time and winners will be announced on stream October 1st at 9:00pm US Eastern Time

Check out the stream here:
https://twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci

Join the discord to talk about data science and sports analytics here:
https://discord.gg/3sHgSPsPzS
----------------------
# Team Requirements

You can be on a team with up to 4 people. To be eligible for the prizes, everyone on the team needs to be at least 18 years or older and live in the US. 
----------------------
# Prizes

Top 3 submissions will qualify for a prize (restricted to people in the US, 18+). 

1st place will get a choice of either: 

hotel room paid for during [Carnegie Mellon Sports Analytics Conference](https://www.stat.cmu.edu/cmsac/conference/2024/). AND a slot to be an invited presenter for the conference. If your team cannot travel to the conference, this prize will be offered to the 2nd place team (and so on).

Please note that the prizes are per team member -- for example, if there are shirts as prizes, a team of 1 would get 1 shirt while a team of 4 would get 4 shirts. Furthermore, the hotel rooms are maximum 2 rooms. So if you are a team of 1, you would get 1 room. If you are a team of 2-4, you would get only 2 rooms. 

OR 

An HP Z2 Mini Workstation 

2nd place will be offered whatever the 1st place team doesn't want. So if the first place team selects the Z2, the 2nd place team will be offered the conference speaker invite + hotel stay. 

The top 3 teams will get Lambda Labs cloud compute credits: 
1st place will get $500 in credit 
2nd place will get $300 in credit 
3rd place will get $100 in credit 

FOR EVERYONE: 
https://lambdalabs.com/mlb-kaggle
Lambda Labs is also giving everyone $20 in compute to check out their service and use some compute for your final push in the competition. Click the link to redeem your free $20 after signup! 

Lastly, the submitted notebooks will all be assessed by a panel of sports analytics judges for clarity and communication, as well as methods and data visualizations. The top notebook will be awarded a Gucci x MLB slide sandals. 

Eligibility: All prize winners need to be 18 years old or older, live in the US, and have all code used to generate the final prediction file posted in Notebooks. Please provide contact info (you can do message me directly about contact info if you win). If you do not meet these eligibility requirements, your prize will be offered to the next team on the leaderboard. Lastly, if you choose to unclaim your prize voluntarily, please let me know.","# Dataset Description

This dataset includes only balls in play and home runs. The newest MLB data (swing length and bat speed) can help provide a lot of new insights on how to predict batted balls, so that is the reason for sampling the data down to balls in play (and further limited to spray angles between -45 and 45 degrees, which would be equivalent to the foul lines). While there are other questions that are also interesting concerning bat speed and swing length (swing decisions, bat speed differences on swinging strikes vs contact), we are attempting to answer bat speed and swing length as it relates to ball contact. The insights of exit velocity and launch angle have inspired new metrics like hard hit rate and barrels so I am excited to see new public insights with the latest available swing data. 

This dataset is from the 2024 MLB season up to the All Star Game. 

The data comes from MLB Statcast via Baseball Savant. Please reference the Baseball Savant Statcast Search CSV documentation for a data dictionary:
https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/csv-docs

Some columns are unique to this competition. These are:

`uid`: a unique ID for each row in the dataset
`is_lhp`: whether a pitcher throws with their left hand
`is_lhb`: whether a batter hits with a left-handed stance 
`spray_angle`: calculated via pybaseball and is described as 
> Spray angle is the raw left-right angle of the hit - Adjusted spray angle flips the sign for left handed batters, making it a push/pull angle. Inspired by this [Alan Nathan post](http://baseball.physics.illinois.edu/carry-v2.pdf). The formula to transform hit coordinates to spray angle used was obtained from [this blog post](https://baseballwithr.wordpress.com/2018/01/15/chance-of-hit-as-function-of-launch-angle-exit-velocity-and-spray-angle/).

`outcome`: the result of the batted ball; can be 'out', 'single', 'double', 'triple', or 'home_run' 
`outcome_code`: for convenience and clarity, the outcome column is represented with integers where 0='out', 1='single', 2='double', 3='triple', and 4='home_run'. 
Note: It is possible to cheat fairly simply. Please do not cheat! 

# The benchmark sample solution
The benchmark is created by Nick Wan using out-of-the-box (aka no model tuning) modeling techniques and all the features in the dataset with no feature engineering. Submissions that can beat this benchmark may have better feature engineering and/or better modeling techniques.",False,True
