8373,ito-5212-rec-sys,"# Description

This dataset for this task is collected from Google reads in which people rate and review the books that they read. The task is to predict the ratings of the books for a given user.

## Data
The user-item interaction data is the main data for this challenge. This data is further split into training and test sets.
- train.csv. The training dataset contains a set of user_item ratings between users and items. The users explicitly rated the items that they interacted with between 1 to 5.
- test.csv. Each user is provided with a list of items in the test dataset, for each user, you will need to predict the ratings for all the items in their list.

Please train your recommender systems and generate the outputs for the test data.

## Rules
- One account per participant. You cannot sign up on Kaggle using multiple accounts.
- No private code or data sharing among students
- You may submit a maximum of **10 entries per day**.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation
The metric to be used in this challenge is the Mean Absolute Error (MAE). A brief description of MAE can be found [here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error).

---

## Submission Format
For every user-item pair in the test set, you need to provide a predicted rating. The submission file should have the following format (see `sample_submission.csv` file under the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/5212-recomsys/data)):

    ID, rating
    100000,2
    100001,1
    ...","# Dataset Description

These files are essential files:

- train.csv - the training set
- test.csv - the test set
- sample_solution.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

Data fields in the training, validation, and test data:

- user_id - an anonymous id unique to a given customer
- item_id - the id of an item
- rating -the interaction between a user and an item, ranges between 1-5
- book_name: the name of the book
- ID: this column only exists in test data and is there to handle Kaggle constraints. Avoid making any changes to this column as it is the main identifier when we mark your submission.",False,True
8374,shaastra-techathon-ai-ml-challenge-2,"# Overview

Welcome, dear participant! Shaastra Magazine of IIT Madras is conducting **Techathon: AI/ML Challenge 2**, a hackathon for colleges across India. This hackathon has prize money worth 50,000 rupees! Please find the magazine website [here](https://shaastramag.iitm.ac.in/). Please ensure that you are registered at the competition website.

Use this incredible opportunity to boost your AI/ML skill and apply it to solving a practical real-life problem. This hackathon is curated for Data Scientists of all calibre! Whether you're a beginner who wants to try out this exciting field or a seasoned veteran honing your skill, this hackathon is for you. All the best!
----------------------
# Description

Predicting Average Temperature for City D Based on Geographic Features

Dataset Overview:
This dataset provides geographical and temperature data for four cities ( A,B and C) to help predict the average temperature for a fourth city (City D). The data includes latitude, longitude, elevation, precipitation, snow depth and temperature (maximum, minimum and average) experienced in a day for each of the three cities. The goal is to build a model that accurately predicts the average temperature in City D based on the provided geographical features.

Understanding and predicting temperature variations across different geographic locations is critical for weather forecasting, environmental monitoring, and climate research. By leveraging data from multiple cities, this dataset allows for the development of predictive models that can estimate the maximum temperature of a target city based on its geographic proximity and features relative to other cities.
----------------------
# Evaluation


Mean Absolute Error (MAE) is a commonly used metric in regression analysis to measure the average magnitude of errors in a set of predictions, without considering their direction (positive or negative). It is calculated as the average of the absolute differences between the predicted values and the actual values.

MAE is easy to interpret, as it gives the average absolute error in the same units as the target variable. A lower MAE indicates better predictive accuracy.

To get a good idea on what MAE is, please look into this : https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error

## Submission File
For each INDEX in the test set, you must predict the TAVG of the target city. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    INDEX,TAVG
    0,51.8
    1,58.2
    2,56.2
    3,48.9
    4,42.1
    5,41.9
etc..","# Dataset Description


Understanding and predicting temperature variations across different geographic locations is critical for weather forecasting, environmental monitoring, and climate research. By leveraging data from multiple cities, this dataset allows for the development of predictive models that can estimate the maximum temperature of a target city based on its geographic proximity and features relative to other cities.

**Dataset Structure**:

**DATE**: The date of the recorded measurements which is given in the format DD-MM-YYYY

**LATITUDE_A, LATITUDE_B, LATITUDE_C**: Latitude coordinates of the locations A, B, and C, respectively. Indicates the north-south position on the Earth's surface.

**LONGITUDE_A, LONGITUDE_B, LONGITUDE_C**: Longitude coordinates of the locations A, B, and C, respectively. Indicates the east-west position on the Earth's surface.

**ELEVATION_A, ELEVATION_B, ELEVATION_C:** Elevation above sea level for the locations A, B, and C, respectively.
Weather Variables for Each Location (A, B, C)

**PRCP_A, PRCP_B, PRCP_C**: Precipitation (PRCP) recorded at locations A, B, and C, respectively. 

**SNWD_A, SNWD_B, SNWD_C**: Snow depth (SNWD) at locations A, B, and C, respectively. 

**TMAX_A, TMAX_B, TMAX_C**: Maximum temperature (TMAX) recorded at locations A, B, and C, respectively. 

**TMIN_A, TMIN_B, TMIN_C:** Minimum temperature (TMIN) recorded at locations A, B, and C, respectively. 

**TAVG_A, TAVG_B, TAVG_C**: Average temperature (TAVG) at locations A, B, and C, respectively.",False,True
