8396,final-hackathon,"# Overview

This competition is the Final Hackathon as part of Machine Learning.AI 2024, a primer course on Data Science and Machine Learning, conducted by IITG.ai club.
----------------------
# Description

IITG.ai is a platform for the AI community of IITG to discuss, learn and collaborate on advancements in the field of AI. We have created a culture of contributing to and improving AI's dynamic, fast-changing world.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

Before starting make sure you go through the [Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/final-hackathon/rules) to understand the format of the leaderboard and know about the rules regarding submissions.

Also, we have provided a [starter notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/himanshusinghal19/ml-ai-week-7-starter-notebook) to get an idea of how to approach this competition.

To understand how your submissions are scored on the leaderboard, go through the evaluation tab, we have provided details on the metrics used to score your submission.

The score given to you on the course leaderboard for this hackathon will be dependent on your score on the private leaderboard, we will also require notebook submissions from all participants. The course leaderboard scores will depend significantly on the quality of your work you show, not just your private LB score.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean Squared Error (MSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error). MSE is a commonly used measure of the differences between predicted and observed values. It is calculated as the average of the squared differences between the prediction and the actual observation. The formula for MSE is given by:

$$\text{MSE} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2$$

where 
$$y_i \text{ = actual value}, \newline
\hat{y}_i \text{ = predicted value, and} \newline
n \text{ = the number of observations.}$$

MSE gives a relatively high weight to large errors, making it particularly useful when large errors are especially undesirable.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a particular value. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Index,value
    2,5.63
    5,3.14
    6,7.89
    etc.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains FPS (Frames Per Second) measurements of video games run on various computers. Each row represents the FPS outcome for a specific game executed on a particular computer configuration. The computer's configuration is described by the technical specifications of its CPU and GPU. These specifications include attributes prefixed with ""Cpu"" and ""Gpu,"" respectively, and reflect the factory settings of these components.

The dataset also characterizes each game by its name, the resolution at which it was displayed, and the quality settings used during the FPS measurement. These attributes are prefixed with ""Game.""

In summary, this dataset provides a comprehensive overview of how different computer configurations (CPU and GPU specifications) and game settings (name, resolution, and quality) impact the FPS performance of video games.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

* `CpuNumberOfCores` - number of physical cores
* `CpuNumberOfThreads` - number of threads
* `CpuBaseClock` - base clock in MHz
* `CpuCacheL1` - total size of level 1 cache in kB
* `CpuCacheL2` - total size of level 2 cache in kB
* `CpuCacheL3` - total size of level 3 cache in MB
* `CpuDieSize` - physical size of the die in square meters
* `CpuFrequency` - frequency in MHz
* `CpuMultiplier` - multiplier of CPU
* `CpuMultiplierUnlocked` - 0=multiplier locked, 1=multiplier unlocked
* `CpuProcessSize` - used process size in nanometers
* `CpuSMPNumberOfCpus` - number of symmetric multiprocessors
* `CpuTDP` - thermal design power in watts
* `CpuTransistors` - count of transistors in millions
* `CpuTurboClock` - turbo clock in MHz
* `GpuBandwidth` - bandwidth in MB/s
* `GpuBaseClock` - base clock in MHz
* `GpuBoostClock` - boost clock in MHz
* `GpuComputeUnits` - number of computing units
* `GpuDieSize` - physical size of die in square meters
* `GpuNumberOfExecutionUnits` - number of execution units
* `GpuFP32Performance` - theoretical Float 32 performance in MFLOP/s
* `GpuMemoryBus` - width of memory bus in bits
* `GpuMemorySize` - size of memory in MB
* `GpuPixelRate` - theoretical pixel rate in MPixel/s
* `GpuProcessSize` - used process size in nanometers
* `GpuNumberOfROPs` - number of render output units
* `GpuNumberOfShadingUnits` - number of shading units
* `GpuNumberOfTMUs` - number of texture mapping units
* `GpuTextureRate` - theoretical texture rate in KTexel/s
* `GpuTransistors` - number of transistors in millions
* `GpuArchitecture` - architecture code
* `GpuMemoryType` - memory type
* `GpuBusInterface` - bus interface
* `GpuOpenCL` - version of OpenCL
* `GpuShaderModel` - version of shader model
* `GpuVulkan` - version of Vulkan
* `GpuOpenGL` - version of OpenGL
* `GameName` - name
* `GameResolution` - resolution
* `GameSetting` - quality setting
* `Dataset` - indicator for the source of measurement
* `FPS` - FPS value",False,True
8397,11785-hw-2-p-2-face-verification-fall-2024,"# Overview

This is the Face Verification Kaggle competition for 11-785 HW2P2, Spring 2024
----------------------
# Description

In this homework, you will work on pattern recognition problems that require position invariance. Specifically, you will work on the problem of verifying faces in images. In typical pictures of faces, the face is rarely perfectly centered. Different pictures of the same person may have the face shifted by varying amounts. The classifier must recognize the face regardless of this indeterminacy of position. This calls for position-invariant models, specifically Convolutional Neural Networks, or CNNs.

Face verification refers to the task of determining whether two face images are of the same person, without necessarily knowing who the person is. Face verification is an instance of a larger class of problems where we attempt to determine if two data instances belong to the same class without necessarily knowing (or having a model for) the class itself.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric: EER (Equal Error Rate)","# Dataset Description

The verification dataset consists of images and a verification pair list (for validation and testing) in a txt file.",False,True
8398,kaggle-sprint-1,"# Overview

The Coding Club is excited to announce the launch of its inaugural Kaggle Competition, ""KAGGLE SPRINT: Leading the way in data-driven Innovation."" This competition is designed to bring together students who are passionate about artificial intelligence and machine learning to tackle real-world problems through the power of data.

Participants will be challenged to apply their knowledge and skills in data preprocessing, feature engineering, model selection, and evaluation to deliver the best predictive models. The competition will feature a carefully curated dataset that reflects current industry challenges, providing a practical platform for students to showcase their abilities.

Throughout the competition, participants will have the opportunity to:

Engage with Real-World Data: Work with a comprehensive dataset designed to simulate actual industry scenarios.
Collaborate and Compete: Team up with fellow students, share insights, and compete against peers in the machine learning community.

Whether you are a beginner or have some experience in AI/ML, this competition offers a unique opportunity to test your skills, learn from others, and contribute to solving meaningful problems through Machine Learning.

Join us in this exciting competition designed for students to unlock the potential of AI/ML and drive innovative solutions. Compete against the brightest student minds, tackle complex challenges, and push the boundaries of your analytical skills.

----------------------
# Evaluation

**1. Objective:**
Participants are required to build a regression model to predict the target variable from the given dataset.

**2. Evaluation Metric:**
The performance of the models will be evaluated using the Mean Squared Error (MSE).
The Mean Squared Error (MSE) is calculated as the average of the squared differences between the predicted values and the actual values. Lower MSE values indicate better model performance.


## Submission File
index: The identifier for each instance as provided in the test dataset.
Strength: The predicted value for the target variable.

    index, Strength
    1, 23.45
    2, 15.67
    3, 78.90
    etc.

The submission file should be in same format as sample_submission.csv.","# Dataset Description

The strength of concrete is influenced by a variety of factors, including the materials used in its mix and the conditions under which it cures. Concrete is a composite material, and its final strength is a result of the interaction between its different components and the curing process. The primary goal of this dataset is to predict the compressive strength of concrete based on these influencing factors.

Description of Fields are as follows:-

index:- Indexing of each datapoint
CementComponent:- Amount of cement is mixed
BlastFurnaceSlag:- Amount of Blast Furnace Slag is mixed
FlyAshComponent:- Amount of FlyAsh is mixed
WaterComponent:- Amount of water is mixed
SuperplasticizerComponent:- Amount of Super plasticizer is mixed
CoarseAggregateComponent:- Amount of Coarse Aggregate is mixed
FineAggregateComponent:- Amount of Coarse Aggregate is mixed
AgeInDays:- How many days it was left dry
Strength:- What was the final strength of concrete- (Target)",False,True
8399,data-poisoning-detection-challenge-01,"# Overview

**SecureAI Inc**., a pioneering leader in artificial intelligence solutions, faces a critical situation. One of their most valuable clients, FutureTech Industries, has reported a severe anomaly in their AI-driven decision-making system. They suspect that their model has been maliciously tampered with through a sophisticated data poisoning attack, potentially compromising its reliability and accuracy.

As a top data engineer at SecureAI, **you have been assigned the critical mission to uncover and identify the poisoned samples in the provided dataset**. Your expertise is crucial in ensuring the integrity of the AI model and restoring the trust of **FutureTech Industries**.
----------------------
# Objective

Develop a robust AI solution to identify poisoned samples within the training dataset. You will be provided with:

1.  A training CSV file containing a mix of clean and potentially poisoned samples.
1.  A test CSV file consisting solely of clean samples, meticulously labeled by industry experts.

**Note:** You are not allowed to train your model based on the labeled samples (test.csv) to find the poisoned samples. You must use the attack methods mentioned in the rules section to mimic data poisoning based on the test samples. Then, develop your own approach to identify the poisoned samples.
----------------------
# Evaluation


-    Your submissions will be evaluated based on the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC) metric.

----------------------
# Challenge Instructions

1. Download the Datasets:

    Training Dataset: Includes a mix of clean and poisoned samples.
    Test Dataset: Contains only clean samples, validated by expert analysts.

2. Develop the AI Solution:

    Your mission is to accurately identify the poisoned samples in the training dataset.
    Leverage cutting-edge machine learning or deep learning techniques.
    Pay attention to feature engineering, model training, and validation processes.

3. Submission Format:

   -     Submit a CSV file with two columns: index and poisoned( poisoned_label), where poisoned should be a numeric value (0 for clean, 1 for poisoned).
-  The file should contain a header and have the following format:

   index,poisoned
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of various features extracted from network traffic and syslog samples. These features are anonymized for security reasons and are represented as F1 to F78. Below is a group-wise description of these features:

1. Network Features:
    F1 to F21: These features include various network-related data such as source and destination IPs, protocols, ports, and service types.
1. Packet and Byte Counts:
    F22 to F28: These features capture information related to packet counts, byte counts, and connection states.
1. Flag Indicators:
    F29 to F34: These features indicate the presence of specific flags in the network packets, such as SYN, ACK, FIN, and RST.
1. Traffic Ratios:
    F35 to F38: These features represent ratios related to packet and byte counts, helping to understand the traffic distribution.
1. Timing and Rate Metrics:
    F39 to F54: These features include various timing metrics and rates, such as average and standard deviation of user time, system time, and input/output wait time.
1. System Activity:
    F55 to F70: These features capture system activity metrics like average and standard deviation of load averages, memory usage, process counts, and context switches.
1. Security Alerts:
    F71 to F78: These features include security-related alerts and activities such as login attempts, file and process activities, and privilege escalation indicators.
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8400,intelligent-systems-ecu-csg-2341-sem-2-2024-02,"# Overview

PerthSecure Bank, a prominent financial institution based in Perth, Western Australia, has encountered a severe security breach. Despite their advanced cybersecurity measures, the bank has been targeted by a sophisticated malware/botnet attack. This breach has led to significant disruptions in their network, threatening the security of sensitive financial data and causing widespread concern among their customers.

In an effort to combat this threat, PerthSecure Bank has partnered with SecureNet Solutions, a leading cybersecurity firm, to collaboratively address the issue. Due to stringent security protocols and the highly sensitive nature of the data, the original PCAP files and detailed log files cannot be shared directly between the two organizations. Instead, PerthSecure Bank has extracted critical features from the network traffic and syslog samples, which will be analyzed by SecureNet Solutions to develop an effective Intrusion Detection System (IDS).

----------------------
# Objective

As a backend engineer at SecureNet Solutions, your task is to develop a robust backend engine for the IDS to identify and classify attack samples within the provided datasets.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be evaluated based on the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC) metric.

----------------------
# Challenge Instructions


- Download the Datasets:
1.         Training Dataset: Contains both normal and attack samples.
1.         Test Dataset: Contains samples that need to be classified.
-  Develop the AI Solution:
1.         Your task is to accurately classify the samples in the test dataset.
1.         Employ advanced machine learning or deep learning techniques.
1.         Focus on feature engineering, model training, and validation processes.
-  Submission Format:
1.         Submit a CSV file named submission.csv with the following structure:
index,Label
0,0
1,1
2,0
...
The index column should correspond to the row number in the test dataset.
The Label column should indicate whether the sample is normal (0) or attack (1).","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of various features extracted from network traffic and syslog samples. These features are anonymized for security reasons and are represented as F1 to F78. Below is a group-wise description of these features:

1. Network Features:
    F1 to F21: These features include various network-related data such as source and destination IPs, protocols, ports, and service types.
1. Packet and Byte Counts:
    F22 to F28: These features capture information related to packet counts, byte counts, and connection states.
1. Flag Indicators:
    F29 to F34: These features indicate the presence of specific flags in the network packets, such as SYN, ACK, FIN, and RST.
1. Traffic Ratios:
    F35 to F38: These features represent ratios related to packet and byte counts, helping to understand the traffic distribution.
1. Timing and Rate Metrics:
    F39 to F54: These features include various timing metrics and rates, such as average and standard deviation of user time, system time, and input/output wait time.
1. System Activity:
    F55 to F70: These features capture system activity metrics like average and standard deviation of load averages, memory usage, process counts, and context switches.
1. Security Alerts:
    F71 to F78: These features include security-related alerts and activities such as login attempts, file and process activities, and privilege escalation indicators.
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
