8497,precision-health-predicting-human-age-with-biomark,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** 

The goal of this competition is to develop a predictive model that accurately estimates the age of individuals based on a range of health, lifestyle, and demographic features. Participants are required to use the dataset, which includes various attributes such as height, weight, cholesterol levels, and other personal factors, to train their models. The objective is to achieve the best possible accuracy in predicting the age of individuals, as measured by the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between the predicted and actual ages. 

This challenge aims to showcase the ability to handle complex datasets and apply advanced machine learning techniques to generate precise predictions in a real-world scenario. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation Metrics Description

The competition will use the **Mean Squared Error (MSE)** as the evaluation metric. MSE measures the average squared difference between the actual values and the predicted values, penalizing larger errors more severely. A lower MSE indicates better model performance.

#### MSE Formula:
\[
\text{MSE} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( y_i - \hat{y}_i \right)^2
\]
- \(n\) = Number of observations
- \(y_i\) = Actual value
- \(\hat{y}_i\) = Predicted value

This metric will be used to rank submissions, with lower scores reflecting more accurate predictions.

## Submission File
```
ID,Age(years)
174,25
166,30
99,22
etc.
```
----------------------
# Timeline

**Start Date** - September 8, 2024                                                                
**Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline                                       
**Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline                                       
**Final Submission Deadline** - October 31, 2024                                   
All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The                     competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary","# Dataset Description

The dataset provided for this competition includes information to predict individuals' ages. It is divided into three main files: training data, testing data, and a sample submission file.

**Training Dataset:**
- **train.csv**: This file contains historical data with various features for each individual, including physical measurements, lifestyle details, and health information. The dataset includes both the features and the target variable, **Age (years)**, which participants need to predict. This data is used to build and train predictive models.

**Testing Dataset:**
- **test.csv**: This file includes similar features as the training dataset but does not include the target variable (**Age (years)**). Participants will use this data to make predictions. The accuracy of these predictions will be evaluated based on the actual age values provided in the hidden test set.

**Sample Submission File:**
- **sample_submission.csv**: This file provides the format in which participants should submit their predictions. It contains two columns: **ID** and **Age (years)**. Participants need to populate the **Age (years)** column with their predictions for the individuals in the test dataset and submit this file for evaluation

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8498,mushroomsdepi,"# Overview

Data set description: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/848/secondary+mushroom+dataset

----------------------
# Description

Binary Prediction of Poisonous Mushrooms
upload your notebook and submission
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,class
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/848/secondary+mushroom+dataset",False,True
