8516,aoml-hackathon-1,"# Overview

This competition is part of the Algorithms  and Optimizations in Machine Learning Course 2024, PES University. The competition is strictly a team event. This competition will account for 5 marks in the final grade. The rubrics have been shared with all the students for their reference.

Please make sure to go through everything given to you in the Overview, Data and Rules Tabs.
Note the Deliverables required for grading. If your submissions & notebooks do not follow the specifications, marks will be deducted.

Feel free to contact the hosts for any queries, the details for the same can be found in the Rules section.
----------------------
# Description

**Welcome to the Pok¨¦mon Classification Competition for the AOML Course 2024 at PES University!**

This team-based competition, hosted on Kaggle, challenges you to build a model that accurately classifies Pok¨¦mon based on their features. Your task is to develop a model that can correctly predict the name of a Pok¨¦mon using various attributes from the dataset, such as type, stats, and other characteristics. The goal is simple: the team with the highest classification accuracy wins.

**Key details:**

- **Team-based**: This is strictly a group effort, encouraging collaboration and effective teamwork.
- **Classification task**: Your model must identify the correct Pok¨¦mon based on the input features. The dataset consists of images of 143 unique pokemons
- **Evaluation**: The performance will be judged on accuracy, with the highest accuracy on the test data determining the leaderboard rankings.
- **Grading**: This competition accounts for 5 marks in your final grade for the course.

The rubrics have been shared for your reference, and you'll be evaluated not only on your model's accuracy but also on:

1. **Model performance** ¨C accuracy.
2. **Code quality and reproducibility** ¨C ensuring others can follow your work.
3. **Documentation and presentation** ¨C a clear explanation of your approach.
4. **Creativity and innovation** ¨C in feature engineering, model selection, or tuning.

As the competition progresses, you¡¯ll be able to see how your solution stacks up against others on the Kaggle leaderboard. Make sure to keep iterating, refining, and experimenting with your models to maximize performance.

Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation and Submission Guidelines**
Submissions for this task will be assessed primarily based on their classification accuracy. The key objective is to accurately predict the pokemon for each image in the test set. The metric used for evaluation is the sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score, which measures the proportion of correct predictions to the total number of samples in the test set.


**Leaderboards**
There are two leaderboards - a public and a private one. Scores for both are evaluated on different subsets of the solution dataset. The public leaderboard will be dynamic throughout the contest and is subject to change based on submissions. The private leaderboard is not open to all participants during the competition, but will display the final scores once the competition is complete.

The purpose of the private leaderboard is to act as a proxy to real, unseen, test data. As teams are not exposed to this subset of the data, it encourages them to ensure that their model is generalizing well. If the model is found to overfit on the training sample, or conversely, if the generalizes very well, it will be reflected in the final ranks on the leaderboard.
Therefore, prepare to see some jumps up and down in your Private Leaderboard ranking - which is truly indicative of the model you've built.","# Dataset Description

Here is the dataset structure:

- **train**: This folder contains subfolders for each Pok¨¦mon, with images in JPG format for training your model.
- **validation**: This folder contains subfolders for each Pok¨¦mon, with images in JPG format for validating your model.
- **test**: This folder contains all the images (JPG format) for which you need to predict the corresponding class.
- **sample_submission.csv**: This file serves as a reference for your final submission. The columns should be labeled as follows:
  - **Image_Name**: The file name of each image in the test folder.
  - **Class**: The predicted class (Pok¨¦mon name) for each image.

Ensure that your final submission follows the same structure and column names as provided in the **sample_submission.csv file**. **This is purely for you to understand the structure of the dataset, but the final submission must have as many records in the test dataset.**",False,True
8517,machine-learning-regresion,"# Overview

Se tiene informaci¨®n sobre 1600 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa y joyer¨ªa que vende por cat¨¢logo.

De acuerdo con lo indicado por el cuerpo directivo, el objetivo es realizar un pron¨®stico de ventas anual para la ropa de mujer basado en la informaci¨®n de la sucursal. De gran importancia es saber si los recursos que se asignan tienen poder para modelar la demanda en las zonas respectivas.

Para ello desean que usted haga un pron¨®stico para las 400 zonas que se encuentran en el archivo de sometimiento (ver datos), cuya informaci¨®n corresponde al mismo a?o de las 1600 zonas originales. El pron¨®stico ser¨¢ evaluado con base en la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE) que se obtenga en estas 400 sucursales.

?Qu¨¦ deben presentar?
Talleres individuales acorde a CRISP-DM con longitud m¨¢xima de 10 p¨¢ginas: 50%.
Predicci¨®n: 50%.
Se dar¨¢ bonificaci¨®n adicional por la realizaci¨®n y respuesta de preguntas en los foros, entrega de notebooks o c¨®digo p¨²blico en la plataforma, valoraciones de los aportes de otros, y en general, por la colaboraci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La mejor predicci¨®n obtendr¨¢ un 50 en el 50% del taller, y todos aquellos que igualen un benchmark ingenuo obtendr¨¢n 20. Valores intermedios se ubicar¨¢n de acuerdo a una escala. Si no se supera el benchmark ingenuo la nota ser¨¢ inferior a 20. La predicci¨®n ser¨¢ valorada basada en el ingenuo ya publicado y usando la m¨¦trica RMSE. La colaboraci¨®n en los kernels y foros en el espacio del concurso puede darle puntos adicionales, valorados por los profesores. Colaborar no necesariamente es responder, tambi¨¦n preguntar y comentar. S¨®lo se permitir¨¢n notebooks de exploraci¨®n/visualizaci¨®n. El taller corresponde al otro 50% de la nota.","# Dataset Description

El archivo de datos que se entrega est¨¢ en formato Excel (xlsx) y tiene dos hojas: Train, para desarrollar el modelo (1600 registros) y Test (400 registros) con todas las variables independientes necesarias para predecir la variable de respuesta (ropamujer). Las predicciones deben ser sometidas a kaggle en formato .csv con dos columnas con encabezados idloc y ropamujer (esta ¨²ltima contiene su predicci¨®n). La separaci¨®n de las columnas es con coma y el decimal debe marcarse con punto(.).

Campos de datos
ropamujer: ventas de prendas para mujer en millones de pesos durante el a?o.
correo: n¨²mero de cat¨¢logos enviados durante el a?o.
p¨¢ginas: n¨²mero de p¨¢ginas del cat¨¢logo.
tel¨¦fono: n¨²mero promedio de l¨ªneas para llamada abiertas para pedidos.
impresa: cantidad gastada en publicidad impresa.
servicio: n¨²mero de representantes del servicio al cliente.
idmercado: tipo de mercado. Solo se listan n¨²meros de clases o tipos de mercado que se desea atacar. No se proveer¨¢ informaci¨®n adicional.
tama?omer: tama?o potencial del mercado, proyectado de acuerdo a cifras del ¨¢rea de marketing.
Idloc: ID de la tienda.
Edadloc: a?os transcurridos desde la llegada a la zona.
promo (promoci¨®n): tipo de promoci¨®n que se llev¨® a cabo durante el a?o. De nuevo, informaci¨®n descriptiva sobre estas promociones es reservada.
nomina: valor total de la n¨®mina durante el a?o.",False,True
8518,stat-5385-6385-project-1,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to estimate the growth of bacteria after some time has elapsed. The metric to evaluate the prediction is the square error.
----------------------
# Description

You are a **Data Scientist** in a biology lab. The lab conducts experiments on the *Paramecium* bacteria. They put a drop of fluid containing the bacteria in a *Petri* dish and let the bacteria grow. After a certain time, they conduct several test on the bacteria. They have conducted this type of experiment several times in the past and they have recorded the time (in days) that they have let the bacteria grow and what is the area that the bacteria covers in the *Petri* dish at the end of the growth period. So they have recorded several measurements of **Area** and **Days**. They will conduct new experiments and want you to predict the **Area** that the bacteria will cover after a certain amount of time. So they will provide you with the **Days** and want the prediction of the **Area**, based on previous measurements.

They know the model can't be too complicated and have told you, that no other variables are used. Furthermore, you can only use the following transformations (for the **Area** and/or for the **Days**):

* Square root: 
$$\sqrt{x}$$
* Square
$$x^2$$
* Natural Logarithm
$$\log{x}$$

Any combination of this function on the independent variable or dependent variable or both is allowed.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your predictions will be scored using the [Mean Square Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error).

Your grade will be computed using the following formula:

$$\max\left(0, \left(2 - \frac{\text{Your Score}}{\text{Minimum Score}}\right) * 100\right)$$

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the **Area**. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    colID,AREA
    1,11
    2,23
    3,5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

You will be provided with individual data sets containing two variables:

* **Area**
* **Days**

The first column of the ""Comma Separated Values"" file is the **Area** and the second column is the **Days**.

This file is personalized for each student, and has the following naming structure:
""data_project_1_studentame.csv"". Your results should be based only in your data file. You will receive this file by email.

Here on Kaggle, you will find the test file, containing only the **Days**, variable that you will use to predict the **Area**. The file name is: ""test_data.csv"".",False,True
8519,modelify-innovision-2k24,"# Overview

# Innovision Modelify 2024

## Welcome to Innovision Modelify 2024!

This beginner-friendly machine learning competition is hosted as part of the CSE Departmental Fest Innovision at RCCIIT. Whether you're new to machine learning or looking to test your skills, this competition offers a great opportunity to dive into the world of data science with a real-world problem.
----------------------
# Description

### Problem Statement

Your client is a retail banking institution, and term deposits are one of the primary sources of income for banks. These are cash investments held at financial institutions for a fixed term and at an agreed-upon rate of interest.

While the bank utilizes various marketing strategies such as email, advertisements, digital marketing, and telephonic marketing to sell term deposits, telephonic campaigns have proven to be highly effective. However, such campaigns are expensive, as large call centers are needed to conduct them. Therefore, it is crucial for the bank to identify potential customers who are most likely to subscribe to term deposits and target them specifically.

Your task is to predict whether a client will subscribe to a term deposit based on their demographic and contact information, including call details.

### Dataset

You are provided with two datasets:

1. **train.csv:** This dataset contains the client and call details, along with the target variable `subscribed`. You will use this dataset to train your model.
2. **test.csv:** This dataset contains a new set of client and call details. You will use your trained model to predict whether these clients will subscribe to a term deposit.

### Data Dictionary

| Variable       | Definition                                      |
|----------------|-------------------------------------------------|
| ID             | Unique client ID                                |
| age            | Age of the client                               |
| job            | Type of job                                     |
| marital        | Marital status of the client                    |
| education      | Education level                                 |
| default        | Credit in default                               |
| housing        | Housing loan                                    |
| loan           | Personal loan                                   |
| contact        | Type of communication                           |
| month          | Contact month                                   |
| day_of_week    | Day of the week of contact                      |
| duration       | Duration of the contact                         |
| campaign       | Number of contacts performed during this campaign to the client |
| pdays          | Number of days since the client was last contacted |
| previous       | Number of contacts performed before this campaign |
| poutcome       | Outcome of the previous marketing campaign      |
| subscribed     | Has the client subscribed to a term deposit? (target variable) |
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **accuracy**. Your goal is to develop a model that accurately predicts whether a client will subscribe to a term deposit.
----------------------
# How to Submit?

1. Train your model using the provided `train.csv` dataset.
2. Make predictions on the `test.csv` dataset.
3. Save your predictions in a new CSV file.
----------------------
# Join Us!

Innovision Modelify 2024 is the perfect way for beginners to learn, experiment, and test their skills in machine learning. Join us and be a part of shaping the future of AI at RCCIIT's Innovision Fest!","# Dataset Description

This page provides an overview of the data files available in this competition. Each file's content and format are described below. Make sure to read this section thoroughly to avoid confusion during the competition.

### Files

- **train.csv** - This is the training dataset used to build your model. It contains client demographic information, call details, and whether the client subscribed to a term deposit (target variable).
  
- **test.csv** - This is the test dataset. It contains a new set of client and call details, but it does not include the target variable. You will make predictions on this dataset.

- **sample_submission.csv** - This is a sample submission file in the correct format. You can use this file as a reference when creating your submission. It contains the `ID` column and a `subscribed` column for your predictions.

### Columns

**train.csv** and **test.csv** both share the following columns:

| Column         | Description                                                   |
|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| `ID`           | Unique client identifier                                      |
| `age`          | Age of the client                                             |
| `job`          | Type of job the client has                                    |
| `marital`      | Marital status of the client                                  |
| `education`    | Education level of the client                                 |
| `default`      | Whether the client has credit in default (binary: yes/no)     |
| `housing`      | Whether the client has a housing loan (binary: yes/no)        |
| `loan`         | Whether the client has a personal loan (binary: yes/no)       |
| `contact`      | Type of communication with the client (e.g., cellular, telephone) |
| `month`        | Month of contact                                              |
| `day_of_week`  | Day of the week when the client was contacted                 |
| `duration`     | Duration of the contact (in seconds)                          |
| `campaign`     | Number of contacts performed during this campaign             |
| `pdays`        | Number of days since the client was last contacted            |
| `previous`     | Number of contacts performed before this campaign             |
| `poutcome`     | Outcome of the previous marketing campaign                    |

The **train.csv** file also includes the target variable:

| Column        | Description                                                  |
|---------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
| `subscribed`  | Whether the client subscribed to a term deposit (binary: yes/no) |

The **test.csv** file does **not** include the `subscribed` column, as this is what you need to predict.

### Prediction

Your task is to predict whether each client in the **test.csv** dataset will subscribe to a term deposit. This prediction will be submitted in a CSV file with the following format:

| ID  | subscribed |
|-----|------------|
| 1   | yes/no     |
| 2   | yes/no     |
| ... | ...        |

### Submission Format

Ensure your submission file matches the format of **sample_submission.csv**, containing two columns: `ID` and `subscribed`.",False,True
