8546,mock-rmit-gen-ai-and-cyber-security-hackathon,"# Overview

Join us for an exciting three-day event where students will collaborate to solve real-world challenges in AI and cybersecurity!

## NOTE: Please check the real event at https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/rmit-gen-ai-and-cyber-security-hackathon/overview.

## Sponsorship

<img src=""https://external-content.duckduckgo.com/iu/?u=https%3A%2F%2Fd1.awsstatic.com%2Fpartner-network%2Fpartner_marketing_web_team%2F600x400-logos%2Frmit-university_600x400px.c880d3e7c86038b6250551be6cd8d120aa87bb5f.png&f=1&nofb=1&ipt=aa1d7dace256bf02ecb835373fab9edd928c536e9ce6337ee1058b837b309cc7&ipo=images"" alt=""RMIT Race Hub Logo"" width=""500"" height=""300"">
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to our hackathon, where participants will engage in three interconnected challenges, each designed to build upon the skills and knowledge developed in previous tasks. Together, these challenges aim to foster innovation in AI deployment, security, and adversarial testing.


**Challenge 1:**  Participants will deploying an OpenAI-like API on AWS EC2, simulating a real-world environment where AI services are accessible to users. This challenge emphasizes setting up infrastructure, managing cloud resources, and ensuring the AI system is operational. Successful deployment will pave the way for securely integrating AI with other services. https://www.kaggle.com/code/aisuko/mock-foundational-level-challenge-1

**Challenge 2:** Implementing Encryption and Decryption in Python Once the AI system is deployed, security becomes a top priority. In this challenge, participants will implement Python-based encryption and decryption to protect communications between the system and users, addressing the threat of man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks. This challenge highlights the importance of securing data in transit, ensuring that messages are protected from interception and tampering. https://www.kaggle.com/code/aisuko/mock-foundational-level-challenge-2

**Challenge 3:** Fine-tuning an AI Model for Binary Classification of Web Traffic With a secure foundation established, the next step is to fine-tune an AI model for binary classification of web traffic, detecting anomalies that could indicate security risks. This challenge focuses on training a model capable of identifying normal versus malicious traffic, directly tying into the need for real-time monitoring and defense mechanisms in AI-driven applications. https://www.kaggle.com/code/aisuko/mock-advanced-level-challenge-1


## Further resources

To further support participants in becoming familiar with AI, we are providing two key resources. 

The first project, [Notebooks](https://github.com/Aisuko/notebooks), is designed for the implementation of various machine learning tasks on the Kaggle platform using GPUs, offering participants practical insights and hands-on experience. 

The second project, [Generative AI](https://github.com/Aisuko/generative-ai), features notebooks focused on generative AI, utilizing tools like PyTorch, Huggingface/diffusers, and transforms, and implementing algorithms from academic research papers. 

These resources will help you dive deeper into AI and build the necessary skills to tackle the hackathon challenges.

## Competition Support: Engage, Learn, and Collaborate

If you have any questions or need clarification during the competition, feel free to open [new discussions](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/mock-rmit-gen-ai-and-cyber-security-hackathon/discussion?sort=hotness) on the platform. Our team and the community are here to support you throughout the event. Don't hesitate to reach out and collaborate!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Private","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Suggestions for GenAI and Cyber Security Hackathon

## 1. Synthetic Cybersecurity Threat Detection Dataset
**Description**: A synthetic dataset mimicking network traffic patterns, including normal traffic and various types of attacks (e.g., phishing, DDoS, malware). This dataset could be used to train models to detect and classify different types of threats.  
**Why**: It provides participants with a realistic cyber security challenge while avoiding privacy concerns associated with real data.  
**Example Challenge**: Build a model that can detect and categorize different types of cyberattacks in real-time network traffic.

---

## 2. Customer Interaction Data for SMEs
**Description**: A dataset containing anonymized customer interaction data (e.g., chat logs, emails) from small and medium enterprises (SMEs). This could include questions about products, service requests, or complaints.  
**Why**: It focuses on creating AI solutions for SMEs, enabling participants to build chatbot systems or automation tools while tackling security challenges like protecting customer data.  
**Example Challenge**: Build a privacy-preserving LLM that can automate customer service responses while ensuring no sensitive information is leaked.

---

## 3. IoT Device Data with Security Vulnerabilities
**Description**: A dataset generated from IoT sensors that simulate smart home or factory environments, with embedded vulnerabilities. This could include sensor logs showing when an attack or anomaly occurred.  
**Why**: This emphasizes the growing importance of securing IoT devices and presents an AI challenge in identifying and mitigating potential attacks on these devices.  
**Example Challenge**: Develop an anomaly detection model that identifies security breaches or device malfunctions in real-time.

---

## 4. Finance Dataset (Fraud Detection)
**Description**: A synthetic or anonymized financial transaction dataset containing normal and fraudulent transactions.  
**Why**: Fraud detection is a classic challenge in cybersecurity, and it directly relates to protecting SMEs from financial threats. Participants could build models to detect fraudulent transactions.  
**Example Challenge**: Create a real-time fraud detection model using financial transaction data while ensuring data privacy and minimizing false positives.

---

## 7. Supply Chain Data for SMEs
**Description**: A dataset containing anonymized supply chain data, including order histories, inventory levels, and supplier information. Participants could focus on predicting supply chain disruptions or optimizing deliveries.  
**Why**: It addresses AI-driven solutions for SMEs and offers a chance to consider security concerns related to sensitive business data.  
**Example Challenge**: Build a model to optimize supply chain operations while securing sensitive business information.

---

## 8. Text-Based Security Log Data
**Description**: A dataset containing anonymized system or application logs with injected security incidents like unauthorized access attempts or configuration changes.  
**Why**: It allows participants to work on cybersecurity problems like intrusion detection or anomaly detection while handling natural language-like log entries.  
**Example Challenge**: Create an NLP model to detect and classify different types of security incidents from system logs.",False,True
8547,code-retrieval-for-hugging-face-transformers,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to design a code retrieval system that can effectively find relevant files to help answer questions about [Hugging Face's Transformers](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers) library. Our dataset consists of natural language questions paired with relevant file names from the repository.

This will give you the chance to experiment with many of the moving pieces involved in a code retrieval pipeline: file chunking, dense vs sparse embeddings, embedding types, rerankers, etc. While we do provide a training set, we expect to see improvements over the provided baseline without any fine-tuning.
----------------------
# Description

## A typical code retrieval pipeline
Setting up a code retrieval pipeline requires multiple steps:

1. Download the document library.
2. Embed each document and place it in a vector store.
3. Define a retriever which, given a query, embeds it and then looks up relevant documents in the vector store.
4. (Optional) Define a reranker which takes top N retrieved documents and reorders them.

## Use [Sage](https://github.com/Storia-AI/sage) to bootstrap your code retrieval pipeline
While you are free to implement the entire pipeline from scratch, you can use [Sage](https://github.com/Storia-AI/sage) for an off-the-shelf solution.

### Installation
To install Sage, create a virtual environment and run `pip install`:
```bash
python -m venv my-venv
source activate my-venv/bin/activate
pip install git+https://github.com/Storia-AI/sage.git@main
```

### Prerequisites
Follow the [Prerequisites](https://github.com/Storia-AI/sage?tab=readme-ov-file#prerequisites) section to set up your environment (including, for instance, the desired API keys).

### Indexing huggingface/transformers
To clone + embed + store the repository in a vector store, run this command:
```bash
sage-index huggingface/transformers --commit-hash=7bb1c99800d235791dace10305731f377db8077b
```
Note that the dataset was created using the commit above. If you choose to clone the `huggingface/transformers` library manually, make sure to use the same commit.

We defaulted `sage-index` to run locally on your machine. For higher quality, you might want to use state-of-the-art third-party providers like OpenAI for embeddings, etc. You can overwrite these defaults from the command line. For instance:

```bash
sage-index huggingface/transformers \
--commit-hash=7bb1c99800d235791dace10305731f377db8077b
--mode=remote \
--embeddings-provider=openai \
--embeddings-model=text-embedding-3-small
```

Please refer directly to Sage's documentation for more details. You're also welcome to fork the repository to add your own extensions (and we accept pull requests if you're particularly proud of your contributions!).

### Optional: chat with the codebase as a sanity-check

If you want to gain an intuition of how well your retrieval algorithm is working, you can bring up a chat interface by running the following command. This will bring up a Gradio app:

```bash
sage-chat huggingface/transformers --commit-hash=7bb1c99800d235791dace10305731f377db8077b
```

### Making a submission file
You can easily produce a submission file in the right format with the [retrieve_kaggle.py](https://github.com/Storia-AI/sage/blob/main/benchmarks/retrieval/retrieve_kaggle.py) script.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Recall@3](https://ir-measur.es/en/latest/measures.html#r), since the dataset contains at most 3 relevant documents per query.

## Submission File
The submission file must contain two columns: `id` (taken from the column with the same name in the `test.csv` file) and `documents` (a JSON-encoded list of file paths). Documents will be treated as a set (i.e. their order in the list is irrelevant). See the `sample_submission.csv` file for an example.

If using Sage, you can simply run the [retrieve_kaggle.py](https://github.com/Storia-AI/sage/blob/main/benchmarks/retrieval/retrieve_kaggle.py) script to get a submission file in the right format.","# Dataset Description

This dataset consists of natural language questions about the [Hugging Face Transformers](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers) library, together with the file names of 1-3 relevant documents that can help answer the question. All documents associated with a query are equally relevant, and their order in the list is irrelevant.

The dataset was created by [Storia AI](https://storia.ai/) in collaboration with [Morph Labs](https://morph.so/).

## Files

*   **train.csv** - The training set. Consists of 700 pairs of natural language questions and relevant file names.
*   **test.csv** - The test set. Consists of 300 natural language questions.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format (with fake documents).

## Columns

*   `id` - A unique identifier for the data instance.
*   `question`?- A question in natural language.
*   `documents` - A list of relevant documents, encoded as a JSON string. Each document is represented by the file path in the [Hugging Face Transformers](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers) repository, including the `huggingface/transformers` prefix.",False,True
