8564,playground-series-s5e1,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2025 Kaggle Playground Series!** We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting and approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month.

**Your Goal:** The objective of this challenge is to forecast sticker sales in different countries. ""At Kaggle, we take stickers seriously!"":tm:

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F59561%2F48dd013ca109783d1abe5389c233f352%2Fkerneler.png?generation=1735682270467088&alt=media"">

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE)](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.mean_absolute_percentage_error.html).

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict the target `num_sold`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,num_sold
    230130,100
    230131,100
    230132,100
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - January 1, 2025
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  January 31, 2025

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# About the Tabular Playground Series

The goal of the Tabular Playground Series is to provide the Kaggle community with a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. The duration of each competition will generally only last a few weeks, and may have longer or shorter durations depending on the challenge. The challenges will generally use fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise
* 2nd Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise
* 3rd Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise

**Please note:** In order to encourage more participation from beginners, Kaggle merchandise will only be awarded once per person in this series. If a person has previously won, we'll skip to the next team.",,True,True
8565,praktikum-iii-feature-selection-dan-model-eval,"# Overview

Mahasiswa diharapkan dapat menerapkan metode pemilihan fitur Filter Method dan Wrapper Method pada task klasifikasi menggunakan dataset yang diberikan. Selain itu, mahasiswa juga diharapkan melakukan hyperparameter tuning dengan membandingkan experiment dengan random search dan grid search yang diimplementasikan ke dalam 3 model klasifikasi dari metode yang sudah dipelajari sebelumnya dan di-evaluasi dengan memilih skema validasi yang tepat","# Dataset Description

Dataset ini digunakan untuk mengeksplorasi faktor-faktor yang berhubungan dengan attrition (tingkat karyawan yang keluar) dalam sebuah perusahaan. Dataset ini terdiri dari berbagai variabel yang mencakup data demografi, informasi pekerjaan, dan evaluasi karyawan, serta satu variabel target berupa status attrition. Berikut adalah keterangan terkait variabel dalam dataset:

* Age: Usia karyawan dalam tahun.
* Attrition: Status apakah karyawan keluar dari perusahaan ('Yes') atau tetap bekerja ('No').
* BusinessTravel: Frekuensi perjalanan bisnis yang dilakukan oleh karyawan ('Travel_Rarely', 'Travel_Frequently', 'Non-Travel').
* DailyRate: Gaji harian yang diterima oleh karyawan.
* Department: Divisi tempat karyawan bekerja ('Sales', 'Research & Development', 'Human Resources').
* DistanceFromHome: Jarak antara rumah karyawan dan tempat kerja dalam satuan mil.
* Education: Tingkat pendidikan karyawan (1: 'Below College', 2: 'College', 3: 'Bachelor', 4: 'Master', 5: 'Doctor').
* EducationField: Bidang studi pendidikan karyawan ('Life Sciences', 'Medical', 'Marketing', 'Technical Degree', 'Other').
* EmployeeCount: Jumlah karyawan (konstanta 1 untuk setiap baris, digunakan sebagai placeholder).
* EmployeeNumber: Nomor identifikasi karyawan unik.
* EnvironmentSatisfaction: Tingkat kepuasan karyawan terhadap lingkungan kerja (1: 'Low', 2: 'Medium', 3: 'High', 4: 'Very High').
* Gender: Jenis kelamin karyawan ('Male', 'Female').
* HourlyRate: Gaji per jam yang diterima oleh karyawan.
* JobInvolvement: Tingkat keterlibatan karyawan dalam pekerjaan (1: 'Low', 2: 'Medium', 3: 'High', 4: 'Very High').
* JobLevel: Tingkatan pekerjaan karyawan dalam perusahaan.
* JobRole: Jabatan atau peran pekerjaan karyawan ('Sales Executive', 'Research Scientist', 'Laboratory Technician', dll.).
* JobSatisfaction: Tingkat kepuasan karyawan terhadap pekerjaan mereka (1: 'Low', 2: 'Medium', 3: 'High', 4: 'Very High').
* MaritalStatus: Status pernikahan karyawan ('Single', 'Married', 'Divorced').
* MonthlyIncome: Pendapatan bulanan yang diterima oleh karyawan.
* MonthlyRate: Gaji bulanan yang diterima oleh karyawan.¡ñ NumCompaniesWorked: Jumlah perusahaan tempat karyawan pernah bekerja sebelum bergabung dengan perusahaan saat ini.
* Over18: Status apakah karyawan berusia di atas 18 tahun (semua 'Yes').
* OverTime: Status apakah karyawan bekerja lembur ('Yes', 'No').
* PercentSalaryHike: Persentase kenaikan gaji yang diterima oleh karyawan dibandingkan dengan gaji tahun sebelumnya.
* PerformanceRating: Penilaian kinerja karyawan (1: 'Low', 2: 'Good', 3: 'Excellent', 4: 'Outstanding').
* RelationshipSatisfaction: Tingkat kepuasan karyawan terhadap hubungan di tempat kerja (1: 'Low', 2: 'Medium', 3: 'High', 4: 'Very High').
* StandardHours: Jumlah jam kerja standar (konstanta 80 untuk semua karyawan).
* StockOptionLevel: Tingkat opsi saham yang dimiliki oleh karyawan.
* TotalWorkingYears: Total jumlah tahun karyawan telah bekerja di berbagai perusahaan.
* TrainingTimesLastYear: Jumlah sesi pelatihan yang diikuti karyawan selama tahun terakhir.
* WorkLifeBalance: Tingkat keseimbangan antara pekerjaan dan kehidupan pribadi karyawan (1: 'Bad', 2: 'Good', 3: 'Better', 4: 'Best').
* YearsAtCompany: Jumlah tahun karyawan telah bekerja di perusahaan saat ini.
* YearsInCurrentRole: Jumlah tahun karyawan telah bekerja dalam peran/jabatan mereka saat ini.
* YearsSinceLastPromotion: Jumlah tahun sejak karyawan terakhir kali mendapatkan promosi.
* YearsWithCurrManager: Jumlah tahun karyawan telah bekerja dengan manajer saat ini.

## Files

*   **HRD Data.csv** - data untuk membangun model dan validasi
*   **predict.csv** - data untuk melakukan unseen test",False,True
8566,artist-identification,"# Overview

Welcome to the Artist Classification Challenge! In this competition, you'll dive into the world of early European art, using machine learning to identify the creators behind various paintings. But there's a twist ¨C we're not just looking for accuracy. We want you to derive insights on what features are useful for distinguishing artists works from each other, ultimately being able to use your model to determine more about an artist's style compared to that of other artists. 
----------------------
# Description

Your goal is to design a model capable of taking an image of any size and classify it by the artist who created it. The competition will be open to submissions until Tuesday, October 22nd at 5:00 AM. After the competition ends, the three highest ranked teams in accuracy will be invited to present their work. 

The goal of this competition is to evaluate the way the models go about classifying these paintings. Moreover, the cash prizes will be distributed to the team that gives the best presentation, demonstrating they know why the model gave the results that it did. However, accuracy will also be considered when determining the winning team. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be in the form of a .csv file. The first column should contain a list of all the image names in the test set. The remaining columns should contain your model's top five predicted classes with the most confident in the left and the least confident in the right. The columns should be named exactly what they are in the sample_submission.csv headers. 

## Example Submission File:
    Image Name,Class1,Class2,Class3,Class4,Class5
    ""ccc83541.jpg"",giovanni-boldini,leonardo-da-vinci,caravaggio,fyodor-vasilyev,george-morland
    ""5adf0be9.jpg"",andrea-del-sarto,giorgio-vasari,thomas-gainsborough,louise-elisabeth-vigee-le-brun,ivan-vladimirov
    ""28d1c9fd.jpg"",ivan-kramskoy,paolo-uccello,alfred-stevens,andrea-del-sarto,william-hogarth

Your submissions will be evaluated using a custom scoring scheme that balances accuracy with fair representation of all artists. For each painting in the test set, your model's prediction will receive a score based on its accuracy: 1 point for correct prediction (top 1), 1/2 point for top 2, 1/3 point for top 3, 1/4 point for top 4, 1/5 point for top 5, and 0 points if not in the top 5. An average score will be calculated for each artist across their paintings. The final score will be the average of all these artist-specific scores, ensuring that each artist contributes equally to the total score, regardless of how many of their works are in the test set. This method incentivizes models to perform well across all artists, not just the most common ones.

Approximately half of the training instances will be made private. The leaderboard will only evaluate on public data during the competition. The final rankings will be determined based on performance on the full dataset, including both public and private instances. This makes things a little more interesting as you won't be sure who truly is winning.
----------------------
# Dataset

The dataset used in this competition is derived from the work described in the following paper:

Tan, W. R., Chan, C. S., Aguirre, H., & Tanaka, K. (2019). Improved ArtGAN for Conditional Synthesis of Natural Image and Artwork. IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, 28(1), 394-409. https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2018.2866698

We do not claim this dataset as our own. Our usage is purely for research purposes only.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **artist_dataset.zip** - Contains the train and test directories
*   **train.csv** - Contains the paths to each training image as well as their classes
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8567,k-2-praktikum-iii,"# Overview

Mahasiswa diharapkan dapat menerapkan metode pemilihan fitur Filter Method dan Wrapper Method pada task klasifikasi menggunakan dataset yang diberikan. Selain itu, mahasiswa juga diharapkan melakukan hyperparameter tuning dengan membandingkan experiment dengan random search dan grid search yang diimplementasikan ke dalam 3 model klasifikasi dari metode yang sudah dipelajari sebelumnya dan di-evaluasi dengan memilih skema validasi yang tepat","# Dataset Description

Dataset ini digunakan untuk memprediksi apakah akan hujan pada hari berikutnya (RainTomorrow) berdasarkan pengamatan cuaca harian di berbagai lokasi di Australia. Dataset ini terdiri dari berbagai variabel cuaca yang diamati setiap hari, serta satu variabel target berupa prediksi hujan. Berikut adalah keterangan terkait variabel dalam dataset:

Date: Tanggal pengamatan cuaca.
Location: Nama lokasi pengamatan cuaca.
MinTemp: Suhu minimum yang tercatat pada hari tersebut (dalam derajat Celcius).
MaxTemp: Suhu maksimum yang tercatat pada hari tersebut (dalam derajat Celcius).
Rainfall: Jumlah curah hujan yang tercatat pada hari tersebut (dalam mm).
Evaporation: Jumlah air yang menguap dalam 24 jam hingga pukul 09:00 (dalam mm).
Sunshine: Jumlah jam matahari bersinar pada hari tersebut.
WindGustDir: Arah tiupan angin terkuat yang tercatat pada hari tersebut.
WindGustSpeed: Kecepatan tiupan angin terkuat yang tercatat pada hari tersebut (dalam km/jam).
WindDir9am: Arah angin pada pukul 9 pagi.
WindDir3pm: Arah angin pada pukul 3 sore.
WindSpeed9am: Kecepatan angin pada pukul 9 pagi (dalam km/jam).
WindSpeed3pm: Kecepatan angin pada pukul 3 sore (dalam km/jam).
Humidity9am: Kelembaban relatif pada pukul 9 pagi (dalam persen).
Humidity3pm: Kelembaban relatif pada pukul 3 sore (dalam persen).
Pressure9am: Tekanan udara pada pukul 9 pagi (dalam hPa).
Pressure3pm: Tekanan udara pada pukul 3 sore (dalam hPa).
Cloud9am: Jumlah tutupan awan pada pukul 9 pagi (dalam oktas).
Cloud3pm: Jumlah tutupan awan pada pukul 3 sore (dalam oktas).
Temp9am: Suhu udara pada pukul 9 pagi (dalam derajat Celcius).
Temp3pm: Suhu udara pada pukul 3 sore (dalam derajat Celcius).
RainToday: Apakah hujan turun pada hari tersebut (Ya/No, dengan curah hujan ¡Ý 1mm).
RainTomorrow (Target Label): Status apakah akan hujan pada hari berikutnya?(Ya/No).

## Files

*   **Data Weather.csv** - data untuk membangun model dan validasi
*   **predict_weather.csv** - data untuk melakukan testing model


Source & Acknowledgements
The observations were gathered from a multitude of weather stations. You can access daily observations from http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/data.
For example, you can check the latest weather observations in Canberra here: Canberra Weather.


Data source: Climate Data and Climate Data Online.
Copyright Commonwealth of Australia 2010, Bureau of Meteorology.",False,True
8568,predicting-conversion-mobile-app,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to train a model to predict user conversion from a trial to paid subscription. Participants can utilize any demographic, technical, and behavioral data available in the dataset to build their models.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The goal of this competition is to predict whether a user will convert from a trial period to a paid subscription. The evaluation metric used for ranking submissions is the F1 Score.

The F1 Score is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall, and it is particularly useful for imbalanced datasets where the number of positive and negative cases differs significantly. It balances the trade-off between Precision (how many of the predicted positive cases are correct) and Recall (how many actual positive cases were correctly identified).

Calculation of the F1 Score:


$$
F1 = 2 \times \frac{{\text{{Precision}} \times \text{{Recall}}}}{{\text{{Precision}} + \text{{Recall}}}}
$$


Where:


$$
Precision = \frac{{\text{{True Positives}}}}{{\text{{True Positives}} + \text{{False Positives}}}}
$$

$$
Recall = \frac{{\text{{True Positives}}}}{{\text{{True Positives}} + \text{{False Negatives}}}}
$$","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains information about user interactions with a mobile app for learning another language on iOS and Android platforms. The dataset includes both training and test data, organized into the following files:


## *__conversion.csv
* **user_id**: Unique identifier for the user.
* **target_feature**: Whether the user converted from the trial (this is the target variable of the competition).

## *__subscription.csv
* **user_id**: Unique identifier for the user.
* **started_at**: Date and time when the trial started.
* **store**: The store where the subscription was made (PLAY_STORE | APP_STORE).
* **store_country_code**: The country specified by the user in the store settings (missing for some users).
* **store_currency**: The currency used for the purchase (missing for some users).

## *__ users.csv
* **user_id**: Unique identifier for the user.
* **user_created_at**: Date and time when the user first joined the app.
* **users_native_language**: The language specified by the user as their native language.
* **locale**: The country code extracted from the user¡¯s device.
* **platform**: The platform through which the user first launched the app (where ¡®i¡¯ stands for iOS and ¡®a¡¯ for Android).
* **notification_enabled**: Whether the user enabled push notifications.
* **os_version**: The operating system version (different formats for iOS and Android).
* **device_model**: The device model used during the first app launch.
* **onboarding_finished**: Whether the user completed onboarding.
* **users_level**: The level of language proficiency that the user specified in the app.
* **logged_in**: Whether the user logged in using an email account.
* **preferred_topics_count**: The number of topics the user is interested in.
* **users_timezone_offset_millis**: The user¡¯s timezone offset from UTC in milliseconds.

## *__sessions.tsv
* **user_id**: Unique identifier for the user.
* **created_at**: Date and time when the session started.
* **updated_at**: Date and time of the last session update (can be considered as the session¡¯s end time).
* **session_length_seconds**: Length of the session in seconds.

## *__practice_type_.tsv

* **user_id**: Unique identifier for the user.
* **created_at**: Date and time when the practice session took place.
* **quality**: A score ranging from 0 to 100 evaluating the user's score in the practice session (quality is not evaluated and hence missing for practice types 5 and 6).

## Key Information

All behavioral features in this dataset are gathered for the period between when the user downloads the app and for a period of time following their trial start (started_at). The goal is to predict how likely a user is to convert from trial to paid based on the user's behaviour before and after starting a trial.",False,True
8569,principios-de-machine-learning-regresion-2403,"# Overview

Se tiene informaci¨®n sobre 1600 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa y joyer¨ªa que vende por cat¨¢logo.

De acuerdo con lo indicado por el cuerpo directivo, el objetivo es realizar un pron¨®stico de ventas anual para la ropa de mujer basado en la informaci¨®n de la sucursal. De gran importancia es saber si los recursos que se asignan tienen poder para modelar la demanda en las zonas respectivas.

Para ello desean que usted haga un pron¨®stico para las 400 zonas que se encuentran en el archivo de sometimiento (ver datos), cuya informaci¨®n corresponde al mismo a?o de las 1600 zonas originales. El pron¨®stico ser¨¢ evaluado con base en la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE) que se obtenga en estas 400 sucursales.

## ?Qu¨¦ deben presentar?

* Talleres individuales acorde a CRISP-DM con longitud m¨¢xima de 10 p¨¢ginas: 50%.
* Predicci¨®n: 50%.
Se dar¨¢ bonificaci¨®n adicional por la realizaci¨®n y respuesta de preguntas en los foros, entrega de notebooks o c¨®digo p¨²blico en la plataforma, valoraciones de los aportes de otros, y en general, por la colaboraci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La mejor predicci¨®n obtendr¨¢ un 50 en el 50% del taller, y todos aquellos que igualen un benchmark ingenuo obtendr¨¢n 20. Valores intermedios se ubicar¨¢n de acuerdo a una escala. Si no se supera el benchmark ingenuo la nota ser¨¢ inferior a 20. La predicci¨®n ser¨¢ valorada basada en el ingenuo ya publicado y usando la m¨¦trica RMSE. La colaboraci¨®n en los kernels y foros en el espacio del concurso puede darle puntos adicionales, valorados por los profesores. Colaborar no necesariamente es responder, tambi¨¦n preguntar y comentar. S¨®lo se permitir¨¢n notebooks de exploraci¨®n/visualizaci¨®n. El taller corresponde al otro 50% de la nota.","# Dataset Description

El archivo de datos que se entrega est¨¢ en formato Excel (xlsx) y tiene dos hojas: Train, para desarrollar el modelo (1600 registros) y Test (400 registros) con todas las variables independientes necesarias para predecir la variable de respuesta (ropamujer). Las predicciones deben ser sometidas a kaggle en formato .csv con dos columnas con encabezados idloc y ropamujer (esta ¨²ltima contiene su predicci¨®n). La separaci¨®n de las columnas es con coma y el decimal debe marcarse con punto(.).

Campos de datos
ropamujer: ventas de prendas para mujer en millones de pesos durante el a?o.
correo: n¨²mero de cat¨¢logos enviados durante el a?o.
p¨¢ginas: n¨²mero de p¨¢ginas del cat¨¢logo.
tel¨¦fono: n¨²mero promedio de l¨ªneas para llamada abiertas para pedidos.
impresa: cantidad gastada en publicidad impresa.
servicio: n¨²mero de representantes del servicio al cliente.
idmercado: tipo de mercado. Solo se listan n¨²meros de clases o tipos de mercado que se desea atacar. No se proveer¨¢ informaci¨®n adicional.
tama?omer: tama?o potencial del mercado, proyectado de acuerdo a cifras del ¨¢rea de marketing.
Idloc: ID de la tienda.
Edadloc: a?os transcurridos desde la llegada a la zona.
promo (promoci¨®n): tipo de promoci¨®n que se llev¨® a cabo durante el a?o. De nuevo, informaci¨®n descriptiva sobre estas promociones es reservada.
nomina: valor total de la n¨®mina durante el a?o.",False,True
8570,classification-bank-churn-ai,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** Happy New Year! This is the 1st episode of Season 4. We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting an approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month. 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to predict whether a customer continues with their account or closes it (e.g., churns). Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `Exited`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Exited
    165034,0.9
    165035,0.1
    165036,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Bank Customer Churn Prediction](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shubhammeshram579/bank-customer-churn-prediction) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `Exited` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of `Exited`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8571,regression-dataset,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.","# Dataset Description

**NOTE:** You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Wild blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shashwatwork/wild-blueberry-yield-prediction-dataset). (Since this is Playground 3.14, it seems like we need a Blueberry Pie joke here?) Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8572,yovvoyi-ko-k-maymunjon-hosildorligini-aniqlash,"# Overview


**Xush kelibsiz,ushbu musobaqa Mohirdev Data Science yo'nalishidagi o'quvchilar uchun vazifa**  

**Raqobat maqsadi:**  
