8597,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-c-64,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.
----------------------
# FAQs

### How do I contact support?

For this competition, any questions, concerns, or technical difficulties will be addressed at the Discussion forum on UpGrad Learn platform. Any discussion posted regarding support on this competition page will have to be redirected to the same portal.

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

The Kaggle leaderboard has a public and private component to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

**Public Leaderboard:** For all participants, the same 70% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the public leaderboard. The score you see on the public leaderboard reflects your model¡¯s accuracy on this portion of the test set.

**Private Leaderboard:** The other 30% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the private leaderboard. The private leaderboard is not visible to participants until the competition has concluded. At the end of a competition, we will reveal the private leaderboard so you can see your score on the other 30% of the test data. The scores on the private leaderboard are used to determine the competition winners.

### What are kernels?

Kaggle Kernels is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels support scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the Kernels tab to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
8598,dlsprint3,"# Overview

Artificial Intelligence is no longer a concept from science fiction. Modern AI has a huge potential to change the world. And it all starts with intelligence. What better way to make AI models intelligent than to make them good at problem solving? 
In this competition you will be creating one of the first AI models that can solve Bengali Math Problems. Your work will establish benchmarks in complex mathematical reasoning in the Bengali language.
----------------------
# Description

The ability to understand and solve math problems is a foundational skill for AI, paving the way for innovations across fields such as science, engineering, and financial modeling. Current AI models are still limited in their ability to tackle mathematical challenges in a low-resource language like Bengali. By addressing problems from the Bengali Math Olympiad, this competition challenges participants to overcome these limitations and expand AI¡¯s reach.

This competition is the first of its kind in the Bengali language. By participating, you'll contribute to advancing AI capabilities in mathematical reasoning, bringing us closer to developing models that can tackle high-level problems with precision and accuracy. Your models which can reason accurately in Bengali can help and guide students and researchers alike to learn and solve more complex problems. 

The event will take place in two phases. 
## Phase I
Teams will compete on Kaggle public leaderboard using the test set. At the end of phase I, teams will need to submit their inference notebooks.

## Phase II
The inference notebook will be rerun on a hidden test set. The hidden test set will contain **100** new unseen problems created in collaboration with BDMO. The final evaluation will be done based on both Phase I and Phase II score. 

Participants are highly encouraged to develop their pipeline in Bengali. Such approaches to the problem will be rewarded with novelty score.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on exact match between correct answers and predicted answers. No partial credit will be given. Each problem has an integer answer.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a answer to the math problem, which must be a non-negative integer. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Answer
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    ...
----------------------
# Timeline

**Phase 1 Date & Place:** October 7, 2024 to October 31, 2024. Hosted online on the Kaggle platform. 
**Phase 2 Date & Place:** November 6 ( tentative ) , 2024. Hosted in the Department of CSE, BUET premises.

**Competition and Registration Start:** October 4, 2024
**Registration Deadline:** October 24, 2024
**Team Formation/Merge Deadline:** October 24, 2024
**Phase 1 Submission Deadline:** October 31, 2024

**Registration Form Link:** [Click Here](https://tinyurl.com/dlsprint-reg-form)


----------------------
# Final Scoring

Teams will be evaluated in the following criteria.
| Criteria | Weight |
| --- | --- |
| Phase I: Leaderboard | 20% |
| Phase II: Hidden Set Score | 40% |
| Novelty| 30% |
| Presentation and paper | 10% |","# Dataset Description

# Public Train and Test Set 

The training set consists of 209 problems from Math Olympiad and their answers. All problems have non-negative integer answers. All problems are text-only with mathematical notation in LaTeX. Although some problems may involve geometry, diagrams are not used in any problem.

Because of the limited number of problems available, we are taking special precautions to secure the test set against probing, hardcoding, overfitting. Among other things, during the Phase 1 of the competition, the test set will comprise only the 100  problems. 

In Phase II, we will rerun submissions on a holdout set which is **completely different** from the training and test dataset. You should attempt to make sure your submission will complete successfully on the holdout private set problems. This may mean ensuring your submission is runnable in Kaggle environment and runs within permissible space and time limit. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
### Train Set

*   `Id` - Unique id for the problem
*   `Problem`?- Problem statement
*   `Answer` - Integer answer of the problem

### Test Set

*   `Id` - Unique id for the problem
*   `Problem`?- Problem statement

### Submission Format
*   `Id` - Unique id for the problem
*   `Answer`?- Predicted answer (**must be an Integer**)


# Holdout Set 
The holdout set will be used Phase II. The set consists of 100 new problems created in collaboration with BDMO. All the problems have non-negative integer answers as well. They will be text-only with mathematical notation in LaTeX and they will not contain diagrams. The difficulty of the problems are moderately higher than the problems in the public train and test set.

## Files
*   **test.csv** - the test set

## Columns
*   `Id` - Unique id for the problem
*   `Problem`?- Problem statement

### Submission Format
*   `Id` - Unique id for the problem
*   `Answer`?- Predicted answer (**must be an Integer**)",False,True
8599,iit-bombay-cs-725-assignment-2-autumn-2024,"# Overview

In this task, you are presented with a synthetic dataset derived from real-world image data, where your objective is to predict target class using feature vectors generated from neural encoder models. These embeddings have been compressed to reduce dimensionality of the problem.

The dataset is structured with unique IDs, features and corresponding labels. Your goal is to train a classification model on this dataset. This problem is designed to test your ability to work with classification models.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Accuracy](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall) between the predicted label and the target label. You must report the predicted label in integer format in the submission file. 

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a class label for the example features. The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID, label
    2,3
    5,1
    6,2
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set that has 1 ID column, 64 features columns, and 1 label column
*   **test.csv** - the test set that has 1 ID column and 64 features columns
*   **sample.csv** - a sample submission file that has 1 ID column and 1 label column

## Columns

*   `ID` - definition of example_id
*   `feature_i`?- definition of feature_i
*   `label` - class label of example_id",False,True
8600,blueberry-reggression,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.","# Dataset Description

**NOTE:** You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Wild blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shashwatwork/wild-blueberry-yield-prediction-dataset). (Since this is Playground 3.14, it seems like we need a Blueberry Pie joke here?) Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8601,unt-hydro-insight-2024,"# Overview

The goal of HydroInsight is to develop machine learning models that accurately predict the probability of flooding in different regions based on a variety of environmental, infrastructural, and socio-economic factors. Participants will work with a dataset that includes both relevant and irrelevant features, along with challenges like missing data and outliers. Your task is to build a robust regression model that can effectively handle these complexities and deliver precise flood probability predictions.
----------------------
# Description

To raise awareness of regression techniques and machine learning applications in disaster prediction, the **UNT Data Science Student Organization** has organized this challenge with the title ""*UNT HydroInsight 2024: Harnessing Data to Predict the Next Flood*.""

Floods are among the most frequent and devastating natural disasters, causing significant loss of life, property, and infrastructure. As climate change intensifies, the ability to predict floods with greater accuracy is crucial for enhancing disaster preparedness and minimizing damage. In this competition, participants are tasked with developing regression models to predict the probability of flooding in different regions, using a variety of environmental, infrastructural, and socio-economic factors.

By participating in HydroInsight, you will gain experience in handling complex data, working with both relevant and irrelevant features, and navigating challenges such as missing data and outliers. This competition will test your ability to build robust regression models capable of making precise flood probability predictions an essential skill for data scientists tackling real-world problems.

## Acknowledgements

We extend our sincere thanks to Professor **Dr. Ting Xiao** for providing invaluable guidance and support in creating this competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>In this competition, the performance of regression models will be evaluated based on their ability to accurately predict the probability of flooding in different regions. In regression analysis, different techniques can be used to evaluate the performance of the model in predicting a continuous target variable based on predictor variables. These include dividing the data into training and testing sets, calculating metrics such as mean squared error and R-squared, using cross-validation to assess the model's performance on multiple subsets of the data, analyzing the distribution of residuals, identifying and removing outliers, and determining the importance of the predictor variables. By utilizing these evaluation techniques, one can determine the accuracy and effectiveness of the regression model in predicting the target variable.</p>

<h3>Evaluation Metric: Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)</h3>

<p>To assess the accuracy of the models, we will use <strong>Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)</strong> as the primary evaluation metric. RMSE is a commonly used measure in regression analysis that quantifies the difference between the predicted and actual values of the target variable. A lower RMSE value indicates better model performance.</p>

<p>The RMSE formula is defined as:</p>

<p><strong>RMSE</strong> = ¡Ì( 1/n * ¦²(<em>y?<sub>i</sub></em> - <em>y<sub>i</sub></em>)<sup>2</sup> )</p>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>n</strong> = the total number of predictions</li>
  <li><strong>y?<sub>i</sub></strong> = the predicted flood probability for observation <em>i</em></li>
  <li><strong>y<sub>i</sub></strong> = the actual flood probability for observation <em>i</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Residual Analysis</h3>

<p>In addition to RMSE, residual analysis will also be a key factor in evaluating model performance. Residuals are the differences between the predicted and actual values of the target variable. Examining the distribution of residuals helps to ensure that the model is accurately capturing the relationship between the predictors and the target.</p>

<h3>Key Points in Evaluation:</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Accuracy</strong>: RMSE will be the main metric used to measure how well the model¡¯s predictions match the actual flood probabilities.</li>
  <li><strong>Residual Distribution</strong>: A well-performing model should show a residual distribution that is normally distributed and free of systematic bias.</li>
  <li><strong>Outliers</strong>: Although outliers are present in the competition data, models are expected to handle them effectively without significant degradation in performance.</li>
</ul>

<p>By using RMSE as the primary evaluation metric and conducting residual analysis, we ensure a comprehensive evaluation of each model's predictive capabilities.</p>

---

Submissions are evaluated on [Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square_deviation) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File

<p>For each <strong>RegionIndex</strong> in the submission dataset, you must predict a probability for the <strong>FloodProbability</strong> target variable. The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>

<pre>
    RegionIndex,FloodProbability
    SubmissionRegion1,0.45
    SubmissionRegion2,0.67
    SubmissionRegion3,0.50
    etc.
</pre>

<h3>File Format</h3>

<p>The submission file should meet the following criteria:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each row must include the <strong>RegionIndex</strong> as provided in the test set (e.g., SubmissionRegion1, SubmissionRegion2).</li>
  <li>The second column must be the predicted <strong>FloodProbability</strong>, which is a value between 0 and 1.</li>
  <li>The file must be submitted in CSV format with two columns: <strong>RegionIndex</strong> and <strong>FloodProbability</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Your predictions will be evaluated based on how close they are to the actual flood probabilities, using the RMSE metric. Lower RMSE values indicate better performance.</p>","# Dataset Description

## About Dataset

Flood detection refers to identifying, monitoring, and alerting authorities or individuals about the presence or likelihood of flooding in a particular area. The flood prediction dataset provided in this competition contains environmental and socio-economic indicators critical for understanding flood risks. This data can assist researchers and practitioners in developing predictive models to enhance disaster preparedness and reduce the adverse impacts of floods.

The dataset consists of 50,000 observations, divided into two parts for the competition:

* Competition Data (35,000 rows): This part of the data will be used to train and validate your models. It contains relevant features as well as some added complexity in the form of outliers, missing data, and irrelevant features.
* Submission Data (15,000 rows): This is unseen data provided for final predictions. No outliers or missing data are included, ensuring clean data for evaluation.

Participants are tasked with predicting the `Flood Probability` for various regions based on a variety of environmental and socio-economic factors. The challenge is structured to test your ability to handle missing data, outliers, and irrelevant features while building a strong predictive model.

## Files

*   **floodprediction-comp.csv** - The dataset includes both training and validation data. Contains both relevant and irrelevant features to increase complexity. Use this data to build, validate, and test your models.the training set
*   **floodprediction-submission.csv** - Participants are required to predict the `FloodProbability` for this dataset. This file has all the columns except `FloodProbability`, which participants need to predict.

## Columns Descriptions

* `MonsoonIntensity`: Measures the intensity of monsoons, which directly impacts the likelihood of flooding.
* `TopographyDrainage`: Describes the region's capacity to manage water drainage based on its topography.
* `RiverManagement`: Evaluates the effectiveness of river management practices, including dredging and riverbank maintenance.
* `Deforestation`: Quantifies the extent of deforestation in a region, which can lead to increased surface runoff and flood risk.
* `Urbanization`: Indicates the level of urbanization. Urban areas are more prone to floods due to impervious surfaces like roads and buildings.
* `ClimateChange`: Captures the influence of climate change, which can lead to unpredictable rainfall patterns and increased flood risk.
* `DamsQuality`: Assesses the condition and maintenance of dams, critical for flood control.
* `Siltation`: Describes the extent of sediment accumulation in rivers and reservoirs, reducing water flow capacity and increasing flood risk.
* `Agricultural Practices`: The types and sustainability of agricultural practices. The intensification of agriculture can lead to deforestation, excessive use of fertilizers and pesticides, and inappropriate irrigation practices, reducing soil biodiversity and increasing the risk of floods.
* `Encroachments`:The degree of encroachment on flood plains and natural waterways. Construction in flood-prone areas impedes the natural flow of water and increases the risk of floods.
* `IneffectiveDisasterPreparedness`: The lack of emergency plans, warning systems, and simulations increases the negative impact of floods.
* `DrainageSystems`: Well-maintained and adequately sized drainage systems help drain rainwater and reduce the risk of floods.
* `CoastalVulnerability`: Low-lying coastal areas are prone to flooding from storm surges and sea level rise.
* `Landslides`: Steep slopes and unstable soils are more prone to landslides.
* `Watersheds`: Regions with more watersheds may have a higher or lower risk of flooding, depending on various factors.
* `DeterioratingInfrastructure`: Clogged culverts, damaged drainage channels, and other deficient infrastructure can increase the risk of floods.
* `PopulationScore`: Densely populated areas can suffer more severe losses.
* `WetlandLoss`: Wetlands act as natural sponges, absorbing excess water and helping to prevent floods.
* `InadequatePlanning`: Urban planning that does not consider the risk of flooding increases the vulnerability of communities.
* `PoliticalFactors`: Factors such as corruption and a lack of political will to invest in drainage infrastructure can make it difficult to manage flood risk.
* `ElevationDifference`: Irrelevant Feature: Added for complexity and does not affect flood probability.
* `RoadDensity`: Irrelevant Feature: A random feature that has no impact on flood prediction.
* `AirPollutionIndex`: Irrelevant Feature: Added to increase complexity in feature selection.
* `FloodZoneRisk`: Categorical feature that represents the flood risk of a region as Low, Medium, or High.
* `RegionType`: Categorical feature denoting the region type as either Urban, Rural, or Coastal.
* `FloodProbability`: Target Variable: The probability of a flood occurring in the region. Participants are tasked with predicting this variable for the submission data.
* `RegionIndex`: A unique identifier for each region (e.g., Region1, SubmissionRegion1). Used to track predictions in the competition and evaluation.

## What to Expect

* Train using the competition data (`floodprediction-comp.csv`), which includes both relevant and irrelevant features along with outliers and missing data.
* Submit predictions for the unseen test data using `floodprediction-submission.csv`.

This competition challenges participants to build regression models that can effectively predict flood probabilities, handling data complexity such as outliers, missing values, irrelevant features, and categorical data.",False,True
