8631,dsaa-6100-large-language-model-fine-tuning,"# Overview

Welcome to the Text Generation Evaluation Challenge! In this competition, participants are tasked with fine-tuning and deploying state-of-the-art language models to generate human-like text content based on the given context.

The world of Natural Language Processing (NLP) has witnessed rapid advancements, especially in the domain of text generation. This competition aims to push the boundaries further, evaluating how closely machine-generated content can resemble human-like textual patterns and fluency.

For newcomers to Kaggle, all content here is written in markdown. If you're transferring information from other platforms like Word or a browser, ensure that the markdown or HTML is cleaned up for an optimal display.


### Goal:
The primary goal of this competition is to harness the capabilities of modern language models and produce text that's as coherent, relevant, and fluent as human-generated content. Participants will be evaluated on the quality of their generated text in comparison to the original content using metrics like the BLEU score and Jaccard similarity.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the quality of the generated text compared to the original content. We primarily use the BLEU score and Jaccard similarity to measure the coherence, relevance, and fluency of the generated content.

## Submission File

For each ID in the test set, you must generate a corresponding text content. The file should contain a header and have the following format:


Id,label
1,""Generated content for ID 1...""
2,""Generated content for ID 2...""
3,""Generated content for ID 3...""
etc.


Please ensure that the generated content is relevant and closely aligned with the context of the given dataset.","# Dataset Description

## Files

* **train_data.csv** - The dataset for training your model.
* **test_data.csv** - The dataset on which you'll make your predictions.

## Columns

* `ID` - A unique identifier for each text entry.
* `Output Content` - The content generated by the model. This is what you'll be predicting in the test set.
* `Input Context` - The context or prompt based on which the content was generated. This might be present in the training data to provide context for the generated content.",False,True
8632,iprm-challenge,"# Overview

This site provides data and instructions to test your model's performance on part of the Test Data of the ICASSP 2025 First Indoor Path Loss Prediction Challenge



----------------------
# Description

This dataset includes the input tensors for some of the test samples of the 2025 ICASSP  First Indoor Path Loss Prediction Challenge, and it is aimed at allowing the participants to check the performance of their solutions prior to final submission. 

The input and output tensor format, as well as their naming conventions, are the same as those described in the official competition documentation.



----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate the performance of your approach, you need to infer the PL heatmaps for all the input images found in the evaluation data set. Each time, you will need to (i) flatten the input tensor, (ii) create an equal-length array that includes the label for each point of the PL heatmap (Naming_convention: ""B"" + B_ID +  ""_Ant""+   Antenna_ID  + ""_f""  + freq_ID+ ""_S"" + Sample_ID + ""_"", Flatten_Spatial_Point_ID ), (iii) input these two arrays in a panda data frame that includes the results for all the spatial points of all the samples, (iv) save and upload the final pd dataframe. 

An example of how the pd should look like: 

            ID                                 PL
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_0                    120
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_1                     125
...
B1_Ant1_f1_S0_1150                 75
...

B5_Ant3_f2_S24_8403           94


An indicative code implementation is provided below: 

  ```

import os
import shutil
import numpy as np 
import pandas as pd
from skimage.io import imread
  
Buildings = [  ""1"",   ""5""  ]

 
task  =3 
 
ant_ids = [      1,3 ]
freq = [1,2 ]
   
solution = pd.DataFrame()
 model =# load_your_model 

for Antenna_ID in (ant_ids):   
    for f_i in  freq:
         for b in (Buildings):     
            for sp in range(0,25):
                 input_image= imread( your_directory  +  ""B"" + str(b) +  ""_Ant""+  str(Antenna_ID) + ""_f""  + str(f_i) + ""_S"" +str(sp)+ '.png')                
                 your_input_tensor =  # Apply any processing you have used to extract new input features 
                
 
                 y_PL = model(your_input_tensor) # Note that y_PL should have the same dimensions, W x H,  as the input image
                 y_flat = np.expand_dims( y_PL.flatten() , 1) 

                 y_names = np.expand_dims( np.core.defchararray.add(  ""B"" + str(b) +  ""_Ant""+  str(Antenna_ID) + ""_f""  + str(f_i) + ""_S"" +str(sp)+ ""_"",  np.linspace(0, y_img.size-1, y_img.size).astype(int).astype(str) )  , 1)  
                 
                 y_data = np.concatenate( (y_names, y_flat ), axis = 1)
                  y_pd = pd.DataFrame(data=y_data,    
                  index = np.linspace(0, y_img.size-1, y_img.size).astype(int) , columns= [""ID"" ,""PL (dB)"" ]) 
                 solution = pd.concat([solution, y_pd], ignore_index=True)
               
                 
solution.to_csv('Solution_check.csv', index=False)  

  ```","# Dataset Description

The data set includes part of the test set inputs for the Indoor Pathloss Radio Map Prediction Challenge  (https://indoorradiomapchallenge.github.io/timeline.html, https://ieee-dataport.org/documents/indoor-radio-map-dataset). Specifically, it includes data from:

(i) 2 indoor geometries (B1 and B5; note B2, B3, B4, B6 are kept for the final evaluation),
(ii) 2 different frequency bands (referred to as f1 and f2). Note that f1 corresponds to 868 MHz, f2 to 2.4 GHz.
(iii) 2 antenna radiation patterns (referred to as Ant1 and Ant3; Ant1 is isotropic, while Ant2 is kept for the final evaluation).

The input and target tensors, as well as the naming conventions. assume the same format described in the official dataset documentation (https://indoorradiomapchallenge.github.io/timeline.html, https://ieee-dataport.org/documents/indoor-radio-map-dataset).",False,True
8633,wikido24,"# Overview

**Dataset link**: https://huggingface.co/datasets/Pavankalyan/WikiDO
**Official Code**: https://github.com/piyushsinghpasi/WikiDO

The WIKIDO Cross-Modal Retrieval Challenge invites participants to push the boundaries of vision-language models (VLMs) by evaluating and improving their performance on out-of-distribution (OOD) image-text retrieval tasks. Building on the WIKIDO benchmark, which consists of 384K diverse image-text pairs sourced from the Wikipedia Diversity Observatory, the competition focuses on testing and enhancing the generalization capabilities of VLMs beyond traditional datasets like MSCOCO and Flickr30K.

**Goal:** Participants will design and fine-tune VLMs to perform cross-modal retrieval (image-to-text and text-to-image) tasks. The primary goal is to achieve high recall rates, especially for OOD test sets that encompass domains and topics unseen during training. The competition aims to demonstrate models that not only excel in zero-shot settings but also show significant improvement upon fine-tuning with WIKIDO data.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Recall Metric Overview

Recall is a metric used to evaluate the performance of retrieval models or classifiers, particularly in the context of information retrieval and classification tasks. It measures the ability of a model to retrieve all the relevant items from a dataset. In simpler terms, recall assesses how well a model captures all the relevant results from a pool of candidates. In retrieval tasks like image-to-text or text-to-image retrieval, recall is used to measure how often the correct match (e.g., the correct text for an image or the correct image for a text) appears within the top K retrieved items.

### Recall@K
- **Recall@1 (R@1)**: This indicates the percentage of queries for which the correct result is ranked first. It is the strictest form of recall, measuring whether the top match is correct.
- **Recall@5 (R@5)**: This measures the percentage of queries for which the correct match appears within the top 5 results returned by the model.
- **Recall@10 (R@10)**: This looks at the percentage of queries where the correct result is within the top 10 retrieved items.

---

Since Kaggle only allows one metric per competition, we request the participants to submit the results on the OOD test set. We find the average of Recall@1, 5, and 10 for both image-to-text (I2T) and text-to-image (T2I) retrieval tasks on this OOD. We only report the average of I2T and T2I as the final metric. We request the participants to submit the results in the following format:
1. Please do not shuffle the order of instances in the OOD test set.
2. The submission file should contain three columns:
 a) file_name: Same column as in OOD test set in the same order.
 b) img2txt_top_10: Each row is a list of indices of top 10 texts for each image 
 c) txt2img_top_10: Each row is a list of indices of top 10 images for each text 
3. The indices correspond to the order of entries in file_name column of OOD test set.


## Submission File
For each file_name in the test set, you must predict a list of indices of top 10 entries:
>file_name,img2txt_top_10,txt2img_top_10
>sublist_5_00006_000062918.jpg,[0,1,2,3...9],[2,3,4,5...9]
>sublist_1_00003_000035614.jpg,[1,2,3,6...8],[8,9,4,5...9]
>sublist_1_00009_000091122.jpg,[5,6,7,8...9],[9,2,3,4...9]
>etc.","# Dataset Description

Cross-modal (image-to-text and text-to-image) retrieval is an established task used in evaluation benchmarks to test the performance of vision-language models (VLMs). Several state-of-the-art VLMs (e.g. CLIP, BLIP-2) have achieved near-perfect performance on widely-used image-text retrieval benchmarks such as MSCOCO-Test-5K and Flickr30K-Test-1K. As a measure of out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization, prior works rely on zero-shot performance evaluated on one dataset (Flickr) using a VLM finetuned on another one (MSCOCO). We argue that such comparisons are insufficient to assess the OOD generalization capability of models due to high visual and linguistic similarity between the evaluation and finetuning datasets. WikiDO offers a strong cross-modal retrieval benchmark for current VLMs, especially for evaluating OOD generalization.

You can access the dataset on Hugging Face: https://huggingface.co/datasets/Pavankalyan/WikiDO

The dataset is organized in the following way:
1. All the images including train, val and test are present in ""images.zip""
2. We created three different kinds of train splits:
2.1 train_100k.jsonl- A balanced train set consisting of almost equal number of samples from each topic amounting to a total of 100K instances. 
2.2 train_200k.jsonl- A balanced training set is created using 200K samples
2.3 train_full.jsonl- the training set containing all 354K samples.
3. To mimic the original data distribution, we randomly sample 1000 and 3000 instances from the train set instances to create validation (val_verified.jsonl) and in-domain (id_verified.jsonl) test splits.
4. Evaluation set of size 3K (comparable to existing test set sizes in MSCOCO and Flickr) from all the samples across the OOD topics- ood_verified.jsonl

Each of these *.jsonl files has following columns:
| Field        | Description                                                       |
|--------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| image_path    | Path of the image                                                 |
| image_id     | Wiki ID of the image                                             |
| orig_cap     | Reference text from Wikipedia                                     |
| image        | Unique image ID given in the dataset                             |
| page_id      | Wiki ID of the page from which the image was extracted           |
| page_title   | Title of the Wikipedia article from which the image was extracted |
| topic        | Topic label from Wikipedia Diversity Observatory                  |
| caption      | Caption obtained by passing orig_cap through LLava (human verified for test, val sets) |",False,True
