# Prizes

* 1st Place - $12,000
* 2nd Place - $10,000
* 3rd Place - $10,000
* 4th Place - $10,000
* 5th Place - $8,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 4.75 hours run-time during the training phase, 12 hours during the forecasting phase.
- GPU Notebook <= 4.75 hours run-time during the training phase, 12 hours during the forecasting phase.
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.
----------------------
# WSDM Cup 2025

This competition was selected for the [WSDM Cup 2025](https://www.wsdm-conference.org/2025/call-for-wsdm-cup-proposals/). Top submissions for the competition will be invited to give talks at the conference. Attending the conference is not required to participate in the competition, however only teams that are attending the conference will be considered to present their work.
Competitions of this form are a crucial part of the research ecosystem, bringing together world-class experts from all over the world to independently evaluate their own best ideas on an important problem. Past experience has shown this process to create both amazing progress on specific problems, but also help to achieve a depth of validation and understanding in a way that lives up to the highest ideals of empirical rigor for AI and ML as research field.

Attendees presenting in person are responsible for all costs associated with travel, expenses, and fees to attend [WSDM Cup 2025](https://www.wsdm-conference.org/2025).","# Dataset Description

The competition dataset consists of user interactions from the [ChatBot Arena](https://lmarena.ai/) (formerly LMSYS). In each user interaction a judge provides one prompt to two different large language models and then indicates which of the models gave the more satisfactory response.

This is a [Code Competition](https://www.kaggle.com/c/wsdm-cup-multilingual-chatbot-arena/overview/code-requirements). When your submission is scored the example test data will be replaced with the full test set. 


## Competition Phases and Data Updates
The competition will proceed in two phases:

1. A model training phase with a test set of historical data. This test set has about 10,000 rows.
2. A forecasting phase with a test set to be collected after the submission deadline. You should expect this test set to contain at most 25,000 rows. There may be fewer if that turns out to be necessary in order to maintain the schedule.

The Chatbot Arena team may release additional data during the model training phase.

## Files

**train.parquet** 

*   `id` - A unique string identifier for the row.
*   `prompt` - The prompt that was given as an input to both models.
*   `response_[a/b]` - The response from model_[a/b] to the given prompt.
*   `winner` - The judge's selection. The ground truth target column.
*   `model_[a/b]` - The identity of model_[a/b]. Only included in train.parquet.
*   `language` - The language used in the prompt. Only included in train.parquet.

**test.parquet** 

*   `id` - A unique integer identifier for the row.
*   `prompt`
*   `response_[a/b]`
*   `scored` - Whether or not the row is currently scored. During the model training phase this will be true for rows used for the public leaderboard; during the forecasting phase this will be true for rows used for the private leaderboard.

**sample_submission.csv** A submission file in the correct format.

*   `id`
*   `winner`

*Note that the dataset for this competition contains text that may be considered profane, vulgar, or offensive.*",True,True
8654,aaai-25-visually-rich-document-vrd-iu-leaderboard,"# Description

Welcome to the Visually-Rich Document(VRD) Intelligence and Understanding Kaggle competition! This competition is the sub-activity of **The Third Competition on Visually Rich Document Intelligence and Understanding (VRDIU)** in conjunction with the **39th Annual AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI 2025)** in **Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA**. Please visit the [VRDIU competition] (https://sites.google.com/view/docui-aaai25/cfp?authuser=0) for a detailed competition schedule and important dates. 

### Task Background 
Document parsing aims to build a structured, hierarchical understanding of visually rich documents by identifying and recognizing logical relationships between semantic entities. This structured understanding is crucial in various domains, such as academic papers, business reports, and government documents, where semantic entities like sections, subsections, tables, and figures contribute to an organized document flow. Document parsing follows document layout analysis as a downstream task, leveraging the layout structure to infer parent-child relationships between entities. For example, in an academic paper, a ¡°Section¡± entity can contain multiple ¡°Subsections,¡± and captions are directly associated with their corresponding figure or table bodies.

In this competition, the objective is to perform document structure parsing on mining reports provided by various companies. The complexity arises as many of these reports are unstructured or scanned, presenting a unique challenge in achieving a consistent and accurate parsing outcome. Understanding the logical structure in these documents is fundamental for downstream content understanding tasks, such as key information extraction, question answering, and hierarchical organization of document content.


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11409544%2F364cc2972ce465d4e42958ae6dc5c91d%2Fworkshop_01.png?generation=1729726632074846&alt=media)


### Task Definition
Each report comprises a collection of detected semantic entity bounding boxes in the format (x, y, w, h), where (x, y) represents the coordinates of the top-left corner, and (w, h) denotes the width and height of the semantic entity. These entities, such as *section*, *paragraph*, *table*, and *caption*, are labelled with semantic categories in the training set. The objective is to predict the logical structure of each report by establishing hierarchical relationships between these semantic entities. This involves defining how the entities are organized and nested to accurately represent the document's logical flow and structure. 
Specifically:

**Hierarchical Relation Prediction:** Determine the parent-child relationships between document entities. For instance, if an entity ¡°Section¡± is found to be the parent of a ¡°Paragraph¡± entity, it indicates that the paragraph belongs to that section.
**Directional Relation:** Relationships are unidirectional, meaning if ""Section"" is the parent of ""Paragraph,"" the reverse does not hold.
**Non-Bidirectional Relationships:** For each document, the aim is to construct a hierarchical tree where each entity only points upward to its parent entity, not downward to child entities.

This task requires participants to use available training data to model these logical relationships, which will help in accurate hierarchical structure prediction in the test set, where entity categories are recognized, but relationships need to be inferred. For example, given a document with *N* detected entities, the desired output format should be a list containing the parent entity for each of the *N* entities. ***The evaluation metric is F1.***

Before conducting this competition, please ensure you read and understand the **Rules** of this competition first. Please get in touch with us anytime if you get confused about this competition. **Note a strong baseline model is provided for starting this competition!**

Therefore, gather your expertise in computer vision, natural language processing, and deep learning, and join us in this competition. Engage with a vibrant community of like-minded individuals, exchange ideas, and strive for the top spot on the leaderboard. Together, let's push the boundaries of document structure parsing and shape the future of intelligent document analysis.

You can scan the following QR code for more details:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11409544%2Fcd2314c436cf478561bc9a645bab0083%2Fframe.jpg?generation=1731375315239589&alt=media)

COMPETITION SPONSOR: Google Research
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5589143%2F63363ecec589e9174bf34746116c85b6%2Fsponsor.png?generation=1712029907388272&alt=media"" width = ""50%"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />


If you have more questions or inquiries about this competition, feel free to fill out this competition QA form. Our organising teams will answer your questions within three working days.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The accuracy score is calculated as the ratio of correctly predicted samples to the total number of samples. It can be defined as:

<div align=""center""> \[ \text{Accuracy} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N} \mathbb{1}(\hat{y}_i = y_i)}{N} \] </div>

Where \( N \) is the number of samples, 


N is the total number of samples. y_hat_i is the predicted label for the i-th sample. y_i is the true label for the i-th sample. 1(y_hat_i = y_i) is an indicator function that returns 1 if the predicted label (y_hat_i) matches the true label (y_i) (correct prediction) and 0 otherwise.

----------------------
# Kaggle Competition Submission Guidelines

****Thank you for participating in our exciting competition! We are delighted to see the enthusiasm and dedication from all of you. To ensure that the competition runs smoothly and that everyone has a fair chance, we have outlined the submission guidelines below. Please follow these guidelines carefully to submit your entries successfully.

The submit button is at the right corner of the competition main page.

**There should be a image**








## Submission Components:





1. **Leaderboard(submission.csv)**
When submitting your predictions for the Kaggle competition, please make sure to adhere to the following guidelines:

    - Submission File Name: The file containing your prediction results must be named ""submission.csv"".

    - Format: Your submission file MUST use the 'component_id' of the prediction layout components (RoIs). P

    - Evaluation: Ensure that the format of your submission file matches the sample submission file provided, ""submission.csv."" This will allow us to evaluate your performance correctly.

2. **Code (Notebook):**





    - The code should be well-documented, organized, and self-explanatory.
    - Use Jupyter Notebook/Kaggle Notebook/Colab Notebook as the preferred format for code submission.
    - Include all necessary libraries and dependencies required to run the code.
    - Ensure your code can be executed end-to-end without any errors.
    - Make sure to handle any external data dependencies and include instructions on how to download and use them, if applicable.
    - The code should be structured to allow your results to be reproducible.
    - Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. For the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:
        - CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
        - GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time


3.  **Abstract (AAAI format):**





    - Prepare a one-page abstract in the AAAI (Association for the advancement of Artificial Intelligence) format to accompany your code submission.
    - The abstract should follow the AAAI guidelines for formatting and describe the results and methods.
    - The results section should present your findings, experiments, and any evaluation metrics used.
    - The methods section should describe the approach and algorithms employed in your solution.
    - Make sure to highlight the strengths and limitations of your approach.
    - Aim for a paper length of one page.

## Submission Process:

### During the competition

  - Submit the ""submission.csv"" file to evaluate your model's performance.



### Final Evaluation. 
  - Compress your submission.csv, code (Notebook) and the Abstract into a single zip file. The zip file should be    named appropriately, e.g., ""YourTeamName_Submission.zip.""
  - Upload your submission to the Kaggle competition platform under the ""Submit"" tab. Participants can submit up to two zip files, which will count towards their final leaderboard score.
  - Select the two zip submissions in the Submissions page. If you submit fewer than two zip files, Kaggle will automatically select from your best-scoring submissions for this competition. Note that for the final evaluation, only teams that submit at least one comprehensive zip file will be considered.

  -  Provide a brief description of your submission and the key highlights in the ""Description"" field.
  -  Ensure your submission whether the three submission components are included and that the code runs without errors.
  -  Submit your entry before the competition deadline. Late submissions will not be considered.


## Evaluation:

  -  Submissions will be evaluated based on the performance on a hidden test dataset using relevant evaluation metrics, as defined in the competition guidelines.
  -  Additionally, the clarity, innovation, and quality of the one-page abstract will also be taken into account during the evaluation process.


## Note:

  -  Plagiarism and copying from other participants or external sources will not be tolerated. Any such action will lead to disqualification.
  -  Participate in the competition with honesty and integrity, following the competition rules and guidelines.



Good luck with your submissions! We are eager to see the remarkable work you all have done. If you have any questions or face any issues during the submission process, feel free to contact our support team
