## Files

*   **Train_churn_ML.csv** - the training set
*   **Test_churn_ML.csv** - the test set
*   **Submission.csv** - Submission file",False,True
8668,prediction-human-gut-biotransformation-pathways,"# Overview

The main goal of this competition is to predict the products of microbiome gut biotransformation reactions and the pathways they follow. We aim to bring together researchers, data scientists, and machine learning enthusiasts from around the world to work together to tackle this complex and extremely important problem. 
Knowing the fate of any chemical compound in the human gut is crucial for drug metabolism, toxicology, and personalised medicine. 
 ![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F69674%2Ff0a89b3a3d2e6b9078a94e84b8249d8e%2Fkaggel_logo_EN.png?generation=1715163875367374&alt=media)
----------------------
# Description

The human gut microbiome plays a vital role in our health. It helps in food digestion, immune system regulation, and protection against harmful bacteria. One of the main roles of these microbes is the biotransformation of any chemical compounds into metabolites, through a succession of chemical reactions and pathways. These metabolites can have profoundly impact on our health, both beneficial and harmful.
The [Department of Molecular Toxicology at Helmholtz Centre for Environmental Research (UFZ)](https://www.ufz.de/index.php?en=34249) investigates the impact of environmental pollutants on the intestinal microbiome, host organism, and chronic diseases like obesity and immune disorders. Scientist of the department have established different tools to detect metabolites from mass spectrometry spectra which require experimental efforts, the costs often become prohibitive for large-scale studies and often lack the ability to capture the full complexity of all the microbiome-pathways. Consequently, it is crucial to create alternatives that closely mimic the in vivo experiments with high biological significance and lower experimental budgets and efforts, and overcome all the above mentioned drawbacks. This could be only achieved in silico by using machine learning (ML) algorithms and employing the vast amounts of open data generated by high-throughput omics technologies. Therefore, we organize this competition to predict and explore biotransformation metabolites and pathways in the human gut while leveraging the wealth of chemical and omics data. 
Overall, predicting the fate of any chemical compounds in the human gut holds immense promise for advancing drug discovery, improving safety assessment practices, and promoting better health outcomes for individuals worldwide. By leveraging computational tools and experimental data, researchers can gain valuable insights into the complex interplay between chemicals and biological systems, ultimately driving innovation and progress in biomedical research and healthcare.
## Potential Impact
Knowing what biotransformation processes occur in the intestinal microbiome holds great promise for a variety of fields. This knowledge contributes to drug discovery, safety assessment of pharmaceuticals, and the general improvement of understanding the vast metabolic capacity in the intestinal microbiome.
The development of machine learning tools will accelerate the experimental testing procedures by generating predictions and thus has the potential to reduce experimental effort and cost tremendously. For the future the interaction between experimentalists and machine learning tools will lead to (1) further improvement of machine learning tools and (2)  on the other hand spare immense amounts of costs for unnecessary experiments. 
Understanding of metabolic networks and their regulation which represent the foundation for precision medicine and innovative therapeutic approaches. Safer chemicals and medications can be developed as a result of understanding how a molecule may change in the human gut and forecasting its toxicity or negative consequences.
----------------------
# Timeline

**October 18th, 2024** - Start Date.

**November 20th, 2024** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

**December 20th, 2024** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 CEST (Berlin Time) on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submitted solution will be evaluated by a Final Score (FS), which represents the average of the pathways¡¯ similarity in the hidden dataset.

$$ Final\text{ }Score\text{ }(FS)=  \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}PS_{i}}{n} $$

The value of **FS** is between 0 and 1, the higher FS is, the more similar the pathway and the better the final prediction. 
where **n** denotes the number of pathways in the hidden dataset. **PS** represents the predicted pathway similarity in relation to the ground truth. **PS** is given by the following equation and it would be the MAXIMUM value from the top 5 predicted possible pathways submitted.

$$ Pathway\text{ }Similarity\text{ }(PS)=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{m}PMS_{j}}{m} $$

where **m** represents the number Steps in a pathway.

**PMS** represents the similarity between the ground truth metabolite **gt** and its respective predicated metabolite **pm**, using the average of Levenshtein Distance (LD). **Len** calculates either the length of **gr** or **pm**, while **max** determines the longest string of (gt, pm).
$$ Similarity (PMS) =1- \frac{LD(gt, pm)}{max(len(gt), len(pm)))}$$

---

## Submission File
For each PWY_ID along with the number of steps that should be predicted and the first chemical compound represented in a SMILE (Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry system ) in the test set, you must predict the next n steps for a given chemical compound and the top 5 posible pathways, ranked from 1 to 5. The file should contain a header and have the following format. The predicted successive metabolites (SMILES) should be separated by (=>) which represents the predicted pathway (pwy). 

    PWY_ID,       Pathway_rank1,  Pathway_rank2, Pathway_rank3, Pathway_rank4, Pathway_rank5
    pwy0001,        pwy0001_1,      pwy0001_2,    pwy0001_3,    pwy0001_4,    pwy0001_5
    pwy0002,        pwy0002_1,      pwy0002_2,    pwy0002_3,    pwy0002_4,    pwy0002_5
    pwy0003,        pwy0003_1,      pwy0003_2,    pwy0003_3,    pwy0003_4,    pwy0003_5
    ...,        ...,   ...,

    etc.

----------------------
# Prizes

### 1st Place: **750 $**
### 2nd Place: **500 $**
### 3rd Place: **300 $**
----------------------
# Code Requirements

**This is a Code Competition**
Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. For the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 120 minutes run-time
- GPU Notebook submissions are enabled. 
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named submission.csv

Please see the Code Competition FAQ for more information on how to submit. And review the code debugging doc if you encounter submission errors.
----------------------
# References

- Ding, S., Tian, Y., Cai, P., Zhang, D., Cheng, X., Sun, D., ... & Hu, Q. N. (2020). novoPathFinder: a webserver of designing novel-pathway with integrating GEM-model. Nucleic Acids Research, 48(W1), W477-W487.
- Farag, M. A., Hariri, M. L. M., Ehab, A., Homsi, M. N., Zhao, C., & von Bergen, M. (2024). Cocoa seeds and chocolate products interaction with gut microbiota; mining microbial and functional biomarkers from mechanistic studies, clinical trials and 16S rRNA amplicon sequencing. Critical reviews in food science and nutrition, 64(10), 3122-3138.
- Hafner, J., & Hatzimanikatis, V. (2021). NICEpath: Finding metabolic pathways in large networks through atom-conserving substrate¨Cproduct pairs. Bioinformatics, 37(20), 3560-3568.
- Sveshnikova, A., MohammadiPeyhani, H., & Hatzimanikatis, V. (2022). Computational tools and resources for designing new pathways to small molecules. Current Opinion in Biotechnology, 76, 102722.
- Sokolova, N., Peng, B., & Haslinger, K. (2023). Design and engineering of artificial biosynthetic pathways¡ªwhere do we stand and where do we go?. FEBS letters, 597(23), 2897-2907.
- Song, X., Dong, M., & Liu, M. (2022). PyMiner: A method for metabolic pathway design based on the uniform similarity of substrate-product pairs and conditional search. Plos one, 17(4), e0266783. 
- Zimmermann, M., Zimmermann-Kogadeeva, M., Wegmann, R., & Goodman, A. L. (2019). Mapping human microbiome drug metabolism by gut bacteria and their genes. Nature, 570(7762), 462-467.","# Dataset Description

The competition dataset consists of a list of the human gut biotransformation reactions and the participants are asked to predict the fate of any given chemical compounds or drugs in the human gut by determining the possible successive biotransformation metabolites and the pathways they follow.

Please note that this is a Code Competition. When your submission is scored, this example test data will be replaced with the full test set. There are 17301 gut biotransformation reactions in the training data.


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set which consists of a list of metabolic reactions.
*   **test.csv** - the test set.  Please note that the pathways visible here are only examples. Your submission will have access to the full set of problems during scoring.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format. See the [Evaluation page](www.kaggle.com/competitions/prediction-of-human-gut-biotransformation-pathways/overview/evaluation) for more information on the submission format.

## Columns
train.csv
* `ID` - Reaction identifier.
* `SMILE` - The SMILEs of reactants and products separated by "">>"".

test/test_demo.csv
*   `PWY_ID` - Identifier of the pathway.
*   `Steps`?- Refers to how long the pathway is (Number of metabolites -1)
*   `Compound`?- Refers to the first compound in the pathway.

submission.csv 
This file should have 6 columns:
*   `PWY_ID` - Identifier of the pathway.
*   `Pathway_rank1` -  The best pathway predicted by the model.
*   `Pathway_rank2` -  Second-ranked prediction by the model.
*   `Pathway_rank3` -  Third-ranked prediction by the model.
*   `Pathway_rank4` -  Fourth-ranked prediction by the model.
*   `Pathway_rank5` -  Fifth-ranked prediction by the model.

Pathway_rank1, Pathway_rank2, Pathway_rank3, Pathway_rank4, & Pathway_rank5 should contain a successive of your predicted metabolites in SMILE format separated by ""=>"". The number of metabolites in a predicted pathway should match the number of Steps in test.csv.",False,True
8669,ml-course-project-2,"# Overview

Hello! In this hackathon, you will showcase your skills in predicting the prices of second hand cars. Our dataset contains information for various vehicles across Turkey, and our goal is to predict prices based on vehicle specifications. The use of machine learning libraries is prohibited in this competition! You can ask your questions in the Discussion tab!
----------------------
# Description

Some key columns in our dataset include:

Brand, Series, Model: The brand, series, and model of the vehicle.
Year, Age: The production year and age of the vehicle.
Fuel Type, Transmission Type: The fuel type and transmission type of the vehicle.
Engine Capacity, Engine Power: The engine capacity and power of the vehicle.
Mileage: The mileage of the vehicle.
Color, Drive, Body Type: The color, drive type, and body type of the vehicle.
Trade-In, Seller Type: Trade-in information and seller type of the vehicle.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Throughout the hackathon, you will build and validate your prediction models. Evaluation will be based on your prediction success on Kaggle.

The evaluation metric used for this competition is MAE(Mean Absolute Error).","# Dataset Description

