8711,sci-pediatric-chest-x-ray-pneumonia-classificatio,"# Overview

## Introducci¨®n

?Bienvenidos a la competencia de clasificaci¨®n de neumon¨ªa en rayos X pedi¨¢tricos de **SCI**!

### Objetivo
El objetivo de esta competencia es desarrollar modelos de clasificaci¨®n de im¨¢genes capaces de identificar casos de neumon¨ªa en rayos X de t¨®rax pedi¨¢tricos. Los participantes entrenar¨¢n y evaluar¨¢n sus modelos utilizando un dataset de im¨¢genes etiquetadas como ""neumon¨ªa"" o ""normal"". Esta tarea presenta desaf¨ªos ¨²nicos debido a la fisiolog¨ªa particular de los ni?os y a la dificultad de capturar im¨¢genes de alta calidad en pacientes pedi¨¢tricos.

Los participantes pueden optar por utilizar t¨¦cnicas de **Transfer Learning** o dise?ar sus propias arquitecturas de redes neuronales convolucionales (CNN). La competencia les brindar¨¢ la oportunidad de experimentar con diferentes enfoques para optimizar el rendimiento del modelo en este importante problema de salud.

Para m¨¢s detalles sobre c¨®mo participar y enviar predicciones, consulta la [Gu¨ªa de Inicio R¨¢pido](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions-setup) y la [Documentaci¨®n de Competencias de Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions).

----------------------
# Description

Las radiograf¨ªas de t¨®rax pedi¨¢tricas son m¨¢s dif¨ªciles de adquirir y estandarizar en comparaci¨®n con las de adultos. Para un ni?o, estar en una habitaci¨®n oscura con maquinaria extra?a y personas observ¨¢ndolo desde una ventana de vidrio no es una experiencia c¨®moda. Adem¨¢s, los ni?os presentan una fisiolog¨ªa diferente, lo cual es importante capturar en los algoritmos de clasificaci¨®n de rayos X, dado que la mayor¨ªa de los conjuntos de datos disponibles tienden a centrarse en adultos.


### Dataset

El dataset de esta competencia consta de **5,856 im¨¢genes de rayos X de t¨®rax pedi¨¢tricos**, etiquetadas como *neumon¨ªa* o *normal*. La distribuci¨®n de las im¨¢genes es la siguiente:
- **Train**: 3,816 im¨¢genes
- **Test**: 2,040 im¨¢genes

Este dataset fue recopilado y publicado por Kermany, Daniel; Zhang, Kang; y Goldbaum, Michael en 2018.

**C¨®mo citar este dataset:**
Kermany, Daniel; Zhang, Kang; Goldbaum, Michael (2018), ¡°Labeled Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) and Chest X-Ray Images for Classification¡±, Mendeley Data, v2  
[http://dx.doi.org/10.17632/rscbjbr9sj.2](http://dx.doi.org/10.17632/rscbjbr9sj.2)

### Importancia del Problema

El diagn¨®stico de neumon¨ªa en ni?os es un reto significativo en el ¨¢mbito de la medicina. Un diagn¨®stico temprano y preciso es esencial para evitar complicaciones graves. La automatizaci¨®n del proceso mediante modelos de clasificaci¨®n de im¨¢genes puede ayudar a mejorar la eficiencia y precisi¨®n del diagn¨®stico, especialmente en ¨¢reas donde el acceso a radi¨®logos expertos es limitado.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Las presentaciones se evaluar¨¢n en funci¨®n del **¨¢rea Bajo la Curva ROC (AUC-ROC)**, que mide la capacidad del modelo para distinguir entre casos de neumon¨ªa y normales. Esta m¨¦trica eval¨²a el balance entre la sensibilidad y la especificidad del modelo, permitiendo obtener una evaluaci¨®n completa de su rendimiento en distintas tasas de umbral.

### Formato del Archivo de Env¨ªo

Para cada ID en el conjunto de prueba, debes predecir una probabilidad para la variable objetivo (*TARGET*), donde 1 indica presencia de neumon¨ªa y 0 indica normal. El archivo de env¨ªo debe contener un encabezado y estar en el siguiente formato:

```
ID,TARGET
1,0.85
2,0.02
3,0.90
...
```","# Dataset Description

### Archivos

- **train** - Este conjunto contiene 3,816 im¨¢genes de rayos X pedi¨¢tricos, cada una etiquetada como *neumon¨ªa* o *normal*. Este es el conjunto de datos que utilizar¨¢s para entrenar tu modelo.
- **test** - Este conjunto contiene 2,040 im¨¢genes de rayos X sin etiquetas. Deber¨¢s predecir si cada imagen muestra signos de neumon¨ªa o no.
- **sample_submission.csv** - Un archivo de ejemplo que muestra el formato correcto para las presentaciones. Utiliza este archivo como referencia para preparar tus env¨ªos. Deber¨¢s presentar las predicciones en este formato.

### Estructura de los Datos

Cada imagen de rayos X en los conjuntos de entrenamiento y prueba est¨¢ clasificada en una de las dos categor¨ªas:

- **NORMAL**: Radiograf¨ªas sin signos de neumon¨ªa.
- **PNEUMONIA**: Radiograf¨ªas que muestran signos de neumon¨ªa.",False,True
8712,data-centric-noisy-pascal-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the **Data-Centric Object Detection Challenge**! In this competition, you'll be tasked with improving object detection performance by focusing on **data quality** rather than tweaking model architecture. Leveraging the Noisy PASCAL VOC 2007 dataset, your goal is to enhance the dataset in multiple phases, each focusing on a specific type of data corruption. 

Throughout the challenge, you will refine the dataset across four distinct phases:
1. **Contrast Enhancement**: Address issues related to poor image contrast.
2. **Noise Reduction**: Mitigate noise and artifacts using frequency domain techniques.
3. **Data Augmentation**: Apply augmentation strategies to enhance model generalization.
4. **Label Error Detection**: Correct errors in object localization and labeling.

Your success in the competition depends on how well you can **enhance, denoise, augment and fix labels errors of the dataset** in these phases, ultimately improving the accuracy of object detection using the YOLOv5 model. Good luck, and let's see who can create the cleanest and most effective dataset! 

### Objective:
Improve object detection performance by enhancing data quality through various methods and demonstrating your skills in data-centric AI.
----------------------
# Description

### The Challenge
In real-world machine learning tasks, the quality of your data often matters more than the complexity of the model. This competition embraces the **data-centric AI** approach, where participants focus on improving the **data** rather than the model itself. Your objective is to enhance object detection performance on the widely-used **PASCAL VOC 2007 dataset** by correcting various types of data corruption.

The competition is structured around **four phases**, each designed to simulate real-world challenges faced when working with visual data. You¡¯ll be working with the **YOLOv5 model**, and your goal is to **optimize its performance by enhancing, denoising, augmenting and correcting the dataset** across these phases.

### Why Data-Centric AI?
In many computer vision tasks, tweaking model architectures can only get you so far. Often, noisy or low-quality data limits model performance, even with state-of-the-art algorithms. Data-centric AI puts the focus on improving **data quality** as a means to enhance model outcomes. By carefully managing contrast, noise, augmentation, and label accuracy, you¡¯ll experience firsthand how **data quality is key to success** in object detection tasks.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric

Submissions will be evaluated using the **mean Average Precision (mAP50-95)** metric for object detection, a standard measure used to evaluate the performance of object detectors. The mAP50-95 score evaluates how well the predicted bounding boxes and class labels match the ground truth across various Intersection over Union (IoU) thresholds.

### Submission File

The submission file for this competition is a CSV formatted as follows: Each row corresponds to a single image and contains four columns: `image_id`, `prediction_string`, `image_width`, `image_height`, `image_height` and 'Usage'. The `image_id` column specifies the unique identifier for each image (image file name), while `image_width` and `image_height` provide the dimensions of the image in pixels. The `prediction_string` column consolidates all detected bounding boxes for an image into a single, space-separated string. Each detection is represented by `{label confidence x_center y_center width height}`, where `label` indicates the class ID, `confidence` represents the detection confidence score, and `x_center`, `y_center`, `width`, `height` denote the bounding box normalized coordinates in YOLO format. The 'Usage' column is a fixed value set to ""Public"" because this competition includes only a public leaderboard. This column exists solely to meet Kaggle's requirements.

For example:

```
002084.jpg,7 0.9342 0.3527 0.5056 0.6568 0.9747 7 0.7538 0.4666 0.5264 0.9202 0.9442,500,375,Public
007875.jpg,7 0.9515 0.4752 0.5047 0.9505 0.9732,414,500,Public

```
**The provided submission code is intended to prevent any potential conflicts, so you only need to prepare a trained YOLOv5 model.** Submit your predictions through the competition platform, and they will be automatically scored based on the ground truth annotations.","# Dataset Description

### Data

This competition uses a noisy version of the **PASCAL VOC 2007 dataset**, where the training data has been corrupted in phases to simulate real-world challenges. Your task is to improve data quality and object detection performance without any changes to the model.

#### Files

- **`voc2007/images/train/`**: Contains training images in `.jpg` format.
- **`voc2007/labels/train/`**: Corresponding YOLOv5 format `.txt` files with annotations for each image.
- **`voc2007/images/test/`**: Test images without labels.

#### YOLOv5 Label Format

Each `.txt` file contains:

```
class_id x_center y_center width height
```
- **class_id**: Object class ID.
- **x_center, y_center**: Bounding box center coordinates (normalized).
- **width, height**: Bounding box size (normalized).

Example:

```
6 0.485 0.533 0.122 0.250
14 0.719 0.442 0.098 0.143
```

#### Prediction Task

You will predict bounding boxes and object classes for test images.",False,True
8713,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-c-65,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.
----------------------
# FAQs

### How do I contact support?

For this competition, any questions, concerns, or technical difficulties will be addressed at the Discussion forum on UpGrad Learn platform. Any discussion posted regarding support on this competition page will have to be redirected to the same portal.

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

The Kaggle leaderboard has a public and private component to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

**Public Leaderboard:** For all participants, the same 70% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the public leaderboard. The score you see on the public leaderboard reflects your model¡¯s accuracy on this portion of the test set.

**Private Leaderboard:** The other 30% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the private leaderboard. The private leaderboard is not visible to participants until the competition has concluded. At the end of a competition, we will reveal the private leaderboard so you can see your score on the other 30% of the test data. The scores on the private leaderboard are used to determine the competition winners.

### What are kernels?

Kaggle Kernels is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels support scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the Kernels tab to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
8714,kaggle-biochallenge-cirrhosis-detection,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far. This is our last episode for the Season 3 and we wish you all a Happy New Year! Stay tuned for the new season next year!

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use a multi-class approach to predict the the outcomes of patients with cirrhosis. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the multi-class logarithmic loss. Each `id` in the test set had a single true class label, `Status`. For each `id`, you must submit a set of predicted probabilities for each of the three possible outcomes, e.g., `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D`.

The metric is calculated

$$log loss = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^My_{ij}\log(p_{ij}),$$

where \\(N\\) is the number of rows?in the test set, \\(M\\) is the number of outcomes (i.e., 3),? \\(log\\) is the natural logarithm, \\(y_{ij}\\) is 1 if row \\(i\\) has the ground truth label \\(j\\) and 0 otherwise, and \\(p_{ij}\\) is the predicted probability that observation \\(i\\) belongs to class?\\(j\\).

The submitted probabilities for a given row?are not required to sum to one because they are rescaled prior to being scored (each row is divided by the row sum). In order to avoid the extremes of the log function, predicted probabilities are replaced with \\(max(min(p,1-10^{-15}),10^{-15})\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict probabilities of the three outcomes `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D` . The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Status_C,Status_CL,Status_D
    7905,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    7906,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    7907,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    etc.",,False,True
8715,ai-driven-autonomous-robotics-challenge,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.",,False,True
8716,dota-2-simplified-cs-677-o-2,"# Overview

## Introduction
![](https://cdn.cloudflare.steamstatic.com/apps/dota2/images/dota2_social.jpg)
Dota 2 is the game that took by storm the world of computer gamers. In this competition we will try to predict the winner of the game based on the statistics of the first several minutes of the game

## ML Problem Definition
This is Binary Classification Problem. The data is structured (tabular, csv data)

## Credits
This competition is the simplified version of competitions held in:
- Yandex Data School by Pavel Gein
- Higher School of Economics by Konstantin Vorontsov
- ODS.AI by Yuriy Kashnitskiy
 
----------------------
# Description

This is Binary Classification problem
----------------------
# Evaluation

The Metric that will be used is ROC AUC. 
More information on ROC AUC is: https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict if radiant wins or not. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID, radiant_win
    2,0
    5,1
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

File descriptions

train_data.csv: basic training set

test_data.csv: basic test set


Data fields

Player¡¯s stats (start with a prefix to identify a player: ¡®r¡¯ for Radiant team or ¡®d¡¯ for Dire team and a player¡¯s number) include the following fields:

hero_id

