8742,bin-318-r-01-2024-edition-in-class-competition,"# Overview

**Welcome to the BIN318R01 2024 InClass Competition!**  

**Context**
Blueberries are perennial flowering plants with blue or purple berries. They are classified in the section Cyanococcus within the genus Vaccinium. Vaccinium also includes cranberries, bilberries, huckleberries, and Madeira blueberries. Commercial blueberries¡ªboth wild (lowbush) and cultivated (highbush)¡ªare all native to North America. The highbush varieties were introduced into Europe during the 1930s.

Blueberries are usually prostrate shrubs that can vary in size from 10 centimeters (4 inches) to 4 meters (13 feet) in height. In the commercial production of blueberries, the species with small, pea-size berries growing on low-level bushes are known as ""lowbush blueberries"" (synonymous with ""wild""), while the species with larger berries growing on taller, cultivated bushes are known as ""highbush blueberries"". Canada is the leading producer of lowbush blueberries, while the United States produces some 40% of the world s supply of highbush blueberries.

**Your Goal:** Your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

# Features
The yield depends on size of blueberry clone, bees, rain and temperature. More details below.

**Features Unit Description**
Clonesize m2 The average blueberry clone size in the field
Honeybee bees/m2/min Honeybee density in the field
Bumbles bees/m2/min Bumblebee density in the field
Andrena bees/m2/min Andrena bee density in the field
Osmia bees/m2/min Osmia bee density in the field
MaxOfUpperTRange ¡æ The highest record of the upper band daily air temperature during the bloom season
MinOfUpperTRange ¡æ The lowest record of the upper band daily air temperature
AverageOfUpperTRange ¡æ The average of the upper band daily air temperature
MaxOfLowerTRange ¡æ The highest record of the lower band daily air temperature
MinOfLowerTRange ¡æ The lowest record of the lower band daily air temperature
AverageOfLowerTRange ¡æ The average of the lower band daily air temperature
RainingDays Day The total number of days during the bloom season, each of which has precipitation larger than zero
AverageRainingDays Day The average of raining days of the entire bloom season

## Content:
The dataset used for predictive modeling was generated by the Wild Blueberry Pollination Simulation Model, which is an open-source, spatially-explicit computer simulation program that enables exploration of how various factors, including plant spatial arrangement, outcrossing and self-pollination, bee species compositions and weather conditions, in isolation and combination, affect pollination efficiency and yield of the wild blueberry agroecosystem. The simulation model has been validated by the field observation and experimental data collected in Maine USA and Canadian Maritimes during the last 30 years and now is a useful tool for hypothesis testing and theory development for wild blueberry pollination researches.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error),

![MAE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visipedia/iwildcam_comp/master/assets/MAE.png)

where each `x_i` represents the predicted target, `y_i` represents the ground truth, and `n` is the number of rows in the test set.

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - November 4, 2024
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  5 pm November 4, 2024

The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Acknowledgements


Qu, Hongchun; Obsie, Efrem; Drummond, Frank (2020), ¡°Data for: Wild blueberry yield prediction using a combination of computer simulation and machine learning algorithms¡±, Mendeley Data, V1, doi: 10.17632/p5hvjzsvn8.1

Walter Reade and Ashley Chow. Regression with a Wild Blueberry Yield Dataset. https://kaggle.com/competitions/playground-series-s3e14, 2023. Kaggle.","# Dataset Description

**NOTE:** You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Wild blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shashwatwork/wild-blueberry-yield-prediction-dataset).  Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8743,dsm-2024-competition,"# Overview

Traditional image classification models heavily rely on accurately labeled data for training, but in real-world scenarios, acquiring large quantities of labeled images can be costly and time-consuming.

Aditionally, available datasets exhibits a notable class imbalance, with benign cases significantly outnumbering malignant ones. This imbalance is consistent with the epidemiological reality that the majority of individuals undergoing screening are found to be cancer-free, as malignancies occur in only a small subset of the tested population.

In this challenge, we provide you with a dataset that poses both obstacles: a significant portion of the training data remains unlabeled and the labeled data is heavily imbalanced.

Your task is to develop innovative deep learning algorithms and techniques to overcome these challenges and build a robust image classification model.

To succeed in this competition, participants are encouraged to explore semi-supervised/unsupervised learning methods that leverage the unlabeled data to improve the model's performance. Developing strategies to mitigate the impact of the class imbalance and enhance the model's ability to generalize effectively will be crucial. We encourage creative ideas.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [F1 score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) between the predicted probability and the observed target. 

The test set is split 50/50 in a public leaderboard (for which you can see the score) and a private leaderboard (which will be revealed after the competition ends).


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the LABEL variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,LABEL
    test/000000.jpg,1
    test/000001.jpg,0
    test/000002.jpg,1","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train_labeled.csv** - paths to the labeled training set, with their corresponding labels
*  **train_unlabeled.csv** - paths to the unlabeled training set
*   **test.csv** - paths for the test set, for which you will need to make predictions

## Columns

*   `ID` - the path to the file
*   `label`?- 0 for benign samples, 1 for malignant samples",False,True
8744,homework-2-si-364-fall-2024,"# Overview

This competition is the homework 2 of ""AI for Drug Design"" (SI364)
----------------------
# Description

This competition is the homework 2 of ""AI for Science and Engineering"" (SI364).

QSAR is a widely used computational method in drug discovery and materials science that predicts properties such as biological activity, toxicity, and solubility using molecular structure descriptors and related activity values.

The theme of this competition is to train an accurate QSAR model using the provided datasets, different molecular descriptors, feature selection, and machine learning algorithms to predict the biological activity of unknown compounds.

This is a binary classification task. The input data is the SMILES string of a Drug Compound. SMILES (Simplified molecular-input line-entry system) is a line notation method to represent molecules. It can be converted into a molecular graph with some python packages such as DGL-LifeSci. To learn more about SMILES, you could refer to https://chemicbook.com/2021/02/13/smiles-strings-explained-for-beginners-part-1.html.

Participants can use common methods such as linear regression, support vector machines, or neural networks, but we encourage trying new neural network models such as Transformer and GNN.

Winners will be selected based on the ROC AUC of participants' prediction and submitted code. Each participant can submit their code 5 times per day, and please upload the final code before the end of the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation Metric**

The evaluation metric for this competition is ROC AUC. It is a performance metric used in machine learning for evaluating the accuracy of a binary classification model. In binary classification problems, the output of the model is either 0 or 1, which corresponds to negative and positive classes, respectively. The ROC AUC metric measures the ability of the model to distinguish between these two classes by calculating the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. The ROC curve plots the true positive rate (TPR) against the false positive rate (FPR) at various classification thresholds.
The ROC AUC value ranges from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating better performance. A ROC AUC value of 0.5 indicates that the model is no better than random guessing, while a ROC AUC value of 1 indicates perfect classification.


## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: `Drug_ID` and `Predicted_Score`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Drug_ID, Predicted_Score
    14747200, 0.348
    24790087, 0.895","# Dataset Description

**Files**

- **train.csv** - the training set
- **test.csv** - the test set

**Columns**

- `Drug_ID` - The ID of Compound
- `Drug` - SMILES of Drug Compound
- `Label` - The Activity Label (0 or 1)",False,True
8745,machine-learning-for-io-t-2024-lab-3,"# Description

### Introduction 
Epilepsy affects more than 64 million people worldwide. Despite substantial progress in the efficacy of anti-epileptic drugs, one-third of the epileptic patients continue to have seizures despite anti-epileptic medication. The unpredictable nature of seizures can be life-threatening, causing epilepsy patients to have a 2-3 times higher mortality rate than healthy control groups. 

Ideally, we would like to warn patients that a seizure is going to occur, so the patient can make sure they are in a safe environment (e.g. not near sharp objects) and can get emergency medication administered by their caregiver. At the moment, the standard procedure for such epileptic patient monitoring is based on simultaneous video recordings and multiple electroencephalogram electrodes (video-EEG). However, video recordings seriously impact the patient¡¯s privacy, and EEG measurements are invasive, requiring multiple electrodes to be attached to the patient¡¯s head at all times. Therefore, it is not possible to monitor patients on a long-term basis and in ambulatory settings based on such video-EEG. 

Wearable devices are light and only minimally invasive, so they do facilitate remote patient monitoring. For example, the e-Glass [1] is a pair of glasses that uses four EEG electrodes embedded and hidden in the temples of the glasses for real-time unobtrusive epilepsy monitoring. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F11405508%2F7432020e9cd9e8cdb4605b9b45ca62b6%2FScreenshot%20from%202023-10-31%2014-21-59.png?generation=1698758571972239&alt=media)

If we want to use the e-Glass to warn patients and caregivers, it needs to have a piece of software that analyzes the incoming EEG signals and determines whether or not the patient has a seizure at a given time. 
In the three labs of this course, you will implement a machine learning system for this purpose. The labs are built up in the following way: 
1.	In lab 1, you will use a Convolutional Neural Network
2.	In lab 2, you will use a Lightweight Machine Learning algorithm
3.	In lab 3, you will use a low-power machine learning network suitable to run on wearable devices. 

[1] Sopic, D., Aminifar, A., & Atienza, D. (2018, May). e-glass: A wearable system for real-time detection of epileptic seizures. In 2018 IEEE International Symposium on Circuits and Systems (ISCAS) (pp. 1-5). IEEE.

----------------------
# Evaluation

You need to train the deep learning model for epilepsy detection in a notebook and generate the `.h` file in c code. The `.h` file contains the architecture information and the weights. Then you should include that `.h`  file in an ARDUINO project. By compiling and uploading the ARDUINO project to an ARDUINO board, you can see the estimated category and inference time. 

1. Submitted your submission.csv file on Kaggle and score below **0.15** on the leaderboard. Your group name on Kaggle should include your student IDs. Note that the daily submission limit is **20**.

2. Submitted your notebook and Arduino Project on Canvas

3. Showed your notebook to one of the TAs during the lab sessions.

---

## Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean squared error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error). We calculate the metric for category and inference time at the same time.

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain three columns: `Id`, `Category`, and `Time`. You can copy the three columns' data from the Serial Monitor in ARDUINO IDE. Then you can paste the three columns' data to the submission.csv in Excel. Finally, you can submit the submission.csv to get the evaluation results.

The file should contain a header and have the following format (here we only take three test samples as an example. Totally, submission.csv should have 40 test samples)):
```
Id, Category, Time
1,      1,     3.3
2,      1,     5.4
3,      0,     0.3
```","# Dataset Description

You need to train the deep learning model for epilepsy detection in a notebook and generate the **net.h** file. The **net.h** file contains the architecture information and the weights. Then you should include that **net.h** file in an ARDUINO project. By compiling and uploading the ARDUINO project to an ARDUINO board, you can see the estimated category and inference time. 


## Files

*   **Arduino.ino** - the ARDUINO project file.
*   **EEG.npy** - EEG data.
*   **Category.npy** - Label data.
*   **testdata.h** - the test data
*   **submission.csv** - you should fill in and submit

## Columns for submission.csv

*   `Id` - the index of test data
*   `Category`?- 1 means seizure and 0 means non-seizure
*   `Time`- the time of inference",False,True
8746,mcml-24-25-lab-2-intrusion-detection-in-iot,"# Overview

The purpose of this competition is to assess the performance of the models developed as part of the second lab exercise in the ""Mobile Computing Technologies with Machine Learning"" course at NTUA ECE.

Students will be tasked with solving an Intrusion Detection problem, which involves classifying network traffic into one of 8 classes within an IoT environment. The primary objective is to train and optimize a model that can accurately detect network attacks occurring within the IoT network, ensuring robust performance under real-world conditions.
----------------------
# Description

The main notebook for this competition is available under the ""Code"" tab. Additionally, please refer to the introductory lab, which can be accessed [here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/ioannispan/mcml-24-25-lab2-intro).

You can find the competition's dataset under the ""Data"" tab.

Students are required to apply a series of essential preprocessing steps, along with any additional optional techniques they deem beneficial to improve the performance of their model. Once the preprocessing is complete, students should construct and train a neural network that is capable of solving the intrusion detection problem effectively.

Given the constraints of the project, the final model must be optimized for deployment on a microcontroller unit (MCU). Specifically, students will need to focus on minimizing the model's size (i.e., reducing the number of parameters) while maximizing its performance metrics.

Since the target MCU is 8-bit, quantization-aware training must be applied to the model to ensure it is compatible with the hardware constraints. This requirement may influence the model architecture choices.

The trained model will be used to generate predictions for the test set of the CICIoT2023-1M dataset, and these predictions will be submitted for evaluation in the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on a single metric that considers the model's top-1 accuracy, macro F1-score, and number of parameters:
``eval_metric = f(accuracy, f1_score, num_params)``

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, the model's softmax output should be provided. Moreover, we dedicate the second column for the model's number of parameters. The submission file must include a header and follow this specific format:

**id,params,Benign,BruteForce,DDoS,DoS,Mirai,Recon,Spoofing,Web**  
771638,9848,2.05E-06,0.00013006,7.50E-06,2.41E-05,6.80E-05,0.999440134,0.000322356,5.79E-06  
528847,9848,0.40586701,0.002583256,2.68E-06,0.000169664,3.05E-06,0.020567397,0.526029587,0.044777382   
etc.","# Dataset Description

The CICIoT2023-1M dataset is a subset of the [CICIoT2023](https://www.unb.ca/cic/datasets/iotdataset-2023.html) dataset, which is widely used for network intrusion detection tasks. This dataset includes 46 features and 8 distinct classes, with a total of 844103 samples in the training set and 93790 samples in the test set.

The provided files are as follows:
*   **train.csv**: This file contains the training set. It includes 47 columns, where the first 46 columns represent the features and the last column contains the labels.
*   **test.csv**: This file contains the test set. It also has 47 columns, with the first column corresponding to each sample's ID and the remaining 46 columns representing the features. The test set does not include labels.
*   **sample_submission.csv**: This file provides a sample submission in the correct format, showing how the predictions should be structured.",False,True
8747,bci-i-idun-eeg-analysis-challenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the BCI Initiative & IDUN competition!

This challenge invites you to explore the fascinating world of sleep science using real EEG data collected with [IDUN Guardian Earbuds](https://iduntechnologies.com/idun-guardian). The goal is to develop machine learning models that can accurately classify sleep events from EEG data.

Sleep analysis offers unique insights into memory, cognition, and health, with broad implications for neuroscience and neurotechnology. In this challenge, you'll receive EEG data from multiple subjects annotated with sleep-related events markers such as sleep spindles, K-complexes, and REM stage. You goal is to develop a machine learning model that can learn to detect these events in the EEG signal, and that can be used to analyze EEG data from new subjects to gain insights into sleep and health. Whether you're a seasoned ML expert or new to EEG analysis, this is a fantastic opportunity to contribute to neurotechnology while building practical skills in EEG data processing and classification. If you're interested in the intersection between machine learning and neurotech, this is the right challenge for you!

Read more about the BCI Initative (https://bci-i.github.io/) and IDUN (https://iduntechnologies.com/)
----------------------
# Description

**Dataset Overview**

The dataset for this competition contains EEG data from **four subjects**, with the following split:


**Training Set**: Data from three subjects, includes time-series EEG recordings and corresponding event labels.
**Test Set**: Data from one subject, includes time-series EEG recordings, but does not include event labels. Event labels for the test subject are *held out* and used to evaluate model quality.


**Data Collection**

The EEG data was collected using the[ IDUN Guardian Earbuds](https://iduntechnologies.com/idun-guardian) (see also this [whitepaper](https://25500273.fs1.hubspotusercontent-eu1.net/hubfs/25500273/Official%20White%20Paper%20Folder/White%20Paper%20IDUN%20Technologies.pdf)), a wearable EEG device with in-ear dry electrodes. Each subject's data includes continuous EEG signals recorded overnight while the subjects slept. The sampling rate of the data is 250 Hz, with filtering applied between 0.75 and 35 Hz (we refer to this as *raw EEG data* despite this filtering - participants are encouraged to explore additional pre-processing steps). 
The IDUN Guardian Earbuds is a single-channel in-ear EEG device that allows for prolonged monitoring of sleep-related neural activity with minimal disruption for the subjects. For this challenge, a second EEG system, with more recording channels, was used as a ground truth to confirm that events detected in the Guardian signal are correct. We *only* provide data from the IDUN Guardian.
Recordings were analyzed to manually identify the onset of various sleep and wake related neural activity signals (e.g., around REM sleep onset). 


**Task Description**
You will be provided with EEG data segmented into 30-second epochs. Each epoch is aligned to the onset of one sleep event marker (see below). Your goal is to develop a machine learning model that, given a 30s EEG epoch as input can predict the event marker associated with it. 

Note that you're given labels for the three train subjects only. You will need to use these to train an algorithm that can then *transfer* to the test subject, for whom you don't have any supervised data. The aim is to develop models that could work for any new subject without requiring additional training.

*Transfer learning* is a challenging but well-studied topic in machine learning. You can implement some of the algorithms described in the literature or develop your own! There are **7 marker types** so you will need to explore various approaches to multi-class classification while considering data preprocessing strategies for EEG data.


**Data Composition**

**Epochs**: Data is segmented into 30-second epochs. Each epoch corresponds to a specific sleep event (if any), such as sleep spindles, K-complexes, or REM sleep. 
**Labels**: Each epoch has a label which indicates the sleep event associated to it. Labels are provided as ""strings"" 
with the event marker (see below).

**Events (Markers)**

The seven possible sleep events in the dataset are:
```text
    SS: Sleep Spindles
    K: K-Complexes
    REM: Rapid Eye Movements
    Son: Sleep Onset
    Soff: Sleep Offset
    A: Arousals
    MS: Microsleep
```

**Continuous EEG data**: we provide data cut into epochs for convenience. The final submission for this challenge will have to be made using data organized into epochs. However, we also provide the ""un-cut"" data (continuous EEG recording over an entire night and associated markers timestamps). This is intended to be used by participants wishing to apply pre-processing steps to the EEG data that don't work well with data already cut into epochs. We have example code [here](https://github.com/BCI-I/BCII-IDUN-Challenge-tutorials) showing how to organize the continuous EEG data into epochs for fitting ML models and preparing a submission file. 


**Baseline Models and Code**

To help participants get started, we provide:

- Starter code for data exploration, preprocessing guidance, and visualization of the class distributions.
- Baseline models to give you a sense of initial performance, some of the main pitfalls in approaching this challenge and inspire your model development.
- Detailed instructions on how to prepare and format your submission.

Check out the example code [here](https://github.com/BCI-I/BCII-IDUN-Challenge-tutorials).

Feel free to experiment with preprocessing techniques, feature extraction, and model optimization to create an effective classification pipeline.



## Tutorials and other useful materials 
[Competition-specific tutorials](https://github.com/BCI-I/BCII-IDUN-Challenge-tutorials)

- **Useful Resources and Toolboxes**:
  - [MNE Python Library](https://mne.tools/stable/index.html) for comprehensive EEG data analysis tools.
  - [pyRiemann Library](https://pyriemann.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html) for covariance matrix manipulation and classification in Riemannian space.
  - [Analysing EEG Data with MNE Tutorial](https://kel-github.github.io/), covering various aspects of EEG processing.

### Tips for success!
- **Multi-Class Machine Learning Challenge**: Participants will tackle a multi-class classification problem with seven EEG markers, requiring sophisticated strategies for classifying multiple classes effectively.
- **Class Imbalance**: The dataset presents challenges with class imbalances. Participants will be provided with exploratory code in the 'Exploratory Data Analysis' section to visualize and understand these imbalances, crucial for developing effective models.
- **Transfer Learning Opportunity**: This competition involves transfer learning, with models trained on data from two subjects and tested on others, simulating real-world scenarios of model generalization.
- **Challenges of EEG Data Analysis**: EEG data analysis involves handling signal noise, variability, and artifacts. Participants are advised to employ preprocessing methods for data cleaning and enhancement.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation data**
Evaluation is performed on data from the test subject. Participants will receive the test subect's EEG data, but not the event labels associated with it. These will have to be predicted from the EEG data **using ML models trained on data from the training subjects**. 

```text
    Tip - you'll need to train your models on data from some individuals and apply to data from a new individual that was not part of the training set. Explore different methods to improve your model's ability to transfer to new data. This will be crucial to perform well in the challenge!
```

Like the training data, the evaluation EEG data includes raw EEG signal organized in 30s epochs. Each epoch is associated with a single event marker, which the models will need to predict.

Refer to the [tutorials](https://github.com/BCI-I/BCII-IDUN-Challenge-tutorials) for more details about the evaluation data, metric and submission file.


**Metric**
Given the dataset¡¯s class imbalance, this competition uses the Weighted F1-Score as the primary evaluation metric. The Weighted F1-Score emphasizes the importance of both precision and recall across classes, providing a balanced assessment of model performance, especially on underrepresented classes.

## Submission File
You will need to provide a submission `.csv` file. We've provided detailed instructions on how to prepare this data in the tutorials linked above.  Your submission file should have as many rows as there are epochs in the test dataset. Each row should have an `ID` (the epoch's number in the dataset, starting at 0) and a `TARGET`, a string with the marker's name predicted by your model. 

    ID,Marker
    1,REM
    2,K
    3,A
    etc...","# Dataset Description

This data includes EEG recordings from 4 subjects (3 train and 1 test subjects) monitored during a night of slip with hand-annotations for sleep-related markers. The task is to predict the markers from the EEG data. 

We provide the **raw**, not organized into epochs, EEG data (`.mat` files) and labels (`.csv`) files for all three test subject, and only the EEG data for the fourth. For convenience, we also provide data organized in **epochs** (30s chunks of data aligned to each event onset with the associated marker; in `.npy` files). Participants are encouraged to use the raw un-cut data, apply their own de-noising and pre-processing steps to it and then organizing the data into epochs for training the models and preparing the submission. This is not a requirement, if preferred the pre-processed data made available here can be used directly. 
We provide [detailed instructions](https://github.com/BCI-I/BCII-IDUN-Challenge-tutorials)  on how to process raw EEG and labels data into epochs. These should be followed closely to ensure that the data is correctly formatted for submission. 

More **details about the files**:
- `.mat` files: raw EEG data (of shape `1 x n_samples`). One for each subject, 4 in total.
- `.csv` files: raw marker labels. Columns include `Marker` and `Timestamp_samples`. 3 in total (test subject excluded)
- `subject_X.npy`: EEG data organized with 30s epochs. Of shape `n_epochs x epoch_length`. (4 in total)
- `subject_Y.npy`: labels for each epoch. Of shape `n_epochs` with the markers names in it. (3 in total). 

The subjects are: `train=[""S001"", ""S002"", ""S003""]` and `test=[""S004""]`. 

We provide [detailed code](https://github.com/BCI-I/BCII-IDUN-Challenge-tutorials) to load and processed the raw data into epochs as well as to use epochs for training and evaluating ML models and preparing a submission.",False,True
