8749,dsaa-6100-llm-fine-tuning-due-by-12-pm-nov-5,"# Overview

Welcome to the Text Generation Evaluation Challenge! In this competition, participants are tasked with fine-tuning and deploying state-of-the-art language models to generate human-like text content based on the given context.

The world of Natural Language Processing (NLP) has witnessed rapid advancements, especially in the domain of text generation. This competition aims to push the boundaries further, evaluating how closely machine-generated content can resemble human-like textual patterns and fluency.

For newcomers to Kaggle, all content here is written in markdown. If you're transferring information from other platforms like Word or a browser, ensure that the markdown or HTML is cleaned up for an optimal display.


### Goal:
The primary goal of this competition is to harness the capabilities of modern language models and produce text that's as coherent, relevant, and fluent as human-generated content. Participants will be evaluated on the quality of their generated text in comparison to the original content using metrics like the BLEU score and Jaccard similarity.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the quality of the generated text compared to the original content. We primarily use the BLEU score and Jaccard similarity to measure the coherence, relevance, and fluency of the generated content.

## Submission File

For each ID in the test set, you must generate a corresponding text content. The file should contain a header and have the following format:


Id,label
1,""Generated content for ID 1...""
2,""Generated content for ID 2...""
3,""Generated content for ID 3...""
etc.


Please ensure that the generated content is relevant and closely aligned with the context of the given dataset.","# Dataset Description

## Files

* **train_data.csv** - The dataset for training your model.
* **test_data.csv** - The dataset on which you'll make your predictions.

## Columns

* `ID` - A unique identifier for each text entry.
* `Output Content` - The content generated by the model. This is what you'll be predicting in the test set.
* `Input Context` - The context or prompt based on which the content was generated. This might be present in the training data to provide context for the generated content.",False,True
8750,phems-hackathon-early-sepsis-prediction,"# Overview

## For complete Kaggle beginners, we recommend going through these resources: https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/overview

For a quick introduction to the challenge check out the brief video: ![https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4N3yYJ0aWFE](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4N3yYJ0aWFE)

# **Background on Sepsis**

Sepsis is a life-threatening condition that occurs when the body¡¯s response to an infection leads to tissue damage, organ failure, or death. Globally, around 30 million people develop sepsis each year, leading to an estimated 6 million deaths. Among the most vulnerable populations are newborns and children, with an estimated 20 million sepsis cases worldwide occurring in children under 5 years of age (WHO, https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/sepsis ). In Europe, pediatric sepsis is a major concern, particularly in pediatric intensive care units (PICUs), where infection is one of the leading causes of morbidity and mortality in children. 

Although improvements in healthcare have reduced overall child mortality rates in Europe, pediatric sepsis remains a critical issue due to its rapid progression and the difficulty of early diagnosis. The economic impact of sepsis on European healthcare systems is significant as early detection is often missed, leading to higher rates of organ dysfunction, longer hospital stays, and increased mortality. Sepsis in children is also a major driver of PICU admissions, creating a substantial strain on healthcare resources. 

Early detection and timely administration of antibiotics are essential for improving outcomes in pediatric sepsis, as delays in treatment increase mortality rates. Each hour of delayed treatment has been associated with a 4-8% rise in mortality. Despite efforts to improve the clinical definitions and protocols for diagnosing sepsis, the challenge of early detection in pediatric patients persists, particularly in the face of diverse clinical presentations. New data-driven approaches are needed to enhance early detection and treatment, offering the potential to save lives and reduce healthcare costs. 

For more details on sepsis [see this document](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1FnuR8JhFQOberqwLVuYWqgJgx7n2h04d/view?usp=sharing).

----------------------
# Goal of the hackathon

**The primary objective is to develop a machine learning (ML) algorithm capable of early detection of sepsis in pediatric patients using retrospective physiological data**. 

Early detection of sepsis is critical, as timely treatment can significantly improve outcomes and save lives. Participants are tasked with predicting sepsis using a timeseries dataset, with early predictions providing a crucial window for intervention. The early prediction of sepsis is potentially life-saving, and we challenge participants to predict sepsis 6 hours before the clinical prediction of sepsis.

For the purpose of the challenge, sepsis is defined using [Phoenix score.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900966/)

Participants will begin with an exploratory data analysis (EDA) to understand the provided patient dataset and identify the most meaningful variables for predicting sepsis. This dataset includes physiological and clinical variables, mapped according to predefined medical concepts. Based on their analysis, participants will design and implement an open-source algorithm that can automatically assess a patient's risk of sepsis and make a positive or negative prediction at each time interval. 

The challenge winners will be the teams whose forecasting algorithms achieve the highest scores based on the evaluation metrics below. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

**Primary Metric**: The primary evaluation metric is precision-recall AUC.

**Secondary Metrics**: Additional metrics like accuracy, F1-score will also be considered.

**Tiebreaks**: If there is a tie between two or more winning teams, we will use a tiebreaker based on the interpretability of the models, execution time and computational resources needed to run the model.

## Submission File
For each ID/time point in the test set, you must predict a probability for the predicted SepsisLabel. The file should contain a header and the same format as the **SepsisLabel_sample_submission.csv** provided


----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: 2500€

1st Place - 1,250€ 
2nd Place - 750€ 
3rd Place - 500€

In addition to the monetary prizes, the first-place winner will be offered the chance to discuss the impact of the challenge results on the PHEMS project, learning more about the application of data science in the healthcare sector. This aspect of the prize will depend on the quality of the winning algorithm and its applicability to the final project and does not imply any offer of employment or trainee-ship.

Prizes will be announced at an online ceremony after the competition. Date TBD.
----------------------
# Video Tutorial

##This video provides an overview of the kaggle website, a review of the data, and tips for working with the data set.
![](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q1i90nZzuQY&feature=youtu.be)","# Dataset Description

## **General**
The data used in this challenge is sourced from a retrospective registry of pediatric patients from the Pediatric Intensive Care Unit (ICU) of Hospital Sant Joan de D¨¦u. The dataset has been split into two subsets: training data, testing data, and a private test set used for final scoring. Only the training and testing data (without the SepsisLabel) will be made available to participants, while the private test set will remain hidden to ensure fair scoring of the challenge. The available data includes several categories of patient information, provided in different standard tables. 

Data is provided in a time series (long) format, where each row represents data collected at a specific time point during their PICU stay. The data is provided in two parts: **_Training and Testing_**. Both folders contain the same files, labeled **_train.cvs**, or **_test.cvs**, accordingly.

## **Files provided will consist of:**

###**Outcome: SepsisLabel_(train|test).csv**
This file contains the outcome, with the column SepsisLabel (yes - 1, no - 0), determining if a positive or negative sepsis assessment is made for each patient, at every hour. Note that all positive Sepsis Labels have been moved 6 hours before the real first sepsis positive value appears, since the goal is to predict sepsis early. 

**In test set, the SepsisLabel column is omitted. Participants need predict SepsisLabel for each row in the test set**. 
**A sample submission file SepsisLabel_sample_submission.csv** is provided, illustrating how the submission file should be formatted. This file contains two columns 
 - person_id_datetime: person_id and datetime concatenated into a single unique identifier
 - SepsisLabel: Model predictions for each row


###**Predictor Data**
**devices.csv**
This dataset tracks the usage of medical devices during patient PICU episodes. Each record represents a point in time when a specific device was used for a particular patient.
_Columns_:
- visit_occurrence_id: Episode identification
- device_dateti- me_hourly: Time when this device was applied
- person_id: Patient identification
- device: This field indicates the type of medical device used.

**drugexposure.csv**
This file contains the administration of drugs to patients during patient PICU episodes.
_Columns_:
- visit_occurrence_id:  Episode identification
- person_id: Patient identificaiton
- drug_datetime_hourly: Time indicating when the drug was administered, recorded at an hourly granularity.
- drug_concept_id: Name for the drug administered.
- quantity: Amount of the drug administered.
- dose_unit_source_value: The unit of measurement for the drug dosage.
- route_concept_id: The route of administration of the drug.

**measurement_lab.csv, measurement_meds.csv, measurement_observation.csv**
These files contain lab measurements and the administration of drugs to patients during patient PICU episodes.



**observation.csv**
This dataset tracks observations recorded for patients during their PICU stay.
_Columns_:
- visit_occurrence_id:  Episode identification
- person_id: Patient identification
- observation_datetime: Time when the observation was made.
- observation_concept_id: Label describing what the observation pertains to.
- valuefilled: The value or description of the observation.

**person_demographics_episode.csv**
This file contains demographic information
_Columns_:
- visit_occurrence_id:  Episode identification
- person_id: Patient identification
- age_in_months: Patient age in months 
- gender: Gender of the patient

**proceduresoccurrences.csv**
This file contains all the procedures performed to a patient during an episode.
_Columns_:
- visit_occurrence_id:  Episode identification
- person_id: Patient identification
- procedure_datetime_hourly: Time when it was performed
- procedure: Actual procedure performed",False,True
