8760,concon,"# Overview

This [challenge](https://www.continualcausality.org/challenge/) is part of the 3rd [Continual Causality](https://www.continualcausality.org/) Bridge Program at [AAAI 2025](https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-25/) held on 25th and 26th February 2025.

A well-known problem encountered by typical machine learning methods is that confounders only present in the training data prevent the model from **generalizing to unseen data**. If a model is trained on datasets one after another in the **continual setting**, certain task-specific confounders could negatively affect model performance more severely compared to a joint setup. This **¡°continual confounding¡±** problem is represented by **ConCon**, an image dataset based on CLEVR where confounders differ across tasks. It has been shown that joint training on ConCon is less sensitive to confounding compared to the continual learning setup. 

In this challenge, we invite participants to submit solutions to this Kaggle challenge, together with a corresponding 3-page report, for overcoming the challenges posed by continual confounding. All solutions can be submitted **until January 7, 2025**. Note that we also offer an **early deadline on December 6, 2024**. Any entries submitted until this date will be reviewed in time for people to register with the AAAI early registration deadline and to give more time to deal with possible visa issues.

**Leaderboard:** The leaderboard covers submissions for both the strict and disjoint splits of the dataset (even though evaluation is done on the same test set). As both splits come with different assumptions and challenges, don't feel discouraged from submitting your approach just because others seem to perform better in terms of raw numbers. :)


# Challenge Submission

Participants are required to submit a short report (including a link to their code) through the following Google form:
https://forms.gle/vrwmCYT32TTZ7Eor8

If you have any questions, please contact us at info[at]continualcausality[dot]org.


----------------------
# Description

# Dataset Information

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4133893%2F534e507c6bf938cf73300e54ccd6d807%2FScreenshot%202024-11-07%20at%2010.11.12AM.png?generation=1730954501154839&alt=media)

**ConCon** represents a learning task of two classes: **positive and negative**. All data instances are images based on the **CLEVR** framework. A ground truth rule can be used to determine the class affiliation of any image (green star in the attached figure). In order to correctly classify any image, learning this ground truth rule is sufficient. Since ConCon is a continual learning dataset, there are **three tasks** that are learned after one another. Each task is confounded by a **task-specific confounder** (spiral in the attached figure), i.e., a feature that is present in the positive but not in the negative images of that task. The task-specific confounder changes across tasks.

As can be seen in the attached figure, there are two dataset variants. There is the

- **Disjoint** dataset, where task-specific confounders **never appear in other tasks**, and the
- **Strict** dataset, where task-specific confounders **may appear in other tasks** as random features in both positive and negative samples.

We hypothesize that the main challenge of the disjoint dataset will be to identify that the actual ground truth features are not the ones that are easiest to learn. For the strict dataset, storing information from previous tasks such that the ground truth feature can be learned in new tasks appears to be important.

**We accept submissions on either dataset**, i.e., a submission is not required to perform well on both tasks. Submissions proposing solutions for both datasets are allowed but not required.

----------------------
# Goals & Evaluation

In this challenge, the goal is to train a model that learns the ground truth rule and, thus, performs well on unconfounded data. To this end, it must avoid only learning the easy solution that is given by the task-specific confounders. To this end, in addition to the data for the confounded tasks, we publish an unconfounded dataset without labels. The goal is to **maximize the performance on this unconfounded dataset**. Specifically, the accuracy is to be calculated as

$$\text{acc} = \sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\mathbb{I}(\hat{y}_i = y_i)}{n},$$

where *n = 1500* is the number of samples in the unconfounded dataset, ?? is the prediction for sample i, and y? is the ground truth label for sample i. Out of these 1500 images, 450 (30%) images are used for evaluation for the public leaderboard and the other 1050 (70%) images are used for evaluation for a private leaderboard.

**Training instructions:** Training should be conducted one step at a time, following the continual learning order given by the dataset. This means a model should first be trained on data from Task 0, then updated using data from Task 1, and after that, updated using data from Task 2. Data used to train and update the model may not simply be accumulated fully, however using a small amount of memory as a memory buffer is in principle permitted. The size of the buffer should be justified and generally as small as possible.

---
## Submission Instructions

Submissions should propose an approach that helps overcome continual confounding. Their success in doing so can be measured by their performance on the confounded tasks and, more importantly, the unconfounded dataset.

For the submission to be presented at the bridge, participants should write a 3-page report (excluding references, acknowledgments, and an optional appendix including figures) detailing their approach and reporting the results on the public dataset. Submission will be reviewed in a single blind review process (author names included, reviewer names anonymous). In addition, a repository of the code used to train the model should be included as part of this submission.

There is an **early deadline on December 6, 2024 11:59pm (Anywhere on Earth, AoE)** and the **final deadline on January 7, 2025 11:59pm (Anywhere on Earth, AoE)**. Entries submitted until the early deadline will be reviewed in time for people to register with the early registration and to give more time to deal with possible visa issues.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set (filename without extension in `unconf/test/images/t0`), you must predict the correct label for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    image_1,0
    image_2,0
    image_3,1
    etc.
----------------------
# Bridge Inclusion Criteria

We accept submissions that propose an effective or innovative strategy for overcoming continual confounding. All accepted submissions get the **possibility to present a poster** at the bridge. However, this participation is not mandatory. 

Out of all accepted submissions, we will highlight a select subset of papers, inviting them for **long presentation in person at the bridge**. Papers can either be highlighted based on their effectiveness of the proposed strategy on the unconfounded dataset or for their innovativeness and novelty in methodology. Additionally, highlighted papers will get a **free Tutorial/Lab/Bridge Program Only Registration**.

----------------------
# FAQ

**Where can I get more information on ConCon and continual confounding?**
The paper ¡°Where is the Truth? The Risk of Getting Confounded in a Continual World¡± introduces ConCon and discusses continual confounding in detail. There, you can get more information and discussions of the problems posed by this challenge. You can find it on [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.06434).

**What is the difference between the disjoint and the strict scenario?**
In the disjoint dataset, task-specific confounders never appear in other tasks, and in the strict dataset, task-specific confounders may appear in other tasks as random features in both positive and negative samples. Note that this means that the disjunction of all confounding features is a valid solution for solving all confounded tasks in the disjoint case but not in the strict case.

**Does my method need to work for both disjoint and strict scenarios?**
No, it is sufficient if a submission only considers one scenario. Of course, it is also allowed to propose solutions that perform well on both.

**How should I submit?** Please submit a 3-page report on Google Forms, along with a link to your code repository if you wish to present your challenge submission at the bridge. 

**What should I evaluate?** The Kaggle competition measures the performance on the unconfounded dataset. In the report, please also include model performance on all confounded task for transparency. 

**How should I train the model?** First, choose a scenario (strict or disjoint) on which to run the experiments. Then, the model should be trained in a Continual Learning setting, i.e., starting by training on Task 0, then on Task 1, and, lastly, on Task 2. The datasets should not be accumulated, but using a small number of samples is permitted (for example, for replay techniques).

**Can we use pre-trained weights?** No, using pre-trained weights is not allowed. The model should be trained from scratch using only the ConCon dataset.","# Dataset Description

# Concon Dataset Structure

Concon contains three image datasets¡ª`unconf`, `disjoint`, and `strict`¡ªdesigned for training, validation, and testing machine learning models. Each dataset is structured with folders for `train`, `test`, and `val` sets (where applicable) and contains PNG images organized by tasks.

## Overview of Datasets

### unconf
The `unconf` dataset is *unconfounded* and generated using only the ground truth rule. Across all tasks, this rule is present in positive images and absent in negative images. This dataset is intended to provide models with data that is free of any confounding factors for accurate testing. It is structured as follows:

- `test`: Contains images for testing, structured with tasks, such as:
  - `images/t0`: Images for testing under task `t0`.

### disjoint
The `disjoint` dataset is a *confounded* dataset split into training, validation, and testing sets. In this dataset, **confounders from any task do not reappear across tasks**. This means that the confounders are fully separated, making it useful for evaluating model performance when trained on data with distinctive confounding factors across tasks.

- `train`: Contains images for training, divided into tasks:
  - `images/t0`: Images for task `t0`, further divided into `0`, `1`, etc., subdirectories.
  - `images/t1`: Images for task `t1`.
  - `images/t2`: Images for task `t2`.
- `test` and `val`: Follow the same structure as `train`, with tasks `t0`, `t1`, etc.

### strict
The `strict` dataset is another *confounded* dataset, following the same structure as `disjoint`. Here, **only the specific confounders are avoided in negative samples for each respective task**, creating a stricter confounding setup. This setup allows for a nuanced test of model performance when some but not all confounding factors are present.

- `train`, `test`, and `val`: Each contains images for tasks (`t0`, `t1`, etc.), following the same format as `disjoint`.

---

## Folder Structure

Below is the structure of the datasets, illustrating the organization of subdirectories within each dataset.

```plaintext
root_directory/
©À©¤©¤ unconf
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ test
©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ images
©¦           ©¸©¤©¤ t0
©À©¤©¤ disjoint
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ train
©¦   ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ images
©¦   ©¦       ©À©¤©¤ t0
©¦   ©¦       ©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 0
©¦   ©¦       ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ 1
©¦   ©¦       ©À©¤©¤ t1
©¦   ©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ t2
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ test
©¦   ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ images
©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ val
©¦       ©¸©¤©¤ images
©¸©¤©¤ strict
    ©À©¤©¤ train
    ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ images
    ©À©¤©¤ test
    ©¦   ©¸©¤©¤ images
    ©¸©¤©¤ val
        ©¸©¤©¤ images
```


### Folder Descriptions

- **`unconf/test/images/t0`**: Contains PNG images for unconfounded testing under `t0`.

- **`disjoint/train/images`**: Holds confounded images for training, with further subdivision by tasks (`t0`, `t1`, `t2`). Within `t0`, subdirectories `0` and `1` may represent different categories or splits within task `t0`.

- **`disjoint/test/images` and `disjoint/val/images`**: Structured similarly to `train/images`, containing confounded test and validation images divided by tasks.

- **`strict/train`, `strict/test`, and `strict/val`**: Follow the same format as `disjoint`, with separate images for each task to simulate confounded conditions.


## Additional Details on Dataset Design

- **unconf (Unconfounded)**:
  - This dataset contains only the ground truth rule for each task.
  - The rule appears in positive images and is absent in negative images.
  - These images are used for testing the trained models.

- **disjoint (Confounded)**:
  - Confounders are completely disjoint across tasks, meaning no confounders reappear between tasks.
  - Suitable for training models where confounding effects are separated by tasks.
  - All positive images `(subdirectory 1)` adhere to the groud truth rule as well as have the `disjoint` property and negative images `(subdirectory 0)` do not.

- **strict (Confounded)**:
  - Confounders specific to each task are avoided in the negative samples only for that task.
  - Ideal for testing models under stricter confounding conditions.
  - All positive images `(subdirectory 1)` adhere to the groud truth rule as well as have the `strict` property and negative images `(subdirectory 0)` do not.

## File Types

All images are stored in **PNG format**. Each task folder contains images relevant to specific tasks, enabling model testing on task-specific data under different configurations (unconfounded, confounded).

---

### Usage Instructions

1. **Loading the Datasets**:
   - Use `unconf` for testing models on unconfounded data.
   - Use `disjoint` and `strict` for training and testing models on confounded data.

2. **Navigating Tasks**:
   - To begin with, either select `disjoint` or `strict` variant of the dataset.  
   - Train a model on `t0` &#10230; `t1` &#10230; `t2` i.e. in a sequential manner. 
   - Each task folder (e.g., `t0`, `t1`, `t2`) represents a different subset of data. Subfolders like `0` and `1` within each task represent negative and positive labels.

---

This dataset organization is designed to support comprehensive experiments on unconfounded and confounded datasets, providing a range of task-specific data under conditions where confounders change over time to facilitate testing machine learning models.


### License 
The dataset is made available under the BSD 2-Clause ""Simplified"" License (see https://opensource.org/license/bsd-2-clause)",False,True
8761,conquer-the-seas-of-commerce,"# Overview

Captain Luffy and the Straw Hat crew sail the Grand Line, discovering stores influenced by the ""**Sales Surge**,"" predicting success based on store size, employee count, and economy. Franky builds a machine to predict which stores bring the most treasure, competing with rival crews for rare treasures that bring them closer to the One Piece.
----------------------
# Description

In a world where seas are ruled by mighty pirates and mysterious powers, **Captain Luffy** and the **Straw Hat** crew sail from island to island on their quest to find the One Piece. But in this special adventure, the crew discovers a chain of unique stores spread across the Grand Line¡ªeach store holding clues to navigating the treacherous waters to the next piece of the map.

### **The Store Saga Begins**
As the Going Merry docks at Merchant's Archipelago, a bustling haven of traders and storekeepers, Nami's eyes sparkle with opportunity. The crew is welcomed by the Grand Merchant, who explains that these stores aren't ordinary. They're enchanted with a mysterious force called the ""Sales Surge,"" which predicts their prosperity based on the intricate dance of variables influencing their success. Luffy, driven by both hunger and curiosity, decides they must unlock these secrets to secure their supplies and gold for future voyages.

### **The Hidden Variables** 
Visit Data Tab

### **The Grand Prediction** 
Franky, ever the shipwright and innovator, is tasked with constructing a model¡ªa machine powered by cola and ingenuity. It would use the logbook data to predict which store would bring in the most treasure. The higher the R2 score, the closer their predictions matched reality, ensuring their strategy was top-notch. ""**Think of it as bounty hunting**,"" says Usopp, ""**but instead of pirates, we're targeting numbers!**"" Chopper nods, furrowing his little brows as he examines local economic scrolls and the stores' customer satisfaction scores.

### **The Competitive Edge**
But there¡¯s a twist¡ªother pirate crews are interested in this game. The Heart Pirates, the Kid Pirates, and even Big Mom¡¯s fleet send their best minds (and swords) to decode the secrets. Each team hustles to perfect their models, analyzing the stores' DiscountRate and SeasonalEvents scores while keeping a watchful eye on competitive stores nearby. ""**Only one crew will master the sales surge and claim the map¡¯s next clue**,"" Law's voice booms across
the plaza.

### **Victory and New Horizons** 
With everyone working day and night, predictions are tested, evaluated, and refined. The Straw Hat crew, guided by the results of Franky's machine, make their final guesses. When the results are revealed, the R2 score lights up like a beacon, signaling the accuracy of each crew's predictions. The winner isn't just crowned the Sales Champion of the Grand Line but receives a rare treasure: a key to a secret market said to house legendary items. The Straw Hats sail on, pockets heavier, and hearts full, ready for the next adventure on their path to finding the One Piece.


----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)

Submissions are scored on the root mean squared error. RMSE is defined as:

$$
\text{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}
$$

Where:

- `?_i` is the predicted value,
- `y_i` is the actual value for each instance `i`,
- `N` is the total number of instances.","# Dataset Description

? **StoreID**: The unique code, like a Jolly Roger, identifying each store.
