8762,gesture-recognition,"# Overview

Gestures play an important role in identifying the early symptoms of a lot of diseases such as Parkinson¡¯s, cerebral palsy and stroke. To identify these symptoms using a machine learning/deep learning model, one should have accurate and clear data.


The main goal of this project  to identify the gestures of the user using a simple deep learning model. The data will be collected  from the class using their phones through apps like Sensor Recorder (IOS) and Sensor Logger (Android). The data collected from these apps will be in a CSV format and preprocessed to be fed into the deep learning model to identify the gestures made.

----------------------
# Description

Gestures, whether they be whole body or minute, can help serve as an early indicator for several neural disorders such as Parkinson¡¯s disease, cerebral palsy or strokes. To simplify and to streamline early detection, developing a machine learning and deep learning model can allow for easier and more consistent gesture recognition. To do this, data was collected from the accelerometer and gyroscope sensor functions off a phone application like Sensor Recorder (IOS) and Sensor Logger (Android) and data collected from these apps will be in a CSV format used to identify the given gestures of a user to apply as data for a simple model

Sensor Logger, accelerometer and gyroscope data was collected from a user for the following gestures(Target classes):

Walking
Running
Waving
Stretching
Squatting
Jumping
Dancing

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Categorical Accuracy, which is given by the equation:


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2079290%2F4894849ad950ac298e8a809f608f0384%2Faccu.png?generation=1730922454578976&alt=media)
---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

  id	Label
54592	waving
51939	walking
5565	waving
24318	dancing
45056	squating
55709	waving
55916	waving",,False,True
8763,itj-labs-workshop-competition-pima-dataset,"# Overview

**Welcome to the PIMA Diabetes Prediction Challenge!**

Diabetes is a growing health concern worldwide, and early detection can be crucial in managing and preventing complications. In this competition, your goal is to predict the likelihood of diabetes onset based on diagnostic measurements from the **PIMA Indian Diabetes Dataset**. This dataset includes important health information, such as age, BMI, blood pressure, and glucose levels, collected from a specific population.

## Why Participate?
This competition is a great way to practice binary classification with structured data and understand the nuances of medical prediction models. You'll gain hands-on experience in:

* **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA):** Understanding feature distributions and correlations that impact diabetes risk.

* **Feature Engineering:** Identifying and creating meaningful features to improve prediction performance.

* **Model Building and Evaluation:** Training, tuning, and evaluating models to maximize accuracy and reliability in medical predictions.

## Dataset Description
The dataset consists of various health-related features, including:

* **Pregnancies:** Number of times the patient was pregnant.

* **Glucose:** Plasma glucose concentration (mg/dL).

* **Blood Pressure:** Diastolic blood pressure (mm Hg).

* **Skin Thickness:** Triceps skinfold thickness (mm).
Insulin: Serum insulin (¦ÌU/mL).

* **BMI:** [CHECK] Body mass index (weight in kg/(height in m)^2).

* **Diabetes Pedigree Function:** A function that scores the likelihood of diabetes based on family history.

* **Age:** Age of the patient in years.

Your task is to use these features to predict the Outcome variable, where `1` indicates diabetes onset, and `0` indicates no diabetes.

## Evaluation
This competition will use the **F1 Score** as the evaluation metric, due to the imbalanced nature of the dataset. The F1 Score helps balance precision and recall, making it ideal for detecting diabetes in a population where the positive class is less common.

## Timeline
* **Start Date:** [Insert Start Date]
* **End Date:** [Insert End Date]

## Rules:
* Only use the provided training dataset for model training.

* Avoid any external data or pre-trained models.

* Collaborate and discuss insights, but keep your final submissions independent.

We hope this competition helps you sharpen your machine learning skills and offers insight into the healthcare domain. Good luck, and happy coding!
----------------------
# Description

The PIMA Diabetes Prediction Challenge is designed to help participants build machine learning models to predict the onset of diabetes based on health diagnostic data. Early detection of diabetes is crucial for effective treatment and prevention of complications. In this challenge, participants will use the **PIMA Indian Diabetes Dataset** to classify patients as either diabetic or non-diabetic.

Your goal is to predict whether a patient is likely to develop diabetes (`Outcome = 1`) or not (`Outcome = 0`) based on a set of diagnostic measurements. This is a **binary classification** problem where you¡¯ll need to apply machine learning techniques to maximize predictive accuracy and model reliability.
----------------------
# Evaluation Metric

This competition will use the **F1 Score** as the primary evaluation metric, due to the **imbalanced nature** of the dataset. The F1 Score is the harmonic mean of **precision** and **recall**, balancing both false positives and false negatives, which is crucial for medical diagnostics.

### F1 Score Formula

$$ F1 = \frac{2 \cdot \text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}} $$

The F1 Score ranges from 0 to 1, where a score closer to 1 indicates a better-performing model.

---

## Submission Requirements

To submit your predictions, upload a CSV file with two columns: `ID` and `Outcome`.

```csv
ID,Outcome
1,0
2,1
3,0
...
```

### Important Notes:
- Your predictions in the `Outcome` column should be either `0` or `1`.
- A sample submission file is provided to guide you in formatting your predictions correctly.","# Dataset Description

This competition is based on the **PIMA Indians Diabetes Database** from the UCI Machine Learning Repository. The original dataset contains medical records of female patients of Pima Indian heritage, with various health diagnostic measurements related to diabetes risk.

The data has been split into training and test sets to allow you to develop and test your models for predicting diabetes onset.

For more information on the original dataset, please refer to the [PIMA Indians Diabetes Database on Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uciml/pima-indians-diabetes-database/).

---

## Files

The dataset provided for this competition includes three files:

- **train.csv** - The training dataset, which includes health diagnostic features along with the target variable `Outcome`. You will use this file to train your model.
- **test.csv** - The test dataset, which includes the same features as `train.csv`, but without the `Outcome` column. Your model should predict the `Outcome` for each row in this file.
- **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file that shows the expected format for your predictions. This file contains two columns:
  - `ID`: A unique identifier for each row in the test set.
  - `Outcome`: Placeholder values (0s) to illustrate the expected format for your predictions.

---

## Column Descriptions

Each file contains the following columns:

- **Pregnancies**: Number of times the patient was pregnant.
- **Glucose**: Plasma glucose concentration over a 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test.
- **BloodPressure**: Diastolic blood pressure (mm Hg).
- **SkinThickness**: Triceps skinfold thickness (mm).
- **Insulin**: 2-hour serum insulin (¦ÌU/mL).
- **BMI**: Body mass index, calculated as weight in kg divided by height in m2.
- **DiabetesPedigreeFunction**: A function that scores the likelihood of diabetes based on family history.
- **Age**: Patient¡¯s age in years.
- **Outcome**: The target variable in `train.csv`, indicating whether the patient has diabetes (`1`) or not (`0`).

In `test.csv`, the `Outcome` column is omitted, as participants are required to predict this value and submit their predictions.

---

## Submission Format

Your submission should be in CSV format, following the structure shown in **sample_submission.csv**. It should contain two columns:

1. **ID**: The unique identifier for each patient in the test set (matching the row index from `test.csv`).
2. **Outcome**: Your model¡¯s prediction for diabetes onset, where:
   - `1` indicates diabetes onset.
   - `0` indicates no diabetes onset.

### Example Submission

```csv
ID,Outcome
1,0
2,1
3,0
...
```

Ensure that your predictions are in this exact format, as incorrect formats may not be evaluated correctly.

---

## Additional Notes

- **Evaluation Metric**: The competition will be scored using the **F1 Score**, a metric that balances precision and recall, which is particularly useful for imbalanced datasets.
- **Dataset Balance**: The `test.csv` file has been carefully balanced to include an equal number of diabetic and non-diabetic cases, ensuring fair evaluation of your model¡¯s performance across both classes.
- **Use Only Provided Data**: Participants are expected to use only the data provided in `train.csv` for training. External data sources are not allowed.

For any questions or clarifications, please refer to the competition¡¯s **Discussion** section. Good luck, and happy coding!",False,True
8764,taxi-trips-2024,"# Overview



----------------------
# Description

Welcome to the Fall 2024 SCOT 530 Kaggle data competition!

Your goal is to predict the number of taxi trips originating during each hour of the year by a major taxicab company/network operating in an unspecified major world city. I've compiled (and cleaned) a dataset covering one actual year's worth of data for you to explore, model, and make predictions. See the Data tab and the Rules tab for more details.

P.S.: I won't tell you just now which city, which year, or which taxicab company/network the dataset comes from (you can ask me after the competition is over :). I recommend you don't spend time trying to identify the source of the dataset - in real life the unknown data is future data that we don't know at the time we perform the analysis.

The purpose of this exercise is to provide you with an opportunity to try out your awesome data analysis / data science - in particular, predictive analytics - on a real-life dataset. Try to be creative, practice some coding, and have fun. 

Here is a handy link to [Kaggle's competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

----------------------
# Evaluation

You'll need to upload a csv file with your predictions on the test set. The submission format must be identical to that of the sample_submission.csv file provided. Kaggle will automatically score the submission for accuracy based on a ""root mean square error"" metric. Kaggle can score your submissions because I have provided it with a hidden ""solutions"" file that contains the true num_trips for each hour in the test set. In fact, the test set hours are randomly divided into two halves: Public and Private. While the competition is live, only the public set is used to calculate the root mean square error metric. That determines the public leaderboard displayed throughout the competition. You can use that to fine tune your approach and compare with other teams. You are allowed two new submission every 24 hour period (following UTC time) between the launch of the competition and its deadline. 

At the deadline you get to choose, or Kaggle chooses, your best submission to date. This is then evaluated on the private test observations to determine the private leaderboard. It is that private leaderboard that determines the competition winners. The purpose of this setup is to minimize the role of luck and random trial and error. 

The competition deadline is Sunday, November 17th, at 11:59 p.m. St. Louis time. The private leaderboard will be published on Monday, November 18th, at noon. The leading teams will discuss their prediction approaches during class that afternoon. When the competition launches, there is one solution already evaluated based on the sample_submission file. This is essentially a random prediction file so it shouldn't be too hard to beat that benchmark!","# Dataset Description

You have access to a train dataset that constitutes a 70% random sample of all hours in the year. Each row corresponds to an hour and each column represents a possibly relevant temporal or weather-related information. Your team can do whatever analysis it deems useful to explore and model this dataset. I recommend using Python on your computer instead of Kaggle's built-in coding ""kernels"".

You also have access to a test dataset that contains the remaining 30% of the hours. In the test set, you have exactly the same columns as the train set except that it doesn't include the number of trips. This is the sample you need to make predictions for.

Ultimately you'll need to upload a csv file with your predictions (see the instructions below and the file). Kaggle will automatically score the submission for accuracy based on a ""root mean square error"" metric. See the evaluation section for more details.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
