8766,medical-note-extraction-h-2-o-gen-ai-world-ny,"# Overview

This competition is a companion event to the **[H2O GenAI World in New York November 21st](https://h2o.ai/events/h2o-genai-world/ny/)**


Dealing with unstructured medical notes presents a major challenge in the medical field. These notes, often written in free text, vary greatly in language, abbreviations, and detail, making it difficult to analyze them systematically. Without a standardized format, extracting meaningful insights across patient records becomes a time-consuming and error-prone process, hindering comprehensive care and research efforts.

If this data were structured, however, it would unlock tremendous potential. By standardizing and structuring information, healthcare providers could leverage advanced analytics, predictive modeling, and AI-driven insights to enhance patient outcomes. Structured data would facilitate smoother information sharing, improve clinical decision-making, and open up new pathways for personalized medicine. The transition from unstructured to structured notes could transform healthcare, enabling faster, data-driven interventions and a deeper understanding of patient populations.

In this competition, you will be working to transform brief synthetically generated medical notes into structured JSON

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1035002%2F64d1008e89109c98975174811c430378%2Fimage.png?generation=1731519211386736&alt=media)

**Prize**
First place winning team gets North Face H2O Jackets ?? and their names announced at the H2O Gen AI New York event
----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition uses a custom metric for evaluation. The code that defines this metric is available here [https://www.kaggle.com/code/ryches/json-metric](https://www.kaggle.com/code/ryches/json-metric). 

This metric will evaluate different aspects of the JSON in specialized ways. The first quality check is verifying that the JSON can be parsed correctly. If a JSON prediction cannot be parsed then the response is given a score of 0. 

If a prediction can be correctly parsed then the elements of the JSON will be evaluated based on their data type in the following ways:

- **Numerical Values (int, float):**
  - Similarity is calculated using an exponential decay function based on the absolute difference:
    Similarity = e<sup>-¦Á ¡Á |a - b|</sup>
  - **Alpha** controls the rate at which similarity decreases as the difference increases.

- **Strings:**
  - Similarity is the proportion of matching characters at the same positions relative to the length of the longer string:
    Similarity = (Number of Matching Characters) / (Length of Longer String)

- **Booleans:**
  - Similarity is 1 if both values are the same, 0 otherwise.

- **NoneType:**
  - Similarity is 1 if both values are `None`, 0 otherwise.

- **Different Data Types:**
  - If the types of `a` and `b` differ, the similarity is 0.

- **Dictionaries:**
  - **Key Similarity:** Calculated as the ratio of shared keys to total unique keys.
  - **Value Similarity:** Recursively computed similarities of the values corresponding to shared keys.
  - **Combined Similarity:** Weighted sum of key similarity and value similarity:
    Similarity = (key_weight ¡Á Key Similarity) + (value_weight ¡Á Value Similarity)

- **Lists:**
  - In this competition, we will be dealing with these as sets because our only list is the list of symptoms, we do not care about the ordering of these, just that all symptoms are present and no false positives are present. 
  - **Set Intersection** - intersection(set(a), set(b))
  - **Max Length** - max(len(set(a), len(set(b)))
  - Similiarity = set_intersection/max_length
  - This metric does not penalize for order and penalizes for missing symptoms or adding symptoms not present in the labels","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - A training CSV containing the notes and target extractions
*   **test.csv** - A test file that provides the ID and notes only
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file with dummy predictions to show the expected format

## Columns

*   `ID` - medical note ID
*   `Note`?- A synthetically generated medical note
*   `json` - Our target variable, the structured information we want to parse from the note

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1035002%2Fba363f32ac89e42d1fb5721f778d0662%2Fimage.png?generation=1731519243348427&alt=media)",False,True
8767,geostatistics-athens-week-2024,"# Description

The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates.

The competition consist in predicting the Cobalt top soil concentration at the validation locations.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean squared error (MSE). The MSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ MSE = \frac1n \sum_{i=1}^n (Z_i-Z^{\star}_i)^2 \]</p>
<p> where Z_i is the actual value, unknown and hosted here, Z_i* is the predicted value and i is the index.</p> 
<p>The MSE measures the accuracy of the prediction. The lower the better!</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p> submission files should contain two columns: the index of the predicted site with name V1 and the predicted cobalt concentration named V2 see </p>
[the sample submission](https://cloud.mines-paristech.fr/index.php/s/n1Vak3TiRvseX0o)","# Dataset Description

<p> The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates. 
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
Two of the provided files will be useful
<ul>
<li><strong>jura_pred.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>jura_val_loc.csv</strong> - validation locations and covariables</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong> Xloc </strong> X coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong> Yloc </strong>Y coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong>Landuse</strong> Land use: 1: Forest, 2: Pasture (Weide(land), Wiese, Grasland), 3: Meadow (Wiese, Flur, Matte, Anger), 4: Tillage (Ackerland, bestelltes Land) </li>
<li><strong>Rock</strong> Rock Types: 1: Argovian, 2: Kimmeridgian, 3: Sequanian, 4: Portlandian, 5: Quaternary. </li>
<li><strong>Cd</strong> mg cadmium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Co</strong> mg cobalt kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cr</strong> mg chromium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cu</strong> mg copper kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Ni</strong> mg nickel kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Pb</strong> mg lead kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Zn</strong> mg zinc kg^-1 topsoil</li>
</ul>",False,True
8768,coolwacv25,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to detect hazards from dashcam videos. The competition comprises of three tasks: Driver State Prediction (has the driver reacted to a hazard yet), Hazard Detection, and Hazard Captioning.
We've assembled and annotated ~200 dashcam videos for use in this competition.

This competition is being held in connection with the COOOL WACV25 Workshop. Please view additional terms and information on our website and GitHub: 

https://sites.google.com/ucr.edu/cooolsworkshop/home
https://github.com/alshami52/COOOL_benchmark

**Based on financial need and challenge performance, we may be able to offer a free WACV25 workshop registration. Please note the winner is not guaranteed a workshop registration, it is not a prize.**

**If you plan to submit a paper which discusses your team's solution to the Kaggle challenge, please cite our paper that introduces the challenge and dataset**

`@article{alshami2024coool,
  title={COOOL: Challenge Of Out-Of-Label A Novel Benchmark for Autonomous Driving},
  author={AlShami, Ali K and Kalita, Ananya and Rabinowitz, Ryan and Lam, Khang and Bezbarua, Rishabh and Boult, Terrance and Kalita, Jugal},
  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.05462},
  year={2024}
}
`
----------------------
# Description

In spite of the rapid expansion and investment into autonomous driving systems, dealing with rare or novel roadway hazards remains fundamental obstacle to the safe deployment of these systems. Foundation models and LLMs have made significant progress in recognizing a wide variety of semantic relations from images. However, utilizing these systems with unseen or rare data distributions (ie images taken from a moving vehicle), remains challenging. Additionally, novel, damaged, and distant/low-resolution hazards present their own challenge, as misclassifying these hazards as a nuisance could prove fatal in deployment.

We've collected ~200 high-quality dashcam videos with a variety of hazards and accompanying roadway objects.
Many videos contain more than one hazard, with some changing over time..
This challenge requires participants to address three tasks:
- When has the driver reacted to a hazard.
- Which bounding box(es) contain hazard(s).
- Describe what the hazard(s) are.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using a results.csv file which participants must upload.
Submission scores will be macro-averaged from three separate accuracies:
- Each frame has an annotation for 'Driver_State_Change', score will be correct_state_change_prediction/total_frames
- The number of present hazards is variable among videos, score will be correctly_predicted_hazards/max(known_hazards, len(predicted_hazards)) PER FRAME summed over all frames
- The number of present hazards is variable among videos, score will be correctly_predicted_hazard_names/max(known_hazard_names, len(predicted_hazards_names)) PER FRAME summed over all frames

Please note, by these metrics, including extra hazard predictions and names will negatively affect your score.
Additionally, please do not pack the caption/Hazard_Name field with dictionaries or illegitimate captions, we will check the top submissions and remove suspected cheaters.

## Submission File
*UPDATE* Please fill empty hazard tracks (where no hazard is predicted) with -1 or a space, and a space for the corresponding hazard name. This update is necessary due to safety checks with the results submission. Do not upload results files with consecutive commas, Kaggle will reject them.

For each row in the submission file, please adhere to the following format:

    ID,Driver_State_Changed,Hazard_Track_0,Hazard_Name_0,Hazard_Track_22,Hazard_Name_22
    video_0001_0,False,-1, ,-1, , 
    video_0001_1,False,-1, ,-1, ,
    video_0001_2,True,0, A dog in the road,-1, ,
    
Please remove any commas from the captions before adding them to the CSV, due to submission file format.
Also, please form the ID column by joining the video name with the frame number(no leading zeros), as in f'{video_name}_{frame}'.
----------------------
# Prizes

The best scoring submission will receive a $500 Amazon Gift Card! The team with the best solution may also be invited to give a talk at our WACV 2025 workshop.

Total Prizes Available: 1
**- 1st Place -?$500 Amazon Gift Card & Block Award**


----------------------
# Baseline

For the convenience of participants, we provide a working baseline using simple methods to address each task. For driving state, we estimate the distance between bounding boxes over frames, and use Linear Regression to detect slow-downs. For Hazard detection, we simply assume the bounding box closest to the center of the image is the hazard and caption them using clip interrogator.

This is a very simple baseline, there is significant room for improvement. For example, computing hazard trajectories, using better captioning models, car speed and orientation detection could all improve results.

We provide the baseline on our GitHub: https://github.com/alshami52/COOOL_benchmark",,False,True
8769,neo-bank-non-sub-churn-prediction,"# Overview

Welcome to the Neo bank!

You have been hired as a data scientist, but the bank has huge problems. The competition gets harder and management has the feeling that churn is becoming a  problem. Some initial experiments with simple behavioral rules have shown that customer behaviour can in fact be predicted, but they require you to do it!

Unfortunately data literacy is not as mature as in other companies, thus even a churn definition does not exist. It is all in your hands now!

It is your goal to create a churn definition, implement it and  predict churn!
----------------------
# Description

Your stakeholders set a very high priority to explainability and do not trust black box modelling, thus it has been decided that you can only deploy a single supervised model architecture: No blending, no stacking. At least the data scientist, whose role you are replacing, has been able to convince the stakeholders that model orchestration is not a black box before he left.

In this competition we expect participants to create a solution that uses a single model architecture per model type only. I.e. you may use Logistic Regression, Xgboost, Catboost or a neural network, but you are not allowed to train more than one model architecture and use more than one model per prediction. However you are allowed to train multiple models if their predictions are not blended and if they are from the same model architecture. Also you are allowed to define a rule system or use unsupervised methods. Model orchestration is allowed in general.


I.e.:

**Blending/Ensembling**
```python
row_1 : avg (Catboost & Xgboost predictions)
row_2 : avg (Catboost & Xgboost predictions) 
```
-> not allowed

```python
row_1 : avg (Xgboost & Xgboost predictions)
row_2 : avg (Xgboost & Xgboost predictions) 
```
-> not allowed

**Single model**
```python
row_1 :Xgboost
row_2 : Xgboost 
```
-> allowed

**Orchestration**
```python
row_1 :Xgboost
row_2 : Catboost 
```
-> not allowed

```python
row_1 : Xgboost model 1
row_2 : Xgboost model 2 
```
-> allowed

You are also allowed to combine manually defined rules with a model, i.e.:

```python
row_1 : rule col_1 * col_2 > 500
row_2 : Xgboost
```
-> allowed

You are also allowed to combine manually defined rules and unsupervised models with a model, i.e.:

```python
row_1 : rule col_1 * col_2 > 500 if outlier_score >= -0.5
row_2 : Xgboost
```
-> allowed

or:

```python
row_1 : Xgboost model 1 (because outlier score decided which model to use)
row_2 : Xgboost model 2 (because outlier score decided which model to use)
```
--> allowed
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submission will be evaluated using the logloss.

Logarithmic loss measures the performance of a classification model where the prediction input is a probability value between 0 and 1. A perfect model would have a log loss of 0. Log loss increases as the predicted probability diverges from the true label:

$$ - \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N [y_{i} \log \, p_{i} + (1 - y_{i}) \log \, (1 - p_{i})].$$

where yi is the label (0 and 1 for binary) and pi is the predicted probability of the data point being 1 for all N points.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the `churn` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,churn
    2,0.5
    5,0.5
    6,0.5
    etc.
----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: $150 of value
- **1st Place** - A free perpetual license for [Mljar studio](https://mljar.com/pricing/) offered by [Piotr P?o¨½ski](https://www.linkedin.com/in/piotr-plonski-mljar/) (worth $99)
- **2nd Place** - [Feature Selection in Machine Learning](https://www.trainindata.com/p/feature-selection-in-machine-learning-book) by [Soledad Galli](https://www.linkedin.com/in/soledad-galli/) (worth $50)
- **3rd Place** - [Interpreting Machine Learning Models with SHAP](https://www.amazon.de/-/en/Interpreting-Machine-Learning-Models-SHAP/dp/B0CHL7W1DL) by [Christoph Molnar](https://www.linkedin.com/in/christoph-molnar/) (worth $35)

Certain criteria have to be fulfilled to be eligible for the prizes. See the criteria in the discussion section under ""This competition has prizes"".",,False,True
