8774,asr-in-wolof,"# Overview

Welcome to the home page of the Wolof ASR Model Kaggle Hackathon! This competition focuses on building an Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) model for Wolof, a low-resource language spoken in Senegal and West Africa. Due to limited available datasets and linguistic resources, creating an accurate ASR model for Wolof presents unique challenges.

Participants should note that there are hardware limitations for this hackathon: GPU resources on Google Colab are limited to the T4 model, and training time is capped at 5 hours per session. These constraints encourage innovative approaches to optimize model performance within these boundaries, enhancing participants' skills in resource-efficient machine learning.

Goal: The goal of this hackathon is to build effective ASR models that can overcome data scarcity and hardware constraints, advancing the field of speech recognition for low-resource languages like Wolof.
----------------------
# Description

Why ASR for Wolof?
Wolof ASR technology has the potential to significantly improve accessibility for Wolof speakers in digital spaces, contributing to language preservation and enhancing inclusivity. This project aligns with the broader goals of expanding language resources in technology, empowering communities, and fostering language diversity.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation Metric: Word Error Rate (WER)

The primary evaluation metric for this competition is **Word Error Rate (WER)**, a widely used measure in Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) to assess the accuracy of transcriptions. WER calculates the difference between the transcribed output of the model and the ground-truth text, providing a straightforward way to quantify transcription accuracy.

#### What is Word Error Rate?
WER is calculated by comparing the predicted words with the actual words in the reference transcription. It accounts for three types of errors:

- **Substitutions**: A word is replaced by a different word.
- **Insertions**: An extra word is added in the transcription.
- **Deletions**: A word from the reference is missing in the transcription.

WER is calculated with the following formula:

$$
\text{WER} = \frac{\text{Substitutions} + \text{Insertions} + \text{Deletions}}{\text{Total Words in Reference}}
$$

The resulting score represents the proportion of words that were incorrect in the transcription. A lower WER score indicates better performance, as it implies fewer errors.

#### Why Use WER for Wolof ASR?
WER is particularly useful in this context because it directly reflects the ability of the ASR model to capture the nuances of spoken Wolof, a low-resource language. Given the limited dataset and hardware constraints, optimizing for WER will encourage participants to develop models that handle linguistic variations and speech patterns in Wolof effectively, even with minimal data.

---


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a sentence in wolof for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    row_id,text
    2,b¨¤yyileen gii Maryiis saa?aa ak i ?o?am ci nim g?n a gaawe !
    5,ku wax ?u ni la r¨¤pp , t?j ci kaso bi !
    6,keneen xool ko  ne kii y?ngootul !
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset

Participants in this competition will work with a Wolof Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) dataset, hosted on Hugging Face. This dataset is designed to enable the development of ASR models capable of transcribing Wolof speech, a valuable step in creating accessible technology for this low-resource language. The dataset is available in two parts:

- **[Training Data](https://huggingface.co/datasets/IndabaxSenegal/asr-wolof-dataset)**: Contains labeled audio samples for model training and validation.
- **[Test Data](https://huggingface.co/datasets/IndabaxSenegal/asr-wolof-dataset-test)**: Contains audio samples for evaluation without transcriptions, as participants will be predicting the transcriptions for this set.

### Dataset Structure

The dataset includes 13,000 samples in the **training set** and 2,000 samples in the **test set**. Each set contains essential metadata, allowing participants to work directly with audio paths and associated information.

### Columns

Both the training and test sets share the following columns, with one exception in the test set as described:

- **text**: The transcription of the audio sample. **This column is only available in the training set.** The test set does not include transcriptions, as these are the targets for model predictions.
- **duration**: The length of each audio file, measured in seconds.
- **file_name**: A unique identifier for each audio file, aiding in data organization and reference.
- **path**: The relative file path to access each audio sample.
- **audio**: Contains the raw audio data, which can be used directly by most machine learning frameworks or preprocessed according to your model¡¯s requirements.

### Prediction Task

Participants will use the test set to generate **text transcriptions** for the provided audio files, and submissions will be evaluated based on **Word Error Rate (WER)**, a metric that measures transcription accuracy.

This structure allows for efficient training, testing, and evaluation, empowering participants to build effective ASR solutions for Wolof.",False,True
8775,loan-default-pred,"# Overview

This competition is an in-class competition for the course, ""Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (Fall 2024)"", in Renmin University of China.

The competition is to predict if a customer will default on a loan.
----------------------
# Description

Assume that you are working for a finance company that lends various types of loans to urban customers. When a customer applies for a loan, the company has to make a decision about whether to accept the loan application or not based on the applicant¡¯s profile and some other details. Two types of risks are associated with the bank¡¯s decision:
- If the applicant is likely to repay the loan, then not approving the loan results in a loss of business to the company.
- If the applicant is not likely to repay the loan, i.e. he/she is likely to default, then approving the loan may lead to a financial loss for the company.

The data set contains information about past loan applicants and whether they ¡®defaulted¡¯ or not. The task is to predict if a person is likely to default, which may be used for taking actions such as denying the loan, reducing the amount of the loan, lending (to risky applicants) at a higher interest rate, etc.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [F1 score] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score#References) between the predicted class and the observed class.


## Submission File
For each ""id"" in the test set, you must predict a value (1 for ""Charged Off"" and 0 for ""Fully Paid"") for the ""loan_status"". The file should contain a header and have the following format:

id,loan_status
213846,0
608415,0
454264,1
1066768,0
882910,0
767054,1
......","# Dataset Description


The data set contains information about past loan applicants and whether they ¡®defaulted¡¯ or not.

## Files

*   **loan_train.csv** - the training set
*   **loan_test.csv** - the test set
*   **loan_sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

| Column | Description |
| --- | --- |
| id | A unique LC assigned ID for the loan listing. |
| member_id | A unique LC assigned ID for the borrower member. |
| loan_amnt | The listed amount of the loan applied for by the borrower. If at some point in time, the credit department reduces the loan amount, then it will be reflected in this value. |
| funded_amnt | The total amount committed to that loan at that point in time. |
| funded_amnt_inv | The total amount committed by investors for that loan at that point in time. |
| term | The number of payments on the loan. Values are in months and can be either 36 or 60. |
| int_rate | Interest Rate on the loan. |
| installment | The monthly payment owed by the borrower if the loan originates. |
| grade | LC assigned loan grade. |
| sub_grade | LC assigned loan subgrade. |
| emp_title | The job title supplied by the Borrower when applying for the loan. |
| emp_length | Employment length in years. Possible values are between 0 and 10 where 0 means less than one year and 10 means ten or more years. |
| home_ownership | The home ownership status provided by the borrower during registration. Our values are: RENT, OWN, MORTGAGE, OTHER. |
| annual_inc | The self-reported annual income provided by the borrower during registration. |
| verification_status | Indicates if income was verified by LC, not verified, or if the income source was verified. |
| issue_d | The month which the loan was funded. |
| loan_status | Current status of the loan. |
| url | URL for the LC page with listing data. |
| desc | Loan description provided by the borrower. |
| purpose | A category provided by the borrower for the loan request. |
| title | The loan title provided by the borrower. |
| zip_code | The first 3 numbers of the zip code provided by the borrower in the loan application. |
| addr_state | The state provided by the borrower in the loan application. |
| dti | A ratio calculated using the borrower's total monthly debt payments on the total debt obligations, excluding mortgage and the requested LC loan, divided by the borrower's self-reported monthly income. |
| delinq_2yrs | The number of 30+ days past-due incidences of delinquency in the borrower's credit file for the past 2 years. |
| earliest_cr_line | The month the borrower's earliest reported credit line was opened. |
| inq_last_6mths | The number of inquiries in past 6 months (excluding auto and mortgage inquiries). |
| mths_since_last_delinq | The number of months since the borrower's last delinquency. |
| mths_since_last_record | The number of months since the last public record. |
| open_acc | The number of open credit lines in the borrower's credit file. |
| pub_rec | Number of derogatory public records. |
| revol_bal | Total credit revolving balance. |
| revol_util | Revolving line utilization rate, or the amount of credit the borrower is using relative to all available revolving credit. |
| total_acc | The total number of credit lines currently in the borrower's credit file. |
| out_prncp | Remaining outstanding principal for total amount funded. |
| out_prncp_inv | Remaining outstanding principal for portion of total amount funded by investors. |
| total_pymnt | Payments received to date for total amount funded. |
| total_pymnt_inv | Payments received to date for portion of total amount funded by investors. |
| total_rec_prncp | Principal received to date. |
| total_rec_int | Interest received to date. |
| total_rec_late_fee | Late fees received to date. |
| recoveries | post charge off gross recovery. |
| collection_recovery_fee | post charge off collection fee. |
| last_pymnt_d | Last month payment was received. |
| last_pymnt_amnt | Last total payment amount received. |
| last_credit_pull_d | The most recent month LC pulled credit for this loan. |
| pub_rec_bankruptcies | Number of public record bankruptcies. |",False,True
8776,london-house-price-prediction-advanced-techniques,"# Overview

# Task Overview

## Problem Statement

The goal of this competition is to predict the future sale price of houses in London based on a range of features that capture the characteristics of the property and the context of the sale. The dataset includes various attributes of each property such as the number of bedrooms, bathrooms, property type, size, geographical location, and energy rating. Additionally, each sale transaction is time-stamped with the month and year of sale, providing an opportunity to incorporate temporal trends into the predictions.

Participants are tasked with developing a machine learning model that can accurately predict the sale price of properties based on these features. The models should take into account not only the static features of each property but also how market conditions and property characteristics evolve over time.

----------------------
# Description


Given the current state of the housing market and the features of each property, participants should:
1. Develop a regression model to predict future house prices based on the provided features.
2. Account for seasonal, regional, and market-specific trends that could affect property prices over time.
3. Handle missing data, outliers, and multicollinearity to improve model performance.
4. Provide insights into which factors most significantly affect house prices in London.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of the model will be evaluated based on the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) between the predicted prices and the actual sale prices. MAE is a metric that measures the average magnitude of errors in a set of predictions, without considering their direction. It gives an idea of how far off the predictions are from the actual values, with lower values indicating better predictive accuracy.","# Dataset Description

London House Price Prediction Dataset
Overview
This dataset contains information about property sales in London, with each entry representing a sale transaction. Each property sale has detailed information such as the full address, geographical location, number of rooms, property type, energy rating, and the sale price. The dataset includes both numeric and categorical features that can be used to predict the sale price of properties based on various factors such as location, property type, and features of the house.

Columns
fullAddress

Type: String
Description: The full address of the property, including street name, city, and postal code.
postcode

Type: String
Description: The postal code of the property.
country

Type: String
Description: The country in which the property is located (e.g., ""England"").
outcode

Type: String
Description: The outward part of the postcode, which typically represents a district or region.
latitude

Type: Float
Description: The geographical latitude of the property.
longitude

Type: Float
Description: The geographical longitude of the property.
bathrooms

Type: Float
Description: The number of bathrooms in the property. Missing values are represented by NaN.
bedrooms

Type: Float
Description: The number of bedrooms in the property.
floorAreaSqM

Type: Float
Description: The floor area of the property in square meters.
livingRooms

Type: Float
Description: The number of living rooms in the property. Missing values are represented by NaN.
tenure

Type: String
Description: The type of ownership of the property, such as ""Freehold"" or ""Leasehold"".
propertyType

Type: String
Description: The type of property, such as ""Flat/Maisonette"" or ""Detached House"".
currentEnergyRating

Type: String
Description: The current energy rating of the property, such as ""A"", ""B"", ""C"", or ""None"" if not available.
sale_month

Type: Integer
Description: The month in which the property was sold (1¨C12).
sale_year

Type: Integer
Description: The year in which the property was sold.
price

Type: Float
Description: The sale price of the property in GBP.",False,True
