8799,fathomnet-2025,"# Overview

Consider marine wildlife monitoring in coastal waters near California. Daily video footage analyzed by a standard ML model shows 2 octopuses, 1 shark, and 10 jellyfish - broad taxonomic categories. But species-level identification could reveal crucial details. Is that an Octopus rubescens, commonly found in this area, or is it an Octopus cyanea usually only observed in warm, tropical waters near Hawai¡¯i? Accurate species classification is essential for understanding ocean ecosystems. 

Hierarchical classification¡ªarchitectures that structure data to capture relationships across taxonomic ranks (e.g., from broad categories like families to specific species)¡ªcan significantly improve classification accuracy, as demonstrated by [recent advances](https://imageomics.github.io/bioclip/) in machine learning. In the field of marine ecology, accurate taxonomic classification is essential for addressing fundamental questions: What species exist in a particular place? What is the ecosystem biodiversity and how does it change over time? Questions like these motivate the focus of our 2025 FathomNet Competition, aiming to push the boundaries of taxonomic accuracy and inspire innovative solutions in this space.
----------------------
# Description

For this competition, we have curated data from the broader [FathomNet Database](https://database.fathomnet.org/fathomnet/#/) image set to tackle hierarchical classification. The training set contains 79 categories of marine animals of varying taxonomic ranks (e.g., family, genus, species), wherein 300 example instances of each category are provided for training. The test set contains the same 79 categories, where roughly 10 example instances of each category are provided for evaluation. The challenge is to develop a model that can accurately classify these taxa and ideally leverage their taxonomic information to do so. Developing these solutions for ocean research will enable scientists to process and explore ocean data more efficiently
Participants should not use other image sets to train their models outside of the Kaggle competition data and the FathomNet Database [website](https://database.fathomnet.org/fathomnet/#/), however they can leverage other types of outside data sources for other aspects of the competition. Pre-trained models can be used for initialization of training (e.g. [BioCLIP](https://imageomics.github.io/bioclip/), [ImageNet](https://www.image-net.org/), or [COCO](https://cocodataset.org/#home) classification or detection models provided by many deep learning packages). Teams should plan on specifying additional data sources and/or pre-trained models used when uploading results.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The final test score is the mean of the hierarchical distances calculated across the test set, where the best average score is 0 and the worst is 12. Read below to see how the hierarchical distance is calculated. 

## Submission Format
Submission format should resemble the following:

| annotation_id | concept_name |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Ophiuroidea |

Where an annotation_id is provided alongside a classification, or concept_name, for each ROI. 

Submissions can be any of the following taxonomic ranks: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family,  genus, and species. These ranks are part of a taxonomic tree, which organizes living things into a branching structure, starting with broad groups and narrowing down to specific species. A visual representation of this can be seen below for a specific animal Abyssocucumis abyssorum,

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F23590972%2Fdc76332649aee257aaf5051e04cfd338%2Ftaxonomic_tree_example.png?generation=1738879903021487&alt=media)

Submissions will be evaluated on taxonomic distance from the ground truth answer. To do so we use the [FathomNet WoRMS Module](https://fathomnet-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api.html#module-fathomnet.api.worms) to populate the full taxonomic name and a reference library of these taxonomic names is stored in the evaluation script. This reference library only considered the 79 categories within the dataset. Each submission mush correspond to one of the original 79 categories to be considered. For example, if the submission concept name is Abyssocucumis abyssorum, the score function will populate a tree like what can be seen in the visual above.

This submission is compared with the ground truth taxonomic name, and a score is given corresponding to the distance between the submission and the ground truth answer along the taxonomic tree. Example scores and visual representations are provided below.

In the following example a score of one is given, and is represented visually via the bolded line. In this case, the submission is Asteronyx of genus rank while the ground truth is Asteronyx loveni, which is at the species rank. The distance between the rank of the ground truth and the submission is 1, so the resulting score is 1. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F23590972%2Fd11b659dae9f99834b04f6aa0e4fea29%2Fsame%20line_example.png?generation=1738879926734632&alt=media)

In the following example a score of six is given, and visually this can be seen in the bolded line. In this case the submission taxon is Ophiacanthidae of family rank while the ground truth is Asteronyx loveni, which is of the species rank. The distance between the rank of the ground truth and the submission is 6, so the resulting score is 6. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F23590972%2Fb1696618c501b7e779278e29cccb7b76%2Fdifferent_rank_example.png?generation=1738879941390101&alt=media)]

## Notes:
- Each submission must be one of the 79 categories that encompasses the dataset. If a submission name is not discoverable within the map of taxonomic names referenced in the evaluation metric, then a maximum score of 12 is assigned. This includes null submissions and misspellings.
- Some classifications have more complex taxonomic ranks such as suborders and infraclasses. For this task, we will only consider the 7 taxonomic rankings: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, and species.
- The ground truth categories vary in taxonomic rank.
- An important note on the test set: the images in the test set are on average smaller than those in the training. For more context on this please review [this](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/fathomnet-2025/discussion/573371) discussion.
----------------------
# Competition Information

This competition is presented as part of the 12th Fine-Grained Visual Categorization (FGVC12) workshop at CVPR 2025 in Nashville, Tennessee.
Please feel free to open an issue on the competition [git repo](https://github.com/fathomnet/fgvc-comp-2025) if you have a question, comment, or problem. We will also respond to threads opened on the competition discussion board here on Kaggle.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **March 3, 2025** - Start Date. 
* **May 26, 2025** - Final Submission Deadline. 

All deadlines are at 5:00 PM PST on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Acknowledgements

The images for this competition have been generously provided by [MBARI](https://www.mbari.org/). Annotations were made possible through the support of [FathomNet](https://fathomnet.org/) and experts from the [MBARI Video Lab](https://doi.org/10.1109/OCEANS.2006.306879). Special acknowledgment is given to Marine Biologist Linda Kuhnz for her invaluable expertise in generating a substantial portion of the groundtruth dataset.
----------------------
# CVPR 2025

This competition is part of the Fine-Grained Visual Categorization [FGVC12](https://sites.google.com/view/fgvc12) workshop at the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)  [Conference](https://cvpr.thecvf.com/Conferences/2024). A panel will review the top submissions for the competition based on the description of the methods provided. From this, a subset of participants may be invited to present their results at the workshop, as a poster presentation. We highly encourage teams to share a public repository with their submission to aid the community in learning from one another¡¯s approach. Attending the workshop is not required to participate in the competition, however only teams that are attending the workshop will be considered to present their work.
There is no cash prize for this competition. PLEASE NOTE: CVPR frequently sells out early, and we cannot guarantee CVPR registration after the competition's end. If you are interested in attending CVPR, please plan ahead.
You can see a list of all FGVC12 competitions [here](https://sites.google.com/view/fgvc12/competitions?authuser=0).
----------------------
# Citation

Laura Chrobak and Kevin Barnard. FathomNet2025. https://kaggle.com/competitions/fathomnet-2025, 2025. Kaggle.",,False,True
8800,vrbbbbbrbr,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use binary classification to predict a patient's smoking status given information about various other health indicators. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the probability for the target variable `smoking`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,smoking
    159256,0.5
    159257,0.5
    159258,0.5
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Smoker Status Prediction using Bio-Signals](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/gauravduttakiit/smoker-status-prediction-using-biosignals) dataset. Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `smoking` is the binary target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the probability of positive `smoking`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
8801,tech-olympiad-2024-bahrain-tamkeen-challenge,"# Overview

 # Who is GDG Manama?

GDG Manama is a community-led group that is part of the global Google Developer Groups (GDG) network, based in Manama, Bahrain. GDG Manama is dedicated to supporting and empowering the local developer community through events, workshops, and meetups. It provides a platform for developers, entrepreneurs, and technology enthusiasts to share knowledge and expertise in software development, cloud computing, AI, machine learning, and more.

Whether you¡¯re a developer, tech entrepreneur, or enthusiast based in Bahrain or nearby, join GDG Manama to connect with like-minded individuals and explore how technology can drive positive change in the world.

# Introduction

Welcome to TechOlympiad 2024, hosted by GDG Manama on Kaggle! This year¡¯s competition offers a unique challenge for participants to build effective machine-learning models to address a critical and timely issue.

Tamkeen is tackling the increasing issue of fraudulent job postings that mislead job seekers and undermine trust in the job market. To address this challenge, Tamkeen is seeking a machine learning solution to effectively detect and filter fake job listings. Participants will develop a binary classification model using a combination of textual, categorical, and Boolean attributes to identify fraudulent postings. The goal is to enhance the safety and transparency of the job market, ensuring that job seekers can trust the listings they encounter. Participants are encouraged to apply advanced machine learning techniques to create an efficient, scalable solution.

To maintain a fair and unbiased competition, all participants will have access to the same dataset and must submit their models for evaluation using standardized metrics. Top performers will be awarded prizes, and their models could contribute to real-world solutions for detecting AI-generated content.

If you¡¯re ready to push your machine learning skills to the next level, join TechOlympiad 2024 by GDG Manama and compete with other talented data scientists from Bahrain and beyond!

# Participant Confirmation:

By participating in this competition, you agree to the following:

1. Abide by the competition rules and guidelines set by GDG Manama and Kaggle.
2. Only use the provided dataset, avoiding external data sources or pre-trained models.
3. Adhere to ethical practices in model building and training, avoiding any form of manipulation or bias.
4. Agree to have your models evaluated for potential use in real-world applications.
5. Consent to having your name and affiliation published in the competition leaderboard and related materials.
6. Refrain from sharing submission code or attempting to hack the competition platform.
7. Acknowledge that competition judges¡¯ decisions are final and not subject to dispute.
8. Agree to indemnify and hold harmless GDG Manama, Kaggle, and sponsors from claims or liabilities arising from your participation in the competition.
----------------------
# Description

GDG Manama, in collaboration with Tamkeen Bahrain, invites you to participate in the TechOlympiad 2024. This competition challenges participants to develop a machine learning solution to detect and filter fraudulent job postings, a growing issue that undermines trust in the job market. With the rise of fake job listings, Tamkeen aims to implement a solution that ensures a safer and more transparent platform for job seekers and employers alike.

Participants will be tasked with creating a binary classification model that can identify fraudulent job postings using a combination of textual, categorical, and Boolean attributes. By addressing this critical challenge, participants will contribute to improving the integrity of the job market in Bahrain, helping protect both job seekers and employers from deceptive practices.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Recall Score**: The **Recall Score** (or Sensitivity) is used to evaluate the model's ability to correctly identify fraudulent job postings. It measures the proportion of actual fraudulent listings that are correctly flagged by the model, with a higher recall indicating better performance. In this competition, the recall score will be computed using a **binary average**, focusing on detecting fraudulent listings (with class 1 as ""fraudulent"" and class 0 as ""genuine"").

A higher recall score is crucial in this challenge, as the goal is to ensure that as many fraudulent job postings as possible are correctly identified and removed, preventing harm to job seekers and maintaining trust in the job market. While recall prioritizes minimizing false negatives (i.e., ensuring that fraudulent posts are not missed), it is important to balance it with precision to avoid overwhelming job seekers with too many false alerts.

The goal is to maximize the recall score, ensuring that the model detects most of the fraudulent job listings while maintaining the efficiency and reliability of the filtering process.

**Awards**: The top performers on the leaderboard, based on recall, will be awarded prizes and recognition for developing effective solutions to combat fraudulent job postings and help create a safer, more transparent job market.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for the **Tamkeen Challenge** is designed to help participants develop a machine learning model for **fraudulent job listing detection**. Tamkeen faces an increasing challenge of fraudulent job postings that mislead job seekers and undermine trust in the job market. The competition invites participants to create a binary classification model capable of detecting fake job listings based on various features, such as textual, categorical, and Boolean attributes.

The dataset consists of the following files:

1. **TrainDataset.csv**:  
   This file contains the training data, where each record corresponds to a job listing with various features, including **textual information** (e.g., job description, job title), **categorical attributes** (e.g., industry, location), and others. Each listing is labeled with a **binary target variable**, indicating whether the job listing is **fraudulent (1)** or **legitimate (0)**.

2. **TestDataset.csv**:  
   This file contains the test data, which is similar in structure to the training data, but it does not include the target labels. Participants will need to predict whether each job listing is fraudulent or legitimate.

3. **SampleSubmission.csv**:  
   This file provides the expected format for submitting predictions. Participants should use this format to submit their predicted labels for the test dataset, with one prediction for each job listing.",False,True
8802,customer-flags-prediction-challenge,"# Overview

# **Customer Flags Prediction Challenge**

*Predicting key customer attributes using activity and balance data.*

---

## **Overview**

Welcome to the **Customer Flags Prediction Challenge**! This competition invites data enthusiasts to delve into real-world customer data and predict essential binary attributes that define customer profiles within our platform. Your mission is to develop models that accurately predict the following customer flags:

- **seeder**
- **virtual_flag**
- **system_flag**
- **cloned_flag**

---

## **The Challenge**

### **Objective**

Develop a machine learning model (or models) to predict the four binary customer flags based on provided customer features and balance data.
---

## **Datasets**

You will work with two primary datasets:

### **1. Customer Features Data**

**Filename**: `customer_features_*.csv`

This dataset contains detailed information about each customer.

**Key Columns:**

- `customer_id`: Unique identifier for each customer.
- `sign_up_date`, `created_at`: Timestamps indicating when the customer signed up and when the account was created.
- `virtual_flag`, `system_flag`, `cloned_flag`: Binary flags indicating specific customer attributes (the target variables).
- `first_trade_time`, `first_offramp_time`, `first_onramp_time`: Timestamps of the customer's first trade, offramp, and onramp activities.
- `gender`, `dob`: Demographic details (may contain missing values).
- `banker_score`: A score representing customer activity levels.
- `seeder`: A binary indicator of whether the customer is a seeder.

### **2. Balance Features Data**

**Filename**: `balance_features_*.csv`

This dataset provides information about the customers' account balances.

**Key Columns:**

- `customer_id`: Unique identifier linking customers to balance information.
- `currency_code`: Numeric code representing the currency type.
- `balance`, `frozen_balance`: Current available and frozen balances for each customer.
- `snapshot_timestamp`: Timestamp when the balance snapshot was taken.

---

## **Evaluation**

Your predictions will be evaluated based on accuracy across all four binary flags. The specific evaluation metric is:

- **Evaluation Metric**: [Macro F1 Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html) for multi-label classification.

---

## **Getting Started**

### **1. Explore the Data**

- Perform exploratory data analysis to understand feature distributions, correlations, and potential data quality issues.
- Identify missing values and consider appropriate imputation methods.

### **2. Feature Engineering**

- Create new features that might enhance model performance (e.g., time since sign-up, age from `dob`).
- Consider encoding categorical variables and normalizing numerical features.

