8803,peptide-lc-retention-time-prediction,"# Overview

Liquid chromatography plays a critical role in mass spectrometry (MS) analysis of bottom-up proteomics. By separating peptides based on their physicochemical properties in the liquid chromatography step, the complexity of the sample presented to the MS instrument is greatly reduced. This reduction means that there is less ionization competition, improved sensitivity for data-dependent or -independent analysis and reduced chimericity in fragmentation spectra (MS2). In addition to these benefits, the retention time measurement itself provides an additional dimension of information to interpret the signals generated by a peptide. However, it is not well understood how the retention mechanism in liquid chromatography exactly works, and this means there is the potential for machine learning to predict the retention times of peptides so they can be compared to experimentally acquired retention times.

Most proteomics experiments use a reverse phase column. In a reverse-phase column, peptides are separated based on their hydrophobicity. The stationary phase contains hydrophobic groups (e.g., C18 chains), which interact with hydrophobic regions of peptides, causing them to be retained on the column longer. Hydrophobic peptides thus elute later, while hydrophilic peptides elute earlier. The retention time can be adjusted by changing the mobile phase composition, usually by increasing the proportion of an organic solvent like acetonitrile, which reduces the hydrophobic interactions and promotes elution.

More information about liquid chromatography can be found [here](https://www.shimadzu.be/service-support/technical-support/liquid-chromatography/overview/overview_of_lc.html).

**Goal:**
Based on the peptide sequence and possible modifications predict the time a peptide is retained in a liquid chromatography column.
----------------------
# Description

You will get two files, one file to train a model and one to make predictions for. Both files contain the columns PeptideSequence and Modifications, however, only the train file contains the RetentionTime. The retention times for the test file are used to determine the leaderboard and are hidden to participants.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submission is evaluated with the [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error) between the predicted and observed retention time.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a float value for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

ID,RetentionTime
35330,0.0
54478,1.0
20826,2.0
77848,3.0
109390,4.0
