# Description

**GDG Manama**, in collaboration with **Zain Bahrain**, invites you to participate in the **TechOlympiad 2024**. This competition challenges participants to develop a machine learning model that can predict air quality and humidity levels, using environmental data from sensors. With climate change increasingly impacting Bahrain, rising pollution and fluctuating humidity levels pose significant threats to both public health and the environment. Zain Bahrain¡¯s initiative seeks to harness the power of AI to forecast these conditions, enabling proactive measures to protect the community and the environment.

Participants will be provided with environmental sensor data, including factors such as gas concentrations, seasonality, and other relevant environmental variables. The goal is to create a model that can accurately predict air quality and humidity levels, contributing to better environmental monitoring and smarter urban planning.

The competition encourages participants to leverage advanced machine learning techniques to design a robust solution that can have a real-world impact on improving environmental and public health outcomes in Bahrain.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**R2 Score**: The R2 (R-squared) score, also known as the coefficient of determination, measures how well the model's predictions align with the actual data. In this competition, the R2 score will assess the model's ability to predict air quality and humidity levels based on environmental factors such as gas concentrations and seasonal variations. The R2 score ranges from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating better model performance. An R2 score of 1 suggests perfect predictions, meaning the model explains all the variability in the data, while a score closer to 0 indicates that the model does not improve much over a simple mean prediction.

The goal of the competition is to maximize the R2 score, ensuring that the model can accurately predict air humidity and quality levels, helping to improve environmental monitoring and public health initiatives. A higher R2 score means the model is able to capture the underlying trends and relationships in the data, making it a more reliable tool for forecasting.

