8813,sdu-inf-376-2024-fall-ner,"# Description

The Named Entity Recognition dataset is a collection of annotated text data that has been tagged with information about named entities such as person names, locations, organizations, and more. Your task in this Kaggle competition will be to create a machine-learning model that can accurately identify and extract named entities from new, unseen text data.

The dataset contains a training set that each with the named entities already labeled. The task is to train a model using the training set and then apply it to the test set to predict the named entities in the unseen text.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of your model will be evaluated using the F1 score, which is a weighted average of precision and recall. Precision is the ratio of correctly predicted positive named entities to the total predicted positive named entities, while recall is the ratio of correctly predicted positive named entities to the total actual positive named entities. The F1 score ranges from 0 to 1, where a score of 1 indicates perfect precision and recall.

To calculate the F1 score, your model's predictions will be compared to the true labels in the test set. Specifically, the F1 score will be calculated separately for each type of named entity (person, location, organization, etc.) and then averaged across all types to produce an overall score.

In addition to the F1 score, the competition will also consider the computational efficiency of your model, as well as the clarity and organization of your code and documentation. Therefore, it is recommended that you include clear and detailed instructions on how to run your code and reproduce your results.","# Dataset Description

Named Entity Recognition (NER) is a common task in natural language processing that involves identifying and categorizing named entities (such as people, locations, and organizations) in text data.

The IOB Tagging system contains tags of the form:
B - {CHUNK_TYPE} ¨C for the word in the Beginning chunk
I - {CHUNK_TYPE} ¨C for words Inside the chunk
O ¨C Outside any chunk
The IOB tags are further classified into the following classes ¨C

The dataset consists English-language sentences, each of which has been manually annotated with the following named entity categories:
 - B-art: Artifacts
 - B-eve: Events
 - B-geo: Geographical locations
 - B-gpe: Geopolitical entities
 - B-nat: Natural phenomena
 - B-org: Organizations
 - B-per: Persons
 - B-tim: Time indicators
 - I-art: Artifacts (continued)
 - I-eve: Events (continued)
 - I-geo: Geographical locations (continued)
 - I-gpe: Geopolitical entities (continued)
 - I-nat: Natural phenomena (continued)
 - I-org: Organizations (continued)
 - I-per: Persons (continued)
 - I-tim: Time indicators (continued)
 - O: Not a named entity


Here are the column descriptions for the provided dataset:
 - lemma: the base form of the word
 - next-lemma: the base form of the next word in the sentence
 - next-next-lemma: the base form of the word two positions ahead in the sentence
 - next-next-pos: the part of speech tag for the word two positions ahead in the sentence
 - next-next-shape: the shape of the word two positions ahead in the sentence (e.g. uppercase, lowercase, digit)
 - next-next-word: the actual word two positions ahead in the sentence
 - next-pos: the part of speech tag for the next word in the sentence
 - next-shape: the shape of the next word in the sentence
 - next-word: the actual next word in the sentence
 - pos: the part of speech tag for the current word
 - prev-iob: the IOB (inside-outside-beginning) tag for the previous word in the sentence
 - prev-lemma: the base form of the previous word in the sentence
 - prev-pos: the part of speech tag for the previous word in the sentence
 - prev-prev-iob: the IOB tag for the word two positions behind in the sentence
 - prev-prev-lemma: the base form of the word two positions behind in the sentence
 - prev-prev-pos: the part of speech tag for the word two positions behind in the sentence
 - prev-prev-shape: the shape of the word two positions behind in the sentence
 - prev-prev-word: the actual word two positions behind in the sentence
 - prev-shape: the shape of the previous word in the sentence
 - prev-word: the actual previous word in the sentence
 - sentence_idx: the index of the sentence the current word belongs to
 - shape: the shape of the current word
 - word: the actual current word in the sentence
 - tag: the named entity tag for the current word (e.g. B-per for beginning of a person's name)

Good luck and have fun!",False,True
8814,get-expertise-in-multi-class-classification,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far. This is our last episode for the Season 3 and we wish you all a Happy New Year! Stay tuned for the new season next year!

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use a multi-class approach to predict the the outcomes of patients with cirrhosis. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the multi-class logarithmic loss. Each `id` in the test set had a single true class label, `Status`. For each `id`, you must submit a set of predicted probabilities for each of the three possible outcomes, e.g., `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D`.

The metric is calculated

$$log loss = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^My_{ij}\log(p_{ij}),$$

where \\(N\\) is the number of rows?in the test set, \\(M\\) is the number of outcomes (i.e., 3),? \\(log\\) is the natural logarithm, \\(y_{ij}\\) is 1 if row \\(i\\) has the ground truth label \\(j\\) and 0 otherwise, and \\(p_{ij}\\) is the predicted probability that observation \\(i\\) belongs to class?\\(j\\).

The submitted probabilities for a given row?are not required to sum to one because they are rescaled prior to being scored (each row is divided by the row sum). In order to avoid the extremes of the log function, predicted probabilities are replaced with \\(max(min(p,1-10^{-15}),10^{-15})\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` row in the test set, you must predict probabilities of the three outcomes `Status_C`, `Status_CL`, and `Status_D` . The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Status_C,Status_CL,Status_D
    7905,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    7906,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    7907,0.628084,0.034788,0.337128
    etc.",,False,True
