8819,foo8d793c01484f40d3a8d16f529a75a0d3,"# Overview

[In Silico Design of 2D and 3D Covalent Organic Frameworks for Methane Storage Applications](https://archive.materialscloud.org/record/2018.0003/v2) introduces a comprehensive database of 69,840 covalent organic frameworks (COFs), each virtually assembled from 666 unique organic linkers and synthesized via four distinct methods. Using grand-canonical Monte Carlo simulations, the study evaluated the methane storage capacity of these frameworks, identifying top-performing materials, outperforming the best methane storage materials currently available.

This competition provides participants with a practical opportunity to explore machine learning by analyzing and modeling a real-world dataset. Participants will apply ML techniques to uncover patterns and develop predictive models for COFs' methane storage capacity. The full dataset is available on [Materials Cloud](https://archive.materialscloud.org/record/2018.0003/v2).

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**, a common metric for regression tasks. This measures the average squared difference between the predicted methane storage capacities (`highUptake_mol`) and the actual values in the test set. Lower values of MSE indicate better model performance.

## Submission File

Your submission must include predictions for the `highUptake_mol` variable for each ID in the test set. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```csv
id,highUptake_mol
1,10.234
2,15.678
3,9.876
...
```

Please ensure that the `id` column in the submission corresponds to the ID from the test dataset, and that the predictions in the `highUptake_mol` column are in the same order. Submissions without the correct format or missing columns will not be accepted.",,False,True
8820,datacation-hackathon,"# Overview

In this Datacation Hackathon we aim to predict customer churn. By using Kaggle notebooks you'll learn and compete using the latest machine learning models. A dataset from the Media industry will allow you to derive insights that would be of most use to your business. Unleash your creativity and explore the possibilities with some examples given below:
?
¡¤?????????Analyzing customer churn and its reasons
¡¤?????????Generating personalized products and loyalty offerings using predictive analytics
¡¤?????????Insights and trend predictions

Besides being able to predict customer churn, think about the reasons for it and how the business can deal with this. What advice can you bring to your hypothetical client? Who is your client? Which features did you create in order to tell a story to the business? 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| **msno**| **is_churn**|
| 9WQJvVyggkLWsp0DLpOljfbKePSZZZeYdQvNOAkQ0bk= | 0 |
| kTCflEFCJk6ahA8ryiGzOBQlhwEOMxuPT7gVPRzoJJ8= |  1 |
| EgBTyrvGcPR2bjlXjPqdSkRzz1NVXVvLLdUaabKBm4I= |  0 |
etc.

Submission has to have 47335 rows plus a header (47336 rows in total). msno needs to match the test_data.csv msno.","# Dataset Description

In this challenge, you are asked to predict whether a user will churn after his/her subscription expires. Specifically, you want to forecast if a user make a new service subscription transaction within 30 days after the current membership expiration date.

The dataset contains information about music streaming service. When users signs up for the service, users can choose to either manual renew or auto-renew the service. Users can actively cancel their membership at any time.

In majority of cases, the subscription length is 30 days and a lot of users re-subscribe every month. The key fields to determine churn/renewal are transaction date, membership expiration date, and is_cancel. Note that the is_cancel field indicates whether a user actively cancels a subscription. Subscription cancellation does not imply the user has churned. A user may cancel service subscription due to change of service plans or other reasons. The criteria of ""churn"" is no new valid service subscription within 30 days after the current membership expires.

The training and the test data are selected from users whose membership expire within a certain month. The train data consists of users whose subscription expires within the month of March 2017, and the test data is with users whose subscription expires within the month of April 2017. This means we are looking at user churn or renewal roughly in the month of March 2017 for train set, and the user churn or renewal roughly in the month of April 2017. Train and test sets are split by transaction date, as well as the public and private leaderboard data.

In this dataset, you can find additional useful information, such as users behaviors or demographic information about members.

## Files

* **train_data.csv** - the training set
* **members.csv** - dataset with information about members
* **transactions.csv** - dataset with information about transactions
* **user_logs.csv** - dataset with logs about user behaviour
* **kaggle_test_data.csv** - the test set

## Columns

**train_data.csv** - the train set, containing the user ids and whether they have churned.

* msno: user id
* is_churn: This is the target variable. Churn is defined as whether the user did not continue the subscription within 30 days of expiration. is_churn = 1 means churn,is_churn = 0 means renewal.

**members.csv** - user information. Note that not every user in the dataset is available.
* msno
* city
* bd: age. Note: this column has outlier values ranging from -7000 to 2015, please use your judgement.
* gender
* registered_via: registration method
* registration_init_time: format %Y%m%d

**transactions.csv** - transactions data until 31/3/2017.
* msno: user id
* payment_method_id: payment method
* payment_plan_days: length of membership plan in days
* plan_list_price: in New Taiwan Dollar (NTD)
* actual_amount_paid: in New Taiwan Dollar (NTD)
* is_auto_renew
* transaction_date: format %Y%m%d
* membership_expire_date: format %Y%m%d
* is_cancel: whether or not the user canceled the membership in this transaction.

**user_logs.csv** - daily user logs describing listening behaviors of a user. Data collected until 31/3/2017.
* msno: user id
* date: format %Y%m%d
* num_25: # of songs played less than 25% of the song length
* num_50: # of songs played between 25% to 50% of the song length
* num_75: # of songs played between 50% to 75% of of the song length
* num_985: # of songs played between 75% to 98.5% of the song length
* num_100: # of songs played over 98.5% of the song length
* num_unq: # of unique songs played
* total_secs: total seconds played

**kaggle_test_data.csv** - the test set with msno for generating predictions
* msno: user id",False,True
8821,asia-data-science-challenge-2025,"# Overview

Spinal cord injury can result in a sudden and substantial loss of function and independence. Predicting long-term recovery from the data available early after the injury has significant value when counseling patients and families and for developing rehabilitation treatment plans. However, this is a challenging problem with people having a wide range of future outcomes. This medical science data challenge, hosted by the [American Spinal Cord Injury Association (ASIA)](http://asia-spinalinjury.org/), seeks to advance performance on this task and invite data scientists into the field of spinal cord injury research.

**Goal:** This challenge aims to **predict walking function** at 6-12 months after traumatic spinal cord injury based on acute phase assessments one week after injury.

**Dual Track:** This competition has a track to predict functional recovery of walking (this track) and recovery of neurologic function measured [which can be found here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/spinal-cord-injury-challenge-impairment-track). Participants can submit to one or both.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, participants will attempt to prognosticate long-term recovery after spinal cord injury by analyzing a subset of the data from the [Sygen multicenter clinical trial](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11805614/). The data used to make the prediction of recovery at 6-12 months will be data available in the first month after spinal cord injury. One piece of data that is commonly used to make such predictions are the [International Standards for Neurological Classification of SCI (ISNCSCI)](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/international-standards-neurological-classification-sci-isncsci-worksheet/). This clinical outcome assessment evaluates motor and sensory impairment across the body and helps localize the neurologic level of the spinal cord injury and grade the severity of that injury. Additional data includes demographic information, comorbidities, surgical interventions, and medical complications.

This data challenge involves two tracks predicting different outcomes. These reflect two constructs within the [World Health Organization International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF)](https://www.who.int/standards/classifications/international-classification-of-functioning-disability-and-health). [The first construct](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/spinal-cord-injury-challenge-impairment-track) is future impairment of body structure and function, assessed through the ISNCSCI scores at 26-52 weeks after SCI. Impairments, such as strength and sensation, are not the same construct as real world function. **This track is to predict the quality of walking** on an 8-point modified Benzel classification, more frequently referred to as the lower extremity subscore of the [modified Japanese Orthopedic Association](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4383381/) score proposed by [Benzel](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1802159/).

Why these outcomes? Essentially all functions of the body are influenced by spinal cord injury and are important to predict, including arm and hand function, bowel and bladder function, respiratory function, blood pressure control, and skin integrity. However, there are no available datasets that capture these rich aspects of function. Our goal with this challenge was to predict both neurological recovery (scored with the ISNCSI) and an example of real world function (walking scored with the Benzel classification).

A representative from the winning team for each track will be invited to present their work at the Data Science precourse at the ASIA Society annual meeting, with travel support available for the representatives.

There are additional open datasets available with data for spinal cord injury. Participants are invited to also use these data if it improves their prediction performance on the Sygen challenge data but need to provide information and links to any data that was used - teams using other datasets not publicly available, including private datasets from their health care system, will not be eligible for invitation to the ASIA Society data science precourse. Any teams with access to the complete Sygen dataset are prohibited from using this data when training their algorithms. Leading teams will be asked to submit a write-up and code to reproduce their solution.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Scoring and test dataset details**: For both tracks, 20% of the Sygen data will be held back as test data and used in the evaluation process. The Kaggle public leaderboard is computed on a subset of the test data for the functional prediction task, so while it will give you a sense of your relative performance it may not exactly match the final evaluation. This prevents overfitting through submissions.

**Functional Prediction**: For prediction of walking performance, which uses an ordinal scale, the Spearman¡¯s rank correlation coefficient will be computed between the predicted and actual scores. This correlation will be the score for the submission.

We provide a template for the submission file in the Data section.",,False,True
