8859,icdar-2025-ColiE_Task1,"# Description

This competition is organized in conjunction with the International Conference on Document Analysis and Recognition (ICDAR 2025).

The ICDAR 2025 Competition on Automatic Classification of Literary Epochs (CoLiE) [https://www.icdar2025.com] aims to advance the field of temporal text analysis by challenging participants to develop automated methods for dating literary texts, that is, automatically predicting the time in which the text was written.



###This competition presents two main tasks: **TASK 1: Literary Epochs Classification** and  **TASK 2: ChronoText Classification.** Each participant can participate in both or only one of them.  Here,  you are competing in TASK 1. To register and compete in [TASK 2](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/icdar-2025-ColiE_Task2/), follow this[ link](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/icdar-2025-ColiE_Task2/).


<mark><b style=""font-size: 1.2em;"">To join the competition, click the ""Join Competition"" button at the top right corner. In addition, please fill in the [registration form](https://forms.gle/TDJ9PHZYDo3mdQUJ9)</b></mark>.
________________________________________________________________________________________
###TASK 1 includes the following subtasks

**Sub-task 1.1:**
 Classification of texts into five literary epochs: (1) Classicism (1660-1798), (2) Romanticism (1798-1837), (3)Victorian Literature (1837-1901), (4) Modernism (1900-1945), (5) Postmodernism (1945-2000), and (6) Contemporary (from 2000).

**Sub-task 1.2:**
Texts are classified into three epoch periods: early (first quarter of the epoch), middle (middle half), and late (final quarter). These periods differ in length between epochs (due to the different lengths of the corresponding epochs).     Also, the epochs of Romanticism,  Victorian Literature,  Modernism, and Postmodernism epochs were divided into three periods, while the Contemporary epoch is divided into only two periods.


We believe this shared task will help advance the field of information retrieval and natural language processing and contribute to a deeper understanding of literary history.

This competition is open to anyone passionate about information retrieval, machine learning, and natural language processing. Whether you are a seasoned expert or a newcomer to the field, we welcome you to participate and extend the boundaries of automated text analysis!

----------------------
# Literary Epochs

There are different ways to classify epochs in literature, and the classification may vary depending on the cultural and historical context. However, one way to classify epochs in literature from 1660 to our days is as follows:

1. Classicism (1660-1798) [1] : Characterized by order, clarity, and rational thought, drawing inspiration from classical antiquity.
2. Romanticism (1798-1837) [2]: Romanticism emphasized emotions, imagination, and the natural world.
3. Victorian era (1837-1901) [3]: Named after Queen Victoria's reign, this era saw a focus on morality, social reform, and a growing interest in science and technology.
4. Modernism (1900-1945) [4]:  Literature during this period often emphasized fragmentation, ambiguity, and experimentation with form and language. 
5. Postmodernism (1945-2000) [5]: Postmodernism rejected the idea of objective truth and grand narratives, and instead emphasized irony, intertextuality, and self-reflexivity.
6. Our days (from 2000) [6]: Contemporary literature is characterized by diverse voices, exploring global themes while embracing hybridity and genre-blurring. 

### References
[1]2009. The Cambridge Companion to English Literature, 1650-1740. Cambridge University Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/CCOL9780521884181
[2] 1993. The Cambridge Companion to British Romanticism. Cambridge University
Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/CCOL0521333555
[3] 1999. The Cambridge Companion to Modernism. Cambridge University Press.
https://doi.org/10.1017/CCOL0521495164
[4] 2000. The Cambridge Companion to the Victorian Novel. Cambridge University
Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/CCOL0521641500
[5] 2004. The Cambridge Companion to Postmodernism. Cambridge University Press.
https://doi.org/10.1017/CCOL0521640520
[6] GUPTA, Suman. Contemporary literature: The basics. Routledge, 2012.?


----------------------
# Evaluation Metrics

###### The evaluation of TASK1 is based on *balanced accuracy* which is defined as follows:

$$
\text{Balanced Accuracy} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{\text{TP}_i}{\text{TP}_i + \text{FN}_i},  \text{where}  \newline
N = \text{Number of classes} \newline
TP_i = \text{True Positives for class } i \newline
FN_i = \text{False Negatives for class } i
$$


###The *final rank* is calculated as a Harmonic Mean of balanced accuracies across Sub-task 1.1 and Sub-task 1.2. :

$$
\text{Final Rank} = \frac{2 \cdot BA_{1.1} \cdot BA_{1.2}}{BA_{1.1} + BA_{1.2}},  \text{where}  \newline
BA_{1.1} = \text{Balanced Accuracy of Sub-task 1.1}, \text{and} \newline
BA_{1.2} = \text{Balanced Accuracy of Sub-task 1.2}
 $$

**In addition, each participant is required to provide a short description of the method.**


## Input and output format specifications
The classifier's input is a chunk (an excerpt) of a book in text format, and its output is a single value (label).  Each chunk is provided as a separate text file with a unique identifier.
Further details are specified in the ""Data"" tab. 


----------------------
# Competition Timeline

- December 17, 2024: Call for participation.
- December 17, 2024: The competition website is live and open to participants. Training and validation
sets together with their labels are available. 
-  April 1, 2025: Test dataset available.
-  April 8, 2025: Deadline for competition participants.
-  April 20, 2025: Submission of methods descriptions.
-  June 30, 2025: Communicate winners to chairs.
-  September 17-21, 2025: Presentation of results at the special session at the ICDAR conference.
----------------------
# Organizing Team

The competition is organized by:
- **Marina Litvak** (marinal@ac.sce.ac.il),
Software Engineering Department,
Shamoon College of Engineering, Beer Sheva,
84100, Israel
-  **Irina Rabaev** (irinar@ac.sce.ac.il),
Software Engineering Department,
Shamoon College of Engineering, Beer Sheva,
84100, Israel
- **Ricardo Campos** (ricardo.campos@ubi.pt),
University of Beira Interior and INESC TEC
Covilh?, Portugal
- **Al¨ªpio Jorge** (amjorge@fc.up.pt)
University of Porto and INESC TEC
Porto, Portugal
- **Adam Jatowt** (adam.jatowt@uibk.ac.at)
University of Innsbruck, 
Innsbruck, Austria
- **Roza Bass** (rozzaba@ac.sce.ac.il),
Software Engineering Department,
Shamoon College of Engineering, Beer Sheva,
84100, Israel
- **Hugo Sousa** (hugo.sousa@fc.up.pt),
University of Porto and INESC TEC
Porto, Portugal","# Dataset Description

##  Dataset description

The participants of this competition are provided with the BookSCE dataset. 

The dataset comprises around 11K book chunks accompanied by labels for subtasks 1.1 and 1.2. 


## Ground truth

To train the models, the participants are provided with training and validation sets consisting of books in the ""txt"" format and the corresponding ground truth files in the ""csv"" format. The ground truth includes the chunk IDs and the corresponding labels.

**Task 1.1: Classification of texts into  literature epochs (epoch_label)**

- Classicism(1660-1798)
- Romanticism (1798-1837)
- Victorian (1837-1901)
- Modernism (1900-1945)
- Postmodernism (1945-2000)
- Contemporary (from 2000)

**Task 1.2:  Classification of texts into  literature subepochs (subepoch_label)**

- Early Classicism (1660-1694)
- Middle Classicism (1695-1763)
- Late Classicism (1764-1798)
- Early Romanticism (1798-1807)
- Middle Romanticism (1808-1827)
- Late Romanticism (1828-1837)
- Early Victorian (1837-1852)
- Middle Victorian (1853-1884)
- Late Victorian (1885-1901)
- Early Modernism (1900-1911)
- Middle Modernism (1912-1934)
- Late Modernism (1935-1945)
- Early Postmodernism (1945-1958)
- Middle Postmodernism (1959-1986)
- Late Postmodernism (1987-2000)
- Early Contemporary (2000-2012)
- Contemporary (2013-present)


## Input and output formats
The classifier's input is a book chunk in text format, and its output is a single value (corresponding label). 
The training and validation sets are released at the beginning of the competition. The test set will be released (without labels) a week before the deadline.


The submission.csv file should have three columns:
`id` - unique identifier for each text chunk (e.g., ""text10.txt"", ""text11.txt"", etc.)
`epoch_label` - literary epoch label prediction (one of six classes described in sub-task 1.1)
`subepoch_label` - sub-epoch label prediction (one of 17 classes described in sub-task 1.2, e.g., `Early Romanticism`, `Middle Victorian`, etc.)

** *As an example of the submission format, we provide 'sample_submission.csv' file*.

## Ranking
The results will be evaluated and ranked based on *balanced accuracy*:

$$
\text{Balanced Accuracy} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{\text{TP}_i}{\text{TP}_i + \text{FN}_i},  \text{where}  \newline
N = \text{Number of classes} \newline
TP_i = \text{True Positives for class } i \newline
FN_i = \text{False Negatives for class } i
$$


The *final rank* is calculated as a Harmonic Mean of balanced accuracies across Sub-task 1.1 and Sub-task 1.2. :

$$
\text{Final Rank} = \frac{2 \cdot BA_{1.1} \cdot BA_{1.2}}{BA_{1.1} + BA_{1.2}},  \text{where}  \newline
BA_{1.1} = \text{Balanced Accuracy of Sub-task 1.1}, \text{and} \newline
BA_{1.2} = \text{Balanced Accuracy of Sub-task 1.2}
 $$

## Files

*   **train.zip** - the training set files 
*   **train.csv** - the training set labels
*   **valid.zip** - the validation set files
*   **valid.csv** - the validation set labels
*   **test.zip** - the test set files (to be released on April 1, 2025)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

**All the files are given in the form of 1000-word chunks.**

## Columns

*   **`id`** - id of a book
*   **`epoch_label`** - literary epoch label prediction (`Romanticism`, `Victorian`, `PostModernism`, `Classicism`, `Modernism`, `Contemporary`)
*   **`subepoch_label`** - sub-epoch label prediction (`Late Romanticism`, `Middle Romanticism`, `Early Romanticism`, `Late Victorian`, `Early PostModernism`, `Late Classicism`, `Early Modernism`, `Middle Victorian`, `Early Victorian`, `Middle PostModernism`, `Late Modernism`, `Middle Modernism`, `Middle Classicism`, `Late PostModernism`, `Contemporary`, `Early Contemporary`)",False,True
8860,data-centric-land-cover-classification-challenge-2,"# Overview

In this competition, you'll develop an AI-based ranking system that ranks the samples based on their **levels of label noise**. Observe that:
1. **the main idea of this competition is to rank the images automatically, and NOT to re-annotate the dataset,**
2. **the ranking should be created using the level of *label noise*, and not the level of image noise.**

To this end, you'll be given a semantic segmentation dataset composed of 5,000 256x256 images and their corresponding (*noisy*) labels. The labels consist of two classes: background (encoded as 0 in the label data) and building (encoded as 1 in the label data).

Success is measured by comparing the submitted ranking with an undisclosed one and calculating the [Kendall Tau score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kendall_rank_correlation_coefficient).

Finally, this challenge is part of the [MVEO workshop](https://mveo.github.io/challenge.html) in conjunction with [BMVC 2024](https://bmvc2024.org/).
----------------------
# Description

In supervised semantic segmentation, obtaining fully accurate and reliable labels is often challenging and costly. Labels are frequently contaminated by noise due to factors such as limited information, human labeling errors, or encoding mistakes. In the real-world, label noise is ubiquitous and can significantly degrade the performance of trained machine learning models. Therefore, it is essential to develop methods capable of automatically identifying noisy samples prior to training, allowing machine learning models to be optimized based on the cleanest and most reliable data available.

This competition invites you to develop AI algorithms that can accurately rank samples based on their levels of label noise. By effectively ranking the data, one can identify and select the most suitable samples for training machine learning models, thus improving both the efficiency and performance of these models.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the [Kendall Tau](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kendall_rank_correlation_coefficient) between the predicted ranking and the undisclosed one.

## Submission File

After generating the ranking of all images (from least to most noisy), you must create a file with a header and the following format:

    id,imageid
    0,1_26_72_418_418.png
    1,2_19_53_0_627.png
    2,2_22_42_627_0.png
    etc.

The `id` (DO NOT use `ID` or `Id`) is **NOT** relevant and is **NOT** used in the evaluation process. The `imageid` column should have the name of images (for example, `1_26_72_418_418.png`) **in order from least to most noisy**.","# Dataset Description

## What should I expect the data format to be?

The dataset consists of 5,000 images, each with 256x256 pixels, associated with their noisy labels. The images and labels are PNGs. The labels consist of two classes: background (encoded as 0 in the label data) and building (encoded as 1 in the label data).

## What am I doing?

In this challenge, you will rank samples based on their levels of label noise. In other words, you'll develop an algorithm that assigns a score to each of the 5,000 training samples based on their level of label noise. This score should then be used to rank the data from least to most noisy.

## Files

Although images and labels are in different folders (explained below), the image and corresponding label have the same name. For example, image `training_patches/0_9_2_418_209.png` has a label with exactly the same name, but in the `training_noisy_labels` folder.

    training_patches/ - image files
    training_noisy_labels/ - image noisy labels",False,True
8861,testing-comp-12313,"# Description

> ####??&nbsp;  Recommended Competition
