----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

<h3>What is a Getting Started competition?</h3>
<p>Getting Started competitions were created by Kaggle data scientists for people who have little to no machine learning background. They are a great place to begin if you are new to data science or just finished a MOOC and want to get involved in Kaggle.</p>
<p>Getting Started competitions are a non-competitive way to get familiar with Kaggle¡¯s platform, learn basic machine learning concepts, and start meeting people in the community. They have no cash prize and are on a rolling timeline.</p>
<h3>How do I create and manage a team?</h3>
<p>When you accept the competition rules, a team will be created for you. You can invite others to your team, accept a merger with another team, and update basic information like team name by going to the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/team"" target=""_blank"">Team</a> page.</p>
<p>We've heard from many Kagglers that teaming up is the best way to learn new skills AND have fun. If you don't have a teammate already, consider asking if anyone wants to team up in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">discussion forum</a>.</p>
<h3>What are Notebooks?</h3>
<p>Kaggle Notebooks is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Notebooks support scripts in Python and R, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. You can visit the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/notebooks"" target=""_blank"">Notebooks</a> tab to view all of the publicly shared code for the Spaceship Titanic competition. For more on how to use Notebooks to learn data science, check out our <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/learn/overview"" target=""&quot;_blank"">Courses</a>!</p>
<h3>Why did my team disappear from the leaderboard?</h3>
<p>To keep with the spirit of getting-started competitions, we have implemented a two month rolling window on submissions. Once a submission is more than two months old, it will be invalidated and no longer count towards the leaderboard.</p>
<p>If your team has no submissions in the previous two months, the team will also drop from the leaderboard. This will keep the leaderboard at a manageable size, freshen it up, and prevent newcomers from getting lost in a sea of abandoned scores.</p>
<p><i>""I worked so hard to get that score! Give it back!""</i> Read more about our decision to implement a rolling leaderboard <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion/6240"" target=""_blank"">here</a>.</p>
<h3>How do I contact Support?</h3>
<p>Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team so you¡¯ll typically find that you receive a response more quickly by asking your question in the appropriate forum. (For this competition, you¡¯ll want to use the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/spaceship-titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">Spaceship Titanic discussion forum</a>).</p>
<p>Support is only able to help with issues that are being experienced by all participants. Before contacting support, please check the discussion forum for information on your problem. If you can¡¯t find it, you can post your problem in the forum so a fellow participant or a Kaggle team member can provide help. The forums are full of useful information on the data, metric, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you'll find that others will share a lot in turn!</p>
<p>If your problem persists or it seems to be effective all participants then please <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact"" target=""_blank"">contact us</a>.</p>","# Dataset Description

In this competition your task is to predict whether a passenger was transported to an alternate dimension during the *Spaceship Titanic*'s collision with the spacetime anomaly. To help you make these predictions, you're given a set of personal records recovered from the ship's damaged computer system.

# File and Data Field Descriptions #

- **train.csv** - Personal records for about two-thirds (~8700) of the passengers, to be used as training data.
    - `PassengerId` - A unique Id for each passenger. Each Id takes the form `gggg_pp` where `gggg` indicates a group the passenger is travelling with and `pp` is their number within the group. People in a group are often family members, but not always.
    - `HomePlanet` - The planet the passenger departed from, typically their planet of permanent residence.
    - `CryoSleep` - Indicates whether the passenger elected to be put into suspended animation for the duration of the voyage. Passengers in cryosleep are confined to their cabins.
    - `Cabin` - The cabin number where the passenger is staying. Takes the form `deck/num/side`, where `side` can be either `P` for *Port* or `S` for *Starboard*.
    - `Destination` - The planet the passenger will be debarking to.
    - `Age` - The age of the passenger.
    - `VIP` - Whether the passenger has paid for special VIP service during the voyage.
    - `RoomService`, `FoodCourt`, `ShoppingMall`, `Spa`, `VRDeck` - Amount the passenger has billed at each of the *Spaceship Titanic*'s many luxury amenities.
    - `Name` - The first and last names of the passenger.
    - `Transported` - Whether the passenger was transported to another dimension. This is the target, the column you are trying to predict.
- **test.csv** - Personal records for the remaining one-third (~4300) of the passengers, to be used as test data. Your task is to predict the value of `Transported` for the passengers in this set. 
- **sample_submission.csv** - A submission file in the correct format.
    - `PassengerId` - Id for each passenger in the test set.
    - `Transported` - The target. For each passenger, predict either `True` or `False`.",False,True
8862,icip-aes-2024-autumn-classification,"# Overview

Automated Essay Scoring (AES) stands as a pivotal application of natural language processing within the realm of education. In this contest, we provide linguistic features and a part of essay scores extracted from the public essay dataset of the Automated Student Assessment Prize (ASAP) [HERE](https://www.kaggle.com/c/asap-aes/overview), and build a training set and a test set. 

Contestants can use these features, combined with feature engineering and machine learning algorithms, to predict essay scores. 

Finally, we will calculate the **R-squared (R2)**, also known as the coefficient of determination, between your predicted scores and expert assigned scores to measure the performance of your solution.

***In accordance with the requirements of the original ASAP competition, we do not provide any specific essay content in our datasets. Only the values of the linguistic features given by our self-designed algorithm are provided.***
----------------------
# Description

In this competition, we provide five categories of features:

1. **Dataset Characteristics**

   - **Essay ID**: Randomly generated strings composed of characters and digits to distinguish different essays.
   - **Genre**: The specific genre or style guidelines the essay should adhere to.

2. **TasK Fulfilment**
   - **TasK Fulfilment**: to what extent does the essay meet the requirements specified in the prompt (`barely`, `partially`, `mostly`, `fully`)

3. **Count Information**

- **Word Level:**
   - **Number of Words in Essay**: The total word count of the essay, irrespective of word type.
   - **Number of Words of Different Parts of Speech**: The count of words categorized by parts of speech, such as content words (including nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, and pronouns) and functional words (including prepositions, determiners, and conjunctions), as well as special tokens such as punctuation marks and symbols like the dollar sign(`$`).
- **Sentence Level:**
   - **Number of Sentences in Essay**: The total number of sentences in the essay.
   - **Mean Sentence Length**: Calculated by dividing the total number of words in the essay by the number of sentences.

3. **Lexical Features**

   - **Mean Word Length**: the average number of characters in all words within an essay.
   - **bigram_lemma_ttr & trigram_lemma_ttr**: Number of unique bigram and trigram lemmas divided by the total number of bigram and trigram lemmas, respectively.

---

In the context of student essay evaluation, ""TTR"" stands for **Type-Token Ratio**. This is a measure used in linguistics to assess the lexical diversity of a text. The TTR is calculated by dividing the number of unique word types (distinct words) by the total number of words (tokens) in the text.

**Formula:**
$$
   \text{TTR} = \frac{\text{Number of Unique Word Types}}{\text{Total Number of Words}}
$$

### Importance of TTR in Essay Evaluation:
- **Lexical Variety**: A higher TTR indicates a greater variety of vocabulary used in the essay, which is often associated with a higher level of language proficiency.
- **Essay Complexity**: Essays with a higher lexical diversity might also indicate more complex thoughts and a deeper understanding of the essay topic.

### Limitations:
- **Text Length Sensitivity**: TTR tends to be higher in shorter texts and decreases as text length increases. This is because, in longer texts, the likelihood of using the same words multiple times increases.
- **Contextual Variation**: Different types of essays or topics might naturally lead to variations in TTR. For instance, technical or specialized essays might have a lower TTR due to the repeated use of specific terminology.

---

4. **Syntactical Features**

   - **Max Syntax Tree Depth**: The maximum depth of the syntax parse trees across sentences in the essay.
   - **Mean Syntax Tree Depth**: The mean depth of the syntax parse trees across sentences in the essay.
   - **Syntax Tree Depth Variation Level**: A binary variable indicating whether the standard deviation of the syntax tree depths across sentences in an essay is relatively small or large.

5. **Error Features**

   - **error_density**: The number of errors divided by the length of the essay, indicating the proportion of errors within the text.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# R-squared (R2) Score Explanation

R-squared (R2) is a statistical measure that represents the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that is predictable from the independent variable(s). It ranges from 0 to 1, where:

- A score of 1.0 (100%) indicates perfect prediction
- A score of 0.0 (0%) indicates the model fails to predict better than a horizontal line

For example:
- R2 = 0.8 means your model explains 80% of the variability in the data
- R2 = 0.3 means your model explains only 30% of the variability

In this essay scoring competition, a higher R2 score means your model better predicts the expert-assigned scores. For instance, an R2 of 0.6 would indicate that your model captures 60% of the patterns in how experts grade essays.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **asap-train-2024autumn.csv** - the training set
*   **asap-test-2024autumn.csv** - the test set (without `score`)
*   **asap-test-submission-sample-2024autumn.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Essay Features Explanation (Columns in the Datasets)

- **essay_id**: A unique 7-character identifier for each essay
- **genre**: Essay type - either source-based or argumentative writing
- **sent_num**: Total number of sentences in the essay
- **max_depth**: Maximum depth of syntactic parse trees in the essay
- **mean_depth**: Average depth of syntactic parse trees across sentences
- **num_word**: Total word count in the essay
- **num_unique_word**: Number of distinct words used
- **num_noun**: Total count of nouns
- **num_unique_noun**: Number of distinct nouns used
- **num_verb**: Total count of verbs 
- **num_unique_verb**: Number of distinct verbs used
- **num_adj**: Total count of adjectives
- **num_unique_adj**: Number of distinct adjectives used
- **num_adv**: Total count of adverbs
- **num_unique_adv**: Number of distinct adverbs used
- **num_pron**: Total count of pronouns
- **num_unique_pron**: Number of distinct pronouns used
- **num_special_token**: Total count of special tokens (punctuation etc.)
- **num_unique_special_token**: Number of distinct special tokens used
- **num_unique_lemma**: Number of distinct word lemmas (base forms)
- **mean_word_length**: Average length of words in characters
- **mean_sent_length**: Average number of words per sentence
- **all_error_num**: Total number of language errors detected
- **all_error_length**: Total length of error spans in characters
- **bigram_lemma_ttr**: Type-token ratio for two-word lemma sequences
- **trigram_lemma_ttr**: Type-token ratio for three-word lemma sequences
- **task_fulfillment**: Degree of meeting task requirements (barely/partially/mostly/fully)
- **depth_variation_level**: Variation in syntactic complexity (low/medium/high)
- **error_density**: Ratio of errors to total word count

## Submission Note
Please ensure that the dataset includes two columns of information: `essay_id` and `score`. Additionally, the `score` column should be of float type. Just like we did in **asap-test-submission-sample-2024autumn.csv**.",False,True
8863,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-c-66,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.
----------------------
# FAQs

### How do I contact support?

For this competition, any questions, concerns, or technical difficulties will be addressed at the Discussion forum on UpGrad Learn platform. Any discussion posted regarding support on this competition page will have to be redirected to the same portal.

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

The Kaggle leaderboard has a public and private component to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

**Public Leaderboard:** For all participants, the same 70% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the public leaderboard. The score you see on the public leaderboard reflects your model¡¯s accuracy on this portion of the test set.

**Private Leaderboard:** The other 30% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the private leaderboard. The private leaderboard is not visible to participants until the competition has concluded. At the end of a competition, we will reveal the private leaderboard so you can see your score on the other 30% of the test data. The scores on the private leaderboard are used to determine the competition winners.

### What are kernels?

Kaggle Kernels is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels support scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the Kernels tab to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
8864,tnm-112-lab-2-2024,"# Overview

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F17779492%2F31e532b5c6b81d1da9b31c106debcacb%2Fpcam.jpg?generation=1700166771794534&alt=media)
The PCam (PatchCamelyon) medical image classification is a challenging task focused on the analysis of histopathologic scans of lymph node sections. The primary objective is to develop an automated method to detect metastatic tissue in these scans, which is a critical task in the diagnosis of breast cancer.

Participants are provided with a large dataset of small patches extracted from digital scans of lymph node sections. Each patch is labeled as either containing metastatic tissue or not. The challenge involves creating a model that can accurately classify these patches. This task is vital because detecting metastatic cancer in lymph nodes is a key factor in determining the stage of breast cancer, which directly influences treatment decisions and patient outcomes. In summary, the goal of the competition is to harness the power of machine learning in medical image analysis to improve the accuracy and efficiency of breast cancer diagnosis. 
----------------------
# Description

This section will introduce some general rules and terms. In this task, you will train a CNN to predict whether each image patch contains metastatic tissue. Of course, you can also predict them by yourself ??.

#**Requirements**
You need to have at least 5 valid submissions (i.e. better than randomly guessing) to pass this task, please describe what you did to improve your CNN on your lab report.

#**Discussion chanel**

You could discuss your solutions with others and ask questions in the Discussion section.

#**Leaderboard**

**Public leaderboard**: Public leaderboard provides a real-time ranking of participants based on the score, it allows participants to gauge their model's performance relative to others. However, your CNN model might ""overfit"" on Public leaderboard, it could be different that your rank on Public leaderboard and Private leaderboard. 30% of test set are used for Public leaderboard.

**Private leaderboard**: Private leaderboard in a machine learning competition provides a more accurate and unbiased assessment of the participants' models, ensuring that the winning solutions are robust, generalized, and effective on unseen data. 70% of test set are used for Private leaderboard.

The final rank will only depend on private leaderboard, you will not know private leaderboard rank until the date of private leaderboard released, but the public leaderboard is always visible.

#**Winners**
It¡¯s not mandatory for you to submit more than 5 valid submissions, but it could always be fun to explore more! Feel free to try, also top-3?????? teams will get some goodies!!! You are encouraged to use more techniques such as transfer learning, regularization, data augmentation, more sophisticated architectures, ensemble learning and whatever you can find from papers or internet. 


#**Beyond this competition...**

There are a lot of open competitions in different domains you could find in Kaggle, and you can win Kaggle Points or bounties from some competitions to become a [Kaggle Grand master or Master](https://www.kaggle.com/progression).  

----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be evaluated on [Balanced Accuracy Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#balanced-accuracy-score) between the predicted results from your CNN and the actual labels.

Think about why [Balanced Accuracy Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#balanced-accuracy-score) is more proper than [Accuracy Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score)?

There is a script in your jupyter notebook to predict test set and generate a **.csv** file, you only need to upload the **.csv** to kaggle.

---




## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the **Class** variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,Class
    1,0
    2,1
    3,0
    
----------------------
# References

PCam dataset is from: Rotation Equivariant CNNs for Digital Pathology. 

Biblatex entry:
```
@ARTICLE{Veeling2018-qh,
  title         = ""Rotation Equivariant {CNNs} for Digital Pathology"",
  author        = ""Veeling, Bastiaan S and Linmans, Jasper and Winkens, Jim and
                   Cohen, Taco and Welling, Max"",
  month         =  jun,
  year          =  2018,
  archivePrefix = ""arXiv"",
  primaryClass  = ""cs.CV"",
  eprint        = ""1806.03962""
}
```","# Dataset Description

The PatchCamelyon (PCam) benchmark is a notable dataset in the field of medical image analysis, particularly for cancer diagnosis. It was derived from the Camelyon16 Challenge, which involved the detection of cancer metastases in lymph node sections using whole-slide images of breast cancer patients. The PCam dataset simplifies this task by providing a large set of small patches (96x96 pixels), each extracted from the whole-slide images.

Note that, we selected only a subsets from PCam benchmark and crop them to 32x32 pixels, which makes the dataset less computational demanding and more suitable for this lab.

## Files

*   **train.h5** - the training set contains 100000 patch images (32x32x3) and corresponding labels.
*   **valid.h5** - the validation set contains 32768 patch images (32x32x3) and corresponding labels.
*   **test_x.h5** - the test set contains 32768 patch images (32x32x3) and no labels.

How to open your data sets:
```
import h5py

with h5py.File('./patchcam/train.h5','r') as f:
                x_test = f['x'][:]
                y_test = f['y'][:]
```",False,True
