8866,casml-generative-ai-hackathon,"# Overview

Welcome to the CASML¡ªGenerative AI Hackathon. This competition challenges you to build a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) system capable of answering questions from a provided book in the database section. Your RAG system should generate accurate answers and provide precise references to sections and page numbers within the book that support your answers.
If this is your first time participating in a Kaggle competition, please refer to our [Getting Started
Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions-setup) for help. You can also find useful information in [Kaggle's competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), including
instructions on submitting predictions.
**Goal**: Develop a RAG system that can accurately answer questions based on the provided book and
Include exact references to the relevant sections and page numbers.
----------------------
# Description


## Competition Overview
Welcome to the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Challenge! This competition tests your ability to build an intelligent system that can accurately answer questions about textbook content while providing precise references to support its answers.


### Dataset License
This competition uses the OpenStax Psychology (2e) textbook, licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). Access for free at openstax.org. Source: https://openstax.org/details/books/psychology-2e

## Description
### Problem Statement
In this competition, you'll work with a psychology textbook dataset. Your task is to:
1. Build a RAG system that can accurately answer questions about the textbook content
2. Provide precise references to relevant sections and page numbers that support each answer
3. Ensure answers are both accurate and faithful to the source material

### Challenge Goals
- Demonstrate effective information retrieval from structured text
- Generate accurate, context-aware answers
- Provide verifiable references to source content
- Balance answer quality with retrieval precision


## Dataset
### What's Provided
- Queries.json file containing questions
- Test set with questions (without answers)
- Full textbook content in structured format
- Sample submission file showing expected format

### For Submission format, refer to dataset description

## Model Constraints
Models must be open source and have ¡Ü3 billion parameters
Allowed models include (but not limited to):
Llama-2 3B
FLAN-T5 3B
MPT 3B
TinyLlama 1.1B
Phi-2 2.7B

### Vector Database
The baseline implementation uses Pinecone as the vector database, but you're free to use any vector database that supports similarity search, including:
- Chroma
- Weaviate
- Qdrant
- Milvus
- FAISS
- MongoDB with vector search

The choice of vector database should support:
- Efficient similarity search
- Storage of embeddings and metadata
- Fast query response times
- Easy integration with Python

Your performance may vary based on vector database selection and configuration. Ensure your chosen solution can handle the textbook embeddings within competition resource constraints.

The evaluation metric remains the same regardless of vector database choice.

## Rules and Requirements
1. Only use publicly available, free models and resources
2. No external API calls during inference
3. Submissions must include all required columns in the specified format
4. Solutions must be reproducible and well-documented

## Resources
- Sample code for evaluation metrics
- Baseline RAG implementation
- Submission format guide
- Discussion forum for technical questions

----------------------
# Evaluation

This is a Code Competition
Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
Internet access disabled
Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
Submission file must be named submission.csv
Submission runtimes have been slightly obfuscated. If you repeat the exact same submission you will see up to 15 minutes of variance in the time before you receive your score.

Your submissions will be evaluated on multiple criteria:
1. **Context Precision** (20%): How relevant is the retrieved context to the question?
2. **Answer Faithfulness** (20%): How well does the answer reflect the retrieved context?
3. **Answer Correctness** (40%): How accurate is the answer compared to the ground truth?
4. **Reference Accuracy** (20%): How precisely do the provided references match the relevant content?

### Submission Format
For each `ID` in the test set, you must provide a complete response. The submission file should be a CSV with a header and have the following format:
```csv
ID,context,answer,references
```

**Field Descriptions**:
- `ID`: The unique identifier for each question (note: we use 'ID' not 'query_id')
- `context`: The retrieved context from the textbook used to generate the answer
- `answer`: The answer generated by your system
- `references`: A JSON-formatted string containing sections and pages, formatted as:
  ```json
  {""sections"": [""section1"", ""section2""], ""pages"": [""page1"", ""page2""]}
  ```

Example submission row:
```csv
1,""<retrieved context from book>"",""The scientific method in psychology involves..."",{""sections"": [""psychological_research/approaches_to_research""], ""pages"": [""41""]}
```
----------------------
# Prizes


Total Prizes Available: 
- **1st Place**?-?6000
- **2nd Place** - 4000
- **3rd Place** - 2500","# Dataset Description

# Kaggle RAG Hackathon: Dataset and Submission Format

## Overview

Welcome to the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Hackathon! This competition challenges participants to develop advanced RAG systems capable of answering questions using a comprehensive psychology textbook as the knowledge base.

## Competition Objectives

- Develop a RAG system that can efficiently retrieve relevant context from an 800-page textbook
- Generate accurate and concise answers to a series of psychology-related queries
- Demonstrate advanced information retrieval and answer generation techniques

## Dataset Components

### 1. Queries File (`queries.json`)

The `queries.json` file contains the questions that participants must answer. Each query includes:

- `query_id`: A unique identifier for the question
- `question`: The specific query to be answered

#### Example Queries
- ""What is the scientific method in psychology?""
- ""What are the basic parts of a neuron?""
- ""What are the stages of sleep?""

### 2. Submission File Requirements (`submission.csv`)

Participants must submit a CSV file with the following columns:

| Column     | Description                                                    | Type         |
|------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|--------------|
| ID         | Unique identifier matching the query_id                        | Integer      |
| context    | Retrieved text from the textbook used to answer the question   | String       |
| answer     | Generated response to the query                                | String       |
| references | JSON string indicating textbook sections and pages             | JSON String  |

## Submission Guidelines

### Key Requirements
- All queries must be answered
- Match query IDs exactly
- Provide context and references for each answer
- Use valid JSON formatting for references
- Include sections and page numbers for verification

### Reference Format Example
```json
{
  ""sections"": [""psychological_research/approaches_to_research""],
  ""pages"": [41, 3]
}
```

## Evaluation Criteria

Submissions will be assessed based on:
- Relevance and accuracy of retrieved context
- Quality and precision of generated answers
- Completeness of references
- Adherence to submission format guidelines

## Competition Tips

1. Optimize context retrieval mechanisms
2. Ensure answer quality and factual accuracy
3. Implement robust reference tracking
4. Test system thoroughly with sample queries
5. Focus on efficiency and scalability

## Resources

- Sample input: [Sample queries.json in Documentation]
- Sample output: [Sample submission.csv in Documentation]

## Final Submission Checklist

- [ ] All queries addressed
- [ ] Correct column names
- [ ] Valid data types
- [ ] No missing values
- [ ] Proper CSV and JSON formatting
- [ ] File named `submission.csv`

## Additional Information

For questions or support during the competition, please use the Discussion Forum or contact competition organizers directly.

**Good luck, and happy coding!**",False,True
8867,113-1-ntut-ee-ai-hw3,"# Description

In this homework we will predict the closing price of NASDAQ index on last day of 2024. You are free to use any AI model, even your own brain, to predict the price. A prediction example can be found on Colab:
https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1S0g4I6qrz_SbnJhKZjR41bmWwD-CJsYd#scrollTo=cf705774

Your final prediction should be submitted to the [Google Form](https://forms.gle/4oPT6936kA8yYwiT6) 24 hours before market opening at 9:30am on 12/31 Eastern Standard Time (GMT-5).
https://forms.gle/4oPT6936kA8yYwiT6

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

This time we only have public dataset. The prediction is only one floating point number: the price of NASDAQ. <b>Mean Absolute Value (MAE)</b> is used as our metric.

## Submission Format

The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Expected
NASDAQ,19000.00
```","# Dataset Description

The historic data of NASDAQ is uploaded (^IXIC.csv), which includes the open, high, low, close price and the volume. The data is downloaded from [Yahoo! Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/%5EIXIC/history?period1=34560000&period2=1639008000&interval=1d&filter=history&frequency=1d&includeAdjustedClose=true).
You can use this as training data.  Or you can also find more data from Kaggle's [Huge Stock Market Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/borismarjanovic/price-volume-data-for-all-us-stocks-etfs).",False,True
