8879,node-classification,"# Overview




----------------------
# Description

## Challenge
In this challenge you are expected to train a classifier that will be able to classify medium articles into one of three categories: 'software-development', 'artificial-intelligence' and 'ux'. 


We provide a dataset of medium articles which have to be tagged to corresponding topics (software-development, artificial intelligence, Ui/UX). Along with articles we have subscriptions lists. The subscription lists are reading lists and the articles may be related by common subscription lists. The goal is to exploit this naturally occurring network structure for classifying articles to topics. Hence, it is a 3-way node classification task
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1038279%2Fe1b31e39b30e8dedd0bb353acf0e5184%2FScreenshot%202024-01-09%20at%209.24.21%20AM.png?generation=1705338423579897&alt=media)
Before you start, we encourage you to read through all descriptions on this Kaggle page to learn more about the evaluation method and data at hand. You can find instructions how to set up Python locally on Brightspace.

# Pipeline
A suggestion for the pipeline to employ would be the pipeline proposed in your assignment.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1038279%2F908a67a1a85e5fd5d5ef6d4753aff3a2%2FScreenshot%202024-01-15%20at%201.03.59%20PM.png?generation=1705338220893553&alt=media)
We strongly suggest that you start simple - you can always improve your algorithms if time permits. You can experiment with different embedding approaches and classification approaches.



## Gaining the bonus
Successfully completing the challenge (beating our baseline performance) will award you with up to 10 points to your assignment grade.
----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate your submission we will calculate the macro F1 score.
----------------------
# Baseline

You have to get a better performance than our baseline to receive the bonus to your grade. We will make this baseline public one week before the competition ends. Of course, we encourage you to compete with other students and get the highest position in the ranking (but your position will not be taken into consideration when determining the bonus).
----------------------
# Submission

Your submissions file should contain two columns: Index and Label in a comma-separated value format. We provide you with an example submission (predicting all labels randomly) that you can reuse for your predictions. Whenever you make a submission with your predictions for the test set, we calculate your accuracy score on 50% of the data to provide you with an estimate of your score. We will make use of the other 50% of the data for the final ranking that will be released when the competition ends. Both test sets have been determined beforehand so all of your submissions will be scored using the same data.

To avoid overfitting you can only submit a limited number of predictions (10) every day.","# Dataset Description

## Files

