# ?? **FINAL EVALUATION HAS BEEN LAUNCHED here: [Kaggle Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ps-3-c-final-evaluation)** ??  
## ??? **Paper Submission is MANDATORY using the [EDAS Website](https://edas.info/N32831)** ???  
## ?? **The Final Evaluation Phase requires sharing of the MODELS and CODES** ??

Cervical cancer is the fourth most common cancer among women globally, with over 600,000 new cases and more than 300,000 deaths annually. Early detection through Pap smear screening plays a vital role in reducing mortality by identifying precancerous lesions. However, traditional methods of analyzing Pap smear samples are resource-intensive, time-consuming, and highly dependent on the expertise of cytologists. These challenges highlight the need for automation in cervical cancer screening, particularly in resource-limited settings. 

The **Pap Smear Cell Classification Challenge (PS3C)**, part of the **ISBI 2025 Challenge Program**, invites participants to address this critical problem in medical imaging: the automated classification of cervical cell images extracted from Pap smears. Using cutting-edge machine learning techniques, participants will develop models to classify test images into one of three categories:  
- **Healthy**: Normal cells without observable abnormalities.  
- **Unhealthy**: Abnormal cells that indicate potential pathological changes.  
- **Rubbish**: Images unsuitable for evaluation due to artifacts or poor quality.  

---

### **Extraction Process**

Cervical cancer screening begins with a Pap smear test, where a healthcare professional collects cell samples from the cervix. These samples are then prepared as smears on glass slides and digitized using high-resolution scanners. The digitization process produces large MRXS images, often ranging between 4¨C12 GB, with dimensions of approximately 100,000 ¡Á 220,000 pixels. These digital images undergo a series of processing steps to extract and classify individual cells.

The first step involves identifying **Regions of Interest (ROI)** within the smear to locate areas that may contain relevant cells. Next, the smear is divided into smaller sections (slicing), allowing for efficient processing. Advanced image processing techniques, including segmentation algorithms, are then applied to isolate and extract individual cells. This step is critical as it removes irrelevant background artifacts while focusing on the cellular structures necessary for classification.

Below is an illustration of the complete process:

<img src=""https://i.ibb.co/kgZhfPD/whole-process.png"" alt=""Pap Smear Process"" width=""100%""/>

---

### **Cell Extraction and Segmentation**

Once the Regions of Interest (ROIs) are identified and segmented, individual cell images are extracted for further analysis. This extraction process isolates each cell from its surrounding environment, ensuring that the resulting dataset focuses only on the cellular features relevant for classification. 

The following image provides an example of the segmented regions and their respective extracted cells:

<img src=""https://i.ibb.co/ystFbZS/cell-extraction.png"" alt=""Cell Extraction Example"" width=""100%""/>

These cell images are then categorized and annotated by expert cytologists, forming the basis for the training and test datasets.

---

### **Annotation and Dataset Details**

The dataset provided for this challenge reflects a robust annotation pipeline involving high-resolution smear digitization, segmentation, and expert annotation. This ensures high-quality data for training and evaluation. Below is an illustration of the process, including segmentation and annotation steps:

<img src=""https://i.ibb.co/QFwW6JS/annotation-process.png"" alt=""Annotation Process"" width=""100%""/>

The **training dataset** contains images categorized into **four classes**:  
- **Healthy**, **Unhealthy**, **Rubbish**, and **Bothcells**.  

These classes are visible in the example below:

<img src=""https://i.ibb.co/z56K1Nc/ex-cell-images.png"" alt=""Example Cell Images"" width=""70%""/>

In contrast, the **test dataset** contains only **three classes**: **Healthy**, **Unhealthy**, and **Rubbish**, with the ""Bothcells"" class excluded. Participants are encouraged to consider treating ""Bothcells"" in the training data as **Unhealthy**, but this decision is left to their discretion, allowing innovative approaches to the classification problem.

---

#### **Important Information for ISBI 2025 Participants**

This challenge serves as **Phase 1** of the ISBI Challenge, also known as the **Preliminary Phase**, which will run until **6th February 2025**. Following this, the **Final Evaluation Phase** will take place from **6th¨C13th February 2025**, determining the winners of the ISBI 2025 challenge.

During the Final Evaluation Phase:  
- A **new test dataset** will be provided on Kaggle.  
- Participants must use the same methods tested during the Preliminary Phase to classify the new dataset and resubmit their results.  

#### **Timeline for ISBI 2025 Challenges**
- **Dec 9**: Challenge initiation.  
  - Training data and contact information made publicly available.  
  - Participants can begin developing their methods and submitting evaluations.  
- **Feb 6**: Submission deadline for the Preliminary Phase.  
- **Feb 6**: Challenge participants submit their papers on the EDAS platform (challenge track).  
  - Review period opens for two weeks, with reviewers assigned by challenge organizers.  
- **Feb 10**: Submission deadline for the Final Evaluation Phase.  
- **Feb 20**: Challenge paper authors receive reviews.  
  - Authors are notified to address comments and finalize methodologies within one week.  
  - Valid paper submitters are invited to present their methods at the conference. 
- **Feb 27**: Final camera-ready paper submission deadline.  
- **March 1**: Notification of oral versus poster presentations.
  - Top 5 performers are invited to present orally at the conference, and co-author the meta-analysis journal manuscript. 
- **April 14¨C17**: ISBI Conference and announcement of challenge winners.  

#### **Submission Requirements for Validity in ISBI 2025**  

Participants must submit the following alongside their predictions to ensure solution validity:  
1. **Well-documented code** used for generating predictions, including:  
   - Model architectures.  
   - Preprocessing and postprocessing steps.  
   - Details of any third-party datasets used.  
   - Descriptions of additional methods or techniques applied.  

2. Code must be uploaded using Kaggle's code feature for full transparency. Submissions failing to meet these requirements will be deemed invalid for ISBI 2025 participation.  

---

Participants will receive access to high-quality training and test datasets, comprising expertly annotated cervical cell images. The training dataset is provided with clear labels organized into respective folders for each class, ensuring ease of use for model development. The test dataset will be distributed as image files alongside a CSV file containing the list of image IDs. Participants must classify each test image and submit their predictions in the specified format for evaluation.

The goal of this challenge is to create models capable of accurately classifying cervical cell images while managing the inherent complexities of medical imaging data, including class imbalance. Submissions will be evaluated based on the **F1-Score**, a metric that balances precision and recall, calculated for each class and averaged across all classes. This ensures fair and comprehensive assessment, emphasizing the correct identification of unhealthy cells, which are crucial for effective cervical cancer screening.

---

This challenge is organized by a multidisciplinary team of experts in medical imaging, pathology, and data science:
- **Bal¨¢zs Harangi** ¨C Department of Data Science and Visualization, University of Debrecen  
- **Andr¨¢s Hajdu** ¨C Department of Data Science and Visualization, University of Debrecen  
- **D¨¢vid Kup¨¢s** ¨C Department of Data Science and Visualization, University of Debrecen  
- **Ilona Kov¨¢cs** ¨C Department of Pathology, Kenezy Gyula University Hospital and Clinic  
- **P¨¦ter Kov¨¢cs** ¨C Department of Numerical Analysis, E?tv?s Lor¨¢nd University  
- **Nicolai Spicher** ¨C Department of Medical Informatics, Universit?tsmedizin G?ttingen  

