8914,pastry-prediction,"# Overview

# Predicting Pastry Sales for Smarter Resource Use

In a world where systematic overproduction drives both economic inefficiency and environmental degradation, the challenge of accurate demand forecasting has never been more pressing. This competition invites you to tackle one such real-world scenario: predicting pastry sales. Framed as a time-series forecasting problem, the task calls for innovative yet efficient machine learning solutions to help reduce waste while meeting customer demands.

The dual objectives of reducing waste and satisfying customer demand embody a broader economic challenge. Can businesses achieve resource efficiency without compromising profitability? This competition highlights the power of data science to address these dilemmas, while also hinting at a deeper truth: sustainable change might require not just better prediction models, but a shift in societal values and consumption habits.


----------------------
# Description


## The Problem: Pastry Planning and Overproduction
Pastry sales present a particularly intriguing forecasting challenge. Freshness is critical, and unsold items often end up as waste at the end of the day. Overproduction leads to increased costs and environmental harm, while underproduction risks lost revenue and dissatisfied customers. The stakes are high: businesses that master this balance can not only improve their profitability but also contribute to global efforts to reduce food waste.

## The Goal: Intelligent Solutions for a Complex Trade-Off
Your mission is to develop models that enable better pastry production planning. By accurately predicting daily and seasonal variations in sales, bakeries can produce just the right amount. However, the solution comes with its own trade-offs. Many state-of-the-art machine learning methods require significant computational resources, raising questions about their sustainability. This competition encourages you to weigh these trade-offs and explore innovative, computationally efficient approaches to forecasting.

## Why Participate?
This competition offers an exciting opportunity for data scientists at all levels to engage with a high-impact problem on real-life data. Whether you are a seasoned expert ready to experiment with cutting-edge techniques or a newcomer looking to build your skills, your participation can make a difference. By contributing your insights, you¡¯ll help advance both the field of machine learning and the fight against wasteful overproduction.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Mean Squared Error](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error) between the predicted daily sales per store and the observed target.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict an estimated sales total of that day and store for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,123
    5,456
    6,789
    etc.",,False,True
8915,hackathon-DL-IndabaX-MDG-2024,"# Overview

A thunderstorm is a weather event with thunder, lightning, heavy rain, and strong winds. Nowcasting uses real-time data to predict such events within the next 0¨C6 hours. Using machine learning for nowcasting is a rapidly growing area in weather forecasting.

Nosy Be, with its airport, significant population, and economic importance, is highly vulnerable to thunderstorms. These storms cause flooding and damage, so accurate nowcasting is crucial for saving lives, protecting property, and aiding emergency responses.

This competition is **exclusively open to all residents of Madagascar**, regardless of your location within the country or academic qualifications. If you win, you can choose to collect your prize in person or have it shipped to you.
----------------------
# Description

**The goal of this hackathon is to predict the probability of a storm in Nosy Be within 1 and 3 hours, using real-time data. This includes the time of observation (t<sub>0</sub>: year, month, day, hour, minute), latitude and longitude, and the intensity, size, and distance of the nearest storm at t<sub>0</sub>, all extracted from satellite observations**.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The mean **Relative Operating Characteristic Skill Score (ROCSS)** of the two lead times (1 hour and 3 hours) will be used to evaluate your models. The **ROCSS** for a lead time is calculated as:

$$
\text{ROCSS} = 1 - \frac{AUC_{\text{NFLICS}}}{AUC_{\text{model}}}
$$

where **AUC** is the [Area Under the ROC Curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic). 

**NFLICS** is an operational storm nowcasting system developed by [Anderson et al. (2024)](https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/qj.4607) and is used as the benchmark for comparison.

**A positive ROCSS value indicates that your model has better skill than the reference model, NFLICS**. 

**Since the NFLICS predictions are only available during inference, you can focus on optimising the [AUC-ROC](https://scikit-learn.org/1.5/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html) for each lead time during the model development phase.**

# Competition Submission Format

For each ID in the test set, you are required to predict the probability of the storm occurring at both 1-hour and 3-hour lead times separately. 

The submission file must be a CSV file and:
- Include a header with three columns: `storm_id`, `Storm_NosyBe_1h`, and `Storm_NosyBe_3h`.
- Follow this example:

storm_id,Storm_NosyBe_1h,Storm_NosyBe_3h
storm_202001011115,0.1,0.09
storm_202001011130,0,1
storm_202001011145,0.78,0

**Hint: You can train two different models, one for each lead time.**

**While the primary requirement is to submit the CSV file with your predictions, we would really appreciate it if you could also briefly describe the model you used for your nowcasting approach (e.g., logistic regression, deep learning, etc.). This is optional but helps us understand your methodology and approach.**
----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: $200

- **1st Place**?- 500 000 MGA
- **2nd Place** - 250 000 MGA
- **3rd Place** - 100 000 MGA
- **4th Place** - Goodies 
- **5th Place** - Goodies
----------------------
# References and Acknowledgments

We acknowledge [Klein et al. (2018)](https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2017JD027432), [EUMETSAT (2022)](https://www.eumetsat.int/) and [Rakotomanga et al. (2024)](https://eps.leeds.ac.uk/maths/pgr/13569/mendrika-rakotomanga), for providing the dataset used in this research.

We also thank the [Deep Learning Indaba](https://deeplearningindaba.com/about/our-mission/), [Professor Douglas Parker](https://environment.leeds.ac.uk/see/staff/1469/professor-douglas-parker), the [University of Leeds](https://www.leeds.ac.uk/) and the [WISER-EWSA](https://wmo.int/activities/projects/project-portfolio/weather-and-climate-information-services-early-warning-southern-africa-wiser-ewsa) project for their funding and support. We also sincerely thank everyone, both locally and internationally, who contributed to the success of IndabaX Madagascar 2024.

Finally, we recognise all our partners for their invaluable contributions: AlgoMada, VirtuoCode, Laboratoire d'Intelligence Artificielle de Madagascar, AMLD Africa-MG, WeR, Zindi, Ikala STEM, Optimize Insights, Club d'Entraide de l'¨¦cole Nationale d'Informatique, and the Centre de Recherche en ¨¦ducation Environnementale.",,False,True
