- `AVISPA_ASIATICA` - Target variable: ""SI"" indicates it is an Asian hornet, ""NO"" indicates it is not.",False,True
8919,molecular-machine-learning,"# Overview

A common challenge in experimental chemistry is trying to infer causality from small datasets. Chemists will often synthesize O(100) molecules, measure some important property, and then try to infer which molecular features are important in determining that property. This process commonly uses expert intuition to identify reasonable features, followed by multivariate linear regression to identify and regress important features. 

Our work from the NSF's [Molecule Maker Lab Institute](https://moleculemaker.org/) recently published in [*Nature*](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07892-1) has shown that a purely data-driven ML approach applied to discovering and enhancing molecular photostability (e.g. for organic solar cells) was capable of 1) identifying important molecular features not previously identify by experts and 2) generating a regression model for photostability that was validated on new molecules.

We want to further exploit this approach and enable data-driven discoveries in chemistry.

Specifically, the challenge posed in this competition is:
**Given a small set of molecules (O(100)), each described by a large number of calculable (but mostly irrelevant) features and a corresponding experimental property: What is the best algorithm to identify the most informative features in predicting the property for new molecules, and regressing the experimental property?**
----------------------
# Description

The approach used in our previous [work](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07892-1) tested Support Vector Regression (SVR) models regressing photostability lifetimes (T80) from ~2.5 million combinations of molecular features.  The dataset & example scripts are available [here](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/dfriday/oligomer-photostability-and-computed-properties). To encourage development & testing of more effective algorithms, we have synthesized 9 new molecules and measured their T80s, allowing users to build algorithms and models to discover the important molecular features for photostability and compete to test their model's performance on the new molecules.

While explicitly posed as a regression problem, we expect that the best models will depend on only a few features that are relevant to the (mostly unknown) physics determining T80. Additionally, feature refinement and new feature representations may be helpful. We provide train & test datasets containing ~150 molecular features (described in more detail in our [Kaggle Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/dfriday/oligomer-photostability-and-computed-properties)), and SMILES strings (a text representation of a molecule) so additional features can be generated via the [RDKit package](https://www.rdkit.org/docs/GettingStartedInPython.html) or other software. An example RDKit script is available in the [SmilesStrings Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/dfriday/smilesstrings). Additionally, users are welcome to use any Pre-Trained models (e.g. [FARM](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02082) or other SMILES-based models) for the prediction task. We will reserve one prize for the best algorithm using pre-trained models and the best algorithm employing only non-pre-models.

Of note, prior to this competition: 
* We identified the three most predictive features as TDOS4.0, NumHeteroatoms, and Mass. 
* We identified & validated the physical importance of TDOS4.0 (or TDOS3.9, which is correlated).
* We did not explore the physical importance of NumHeteroatoms (the number of non-carbon, non-hydrogen atoms) or Mass.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Mean Squared Log Error between predictions and experimental T80 values.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the T80 for each molecule. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,T80
    Test-01,1.5
    Test-02,2.5
    Test-03,3.5
    etc...
----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: 3
- Top Pre-trained Model:?$50 Amazon.com gift card, sent to team leader
- Top Not Pre-trained Model: $50 Amazon.com gift card, sent to team leader
- Next Best Model: $50 Amazon.com gift card, sent to team leader

All prize winners will also be highlighted in the MMLI Newsletter if desired.",,False,True
