Thank you, and we look forward to your participation! ??
----------------------
# ??Important Announcement ??

We have updated our dataset to version **Data_122824**. Please use the most up-to-date version to train your model.
What has been changed in this version:
1. Re-annotated unclear images for better training results.
2. Fixed the unmatched JSON and JPG files.
3. Added one more test sample (you may need to use the most up-to-date *Submission_template.csv*).

**The data format and evaluation metric remained the same. You don't have to worry about changing your model structure.**
----------------------
# Description

In the Glioma-MDC 2025 Challenge, a *case* is defined as a single histopathological image patch of glioma tissue, with multiple cases provided for analysis. Each *case* includes **varied locations** represented by coordinates and their **corresponding labels**. The objective is to develop a model capable of accurately predicting labels in these pre-annotated regions.

## Training Cases
Each training case comprises a stained image patch accompanied by a JSON file detailing the coordinates identifying specific cell locations. These cells are labeled as either *""Non-mitosis""* or *""Mitosis""*, providing the ground truth necessary for model training.

## Test Cases
Each test case also includes a stained image patch and an associated JSON file with multiple coordinate locations. However, the labels for these coordinates are left *blank*. Challenge participants must analyze these unlabeled coordinates and classify each one as either *""Non-mitosis""* or *""Mitosis""*.


### Example of imaging
Showing training0001.jpg. Each H&E slide is standardized as 512-by-512 pixels `jpg` image.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22565116%2F0819052a29a3dc7f0752c39dea804af4%2Ftraining0001.jpg?generation=1728408278142408&alt=media)

### Example of annotation in JSON file
Showing training0001.json annotation file.
- `label` is the classification label
- `points` are the polygon points in pixel coordinate
```
{
  ""version"": ""5.4.1"",
  ""flags"": {},
  ""shapes"": [
    {
      ""label"": ""Non-mitosis"",
      ""points"": [
        [
          262.3829787234043,
          187.56028368794324
        ],
        [
          269.4751773049645,
          187.56028368794324
        ],
        ...
      ],
      ""group_id"": null,
      ""description"": """",
      ""shape_type"": ""polygon"",
      ""flags"": {},
      ""mask"": null
    }
  ],
  ""imagePath"": ""training0001.jpg"",
  ""imageHeight"": 512,
  ""imageWidth"": 512
}
```

----------------------
# Evaluation

The primary metric used to assess the performance of the algorithms in the Glioma-MDC 2025 challenge is the F1 Score. The F1 Score provides a balanced measure of the algorithm's detection accuracy by combining both precision (the proportion of correctly identified mitotic figures out of all detected figures) and recall (the ability to identify all true mitotic figures). It is particularly useful in scenarios where there is an imbalance between the number of true positives and false positives/negatives.

- Precision (Positive Predictive Value): The proportion of true positive mitosis detections among all detected mitoses. 

- Recall (Sensitivity): The proportion of true positive mitosis detections among all actual mitoses in the ground truth. 

- F1 Score: The harmonic mean of precision and recall, which provides a single measure of the detection accuracy. 

This metric will be the primary determinant in ranking the participating algorithms, reflecting their overall ability to accurately detect and classify mitotic figures within the provided glioma tissue images. Additionally, precision and recall will be analyzed separately to provide further insights into each algorithm's classification accuracy and detection sensitivity, respectively. 


## Submission File
We have provided a test outcome table `Submission_template.csv`, please fill in the column prediction with your predicted labels (Mitosis: 1; Non-mitosis: 0). 
(**Note**: Each image in the test set corresponds to a JSON file, and the JSON file contains cell coordinates marked with ""Blank1"", ""Blank2"" and so on.)

e.g.,
```
testing0001,Blank1,0
testing0001,Blank2,1
...
```
----------------------
# Organizer

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22565116%2Fcfca720a0ebb7ad4248299f5793c950c%2Forganizers.png?generation=1729607815506606&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

The target cohort for the Glioma-MDC 2025 challenge consists of patients diagnosed with glioma, specifically those who have undergone surgical resection or biopsy. The data used in the final biomedical application would be acquired from human glioma tissue samples, typically stained with Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) for histopathological examination.

The challenge cohort for the Glioma-MDC 2025 challenge consists of histopathological image patches obtained from glioma tissue samples. These samples were sourced from patients treated at Beijing Tiantan Hospital,  Sanbo Brain Hospital and supplemented with data derived from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) database.

**All of the data provided for the Glioma-MDC 2025 challenge is licensed under CC BY-NC-SA (Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike)**.

Participants should read the data description before starting:

## Files

*   **Glioma_MDC_2025_training.zip** - compressed zip file contains following two types of training data, 1,439 annotated images in total
*   *training[4-digit-ID].jpg* - the training HE slide image with`512` by `512` pixels
*   *training[4-digit-ID].json* - the training annotation with HE slide ID correlated to image
*   **Glioma_MDC_2025_testing.zip** - compressed zip file contains following two types of testing data, 200 annotated images in total
*   *testing[4-digit-ID].jpg* - the testing HE slide image with`512` by `512` pixels
*   *testing[4-digit-ID].json* - the testing annotation with HE slide ID correlated to image
*   **Submission_sample.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns of `Submission_sample.csv`

*   `Image ID` - Image ID for the HE slide
*   `Label ID`?- Location ID for the HE slide
*   `Prediction` - Prediction of labels in specific location of an HE slide. If the cell is identified as mitosis, a 1 is filled in the corresponding position in the result table. If it is ""Non-mitosis"", a 0 is filled in the corresponding position.

## Important notes

* Each image may have multiple ROIs (Region of Interests), and the number are not always the same. We have annotated all of these ROIs so you don't have to, the task of this challenge is to identify the label of every ROIs: `Mitosis` or `Non-mitosis`
* The ROI is circled by polygon shapes. For example in the `training0001.json` file you will see:
```
""shapes"": [
        {
            ""label"": ""Non-mitosis"",
            ""points"": [
                [
                    262.3829787234043,
                    187.56028368794324
                ],
                [
                    269.4751773049645,
                    187.56028368794324
                ],
                ...
            ],
            ""group_id"": null,
            ""description"": """",
            ""shape_type"": ""polygon"",
            ""flags"": {},
            ""mask"": null
        }
    ]
```
This location is labeled as `Non-mitosis` circled by the points in x-axis and y-axis coordinates. To view the annotation on top of the image, you can download open-source software [*labelme*](https://github.com/wkentaro/labelme). The following is an example view of `training0001.jpg` with annotation `training0001.json`. ![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F22565116%2F279d08a490084983024036bb800e0394%2Fexample.jpg?generation=1728854127994477&alt=media)
* The label in the annotation files of the **testing** set is `Blank[position-ID]`, your task is to predict the label. For example in the `testing0001.json` file you will see:
```
 ""shapes"": [
        {
            ""label"": ""Blank1"",
            ""points"": [
                [
                    303.23756906077347,
                    229.75690607734808
                ],
              ...
            ],
            ""group_id"": null,
            ""description"": """",
            ""shape_type"": ""polygon"",
            ""flags"": {},
            ""mask"": null
        },
        {
            ""label"": ""Blank2"",
            ""points"": [
                [
                    164.0110497237569,
                    243.56906077348066
                ],
         ...
            ],
            ""group_id"": null,
            ""description"": """",
            ""shape_type"": ""polygon"",
            ""flags"": {},
            ""mask"": null
        },
        ...
    ],
```
You will see there will be `Blank1-6` in the JSON file, which means there are **6** ROIs to be predict in total. If the cell is identified as mitosis, a 1 is filled in the corresponding position in the result table. If it is ""Non-mitosis"", a 0 is filled in the corresponding position. **Make sure filling the prediction accordingly in the `Submission_template.csv` file**.",False,True
8922,innovative-ai-challenge-2024,"# Overview

The Innovative AI Challenge 2024 is an online competition to solve real-world problems using artificial intelligence. Participants will work on one of two challenges:

1. Developing AI models to increase agricultural productivity.
2. Creating a visual AI chatbot that emulates human emotions for education and elderly support.

**We are excited about the interest from students worldwide! As this is our first edition, participation is limited to India to ensure smooth execution. We look forward to welcoming international participants in future editions. Thank you for your understanding and support!**

With a total prize pool of INR 20,000 and a pre-seed funding opportunity of INR 50,000 for one standout solution, this is your chance to showcase your creativity, problem-solving skills, and technical expertise. The competition is open to Indian students and tech enthusiasts aged up to 25 years.

[Visit challenge website](https://www.venusmoon.in/ai-challenge)
[Participate now!](https://www.venusmoon.in/event-details/innovative-ai-challenge-2024)
[Submit here](https://www.venusmoon.in/submissions)
![How to participate on challenge website?](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KyRdljezI1Y)

----------------------
# Description

The problems are now live. You can download them from the challenge website.

## Agriculture Productivity Prediction
Objective: Build an AI/ML model that predicts agricultural productivity based on crop type, weather conditions, soil properties, and other relevant factors.
Requirements:

- Ensure the model is accurate and farmer-friendly.
- Provide a simple, accessible user interface for farmers to use effectively. Create an interface or a small website to showcase your AI/ML model.
- Problem analysis, solution overview, methodology, and implementation steps. Short video, codes via challenge website. 

##Emotion-Emulating Visual AI Chatbot
Objective: Develop a chatbot that mimics human emotions through visual and conversational cues, improving user interaction.
Use Cases:

- Support education by enhancing engagement with students.
- Aid elderly individuals with personalized and empathetic interactions.
- Problem analysis, solution overview, methodology, and implementation steps. Short video, codes via challenge website.

[Submit on the challenge website to receive your prizes if you win.](https://www.venusmoon.in/submissions)


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated based on creativity, problem-solving ability, and real-world application, with a focus on the relationship between the predicted crop yield (kg/ha) and the observed target value.

For chatbot, it will be evaluated by using your developed interface.

Note: *Submissions will be evaluated based on Mean Squared Error. Final prizes will be awarded considering creativity, innovation, user-friendly interface, and achieving minimal error.*

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a value for the TARGET variable: crop yield (kg/ha). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,Target
    1001,4200
    1002,4400
    1003,4500
    etc.

For Emotion-Emulating Visual AI Chatbot, submissions will be evaluated based on creativity, problem-solving ability.
----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: 17
- **2 Best Creative & Innovative Solution**?-?INR 5,000 each
- **Top 5 Best Solutions** - INR 1000 each
- **Top 10 Next Best Solutions** - INR 500 each

Pre-seed Funding Opportunity - For one solution with startup potential (up to INR 50,000)
----------------------
# Timeline 

- Start Date: December 1, 2024
- Submission Deadline: January 15, 2024
- Review: January 16, 2024 onwards 
- Prizes: February 28, 2024

Prizes via UPI.",,False,True
8923,fsiv-p-6-2-pollen-classification-2024,"# Overview

This competition aims to introduce participants to the development of image classification models. To achieve this, you must complete the base code to implement the use of multiple classifiers and feature extraction functions. Using your implementation, you must train your models on the pollen dataset available on Moodle and subsequently generate labels for the test set. You must upload the file containing your predicted labels to this Kaggle competition, where you will receive a measure of the achieved accuracy. A portion of your grade for this assignment will be determined proportionally based on the highest score obtained by any participant in this competition.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, you will work with the BAA500 pollen dataset. This dataset includes 15 different pollen species, each represented by a collection of segmented pollen grain images (one grain per image).

For this assignment, a reduced subset of the full dataset has been provided, consisting of 24,000 training images, 3,000 validation images, and 3,000 test images, with a resolution of 128 x 128 pixels, grayscale. The sample names have been randomized, ensuring no correspondence with the original BAA500 dataset samples. The training and validation datasets are labelled in .csv files, where each row contains the name of a sample (image) and its label. However, the test dataset does not include labels; to maintain consistency, a .csv file is provided for the test dataset where all samples are labelled as `unknown`.

You must develop your models exclusively using the training and validation datasets. Once your model is ready, you will test it on the test dataset and upload the generated .csv file containing your classifier's predictions for each sample. Your submission will be evaluated in a competitive ranking. A portion of your grade for this assignment will be determined proportionally based on the highest score in the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be evaluated by calculating the classification accuracy on the test set. To this end, you must upload a .csv file with the following structure:

```
sample,species
020665.png,urticaceae
103109.png,cupressaceae
073911.png,tilia
...
```

Where the first column corresponds to the sample (image), and the second column corresponds to the obtained label (in this case, the pollen species).","# Dataset Description

A subset of the BAA500 dataset has been uploaded to Moodle. You will find three files: `train.tar.gz`, `valid.tar.gz` and `test.tar.gz`. It is recommended that you extract the three files in the `data` directory at the root of your project. The resulting file structure should resemble the following:

```
data
©À©¤©¤ test
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 000040.png
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 000041.png
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 000070.png
              ...
©À©¤©¤ test.csv
©À©¤©¤ train
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 000000.png
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 000005.png
©¦   ©À©¤©¤ 000011.png
              ...
