8933,multi-org-challenge,"# Overview

We invite the community to benchmark object detection on our **large lung organoid 2D microscopy imaging dataset**, while taking into account label noise and annotator biases.
----------------------
# Description

Organoids are self-assembling three-dimensional spheres, growing in a gel-like environment. Depending on the cells from which they derive, organoids can mimic different organs and organ systems. Compared to the usage of a simple cell culture, organoids allow for studying organic development, interaction between different cell types, and behaviour of stem cells ex-vivo in a 3D environment.

The detection and tracking of organoids, allows to distinguish the effects of different treatments, or different cell types. By evaluating the colony-forming efficiency through the changing number of organoids, one can draw conclusion on their regenerative capacity and plasticity. It is of great importance, therefore, to have an objective quantification method of organoid counts, to prevent as many biases as humanly possible. In most cases, organoids are manually counted after being imaged by a microscope, an immensely time-consuming task which is also very susceptible to human errors and biases. Moreover, different organoid sizes can represent different organoid types and automatic quantification of their size helps determine their regenerative capacity.

While the need for automated processes that allow for organoid quantification is apparent, one must also pay close attention when developing automated models. It is important to consider the aspect of human biases and errors, not only during manual procedures, but also when automatic methods, i.e. deep learning models, are being created, or trained. For this, supervised learning techniques are most commonly employed, which involve comparing predicted outcomes to the true values, or else ground truth, during the learning process, as well as for model evaluation. We collected three sets of annotations, which we name **label sets**, for the test set of our dataset, so these aspects can be considered when developing methods for organoid detection.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The three label sets of the test set are:
* **test<sup>0</sup>:** Annotations at time point *t*, for each image can belong to *Annotator A or B*. Images with IDs 1-22 were annotated by A and 23-55 by B.
* **test<sup>1</sup><sub>A</sub>:** Annotations at time point *t<sup>1</sup>*, annotated by *Annotator A*
* **test<sup>1</sup><sub>B</sub>:** Annotations at time point *t<sup>1</sup>*, annotated by *Annotator B*

While the label set **test<sup>0</sup>** is made directly available here, the labels for **test<sup>1</sup><sub>A</sub>** and **test<sup>1</sup><sub>B</sub>** can be estimated via participating in the competition and submitting your results to the leaderboard.

---

Submissions are evaluated on the mAP@0.5IoU  between the predicted bounding boxes and label sets **test<sup>1</sup><sub>A</sub>** and **test<sup>1</sup><sub>B</sub>** according to the PASCAL VOC challenge. The average of the two scores is computed for the leaderboard. Average scores per label set are also reported, but do not affect the leaderboard.


## Submission File
For each image in the test set, you must provide all bounding boxes predicted by the models.  The submission file should be a .csv, containing the following columns and have the format:

    ID, Predicted Boxes, Model scores, Predicted Labels
    Normal_Plate_4_image_0, {bboxes_dict}, {scores_dict}, {labels_dict}

**ID:** Each image has a unique ID which should be created by: {Experimental setup}\_{Plate}\_{Image}

**bboxes_dict:** Should be a dict where keys are the id of the predicted box and values hold a list of the four coordinates of the bounding box, e.g. {0: [[y1, x1, y2, x2], 1: ...}. Note that this follows the PASCAL VOC format for representing bounding boxes where in our case x and y representations are swapped, i.e. x represents rows and y columns.

**scores_dict:** Should be a dict where keys correspond to the keys in the bboxes_dict and values hold the model confidence for each predicted bounding box 

**labels_dict:** Should be a dict where keys correspond to the keys in the bboxes_dict and values hold the label for each predicted bounding box, in this version of the dataset always 0 for single-class task

The notebook [here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/christinabukas/multiorg-challenge-make-dummy-submission) gives an example for running inference on a benchmark model and generating a submission file for five images of the test set.","# Dataset Description

The dataset and corresponding documentation needed for this challenge is provided on Kaggle [here](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/christinabukas/mutliorg).",False,True
8934,predicting-training-rate,"# Overview

??????¡á? Welcome to the Gym Analytics Challenge! You¡¯re in the right place.

This competition is designed to be your perfect introduction to machine learning challenges and to help you familiarize yourself with predicting real-world behaviors from data.

The task is simple yet exciting: use machine learning to build a model that predicts how many days per week gym clients attend based on a dataset containing information about their workout habits, including frequency, training intensity, and more.

Once you're ready to get started, click the ""Join Competition"" button to create an account and access the dataset. 

Let¡¯s get started and see how well you can predict gym attendance! ????¡â???

