8955,spr-screening-mammography-recall,"# Overview

The [Radiology Society of S?o Paulo (SPR - Sociedade Paulista de Radiologia)](http://www.spr.org.br) is organizing an Artificial Intelligence Challenge for Breast Cancer. All healthcare professionals and data scientists worldwide are invited to participate.

The challenge is to create an AI model that accurately predicts mammography recall. Recall means the radiologist identified something that needs more work-up because there is a chance the patient may have breast cancer. To support this initiative, SPR has partnered with institutions that have donated thousands of mammography images, enabling participants to develop and test artificial intelligence algorithms to enhance breast cancer screening and early detection.

This initiative aims to advance AI-driven mammography analysis, foster scientific and educational activities in the Radiology and Diagnostic Imaging community, and encourage research on imaging biomarkers.

The top three winners of the competition will be acknowledged during Jornada Paulista de Radiologia (JPR) 2025. All participants are invited to [register ](https://www.jpr.org.br/) and participate in JPR.

## About SPR

For 56 years, SPR has promoted scientific updating and continuing education within the highest principles of professionalism.

With an eye on the future, the organization is often looking to bring dynamism to its work and great prominence to everyone in its community, aiming to be well-recognized nationally and intentionally.

SPR is responsible for the S?o Paulo Radiological Meeting (JPR), its most significant event, and is also considered the largest congress of the specialty in Latin America.

JPR2025 will take place in the Transamerica Expo Center in S?o Paulo - SP, on May 1-4.




----------------------
# Description

Mammography is a critical tool for breast cancer screening and diagnosis, and radiologists use the Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System (BI-RADS) to classify findings.

In this competition, we focus on identifying positive recall cases, which require further evaluation. These include:

**BI-RADS 3**: Probably benign, but follow-up is needed.
**BI-RADS 4**: Suspicious abnormality, biopsy may be required.
**BI-RADS 5**: Highly suggestive of malignancy.
**BI-RADS 0**: Inconclusive, requiring additional imaging.
For this challenge, **BI-RADS 1 and 2**, (normal and benign findings) are considered negative recall cases, meaning no further action is needed.

Your goal is to build a model that can accurately differentiate between positive and negative recall mammograms, helping improve early detection and reduce unnecessary follow-ups.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using the **AUC-ROC (Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve)**, which measures the model's ability to distinguish between positive and negative recall cases across various classification thresholds.

### AUC-ROC Calculation:
The AUC-ROC is computed as the area under the **Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve**, which is defined using:

- **True positive rate:**
$$
TPR = \frac{\text{True Positives (TP)}}{\text{True Positives (TP)} + \text{False Negatives (FN)}}
$$

- **False positive rate:**
$$
FPR = \frac{\text{False Positives (FP)}}{\text{False Positives (FP)} + \text{True Negatives (TN)}}
$$

- **AUC:**
$$
AUC = \int_{0}^{1} TPR(FPR) \, dFPR
$$


which is numerically approximated using the **trapezoidal rule** based on the ROC curve points.

A higher AUC-ROC score indicates better model performance in distinguishing **positive recall cases** (BI-RADS 3, 4, 5, and 0) from **negative recall cases** (BI-RADS 1 and 2).


Submission File Format
Your submission file should be a CSV with the following format:

```python
ID,TARGET
2,1
5,0
6,1
etc.
```

----------------------
