8959,immrep25,"# Overview

IMMREP25 will run from March 3rd to March 23rd, 2025 and test teams' ability to predict the specificity of human TCRs for a set of 'unseen' peptide:HLA complexes.
----------------------
# Description

**IMMREP25** - the third IMMREP benchmark - will run from **March 3rd to March 23rd, 2025**. IMMREP is a series of evolving competitions, benchmarking algorithms for TCR-pMHC binding prediction.

Following the results of [IMMREP22](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2667119023000046) and [IMMREP23](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2667119024000156), we will assess model performance specifically on ¡°unseen¡± peptide:MHC (pMHC) complexes - pMHC complexes for which no TCR binding data exists in public databases, and the most challenging class of prediction. Participants will be able to test their models on purpose-generated data provided by Adaptive Biotechnologies.
----------------------
# Dataset

Adaptive Biotechnologies has generously granted us access to a set of human TCRs with sequenced alpha and beta chains with known epitope specificity. 20 peptide:MHC complexes (pMHC) are included, 10 peptides associated with HLA-A\*02:01 and 10 associated with HLA-B\*40:01. For each peptide, the name of the restricting MHC molecule will be provided.

To our knowledge, no TCR information is publicly available for these pMHC, so this constitutes an 'unseen' dataset which we expect to be challenging to predict. The peptides are derived from common viral pathogens, but their exact sequences will be kept hidden until the competition starts to avoid groups generating their own experimental data. The TCR specificity was determined by Adaptive using their [MIRA assay](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7418738/) format which measures functional T cell responses.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F49258%2F37fdd5816e6672434df1ec4b64e17926%2Fadaptivebiotech-logo-adaptiveblue-RGB.png?generation=1738800253501838&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

For each pMHC and TCR, participants will assign a score that predicts it as more likely (high score) or less likely (low score) for the TCR to be specific to the pMHC. For the main evaluation, we will separately assess the prediction performance for each pMHC, and average it as follows: 

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Macro AUC0.1](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html). The ROC AUC score, commonly used in binary classification, is calculated as: 

$$ \ AUC = \int_0^1 TPR(FPR)\, dFPR $$

Where:
- ROC AUC represents the Area Under the ROC Curve.
- TPR stands for True Positive Rate, also known as Sensitivity or Recall.
- FPR stands for False Positive Rate, which is the complement of Specificity.
- dFPR represents the differential of False Positive Rate.

In our case we are calculating the partial AUC up to a FPR of 0.1:
$$ \ AUC0.1 = \int_0^{0.1} TPR(FPR)\, dFPR $$

We calculate this AUC0.1 independently for each peptide in the test set and then calculate the arithmetic mean of these peptide-specific AUC0.1 scores in order to get the Macro AUC0.1.
$$ \ Macro\, AUC0.1 = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{1}^{N} AUC0.1(n) $$

Where N is the number of peptides in the test set. Note that since we are calculating a partial AUC, [McClish standardisation](https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X8900900307) is used.","# Dataset Description

Test *(test.csv)* and sample submission *(sample_submission.csv)* files are provided for competitors to complete with binding probability predictions in a *'Prediction'* column indicating a probability between 0 and 1 that the peptide and TCR in a given row will bind. *NB: If there are peptides for which your model cannot make predictions, please use a default prediction of zero for these rows. A file of only zero-valued predictions will yield an AUC0.1 of 0.5.*

For training data, we provide a sample training dataset from the IEDB<sup>1</sup> as well as a [special VDJdb release](https://github.com/antigenomics/vdjdb-db/releases/tag/pyvdjdb-2025-02-21)<sup>2</sup>.

## Files

*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format (*test.csv* + *Prediction* column)
*   **iedb_positives.csv** - a sample training dataset of paired TCR-pHLA pairs compiled from the IEDB<sup>1</sup>.
*   **vdjdb_positives.csv** - a sample training dataset of paired TCR-pHLA pairs compiled from VDJdb<sup>2</sup>.

### Test/submission columns
*   `ID` - an identifier of the TCR-peptide pair used in test and submission files (NB: row order matters, do not change the row order or ID values)
*   `Peptide` - the peptide/epitope
*   `HLA`?- the HLA/MHC allele name
*   `Va` - the TCR alpha V gene name (IMGT reference as mapped by tcrdist3)
*   `Va_adaptive` - the TCR alpha V gene name (Adaptive reference set)
*   `Ja` - the TCR alpha J gene name (IMGT reference as mapped by tcrdist3)
*   `Ja_adaptive` - the TCR alpha J gene name (Adaptive reference set)
*   `TCRa` - the TCR alpha amino acid sequence
*   `CDR1a` - the TCR alpha CDR1 amino acid sequence
*   `CDR2a` - the TCR alpha CDR2 amino acid sequence
*   `CDR3a` - the TCR alpha CDR3 amino acid sequence (positions 105-117)
*   `CDR3a_extended` - the TCR alpha CDR3 amino acid sequence (positions 104-118)
*   `Vb` - the TCR beta V gene name (IMGT reference as mapped by tcrdist3)
*   `Vb_adaptive` - the TCR beta V gene name (Adaptive reference set)
*   `Jb` - the TCR beta J gene name (IMGT reference as mapped by tcrdist3)
*   `Jb` - the TCR beta J gene name (Adaptive reference set)
*   `TCRb` - the TCR beta amino acid sequence
*   `CDR1b` - the TCR beta CDR1 amino acid sequence
*   `CDR2b` - the TCR beta CDR2 amino acid sequence
*   `CDR3b` - the TCR beta CDR3 amino acid sequence (positions 105-117)
*   `CDR3b_extended` - the TCR beta CDR3 amino acid sequence (positions 104-118)
*   `Prediction` - your prediction of the probability of binding [0,1]

## Example training data

We include example training data from the IEDB (immrep_IEDB.csv) which includes the above sequence columns with the exception of ID and Prediction, and the additional columns: ""receptor_id"", ""references"" and ""just_10X"" as [described](https://github.com/erichardson97/IEDB_IMMREP).

In addition, VDJdb<sup>2</sup> has kindly created a [special release](https://github.com/antigenomics/vdjdb-db/releases/tag/pyvdjdb-2025-02-21) with the standard VDJdb format which includes multiple new datasets.

## Gene reference sets
IMGT TCR V and J gene labels have been included based on the lookup provided with tcrdist3<sup>3</sup> https://github.com/kmayerb/tcrdist3/blob/c856398ec6a52f320651baedbe9aea0d371105b3/tcrdist/db/adaptive_imgt_mapping.csv . Note that some of the gene names in this mapping may not be correct so teams are encouraged to map from TCRa and TCRb to their chosen IMGT reference version if 100% accuracy is material to predictions.

## References
<sup>1</sup> Vita, Randi, et al. The Immune Epitope Database (IEDB): 2024 update. Nucleic Acids Research 53.D1 (2025): D436-D443. https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkae1092
<sup>2</sup> Goncharov, M., Bagaev, D., Shcherbinin, D. et al. VDJdb in the pandemic era: a compendium of T cell receptors specific for SARS-CoV-2. Nat Methods 19, 1017¨C1019 (2022). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-022-01578-0
<sup>3</sup> Mayer-Blackwell K, Schattgen S, Cohen-Lavi L, Crawford JC, Souquette A, Gaevert JA, Hertz T, Thomas PG, Bradley PH, Fiore-Gartland A. eLife (2021).",False,True
8960,juridia-hackhaton-fine-tuning-llm-v,"# Overview

# Welcome to the **Juridia fine-tuning LLM Challenge**!  
| This competition is reserved to moroccan students.

Welcome to the **Juridia Hackathon**, where participants will explore the power of **large language models (LLMs)** in the legal domain! This competition challenges you to fine-tune open-source LLMs using bilingual legal datasets (French) to create high-quality, accurate, and efficient systems for understanding and responding to legal queries.  

---

## **Goal**  

The objective of this competition is to fine-tune an open-source LLM on legal data to deliver:  
- **Accurate Legal Understanding**: The ability to comprehend and process complex legal queries.  
- **Relevant and Precise Responses**: Generating legally sound, clear, and contextually appropriate answers in **French** and **Arabic**.  

Your model will be evaluated on its ability to produce responses that are **relevant**, **complete**, **legally accurate**, and **efficient**, while maintaining linguistic **fluency** and **consistency**.  

---

## **Be Careful**  

Fine-tuning an open-source large language models (LLMs) can require significant **time** and **resources**. To ensure smooth participation:  
- **Choose the appropriate model** size and architecture based on the competition constraints and your computational resources.  
- Utilize Kaggle¡¯s **CPU, GPU, or TPU** environments effectively. Larger models will benefit from GPU or TPU acceleration to reduce training time.  
- Monitor your resource usage and be mindful of submission limits to maximize your productivity during the competition.  
- Optimize your workflows: Consider batch sizes, learning rates, and fine-tuning techniques (e.g., **LoRA** or **PEFT**) to minimize computational overhead.  

Efficient resource management is key to succeeding in this competition! ??  
----------------------
# Description

The **Juridia Hackathon** is a unique opportunity to explore the fine-tuning of an open-source **Large Language Models (LLMs)** on a structured legal dataset. This competition focuses on adapting LLMs to understand and process legal data in **French** and **Arabic**.  

---

## The Domain: Legal Technology  

The legal field relies on **complex, formal, and structured documents** containing specialized terminology. Adapting LLMs to legal language is essential for building AI solutions that are **accurate**, **relevant**, and **trustworthy** in real-world legal applications.  

---

## The Challenge  

Participants will fine-tune pre-trained LLMs using a structured dataset of legal documents. The dataset includes the following fields:  

- `file_name`: Name of the legal file.  
- `long_title`: Full descriptive title of the document.  
- `date`: Publication date of the document.  
- `doc_type`: The type of legal document (e.g., loi, d¨¦cr¨ºt, arr¨ºt¨¦, etc.).
- `Id`: A unique identifier for each document.  

The task is to fine-tune your model to **generate high-quality outputs** that align with legal terminology, structure, and style in both **French** and **Arabic**.  

---

## Why This Matters  

Fine-tuning open-source LLMs for domain-specific tasks is a critical step in deploying AI solutions in specialized industries. The legal domain, with its **high standards for accuracy and precision**, represents a perfect use case for fine-tuning.  

By participating in this competition, you will:  
- Gain hands-on experience fine-tuning LLMs on **structured multilingual data**.  
- Tackle the challenges of processing legal texts in **French** and **Arabic**.  
- Contribute to advancing AI capabilities in the legal sector.  

---

## **The Dataset**

The dataset provided for this competition consists of **French-only** legal documents with clear structure and metadata. Participants are encouraged to leverage this data to fine-tune models that:  
- Understand legal terminology.  
- Adapt to legal domain-specific language in French.  
- Produce outputs that are **accurate**, **fluent**, and **legally sound**.  

---

## Tools and Resources  

To help you get started, here are some useful resources:  
- Pre-trained LLMs (e.g., Hugging Face Transformers).  

---

This is your chance to fine-tune models to tackle **real-world legal challenges** and showcase your skills in **AI and NLP**. Let¡¯s push the boundaries of LLMs together! ??  
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using a **weighted composite score** that balances **relevance**, **completeness**, **legal soundness**, **fluency**, **consistency**, and **response speed**. This ensures that models produce high-quality, accurate, and efficient outputs tailored to legal queries.  

The final score is calculated as:  

Final Score = w_1 * Relevance + w_2 * Completeness + w_3 * Legal Soundness + w_4 * Fluency - w_5 * Latency Penalty


Where:  
- **w? = 0.4**: **Relevance** - Measures how accurately the response matches the query intent.  
- **w? = 0.25**: **Completeness** - Assesses whether all essential details are included in the response.  
- **w? = 0.2**: **Legal Soundness** - Evaluates adherence to legal principles and accuracy of legal content.  
- **w? = 0.1**: **Fluency** - Measures grammatical correctness and clarity of the language (French/Arabic).  
- **w? = 0.05**: **Latency Penalty** - Penalizes slower response times.    

The **final weighted score** will range between **0** and **1**, with higher values indicating better performance.

---

## **Submission File**

For each input query, participants must submit a response in a **CSV format** with the following columns:

- **Id**: The unique identifier for each query.
- **relevance**: The mean relevance score for all predictions.
- **completeness**: The mean completeness score for all predictions.
- **legal_soundness**: The mean legal soundness score for all predictions.
- **fluency**: The mean fluency score for all predictions.
- **latency**: The mean latency (response time) for all predictions.

---

### **Submission Format Example**

The file should follow this structure:

| Id  | relevance  | completeness | legal_soundness | fluency | latency   |
|-----|------------|-------------|-----------------|---------|-----------|
| 0   | 0.349147   | 0.349147    | 1.0             | 0.95    | 0.000077  |

- The `Id` column represents the query ID.  
- All other columns (`relevance`, `completeness`, `legal_soundness`, `fluency`, `latency`) are **averaged values** across multiple rows (predictions) for that query.

---

### **Notes**
- Submissions will be evaluated using the competition's final score formula.  
- Ensure that the file adheres strictly to the example structure with appropriate column headers.  
- The file format must be a **CSV** with no missing values.  

Good luck, and happy fine-tuning! ??
