8961,juridia-hackhaton-fine-tuning-translation-model-v,"# Overview

# Welcome to the **Juridia Multilingual Legal Translation Challenge**!  
| This competition is reserved to moroccan students.

At **Juridia**, Morocco's leading legal AI company, we are dedicated to revolutionizing legal workflows with artificial intelligence. In this competition, we challenge you to **fine-tune an open-source machine translation model** that translates legal text:  
- **From French (`fr`)** to other target languages: Spanish (`es`), English (`en`), Chinese (`zh`), Arabic (`ar`), and Russian (`ru`).  

---

## **Goal**  
Your objective is to fine-tune a robust open-source translation model that accurately predicts translations for sentences provided in French. By solving this challenge, you contribute to improving **legal accessibility and efficiency** across linguistic barriers.  

---

## **Be Careful**  

Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) can require significant **time** and **resources**. To ensure smooth participation:  
- **Choose the appropriate model** size and architecture based on the competition constraints and your computational resources.  
- Utilize Kaggle¡¯s **CPU, GPU, or TPU** environments effectively. Larger models will benefit from GPU or TPU acceleration to reduce training time.  
- Monitor your resource usage and be mindful of submission limits to maximize your productivity during the competition.  
- Optimize your workflows: Consider batch sizes, learning rates, and fine-tuning techniques (e.g., **LoRA** or **PEFT**) to minimize computational overhead.  

Efficient resource management is key to succeeding in this competition! ??  
----------------------
# Description

# The **Juridia Multilingual Legal Translation Challenge**  

The **Juridia Multilingual Legal Translation Challenge** focuses on solving a real-world problem: **accurate legal translations**. Legal professionals often work with multilingual documents, such as contracts, statutes, and court decisions. Errors in translation can lead to misunderstandings, legal disputes, or inefficiencies.  

To address this challenge, participants are tasked with **fine-tuning an open-source machine translation model** to translate legal sentences from **French (`fr`)** into five major target languages:  
- **Spanish (`es`)**  
- **English (`en`)**  
- **Chinese (`zh`)**  
- **Arabic (`ar`)**  
- **Russian (`ru`)**  

---

## The Dataset  

The dataset consists of over **400,000 aligned sentences** across 6 languages. Each row represents the same sentence translated into:  
- **`ar`**: Arabic  
- **`fr`**: French  
- **`es`**: Spanish  
- **`en`**: English  
- **`zh`**: Chinese (Simplified)  
- **`ru`**: Russian  

### Example Row  
| ar                                | fr                                | es                                | en                                | zh                               | ru                               |
|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| ???? ?????? ??????? ?????? ...     | R¨¦solution adopt¨¦e par ...        | Resoluci¨®n aprobada por ...       | Resolution adopted by ...         | 2011Äê12ÔÂ19ÈÕ´ó»á¾öÒé            | §²§Ö§Ù§à§Ý§ð§è§Ú§ñ, §á§â§Ú§ß§ñ§ä§Ñ§ñ ...          |

Each column contains the sentence in the respective language.

---

## Objective  

Participants will fine-tune open-source machine learning models to predict translations:  
- **Input**: Sentences in French.  
- **Target**: Translations in Spanish, English, Chinese, Arabic, or Russian.  

---

## Why This Matters  

By fine-tuning accurate translation models, you contribute to:
- Enhancing legal accessibility across linguistic barriers.  
- Automating translation workflows for legal professionals.  
- Supporting Juridia¡¯s mission to develop cutting-edge legal AI solutions.  

We look forward to seeing your innovative approaches! ??  
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the **BLEU score** (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy). BLEU measures the accuracy of the predicted translations by comparing them to the ground truth reference translations. A higher BLEU score indicates better alignment with human translations.

---

## Submission File  

Participants must submit a CSV file with the following specifications:  
- Each row corresponds to a unique ID from the test set.
- Columns represent the target languages, with predictions for each language.

Your submission file should be a CSV with separator `|` with the following format:
| id  | ar               | fr               | es               | en               | zh               | ru               |
|-----|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|
| 1   | ???? ????????    | Texte en fran?ais| Texto en espa?ol | Text in English  | ÖÐÎÄÎÄ±¾           | §´§Ö§Ü§ã§ä §ß§Ñ §â§å§ã§ã§Ü§à§Þ |
| 2   | ????? ???????     | Bonjour le monde | Hola mundo       | Hello world      | ÄãºÃ£¬ÊÀ½ç         | §±§â§Ú§Ó§Ö§ä §Þ§Ú§â       |

---

### Notes  

- Ensure the column names match the example exactly: `id`, `ar`, `fr`, `es`, `en`, `zh`, `ru`.
- Use `|` as the column separator, not the default `,`.
- Submissions will be evaluated automatically using the BLEU metric.
- Predictions should strictly follow the example format to avoid scoring errors.

----------------------
# Prizes

Total Prizes Available: **1 of 4**

We are thrilled to reward the top participants with the following prizes:

?? **10,000 MAD** (cash prize)
?? **High-performance PC** *(valued at 15,000 MAD)*  
?? **10,000 USD in Microsoft Azure credits**  
?? **6-month internship at Juridia**  

### Non-Monetary Prizes:  
- The 6-month internship at Juridia will provide an opportunity to work on real-world AI solutions in the legal domain, contributing to groundbreaking projects in Morocco's legal tech industry.

Good luck, and may the best models win! ??","# Dataset Description

# Multilingual Machine Translation Competition  

Welcome to the multilingual machine translation competition. The primary objective of this competition is to **fine-tune an open-source translation model** that can translate sentences **from French (`fr`) to other languages** and **from Arabic (`ar`) to other languages**. Participants will use a multilingual dataset consisting of aligned translations across **5 languages**: French (`fr`), Arabic (`ar`), Spanish (`es`), English (`en`), Chinese (`zh`), and Russian (`ru`).  

This page outlines the provided files, the structure of the data, and the columns you¡¯ll encounter. Use this information to understand the dataset and prepare your submissions.  

---

## Files  

- **train.csv** - The training set containing sentences in 5 languages for supervised learning.  
- **test.csv** - The test set containing input sentences in **French (`fr`)**. Participants must predict their translations into other target languages.  

---

## Columns  

The dataset is organized as follows, with each column representing a language translation of the same sentence:  

- **example_id** *(if applicable)* - A unique identifier for each sentence.  
- **ar** - The Arabic translation of the sentence.  
- **fr** - The French translation of the sentence.  
- **es** - The Spanish translation of the sentence.  
- **en** - The English translation of the sentence.  
- **zh** - The Chinese (Simplified) translation of the sentence.  
- **ru** - The Russian translation of the sentence.  

Each row represents a single sentence, translated into these 5 languages.  

---

## Objective  

### What You¡¯re Fine-Tuning:  
Participants are tasked with fine-tuning open-source machine translation models to accurately predict translations for input sentences **from French (`fr`)** into other languages (`es`, `en`, `zh`, ¡®ar¡¯, or `ru`).  

**For example**:  
- **Input**: A sentence in `fr` (French).  
- **Target**: Predict its translation in `en` (English), `es` (Spanish), `zh` (Chinese), `ar` (Arabic), or `ru` (Russian).  

---

## Data Format  

### Example Row in `train.csv`:  
| id  | ar                                | fr                                | es                                | en                                | zh                               | ru                               |
|-----|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| 1   | ???? ?????? ??????? ?????? ...     | R¨¦solution adopt¨¦e par ...        | Resoluci¨®n aprobada por ...       | Resolution adopted by ...         | 2011Äê12ÔÂ19ÈÕ´ó»á¾öÒé            | §²§Ö§Ù§à§Ý§ð§è§Ú§ñ, §á§â§Ú§ß§ñ§ä§Ñ§ñ ...          |  

- **Each column** contains the sentence in the respective language.  
- Sentences are aligned across columns (i.e., row 1 contains the same sentence in all 5 languages).  

---

## Submission Format  

Your submission file should be a CSV with separator `|` with the following format:  

| id  | ar               | fr               | es               | en               | zh               | ru               |
|-----|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|
| 1   | ???? ????????    | Texte en fran?ais| Texto en espa?ol | Text in English  | ÖÐÎÄÎÄ±¾           | §´§Ö§Ü§ã§ä §ß§Ñ §â§å§ã§ã§Ü§à§Þ |
| 2   | ????? ???????     | Bonjour le monde | Hola mundo       | Hello world      | ÄãºÃ£¬ÊÀ½ç         | §±§â§Ú§Ó§Ö§ä §Þ§Ú§â       |  

---

## Notes and Acronyms  

- **Sentence Pairs**: Rows are aligned translations of the same sentence.  
- **Languages**:  
  - **ar** - Arabic  
  - **fr** - French  
  - **es** - Spanish  
  - **en** - English  
  - **zh** - Chinese (Simplified)  
  - **ru** - Russian  

Use the training data to model the relationships between these languages, with a focus on translating from **French** and **Arabic**.  

---

## Questions to Consider  

- How will you preprocess sentences (e.g., tokenization, case normalization)?  
- Will you fine-tune separate models for translating from `fr`, or use one unified multilingual model?  
- How can you fine-tune pretrained multilingual models like **mBART**, **MarianMT**, or **NLLB-200** for this task?  
- What techniques will you use to handle low-resource languages (if applicable)?  

---

Good luck, and happy modeling! ??",False,True
8962,faim-sgp-25-j-sarah-gets-a-diamond,"# Description

Case: ¡°Sarah Gets a Diamond¡±, UVA-QA-0702 + data

The file ¡°Sarah Gets a Diamond data.xls¡± contains data about various attributes for over 9000 diamonds. For the first 6000 you also have the price. 

The goal of this competition is to use the concepts and tools you've learned in Sessions 2-4 to predict the prices of the remaining diamonds (i.e., those with IDs 6001 and above). 

When done, create a spreadsheet containing two columns: ID and Price. For IDs, put 6001, 6002, etc., and for the prices, put the numerical values of your predictions (without dollar signs, or any other strings), e.g., 12345.6, 23456.7, etc.

Save the resultant file in the CSV format and submit it. Make sure the column names follow the uppercase and lowercase format and are exactly ID and Price:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1489777%2F9cc19c90fa3db1214edc371261ef142c%2FSarah%20Gets%20a%20Diamond%20solution%20-%20sample.JPG?generation=1734625749156314&alt=media)

Your submission will be immediately scored on a fraction of the diamonds according to the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) metric that we will discuss in class. The lower, the better. 

## Acknowledgements

http://store.darden.virginia.edu/sarah-gets-a-diamond 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The goal of this competition is to use the tools you've learned in Sessions 2-4 to predict the prices of the remaining diamonds (i.e., those with IDs 6001 and above).

When done, create a spreadsheet containing two columns: ID and Price. For IDs, put 6001, 6002, etc., and for the prices, put the numerical values of your predictions (without dollar signs, or any other strings), e.g., 12345.6, 23456.7, etc.

Save the resultant file in the CSV format and submit it. Make sure the column names follow the uppercase and lowercase format and are exactly ID and Price. See the example in the Description section. 

Your submission will be immediately scored on a fraction of the diamonds according to the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) metric that we will discuss in class. The lower, the better.","# Dataset Description

