8964,reds-hackathon-2025,"# Overview

The Cincinnati Reds are proud to present our second annual Hackathon, where teams have the opportunity to work with MLB data to design solutions to challenges faced by MLB front offices. This year, we focus on the broader challenge of predicting playing time, a challenge with wide ranging impacts.
----------------------
# Description

The quote ¡°the greatest ability is availability¡± comes from football but serves true in baseball as well. Players can only provide value as long as they are on the field. That is why the challenge of predicting how much a player will play has such broad impacts. We measure playing time in plate appearances (PA) for batters and batters faced (BF) for pitchers.

Accurate knowledge of how much a player will play can influence player valuation, contract structure, team planning, and many more areas. As such, having better playing time predictions allows front offices to be more efficient in their team building and planning.

## Question
With the provided data, we challenge you to predict the total playing time (PA/BF) players will receive in 2024. You will predict the sum total of plate appearances as a batter and batters faced as a pitcher for each player.

One piece of guidance to share is that it is essential to have a 'roster-agnostic' understanding of a player's predicted playing time -- meaning that other players on the roster do not affect that player's playing time. Front offices are interested in how much they can expect that player to play in a vacuum. In the case that they play less time than that because they are blocked at the position by someone performing better, that is an okay tradeoff.

How you answer this question is entirely up to you, and we encourage you to be creative with your data-driven approaches, player selection, and role proposals within your submission. But at a minimum, our team is expecting to see a(n):
- Clear, defined analytical process;
- Detailed explanation of your model(s) and modeling decisions; and
- Explanation of your findings about driving factors of batter and pitcher playing time.

----------------------
# Participation Options

The Cincinnati Reds welcome individuals participating for fun. Additionally, current college students are invited to participate in the notebook competition from which prizes will be awarded. In order to identify your team as a college team, you must fill out [this form](https://forms.office.com/Pages/ResponsePage.aspx?id=tz1cC3AJmUmRXtGdOAGqQPvTxXJS-flPhx-uKREel-VURUNYRzBNRVFQMjUwQlcwUjZUWVE2MzJMSi4u) with your .edu email addresses.
----------------------
# Finalist Prizes

Our four finalist teams will be rewarded with:

1. Presenting their work to Reds Analytics and Operations staff;
2. Public mention and highlighting of finalists' work; and
3. Reds prize packs.

Additionally, last year's finalists felt they had an easier time navigating the baseball hiring process and the Reds Analytics trainee application in particular.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Scoring
Submissions will be initially evaluated on accuracy of their playing time predictions (total plate appearances as a batter + total batters faced as a pitcher) predictions using [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_mean_square_deviation).

Subsequently, the Reds will evaluate the top 15% (maximum of 15) of notebook submissions detailing their approach and results in the college competition on three equally weighted criteria.

1. Data Science: Is the approach fundamentally sound? Are the selected statistical models appropriate given the data and the problem at hand? Do the analytical applications serve to answer the questions in a technical and thoughtful manner? 
2. Baseball: Are the conclusions derived from the data? Does the analysis demonstrate knowledge of baseball-specific data and nuances? Are the suggestions applicable for an MLB team to use and implement? 
3. Communication: Is the write-up easy to follow? Do the figures enhance the report and analysis? Are you able to clearly and concisely communicate your process, proposals, and justifications?

The top four teams after this review will be invited to present their approach and identify players who they believe should have had more playing time in the 2024 season in front of Reds Analytics staff.

## Initial Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict an playing time estimate for the target variable. The target variable is the OPS each batter would produce after the trade deadline in the 2025 season. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    PLAYER_ID,PLAYING_TIME
    2,475
    5,623
    6,57
    etc.

## Notebook Requirements
If you are participating in the college competition, you should submit a code notebook containing no more than 1,500 words and 8 figures or tables. This notebook should detail your process and the decisions you made along the way. Notebook submissions will not be penalized for any number of words or figures below these restrictions. We think it will be valuable to include code, but would prefer for it to be included in an appendix. We will not count code towards word count. 

A good example of a well-formatted notebook is the [STRAIN submission](https://www.kaggle.com/code/statsinthewild/strain-sacks-tackles-rushing-aggression-index) from the 2023 Big Data Bowl.
----------------------
# Get Started

If you are looking for some help getting started, check out [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/bebrahim/getting-started-and-benchmark-solution), which demonstrates how we made the sample submission.

We recommend downloading the data and following along to learn more about making the outcome variables and generating a solution set.

From there, you should be all set to build more complex models and submit them to the competition!","# Dataset Description

Data from Baseball Savant and the Lahman database is provided for use on this project. Please do not add any data beyond the provided datasets.

## Files

*   **savant_data_2021_2023.csv** - Baseball savant pitch-by-pitch data for regular season MLB games from 2021-23
*   **lahman_people.csv** - Lahman database's people file with data about players
*   **codebook.xlsx** - Codebook for Savant and Lahman data including descriptions of all columns
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format with all of the hashed player IDs for which you need to make predictions (just replace the values in the `PLAYING_TIME` column)

## Codebook
### Savant Pitch-by-Pitch Data
`pitch_type`: The type of pitch derived from Statcast
`game_date`: Date of the game
`release_speed`: Pitch velocities from 2008-16 are via Pitch F/X, and adjusted to roughly out-of-hand release point. All velocities from 2017 and beyond are Statcast, which are reported out-of-hand
`release_pos_x`: Horizontal Release Position of the ball measured in feet from the catcher's perspective
`release_pos_z`: Vertical Release Position of the ball measured in feet from the catcher's perspective
`batter`: MLB player id tied to the play event for the batter, anonymized via hashing
`pitcher`: MLB player id tied to the play event for the pitcher, anonymized via hashing
`events`: Event of the resulting plate appearance
`description`: Description of the resulting pitch
`zone`: Zone location of the ball when it crosses the plate from the catcher's perspective
`game_type`: Type of Game (E = Exhibition, S = Spring Training, R = Regular Season, F = Wild Card, D = Divisional Series, L = League Championship Series, W = World Series); This value should be R for all data in this dataset
`stand`: Side of the plate batter is standing
`p_throws`: Hand pitcher throws with
`home_team`: Abbreviation of the home team, anonymized via hashing
`away_team`: Abbreviation of the away team, anonymized via hashing
`type`: Short hand of pitch result (B = ball, S = strike, X = in play)
`hit_location`: Position of first fielder to touch the ball
`bb_type`: Batted ball type, ground_ball, line_drive, fly_ball, popup
`balls`: Pre-pitch number of balls in count
`strikes`: Pre-pitch number of strikes in count
`game_year`: Year the game took place
`pfx_x`: Horizontal movement in feet from the catcher's perspective
`pfx_z`: Vertical movement in feet from the catcher's perspective
`plate_x`: Horizontal position of the ball when it crosses home plate from the catcher's perspective
`plate_z`: Vertical position of the ball when it crosses home plate from the catcher's perspective
`on_3b`: Pre-pitch MLB Player Id of Runner on 3B, anonymized via hashing
`on_2b`: Pre-pitch MLB Player Id of Runner on 2B, anonymized via hashing
`on_1b`: Pre-pitch MLB Player Id of Runner on 1B, anonymized via hashing
`outs_when_up`: Pre-pitch number of outs
`inning`: Pre-pitch inning number
`inning_topbot`: Pre-pitch top or bottom of inning
`hc_x`: Hit coordinate X of batted ball
`hc_y`: Hit coordinate Y of batted ball
`fielder_2`: Pre-pitch MLB Player Id of Catcher, anonymized via hashing
`sv_id`: Non-unique Id of play event per game
`vx0`: The velocity of the pitch, in feet per second, in x-dimension, determined at y=50 feet
`vy0`: The velocity of the pitch, in feet per second, in y-dimension, determined at y=50 feet
`vz0`: The velocity of the pitch, in feet per second, in z-dimension, determined at y=50 feet
`ax`: The acceleration of the pitch, in feet per second per second, in x-dimension, determined at y=50 feet
`ay`: The acceleration of the pitch, in feet per second per second, in y-dimension, determined at y=50 feet
`az`: The acceleration of the pitch, in feet per second per second, in z-dimension, determined at y=50 feet
`sz_top`: Top of the batter's strike zone set by the operator when the ball is halfway to the plate
`sz_bot`: Bottom of the batter's strike zone set by the operator when the ball is halfway to the plate
`hit_distance_sc`: Projected hit distance of the batted ball
`launch_speed`: Exit velocity of the batted ball as tracked by Statcast
`launch_angle`: Launch angle of the batted ball as tracked by Statcast
`effective_speed`: Derived speed based on the the extension of the pitcher's release
`release_spin_rate`: Spin rate of pitch tracked by Statcast
`release_extension`: Release extension of pitch in feet as tracked by Statcast
`game_pk`: Unique Id for Game
`pitcher_1`: MLB Player Id tied to the play event, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_2_1`: MLB Player Id for catcher, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_3`: MLB Player Id for 1B, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_4`: MLB Player Id for 2B, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_5`: MLB Player Id for 3B, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_6`: MLB Player Id for SS, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_7`: MLB Player Id for LF, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_8`: MLB Player Id for CF, anonymized via hashing
`fielder_9`: MLB Player Id for RF, anonymized via hashing
`release_pos_y`: Release position of pitch measured in feet from the catcher's perspective
`estimated_ba_using_speedangle`: Estimated Batting Avg based on launch angle and exit velocity
`estimated_woba_using_speedangle`: Estimated wOBA based on launch angle and exit velocity
`woba_value`: wOBA value based on result of play
`woba_denom`: wOBA denominator based on result of play
`babip_value`: BABIP value based on result of play
`iso_value`: ISO value based on result of play.
`launch_speed_angle`: Launch speed/angle zone based on launch angle and exit velocity. (1: Weak, 2: Topped, 3: Under, 4: Flare/Burner, 5: Solid Contact, 6: Barrel)
`at_bat_number`: Plate appearance number of the game
`pitch_number`: Total pitch number of the plate appearance
`pitch_name`: The name of the pitch derived from the Statcast Data
`home_score`: Pre-pitch home score
`away_score`: Pre-pitch away score
`bat_score`: Pre-pitch bat team score
`fld_score`: Pre-pitch field team score
`post_away_score`: Post-pitch home score
`post_home_score`: Post-pitch away score
`post_bat_score`: Post-pitch bat team score
`post_fld_score`: Post-pitch field team score
`if_fielding_alignment`: Infield fielding alignment at the time of the pitch
`of_fielding_alignment`: Outfield fielding alignment at the time of the pitch
`spin_axis`: The Spin Axis in the 2D X-Z plane in degrees from 0 to 360, such that 180 represents a pure backspin fastball and 0 degrees represents a pure topspin (12-6) curveball
`delta_home_win_exp`: The change in Win Expectancy before the Plate Appearance and after the Plate Appearance
`delta_run_exp`: The change in Run Expectancy before the Pitch and after the Pitch
`role_key`: The role of the pitcher; SP is a starting pitcher, RP is a relief pitcher
`sp_indicator`: An indicator of whether or not the pitcher on the pitch is the starting pitcher, 1 indicates that it is the starting pitcher, 0 indicates that it is not 
`rp_indicator`: An indicator of whether or not the pitcher on the pitch is the relief pitcher, 1 indicates that it is the relief pitcher, 0 indicates that it is not 
`pitch_number_appearance`: The pitch number within the appearance for a pitcher (ex. A pitcher's 76th pitch of their outing)
`pitcher_at_bat_number`: The batter number that the pitcher has faced within the appearance (ex. The 13th batter a pitcher faced in a given outing)
`times_faced`: The number of times the batter and pitcher have faced each other within a given game (ex. Batter A is facing Pitcher B for the 2nd time today)

### Lahman People Data
`player_mlb_id`: MLB player ID, anonymized via hashing
`playerID_LAHMAN`: A unique code assigned to each player in the Lahman database, anonymized via hashing
`birthYear`: Year player was born
`birthMonth`: Month player was born
`birthDay`: Day player was born
`birthCountry`: Country where player was born
`weight`: Player's weight in pounds
`height`: Player's height in inches
`bats`: Player's batting hand (left, right, or both)
`throws`: Player's throwing hand (left or right)
`debut`: Date that player made first major league appearance
`birthDate`: Date player was born",False,True
8965,traffic-signal-detector-classifier,"# Overview

Welcome to the Traffic Signal Detector Configuration Challenge! This competition aims to automate the generation of detector configurations for Automated Traffic Signal Performance Measures (ATSPM) using machine learning.

Participants are provided with a week of high-resolution event data from over 400 traffic signals and tasked with training a model to predict two key attributes for each detector: its phase (1-8) and its function (Presence, Count, or Advanced detection). This challenge presents an exciting time series analysis problem with real-world applications that could improve traffic flow and even impact your daily commute!
----------------------
# Description

Traffic signal detector configurations are used to generate Automated Traffic Signal Performance Measures (ATSPM), which are used by traffic engineers for monitoring, troubleshooting, and evaluating signal performance. This competition focuses on just the two most crucial pieces of detector information needed for ATSPMs:
- The signal phase it's associated with (1-8)
- Its function (Presence, Count, Advanced detection)

Currently, this configuration is done manually by traffic engineers, which is time-consuming and prone to errors. This competition challenges participants to develop models that can automatically predict these configurations using high-resolution event data from traffic controllers.

**Goal:** Develop a machine learning model that can accurately predict both the phase assignment and detector function for traffic signal detectors based on their event patterns in high-resolution data logs.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using weighted F1-scores for both phase and function predictions, with the final score being the average of both scores.

The F1-score was chosen over simple accuracy because it better handles imbalanced classes, which is important for traffic signal data where some phases or functions may be more common than others. While accuracy simply measures the percentage of correct predictions, F1-score considers both precision (avoiding false positives) and recall (avoiding false negatives). This means a model can't achieve a good score by simply predicting the most common class.

The scoring breakdown is:
- Phase Prediction: Weighted F1-score for multi-class prediction (1-8)
- Function Prediction: Weighted F1-score for categorical prediction (Presence, Stopbar Count, Advance)

Final Score = (Phase_F1 + Function_F1) / 2

A perfect score is 1.0, indicating perfect predictions for both Phase and Function. A score above 0.7 is generally considered good performance, while scores below 0.5 may indicate room for improvement.

## Submission File
Your submission file must be a CSV with the following format:

    Row,DeviceId,Detector,Phase,Function
    1,e973d7bf-a676-4908-99f0-346a69ce5f11,1,7,Presence
    2,e973d7bf-a676-4908-99f0-346a69ce5f11,22,6,Count
    3,e973d7bf-a676-4908-99f0-346a69ce5f11,2,4,Advance
    etc.

Where:
- Row: Sequential numbers from 1 to 590 (required by Kaggle for submission alignment)
- DeviceId: The unique identifier for the traffic signal controller
- Detector: The detector number within that controller
- Phase: Integer between 1-8
- Function: One of the predefined detector functions (Presence, Count, Advance)

You can download a sample submission file (sandbox_submission.csv) to see the expected format.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains high-resolution event logs from 413 traffic signals operated by the Oregon Department of Transportation, along with their verified detector configurations.

## Files
- **train_events_1.parquet - train_events_7.parquet** - High-resolution event logs for 370 traffic signals (Dec 1-7, 2024)
- **test_events.parquet** - High-resolution event logs for 43 traffic signals (Dec 1-7, 2024)
- **train_config.csv** - Known detector configurations for training signals
- **test_config.csv** - Sample submission format (DeviceId and Detector numbers only)
- **sample_notebook.ipynb** - Starter code demonstrating data processing and basic LSTM model in PyTorch

## Data Fields

### Event Logs:
- `DeviceId` - Unique identifier for the traffic signal controller
- `Timestamp` - Time of the event
- `EventID` - Type of event:
  - 81: Detector Off
  - 82: Detector On
  - 1: Phase Begin Green
  - 7: Phase End Green
- `Parameter` - Context-dependent value:
  - For detector events (81,82): detector number
  - For phase events (1,7): phase number

### Configuration Files (train_config.csv, test_config.csv):
- `DeviceId` - Unique identifier for the traffic signal controller
- `Detector` - Detector number within the controller
- `Phase` - Signal phase assignment (1-8)
- `Function` - Detector function:
  - Presence: Vehicle presence detection at stopbar (20 feet long)
  - Stopbar Count: Count vehicles at stopbar
  - Advance: Upstream count detection",False,True
8966,eeg-stew-signal-enhancement-and-modeling-challeng,"# Overview

Test deep learning models for 7¨C32 Hz & 7¨C40 Hz bandpass filtering to enhance accuracy.
Extend ICA: remove components, reconstruct signals, research missing steps, and test impact.
Improve EEG channel selection by implementing better algorithms and validating relevance.
Address low PSNR; analyze SNR before/after preprocessing and explore additional metrics.
Debug pretrained model results; refine code and ensure accurate performance evaluation.
Design a hybrid model combining pretrained and custom components for EEG analysis.
----------------------
# Description

1. Preprocessing ¨C Bandpass Filtering
Tasks:
Experiment with deep learning models using specific bandpass frequency ranges:
7¨C32 Hz.
7¨C40 Hz.
Analyze if narrowing or expanding the frequency range improves model accuracy.
Validate these frequency ranges' impact on noise reduction and feature enhancement in EEG signals.
2. Preprocessing ¨C ICA (Independent Component Analysis)
Tasks:
Expand the current ICA implementation:
Add steps for removing unnecessary components after applying ICA.
Reconstruct the EEG signal post-component removal.
Research missing steps in the current implementation of ICA.
Investigate alternate ICA techniques or additional steps for better artifact removal and improved signal quality.
Test the effect of these enhancements on model performance.
3. Research Contribution ¨C Channel Selection
Tasks:
Re-evaluate the algorithm for EEG channel selection:
The current method includes all channels, reducing its effectiveness.
Explore and implement more robust algorithms tailored to EEG signal analysis.
Focus on algorithms that identify and select the most relevant channels for the task.
Assess how improved channel selection impacts the final results.
4. Comparison Metrics ¨C Before and After Preprocessing
Tasks:
Address the low PSNR (Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio):
Investigate if it is caused by incomplete ICA preprocessing.
Modify preprocessing steps accordingly.
Compute and analyze SNR (Signal-to-Noise Ratio) before and after preprocessing to measure improvement.
Consider using additional comparison metrics (e.g., MSE, SSIM) to provide a comprehensive evaluation of preprocessing.
5. Pretrained Model Results
Tasks:
Debug and refine the code for the pretrained models:
Currently, the results table shows identical accuracy, precision, and recall for all models, suggesting errors in implementation or evaluation.
Cross-validate the models with updated datasets and preprocessing methods.
Generate new performance metrics and plots reflecting improvements.
6. Final Model Architecture
Tasks:
Design a novel model architecture combining:
Pretrained models.
Custom-designed components tailored to EEG analysis.
Implement and test this combined architecture.
Fine-tune hyperparameters for optimal performance on the EEG dataset.
Additional Suggestions:
Regularly benchmark improvements using updated metrics.
Document all changes for reproducibility and comparison
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.",,False,True
8967,induction-task,"# Overview


Welcome to The Gradient Hunt!

This is Task 1 of the Cynaptics Induction Challenge. In this exciting competition, you'll dive deep into the world of pixels and tensors, putting your image processing and deep learning skills to the test. Your mission: develop an ML algorithm capable of distinguishing between real and AI-generated images.

To assist you, we've provided a dataset featuring a diverse array of images, including themes such as technology, landscapes, food, and festive celebrations.

This project is not just fun but also impactful. As AI continues to advance, distinguishing real content from AI-generated content is becoming increasingly challenging. A robust algorithm in this domain could significantly enhance authenticity and trust across various applications, including media and content creation.

----------------------
# Description

##Problem Statement
Develop and algorithm to distinguish between Real world and AI generated images utilizing various ML techniques such as image classification. The second sub task of the problem demands training a GAN and using it to classify images. Given a labelled dataset of AI and Real images you need to use it for training and submit your predictions on test set.

##Dataset
The dataset comprises of 2 folders, Train consisting of AI containing AI generated images, Real containing Real world images and Test on which you need to make the prediction. The dataset contains around 800 images in the training set almost uniformly distributed between both the labels and 200 images in the test set.

##Duration

##Submission
The participants need to make their submissions in the csv file format. The csv should follow the format as mentioned below:

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation will be done using the accuracy metrics:

The formula for accuracy is:

**Accuracy = Number of Correct Predictions / Total Number of Predictions**

---

Submissions are evaluated on [area under the ROC curve](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,Label
    image_1,AI
    image_2,Real
    image_3,Real
    etc.","# Dataset Description

In the data you will find a zip file, you need to download and extract it. The dataset consists of 2 sets train and test. The train folder contains further 2 folders AI which contains the AI generated images and Real containing real images scraped from internet. Test folder contains images on which you need to make your submission, images are named as image_1, image_2, etc. 

## Columns

*   `Id` - Name of the Image
*   `Label`?- Predicted label",False,True
