### !!!ATTENTION EVERYONE!!! Google form release for report and code submission

Hey everyone, here is the final Google form for submission of the report and the codebase. (Keep in mind that your submission is in the format below). You must submit this Google form with the proper report and code to be eligible for the interviews, as it is a major part of the evaluation criteria.

**NOTE: YOUR SUBMISSION WILL NOT BE CONSIDERED IF YOUR NAME IS NOT IN THE FORMAT `<NAME> <ENROLLMENT NUMBER>` ON THE KAGGLE LEADERBOARD**
- Codebase: A well-documented codebase on GitHub, including a README file with precise details about the code and mention of the vital part of your implementation. Comments throughout your code to explain key steps in your implementation.
- Performance Report: A concise report that includes Insights into the challenges faced during development, how you addressed them, and observations about the relationships in the visual data. Explain your model and it's working with an ""Explainability report"", talking about how and what it's processing. [Sample Report](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1WjKSw1PwhrIYH9tfSfFc06Dq_0-rLS2oxxT6KnZNEt8/edit?usp=sharing)

**Form link**: https://forms.gle/yFdGLqRWuM2gp9Xn6
(The form will close on 14th January 11:59 pm)

-----

### A NEW PACKAGE ARRIVES!!!! 

Check out our new HOTS extension to Pixel Play'25 (Note: this is not compulsory to do and do it if you are completely comfortable with the main challenge and have completed your report). Check out the details in this notion page:
https://golden-crush-c7c.notion.site/A-Mysterious-Package-Arrives-c880bb10b7b54d02956a528141c83636

-----

### **The Case of Sherlock Holmes and the Lost Bestiary**

One foggy evening at 221B Baker Street, Sherlock Holmes sat immersed in a most peculiar case. His client, the renowned explorer Sir Arkin Willborne, burst into the room in a state of disarray.

¡°Mr. Holmes,¡± he began, ¡°my life¡¯s work is in shambles! The Bestiary¡ªa collection cataloging the most extraordinary creatures of the animal kingdom¡ªhas been scattered! Their portraits, captured over decades, were destroyed in a storm. Only the essence of these creatures remains. How can I identify them now?¡±
![sherlock](https://res.cloudinary.com/teepublic/image/private/s--m4hUZDDA--/t_Preview/b_rgb:ffffff,c_lpad,f_jpg,h_630,q_90,w_1200/v1494046236/production/designs/1573165_1.jpg)
Ever intrigued by the challenge of deduction, Holmes leaned forward, his piercing eyes alive with interest. ¡°Fear not, Sir Willborne. If there is one thing I understand, it is the power of inference. But this task, I believe, may be better suited to a new kind of detective.¡±
----------------------
# Description

### **Your Mission**

You have been summoned by none other than Sherlock Holmes to assist in solving this most enigmatic mystery. Your task is to build a model that acts as Holmes's assistant, identifying unseen animals using only the remaining clues. During the ultimate test, your model must identify creatures from *unseen classes* based solely on the left behind information. Much like the famous Reichenbach Falls, the images will be lost to sight. 

""Watson,"" Holmes said, lighting his pipe, ""the game is afoot! But this time, it is a game of machines and minds. Let us see if our collaborators can crack the case."" The question remains: can you rise to the occasion and restore Sir Wildborne's Bestiary, solving the greatest mystery of the animal kingdom? 

You MUST register first on the Kaggle competition page and make submissions with the account name as: ""`<name> <enrollment number>` ""By the end of the competition, the following will be the deliverables,

### **Deliverables**

#### Throughout the length of the competition
- **Predictions on the Kaggle Competition page**: A file containing your model's predictions for the test images, formatted according to the provided guidelines. Submit predictions in a CSV file with the following format:
        
        ```
        image_id, class
        00001.jpg, Class_1
        00002.jpg, Class_2
        ...
        ```
You are allowed 5 prediction submissions, so try various methods; all creative approaches are welcome.
 
#### Towards the end of the competition
- **Codebase**: A well-documented codebase on GitHub, including a README file with precise details about the code and mention of the vital part of your implementation. Comments throughout your code to explain key steps in your implementation.
- **Performance Report**: A concise report that includes Insights into the challenges faced during development, how you addressed them, and observations about the relationships in the visual data. Explain your model and it's working with an **""Explainability report""**, talking about how and what it's processing.

We will share a Google form towards the end of the challenge for the report.

Note: Don't fret if your initial models don't perform as well as expected. Even a 50% accuracy is good for this challenge ??
----------------------
# Evaluation

### **Evaluation Criteria**

Submissions will be evaluated based on the following criteria:

1. **Classification Accuracy**:
    - The accuracy of your model in predicting the correct labels for unseen class images.
    - Higher accuracy indicates better generalization to unseen classes.
2. **Documentation and Reproducibility**:
    - The clarity and structure of your code and documentation ensure the solution is easy to understand and reproduce.
    - While focusing on technical accuracy, creative approaches will also be considered. So, Don't worry about minor differences in performance.


Here is a Sample Report with the sections we expect in an ideal report:

[**Animal Classification task [Sample Report]**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1WjKSw1PwhrIYH9tfSfFc06Dq_0-rLS2oxxT6KnZNEt8/edit?usp=sharing)

You can also check the **sample code and implementation in the Code Tab of the competition.**
For more help with set up refer to this: [**HackMD**](https://hackmd.io/@HXdLo96_R_-Z9OVYMXDDAg/r1hPkX-U1l)


---

Maintain the codebase repository, and towards the end of the challenge, we will float a Google form that will be required to fill out for your submission to be considered. Meanwhile, you can submit your predictions on the Kaggle completion, with up to 5 daily submissions. 

Join the VLG Open Slack for doubts and reporting issues, join the `#pixel-play-25-challenge` channel: [**Open Slack**](https://join.slack.com/t/vlgopenspace/shared_invite/zt-2w5xy45k0-pw0227Ltgdyys_0VjIAsXg)

Good luck, and may the best model win!","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains the train and test folders containing the images and some files about all the animal classes and features. Your task is to submit the correctly labeled test.csv in the following format (You can also refer to sample_submission.csv): 

        ```
        image_id, class
        00001.jpg, Class_1
        00002.jpg, Class_2
        ...
        ``` 
You can access the data using this link as well: [**Google Drive**](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1TXb6URsoBwNQ8I4BCzObZc092vwd4rwz?usp=sharing)

## Files

*   **Train Folder** - the training set for your model, categories, and images has been given for the remaining classes
*   **Test Folder** - the test set containing all images that need to be classified. Make a test.csv file for the submission
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format of how the test.csv should look like
*   **classes.txt** - All (50) classes that the test folder contains (both seen and unseen)
*   **predicates.txt** - All (85) features that the test folder contains (both seen and unseen)
*   **predicate-matrix-binary.txt** - A mapping matrix of size (50x85) (binarized)
*   **predicate-matrix-continous.txt** - A mapping matrix of size (50x85) (continous)",False,True
8984,project-4-whale-calls-2024,"# Overview

**Dataset: Whale Calls to Prevent Collisions**
The problem is to classify a case as a right whale or not based on an audio signal. Those positive cases contain a set of right whale up-calls. Up-calls are the most commonly documented right whale vocalization with an acoustic signature of approximately 60Hz-250Hz, typically lasting 1 second. Each series is labeled as either containing a right whale or not with the aim of identifying the series that contains up-calls correctly. Right whale calls can often be difficult to hear as the low-frequency band can become congested with anthropogenic sounds such as ship noise, drilling, piling, or naval operations.

Each case is a two-second audio segment sampled at 2kHz, giving a series length of 4000. 

**Instructions**
- Analyze and extract the features of the dataset pertinent to the problem you selected in the previous step. Justify your decisions.

- Choose at least two methods (including at least one learned in class) to solve the proposed challenge you selected. Justify your decisions.
    
- Report the appropriate metrics. Analyze and discuss the results of each method.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **test** - folder with audios of the testing set
*   **train** - folder with audios of the training set
*   **train.csv** - ids and classes for the training set
*   **sample_submission.txt** - sample submission file",False,True
8985,agent-society-challenge,"# Overview

The **AgentSociety Challenge** is an innovative competition designed to explore the potential of large language model (LLM) agents in modeling online social interaction and enhancing information retrieval systems. **The competition organizers will select the winners and recommend participants to submit their solutions as research papers for inclusion in the WWW¡¯25 companion proceedings.** Authors of accepted papers will have the opportunity to present their work at the conference which is held in Sydney. [WWW Accepted-Competitions](https://www2025.thewebconf.org/accepted-competitions ).

### About Token Support    
To support participants, we will provide API-key access during the development phase. This will be offered through InfinigenceAI, with the following specifications: RPM=100, TPM=60000, RPD=10000,and the ability to call models such as Qwen-72b-instruct. If you wish to use the API, please send your identification documents via email. Required documents include your full name, organization, proof of participation in this competition, track of participation and any other relevant identification details. Once your verification is completed, we will send you the API key. Please note that the number of API keys is **limited**.

## Award  
To encourage and reward excellence, the competition will offer prizes for first, second, and third place **FOR EACH TRACK**. The first place team will receive 3,000 USD, the second place team will receive 1,000 USD, and the third place team will receive 500 USD. (all pre-tax)
----------------------
# Timeline

The schedule for the competition is as follows£º
1. Start of the Competition £¨Registration£©£º Dec 10th, 2024-Jun 18th, 2025.
2. Official Competition: Dec 18th, 2024 -Jan 25th, 2025.
	* Development Phase: Dec 30th, 2024 - Jan 23th, 2025.
	* Final Phase: Jun 24th, 2025 - Jun 30th, 2025.
3. Winner Notification and Per Invitation: Feb 3rd, 2025.
4. Camera-ready Submission: Feb 17th, 2025.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, we are inspired by the concept of modular agent design and adopt an actionable modular design space with four key modules: **Planning**, **Reasoning**, **Tool use**, and **Memory**. These modules are orchestrated in a designed workflow: upon receiving a task query, the agent begins by decomposing it into sub-tasks through the Planning module. The Reasoning module then processes each sub-task, and when necessary, activates the Tool use module to select external tools for problem-solving. Additionally, the Memory module supports the reasoning process by accessing past observations and experiences, ensuring the agent continuously refines its actions based on feedback from the environment. The participants are tasked to construct effective agent modules that work together to enhance the agent¡¯s ability to process information, make decisions, and interact with dynamic environments in IR tasks. 

### Modular Agent Design Space
Within this framework, participants can explore LLM agent design with three levels:
1. Module Recombination: Designing novel agent by recombining the classic modules (reasoning, planning, tool use, and memory).
2. Module Design: Developing or modifying modules to implement new functionalities while ensuring compatibility with the standardized framework.
3. Workflow Construction: Designing the overall structure and interaction flow of the agentic system to effectively integrate the modules and achieve the desired outcomes.  

With the standard IO interfaces of these modules, participants can easily integrate different classic modules, facilitating the discovery of novel agent architectures through module recombination. This approach allows for the reuse of prior successful designs, significantly lowering the barrier for participants who may not have deep knowledge of LLM agents.Besides, this challenge also supports more in-depth agent design, allowing participants to reprogram each module and even rewrite the entire workflow. One key motivation of our challenge design is to ensure the discovered modules, with standard IO interfaces, can be reused in future research and applications, benefiting the LLM agent and web community. In the final phase, submissions will be required to adhere to a modular design.

Specificly, this challenge consists of two tracks: **User Behavior Modeling Track**, which focuses on simulating user behavior in specific scenarios by leveraging their historical actions and accessible environmental data.In this track, we utilized datasets from **Yelp**, **Amazon**, and **Goodreads** to build a text simulation environment that allows participants to come up with new solutions to these problems in an interactive environment in agentic mode, ensuring a comprehensive and diverse evaluation of user behaviors; **Recommendation Track**, which emphasizes building personalized recommendation assistants that provide users with appropriate suggestions tailored to particular contexts. These tracks focus on key problems in information retrieval (IR), like understanding user intent, context-aware decision-making, and adaptive response generation. They aim to advance research in behavioral modeling and recommendation systems, driving innovation in how information is retrieved and applied to support users in various situations.In the User Behavior Modeling Track, participants must design agents to simulate user reviews and star ratings. In the Recommendation Track, participants are tasked with using agents to generate rankings based on the current user's preferences. 

Here is the **Resource & Code base**: [This Link](https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/WebSocietySimulator).
----------------------
# User Behavior Modeling Track

### Introduction

In this track, participants will use a classic open-source dataset to simulate social interactions and information access behaviors on social media. This is crucial for designing effective information retrieval algorithms for web applications. Using LLM-based agents, participants will combine historical user behavior records and interact with the environment to model how users might review businesses and engage with reviews. The track focuses on **User Preference Simulation**: Participants should design LLM agents that can understand user preferences and behavior patterns to model user preferences. The agent may integrate user characteristics, business attributes, and other users' reviews to form a comprehensive judgment. Each review must include two components: a star rating (a numerical score reflecting the user's opinion) and review text (detailed commentary providing specific feedback).  This track explores the potential and scalability of LLM agents in simulating human behavior, offering insights into their applications in behavioral modeling.

### Evaluation
In the User Behavior Modeling Track, participants are expected to use LLM agents to model user interactions with recommended items (e.g., businesses on Yelp), including star ratings and review texts. The evaluation for this track will use quantitative metrics to assess the accuracy of the models in simulating user behavior. Specifically, the predicted star ratings are compared with the ground truth using Mean Absolute Error (MAE) after normalizing the values to the range [0,1]. The MAE is calculated as: $MAE = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} |\hat{s_{ni}} - s_{ni}|$ , where $N$ is the total number of reviews, and $\hat{s_{ni}}$ and$s_{ni}$ are the $i$-th normalized predicted star and normalized ground truth, respectively. For the generated review texts, semantic similarity will be evaluated across predefined sentiment dimensions, such as emotional tone, sentiment attitude, and topic relevance. For emotional tone and sentiment attitude, we first calculate the values using predefined models, normalize them to the range [0,1], and compute the MAE. For topic relevance, we calculate the cosine distance between text embeddings, defined as: $d = 1 - \frac{u \cdot v}{\|u\|_2 \cdot \|v\|_2}$. Finally, after obtaining the individual results, the final error score will be a weighted sum of these metrics.

Checkout [User Behavior Modeling Evaluation](https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/WebSocietySimulator/blob/main/websocietysimulator/tools/evaluation_tool.py) for more calculation details.
----------------------
# Recommendation Track

### Introduction
In this track, participants will develop an LLM agent that acts as a recommendation assistant. Participants are expected to design recommendation agents capable of handling tasks across diverse scenarios. Each task involves a specific user and a list of item candidates. Participants will need to extract the user's preferences and item information from datasets to generate personalized recommendations. This track explores the potential of LLM agents in recommendation tasks, offering new perspectives and application scenarios for advancing next-generation information retrieval and recommendation systems.

### Evaluation
In the Recommendation Track, participants are tasked with designing a recommendation agent that ranks the most likely item a user would select from a list of candidates. The ground truth for each task is generated using a predefined LLM agent that simulates user choices from a list of items based on the provided context and task query. The evaluation will measure how accurately the designed LLM agents rank the ground truth items. For each task, the model should rank a list of 20 candidate items, which will be then evaluated using the Top-N Hit Rate (with $N=1,3,5$).

Checkout [Recommendation Evaluation](https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/WebSocietySimulator/blob/main/websocietysimulator/tools/evaluation_tool.py) for more calculation details.
----------------------
# Submission Guidance

**Coming soon**
----------------------
# Prizes

To encourage and reward excellence, the competition will offer prizes for first, second, and third place **FOR EACH TRACK**. The first place team will receive 3,000 USD, the second place team will receive 1,000 USD, and the third place team will receive 500 USD. (all pre-tax)

Total Prizes Available: [6]
- **1st Place**?-?[3000 USD]
- **2nd Place** - [1000 USD]
- **3rd Place** - [500 USD]","# Dataset Description

