9025,pixel-odyssey-beyond-convolutions-2-0,"# Overview

Participants will venture into image classification using the CIFAR-100 dataset, crafting models that defy tradition - no convolutions. Innovate and redefine image classification! Aim high: achieve over 95% accuracy to conquer the challenge!
----------------------
# Description


### Objective:
Push the boundaries of convolutional neural network performance on a challenging multi-class image recognition task using the CIFAR-100 dataset.

### Dataset Overview:
- 60,000 color images (32x32 pixels)
- 100 distinct classes across 20 superclasses
- 500 training images per class
- 100 testing images per class

### Challenge Specifications:
- Minimum Performance Target: None
- Architectural Constraint: No Convolutional Layers and Transformer Layers allowed.

### Code Submission:
Please submit your kaggle notebook in this [link](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdYntb3SxfozP5a0rRlSFaDxFR8cxqNxs8oJXtu0bKihvfLew/viewform?usp=header).
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the accuracy of the model on the test set.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

## Columns

*   `ID` - (`int`) ID denoting the given image.
*   `image`?- (`np.ndarray`) Numpy array of the images shape (H,W,C).",False,True
9026,spinal-cord-injury-challenge-impairment-track,"# Overview

Spinal cord injury can result in a sudden and substantial loss of function and independence. Predicting long-term recovery from the data available early after the injury has significant value when counseling patients and families and for developing rehabilitation treatment plans. However, this is a challenging problem with people having a wide range of future outcomes. This medical science data challenge, hosted by the [American Spinal Cord Injury Association (ASIA)](http://asia-spinalinjury.org/), seeks to advance performance on this task and invite data scientists into the field of spinal cord injury research.

**Goal:** This challenge aims to **predict neurologic status** at 6-12 months after traumatic spinal cord injury based on acute phase assessments one week after injury.

**Dual Track:** This competition has a track to predict recovery of neurologic function (this track) and functional recovery of walking [which can be found here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asia-data-science-challenge-2025/). Participants can submit to one or both.
----------------------
# Description

In this challenge, participants will attempt to prognosticate long-term recovery after spinal cord injury by analyzing a subset of the data from the [Sygen multicenter clinical trial](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11805614/). The data used to make the prediction of recovery at 6-12 months will be data available in the first month after spinal cord injury. One piece of data that is commonly used to make such predictions are the [International Standards for Neurological Classification of SCI (ISNCSCI)](https://asia-spinalinjury.org/international-standards-neurological-classification-sci-isncsci-worksheet/). This clinical outcome assessment evaluates motor and sensory impairment across the body and helps localize the neurologic level of the spinal cord injury and grade the severity of that injury. Additional data includes demographic information, comorbidities, surgical interventions, and medical complications.

This data challenge involves two tracks predicting different outcomes. These reflect two constructs within the [World Health Organization International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF)](https://www.who.int/standards/classifications/international-classification-of-functioning-disability-and-health). The first construct is future impairment of body structure and function, assessed through the ISNCSCI scores at 26-52 weeks after SCI. **This neurologic recovery is the outcome assessed in this challenge track**.
Impairments, such as strength and sensation, are not the same construct as real-world function. Thus [the second track](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asia-data-science-challenge-2025) is to predict the quality of walking on an 8-point modified Benzel classification, more frequently referred to as the lower extremity subscore of the [modified Japanese Orthopedic Association](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4383381/) score proposed by [Benzel](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1802159/).

Why these outcomes? Essentially all functions of the body are influenced by spinal cord injury and are important to predict, including arm and hand function, bowel and bladder function, respiratory function, blood pressure control, and skin integrity. However, there are no available datasets that capture these rich aspects of function. Our goal with this challenge was to predict both neurological recovery (scored with the ISNCSI) and an example of real world function (walking scored with the Benzel classification).

A representative from the winning team for each track will be invited to present their work at the Data Science precourse at the ASIA society annual meeting, with travel support available for the representatives.

There are additional open datasets available with data for spinal cord injury. Participants are invited to also use these data if it improves their prediction performance on the Sygen challenge data but need to provide information and links to any data that was used - teams using other datasets not publicly available, including private datasets from their health care system, will not be eligible for invitation to the ASIA Society data science precourse. Any teams with access to the complete Sygen dataset are prohibited from using this data when training their algorithms. Leading teams will be asked to submit a write-up and code to reproduce their solution.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Scoring and test dataset details**: For this track, 20% of the Sygen data will be held back as test data and used in the evaluation process. The Kaggle public leaderboard is computed on a subset of the test data for the functional prediction task, so while it will give you a sense of your relative performance it may not exactly match the final evaluation. This prevents overfitting through submissions. Also note that the leader board is based on a simple root mean squared error across all entries, including myotomes above the NLI and the ""time"" column. 

**Impairment Prediction**: For predictions of the ISNCSCI scores, for each participant the final prediction will be scored by first measuring the error between the predicted strength at each myotome. Then, the sum of those residuals below the neurologic level of injury at the acute phase will be computed to provide a score for each individual. This will be averaged across individuals in the test data set to determine the final score.

We provide a template for the submission file in the Data section.",,False,True
9027,dla-4-a-test,"# Description

This challenge is a continuation and extension of the Project-A Exercises notebook.

Do the best job you can to predict discharge across 100+ U.S. basins extracted from the Catchment Attributes and Meteorology for Large-sample Studies (CAMELS) dataset.

Additional information and a sample template are provided in the Project-A Challenge notebook we are distributing from our CodeCommit repo. Information on how to get set up for the challenge is provided in the notebook and also in our ""Project A"" Teams channel.

IMPORTANT: As questions or issues arise during the challenge, we will use Teams (not Kaggle) to communicate and provide updates.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Model performance will be an important part of the overall evaluation for the Project-A Challenge, but not the only factor. Several other factors that address Knowledge Sharing will also be considered. (Point scheme will be announced at a later date via Teams) 
<br>
- Leaderboard results at the time the Kaggle competition closes, as measured by R-squared; plus assessment of results for the 10 additional (unseen) basins. 
<br>
- Quality of code and documentation provided in the notebook, assessed from the perspective of assisting others who may reuse your notebook in the future. 
<br>
- Ability to effectively communicate results and insights to a broad, non-expert audience.","# Dataset Description

The data provided for this challenge is a subset of the same one used by Kratzert et al. to show that an LSTM approach significantly outperforms the NOAA model.

**File descriptions**
- **DLA_Project-A_challenge_train.csv** - 20 years of data for 100 of the CAMELS U.S. basins; use this dataset for both training and validation, partitioning it however you think is best (e.g. to avoid overfitting).
- **DLA_Project-A_challenge_test.csv** - 10 years of data for the same 100 CAMELS U.S. basins provided in the training and validation set.
- **DLA_Project-A_challenge_test_unseen_basins.csv** - Data for 10 U.S. basins not included in the training and validation set; to be used for more fully evaluating model generalization performance.",False,True
9028,flumpdingus,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the quality of wine based on various properties. Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored based on the?quadratic weighted kappa, which measures the agreement between two outcomes. This metric typically varies from 0 (random agreement) to 1 (complete agreement). In the event that there is less agreement than expected by chance, the?metric may go below 0.

The quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as follows. First, an N?x?N histogram matrix <em>O</em> is constructed, such that <em>O<sub>i,j</sub></em> corresponds to the number of `Id`s <em>i</em> (actual) that received a predicted value <em>j</em>.?An <em>N-by-N </em>matrix of weights, <em>w</em>, is calculated based on the difference between actual and predicted values:

$$w_{i,j} = \frac{\left(i-j\right)^2}{\left(N-1\right)^2}$$

An <em>N-by-N</em> histogram matrix of expected outcomes, <em>E</em>, is calculated assuming that there is no correlation between values.? This is calculated as the outer product between the actual histogram vector of outcomes and the predicted histogram vector, normalized such that <em>E</em> and <em>O</em> have the same sum.

From these three matrices, the quadratic weighted kappa is calculated as:?

$$\kappa=1-\frac{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}O\_{i,j}}{\sum\_{i,j}w\_{i,j}E\_{i,j}}.$$


## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `quality`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,quality
    2056,5
    2057,7
    2058,3
    etc.",,False,True
