Please check this [news post](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/event-based-eye-tracking-cvpr-2025/discussion/568262) to understand the final submissions!!

~~~~~~~~
This challenge aims to develop an event-based eye-tracking system for precise tracking of rapid eye movements to produce lighter and more comfortable devices for a better user experience.
The task is to predict the eye center position (x,y) given the event camera inputs. The event recordings are captured with an Inivation DVXplorer Mini event camera. See the youtube video below using event-based eye-tracking system for playing a simple video game!

Official Challenge Site https://lab-ics.github.io/3et-2025.github.io/

Official GitHub Demo Code Repository https://github.com/EETChallenge/3et_challenge_2025

<iframe width=""1237"" height=""700"" src=""https://www.youtube.com/embed/aRB5mDNfrHM"" title=""Neuromorphic eye control game: EyeBite: Feast with a Gaze"" frameborder=""0"" allow=""accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"" referrerpolicy=""strict-origin-when-cross-origin"" allowfullscreen></iframe>
----------------------
# Description

For the full coding pipeline and tutorial please refer to the GitHub repository: https://github.com/EETChallenge/3et_challenge_2025

## Training Pipeline Description
The default training pipeline is very handy to use. Simply run the following command with the code in the GitHub repository:
```
python train.py --config train_baseline.json
```
This should give a baseline result for a basic CNN+GRU model trained on voxel-grid event representation. The training progress, checkpoints and metrics are all logged with the MLFlow library. See the repo for more details.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F19454843%2Fab2c86486ac9bb86d9097e691a92174b%2Finbox_18771626_e78190165f02d967a7bd8bc992e5ca64_event-based-eye-movement.gif?generation=1737084986065375&alt=media)
----------------------
# Evaluation

#### Scoring Functions
There is only one evaluation metric for the challenge: **Average Euclidean Distance**.
The mean Euclidean distance is defined as the average Euclidean distance between the predictions and the ground truths. For each pair of predicted and true coordinates (x, y), the Euclidean distance is computed as follows:
`
distance = math.sqrt((x_true - x_pred)**2 + (y_true - y_pred)**2)
`
----------------------
# Submission File

Running test.py by loading your model checkpoint will generate the submission.csv file. The file should contain a header (already included in the script) and have the following format:
```
row_id,x,y
0,35.20940399169922,22.15359878540039
1,34.5449104309082,25.2341251373291
2,40.37540817260742,21.507827758789062
3,37.30218505859375,23.70339012145996
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description
There are 13 subjects in total, each having 2-6 recording sessions. The subjects are required to perform 5 classes of activities: random, saccades, read text, smooth pursuit and blinks. Figure 2 visualizes one real recording sample by making the raw events into event frames. The total data volume is approximately 1 GB in the compressed .h5 form.",False,True
