9048,product-defect-detection,"# Overview

Welcome to this Kaggle competition! This challenge has been specially designed as part of a student project to provide hands-on experience in working with real-world data and solving machine learning problems.
----------------------
# Description

You are a Data Scientist at a leading electronics manufacturing company. The company has been facing challenges with maintaining consistent product quality, as occasional defects are reported in the products leaving the production line. These defects negatively impact the company¡¯s reputation, customer satisfaction, and profitability.
The company wants to leverage data science to understand the factors contributing to product defects and develop a predictive model to identify defective products early in the production process. This will allow for timely interventions, reducing the cost of recalls and ensuring high product quality.
To address this issue, the company has launched a Kaggle competition, inviting the global data science community to participate. The goal is to create the most accurate and effective defect detection model, harnessing the collective expertise and innovation of participants worldwide.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [Recall Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Precision_and_recall) between the predicted and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the TARGET variable (Defective).  The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ProductID,Defective
    2,0
    5,1
    6,0
    etc.",,False,True
9049,dhi-club-tired-weekends-4thsem-1,"# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on [Accuracy Score](https://medium.com/@niitwork0921/what-is-an-accuracy-score-and-how-to-check-it-13b23eeed6a3), which measures the percentage of correctly classified Pok¨¦mon images. Accuracy is calculated as the ratio of correctly predicted labels to the total number of predictions made by the model.","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description  
The dataset consists of Pok¨¦mon images from 150 categories, with around 55 images per category. These images have been curated to represent different Pok¨¦mon from the iconic franchise.

## Files  

### 1. Train Data  
- The train dataset is organized into 150 subfolders, each named after a Pok¨¦mon.  
- Each subfolder contains images corresponding to that Pok¨¦mon. These images serve as labeled data to train your classification model.  


### 2. Test Data  
- The test dataset contains **2,195 images**, labeled sequentially from `1.jpg` to `2195.jpg`.  
- These images are unlabeled, and participants need to predict the corresponding Pok¨¦mon category for each image.  

### 3. Submission Template  
- The submission template provides the required format for participants to submit their predictions.  
- Submissions must contain two columns:  
- **Img_name :** The name of the test image (e.g., `1.jpg`).  
- **Class Prediction:** The predicted Pok¨¦mon name corresponding to the test image.  
- **Example structure:**  

## Key Information  

### What files do I need?  
You need the **train data**, **test data**, and the **submission template**.  

### What is the data format?  
- **Training data:** Images organized into subfolders named after Pok¨¦mon categories.  
- **Test data:** Unlabeled images named sequentially (e.g., `1.jpg`, `2.jpg`).  
- **Submission:** CSV file with `Img_name` and `Class Prediction` columns.  

### What are you predicting?  
You are predicting the **Pok¨¦mon name** (category) for each test image.  

### What acronyms or terms might I encounter?  
- **CNN:** Convolutional Neural Network, a type of deep learning model.  
- **Img_name :** The file name of the test image.  
- **Class Prediction:** The Pok¨¦mon category predicted by the model.  

---

Take a moment to explore the dataset files before starting your modeling process. Good luck, and may your CNN catch ¡®em all! ??",False,True
9050,ps-3-c-final-evaluation,"# Overview

# **??? Paper Submission is MANDATORY using the [EDAS Website](https://edas.info/N32831) ???**

# **?? The Final Evaluation Phase requires sharing of the MODELS and CODES ??**

This is a continuation of the [Pap Smear Cell Classification Challenge (PS3C)](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pap-smear-cell-classification-challenge), where participants can **evaluate their methods using the final evaluation dataset**, the results of which will be used for the official leaderboard on the ISBI Conference.

#### **Important Information for ISBI 2025 Participants**

This challenge serves as **Phase 2** of the ISBI Challenge, also known as the **Final Evaluation Phase**, which will take place from **6th¨C13th February 2025**, determining the winners of the ISBI 2025 challenge.

During the Final Evaluation Phase:  
- A **new test dataset** will be provided on Kaggle.  
- Participants must use the same methods tested during the Preliminary Phase to classify the new dataset and resubmit their results.  

#### **Timeline for ISBI 2025 Challenges**
- **Dec 9**: Challenge initiation.  
  - Training data and contact information made publicly available.  
  - Participants can begin developing their methods and submitting evaluations.  
- **Feb 6**: Submission deadline for the Preliminary Phase.  
- **Feb 6**: Challenge participants submit their papers on the EDAS platform (challenge track).  
  - Review period opens for two weeks, with reviewers assigned by challenge organizers.  
- **Feb 13**: Submission deadline for the Final Evaluation Phase.  
- **Feb 20**: Challenge paper authors receive reviews.  
  - Authors are notified to address comments and finalize methodologies within one week.  
  - Valid paper submitters are invited to present their methods at the conference. 
- **Feb 27**: Final camera-ready paper submission deadline.  
- **March 1**: Notification of oral versus poster presentations.
  - Top 5 performers are invited to present orally at the conference, and co-author the meta-analysis journal manuscript. 
- **April 14¨C17**: ISBI Conference and announcement of challenge winners.  

#### **Submission Requirements for Validity in ISBI 2025**  

Participants must submit the following alongside their predictions to ensure solution validity:  
1. **Well-documented code** used for generating predictions, including:  
   - Model architectures.  
   - Preprocessing and postprocessing steps.  
   - Details of any third-party datasets used.  
   - Descriptions of additional methods or techniques applied.  

2. Code must be uploaded using Kaggle's code feature for full transparency. Submissions failing to meet these requirements will be deemed invalid for ISBI 2025 participation.  

---

Participants will receive access to high-quality training and test datasets, comprising expertly annotated cervical cell images. The training dataset is provided with clear labels organized into respective folders for each class, ensuring ease of use for model development. The test dataset will be distributed as image files alongside a CSV file containing the list of image IDs. Participants must classify each test image and submit their predictions in the specified format for evaluation.

The goal of this challenge is to create models capable of accurately classifying cervical cell images while managing the inherent complexities of medical imaging data, including class imbalance. Submissions will be evaluated based on the **F1-Score**, a metric that balances precision and recall, calculated for each class and averaged across all classes. This ensures fair and comprehensive assessment, emphasizing the correct identification of unhealthy cells, which are crucial for effective cervical cancer screening.

---

This challenge is organized by a multidisciplinary team of experts in medical imaging, pathology, and data science:
- **Bal¨¢zs Harangi** ¨C Department of Data Science and Visualization, University of Debrecen  
- **Andr¨¢s Hajdu** ¨C Department of Data Science and Visualization, University of Debrecen  
- **D¨¢vid Kup¨¢s** ¨C Department of Data Science and Visualization, University of Debrecen  
- **Ilona Kov¨¢cs** ¨C Department of Pathology, Kenezy Gyula University Hospital and Clinic  
- **P¨¦ter Kov¨¢cs** ¨C Department of Numerical Analysis, E?tv?s Lor¨¢nd University  
- **Nicolai Spicher** ¨C Department of Medical Informatics, Universit?tsmedizin G?ttingen  

----------------------
# Description

## **Challenge Description**

The Pap Smear Cell Classification Challenge (PS3C) aims to advance the automation of cervical cell analysis, a critical component in cervical cancer screening. This competition is part of the **ISBI 2025 Challenge Program** and focuses on the classification of individual cervical cell images into three categories:  

- **Healthy**: Normal cells without observable abnormalities.  
- **Unhealthy**: Abnormal cells that indicate potential pathological changes.  
- **Rubbish**: Images unsuitable for evaluation due to artifacts or poor quality.  

### **The Domain**  
Cervical cancer remains one of the leading causes of cancer-related deaths among women worldwide. Early detection through Pap smear testing significantly reduces mortality rates. However, traditional manual analysis of Pap smears is time-consuming, resource-intensive, and highly dependent on expert cytologists.  

By leveraging advancements in machine learning and computer vision, this challenge aims to improve diagnostic efficiency and accuracy, potentially enabling wider access to effective cervical cancer screening, especially in resource-limited settings.

### **The Problem**  
Participants will receive a high-quality dataset of expertly annotated cervical cell images, extracted from digitized Pap smears. The dataset contains thousands of images divided into three predefined categories: healthy, unhealthy, and rubbish. The task is to develop models capable of accurately classifying these cell images.

The dataset reflects real-world conditions, with class imbalance¡ªsignificantly fewer unhealthy cells compared to healthy and rubbish ones¡ªposing an additional challenge. This imbalance mimics the clinical scenario, emphasizing the need for robust solutions that perform well across all classes.

### **Evaluation Metric**  
Submissions will be evaluated using the **F1-Score**, calculated for each class and averaged across all classes. This metric ensures balanced assessment across the categories, with a particular focus on identifying unhealthy cells, which are critical for effective screening.  

### **Expected Impact**  
This challenge provides an opportunity to contribute to the development of robust, scalable, and interpretable AI models for medical diagnostics. The results will benchmark current state-of-the-art methods and may lead to practical implementations that save lives.

### **Resources**  
Participants will have access to:
- A high-quality training dataset organized by class for model development.  
- A dedicated test dataset for evaluation.  

----------------------
# Evaluation

## **Evaluation**

Submissions in the Pap Smear Cell Classification Challenge (PS3C) will be evaluated based on their ability to accurately classify cervical cell images into three categories: **Healthy**, **Unhealthy**, and **Rubbish**. 

### **Metric**  
The primary metric for this challenge is the **F1-Score**. This score will be calculated for each class individually and then averaged across all classes to produce a single overall F1-Score. The F1-Score is particularly suited for this challenge as it balances precision and recall, ensuring a robust evaluation for imbalanced datasets.  

The formula for calculating the F1-Score for each class is as follows:  
$$ F1 = 2 \cdot \frac{\text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}} $$  
Where:  
**Precision**:  
$$ \text{Precision} = \frac{\text{True Positives}}{\text{True Positives} + \text{False Positives}} $$  

**Recall**:  
$$ \text{Recall} = \frac{\text{True Positives}}{\text{True Positives} + \text{False Negatives}} $$  


The final score is the weighted average of the F1-Scores across all classes.

### **Submission Format**  
Participants are required to submit a single CSV file for evaluation. The CSV file should have the following structure:

| **image_name**     | **label** |  
|-------------------|----------------|  
| `image_001.png`  | healthy        |  
| `image_002.png`  | unhealthy      |  
| `image_003.png`  | rubbish        |  

- **image_name**: The identifier of the test image (provided in `isbi2025-ps3c-test-dataset.csv`).  
- **label**: The predicted class for each test image (must be one of: Healthy, Unhealthy, or Rubbish).  

Ensure that all images in the test set are included in the submission file.

### **Scoring**  
Submissions will be evaluated using an automated system. The test dataset labels will remain private, and scores will be computed based on the agreement between the predicted classes and the ground truth labels.  

Participants may make up to **five (5) submissions per day** during the competition. Only the top one (1) selected submission will be used for final scoring.

### **Important Notes**  
- Predictions that do not adhere to the required format or contain invalid class labels will result in submission errors or disqualification.  
- The leaderboard will display provisional scores based on a subset of the test data. Final rankings will be determined using the full test dataset at the end of the competition.

By focusing on the F1-Score and ensuring a standardized submission format, this evaluation setup ensures fairness and encourages models that perform well across all classes.",,False,True
