## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

msno,is_churn
    9WQJvVyggkLWsp0DLpOljfbKePSZZZeYdQvNOAkQ0bk=,0
    kTCflEFCJk6ahA8ryiGzOBQlhwEOMxuPT7gVPRzoJJ8=,1
    EgBTyrvGcPR2bjlXjPqdSkRzz1NVXVvLLdUaabKBm4I=,0
    etc.

Submission has to have 24274 rows plus a header (24275 rows in total). msno needs to match the test.csv msno.","# Dataset Description

In this challenge, you are asked to predict whether a user will churn after his/her subscription expires. Specifically, you want to forecast if a user make a new service subscription transaction within 30 days after the current membership expiration date.

The dataset contains information about music streaming service. When users signs up for the service, users can choose to either manual renew or auto-renew the service. Users can actively cancel their membership at any time.

In majority of cases, the subscription length is 30 days and a lot of users re-subscribe every month. The key fields to determine churn/renewal are transaction date, membership expiration date, and is_cancel. Note that the is_cancel field indicates whether a user actively cancels a subscription. Subscription cancellation does not imply the user has churned. A user may cancel service subscription due to change of service plans or other reasons. The criteria of ""churn"" is no new valid service subscription within 30 days after the current membership expires.

The training and the test data are selected from users whose membership expire within a certain month. The train data consists of users whose subscription expires within the month of March 2017, and the test data is with users whose subscription expires within the month of April 2017. This means we are looking at user churn or renewal roughly in the month of March 2017 for train set, and the user churn or renewal roughly in the month of April 2017. Train and test sets are split by transaction date, as well as the public and private leaderboard data.

In this dataset, you can find additional useful information, such as users behaviors or demographic information about members. 

## Files

*   **train_data.csv** - the training set
*   **members.csv** - dataset with information about members
*   **transactions.csv** - dataset with information about transactions
*   **user_logs.csv** - dataset with logs about user behaviour 
*   **kaggle_test_data.csv** - the test set


**train_data.csv** - the train set, containing the user ids and whether they have churned.
- msno: user id
- is_churn: This is the target variable. Churn is defined as whether the user did not continue the subscription within 30 days of expiration. is_churn = 1 means churn,is_churn = 0 means renewal.

**members.csv** - user information. Note that not every user in the dataset is available.
- msno
- city
- bd: age. Note: this column has outlier values ranging from -7000 to 2015, please use your judgement.
- gender
- registered_via: registration method
- registration_init_time: format %Y%m%d

**transactions.csv** - transactions data until 3/31/2017.
- msno: user id
- payment_method_id: payment method
- payment_plan_days: length of membership plan in days
- plan_list_price: in New Taiwan Dollar (NTD)
- actual_amount_paid: in New Taiwan Dollar (NTD)
- is_auto_renew
- transaction_date: format %Y%m%d
- membership_expire_date: format %Y%m%d
- is_cancel: whether or not the user canceled the membership in this transaction.

**user_logs.csv** - daily user logs describing listening behaviors of a user. Data collected until 3/31/2017.
- msno: user id
- date: format %Y%m%d
- num_25: # of songs played less than 25% of the song length
- num_50: # of songs played between 25% to 50% of the song length
- num_75: # of songs played between 50% to 75% of of the song length
- num_985: # of songs played between 75% to 98.5% of the song length
- num_100: # of songs played over 98.5% of the song length
- num_unq: # of unique songs played
- total_secs: total seconds played

 **kaggle_test_data.csv** - the test set with msno for generating predictions
- msno: user id",False,True
9113,alzheimers-disease-risk-prediction-eu-business,"# Overview

Welcome to the Alzheimer's Disease Prediction Competition! In this challenge, you'll develop machine learning models to predict whether a patient has Alzheimer's Disease based on health-related features. This competition aims to advance early detection techniques and support healthcare research through data-driven insights.

Participants will analyze data that includes demographic details, lifestyle factors, medical history, clinical measurements, cognitive assessments, and symptoms. This task is critical as early diagnosis can significantly improve patient care and management.

----------------------
# Description

The description section is used to provide detailed information about the competition. This includes the objective, dataset, and problem statement. Participants should gain a clear understanding of the task after reading this section.

## ?? **About the Competition**
In this competition, you'll develop machine learning models to predict whether a patient has Alzheimer's Disease based on health-related features. The dataset includes demographic details, lifestyle factors, medical history, clinical measurements, cognitive assessments, and symptoms.

## ?? **Objective**
The goal is to predict the **`Diagnosis`**:
- **0** = No Alzheimer's Disease
- **1** = Alzheimer's Disease

Participants will analyze multiple health indicators to improve diagnostic accuracy.
----------------------
# ?? Evaluation


Submissions will be evaluated using the **F1 Score**, which balances precision and recall. This is crucial for medical diagnosis tasks where both false positives and false negatives can have significant implications.

**F1 Score Formula:**

$$
F1 = 2 \cdot \frac{precision \cdot recall}{precision + recall}
$$

Where:
- **Precision** = TP/(TP + FP)
- **Recall** = TP/(TP + FN)

Maximizing the F1 Score ensures that models perform well for both positive and negative classes.","# Dataset Description

# Data Description

This page accompanies the dataset and provides essential information about the files included, their format, and the objective of the competition. Understanding this will help participants focus on building effective models without needing constant clarifications.

Participants should be able to answer the following after reading this description:

- **What files do I need?**  
- **What should I expect the data format to be?**  
- **What am I predicting?**  
- **What acronyms or key features will I encounter?**

## Files

- **train.csv** - The training dataset containing both features and the target variable (`Diagnosis`).  
- **test.csv** - The test dataset with the same feature columns as `train.csv`, excluding the target (`Diagnosis`).  
- **submission.csv** - A sample submission file showing the correct format for submitting predictions.  
- **submission_template.csv** - A template with `PatientID` and empty `Diagnosis` columns for submission formatting.  
- **data_Description.txt** - information about the dataset 

## Objective

The goal is to predict whether a patient has Alzheimer's Disease based on various demographic, lifestyle, medical, and cognitive factors. The target variable is `Diagnosis`, where **0 indicates No Alzheimer's** and **1 indicates Yes**.

## Columns

### Demographic Details
- **Age**: Patient¡¯s age (60¨C90 years).
- **Gender**: 0 = Male, 1 = Female.
- **Ethnicity**: 0 = Caucasian, 1 = African American, 2 = Asian, 3 = Other.

### Lifestyle Factors
- **BMI**: Body Mass Index (15¨C40).
- **Smoking**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **AlcoholConsumption**: Weekly alcohol units (0¨C20).
- **PhysicalActivity**: Weekly hours of physical activity (0¨C10).
- **DietQuality**: Diet score (0¨C10).
- **SleepQuality**: Sleep quality score (4¨C10).

### Medical History
- **FamilyHistoryAlzheimers**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **CardiovascularDisease**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **Diabetes**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **Depression**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **HeadInjury**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **Hypertension**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.

### Clinical Measurements
- **SystolicBP**: Systolic blood pressure (90¨C180 mmHg).
- **DiastolicBP**: Diastolic blood pressure (60¨C120 mmHg).
- **CholesterolTotal**: Total cholesterol (150¨C300 mg/dL).
- **CholesterolLDL**: LDL cholesterol (50¨C200 mg/dL).
- **CholesterolHDL**: HDL cholesterol (20¨C100 mg/dL).
- **CholesterolTriglycerides**: Triglycerides (50¨C400 mg/dL).

### Cognitive & Functional Assessments
- **MMSE**: Mini-Mental State Exam score (0¨C30).
- **FunctionalAssessment**: Functional score (0¨C10).
- **MemoryComplaints**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **BehavioralProblems**: 0 = No, 1 = Yes.
- **ADL**: Activities of Daily Living score (0¨C10).

### Symptoms
- **Confusion**, **Disorientation**, **PersonalityChanges**, **DifficultyCompletingTasks**, **Forgetfulness**: All binary (0 = No, 1 = Yes).

### Target Variable
- **Diagnosis**: Alzheimer¡¯s Disease diagnosis (0 = No, 1 = Yes).

---",False,True
9114,cmldl-bse-2425-svm,"# Overview

Prediction project: probability of death

In this project, you have to predict the probability of death of a patient that is entering an ICU (Intensive Care Unit), using SVM.

The dataset comes from MIMIC project (https://mimic.physionet.org/). MIMIC-III (Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care III) is a large, freely-available database comprising deidentified health-related data associated with over forty thousand patients who stayed in critical care units of the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center between 2001 and 2012.

Each row of mimictrain.csv corresponds to one ICU stay (hadm_id+ icustay_id) of one patient (subject_id). Column HOSPITAL_EXPIRE_FLAG is the indicator of death (=1) as a result of the current hospital stay; this is the outcome to predict in our modelling exercise. The remaining columns correspond to vitals of each patient (when entering the ICU), plus some general characteristics (age, gender, etc.), and their explanation can be found at mimic_patient_metadata.csv.

Please don't use any feature that you infer you don't know the first day of a patient in an ICU.

Note that the main cause/disease of patient condition is embedded as a code at ICD9_diagnosis column. The meaning of this code can be found at MIMIC_metadata_diagnose.csv. But this is only the main one; a patient can have co-occurrent diseases (comorbidities). These secondary codes can be found at extra__data/MIMIC_diagnoses.csv.

As performance metric, you can use AUC for the binary classification case, but feel free to report as well any other metric if you can justify that is particularly suitable for this case (Extended project).

----------------------
# Description

Main tasks are:
Using mimictrain.csv file build a predictive model for HOSPITAL_EXPIRE_FLAG.
For this analysis there is an extra test dataset, mimictest.csv. Apply your final model to this extra dataset and produce a prediction csv file in same format as mimic_kaggle_death_sample_submission.csv.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is ROC AUC (Area Under the Curve). The AUC, commonly used in binary classification models, measures the area under a curve that is obtained by varying the threshold for binary classification (0.5 by default) and computing True Positive Rates and False Positive Rates (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic).


## Submission File
Submission Format
For every patient in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: icustayid (this defines the individual prediction, and it's extracted from the test dataset) and HOSPITAL_EXPIRE_FLAG (float number between 0 and 1, the probability of death)..
 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    icustay_id,HOSPITAL_EXPIRE_FLAG
    2,0.651
    5,0.004
    6,0.104
    etc.","# Dataset Description

 
The dataset comes from MIMIC project (https://mimic.physionet.org/). MIMIC-III (Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care III) is a large, freely-available database comprising deidentified health-related data associated with over forty thousand patients who stayed in critical care units of the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center between 2001 and 2012.

Each row of mimic_train.csv correponds to one ICU stay (hadm_id+icustay_id) of one patient (subject_id). Column HOSPITAL_EXPIRE_FLAG is the indicator of death (=1) as a result of the current hospital stay; this is the outcome to predict in our modelling exercise. The remaining columns correspond to vitals of each patient (when entering the ICU), plus some general characteristics (age, gender, etc.), and their explanation can be found at mimic_patient_metadata.csv.

Please don't use any feature that you infer you don't know the first day of a patient in an ICU.

Note that the main cause/disease of patient condition is embedded as a code at ICD9_diagnosis column. The meaning of this code can be found at MIMIC_metadata_diagnose.csv. But this is only the main one; a patient can have co-occurrent diseases (comorbidities). These secondary codes can be found at extra_data/MIMIC_diagnoses.csv.

There is an extra test dataset, mimic_X_test.csv. Apply your final model to this extra dataset and produce a prediction .csv file in same format as test_kaggle1.csv.",False,True
9115,competitions-ai-challenge,"# Overview

This dataset contains of individuals who have completed their Bachelor's degree and are deciding whether to pursue an MBA. It includes demographic information, academic background, work experience, financial factors, and career aspirations to analyze trends and key drivers behind MBA decisions.

The dataset can be used for:
?? Predictive modeling ¨C Identify patterns in MBA decisions
?? Career insights ¨C Understand how work experience & salary impact choices
?? Business education research ¨C Study MBA trends across industries
----------------------
# Description

This dataset contains of individuals who have completed their Bachelor's degree and are deciding whether to pursue an MBA. It includes demographic information, academic background, work experience, financial factors, and career aspirations to analyze trends and key drivers behind MBA decisions
----------------------
# Evaluation

The student will be evaluated based on their proficiency in key stages of the data science workflow, including data cleaning, data preparation, data preprocessing, and model training. Their ability to handle missing values, remove inconsistencies, and standardize datasets will be assessed in the data cleaning phase. In data preparation, their skills in feature selection, transformation, and data integration will be considered. The evaluation will also focus on their expertise in data preprocessing, including normalization, encoding categorical variables, and handling imbalanced data. Finally, the student's ability to train models effectively, optimize hyperparameters, and assess model performance using appropriate metrics will be a crucial aspect of the evaluation.",,False,True
9116,2025-spring-sfu-cv-image-classification,"# Overview

In this part of the homework, you will use Pytorch to design a deep network to classify images into 100 categories. You are provided with a BaseNet in your Github repository and you will work on improving the architecture and fine-tuning the hyper-parameters to improve the classification accuracy of the network.

**Goal:** Get hands-on experience designing and training deep convolutional neural networks using PyTorch.

Note that the number of submissions is limited to a few times per day, so try to tune your model before uploading CSV files. Also, you must make at least one submission for your final system. The best performance will be considered.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on *classification accuracy* between the predicted class and the ground truth class.","# Dataset Description

For this part of the assignment, you will be working with the CIFAR100 dataset. This dataset consists of 60K 32x32 color images from 100 classes, with 600 images per class. There are 50K training images and 10K test images. The images in CIFAR100 are of size 3x32x32, i.e. 3-channel color images of 32x32 pixels.

We have modified the standard dataset to create our own CIFAR100 dataset which consists of 45K training images (450 of each class), 5K validation images (50 of each class), and 10K test images (100 of each class). The training and val datasets have labels while all the labels in the test set are set to 0. You can tune your model on the validation set and obtain your performance on the test set by uploading a CSV file to this Kaggle competition.",False,True
9117,stratford-workshop,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2024 Kaggle Playground Series!** Happy New Year! This is the 1st episode of Season 4. We plan to continue in the spirit of previous playgrounds, providing interesting an approachable datasets for our community to practice their machine learning skills, and anticipate a competition each month. 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to predict whether a customer continues with their account or closes it (e.g., churns). Good luck!
