9119,gvu-spring-2025-data-454-project-1,"# Overview

Welcome to the second competition of DATA-454 in Spring-2025. In this competition, students and competitors will build regression models to predict a numerical feature.

**Goal:** 

A model will be developed using historical house purchase data from a major city in the USA. This historical data includes standard information about the house, as well as the price for which the house was sold.
----------------------
# Description

The main objective is to develop machine-learning models that can predict the selling price of single-family homes in a major U.S. city.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [mean absolute error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error) between the predicted value and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each id in the test set, you must predict a number for the SALE_PRC variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

id, SALE_PRC
11145,399941.93
11146,399941.93
11147,399941.93
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Description

The dataset contains information on 13,932 single-family homes sold in a major U.S. city. 


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

- `SALE_PRC`: sale price ($)
- `LND_SQFOOT`: land area (square feet)
- `TOT_LVG_AREA`: floor area (square feet)
- `SPEC_FEAT_VAL`: value of special features (e.g., swimming pools) ($)
- `RAIL_DIST`: distance to the nearest rail line (an indicator of noise) (feet)
- `OCEAN_DIST`: distance to the ocean (feet)
- `WATER_DIST`: distance to the nearest body of water (feet)
- `CNTR_DIST`: distance to the Miami central business district (feet)
- `SUBCNTR_DI`: distance to the nearest subcenter (feet)
- `HWY_DIST`: distance to the nearest highway (an indicator of noise) (feet)
- `age`: age of the structure
- `avno60plus`: dummy variable for airplane noise exceeding an acceptable level
- `structure_quality`: quality of the structure
- `month_sold`: sale month in 2016 (1 = jan)
- `LATITUDE`
- `LONGITUDE`",False,True
9120,mission-data-impossible,"# Overview

#**?? MISSION: DATA IMPOSSIBLE ¨C Overview**
Mission: Data Impossible is a thrilling competition where participants push their data science and machine learning skills to the limit. Teams will race against time to solve puzzles, unlock a hidden dataset, and then analyze it to extract valuable insights. The competition tests both problem-solving speed and analytical expertise, offering an exciting challenge for all participants, regardless of experience level.

Important Requirements for Judging:
The following metrics will be the key criteria for evaluating your performance in this round:

Accuracy ¨C Ensure your predictions are as precise as possible, minimizing errors.
F1 Score ¨C Strike the right balance between precision and recall for effective classification.
Confusion Matrix ¨C Understand and analyze your model's performance and error distribution.
Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) ¨C Provide a deep, insightful analysis of the dataset, uncovering patterns and key features.
These four factors will be the sole basis for judging the round, so focus on each to maximize your score.
----------------------
# ??Evaluation

The performance of teams in Mission: Data Impossible will be evaluated based on the following key metrics:

**Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** ¨C Teams will be judged on their ability to perform a thorough Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA). The depth and clarity of insights derived from the dataset, including feature relationships and data patterns, will be a crucial factor in scoring.

**Accuracy** ¨C Teams must ensure their model predictions are as accurate as possible with minimal errors. High accuracy will demonstrate precision in solving the given problems.

**F1 Score** ¨C The F1 score will assess how well the model balances precision and recall. A higher F1 score indicates a more effective model, particularly in handling class imbalances.

**Confusion Matrix** ¨C The confusion matrix will be used to evaluate the model's performance by showing the true positive, false positive, true negative, and false negative counts. This helps assess errors and refine the model.

All evaluation will be based solely on these four factors, so teams should focus on providing robust solutions across all these areas to maximize their chances of winning.","# Dataset Description

The dataset follows a structured tabular format with the following attributes:

**Type**: Structured tabular data (CSV or Excel format).
**Rows**: Represent different astronomical objects.
**Columns**: Represent different features describing these objects.

**Column Description:**

**id:** Unique identifier for each object.

**f1 - f17:** Feature columns that contain numerical and possibly categorical values representing different physical or observational characteristics of the object. These features are encoded in such a way that completing this challenge won¡¯t be easy¡ªso get ready for a real puzzle! ?? The values are obfuscated and designed to keep you on your toes ?. You'll need to apply creative data preprocessing, feature engineering, and machine learning magic ???¡á? to uncover the true value behind these columns. The path to success? A bit of a detective mission ??!

**class:** Target variable indicating the category of the object. The classes are:

TRUTH ¨C Objects that are classified as ¡°true¡± in nature.
LIE ¨C Objects that are classified as ¡°false.¡±
SUS ¨C Objects that are classified as ¡°suspicious,¡± uncertain, or ambiguous.",False,True
