9131,nexar-collision-prediction,"# Overview


Autonomous vehicles and advanced driver assistance systems (ADAS) rely on accurate accident prediction to enhance road safety. In this competition, you will analyze dashcam videos and develop models capable of predicting vehicle collisions before they occur. The challenge includes real-world scenarios, such as diverse weather conditions, occlusions, and unexpected road events.

Your goal is to develop a model that accurately predicts vehicle collisions as early as possible in dashcam video sequences. The earlier and more accurately you can detect an impending accident, the better your score.

----------------------
# Prizes

TOTAL PRIZES AVAILABLE: $5000

- **1st Place**?- An all-expenses-covered trip to Portugal during the year of 2025,  and a VIP visit to Nexar's offices, up to a maximum budget of $5,000. Expenses exceeding this budget will not be covered.
- **2nd Place** - Recognition in a widely distributed blog post by Nexar + announcement on CVPR DriveX Workshop, 2025
- **3rd Place** -Recognition in a widely distributed blog post by Nexar + announcement on CVPR DriveX Workshop, 2025



----------------------
# Description


You will receive a balanced dataset of high-quality annotated dashcam footage, including three types of scenarios:
- **Collision**: Videos where an accident actually occurs;
- **Near-miss**: Videos where a dangerous situation almost leads to an accident but is avoided at the last moment. Your model should still recognize these events as potential accidents;
- **Non-Collision**: Normal driving sequences where no accident or near-miss occurs. <br>

 
The training dataset is balanced and consists of 750 non-collision cases, as well as 400 collision cases and 350 near-miss cases, resulting in a total of 750 positive examples. <br>

 
Each video in the training dataset is annotated with:
- **Event type** - Whether the video contains collision/near-collision or normal driving;
- **Event time** - Time at which a (near-)collision happens (if appliable);
- **Alert time** ¨C The earliest time in the video when the accident (or near-collision) could be predicted. <br>
 

Your model should aim to predict this moment as early and accurately as possible.
This dataset will help you develop a model that can differentiate between high-risk situations and normal driving, ensuring accurate and reliable predictions. Both collisions and near-misses are treated as positive examples, and your model should be able to identify these scenarios as potential accidents. <br>
 

Our testing dataset consist of 1344 videos with an average of 10 seconds duration. Part of these videos were collision or near-miss cases, and they have been trimmed within the time-to-accident interval before these events occur. The others are normal driving videos. The actual time-to-accident values for each video are kept private and will be used to evaluate your models. Please note that test data cannot be used in the model training.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Based on the provided annotations we define **time to accident** as the gap between event time and alert time. Submissions are evaluated using the Mean Average Precision over different times to accident. In particular, we compute 3 Precision-Recall (PR) curves, each corresponding to a specific time to accident (500ms, 1000ms and 1500ms). The Average Precision (AP) is calculated for each curve, and the final score is the mean of these three AP values. This method quantifies the prediction power of the solution in terms of Recall, Precision and anticipation times.


## Submission File

The submission file should have one line per test video with the video id (filename without extension) and a 0..1 score (higher scores imply a higher likelihood of collision/near-miss).

```
id,score
00204,0.4627
00030,0.0824
00146,0.7864
00020,0.1278
```

----------------------
# License 

Please check Competition Rules for details
----------------------
# Paper and Citation

A [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03848) is available describing the dataset and the evaluation framework used on this competition.

Please **use the following reference when citing** this competition or the dataset:

>Daniel C. Moura, Shizhan Zhu, and Orly Zvitia . **Nexar Dashcam Collision Prediction Dataset and Challenge**. https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03848, 2025. 

BibTeX:

```bibtex
@misc{nexar2025dashcamcollisionprediction,
      title={Nexar Dashcam Collision Prediction Dataset and Challenge}, 
      author={Daniel C. Moura and Shizhan Zhu and Orly Zvitia},
      year={2025},
      eprint={2503.03848},
      archivePrefix={arXiv},
      primaryClass={cs.CV},
      url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03848}, 
}
```",,False,True
9132,mo-i-competition-mnis-tx-cifar,"# Evaluation

Evaluation will be based on the accuracy score. The submissions.csv file that your code will create will be used to map your answers against the correct solution.
----------------------
# Description

This is the final evaluation for the deep learning MoI course.","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Name: MNISTxCIFAR

### Description:

The MNISTxCIFAR dataset is a unique blend of two of the most popular computer vision datasets: MNIST and CIFAR10. The dataset was created by overlaying the grayscale MNIST digit images onto the color images of CIFAR10, resulting in a new, more complex dataset for multiclass classification tasks.

Each image in the dataset is a 28x28 pixel image, where the background is from the CIFAR10 dataset and the foreground is a colored digit from the MNIST dataset. The colorization of the digits is done randomly. The goal of the classification task is to predict the MNIST digit present in the image. 

### File Descriptions:

- `./MNISTxCIFAR/train/`: This directory contains 10 sub-directories labeled from 0 to 9. Each sub-directory represents one class and contains images from the respective class. Each image is named as a unique number from 0 to N, where N is the number of images in the class. The total number of images in the training set is around 40,000.

- `./MNISTxCIFAR/test/`: This directory contains the test images saved with sequential filenames from `1.png` to `N.png`, where N is the total number of test images. The class labels of these images are not provided directly to prevent direct access to the ground truth in the competition setting.

### Objective:

The goal of the competition is to develop a model that can accurately predict the MNIST digit present in the images from the test set. The model will be evaluated based on its accuracy on the test set.",False,True
