9144,lrx-oeis-a-186783-brainstorm-math-conjecture,"# Overview

The task is to sort  given input sequences using only three allowed transformations: L - left cyclic shift, R - right cyclic shift, X - swap positions 0 and 1:  X(A,B,C,D,....) = (B,A,C,D,....),  Left shift: L(A,B,C,D,...U,V,Z) = (B,C,D,...U,V,Z,A), and Right shift: R(A,B,C,D,...U,V,Z) = (Z,A,B,C,D...,U,V). It is known that every permutation of (0,1,2,3,...,n-1) can be transformed to the sorted form using L,R,X transformations.

**Goal** is to find the sequence of L, R, and X transformations that sorts the given elements, your score - the number of moves - make it as fewest as you can.

The exact values of such transformations in the worst case ([diameters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diameter_(graph_theory)) of Cayley graphs or ""[God's number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God%27s_algorithm)"") conjectured to be n(n-1)/2 for permutations of n letters. But that is known only up to n=13  ([OEIS A186783](https://oeis.org/A186783)). The given sequences are precisely those which we expect to have a length of n(n-1)/2. 

----------------------
# Description

The problem represents a typical challenge in mathematics: decomposing an element of a [group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Group_(mathematics)) into given [generators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_metric), or in other words, finding a path on the [Cayley graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley_graph) of a group. Research on Cayley graphs is of fundamental importance for mathematics and the  field contains many notoriously hard problems, which has been addressed by renowned mathematicians such as M. Gromov [1] and T. Tao [2], among others. Moreover, it has numerous applications, ranging from bioinformatics (e.g., estimating evolutionary distance¡ª""Transform men into mice"" [3]) to the optimisation of interprocessor networks [4], and beyond.


The specific challenge is to decompose the particular element of the [permutation (symmetric) group  \( S_n \)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetric_group) into three specific generators: \( L, R, X \)¡ªrepresenting cyclic shifts and a swap. Our conjecture states that the length of the optimal decomposition is equal to $$ \frac{n(n-1)}{2}  $$ The goal is to find such a decomposition for n = 2,..., 200.  This conjecture is motivated by the open problem described in [The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences A186752](https://oeis.org/A186752), which predicts that the diameter of the group is n(n-1)/2. Our conjecture is more precise: the given element has the maximum length and is the only one of this length.  Conjecture originates from  [OIES-A186752](https://oeis.org/A186752), [5], discussion on [MSE](https://math.stackexchange.com/q/5026192/21498) and our further analysis.  The description of that longest element, other long elements and some more conjectures can be found in the notebook [long permutations](https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexandervc/lrx-long-elements-f-petrov-oeisa186752).

This challenge is similar to the [Kaggle Santa 2023 challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2023), but it focuses on a different group and a specific mathematical conjecture.

By participating, you will help unlock the mysteries of mathematics, and your ideas might contribute to solving problems that have remained challenging for many years.

###  References 

[1] M.Gromov ""Geometric Group Theory: Asymptotic invariants of infinite groups"", 1993
[2] T.Tao ""Expansion in finite simple groups of Lie type"", 2015
[3] P.Pevzner, S. Hannenhalli ""Transforming men into mice (polynomial algorithm for genomic distance problem)"",1995
[4] S. Akers, B. Krishnamurthy ""A group-theoretic model for symmetric interconnection networks"", 1989
[5] L. Babai, W. Kantor, A. Lubotsky ""Small-diameter Cayley graphs for finite simple groups"", 1989
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submission will be scored by the sum of the lengths of correct answers for all n. If the answer for any n does not result in a sorted permutation, the solution will not be accepted.

$$ score = \sum_{2}^{200}  len(solution_n)  $$

## Submission File
For each n in the test set, you must predict a sequence of moves that transforms the sequence of length n from test.csv into one sorted in ascending order. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    n, solution
    2	L
    3	X
    4	XRXLXLLL
    5	XRXLXLLXLL
    etc.","# Dataset Description

In this competition, there is exactly one permutation for each number **n** in **test.csv**. It is necessary to find the shortest number of steps that would lead it to an ascending order. 

The moves are encoded as follows:
**X** is the permutation of the first two elements,
**L** is a cyclic shift to the left,
**R** is a cyclic shift to the right.

In the submission file, you must output **n** and a string consisting of the characters X,L,R.

## Files

*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `n` - length of the permutation
*   `permutation`?- comma-separated permutation of numbers from 0 to n-1 
*   `solution` - sequence of moves to sort the sequence",False,True
