9151,lrx-discover-math-gods-algorithm,"# Overview

The task is to sort  given input sequences using only three allowed transformations L,R,X: L - left cyclic shift, R - right cyclic shift, X - swap positions 0 and 1:  X(A,B,C,D,....) = (B,A,C,D,....),  Left shift: L(A,B,C,D,...U,V,Z) = (B,C,D,...U,V,Z,A), and Right shift: R(A,B,C,D,...U,V,Z) = (Z,A,B,C,D...,U,V). It is known that any sequence can be transformed to the sorted one using L,R,X transformations.

**Goal** is to find the shortest sorting sequence of L,R,X permutations - for each entry in test data. Your score - is the sum of lengths.

The test set contains random permutations of lengths 8,16,32,64,128 each 100 random permutations.  Ideal goal is to discover the optimal algorithm which can work not only for permutations given in test set - but any random permutation. Optimal algorithm sometimes called ""[God's algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God%27s_algorithm)"".  Such algorithm is unknown for LRX transformations. It is conjectured  ([OEIS A186783](https://oeis.org/A186783)) it can sort any permutations not more than n(n-1)/2 transformations and we expect that generic random permutation needs approximately 0.37n?2-0.85n+2 moves. 

It is follow-up to the https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lrx-oeis-a-186783-brainstorm-math-conjecture, where the longest (conjecturally) permutations were asked  to be sorted. 
----------------------
# Description

The problem represents a typical challenge in mathematics: decomposing an element of a [group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Group_(mathematics)) into given [generators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_metric), or in other words, finding a path on the [Cayley graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley_graph) of a group. Research on Cayley graphs is of fundamental importance for mathematics and the  field contains many notoriously hard problems, which has been addressed by renowned mathematicians such as M. Gromov [1] and T. Tao [2], among others. Moreover, it has numerous applications, ranging from bioinformatics (e.g., estimating evolutionary distance¡ª""Transform men into mice"" [3]) to the optimisation of interprocessor networks [4], and beyond.

This challenge is similar to the [Kaggle Santa 2023 challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2023), but it focuses on a different group and a specific mathematical conjecture.

By participating, you will help unlock the mysteries of mathematics, and your ideas might contribute to solving problems that have remained challenging for many years.

###  References 

[1] M.Gromov ""Geometric Group Theory: Asymptotic invariants of infinite groups"", 1993
[2] T.Tao ""Expansion in finite simple groups of Lie type"", 2015
[3] P.Pevzner, S. Hannenhalli ""Transforming men into mice (polynomial algorithm for genomic distance problem)"",1995
[4] S. Akers, B. Krishnamurthy ""A group-theoretic model for symmetric interconnection networks"", 1989
[5] L. Babai, W. Kantor, A. Lubotsky ""Small-diameter Cayley graphs for finite simple groups"", 1989
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submission will be scored by the sum of the lengths of correct answers for all permutations. If the answer for any permutation does not result in a sorted permutation, the solution will not be accepted.

$$ score = \sum_{p \in permutations}  len(solution_p)  $$

## Submission File
For each permutation in the test set, you must predict a sequence of moves that transforms the permutation from test.csv into one sorted in ascending order. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    permutation	    solution
    7,4,0,5,3,6,2,1	X.R.R.X.R.X.L.X.L.X.R.R.R.X.L.X.R.R.X.L.X.L.X
    2,0,6,5,7,1,4,3	X.R.X.L.X.L.L.X.L.L.X.L.X.R.R.X.L.X.R.R.X.L.X
    etc.","# Dataset Description

In this competition, there are 100 permutations for each **n** in **test.csv**. It is necessary to find the shortest number of steps that would lead them to an ascending order. 

The moves are encoded as follows:
**X** is the permutation of the first two elements,
**L** is a cyclic shift to the left,
**R** is a cyclic shift to the right.

In the submission file, you must output **permutation** and a string consisting of the characters X,L,R.

## Files

*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `n` - length of the permutation
*   `permutation`?- comma-separated permutation of numbers from 0 to n-1 
*   `solution` - dot-separated sequence of moves to sort the sequence",False,True
