Deze map bevat 500 foto's van huizen. De afbeeldingen zijn gemaakt van telkens 1 foto van de slaapkamer, de badkamer, de keuken en het vooraanzicht van het huis. De vier foto's worden vervolgens samengevoegd tot ¨¦¨¦n beeld per huis. 

train.csv 

Hierin vind je de metadata die bij de foto¡¯s horen. De kolom ""Price"" bevat de targetvariabele. Deze kolom geeft de verkoopprijs van het huis in Amerikaanse dollars weer. De andere kolommen geven het aantal slaapkamers, het aantal badkamers, de oppervlakte (in vierkante voet) en de lengte- en breedtegraad van de locatie weer. 

Map Test: 

Deze map bevat 35 afbeeldingen van huizen. Net als in de map ¡®Train¡¯ komen ook deze afbeeldingen tot stand door de foto¡¯s van de slaapkamer, badkamer, keuken en vooraanzicht aan elkaar te plakken. 

test.csv 

Hierin vind je de metadata die bij de testfoto¡¯s horen, maar de ¡°Price¡± kolom ontbreekt. Het getrainde model pas je toe op de foto¡¯s in map Test en de gegevens in dit bestand. De voorspellingen die hieruit komen gebruik je om in te leveren op Kaggle. De voorspellingen lever je in met een csv-bestand zoals submission_example.csv. 

submission_example.csv 

De eerste kolom bevat de huis id¡¯s (¡®House ID¡¯) van alle huizen in de map Test en test.csv. Nadat je je voorspellingen hebt gedaan plaats je deze in de kolom ¡®Price¡¯ van dit bestand. Vervolgens kun je dit bestand uploaden op Kaggle.",False,True
9157,car-price-prediction-khazani-ahmed,"# Overview

Problem Statement
A Chinese automobile company Geely Auto aspires to enter the US market by setting up their manufacturing unit there and producing cars locally to give competition to their US and European counterparts.

They have contracted an automobile consulting company to understand the factors on which the pricing of cars depends. Specifically, they want to understand the factors affecting the pricing of cars in the American market, since those may be very different from the Chinese market. The company wants to know:

Which variables are significant in predicting the price of a car
How well those variables describe the price of a car
Based on various market surveys, the consulting firm has gathered a large data set of different types of cars across the America market.

Business Goal
We are required to model the price of cars with the available independent variables. It will be used by the management to understand how exactly the prices vary with the independent variables. They can accordingly manipulate the design of the cars, the business strategy etc. to meet certain price levels. Further, the model will be a good way for management to understand the pricing dynamics of a new market.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Metric: Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)


RMSE is the square root of the mean squared differences between the actual and predicted prices:


Why RMSE?
? Punishes Large Errors: Since errors are squared before averaging, RMSE gives more weight to large mistakes, encouraging more accurate predictions.
? Interpretable: It is in the same unit as the target variable (car price).",,False,True
9158,slicee-my-face,"# Overview

Welcome to SLICE MY FACE, a thrilling computer vision challenge where participants must develop models to accurately segment different facial features in images!

Given a dataset of facial images with corresponding segmentation masks, your goal is to train a model that can identify and separate key facial components
1) Skin
2) Eyebrows 
3)Lips 
....  and others 
Your model will take an input image and generate a pixel-wise mask that precisely labels these regions.
----------------------
# Description

##Task
Given an input image, the objective is to generate a binary mask that correctly identifies the segmented region. The masks must be submitted in RLE format, where each image's segmented region is represented as a sequence of pixel runs
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using the Dice Coefficient, which measures the overlap between the predicted and ground truth masks. The score ranges from 0 to 1, where 1 indicates a perfect match between the predicted and true segmentation masks.

---



## Submission File
For each image in the test set, you must predict a segmentation mask in run-length encoding (RLE) format. The submission file should contain a header and follow the structure below:

id,predicted
image_001,rle_encoded_mask_1
image_002,rle_encoded_mask_2
image_003,rle_encoded_mask_3
...
##Column Descriptions:
id: The filename of the test image (without the file extension).
predicted: The predicted segmentation mask in Run-Length Encoding (RLE) format.
Each row corresponds to a single test image","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of two main folders:

1) **images/** - Contains the original images.  
2) **annotations/** - Contains the corresponding segmentation masks.

Each of these folders is further divided into three subsets:

- **train/** - Images and masks for model training.  
- **test/** - Images for which participants need to generate segmentation masks.  
- **val/** - Images and masks for validation.  

### Important Note:
Participants have been provided with the **masks for test images** as well. However, for the competition, you are required to submit the **Run-Length Encoded (RLE) masks** for your predicted segmentations. Additionally, you will be asked to submit your notebooks after the competition for verification.",False,True
9159,airise-image-regression-challenge-challenge,"# Overview

Milling chatter is a major challenge in manufacturing: it induces vibrations that can hinder machine performance and lifetime, precision, product quality and tool ware. As such, it should be prevented.
The current techniques for preventing milling chatter rely on trial and error processes, as well as expert operation. Such techniques do not allow for high levels of automation.

One further challenge in predicting this chatter vibration lies in a phenomenon known as *Concept Drift*, describing how statistical properties of a data set can change as time goes by. This means that a model trained on data acquired over a specific time period may decrease in performance over time.

**Goal:** Your objective in this hackathon is to build a Machine Learning based solution that predicts milling chatter based on vibration sensor data acquired from the milling machine spindle, and yet remains valid despite drifting of the dataset properties (test set).
----------------------
# Description

In this section, learn more about the details of the competition. You will find here all you need to know about milling chatter, the motivation behind this competition, the experimental setup and all the information that you need to know behind the dataset.
<!-- NB: Images can be included from the Kaggle webppage -->

## Milling Chatter

Milling chatter is a phenomenon characterized by unwanted vibrations during the milling process. These vibrations can significantly affect the performance and longevity of milling machines, as well as the precision and quality of the manufactured products. Understanding and detecting how milling chatter occurs and the problems it creates is crucial for optimizing milling manufacturing processes, and automating them.

### How Milling Chatter Occurs

Milling chatter typically occurs because of the interaction between the cutting tool and the workpiece. When the tool engages with the material, it generates cutting forces that can cause the tool, the workpiece and the machine to vibrate. If these vibrations reach a frequency matching the machine's natural frequency, they can become self-sustaining, leading to a feedback loop where the vibrations amplify themselves, further enhancing the phenomenon. This is often influenced by factors such as:

- **Tool geometry**: The shape and size of the cutting tool can affect the stability of the milling process.
- **Material properties**: Different materials have varying resistance to cutting forces, which can influence vibration behavior.
- **Cutting parameters**: Speed, feed rate, and depth of cut are critical parameters that can either mitigate or exacerbate chatter.
- **Machine dynamics**: The stiffness and damping characteristics of the milling machine play a significant role in vibration control.

### Problems Created by Milling Chatter

Milling chatter can lead to several issues in manufacturing, including:

- **Reduced precision**: Vibrations can cause deviations in the tool path, leading to inaccuracies in the final product dimensions.
- **Poor surface finish**: Chatter marks can appear on the surface of the workpiece, compromising its quality.
- **Increased tool wear**: Excessive vibrations can accelerate the wear and tear of cutting tools, reducing their lifespan.
- **Machine damage**: Persistent chatter can cause mechanical stress on the milling machine, potentially leading to breakdowns and costly repairs.
- **Production inefficiencies**: The need to frequently adjust parameters to control chatter can result in wasted time, resources, and materials.


## Experimental Setup
<!-- Axes directions must be verified -->

The challenge you will be working on stems from a real-world implementation taking place at CNC Solutions, a CNC specialist based in Greece.
To acquire data, we have set-up sensors on the milling vice capable of retrieving vibration data along three axes:
- The X axis (Along machine depth)
- The Y axis (Along machine width)
- The Z axis (Along machine height)

Curious about how this all looks? Check out the [CNC Solutions blog](https://www.cncsolutions.com/en/blog/cnc-solutions-in-the-age-of-artificial-intelligence).

## Baseline
For more information about a baseline, check [this article](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.procir.2024.10.171)
----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition is framed as a binary classification challenge, and its evaluation is based on the **F1 Score**, which you can find information on [GeeksforGeeks](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/f1-score-in-machine-learning/) or [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_score) for a more academic explanation.

The F1 Score is defined as the harmonic mean of **Precision** and **Recall**, and is calculated using the following equation:

$$
\text{F1 Score} = 2 \cdot \frac{\text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}}
$$

Where:
- **Precision** is the proportion of true positive predictions out of all positive predictions:
  $$
  \text{Precision} = \frac{\text{True Positives}}{\text{True Positives} + \text{False Positives}}
  $$
- **Recall** is the proportion of true positive predictions out of all actual positives:
  $$
  \text{Recall} = \frac{\text{True Positives}}{\text{True Positives} + \text{False Negatives}}
  $$

The F1 Score provides a balance between Precision and Recall, making it a useful metric for imbalanced datasets where one class may dominate over the other.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a target value. In this file, the TARGET column contains numeric values according to the following mapping:  
- **""stable""** ¡ú 0  
- **""chatter""** ¡ú 1  

The file should contain a header, saved in a `.csv` format, and have the following structure:

    ID,TARGET
    2,0
    5,0
    6,1
    etc.",,False,True
9160,ufaz-news-classifier-challenge,"# Overview

## Goal of the Competition
The objective of this competition is to develop a solution capable of classifying Azerbaijani news articles into predefined categories. Participants will be provided with a dataset of news articles scraped from popular Azerbaijani news websites. Your task is to build a robust solution that can accurately assign a category to each article based on its content.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The performance of your solution will be evaluated using the **F1-score**, calculated as a **weighted average** across all labels. This means the F1-score is computed independently for each label, and the results are averaged, with each class's contribution weighted by its support (the number of true instances for each class).

The formula for the F1-score is:

$$
\text{F1} = 2 \cdot \frac{\text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}}
$$

Where:
- **Precision** = $$\frac{\text{True Positives}}{\text{True Positives} + \text{False Positives}}$$
- **Recall** = $$\frac{\text{True Positives}}{\text{True Positives} + \text{False Negatives}}$$

The final score will be the weighted average F1-score across all classes, where each class's score is weighted by its number of true instances.

## Submission File Format
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the corresponding category in text format. Your submission file should include a header and follow the format below:
```
id,category
0,cemiyyet
1,cemiyyet
```
----------------------
# Timeline

- **Start Date:** February 12, 2025
- **Final Submission Deadline:** April 12, 2025

*Note:* The competition organizers reserve the right to update the timeline if necessary.
----------------------
# Code Requirements

## This is a Code Competition
Submissions must be made through **Notebooks**. To ensure your submission is valid, the following conditions must be met:

- **CPU Notebook:** Maximum run-time of 9 hours.
- **GPU Notebook:** Maximum run-time of 9 hours.
- **Internet Access:** Disabled.
- **External Data:** Not allowed.
- **Pre-trained Models:** Only models publicly available before the competition start date are allowed. These must be used within your code (no external fine-tuning). These models must be sourced.
- **Submission File Name:** Must be named `submission.csv`.

For more details on how to submit, please refer to the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-faq). If you encounter any issues, check out the [code debugging documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/code-debugging-doc) for assistance.

---",,False,True
9161,depi-r-2-emotion-analysis,"# Overview

An emotion classification problem. Given a tweet or short text, the goal is to predict the emotion category the text expresses. There are 6 emotion categories: anger, fear, joy, love, sadness, and surprise.

----------------------
# Description


## Q1: Exploration
#### (1) How many tweets (sentences) were used to train and validate sets?
#### (2) What are the target predictive labels?
#### (3) What are the distributions of labels in training and validation sets?
#### (4) Analyze the words per tweet in the train and validation (e.g., use wordcloud).
#### (5) Drop down your observations and insights: 

- Are the classes balanced? What are the potential implications of class imbalance?
- Are there any words that seem particularly indicative of certain emotions?
- Do the training and validation sets appear similar in terms of vocabulary and tweet length?

## Q2:Preprocessing
#### (1) Use 2 different tokinzers (e.g., e.g., bert-base-uncased, distilbert-base-uncased, roberta-base) from hugging face.
#### (2) Report the tokenizer's vocabulary size.
#### (3) Present the tokenization results for the selected tokenizer on three examples.
#### (4) Drop down your observation and insights:

- How do the two tokenizers differ in their handling of these examples?
- What are the advantages and disadvantages of each tokenizer, based on your observations?
- How does the tokenizer handle out-of-vocabulary words?
- Explain the purpose of [CLS] and [SEP] tokens and how they are used in the context of BERT.

## Q3: Modeling (Fine-Tuning & Performance)
#### (1) Build and Fine-Tune a BERT-Based Emotion Classifier:  

a. *Encoder Selection:*  Use the bert-base-uncased pre-trained model from the Hugging Face Transformers library.
b. *Classifier Head:* Utilize `transformers.AutoModelForSequenceClassification` to easily add a classification head on top of the BERT model. This class automatically configures the model for sequence classification tasks. 
c. *Fine tuning process:* Fine-tune the pre-trained BERT model on the provided emotion classification training dataset. This involves updating the model's weights to better adapt it to the specific task of emotion recognition.

#### (2) Document your observations and insights regarding the modeling process, including:
- What were the main challenges you encountered during fine-tuning?
- Which hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, epochs) did you experiment with, and how did they impact the model's performance?.
- Report the model's performance on the validation set (report all metrics calculated). Is there any evidence of overfitting? If so, what steps did you take to mitigate it?
- What are the limitations of your model? Are there specific emotions that the model struggles to classify correctly? Based on your analysis of the confusion matrix, can you identify any common patterns in the misclassified examples?
- How could the model be further improved? Consider data augmentation, different model architectures, or other advanced techniques.

**> Optionally use pytorch**
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation section describes how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. Below is an example of a typical evaluation page.

---

Submissions are evaluated on [balanced accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Precision_and_recall) between the predicted probability and the observed target.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the **label** variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,label
    2,0
    5,0
    6,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description


The dataset contains English tweets annotated with one of the 6 emotion categories. It consists of:

- **416,809** tweets in total, split into:
    - Train: **416,809** tweets   
    - Validation: **2,000** tweets
    - Test: **2,000** tweets

- Each tweet has:
    - `text`: The tweet text  
    - `label`: The emotion category, an integer from 0 to 5 representing one of the 6 emotions.",False,True
9162,hw-2-for-cs-2731-spring-2025,"# Overview

This is the optional challenge portion of [HW2](https://michaelmilleryoder.github.io/cs2731_fall2024/hw2) of the CS 2731 course at the University of Pittsburgh, run in the Fall 2024 term.

**This challenge is for logistic regression models that do not incorporate any metadata columns outside the `text` column. There is [another challenge for neural network models](https://www.kaggle.com/t/8d83a90ef8a94dbca5b257424eecd13e), which can incorporate features from any column.**

## Training a model
You can use all of the data in `deception_cv.csv` to train a model to make predictions on the test set for the challenge. This is the same data that you reported cross-validation scores on for Part 2 of the assignment.

## Submitting
You will submit your predictions on the `deception_test.csv` file provided on the [HW2 website](https://michaelmilleryoder.github.io/cs2731_fall2024/hw2).

Each submission on Kaggle shows both a ""team name"" and the members of that team. You are only allowed to make submissions individually, i.e. your team will just be you. **This competition is for submitting a model trained with logistic regression, with any features you choose to extract from the `text` column only.**

Please see the following section on Evaluation on how to format your submission.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on accuracy between the predicted intent labels and the gold annotated labels on the test sets.

## Submission File
For each datapoint in the test set, your system must make a prediction of truth (0) or lie (1) for the `intent` column. The submitted file should **only contain 2 columns: `id` and `intent`** (other columns must be removed before submitting otherwise they will not be scored). The `id` column corresponds to the same column in `diplomacy_test.csv`.

Here's an example with made-up labels:

    id,intent
    Game1-england-italy-191,0
    Game10-england-france-3,1
    Game1-germany-france-86,1
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Data files needed for training models and for this submitting to this challenge are available at the [HW2 instructions page](https://michaelmilleryoder.github.io/cs2731_fall2024/hw2). As noted in the instructions, this data is sampled from the Diplomacy deception corpus, [Peskov et al. 2020](https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.353/). It consists of negotiations between players, annotated for whether the intent of the messages was truthful (0) or lying (1).

In particular for this challenge, you will be making predictions on the `diplomacy_test.csv` file provided on the HW2 website.

**This challenge is just for models based on logistic regression, that only use the `text` input column and not other metadata columns. There is also a challenge for neural network models where use of non-text metadata is allowed.**

## Necessary columns

 * `text`: the text of the comment
 * `intent`: a binary class label of whether the comment was annotated by the author as truthful (0) or lying (1). This is just a placeholder in the test set",False,True
