9184,rnd-42-practice,"# Overview

This is an educational competition for the members of the RnD-42 team and all other participants are welcome also.
----------------------
# Description

The competition is about solving a regression problem over a dataset almost without a description of its columns.

Train any regression model you like on the data from the training dataset and submit the result of the prediction for the test dataset. Please beat the baseline at least and then do your best to improve the MAE score.

Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <strong>MAE</strong></p><p> 
</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission file should contain two columns delimited by comma: ID (each row in the test dataset has unique ID) and Y (the result of prediction, float).</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,Y<br>1,1.5435<br>2,0.0<br>3,5.75<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

We solve a regression problem over a dataset without a description of its columns. The only known things about the data are:

Each row has unique ""ID"" (to merge with targets)

Each row in the dataset represents the total number of usage of some service by user identified by ""UID"" during the period that starts from ""DT"" and lasts 21 days.

Columns with the ""N_"" prefix represent numeric features, with the ""C_"" prefix - §ãategorial features. ""N_D1_TRS"" .. ""N_D9_TRS"" are the same number of usage during 1,2..9 same 21-day periods ago and all other features describe somehow the user and a consumption picture.

## Files

*   **BG_train_RG_2.csv** - the training set
*   **BG_train_RG_2_Result.csv** - targets for the training set
*   **BG_test_RG_2.csv** - the test set
*   **BG_sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
