9187,test-run-2,"# Overview

Advancements in **AI** have made it increasingly difficult to distinguish **real images** from **AI-generated images**, such as those created by **diffusion models**. This competition is aimed at **students** looking to enhance their **AI skills and experience** by developing models that can detect these AI-generated images with **accuracy** and **efficiency**.  

### *Forking [this](https://github.com/ivanrj7j/DSC-Test-Run) repository is encouraged (but not mandatory)*

### ?? Submission Format  

Participants must submit their predictions in a CSV file, which will be evaluated against a hidden ground-truth file. The submission should contain two columns:  

- **id** ¨C The unique identifier for each image, starting from **0**  
- **label** ¨C The predicted class (0 for **AI-generated**, 1 for **real**)  

Below is an example of a correctly formatted submission file:  

| id  | label |
|----|------|
| 0  | 1  |
| 1  | 0  |
| 2  | 1  |
| 3  | 0  |
| 4  | 1  |

### ?? Important Notes  
- The **id** column must match the **id** values in the provided **test.csv** file.  
- The **label** column should contain only **0 or 1**, indicating the model¡¯s prediction.  
- The submission file **must not** contain any extra columns.  
- The final evaluation will compare the submitted predictions with the ground-truth labels using **Area Under Curve (AUC)** as the primary metric.  
- The filename can be **anything**, as long as it is a valid CSV file.  

### ?? How to Submit  
1. **Prepare the submission file**  
   - Ensure the format matches the above table.  
   - Save the file as a CSV (e.g., `submission.csv`).  

2. **Submit through GitHub (Optional but Encouraged)**  
   - Push your submission file to your **GitHub repository**.  
   - Share the repository link with the organizers for evaluation.  
   - A README file describing your approach is recommended.  

3. **Use Kaggle for Coding**  
   - Participants can utilize **Kaggle's code tab** to write, test, and refine their models.  
   - Kaggle notebooks allow easy experimentation and dataset access.  

4. **Dataset Availability**  
   - The dataset is available in the **Data section** of this competition.  
   - You can also generate your own datasets if desired.  

5. **Go to the submission page**  
   - Navigate to the competition¡¯s **""Submit Predictions""** section.  

6. **Upload the CSV file**  
   - Click on the upload button and select your **submission CSV file**.  
   - Ensure the file is properly formatted before submitting.  

7. **Submit and check the results**  
   - After submission, you will see an evaluation of your model¡¯s performance.  
   - Public scores will be displayed during the competition, while final rankings will be based on private scores.

## **Guidelines**  
- Participants must create their **own datasets** or use **publicly available ones**.  
- Submitting an **AI model is not mandatory**, but if you choose to submit one, you can release it on your **GitHub repository**.  
- If submitting a model, refer to the **README.md** in the repository for instructions on how to release it.  
- **The simplest effective model is preferred**, but complex architectures are allowed.  
- **Use of pre-trained models or fine-tuning is not allowed.**  
- All **code, datasets, and results** must be **well-documented**.  
- Collaborative discussions on **Kaggle forums** are encouraged.  
- Ensure **ethical data usage** and avoid **bias in model training**.  
- Winners will be selected based on **model performance, efficiency, and innovation**.  

## **Competition Structure**  
- Participants will work with **real and AI-generated image datasets**, either by **generating their own** or utilizing **publicly available sources**.  
- The goal is to build the **most effective model** while maintaining **simplicity in architecture**.  

## **Evaluation Criteria**  
Submissions will be assessed based on:  
- **Area Under Curve (AUC)** (Primary metric)  
- **Accuracy**  
- **Precision**  
- **Recall**  
- **Model simplicity (simpler models preferred)**  

## **Submission Requirements**  
- Proper **documentation** and explanation of methodology are required.  
- Participants are encouraged to create a **README file** in their repository explaining their approach, model selection, dataset details, and key findings.  

This competition not only tests **AI capabilities** but also fosters **innovation in digital forensics** and **ethical AI development**.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Participants are encouraged to **split their training data** into **train and test partitions** to ensure better model validation. However, final evaluation will be conducted using a **separate testing dataset** provided by the competition.  

### **Evaluation Metrics**  
Models will be assessed based on the following metrics:  
- **F1 Score** (highest weight in evaluation)  
- **Accuracy**  
- **Recall**  
- **Precision**  

A **higher F1 score** indicates a well-balanced model that effectively detects both **real** and **fake** images, minimizing false positives and false negatives. The final ranking will be determined based on **overall performance across all metrics**, with an emphasis on **generalization and robustness**.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains images labeled as **real or fake** to train AI models for deepfake detection. Participants will use the **training dataset** to develop models and submit predictions in **test.csv**, which will be compared with a hidden ground-truth file for evaluation, primarily based on the **F1 score**.  

### **Files**  
- **`train.csv`** ¨C The training set containing image file names and corresponding labels.  
- **`test.csv`** ¨C The test set containing image file names; participants must predict and submit labels in this file.  

### **Columns**  
- **`Unnamed: 0`** ¨C Row index (can be ignored).  
- **`file`** ¨C Image file name.  
- **`label`** ¨C Image classification:  
  - **0** ¨C Fake image  
  - **1** ¨C Real image  
  - **NaN** ¨C (In `test.csv`) Participants must fill in their predictions.  

Participants must train their models using **train.csv**, generate predictions for **test.csv**, and submit the completed file. The results will be compared against a hidden **ground-truth file** for evaluation.",False,True
