9207,nwds-k,"# Overview

# Nick Wan's Strikeout Prediction Community Competition 

Using the latest data from MLB, can you predict when a strikeout occurs? The newest feature available, `arm_angle`, allows us to see how a pitch moves relative to the pitcher's unique release characteristics. While previous release characteristic data was helpful (e.g. release point XYZ), arm angle allows us to know more about whether a pitcher is throwing more overhead, more to the side, or more submarine, and anywhere in between. The combination of arm angle and pitch movement can elevate our understanding of how a pitcher generates strikes -- and ultimately strikeouts. 

The competition lasts until March 19th at 8:00pm US Eastern Time and winners will be announced on stream March 19th at 9:00pm US Eastern Time.

Check out the stream here:
https://twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci

Join the discord to talk about data science and sports analytics here:
https://discord.gg/3sHgSPsPzS  

NOTE: This has no relation to the Cincinnati Reds, it's all for my community who support my streams every night! For those looking for the next Cincinnati Reds Hackathon, this is not the Reds Hackathon. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

LOG LOSS 

If you are interested in sharing your notebook and walking through your code with Nick on stream, leave a message in the discord, on the discussion board, or submit your notebook!

The competition lasts until March 19th at 8:00pm US Eastern Time and winners will be announced on stream March 19th at 9:00pm US Eastern Time.

Check out the stream here:
https://twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci

Join the discord to talk about data science and sports analytics here:
https://discord.gg/3sHgSPsPzS
----------------------
# Team Requirements

You can be on a team with up to 4 people. To be eligible for the prizes, everyone on the team needs to be at least 18 years or older and live in the US.","# Dataset Description

This dataset is from the 2024 MLB season. This includes a subset of columns provided by Baseball Savant. New as of the end of 2024 is the `arm_angle` data, which is the arm angle of the pitcher at pitch release. 

The `train.csv` file contains all the data you need for this competition. The `test.csv` file contains specifically pitches thrown in 2-strike counts -- in other words, when there is a strikeout possibility. The `train.csv` file contains pitches in _all_ counts -- in other words, when there is strikeout possibilities and also when there is not strikeout possibilities. 

Please reference the Baseball Savant Statcast Search CSV documentation for a data dictionary:
https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/csv-docs

Some columns are unique to this competition. These are:

`index`: a unique ID for each row in the dataset
`is_strike`: whether the result of the pitch was a strike or not 
`k`: whether the result of the pitch was a strikeout or not 

Note: It is possible to cheat fairly simply. Please do not cheat!",False,True
9208,calcs-shared-task-2025,"# Overview

## Task Description
This shared task focuses on developing automatic evaluation metrics for code-switched (CS) text generation. Participants are tasked with creating systems that can accurately assess the quality of synthetically generated CS text, considering both fluency and accuracy.
----------------------
# Description

## Why is this important?
- Scarcity of CS Data: CS text data is limited, making automatic generation vital for data augmentation and improving model performance.
- Growing Demand: The need for CS text is increasing, particularly in dialogue systems and chatbots, to enable more natural and inclusive interactions.
- Lack of Robust Evaluation: Current methods for evaluating CS text are insufficient, hindering progress in this field.
## Languages Supported
### Public Leaderboard:
- English-Hindi
- English-Tamil
- English-Malayalam
### Private Leaderboard:
- English-Indonesian
- Indonesian-Javanese
- Singlish (English-Chinese)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Criteria
Evaluation Criteria
Metric
Systems will be evaluated based on their accuracy in predicting human preferences for CS text. This will be measured by comparing the system's ranking of generated sentences (Sent 1 vs. Sent 2) with human annotations in the CSPref dataset.

Dataset Structure
The CSPref dataset contains:

Original L1: English sentences
