9210,esa-adb-challenge,"# Summary

Your objective is to design an algorithm to detect anomalies in a multivariate time series setting. The data used in the Challenge is real spacecraft data coming from one of the [European Space Agency's missions](https://www.esa.int/). 

You have access to a large set of historical data, baseline algorithms, and detailed descriptions.

The Spacecraft Anomaly Challenge on ESA dataset starts now! ?? 
----------------------
# Description

*Please note that submissions are only considered from nationals of one of the following States:
Austria, Belgium, Czechia, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Greece, Hungary, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway, Poland, Portugal, Romania, Slovenia, Spain, Sweden, Switzerland, the United Kingdom, Canada, Latvia, Lithuania and Slovakia. 
Additional countries: Argentina, Australia, Bulgaria, Croatia, Cyprus, India, Japan, Malta, South Korea, United States of America.*

The competition is based on the **European Space Agency Anomaly Detection Benchmark** (ESA-ADB) dataset, released in 2024 [1] and publicly available on Zenodo https://zenodo.org/records/12528696. 

In 2021, ESA's European Space Operations Centre initiated a project to outline a clear direction for AI implementation in mission operations. This initiative led to the creation of the [Artificial Intelligence for Automation (A2I) Roadmap](https://esoc.esa.int/a2i-roadmap-0) [2], developed by a team of over 70 specialists at ESOC, with support from an industry consortium. The roadmap underwent further validation and refinement in collaboration with the European space and IT sectors.

Over the years, the A2I Roadmap evolved into the [AI and Data Foundation Project](https://esoc.esa.int/a2i-roadmap-0) that focuses on five key capabilities, one of which is ''Automated system health monitoring and control''. Anomaly detection plays a critical role in this capability, as early detection of anomalies can prevent potential dangers to the spacecraft. 

In this Challenge, your objective is to identify the anomalies experienced by one of ESA's missions using Artificial Intelligence. We hope that this unique Challenge that uses real world data will allow researchers and scientists from academia, research institutes, national and international space agencies, and industry to develop and benchmark novel approaches for anomaly detection for spacecraft telemetry data.

<img title=¡±id_155¡± src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1053275%2F37e3dd0059c77070d38f036da1e83467%2Fid_155.svg?generation=1741388116766180&alt=media"" style=""float:right; height:221px; width:444px"">

### Data
More details in the [Data tab](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/esa-adb-challenge/data)


### Code
The baseline algorithms were trained and run using the dedicated ESA-ADB framework **https://github.com/kplabs-pl/ESA-ADB** which is a recommended environment to work on new models for the task. Besides, the competition offers [notebooks](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/esa-adb-challenge/code) and [model files](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/esa-adb-challenge/models) to reproduce results of baseline algorithms directly at Kaggle.

All materials are released under a permissive [CC BY 3.0 IGO](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/igo/legalcode.en) license.

----------------------
# Awards ??

<img title=¡±aistar¡± src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1053275%2F1dc78398790e0eef0e6c592ee3fc6c77%2Fai_star.png?generation=1741627992267212&alt=media"" style=""float:right; height:200px; width:220px"">
<img title=¡±ecml¡± src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1053275%2Fcfcadc8e53bc383016ff435635f8daf4%2Fecml.png?generation=1741640788769061&alt=media"" style=""float:right; height:200px; width:220px"">


This Challenge is a qualifier to the final validation phase where algorithms will be tested on a hidden test set. Best teams will be invited to this 2nd phase that will determine the overall winner(s) eligible for prizes. The full list of prizes currently includes:
- **1-month remote access to the Leopard processing unit by KP Labs** via the brand-new [Smart Mission Lab platform](https://sml.kplabs.space/) to benchmark your models on flight-proven hardware with KP Labs¡¯ expert support (+ an option to widely promote the results through KP Labs¡¯ social media) - estimated value €5,000.
- Gifts from ESA and KP Labs

The award ceremony and best teams presentations will take place during the [**ESA AI STAR 2025**](https://atpi.eventsair.com/ai-star-2025). Winner(s) will be invited as co-authors of a joint paper summarizing the Challenge. 

All participants are invited to submit regular or short papers describing their algorithms to the dedicated session at the [ML4ITS workshop](https://ml4its.github.io/ml4its2025) of the [**ECML PKDD 2025 conference**](https://ecmlpkdd.org/2025/submissions-workshop-track/). The foreseen deadline for submissions is **~~14 June 2025~~** **21 June 2025** - nine days after competition ends, so participants are expected to work on the paper already before the Challenge finishes. The final Challenge results should be included in the paper. **Submitting at least a short paper or a presentation to the workshop is necessary to be eligible for the II phase and the final prize! The novelty is not essential and you do not need to present or participate at the conference to win the competition (however, in this case, your paper will not be included in the official conference proceedings).**

Intermediate results will be presented at [**SpaceOps 2025 conference**](https://www.spaceops2025.org/) 26-30 May 2025. 

## Hackathon (closed)
The Challenge started with a hackathon on 13 March 2025 during the [AI Symposium on Ground Segment Engineering, Operations, and Space Safety](https://commercialisation.esa.int/event/ai-symposium-on-ground-segment-engineering-operations-and-space-safety/) at ESA's European Space Operations Centre. The winner was **Simon Schaefer** (SHS Telespazio team, user: [shsshs](https://www.kaggle.com/shsshs))
----------------------
# Evaluation metric ?

Submissions are evaluated using the **corrected event-wise F0.5 score for anomaly detection in time series** introduced by Sehili & Zhang [3] and adopted as a primary metric in the ESA-ADB benchmark [1]. The metric is a harmonic mean of corrected event-wise precision and event-wise recall, with precision having 2 times higher importance than recall:

$$F_{0.5}=\frac{(1 + 0.5^2) * Precision_{e_{corr}} * Recall_e}{0.5^2 * Precision_{e_{corr}}  + Recall_e}$$

Event-wise precision and recall treat each continuous anomalous segment as a single event, regardless of its size. A segment is considered an event-wise true positive *`TPe`* if at least one of its samples is detected; otherwise, it is an event-wise false negative *`FNe`*. Any continuous detection that does not overlap with an anomalous segment is counted as an event-wise false positive *`FPe`*. Additionally, due to the correction applied to the event-wise precision, each falsely detected sample *`FPt`* decreases the value of the corrected event-wise precision relatively to the number of nominal sample *`Nt`*. 

$$Precision_{e_{corr}} = \frac{TP_e}{TP_e + FP_e} * (1 - \frac{FP_t}{N_t})$$

$$Recall_e = \frac{TP_e}{TP_e + FN_e}$$

In summary, **the proposed solutions should focus on**:
1. minimizing the number of event-wise and sample-wise false positives
1. producing short and compact detections (not necessarily covering the whole anomalous segment, it is enough to detect at least one sample)

The metric does **NOT** care about:
1. the level of overlap between correct detections and anomalous segments
1. detection timing

----------------------
# Submission file ??

For each index representing a consecutive time point in the test set, the solution file should contain a binary information if this time point contains an anomaly (1) or not (0). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| id | is_anomaly |
| --- | --- |
|14728321|0|
|14728322|0|
|14728323|1|
|14728324|0|
| ... | ...  |
|15249600|0|

The file can be in CSV, but we highly recommend using the [Apache Parquet format](https://parquet.apache.org/) to significantly decrease the file size
