9228,proto-p-net-competition,"# Overview

# ?? ProtoPNet for Interpretable Bird Classification  

Welcome to the **ProtoPNet Bird Species Classification** competition! In this challenge, your goal is to develop an **interpretable deep learning model** for bird species classification using **Prototypical Part Networks (ProtoPNets)**.  

Unlike traditional black-box models, ProtoPNet provides **human-interpretable justifications** for its classifications by associating predictions with learned prototypical image patches. These prototypes serve as **visual explanations**, helping us understand *why* the model made a particular decision.  

## ?? The Challenge  
Participants must train a **ProtoPNet-based model** to classify bird species from a paired down version of the **CUB-200-2011** dataset. Your final model should not only achieve good classification accuracy but also provide meaningful prototype visualizations that explain its predictions.  

## ?? What is ProtoPNet?  
ProtoPNet (Prototypical Part Network) is a **self-explainable** convolutional neural network that learns a set of prototypical image patches during training. These patches represent key features of each class, allowing the model to classify new images based on their similarity to learned prototypes.  

In this competition, we encourage participants to experiment with:  
? Different backbone architectures (e.g., VGG, ResNet, DenseNet)  
? Techniques for prototype pruning and refinement  
? Enhancing interpretability while maintaining classification performance  

----------------------
# Description

## ?? Evaluation  
Your submissions will be evaluated based on:  

Part 1) **Classification Accuracy:** 

An initial baseline submission and then two further prediction submissions that improve on your first. 

Part 2) **Prototype Interpretability:** 

Canvas assignment reflecting on prediction insights derived from the model's prototypes.

## ?? Getting Started  
- Refer to the [Kaggle competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions) for submission instructions.  
- Read about **ProtoPNet** in the paper [""This Looks Like That""](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.10574).  
- Check out the provided **starter notebook** to see how to train a ProtoPNet model.  
- Watch the provided **guide video** to get familiar with the algorithm and the organization of the provided code.
----------------------
# Guide Video

[![Watch on YouTube](https://img.youtube.com/vi/spU-9B5pIHE/hqdefault.jpg)](https://youtu.be/spU-9B5pIHE)

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on **classification accuracy**, which measures the proportion of correctly predicted labels in the test set. Accuracy is defined as:

$$
\text{Accuracy}=\frac{\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\text{Total Number of Samples}}
$$

where a correct prediction means that the predicted label exactly matches the ground truth label.

We use [scikit-learn¡¯s accuracy_score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html) function to compute the final score.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the corresponding label. The submission file should be a CSV with two columns:  
- `id`: A unique identifier corresponding to each test sample  
- `label`: The predicted class label  

The file should contain a header and follow this format:
```
id,label
0,4 
1,1 
2,3 
3,0
```

Submissions that do not follow this format may not be scored correctly.

The provided code should automatically create prediction files in this format.",,False,True
