9230,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-c-69,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.
----------------------
# FAQs

### How do I contact support?

For this competition, any questions, concerns, or technical difficulties will be addressed at the Discussion forum on UpGrad Learn platform. Any discussion posted regarding support on this competition page will have to be redirected to the same portal.

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

The Kaggle leaderboard has a public and private component to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

**Public Leaderboard:** For all participants, the same 70% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the public leaderboard. The score you see on the public leaderboard reflects your model¡¯s accuracy on this portion of the test set.

**Private Leaderboard:** The other 30% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the private leaderboard. The private leaderboard is not visible to participants until the competition has concluded. At the end of a competition, we will reveal the private leaderboard so you can see your score on the other 30% of the test data. The scores on the private leaderboard are used to determine the competition winners.

### What are kernels?

Kaggle Kernels is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels support scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the Kernels tab to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
9231,el-hackathon-2025,"# Overview

The primary objective of this challenge is to develop AI models that can accurately predict the spatial distribution of biological cell types in a tissue slice using routine H&E slides. This will enable deeper insights into tissue biology, bridging the gap between traditional histology and advanced spatial transcriptomics.
----------------------
# Description

## Introduction/Background
Histology, a century-old technique that uses dyes to reveal the structure of biological tissue under the microscope, has been the cornerstone for clinical diagnosis and characterization of diseases like cancer. While histology images of tissue samples can be routinely obtained in the lab, their accurate interpretation relies on clinical expertise of the pathologist, is time-consuming and subjective, and allows for only coarse-grained interpretations based on visually detectable features. 

On the other hand, technologies that can tag and measure the levels of molecules in a tissue sample enable a much more detailed characterization of tissue biology, and are capable of revealing clinical biomarkers informing disease prognosis as well as diagnosis. Such techniques have undergone a progressive evolution, from immunohistochemistry that can help visualize a few targeted features (protein markers) at a time, to more advanced genomic deep sequencing technologies that can simultaneously capture the abundances of tens of hundreds of molecular features. The recent advent of spatially resolved sequencing like spatial transcriptomics (SRT) has further expanded the possibilities. Today, it is possible to capture spatial patterns of expression of large numbers of genes at single-cell resolution, offering a highly nuanced view into the spatial cellular architecture of healthy tissue and its distortion in disease. For instance, the spatial cellular organization of the immune microenvironment of a tumor (TME) as revealed by SRT can inform fundamental questions of clinical significance, such as the severity of the disease and its expected progression in the patient, and what features might correlate with the effectiveness of a particular treatment.

Despite its promise, the widespread adoption of genomic deep sequencing in the clinical setting remains challenging, given the relatively high costs, and practical difficulties in preparing and preserving tissue samples obtained from a patient for the sequencing assay, especially in low-resource settings. This has spurred much work over the past decade to effectively leverage machine learning to bridge the gap between histology images, which are easier to obtain and share, and the granular markers revealed by high-end molecular sequencing. Although there has been substantial progress in computationally predicting molecular markers from HE images in bulk tissue samples, the limited spatial resolution of this data obscures the information in the underlying spatial organization that could support much more detailed characterization of the disease phenotype. As such, recent work combining histology imaging with spatially resolved transcriptomics to build predictive models, endeavors to push the envelope even further. Despite the increasing availability of paired histology and SRT data for training models, the field faces significant challenges in making these predictive approaches more accurate, clinically meaningful, and practically usable to have a shot at real-world adoption (e.g.[ this Review](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-56618-y.epdf?sharing_token=Lgqxbmk2Lub9_hRV9NHLENRgN0jAjWel9jnR3ZoTv0NtQlPNfoXX1H7ym5LeSJOBDfgwzmg3rtHFuPqGL5fHLpUEI_aPhN3ID5SEouoTsiY921lq3osQEHBYMJWaiwDnLjwCU9y5njHdSeAhEXH7XSON8eGeJXfYfD6YmMB6ErQ%3D)). 

We present this Challenge with the intent of highlighting this fundamental problem at the cutting edge of digital pathology to a wider community, and we hope that it will catalyze novel advances in AI-driven generation of insights from complex multimodal biological data.

## Why Cell Composition:

Although most existing approaches in the scientific literature tackle the prediction of individual genes' spatial expression profile from histology image features, this Challenge invites submissions to predict the spatial pattern of cell composition. This is a deliberate choice and our rationale is two-fold:

1. First, it remains a challenge for models to make consistently accurate predictions over all measured genes. This can distort the interpretation of models from a clinical utility perspective. Not all genes are likely to be of clinical relevance in a particular setting. Moreover, the inherently noisy nature of single-cell sequencing data and SRT measurements imposes a limitation on fair assessment of models by comparison against this imperfect ¡®ground truth¡¯. 

2. The prediction of cell composition presents a more meaningful goal, and differences in cell composition can by itself reveal interesting insights into tissue biology and disease-associated changes (for e.g., the spatial distribution of immune cell types in the TME, or the diversified organization of cell types across brain layers, can be very informative). By focusing on these higher-level aggregated biological signals (represented by cell types) revealed by the image inputs, rather than the lower-level noisier measurement signals at the gene-level, we are trading off granularity while still requiring that the model be capable of predicting robust spatially resolved patterns of biological interest.   

## Core Challenge
### Design a computational pipeline to predict the spatially resolved cell-type composition of a tissue sample, given its Hematoxylin-and-Eosin-stained (HE) slide image.
You are provided the positional coordinates of an array of ~2000 spots on a two-dimensional tissue slice from a tumor sample, accompanied by the corresponding aligned HE slide image. For each spot (~55 um diameter), you will be predicting the abundances of a set of predefined cell types, given the image information alone.

Teams are expected to develop predictive models for this purpose. We provide training data in the form of high-resolution histology images for 6 slides (tissue samples) along with the paired ground truth labels for the cellular composition of ~10k spots with ~55 um resolution. These ground truth labels represent the abundances of 35 biologically distinct cell types at every spot, and were assigned by a computational algorithm. The cell types have been inferred from the corresponding 10x Visium spatial transcriptomics data for the tissue sections. Given its relatively low resolution, each Visium spot can be associated with ~1-10 cells, requiring the application of deconvolution techniques to fine-map the composition of cell types at every spot. 

To encourage generalizable approaches that make use of the image features alone, the tissue identity and disease subtype of the samples are not disclosed. The original sample identifiers and the original cell type names have been masked (sample ids are replaced by generic labels S1-S7 and cell names by C1, C2,¡­ C35).

Entries will be evaluated on a hold-out test slide, the data for which comprises the positional coordinates of a grid of spots on a two-dimensional tissue slice, accompanied by the corresponding whole-slide high-resolution HE color image. The corresponding ground truth (spot-level cell type composition information) is withheld until the end of the Challenge deadline. Submissions will be compared in terms of their cell composition predictions against the ground truth cell composition across all the spots marked on the test slide. This will happen on two levels - a public leaderboard available through the course of the Challenge wherein teams will be ranked by the performance on a public test set, and a final private leaderboard based on a private test set, which will be released upon the completion of the Challenge.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Models will be evaluated on their ability to accurately predict the spatial cell-type composition across the test tissue slice. Each model will be scored by the [**Spearman rank correlation**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spearman%27s_rank_correlation_coefficient) between the predicted and ground truth cell type abundance vectors. The Spearman score will be calculated for each test spot first, then averaged across spots to obtain an aggregated score. 

The test slide has been divided into a *public test set* and a *private test set*. The aggregated score will be computed on the two groups separately. The cellular profiles of these two groups are weakly correlated, so performance on the public set would not necessarily reflect the projected performance on the private test set. The identity of each test spot (i.e. whether it falls in the public or private set) is provided along with the spot 2D coordinates in the Challenge data .h5 file.

To rank submissions on the final leaderboard, the aggregated Spearman score on the held-out *private test set* will be used. This leaderboard will be released only upon the end of the Challenge (post the final submission deadline). 

While the challenge is ongoing, teams will have an opportunity to assess where they stand on a public leaderboard, which will be based on scores on the *public test set*. The submission file must contain the predictions on both the public and private sets of spots, with the spot ids expected in the same order as in the Test slide (slide id S_7). Team scores on the private test set (which will be used for assigning the final team ranks) will only be revealed after the end date of the Challenge.


## Submission Requirements
### This is a Code Competition
Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time

GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time

Internet access disabled

Submission file must be named *submission.csv*. Predictions for each test sample (spot) are expected to be provided as a comma-separated list of floats (predictions for one spot per line) in the following format: `ID,C1,C2,¡­ C35`, where ID is the identifier for each spot in the test set, and C1-C35 are the abundances of the 35 possible cell types assigned to the spot.
----------------------
# Prizes

**Total Prizes Available: $10,000**
- 1st Place?-?**$5,000**
- 2nd Place - **$3,000**
- 3rd Place - **$2,000**",,False,True
9232,ts-1-e-9,"# Overview

**Welcome to the 2023 Kaggle Playground Series!** Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

**Your Goal:** For this Episode of the Series, your task is to use regression to predict the yield of wild blueberries. Good luck!

## NOTE:

You can now create your own synthetic versions of this dataset by forking and running [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/inversion/make-synthetic-blueberry-yield-data/).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using [Mean Squared Error (MSE)]

## Submission File
For each `Id` in the test set, you must predict the target `yield`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    Id,yield
    15289,6025.194
    15290,1256.223
    15291,357.44
    etc.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [Wild blueberry Yield Prediction Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shashwatwork/wild-blueberry-yield-prediction-dataset). (Since this is Playground 3.14, it seems like we need a Blueberry Pie joke here?) Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `yield` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict the `yield` given the other features
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9233,ai-based-modeling-for-energy-efficient-buildings,"# Overview

## Introduction and context

**Update:** winner announcement and final remarks were [posted in the discussion forum](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ai-based-modeling-for-energy-efficient-buildings/discussion/640972).

Modern commercial and industrial buildings increasingly rely on sophisticated Building Management Systems to coordinate their Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC). Such HVAC systems are very energy-intensive, often accounting for up to 50% of a typical facility's energy consumption. Efficient HVAC operation is therefore critical for achieving sustainability goals, reducing utility costs, and ensuring comfortable indoor environments. However, the inherent complexity of HVAC systems - which must respond dynamically to variations in weather, occupancy, equipment efficiency, and process requirements - makes them challenging to model and optimize  manually.

Join this competition to build cutting-edge Machine Learning (ML) methods that learn models of such HVAC systems from measurement data of such buildings.

We collected a large dataset including hundreds of sensor time series over a period of 1.5 years, of a large real-world building - the [Bosch Budapest Campus](https://www.homeofbosch.com/en/projects/campus-with-rooftop-gardens/). The focus is on a chilled water plant that cools the building. In the current competition, the idea is to model the cooling load's relation to other factors, and predict ahead this load. Such models and predictions are an important ingredient to optimize the energy efficiency of such HVAC systems.

**Example code available:** A [GitHub repository is available with example code for this competition](https://github.com/boschresearch/elias_aifbo), containing  simple, initial versions of the data loading/preprocessing/training/submission building blocks of a method for this competition. This allows a quick start. But note that all of the blocks may have to be adapted for the optimal method, in particular to load additional 2024 data.

**Stay tuned for a planned follow-up competition** that will focus on the optimization/control aspects, based on the current competition's models.

This competition is part of a project provided by [ELIAS - European Lighthouse of AI for Sustainability](https://elias-ai.eu/). This project has received funding from the European Union¡¯s Horizon Europe Research and Innovation Programme under Grant Agreement No. 101120237.

<img src=""https://elias-ai.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/elias_logo_big-1.png"" height=""40"" /><img src=""https://elias-ai.eu/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/EN_FundedbytheEU_RGB_WHITE-Outline-1.png""  style=""background-color:black;"" height=""40"" />


----------------------
# Description

The main goal of this competition is to learn models that accurately and robustly predict the building's cooling load based on available predictor variables. Additionally, interpretability of models and usefulness for building control are relevant. Specifically:
* **The to-be-predicted target variable** is the return temperature of chilled water captured in sensor variable ""B205WC000.AM02"", which we consider a good proxy for the cooling load of the building. See [Data section](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ai-based-modeling-for-energy-efficient-buildings-by-elias/data) and ""metadata.xlsx"" for more details on the sensors and background info.
* In the **test set(s)**, consisting of June and July 2025, this target variable is completely removed, and it has to be predicted from the remaining variables in the dataset (plus potential other information/data). Note that this means the task is not a classic forecasting task, but rather about understanding the relation to the other variables.
* **Evaluation criteria:** 
  * Evaluation happens via the **mean squared error (MSE, see details below)** on the test set, 
  * Additionally, we expect methods to be **""time-causal""** (predict future from past, not vice versa) with a lead (ahead) time of 3 hours. That is, to predict the target variable at time point t, only input variable values up to 3 hours before t should be used. We may ask potential winners to provide their code to prove their compliance with this criterion. (Note: the lead/ahead time requirement may be slightly adapted in the course of the competition if we see reasons for such an adaptation.)
  * Other aspects, like creative ways to enable **interpretability, robustness or multi-task learning** beyond the specific prediction task, while not a quantitative evaluation criterion, are nonetheless highly relevant and may be considered for honorable mentions. 
* In the **training data set**, consisting of January till May 2025, all variables including the target variable are contained.
* To further improve your method with **additional training data**, consider using the additional 2024 building data from Zenodo: [part 1](https://zenodo.org/records/12590466), [part 2](https://zenodo.org/records/14591934). To use it, first you have to download it, and then also adapt the [example code's](https://github.com/boschresearch/elias_aifbo) training set definition. Also, it is allowed to use reasonable, additional, publicly available data like bank holidays or weather data.

**Important note**: In spite of careful inspection and preprocessing of the provided data, undesired data leakage cannot completely be ruled out, due to the high number of sensors included. In case - contrary to all expectations - information leakage turns out to be still present, the competition administrators may adapt the data set during competition.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by calculating the [mean squared error (MSE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error) between the predicted values for the target variable and the ground truth values of the target variable, over the evaluation period. The target variable is the ""RETURN TEMPERATURE CHILLED WATER"" with filename ""B205WC000.AM02"". The evaluation period consists of June 2025 (folder ""RBHU-2025-06"") for the ""public test set"" and July 2025 (folder ""RBHU-2025-07"") for the ""private test set"" with a sampling period of 10 minutes, see below for details. 

Additional evaluation criteria apply, see above.


## Submission File

Note that the submission file creation can be done by the functions already given in the [example code](https://github.com/boschresearch/elias_aifbo). For each datetime (the ""ID"") in the period from June to July 2025, in 10 minute steps, you have to predict the target variable's value. The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ""ID"",""TARGET_VARIABLE""
    ""2025-06-01_00:00:00"",""...""
    ""2025-06-01_00:10:00"",""...""
    ""2025-06-01_00:20:00"",""...""
    ...
    ""2025-07-31_23:30:00"",""...""
    ""2025-07-31_23:40:00"",""...""
