9237,data-engineering-ds-gita,"# Overview

**Predict Who Will Survive? ????**

**Welcome to the Challenge!** 
Your task is simple: Use machine learning to predict which passengers survived the Titanic disaster. This challenge will test your ability to analyze data, find patterns, and build a predictive model.



----------------------
# Description

**The Story of the Titanic**

On April 15, 1912, the Titanic, one of the largest and most luxurious ships of its time, hit an iceberg and sank. Over 1,500 people lost their lives, while some managed to survive. But who had the highest chances of survival? Factors like age, ticket class, and gender played a key role. Your goal is to analyze passenger data and predict who was most likely to survive.


----------------------
# How It Works

- **Download the Dataset** ¨C You'll receive two files:
1. train.csv (includes passengers and whether they survived)
1.  test.csv (similar but missing survival info¡ªyou predict this!)
- **Train Your Model** ¨C Use machine learning to find patterns in the training data.
- **Make Predictions** ¨C Apply your model to the test data to predict survival.
- **Submit Your Results** ¨C Upload a CSV file with your predictions.

##Submission File Format
Your submission file must meet these requirements:
? Exactly 418 entries + a header row
? Only 2 columns: ***PassengerId*** and ***Survived***
? ***PassengerId*** can be in any order
? ***Survived*** column must contain only 1 (survived) or 0 (deceased)
? Extra columns or rows will cause an error


**Evaluation**

Model performance will be measured by *accuracy*, which is based on how many passengers you correctly predict as survivors (1) or non-survivors (0).

**Download the dataset, start analyzing, and see how well your model predicts survival! ??**","# Dataset Description

**Overview**
The dataset is split into two parts:
- Training set (**train.csv**) ¨C This contains passengers and their survival outcomes. You¡¯ll use it to train your model.
- Test set (**test.csv**) ¨C This has similar data but without survival labels. Your model will predict these.
- Additionally, you will find **sample_submission.csv**, a sample submission file in the correct format


## Data Dictionary

| Variable | Definition | Key |
| ------------- |:-------------:| -----:|
| Survival  | Survival |0 = No, 1 = Yes |
| Pclass     | Ticket class (proxy for socio-economic status)   |   1 = Upper, 2 = Middle, 3 = Lower |
| Sex | Passenger¡¯s gender   |    Male / Female|
| Age | Age in years    |   Fractional if under 1 year |
| SibSp| Number of siblings/spouses aboard  |  - |
| Parch| Number of parents/children aboard   |  	- |
| Ticket| Ticket number	  |    	- |
| Fare| Passenger fare	   |   	- |
| Cabin|Cabin number  |   - |
| Embarked| Port of Embarkation  |    C = Cherbourg, Q = Queenstown, S = Southampton |


## Notes on Variables:
- *Pclass*: A proxy for socio-economic status (SES).
1st = Upper class
2nd = Middle class
3rd = Lower class

- *Age* : If less than 1 year old, the age is fractional. If estimated, it appears as xx.5.

- *SibSp* (Siblings/Spouses aboard):
Sibling = Brother, sister, stepbrother, stepsister
Spouse = Husband, wife (excluding fianc¨¦s and mistresses)

- *Parch* (Parents/Children aboard):
Parent = Mother, father
Child = Son, daughter, stepson, stepdaughter
Some children traveled only with a nanny, so parch = 0 for them.",False,True
9238,ex-8-parameter-tuning-io-t-b,"# Overview

## Welcome to the 2023 edition of Kaggle's Playground Series!

Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to Season 3 Playground Series so far! 

With the same goal to give the Kaggle community a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science, we will continue launching the Tabular Tuesday in September every Tuesday 00:00 UTC, with each competition running for 3 weeks. Again, these will be fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc. 

**Your Goal:** Given various medical indicators, predict the health outcomes of horses.
----------------------
# Description

### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [micro-averaged F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) between pricted and actual values. 


### Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the corresponding `outcome`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,outcome
    1235,lived
    1236,lived
    1237,died
    etc.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - September 12, 2023
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  October 2, 2023

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on a portion of the [Horse Survival Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/yasserh/horse-survival-dataset). Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

Good luck!

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `outcome` is the (categorical) target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict `outcome`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9239,slack-11785-hw-2-p-2-face-verification-spring-2025,"# Overview

This is the Face Verification Kaggle competition for 11-785 HW2P2, Spring 2025
----------------------
# Description

In this homework, you will work on pattern recognition problems that require position invariance. Specifically, you will work on the problem of verifying faces in images. In typical pictures of faces, the face is rarely perfectly centered. Different pictures of the same person may have the face shifted by varying amounts. The classifier must recognize the face regardless of this indeterminacy of position. This calls for position-invariant models, specifically Convolutional Neural Networks, or CNNs.

Face verification refers to the task of determining whether two face images are of the same person, without necessarily knowing who the person is. Face verification is an instance of a larger class of problems where we attempt to determine if two data instances belong to the same class without necessarily knowing (or having a model for) the class itself.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric: EER (Equal Error Rate)","# Dataset Description

The verification dataset consists of images and a verification pair list (for validation and testing) in a txt file.",False,True
9240,glorys-12-eddy-detection,"# Overview

Welcome to Mercator Ocean AI competition. The project is a part of ISAE-SUPAERO Hackathon 2024.
----------------------
# Description

Welcome to Mercator Ocean AI competition. You chose this topic as you may be interested in geophysics and environmental field. Do not hesitate to take contact with Mercator Ocean team members to know more about our activities and missions.

You will find below some useful information about your project. 

Oceanic Eddies are  vortices of the order of ~10km in horizontal scale, whose signature is clearly visible in satellite products and well reproduced in ocean models. Sea Level Anomalies (SLA) are used for detecting such object by means of numerical techniques, yet such approach lacks accuracy in detection, namely because Sea Level Anomaly contains errors. Eddy signature is also visible in other variables such as Sea surface Temperature (SST) that do not suffer from such limitation.

The objective here is to develop a Deep Learning approach to identify eddies using SST, SLA and the ocean velocity field. For this purpose you have a dataset consisting of  SLA, SST, and velocity components images which are slightly distorted relative to reality (we call this distorted reality - the reanalysis product), together with labels (the eddy contours, see figure) obtained directly from reality.

Since we can not obtain the real state of the ocean, the so called reality comes from it's virtual emulation. Consequently, we suppose that the ocean is a perfect fluid which can be described by primitive equations (e.g. Navier-Stokes). This virtual reality is then considered as a real time ocean state. 

On the other hand, in reality oceanographers have to model the ocean dynamics driven among other by various sources forcing, such as wind or atmosphere-ocean interactions. Furthermore, thanks to the remote sensing advances, a significant amount of satellite tracked and in-situ observations data are available all around the globe. This data is extremely useful as it is the only source providing the knowledge of the real ocean dynamics. In operational oceanography the process of incorporating the real ocean observations (remote and in-situ) into the modeled ocean an assimilation. However, the assimilation process is in it's core a numerically challenging task, firstly due to the sparse spatial and temporal distribution of observations, and secondly due to the resolution issues. It is worth mentioning, that a number of compromises have to be accepted during the assimilation process so that the modeled ocean contains as much real observations data as possible whilst the overall flui dynamics is not disturbed. Those compromises are source of inaccuracies in modeled ocean representation. Operational oceanography is in constant research of novel ways of data assimialtion, model validation and correction. 

This project presents a big interest for operational oceanography regarding the importance of a reliable ocean dynamics modeling for real time analysis,  forecast, as well as for numerous applications, for instance floating trajectories simulation. being able to detect eddies in the distorted virtual reality, called here and after "" NatRun OSSE reanalysis "", would provide a novel way for oceanographers to detect eddies in real modeled ocean, called here and after "" Reanalysis product "". Thus, your contribution may open a brand new way of reanalysis product validation through AI eddies detection framework.  
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Weighted Accuracy estimated as follows : 

$$
Acc_w = \frac{\sum_i w_i \cdot \mathbb{1}(y_i = \hat{y}_i)}{\sum_i w_i}, \quad \text{where } w_i =
\begin{cases} 
0.4, & \text{if } y_i = 1 \text{ (cyclonic)} \\
0.4, & \text{if } y_i = 2 \text{ (anti-cyclonic)} \\
0.2, & \text{if } y_i = 0 \text{ (no eddy)}
\end{cases}
$$


This metric will concern all three classes, as so you will be evaluated regarding three values : 
$$ Acc_{cyclonic}, Acc_{anti-cyclonic}, Acc_{no-eddies} $$

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the eddy type: 0 for no eddy, 1 for cyclonic eddy and 2 for anticyclonic eddy. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,PREDICTED
    0_0_0,0
    0_0_1,1
    0_0_2,1
    ...
    42_0_1, 2
    etc.","# Dataset Description

In this section we will explain you the data and how you can use it. 
As described in Overview section, the main goal of the project is to build a model performing segmentation through supervised learning. We will study the Gulf Stream region. 
Data from various sources is available : classified eddies maps from PyEddyTracker tool run on NatRun product (virtual reality ~ real ocean); physical variables from NatRun OSSE 3NADIRS product (distorted reality ~ modeled ocean). Thus, we aim to train a model which detects eddies from the distorted reality, i.e. NatRun OSSE 3NADIRS product (as generally it is the only information about the ocean state available), given the ground-truth provided by a numerical (statistical) tool. 

You will work here with .nc files, this is a usual format for climatology and geophysical data storage. Each .nc file contains dimensions (coordinates, e.g. Latitude, Longitude, Time) and associated variables (velocities, temperature, salinity, sea surface height). A useful tool to work with such file format is xarray library. 

## Files

*   **eddies_train.nc** - the training set of eddies maps for the given region
*   **OSSE_U_V_SLA_SST_train.nc** - the training set of OSSE 3NADIRS physical variables for the given region
*   **OSSE_U_V_SLA_SST_test.nc** - the test set of OSSE 3NADIRS physical variables for the given region

The eddies maps are images of [Time, Latitude, Longitude] dimension. Each map (image) is regularly sampled, one cell corresponds to 1/12¡ã (~100km). They contain the information on the eddy type per individual pixel (cell). We distinguish 3 classes (that should be predicted by your model) : 0 - no eddy detected, 1 - cyclonic eddy, 2 - anti-cyclonic eddy. An additional fourth class is given to take into account the coast (Gulf Stream being closely situated to the coast), we gave it a 999 identifier. Your model is not supposed to predict this class, however it should be able to process the ground correctly. 
Physical variables are also given as images of the same spatio-temporal resolution as eddies maps. Note that in physical variables maps the ground corresponds to NaN values. It is important to be careful with NaN values while training your model, take time to brainstorm with your team mates on how you can work with such values (pre-processing / mask / etc). You will find following variables in your .nc files : vozocrtxT - horizontal velocity component; vomecrtyT - vertical velocity component; sossheig - Sea Surface Height (SSH); votemper - Sea Surface Temperature (SST). You do not need to use all physical variables at a time. Take time to test and conclude on the influence of each variable on your model's performance (both in prediction ability and computational effectiveness). 


## Columns in your submission file
Your submission file should contains two columns : Id and Predicted. The Id column corresponds to each individual pixel of each spatio-temporal map. Each Id is a string: DayNumber_LatitudePixelNumber_LongitudePixelNumber. For this purpose, once eddies types are predicted (attention to the fourth ground's class), you should flatten your final predicted array of size [Time, Latitude, Longitude] to the new size [Time x Latitude x Longitude], fill the 'Predicted' column in the corresponding .csv file and add the corresponding Id column.  Please, refer to our team members for further details if necessary.",False,True
9241,comp-5554-wk-7-code-chanllenge,"# Overview

Welcome to the COMP5554 Advanced Artificial Intelligence weekly code challenge! This friendly competition is designed to test your skills in building predictive models using machine learning. Participation is optional but encouraged for all students in the class. Good luck, and happy coding! ??

**Objective:**
In this project, your goal is to build a LLM reasoning framework to give answers on the given multiple choice questions that require different types of commonsense knowledge to predict the correct answers.

**Submission Format:** 
Your predictions must follow the format specified in the data.

**Submission Rules:**
- Each participant or team can make up to 5 submissions to the public leaderboard.
- You must select one submission for evaluation on the private leaderboard.

**Teams:**
- Update your team name under the ""Team"" column to reflect your current group name.
- You can invite groupmates to join your team or have a single representative submit predictions for your group.","# Dataset Description

This dataset is a multiple-choice question answering dataset that requires different types of commonsense knowledge to predict the correct answers

## Files

*   **example.json** - some examples for the dataset
*   **testing.json** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
