9264,annual-statistics-of-the-banking-industry,"# Overview

The banking industry relies on accurate financial forecasting to make strategic decisions. In this competition, participants will use historical Annual Statistics of the Banking Industry from Central Bank of Sri Lanka to predict key financial metrics for the years 2019, 2020, and 2021. 
----------------------
# Description

Accurate financial forecasting is essential for the banking industry. In this competition, you'll use historical banking data (1998¨C2018) of the Banking Industry from Central Bank of Sri Lanka to predict key financial metrics for 2019, 2020, and 2021. Can you build the best predictive model?`

Your goal is to build a predictive model that forecasts the following financial indicators for the specified years

(1) **Assets**,(2) **Gross Loans and Advances**, (3) **Net Loans and Advances**

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using **Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE)**, ensuring that predictions closely match real-world trends.","# Dataset Description

Participants will receive a dataset containing historical financial data from 1998 to 2018. The dataset includes annual statistics for multiple financial indicators across different banking sectors in Sri Lanka.

Dataset Link : https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1ulonw5N-IPTR677q5Oo_HWtgYYEBjcr5/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=111612278374439388930&rtpof=true&sd=true

Training Data: 1998¨C2018 (complete values)

Test Data: 2019¨C2021 (target values missing)

Format: CSV file with columns representing financial indicators and years.",False,True
9265,gds-exercise-3-2025-,"# Overview

# Welcome to the Exoplanet Habitability Challenge!

In the last few decades, astronomers have confirmed the existence of 5,834 exoplanets across 4,356 planetary systems. Among these countless alien worlds, a vital question arises: **Which of these exoplanets could be habitable for humans?**

This competition brings astronomy and machine learning together to predict exoplanet habitability. With the NASA Exoplanet Archive in your hands, your task is to build a classifier that distinguishes potentially habitable planets from non-habitable ones. 

Your leaderboard rank will be based on the **F1-score**.
As you design your models, consider the features that could indicate habitability.

[Read these instructions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission) on how to make your submissions.
----------------------
# Description

Your task is to build a model that predicts whether an exoplanet is potentially habitable (1) or non-habitable (0) based on 377 features (see metaData.csv).

Due to the imbalanced nature of the dataset¡ªwhere habitable planets are much rarer than uninhabitable ones¡ªthe evaluation metric is the F1-score, which balances precision (the proportion of correct positive predictions) and recall (the proportion of actual positives identified). This is more informative than accuracy alone; for example, a model that always predicts the majority class could achieve high accuracy but would fail to detect habitable planets.

You are encouraged to experiment with oversampling techniques (e.g., SMOTE), class weighting, or other methods to address the imbalance and improve detection of the minority class.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [the F1-score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score).

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for the TARGET variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,P_HABITABLE
    5198,0
    2000,1
    4236,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

- **train.csv** ¨C your labeled set of data, consisting of our *existing* knowledge of habitable exoplanets.

```python
import pandas as pd

data = pd.read_csv('train.csv')
X_train = data.drop(columns=['y_train'])  # DataFrame of shape (n_samples, 372) where each row represents an exoplanet and each column is a feature.
y_train = data['y_train']                # Series of shape (n_samples,), containing binary habitability labels (1 for potentially habitable, 0 otherwise).
```

- **test.csv** ¨C exoplanets in the test set, but with unknown habitability (without labels)¡ªit is your job to predict these.

- **sample_submission.csv** ¨C a sample submission file in the correct format for reference.

When preparing your submission, load sample_submission.csv and insert your predictions to validate the order of the ""ID"" column. For example:

```python
sample = pd.read_csv('sample_submission.csv')
sample['P_HABITABLE'] = y_pred  # assuming y_pred contains your predictions
sample.to_csv('submission.csv', index=False)
```

- **metaData.csv** ¨C supplemental information about the data

- `y_train` ¨C Binary label indicating exoplanet habitability:
  - `1`: The exoplanet is potentially habitable.
  - `0`: The exoplanet is non-habitable.

- **exampleESI.csv** ¨C an example file consisting of 50 exoplanets with their `ESI` values for reference.",False,True
9266,hat-or-no-hat-that-is-the-question-spring-25,"# Overview

The problem is simple. Bring your recently learned Deep Learning knowledge to detect if a given image contains a person with a hat or not. Simple, right?
----------------------
# Description

You are not given any specific training dataset! Strange, right? But, you do need a training dataset to build model. You can do this in at least one of two approach: (i) build your own labeled training dataset, by going out taking photos, or web search, (ii) in the past I found students using the CelebA dataset ( [https://mmlab.ie.cuhk.edu.hk/projects/CelebA.html](https://mmlab.ie.cuhk.edu.hk/projects/CelebA.html) ) which coincidentally  has an attribute called `Wearing hat`, which you will find advantageous for this project. For the first approach, if it seems collecting images is easy than to label them, you may resort to using the `semi-supervised learning` technique. You may not need to go down that road, but I am just saying :) 

You need to build a deep learning model and predict all the test images for the `hat-ness` test, i.e., if image contains a person with a hat or not. This provided test set should be considered for judging, and of course is unlabeled, and used to produce the rank of your entry in the live ranklist.

My policy while constructing the `test-set` was:
1. Photos of real people, i.e., absolutely no cartoons, nor apparitions.
2. Photos may contain multiple people with their heads (with or without hat) visible. If I see at least one person wearing hat, I would label it as a `Hat` instance.
3. If a person's head is covered either with a scarf, or bandana, hijab, beanie, etc, might as well be considered person with `hat` during evaluation.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on `Categorization accuracy`. Higher the better.

Please note, a sample with `id` should contain a corresponding `class` value that belongs to this set: `{'Hat', 'No Hat'}`.


## Submission File
For each `id` corresponding to an image file in the `test-set/` directory, you must predict a class label for the `class` variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,class
    a51f75effb1ddecbdc415f2c04856dca,No Hat
    4f1a9f55fec2af7a38e425098955b53e,No Hat
    494027738fc26f214792b9c389455528,No Hat
    07af6e551654de3d522a79f87801b288,No Hat
    fc965946777a2192bd76fc768a7a87d2,No Hat
    d9cee025cb3007e59f903724fc4ebab4,No Hat
    8207a51323622887aef5c7060705f9b8,No Hat
    877c11ba4e9f4f991bdd959b9a940929,No Hat
    9427c232457eb4f35230ef9557237956,No Hat
    38b77bdf40c69d39affe50e83f9ce1dd,No Hat
    etc.

Please note:
1. The `class` values mentioned above as well as in the `sample_submission.csv` file are intentionally set to a constant value `No Hat`. It was set for illustration purposes only. True values will be used during evaluation.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **test_set/** - the test set directory containing exactly `1490` image files.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
9267,birth-weight-prediction-spring-25,"# Description

I am quoting from the WebMD article:
¡°A new baby's gender, name, time of birth, and birth weight are nice information for a birth announcement, but birth weight is especially important for an obstetrician. A large size at delivery has long been associated with an increased risk of injuries to a newborn and its mom. So the better a doctor can predict birth weight, the easier the delivery may be.¡±

Ultrasound is a popular way of doing it. But, aha! You are a Data Scientist (or a ¡°going to be one¡±(!)). You can amaze people by predicting the birth weight way earlier than ultrasound, right? In this assignment, let¡¯s accomplish this.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSLE, Root Mean Square Logarithmic Error. 

RMSLE is defined as : 
![RMSLE = ](https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=12yVAH2wZO17u4KTC1UN8kplaehvZQd-P&amp;sz=w700-h130)

where, 
* `n` is total number of samples in the evaluation dataset.
* `y_i` is the true/known target label for the `i_th` sample
* y&#770;_i is the predicted target for the input sample xi.
* `log(.)` is the natural logarithm.
<hr>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and BWEIGHT </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID,BWEIGHT<br>1,10.1875<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Dataset Description</h2>
In the ¡°dataset/¡± you will find two csv and a txt files: 
<ul>
<li>baby-weights-dataset.csv</li>
