9277,stem-challenge-task-1-competition,"# Overview

The skies are bustling with activity, but not all aircraft belong there. Your mission is to develop the skills needed to **detect and track unauthorized aircrafts using machine learning**. In this first task of *Operation Sky Watchman*, your team will use a **pre-trained YOLOv11 mode**l to perform object detection on images of aircraft. This task focuses on understanding how to run inference, submit predictions, and interpret model outputs.
----------------------
# Description

#### Mission Briefing: Understanding Inference
In this competition, your objective is to load and apply a pre-trained YOLOv11 model to identify objects in aerial images. You will be engaged in operations involving exclusively two distinct categories of aircraft, both designated as 'airplane,' for the duration of this task. Your task is to run the provided model on the dataset, generate predictions, and submit your results for evaluation. This stage is all about understanding how machine learning models make predictions without requiring you to train or modify the model itself.

[How Math makes Machine Learning easy (and how you can learn it)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?app=desktop&v=wOTFGRSUQ6Q&t=0s)
#### The Science Behind Object Detection
Object detection is a critical tool in monitoring and protecting airspace. YOLO (You Only Look Once) is a powerful real-time object detection model used in various industries. By running inference on a pre-trained YOLOv11 model, you will observe how AI can identify aircraft in images and begin to understand how these models work in practical scenarios.

#### Your Mission Objectives
1. Load the provided YOLOv11 model.
2. Run inference on the dataset to identify and locate aircraft.
3. Submit your results and evaluate your performance on the leaderboard.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be evaluated based on the Mean Average Precision (mAP50) metric, a standard performance measure for object detection models. This metric calculates the precision and recall at an Intersection over Union (IoU) threshold of 0.50.

#### Evaluation Criteria:
- Accuracy (mAP50): Higher mAP indicates better detection and classification of objects.
- Submission Quality: Ensure your predictions are correctly formatted for scoring.
- Understanding Inference: You should be able to explain how the model detects objects and how changes in the input affect the output.

The leaderboard will rank participants by their mAP50 score, with ties broken by the speed of inference.
----------------------
# Terminologies

- The **Intersection over Union (IoU)** is a metric used to assess the degree of overlap between a predicted bounding box (the box created by a model to enclose an object) and the true bounding box (the actual location of the object). A large overlap indicates a good prediction, while a small overlap signifies a poor prediction.
![Alt text](https://framerusercontent.com/images/i3Kpx2SJ9ji7C3vwiMm0wJbjZ8.png)
Image from https://www.v7labs.com/blog/mean-average-precision
- **Confusion Matrix**
![Alt text](https://framerusercontent.com/images/6fYj0ziVKvUIv8eXRrdxjyxGFN4.png =500x500 ""Optional title"")
Image from https://www.v7labs.com/blog/mean-average-precision
 
   For this competition, we focus on the following three terms (True Negatives do not apply to object detection). 
              - True Positives (TP): Correct predictions where the model identified an object correctly
              - False Positives (FP): Incorrect predictions where the model falsely identified an object where there is none.
              - False Negatives (FN): Cases where the model missed identifying an object that was actually present.
    For mAP50, we set a threshold where the model is considered correct if IoU ¡Ý 0.50 (50%). More specifically,
              - If the IoU is greater than or equal to 0.50, the predicted bounding box is considered a True Positive (TP).
              - If the IoU is less than 0.50, the predicted bounding box is a False Positive (FP).
              - If a ground truth object does not have any predicted bounding box with an IoU ¡Ý 0.50, it is considered a False Negative (FN).
![](https://framerusercontent.com/images/hhh8iuuEVrIdelh9HQG7dFCxP5Y.png)
Image from https://www.v7labs.com/blog/mean-average-precision
- Precision and Recall are two important metrics used to evaluate the performance of an object detection model.
    - **Precision** measures how many of the *predicted* objects are actually correct. In other words, it tells you how accurate the model is when it makes a positive prediction. It is important to measure precision so you know a high recall level has not been reached only while making an abundance of erroneous predictions.
$$\mbox{Precision} = \frac{\mbox{TP}}{\mbox{TP}+\mbox{FP}}$$
    - **Recall** measures how many of the *actual* objects in the dataset were correctly identified by the model. In other words, it tells you how well the model is capturing all the objects that are present. Since you could reach high precision only by accurately locating a few objects in the dataset while missing the rest, it is important to measure recall.
$$\mbox{Recall} = \frac{\mbox{TP}}{\mbox{TP}+\mbox{FN}}$$
    - When a model has high recall but low precision, it correctly identifies most positive samples but also has many false positives (incorrectly classifying negative samples as positive). In contrast, when a model has high precision but low recall, it is accurate when it classifies a sample as positive but misses many true positive samples.
- The mAP is the mean of average precision (AP) values calculated across recall values from 0 to 1. It balances precision and recall, accounting for both false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN), making it ideal for most detection applications.
----------------------
# How Mean Average Precision at IoU threshold 0.5 (mAP50) is Calculated?

1. Generate the prediction: The object detection model takes an image as input and processes it through its layers (such as convolutional layers in CNNs). The model then outputs 
     - Object's class (e.g., ""aircraft"") 
     - Bounding Box Coordinates: The coordinates of the predicted bounding box (e.g., [xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax]) that indicate where the object is located in the image
     - Confidence Score: The likelihood or probability of the object being correctly identified within the predicted bounding box
2. Calculate the IoU for each predicted bounding box to all ground truth bounding boxes for the same class
3. For each class:
     - Sort predicted bounding boxes based on their confidence scores in descending order. The confidence score is a number the model adds to each of its predictions; it tells you how certain the model is that its prediction is correct. In practice, a *confidence threshold* is chosen and predictions below this threshold are ignored. Since different models work best with different confidence thresholds, mAP is measured across *all* confidence thresholds.
     - Calculate the precision and recall for each confidence threshold. 
     - Compute Average Precision (AP): AP is the area under the precision-recall curve. A **precision-recall curve** illustrates the trade-off between precision and recall at various confidence thresholds. As the threshold changes, the model adjusts its classification of positive and negative samples, leading to different values for precision and recall. Typically, as recall increases (by lowering the threshold), precision tends to decrease, and vice versa. The curve helps visualize how well the model performs across different thresholds.
![Alt text](https://doimages.nyc3.cdn.digitaloceanspaces.com/010AI-ML/content/images/2020/09/Fig01.png =500x500 """")
Image from https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/mean-average-precision
4. Measure the mean average precision (mAP), the average of the average precisions for each class:
$$mAP = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \mbox{AP}_i$$
where N is the number of classes and \\( \mbox{AP}_i \\)  is the AP for class i.

### Source: 
- https://www.v7labs.com/blog/mean-average-precision
- https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/mean-average-precision
- https://docs.ultralytics.com/guides/yolo-performance-metrics/#introduction
