Nh?ng suy ngh? tr¨ºn s? ???c c? th? h¨®a trong cu?c thi n¨¤y ?? nh?n m?nh ?i?u ?¨® h?n n?a v¨¤ qua ?¨®, c¨® th? t?p trung s? ch¨² y c?a nh?ng ng??i ?am m¨º khoa h?c d? li?u v¨¤o m?t cau h?i quan tr?ng kh?ng th? b? qua: *Li?u b?n c¨® th? t¨¬m ra t?p h?p t?i thi?u ??i di?n cho to¨¤n b? 10.000 ?i?m d? li?u n¨¤y kh?ng?*

**M?c ti¨ºu c?a b?n:** T¨¬m *t?p ??i di?n* c?a t?t c? 10.000 ?i?m d? li?u ???c d¨´ng ?? m? ph?ng n¨ºn d? li?u c?a cu?c thi n¨¤y. Ch¨²c may m?n!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Maximizing the following score (in Python) will help you achieve the criteria of a *representative set*:
`score = np.sum((counts>0).astype(float))/len(counts)-0.5*(np.mean(counts)-1)`

Where, `counts` is a numpy 1D array (with a length equal to the size of the ground-truth *representative set*) such that the value of `counts[i]` is the number of data points in the submission file that actually represented by the *i*-th data point in the ground-truth *representative set*. 

The maximum possible score will be 1.0 if and only if all representative points are found (the non-negative term of the formula) and no two points represent each other (the penalty term).

