All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

Technically, this dataset condenses the training process (batch size for each parameter update is 1,024 data points) consisting of 40,000 parameter updates on a dataset of 10,000 data points. There are two relevant pieces of information to know:
1. The file ""data_indices.npy"" contains an array of size `[40000, 1024]`. Accordingly, each row represents a data batch of each iteration of the training process. Row *i* of the array is the indices of the data points of the training batch at the *i+1*-th update, and the training information of this row will correspond to row *i* of the array stored in the ""gradient_indicators.npy"" file, which will be discussed below.
2. Row *i* of the 2D array in the file ""gradient_indicators.npy"" is information about the training process of the neural network and will not be stored as the value of the objective function (loss function) but as indicator of the increase(+1)/decrease(-1) of the objective function, compared to the state of the neural network at the end of the previous update, at the data point whose index is stored in the ""data_indices.npy"" file (as mentioned).

You may also want to review [this example notebook for the underlying data generation process and evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/code/thienanlnguyen/data-generation-scoring-sample-code).

*Hint for starting: While abstracting the model training process in this way helps focus the effort on exploiting the representative structure of the data (rather than having to apply traditional black-box methods to the raw data set), it can also be somewhat daunting for those accustomed to the traditional approach. So, if that¡¯s the case for you, perhaps converting the training information into a graph structure using the concept of [cohesive convergence groups](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.05610v1) might be a starting point.*

## Files

*   **data_indices.npy** - indices of training batches
*   **gradient_indicators.npy** - training information
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
